window.SPC_INDEX={"cats":[{"id":"history","t":"History & Roadmap","s":"History","c":"#a8781a","u":"categories/history/index.html","n":6},{"id":"sharepoint-onprem","t":"SharePoint On-Premises","s":"SP On-Prem","c":"#2f7d72","u":"categories/sharepoint-onprem/index.html","n":29},{"id":"sharepoint-online","t":"SharePoint Online & Microsoft 365","s":"SP Online","c":"#2b6f9e","u":"categories/sharepoint-online/index.html","n":31},{"id":"spfx","t":"SharePoint Framework (SPFx)","s":"SPFx","c":"#4f56a6","u":"categories/spfx/index.html","n":32},{"id":"power-apps","t":"Power Apps","s":"Power Apps","c":"#8e4a8f","u":"categories/power-apps/index.html","n":26},{"id":"power-automate","t":"Power Automate","s":"Power Automate","c":"#2f62b0","u":"categories/power-automate/index.html","n":22},{"id":"dataverse","t":"Dataverse","s":"Dataverse","c":"#2f8059","u":"categories/dataverse/index.html","n":16},{"id":"dataflows","t":"Dataflows & Power Query","s":"Dataflows","c":"#5a7f2b","u":"categories/dataflows/index.html","n":8},{"id":"nintex","t":"Nintex","s":"Nintex","c":"#c0622b","u":"categories/nintex/index.html","n":18},{"id":"infopath-legacy","t":"InfoPath & Legacy Workflows","s":"InfoPath & Legacy","c":"#7a5aa6","u":"categories/infopath-legacy/index.html","n":11},{"id":"migration","t":"Migration & ShareGate","s":"Migration","c":"#b8434b","u":"categories/migration/index.html","n":20},{"id":"azure","t":"Azure for Microsoft 365 Devs","s":"Azure","c":"#1f7fa8","u":"categories/azure/index.html","n":15},{"id":"apis","t":"Graph, REST & PnP","s":"APIs & PnP","c":"#8f7414","u":"categories/apis/index.html","n":12},{"id":"ai-copilot","t":"Copilot & AI","s":"Copilot & AI","c":"#b24a7c","u":"categories/ai-copilot/index.html","n":6},{"id":"architecture-docs","t":"Solution Design & Documentation","s":"Design & Docs","c":"#5d5aa0","u":"categories/architecture-docs/index.html","n":14},{"id":"interview","t":"Interview Q&A","s":"Interview","c":"#5f7f1f","u":"categories/interview/index.html","n":13}],"items":[{"t":"SharePoint & Microsoft 365 Timeline, 2001–2026","c":"history","u":"articles/history/sharepoint-timeline.html","s":"Every major release, platform shift and retirement from SharePoint Portal Server 2001 to the agents era, in one interactive timeline with context on why each change happened.","k":"history, timeline, sharepoint versions, roadmap, retirement","h":"The four eras at a glance · Year by year · Server versions compared · How the development model evolved · What this means for you in 2026 · Test yourself · Which SharePoint version introduced the add-in (app) model and the /_api REST endpoint? · Why did Microsoft deprecate farm and sandboxed solutions for SharePoint Online? · Which on-premises SharePoint version is still supported after July 2026?","l":"Beginner","x":"Timelines","b":"SharePoint started as a document-management portal on top of Exchange storage. Twenty-five years later it is the content backbone of Microsoft 365: it stores Teams files, feeds Copilot, and hosts low-code apps and agents. Knowing that path explains why so many technologies on this site exist, and why some are being retired. 25 years from Portal Server 2001 to 2026 8 server versions 2001, 2003, 2007, 2010, 2013, 2016, 2019, SE 4 dev models Farm → Sandbox → Add-ins → SPFx 3 workflow engines WF 3.5 (2010), Workflow Manager (2013), Power Automate # The four eras at a glance timeline title SharePoint eras section Portal era 2001 : SharePoint Portal Server 2001 : SharePoint Team Services 2003 : WSS 2.0 + SPS 2003 (SQL + ASP.NET) section Platform era 2007 : MOSS 2007 – content types, features, WF workflows 2010 : SharePoint 2010 – service apps, CSOM, sandbox section Cloud & app-model era 2011 : Office 365 / SharePoint Online 2013 : Add-in model, REST _api, Workflow Manager 2016 : SP 2016 MinRole, modern lists, PowerApps & Flow 2017 : SPFx GA, communication sites, Teams section Platform-of-platforms & AI era 2020 : Dataverse, Microsoft Lists 2021 : Subscription Edition, Viva 2023 : Microsoft 365 Copilot, SharePoint Premium 2026 : Add-ins, ACS & 2013 workflows retired · agents # Year by year 2001 Server SharePoint Portal Server 2001 Built on the Exchange Web Storage System. It offered document management, search and digital dashboards with \"web parts\". SharePoint Team Services shipped with FrontPage 2002 for team collaboration. 2003 Server Windows SharePoint Services 2.0 & SharePoint Portal Server 2003 A rewrite on SQL Server + ASP.NET . WSS became a free Windows Server component, and the site/list/library model we still use today appeared. 2003 Forms InfoPath 2003 An XML-based electronic forms designer. Its forms became the default way to build complex SharePoint forms for the next decade. 2006–07 Server MOSS 2007 & WSS 3.0 Content types, site columns, Features & Solutions (WSP) , Workflow Foundation workflows, InfoPath Forms Services, Excel Services, the BDC, publishing (CMS merged in), and recycle bins. 2007 Tools SharePoint Designer 2007 Replaced FrontPage for SharePoint. Power users could build no-code declarative workflows and data views. 2007–08 Ecosystem Nintex Workflow 2007 A drag-and-drop browser workflow designer for MOSS. Nintex became the de-facto enterp"},{"t":"Power Platform & Microsoft 365 Developer Timeline (2015–2026)","c":"history","u":"articles/history/power-platform-timeline.html","s":"How PowerApps, Microsoft Flow, the Common Data Service, Power BI and Power Virtual Agents became the Power Platform, then Dataverse, Copilot Studio and agents, plus the parallel evolution of Microsoft Graph, Teams and SPFx.","k":"history, power platform, power apps, power automate, dataverse, copilot studio, timeline","h":"Interview questions · What was Power Automate called before 2019? · What did Power Virtual Agents become?","l":"Beginner","x":"Timelines","b":"2015 Low-code Project Siena → PowerApps preview; Microsoft Graph GA PowerApps and Microsoft Flow previews were announced; the unified Graph API reached GA. 2016 Low-code PowerApps & Microsoft Flow GA General availability (late 2016), with the first Common Data Service and connectors such as SharePoint. 2017 Dev Microsoft Teams & SPFx GA Teams launches; SPFx becomes the SharePoint dev model; PowerApps forms for SharePoint lists arrive (2018 GA). 2018 Low-code Model-driven apps join PowerApps Dynamics 365's model-driven UI becomes part of PowerApps; CDS for Apps. 2019 Low-code Power Platform brand; Flow → Power Automate Ignite 2019 renames Flow, adds RPA (UI flows) and Power Virtual Agents. 2020 Data Dataverse & Dataverse for Teams CDS renamed Microsoft Dataverse; Dataverse for Teams launched; Power Automate Desktop free for Windows (2021). 2021 Low-code Power Fx & Power Pages roots Power Fx announced as the low-code language; portals evolve (Power Pages in 2022). 2022 Governance Power Pages, Managed Environments Power Apps portals become Power Pages; Managed Environments for governance (2023 GA). 2023 AI Copilot everywhere; Copilot Studio Copilot in Power Apps/Automate; Power Virtual Agents becomes Copilot Studio; Microsoft 365 Copilot GA (Nov). 2024 AI Agents & Fabric Agents in Copilot Studio (autonomous), SharePoint agents; Microsoft Fabric integrations (Fabric link for Dataverse). 2025 AI Agent tooling matures Microsoft 365 Agents Toolkit/SDK; declarative agents; deeper Dataverse + Copilot integration. 2026 AI Agents as a mainstream workload Organizations govern fleets of agents; legacy SharePoint workflows/add-ins retired; Power Platform is the default M365 app platform. flowchart LR A[PowerApps<br/>2016] --> B[Power Apps<br/>canvas + model-driven] C[Microsoft Flow<br/>2016] --> D[Power Automate<br/>2019] E[CDS<br/>2016] --> F[Dataverse<br/>2020] G[Power Virtual Agents<br/>2019] --> H[Copilot Studio<br/>2023] B & D & F & H --> P[Power Platform + Copilot<br/>2026] # Interview questions Q1 What was Power Automate called before 2019? Microsoft Flow . It was renamed Power Automate at Ignite in November 2019, when RPA (UI flows) was added. Q2 What did Power Virtual Agents become? Copilot Studio (2023), which builds agents with generative orchestration, knowledge and actions."},{"t":"The Evolution of SharePoint Development – Farm Solutions → Sandbox → Add-ins → SPFx → Agents","c":"history","u":"articles/history/dev-models-evolution.html","s":"Why each SharePoint development model was introduced and later replaced, with a comparison of runtime, hosting, security and upgrade impact, and what a modern SharePoint developer's toolbox looks like in 2026.","k":"history, development models, farm solutions, add-ins, spfx, agents","h":"The 2026 toolbox · Interview questions · Why did Microsoft move customization out of the SharePoint process? · What replaced provider-hosted add-ins?","l":"Beginner","x":"Timelines","b":"Five generations of SharePoint customization 2003–2010 · FULL-TRUST FARM SOLUTIONS C# in GAC SSOM Event receivers timer jobs Master pages & web parts risk: code inside SharePoint process 2010 · SANDBOXED SOLUTIONS Restricted SSOM Resource points Site collection scope too limited; deprecated in SPO 2013 · ADD-IN MODEL SharePoint-hosted (JS) Provider-hosted (any server) OAuth (ACS / S2S) App parts (iframes) heavy, iframes, ACS dependency → retired 2026 2017 · SHAREPOINT FRAMEWORK Client-side TypeScript/React In-page CDN hosted Teams/Viva/Outlook hosts combined with 2020S · CLOUD & AI Power Platform Graph & Azure Functions Copilot agents (declarative, Copilot Studio) Five generations of SharePoint customization Farm Sandbox Add-ins SPFx + Azure + Power Platform Runs Server (w3wp) Server (isolated) Remote web / app web Browser + cloud APIs SPO support ❌ ❌ Retired 2026 ✅ Upgrade impact High Medium Medium Low UX integration Full Limited iframes Native Security Full trust Restricted OAuth app principal User context + Entra apps # The 2026 toolbox Cheat sheet Modern SharePoint developer toolbox 01 UI SPFx (web parts, extensions, ACEs, form customizers), formatting JSON, Power Apps. 02 Logic Power Automate, Azure Functions, Dataverse plug-ins & custom APIs. 03 Data & APIs Microsoft Graph, SharePoint REST via PnPjs/PnP Core, Dataverse Web API. 04 Identity Entra ID apps, Sites.Selected, managed identities. 05 Ops Azure DevOps / GitHub Actions, CLI for M365, PnP PowerShell, Application Insights. 06 AI SharePoint agents, Copilot Studio, declarative agents, Azure OpenAI. # Interview questions Q1 Why did Microsoft move customization out of the SharePoint process? To make SharePoint stable, secure and continuously upgradable, especially in multi-tenant SharePoint Online. Code inside the server process could crash farms and block upgrades. Q2 What replaced provider-hosted add-ins? SPFx for UI, Azure Functions or App Service APIs for server logic, and Entra ID app registrations (certificates, Sites.Selected) instead of ACS."},{"t":"The Evolution of Workflows & Forms – From SPD and InfoPath to Power Platform, Nintex NAC and Agents","c":"history","u":"articles/history/workflow-forms-evolution.html","s":"Twenty years of business process automation on SharePoint, covering 2007/2010 workflows, SharePoint Designer, InfoPath, Nintex, 2013 Workflow Manager, Power Automate and Power Apps, NAC, and today's AI agents, with the reasons for each shift.","k":"history, workflows, forms, infopath, nintex, power automate, power apps","h":"Interview questions · Why did InfoPath decline? · What drove organizations from SPD workflows to Power Automate?","l":"Beginner","x":"Timelines","b":"2003 Forms InfoPath 2003 XML forms designer; desktop forms submitted to SharePoint libraries. 2006–07 Workflow MOSS 2007 workflows + InfoPath Forms Services Approval workflows on WF 3.0; browser forms; SharePoint Designer 2007 workflows. 2007–08 Ecosystem Nintex Workflow 2007 Browser drag-and-drop workflows become hugely popular. 2010 Workflow SPD 2010 reusable workflows, InfoPath 2010 list forms Task processes, impersonation step; customizing list forms with InfoPath. 2012 Ecosystem Nintex Forms Friendlier forms designer with mobile layouts. 2013 Workflow Workflow Manager (2013 platform) Stage-based workflows with loops and REST calls; InfoPath 2013 is the last version. 2016 Cloud Microsoft Flow & PowerApps Cloud automation and low-code apps; customized SharePoint list forms with PowerApps (2017–18). 2019 Cloud Power Automate Flow renamed; RPA added. 2020 Retirement 2010 workflows retired in SPO Push to Power Automate. 2022 Ecosystem Nintex Automation Cloud Nintex's cloud platform for forms, workflows and documents. 2026 Retirement 2013 workflows, add-ins & InfoPath retiring Power Platform, NAC and SPFx form customizers become the standard; AI agents automate triage and assistance. flowchart LR subgraph Then IP[InfoPath forms] --- SPD[SPD workflows] --- NW[Nintex on-prem] end subgraph Now PA[Power Apps / form formatting<br/>SPFx form customizer] --- PAU[Power Automate] --- NAC[Nintex Automation Cloud] AG[Agents: Copilot Studio,<br/>SharePoint agents] --- PAU end IP --> PA SPD --> PAU NW --> NAC # Interview questions Q1 Why did InfoPath decline? Microsoft stopped investing after InfoPath 2013 (announced 2014). Its desktop-centric design, XML storage and code-behind didn't fit cloud and mobile, and Power Apps, Nintex and SPFx offered modern alternatives. Q2 What drove organizations from SPD workflows to Power Automate? Retirement of 2010 and 2013 workflows in SharePoint Online, the connector ecosystem beyond SharePoint, modern approvals in Teams and Outlook, better monitoring and ALM, and Microsoft's investment focus."},{"t":"Retirement & End-of-Support Tracker (2020–2026) – What Ended, When, and What to Do","c":"history","u":"articles/history/retirement-tracker.html","s":"A consolidated tracker of SharePoint and Microsoft 365 retirements relevant to SharePoint professionals, covering 2010/2013 workflows, add-ins and ACS, InfoPath, SharePoint Designer, SharePoint Server 2016/2019, alerts and more, with recommended replacements and action plans.","k":"retirement, end of support, roadmap, migration, sharepoint, 2026","h":"Interview questions · Which SharePoint Online retirements happened on 2 April 2026? · What is the supported on-premises SharePoint version after July 2026?","l":"Beginner","x":"Retirements & Roadmap","b":"Always verify dates This tracker summarizes Microsoft announcements known as of 2026. Lifecycle dates can change, so confirm on Microsoft's Lifecycle site and your tenant's Message Center before committing plans. Date Item Where Replace with Guide Apr 2021 SharePoint 2010 end of support On-prem SE or SPO Upgrade paths Aug–Nov 2020 SharePoint 2010 workflows SPO Power Automate Legacy → Power Automate Apr 2023 SharePoint 2013 end of support On-prem SE or SPO On-prem to Online 2024 Stream (Classic) retired M365 Stream on SharePoint – Apr 2024 2013 workflows off for new tenants SPO Power Automate – Nov 2024 ACS/add-ins blocked for new tenants SPO SPFx + Entra apps – 2 Apr 2026 SharePoint Add-ins retired SPO SPFx, Azure, Entra apps PHA challenges & migration 2 Apr 2026 Azure ACS for SPO retired SPO Entra app registrations (certs, Sites.Selected) Sites.Selected 2 Apr 2026 SharePoint 2013 workflows retired SPO Power Automate / NAC Workflow migration 14 Jul 2026 SharePoint Server 2016 & 2019 end of support On-prem Subscription Edition or SPO Subscription Edition 14 Jul 2026 SharePoint Designer 2013 end of support Desktop Power Automate, Power Apps, formatting SharePoint Designer Jul 2026 InfoPath 2013 end of support; Forms Services retiring in SPO Desktop / SPO Power Apps, NAC, SPFx InfoPath retirement 2025–2026 SharePoint alerts retirement (announced) SPO List rules, Power Automate Microsoft Lists flowchart LR A[Inventory<br/>M365 Assessment tool<br/>SMAT / scripts] --> B[Prioritize by<br/>retirement date & criticality] B --> C[Replace<br/>SPFx · Power Platform · NAC · Azure] C --> D[Decommission &<br/>update governance] # Interview questions Q1 Which SharePoint Online retirements happened on 2 April 2026? SharePoint Add-ins (SharePoint-hosted and provider-hosted), Azure ACS app authentication for SPO, and SharePoint 2013 workflows. Q2 What is the supported on-premises SharePoint version after July 2026? SharePoint Server Subscription Edition . SharePoint 2016 and 2019 reached end of extended support on 14 July 2026."},{"t":"Choosing the Right Tool in 2026 – A Decision Guide Across SharePoint, Power Platform, SPFx, Nintex and Azure","c":"history","u":"articles/history/choosing-platform-2026.html","s":"One decision guide for the most common requirements (forms, workflows, apps, intranet UI, integrations, documents, AI), mapping each to the right Microsoft 365 or ecosystem tool with reasoning, licensing notes and anti-patterns.","k":"decision guide, architecture, power platform, spfx, nintex, azure, 2026","h":"Interview questions · How do you decide between Power Apps and SPFx for a requirement? · What is the modern replacement for remote event receivers in SharePoint Online?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Retirements & Roadmap","b":"flowchart TD R{Requirement} --> F[Form] R --> W[Workflow / approval] R --> A[Business app] R --> U[Intranet / page UI] R --> I[Integration / background job] R --> D[Document process] R --> AI[Q&A / assistant] F --> F1{Simple show/hide?} -->|yes| FF[List form formatting] F1 -->|no| F2[Power Apps form · NAC form · SPFx form customizer] W --> W1[\"Power Automate · NAC<br/>(Logic Apps for IT integration)\"] A --> A1{Relational, secure,<br/>many users?} -->|yes| MDA[Dataverse + model-driven/canvas] A1 -->|no| CA[Canvas app on SharePoint] U --> U1[OOTB web parts first → SPFx] I --> I1[Azure Functions + Entra apps<br/>Sites.Selected · webhooks] D --> D1[SharePoint libraries + Premium /<br/>Word templates · NAC DocGen] AI --> AI1[SharePoint agents → Copilot Studio →<br/>declarative agents → Azure OpenAI RAG] Requirement First choice Alternatives Avoid Simple list form tweaks Form formatting Power Apps list form Custom code Complex form with rules Power Apps NAC forms, SPFx form customizer InfoPath (retired) Approval workflow Power Automate NAC SPD workflows Data-heavy business app Dataverse + Power Apps Azure SQL + custom app Huge SharePoint lists Intranet UI OOTB web parts + formatting SPFx Script Editor Background job Azure Function (timer) Scheduled flow Personal-account flows React to list changes Webhooks + Functions / flows — Remote event receivers (SPO) App-only SharePoint access Entra app + cert + Sites.Selected Managed identity ACS (retired) Documents from data Word template + flow / Premium content assembly NAC DocGen Manual copy/paste AI assistant over docs SharePoint agent / Copilot Studio Custom RAG Unsecured indexes Rule of three Prefer configuration (OOTB, formatting, settings) → then low-code (Power Platform, NAC) → then pro-code (SPFx, Azure). Move up a level only when there is a concrete requirement the previous level can't meet, and write that reason down in the HLD. # Interview questions Q1 How do you decide between Power Apps and SPFx for a requirement? Consider the audience and licensing, where the UI lives (SharePoint page vs app), UX and performance needs, data sources and security, who will maintain it (makers vs developers), and ALM expectations. Prefer Power Apps for data-entry apps owned by makers, and SPFx for page-integrated, high-performance or broad-audience UI. Q2 What is the modern replacement for remote event receivers in SharePoint "},{"t":"What is SharePoint On-Premises? Overview & When to Use It","c":"sharepoint-onprem","u":"articles/sharepoint-onprem/overview.html","s":"SharePoint Server explained from the ground up, covering what it is, the building blocks, editions, how it differs from SharePoint Online, and when an on-premises farm still makes sense in 2026.","k":"sharepoint server, on-premises, overview, subscription edition, architecture","h":"The building blocks · Editions and versions · On-premises vs SharePoint Online · When on-premises still makes sense in 2026 · A typical on-premises request flow · What you will find in this tab · Interview questions · What is the difference between a site collection and a site? · Why would a company still choose SharePoint Server over SharePoint Online? · What is stored in the configuration database vs a content database?","l":"Beginner","x":"Fundamentals","b":"SharePoint On-Premises (officially SharePoint Server ) is Microsoft's collaboration, content-management and intranet platform installed on servers you own and operate, in your own datacenter or on IaaS virtual machines. You install the binaries, connect them to SQL Server, create a farm , and from then on you are responsible for patching, scaling, backups, security and upgrades. The same product family powers SharePoint Online in Microsoft 365. Microsoft runs SharePoint Online as a multi-tenant service, while SharePoint Server gives you full control in exchange for full responsibility. SE Subscription Edition is the only supported version after Jul 2026 3 tiers Web, application, SQL database 5,000 list view threshold (default) 25 years of backwards-compatible concepts # The building blocks Everything in SharePoint is a hierarchy. Once you understand it, the admin and developer tasks make sense. SharePoint Server – logical hierarchy FARM Config database Central Administration Servers (MinRole) Service applications hosts one or more WEB APPLICATION IIS website + app pool Zones & URLs (AAM) Authentication provider stores data in CONTENT DATABASE SQL database Holds many site collections Unit of backup/restore contains SITE COLLECTION Top-level site Site collection features Quota & owners contains SITES (WEBS) Subsites Navigation Permissions contain LISTS & LIBRARIES Items & documents Columns & content types Views & versions SharePoint Server – logical hierarchy Concept What it is Why it matters Farm A set of servers that share one configuration database Unit of installation, patching and topology Web application An IIS site with its own URL(s), app pool and authentication Security boundary for auth; separates intranet vs extranet Content database A SQL database that stores site collections Unit of backup, restore, upgrade and scale Site collection A top-level site plus all its subsites Unit of ownership, quota, features and (often) migration Site / web A container for lists, libraries and pages Unit of navigation and permissions List / library Structured data (items) or files (documents) Where business data lives Service application Shared services: Search, Managed Metadata, User Profiles… Shared across web applications via proxies # Editions and versions Version Editions Status in 2026 SharePoint 2013 Foundation (free), Standard, Enterprise Out of support since"},{"t":"Logical Architecture – Web Applications, Zones, AAM, Content Databases & Site Collections","c":"sharepoint-onprem","u":"articles/sharepoint-onprem/logical-architecture.html","s":"How URLs, authentication zones, alternate access mappings, host-named site collections and content databases fit together, and how to design them for intranets, extranets and upgrades.","k":"web application, aam, zones, host named site collections, content database, managed paths","h":"The pieces · Web applications · Zones and Alternate Access Mappings (AAM) · Path-based vs host-named site collections · Content databases · Designing for an intranet + extranet · Interview questions · What problem do Alternate Access Mappings solve? · Why does Microsoft recommend host-named site collections? · What is the unit of upgrade when moving from SharePoint 2016 to Subscription Edition?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Fundamentals","b":"The physical architecture (servers) is about capacity. The logical architecture, meaning web applications, zones, URLs and databases, is about how users reach content, how they authenticate, and how you back it up and upgrade it . Getting it right makes every later migration easier. # The pieces Logical architecture of one web application URLS & ZONES Default zone (intranet.contoso.co… Intranet zone Internet zone (extranet.contoso.co… Custom zone Alternate Access Mappings translate public URL ↔ internal URL WEB APPLICATION IIS site per zone Application pool Managed paths (/sites, /teams) Policies & blocked file types site collections are assigned to CONTENT DATABASES WSS_Content_HR (≤ 200 GB) WSS_Content_Projects WSS_Content_Archive each holds many SITE COLLECTIONS Path-based (/sites/hr) Host-named (hr.contoso.com) Root site collection (/) Logical architecture of one web application # Web applications A web application is an IIS website plus SharePoint configuration. Each one has: one or more URLs , one per zone, with an authentication provider per zone; an application pool (identity and process isolation); managed paths ( /sites/* wildcard, /search explicit); policies such as User Policy , Resource Throttling (list view threshold), blocked file types and the recycle bin. Most modern farms use one web application for all collaboration content with host-named site collections , plus separate web applications only when authentication or isolation demands it (for example My Sites or an extranet). # Zones and Alternate Access Mappings (AAM) Each web application can be extended to up to five zones : Default, Intranet, Internet, Custom and Extranet. Each zone is a separate IIS site with its own URL and authentication. Alternate Access Mappings tell SharePoint which public URL to write into links when a request arrives on an internal URL. This matters behind reverse proxies and SSL offloading. Term Example Meaning Public URL https://extranet.contoso.com What users type; SharePoint renders links with this Internal URL http://spwfe01:8080 What actually hits IIS (e.g. from a proxy) Zone Internet Ties a public URL to an auth provider The crawl account must use the Default zone Search must crawl the Default zone , which should use Windows authentication . If the Default zone uses only SAML or forms auth, crawls fail or links in search results point to the wrong URL. # P"},{"t":"SharePoint Farm Architecture, Tiers, MinRole & High Availability","c":"sharepoint-onprem","u":"articles/sharepoint-onprem/farm-architecture.html","s":"How SharePoint Server farms are built, covering the three tiers, MinRole server roles, small to large topologies, high availability with SQL AlwaysOn, and sizing rules of thumb.","k":"farm, minrole, topology, high availability, sql server, distributed cache, architecture","h":"The three tiers · MinRole (SharePoint 2016, 2019 and SE) · Reference topologies · High availability and disaster recovery · Network ports you will need · Common design mistakes · Interview questions · What is MinRole and why was it introduced? · Why should a Distributed Cache cluster never have exactly two hosts? · How would you design a highly available SharePoint farm with the fewest servers?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Architecture & Administration","b":"A SharePoint farm is a group of servers that share one configuration database and act as a single system. The design of that farm, its topology , determines performance, availability and how painful your patch nights are. # The three tiers Classic three-tier SharePoint farm CLIENTS Browsers Office clients OneDrive sync Mobile apps HTTPS via load balancer (F5 / NetScaler / ARR) WEB TIER Front-end server 1 Front-end server 2 Distributed Cache WCF (service app endpoints, port 32843/32844) APPLICATION TIER Application server Search servers Office Online Server (separate farm) SQL (TDS 1433) via AG listener DATABASE TIER SQL Server AlwaysOn AG Config DB Content DBs Service app DBs Search DBs Classic three-tier SharePoint farm Web tier : handles user requests (IIS + SharePoint web applications). Servers are stateless and load-balanced. Application tier : runs back-end services such as search crawling and indexing, timer jobs, User Profile sync, Managed Metadata and workflow. Database tier : SQL Server holds everything , including configuration, content, service application data and the search index metadata. # MinRole (SharePoint 2016, 2019 and SE) Before 2016 admins decided manually which services ran where, and mistakes were common. MinRole assigns each server a role . SharePoint then starts exactly the services that role needs and continuously checks for compliance . Role Runs Typical count Front-end Services for user requests, optimized for low latency 2+ Application Back-end services (timer jobs, UPS, MMS, BCS) 2+ Distributed Cache AppFabric / Distributed Cache service 1 or 3 (never 2) Search Search components (crawl, index, query, analytics) 2+ Custom Anything you assign manually Optional Single-Server Farm Everything (dev/test only) 1 Front-end with Distributed Cache Shared role for small farms 2–3 Application with Search Shared role for small farms 2 Shared roles keep small farms affordable Shared roles were added in SharePoint 2016 Feature Pack 1. They let a 4-server highly available farm (2× Front-end with Distributed Cache, 2× Application with Search) still be MinRole-compliant. Check and change server roles PowerShell Copy # Which role does each server have, and is it compliant? Get-SPServer | Select-Object Name, Role, CompliantWithMinRole # Change the role of a server (runs the role conversion timer job) Set-SPServer -Identity \"SPAPP02\" -Role Applicat"},{"t":"Service Applications – Architecture, Proxies & the Full Catalog","c":"sharepoint-onprem","u":"articles/sharepoint-onprem/service-applications.html","s":"How SharePoint shares services such as Search, Managed Metadata and User Profiles across web applications, covering the service app, proxy and proxy group model, plus a catalog of every major service and how to provision it with PowerShell.","k":"service applications, proxy group, managed metadata, user profile, secure store, search, powershell","h":"How the pieces connect · The catalog · Provisioning a service application with PowerShell · Custom proxy groups (e.g. extranet without profiles) · Cross-farm service sharing · Troubleshooting checklist · Interview questions · What is the difference between a service application, a service instance and a proxy? · How can you prevent an extranet web application from using the User Profile service? · Why script service applications instead of using the Farm Configuration Wizard?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Architecture & Administration","b":"SharePoint 2010 replaced the old Shared Services Provider (SSP) of MOSS 2007 with service applications . These are independent, shareable services that web applications consume through proxies . The model is still the same in Subscription Edition. # How the pieces connect Service application model WEB APPLICATIONS Intranet web app My Sites web app Extranet web app associated with a PROXY GROUP default proxy group custom proxy group (e.g. extranet: no UPS) contains SERVICE APPLICATION PROXIES Search proxy MMS proxy UPS proxy Secure Store proxy WCF calls (32843/32844) to SERVICE APPLICATIONS Search Service App Managed Metadata User Profile Secure Store BCS App Management running on SERVICE INSTANCES (SERVERS) Application servers Search servers App pool identity per service storing data in DATABASES Search DBs (admin, crawl, links,… MMS DB Profile / Social / Sync DBs Secure Store DB Service application model A service application is the logical service plus its configuration, app pool and database(s). A service instance is the service actually running on a server . MinRole starts instances automatically. A proxy is how a web application talks to a service application. A proxy group is a named set of proxies that you associate with web applications. That is how you give an extranet a different set of services than the intranet. # The catalog Cheat sheet Service applications you will meet Standard (S) and Enterprise (E) availability varies by version, so check your edition 01 Search Service Application Crawling, indexing, query processing, result sources, query rules. The heaviest service with its own topology. 02 Managed Metadata (MMS) Term store (taxonomy), content type hub syndication, managed navigation. 03 User Profile Service (UPS) Profiles, My Sites, audiences, social data. AD import or Microsoft Identity Manager sync. 04 Secure Store Encrypted credential store used by BCS, Excel/Visio/Power BI connections. Needs a master key. 05 Business Data Connectivity (BCS) External content types and external lists over SQL, WCF or .NET connectors. 06 App Management + Subscription Settings Required for SharePoint add-ins (app web URLs, licensing, tenancy). 07 Machine Translation Variations and document translation (deprecated in later versions). 08 State Service / Session State Used by InfoPath Forms Services, charts and some workflow features. 09 Word Automation / Po"},{"t":"Central Administration & SharePoint Management Shell – Admin Essentials","c":"sharepoint-onprem","u":"articles/sharepoint-onprem/central-admin-powershell.html","s":"The day-to-day admin toolkit, covering what lives in Central Administration, the 40 PowerShell cmdlets you will actually use, ULS log hunting with correlation IDs, and safe scripting habits.","k":"central administration, powershell, uls, correlation id, administration, farm","h":"Central Administration map · The Management Shell · The cmdlets you actually use · Five scripts every admin should keep · How to troubleshoot an error with a correlation ID · Safe scripting habits · Interview questions · How do you find the root cause of a \"Sorry, something went wrong\" error? · Why must you call Dispose on SPSite and SPWeb objects? · What permissions does an admin need to run SharePoint PowerShell?","l":"Beginner","x":"Architecture & Administration","b":"Every SharePoint Server admin lives in two places: Central Administration , the browser-based admin site, and the SharePoint Management Shell , PowerShell with the SharePoint snap-in or module. Central Admin is good for discovering settings. PowerShell is how you make changes repeatably. # Central Administration map Cheat sheet Central Administration at a glance 01 Application Management Web applications, site collections, content databases, service applications, quotas. 02 System Settings Servers in farm, MinRole compliance, outgoing email, farm features, alternate access mappings. 03 Monitoring Health Analyzer, timer job status and history, reporting, diagnostic logging (ULS) levels. 04 Backup and Restore Farm and granular backups, site collection backup, export of sites/lists, unattached content DB recovery. 05 Security Farm administrators, managed accounts, service accounts, blocked file types, web part security. 06 Upgrade and Migration Patch status, database status, product versions, upgrade status. 07 General Application Settings Search, InfoPath Forms Services, SharePoint Designer settings, content deployment. 08 Apps App catalog, app URLs and domains, store settings (for the add-in model). # The Management Shell Starting a session PowerShell Copy # SharePoint 2013–2019: snap-in Add-PSSnapin Microsoft.SharePoint.PowerShell -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue # Subscription Edition: the module also works Import-Module SharePointServer -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue # Who can run scripts? You need: local admin on the server + SharePoint_Shell_Access on the DBs Add-SPShellAdmin -UserName \"CONTOSO\\sp_admin_jane\" -Database ( Get-SPContentDatabase \"WSS_Content_HR\" ) # The cmdlets you actually use Task Cmdlet(s) Farm & servers Get-SPFarm , Get-SPServer , Get-SPProduct -Local , Get-SPServiceInstance Web apps Get-SPWebApplication , New-SPWebApplication , Set-SPWebApplication Site collections Get-SPSite -Limit All , New-SPSite , Remove-SPSite , Move-SPSite , Set-SPSite -LockState Sites Get-SPWeb , New-SPWeb , Export-SPWeb , Import-SPWeb Content DBs Get-SPContentDatabase , Mount-SPContentDatabase , Dismount-SPContentDatabase , Test-SPContentDatabase Solutions Add-SPSolution , Install-SPSolution , Update-SPSolution , Uninstall-SPSolution Features Get-SPFeature , Enable-SPFeature , Disable-SPFeature Users Get-SPUser , Set-SPUser , Move-SPUser (account migration) Timer j"},{"t":"Authentication in SharePoint Server – Claims, Kerberos, SAML & OpenID Connect","c":"sharepoint-onprem","u":"articles/sharepoint-onprem/authentication-claims.html","s":"How claims-based authentication works in SharePoint, with Windows (NTLM/Kerberos), SAML through ADFS, forms-based auth and the OpenID Connect support added in Subscription Edition, plus the people picker, account migration and common pitfalls.","k":"authentication, claims, kerberos, saml, adfs, oidc, entra id, security","h":"The authentication pipeline · Windows authentication: NTLM vs Kerberos · SAML with ADFS (or any SAML 2.0 IdP) · OpenID Connect in Subscription Edition · Migrating users between providers · Common pitfalls · Interview questions · What does i:0#.w|contoso\\jane mean? · Why is Kerberos preferred over NTLM for SharePoint? · What changes when you enable OIDC with Entra ID on Subscription Edition?","l":"Advanced","x":"Architecture & Administration","b":"Since SharePoint 2010, every web application uses claims-based authentication . Whatever the identity source, whether Windows, SAML or forms, the user is converted into a SharePoint security token containing claims (who you are, which groups you are in). Permissions are granted to those claims. # The authentication pipeline Claims authentication in SharePoint Server IDENTITY PROVIDERS Active Directory (Windows) ADFS / SAML 2.0 IdP Microsoft Entra ID (OIDC, SE) ASP.NET membership (FBA) user signs in; IdP issues a token or Windows identity WEB APPLICATION ZONE Authentication provider per zone Trusted identity token issuer Claims provider (people picker) claims augmentation SHAREPOINT STS Security Token Service app Issues SP security token (FedAuth cookie) Token cache (Distributed Cache) authorize using claims AUTHORIZATION SharePoint groups Permission levels Audiences / UPS claims Claims authentication in SharePoint Server The encoded claim identity tells you which provider the user came from: Login name example Meaning `i:0#.w contoso\\jane` `i:05.t adfs `i:0#.f membership `c:0+.w s-1-5-21-…` `c:0(.s true` # Windows authentication: NTLM vs Kerberos NTLM Kerberos Setup Works out of the box Requires SPNs + delegation config Performance Extra round trips on each connection Ticket-based, fewer round trips Double hop (delegate to SQL, SSRS…) ❌ Not possible ✅ With constrained delegation Recommendation Dev / small farms Production intranets Register SPNs for a web application (run as domain admin) PowerShell Copy # App pool account for the web application setspn -S HTTP/sp.contoso.com CONTOSO\\sp_webapp_pool setspn -S HTTP/ sp CONTOSO\\sp_webapp_pool # Check for duplicates (duplicate SPNs break Kerberos!) setspn -X # SAML with ADFS (or any SAML 2.0 IdP) Register ADFS as a trusted identity token issuer PowerShell Copy $cert = New-Object System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates.X509Certificate2( \"C:\\certs\\adfs-signing.cer\" ) New-SPTrustedRootAuthority -Name \"ADFS Signing\" -Certificate $cert $email = New-SPClaimTypeMapping -IncomingClaimType \"http://schemas.xmlsoap.org/ws/2005/05/identity/claims/emailaddress\" -IncomingClaimTypeDisplayName \"Email\" -SameAsIncoming $role = New-SPClaimTypeMapping -IncomingClaimType \"http://schemas.microsoft.com/ws/2008/06/identity/claims/role\" -IncomingClaimTypeDisplayName \"Role\" -SameAsIncoming New-SPTrustedIdentityTokenIssuer -Name \"AD"},{"t":"Search Architecture – Crawl, Content Processing, Index & Query Components","c":"sharepoint-onprem","u":"articles/sharepoint-onprem/search-architecture.html","s":"The six search components and four search databases explained, with how a document travels from crawl to result, how to build a scalable topology in PowerShell, and how managed properties, result sources and query rules work.","k":"search, crawl, index, managed properties, kql, topology, query rules","h":"The search pipeline · Building a highly available topology · Crawled properties, managed properties and the schema · Result sources and query rules · Querying search from code · Troubleshooting · Interview questions · Name the six search components and what each does. · What is the difference between a crawled property and a managed property? · How do you make search highly available?","l":"Advanced","x":"Architecture & Administration","b":"SharePoint 2013 merged FAST Search into the product. The resulting architecture, which is still used in Subscription Edition, splits search into six components that you can scale independently, backed by four databases . # The search pipeline How content becomes a search result CONTENT SOURCES SharePoint sites (sps3://, https://) File shares BCS / external content Websites crawl (full / incremental / continuous) CRAWL COMPONENT Crawler (gatherer) Crawl DB (history, links) Default content access account crawled properties CONTENT PROCESSING COMPONENT Parsing & IFilters Schema mapping (crawled → managed… Entity extraction Link DB index items INDEX COMPONENT Index partitions Replicas (for HA) Index files on disk query QUERY PROCESSING COMPONENT Query rules Result sources Linguistics Security trimming results to SEARCH CENTER / WEB PARTS Search results web part Content Search web part REST /_api/search How content becomes a search result Analytics processing runs alongside. It analyzes usage and links to calculate relevance (click distance, popularity) and powers \"Popular items\" and recommendations. The search administration component orchestrates everything. Component Job Scale out when Crawl Downloads content and metadata from content sources Crawl windows run too long Content processing Parses documents, maps properties, extracts entities CPU is saturated during crawls Index Stores the inverted index in partitions with replicas Item count grows (~20M items per partition guidance) or query load increases Query processing Parses queries, applies rules, trims by security High QPS Analytics processing Usage and link analysis Many items or heavy usage reporting Admin Manages the search topology Always 2 for HA Database Stores Search admin DB Configuration: content sources, schema, rules Crawl DB Crawl history and state Link DB Links and anchor text, click data Analytics reporting DB Usage analytics results # Building a highly available topology Two-server HA search topology (clone, edit, activate) PowerShell Copy $ssa = Get-SPEnterpriseSearchServiceApplication $si1 = Get-SPEnterpriseSearchServiceInstance -Identity \"SPSRCH01\" $si2 = Get-SPEnterpriseSearchServiceInstance -Identity \"SPSRCH02\" Start-SPEnterpriseSearchServiceInstance -Identity $si1 Start-SPEnterpriseSearchServiceInstance -Identity $si2 $active = Get-SPEnterpriseSearchTopology -SearchApplication $ssa -A"},{"t":"User Profile Service & Managed Metadata – Profiles, Sync, Term Store & Taxonomy","c":"sharepoint-onprem","u":"articles/sharepoint-onprem/user-profiles-managed-metadata.html","s":"How profile data flows from Active Directory into SharePoint, how My Sites and audiences use it, and how the Managed Metadata Service term store, content type hub and managed navigation work, with PowerShell and CSOM examples.","k":"user profile service, managed metadata, term store, taxonomy, my sites, content type hub","h":"User Profile Service · Managed Metadata Service (MMS) · What MMS gives you · How UPS and MMS work together · Interview questions · What is the difference between AD Import and MIM synchronization? · What is a content type hub? · Why do managed metadata values break after a migration?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Architecture & Administration","b":"Two service applications quietly power much of SharePoint's \"smart\" behaviour: the User Profile Service (UPS) knows who people are , and the Managed Metadata Service (MMS) knows what content is about . # User Profile Service flowchart LR AD[(Active Directory)] -->|AD Import<br/>or MIM sync| UPS[User Profile<br/>Service App] HR[(HR system)] -->|BCS / MIM| UPS UPS --> PDB[(Profile DB)] UPS --> SDB[(Social DB)] UPS --> SYNC[(Sync DB)] UPS --> MS[My Sites host<br/>OneDrive-like personal sites] UPS --> AUD[Audiences<br/>target web parts & nav] UPS --> PP[People search<br/>& people picker data] UPS -->|claims augmentation| SEC[Security trimming<br/>for audiences] Option Available in Notes Active Directory Import 2013 → SE Fast, one-way, AD only. Good default. Microsoft Identity Manager (MIM) 2016 2016 → SE External sync engine for complex mappings, multiple sources and write-back. Replaced the built-in FIM sync from 2010/2013. Custom import (CSOM/PowerShell) All Push extra attributes (e.g. cost centre from HR) into profile properties. Set a custom profile property for many users from CSV PowerShell Copy $site = Get-SPSite \"https://mysites.contoso.com\" $ctx = Get-SPServiceContext $site $upm = New-Object Microsoft.Office.Server.UserProfiles.UserProfileManager( $ctx ) Import-Csv \"C:\\HR\\costcentres.csv\" | ForEach-Object { $account = \"i:0#.w|contoso\\ $ ( $_ .SamAccountName)\" if ( $upm .UserExists( $account )) { $p = $upm .GetUserProfile( $account ) $p [ \"CostCentre\" ].Value = $_ .CostCentre $p .Commit() } } $site .Dispose() Profile property privacy Each profile property has a privacy level (Everyone → Only Me) and a replicable flag. Sensitive HR attributes should not default to Everyone , because they will appear in people search. # Managed Metadata Service (MMS) The MMS hosts the term store , a central, hierarchical taxonomy shared by every site that uses the MMS proxy. Term store hierarchy TERM STORE One per MMS app Term store admins Languages contains GROUPS Global \"Enterprise\" group Site collection groups (local) Group managers & contributors contain TERM SETS Departments Locations Document Types Navigation term set contain TERMS Finance › Accounts Payable India › Bangalore Synonyms & translations Custom properties Term store hierarchy # What MMS gives you Managed metadata columns where users pick from a controlled vocabulary with type-ahead, synonyms and translati"},{"t":"Business Connectivity Services (BCS) – External Content Types & External Lists","c":"sharepoint-onprem","u":"articles/sharepoint-onprem/bcs-external-content.html","s":"Surface SQL Server, WCF and custom .NET data inside SharePoint as external lists, with the BCS architecture, Secure Store authentication modes, building an external content type in SharePoint Designer, CRUD code and its modern replacements.","k":"bcs, external content type, external list, secure store, sql server, sharepoint designer","h":"Architecture · Authentication options · Building an ECT in SharePoint Designer (no code) · Reading an external list with CSOM · Modern alternatives (SharePoint Online and new builds) · Interview questions · What are the minimum operations an external content type needs for an external list? · How do you avoid the double-hop problem with BCS and SQL Server? · What would you use instead of BCS in SharePoint Online?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Architecture & Administration","b":"Business Connectivity Services (BCS) (called Business Data Catalog in MOSS 2007) lets SharePoint read and write data that lives outside SharePoint, such as a SQL table, a WCF service or a custom .NET API, and show it as an external list that looks almost like a normal list. # Architecture BCS runtime architecture SHAREPOINT UI External list External data column Business Data web parts Search (crawl external data) uses BDC METADATA STORE External content type (ECT) model XML Operations (Read List, Read Item, Create, Update,… Associations calls through a connector CONNECTORS SQL Server WCF / web service .NET assembly (custom) OData (2013+) authenticates via IDENTITY User's identity (Kerberos double hop) RevertToSelf (app pool) Secure Store target application reads / writes EXTERNAL SYSTEM SQL database ERP / CRM API Legacy system BCS runtime architecture An External Content Type (ECT) describes an entity, for example Customer , with fields, an identifier and operations : Operation (stereotype) Required for Read List (Finder) Showing the external list Read Item (SpecificFinder) Opening an item, external data columns Create / Update / Delete Editing from SharePoint Association Related entities (Customer → Orders) # Authentication options Mode How it works When to use User's identity (PassThrough) The user's Windows identity flows to SQL Requires Kerberos constrained delegation (double hop) BDC identity / RevertToSelf The app pool account connects Simple but risky (all users get the app pool's rights); disabled by default Secure Store (impersonated) SharePoint maps the user or group to stored credentials Most common : a group of users maps to one SQL login Create a Secure Store target application for BCS PowerShell Copy $ctx = Get-SPServiceContext \"https://sp.contoso.com\" $f1 = New-SPSecureStoreApplicationField -Name \"SQL User\" -Type UserName -Masked : $false $f2 = New-SPSecureStoreApplicationField -Name \"SQL Password\" -Type Password -Masked : $true $app = New-SPSecureStoreTargetApplication -Name \"CRMSql\" -FriendlyName \"CRM SQL\" ` -ContactEmail \"spadmin@contoso.com\" -ApplicationType Group $admins = New-SPClaimsPrincipal -Identity \"CONTOSO\\sp_admins\" -IdentityType WindowsSamAccountName $users = New-SPClaimsPrincipal -Identity \"CONTOSO\\Sales\" -IdentityType WindowsSamAccountName New-SPSecureStoreApplication -ServiceContext $ctx -TargetApplication $app ` -Fields $f1 ,"},{"t":"Boundaries, Limits & the 5,000-Item List View Threshold","c":"sharepoint-onprem","u":"articles/sharepoint-onprem/boundaries-limits.html","s":"The SharePoint Server limits that bite in real projects, covering the list view threshold, unique permissions, content database size, file size and lookup columns, plus why they exist and how to design around them.","k":"limits, list view threshold, performance, large lists, indexing, capacity planning","h":"Why the 5,000-item threshold exists · Designing around the threshold · Other limits that matter · Interview questions · Why does SharePoint enforce a 5,000-item list view threshold? · A view on a 60,000-item list fails. How do you fix it? · What is the recommended limit for unique permissions in a list, and why?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Architecture & Administration","b":"SharePoint limits come in three flavours: boundaries (hard, cannot be exceeded), thresholds (configurable defaults), and supported limits (tested guidance). Most production incidents trace back to a handful of them. 5,000 list view threshold (items scanned per query, users) 20,000 threshold for admins / auditors (default) 50,000 unique permission scopes per list (recommended ≤ 5,000) 12 lookup, people & managed metadata columns per view (threshold) 200 GB content DB for general collaboration (guidance) 15 GB max file size (SharePoint 2019 / SE) # Why the 5,000-item threshold exists SQL Server escalates row locks to a table lock when a single query touches roughly 5,000 rows. All list items for a content database live in the same AllUserData table , so one unindexed query on a big list could lock the table for every site in that database. The list view threshold (LVT) stops queries that would scan more than 5,000 rows. flowchart TD Q[View or query on a list] --> I{Filter on an<br/>indexed column?} I -- yes --> S{Filtered result<br/>≤ 5,000 rows?} I -- no --> T{List has<br/>> 5,000 items?} T -- no --> OK[✅ Query runs] T -- yes --> BLOCK[⛔ 'The attempted operation is prohibited<br/>because it exceeds the list view threshold'] S -- yes --> OK S -- no --> BLOCK The threshold is about rows scanned, not rows stored A list can hold millions of items, up to 30 million per list. You just cannot run a query that has to scan more than 5,000 of them without an index. # Designing around the threshold Cheat sheet Large-list survival kit 01 Index early Create column indexes on the columns you filter and sort by before the list passes 5,000 items. Adding indexes to huge lists may itself be blocked. 02 Filter first The first filter in a view must use an indexed column and reduce the result below 5,000. 03 Folders & metadata Folders limit items per container. Metadata navigation with key filters uses indexes automatically. 04 Daily time window Admins can set a happy hour ( Daily time window for large queries ) for maintenance jobs. 05 Code: paginate CSOM/REST: use RowLimit + ListItemCollectionPosition / $skiptoken . SSOM: SPQuery.RowLimit + ListItemCollectionPosition . 06 Archive Move closed or old items to an archive list or library on a schedule. CSOM paging through a large list safely C# Copy var list = ctx.Web.Lists.GetByTitle( \"Deviations\" ); var query = new CamlQuery { /"},{"t":"Server Object Model (SSOM) – C# CRUD with SPSite, SPWeb & SPList","c":"sharepoint-onprem","u":"articles/sharepoint-onprem/server-object-model.html","s":"The full-trust C# API that runs inside SharePoint, covering the object hierarchy, disposal rules, create/read/update/delete code, CAML queries, elevated privileges, batch updates and when (not) to use SSOM.","k":"ssom, csharp, server object model, caml, crud, spsite, spweb, full trust","h":"The golden rule: dispose what you create · CRUD template · Uploading a document with metadata · Running with elevated privileges · Batch operations for speed · SSOM vs client APIs · Interview questions · What is the difference between Update() and SystemUpdate() ? · Why must you create a new SPSite inside RunWithElevatedPrivileges? · When should you dispose SPWeb objects?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Server-Side Development","b":"The Server Object Model (SSOM) , the Microsoft.SharePoint.dll namespace, is the original, most powerful SharePoint API. Code runs on a SharePoint server , in the same process as SharePoint (web parts, event receivers, timer jobs) or in a console app or PowerShell on a farm server. It can do anything SharePoint can do, which is exactly why SharePoint Online does not allow it. SSOM object hierarchy SPFARM Servers Services Solutions SPWebApplication SPWEBAPPLICATION Zones & policies Content databases SPSite (site collection) SPSITE RootWeb AllWebs Features RecycleBin SPWeb (site) SPWEB Lists Fields ContentTypes Groups Users SPList SPLIST / SPDOCUMENTLIBRARY Items (SPListItem) Fields Views EventReceivers SSOM object hierarchy # The golden rule: dispose what you create Correct disposal patterns C# Copy // ✅ You created them, so you dispose them using (SPSite site = new SPSite( \"https://sp.contoso.com/sites/quality\" )) using (SPWeb web = site.OpenWeb()) { // work with web } // ✅ From context (web part / page / event receiver): DO NOT dispose SPWeb current = SPContext.Current.Web; // ❌ Leak: OpenWeb() inside a loop without disposing foreach (SPWeb w in site.AllWebs) // each item must be disposed! { try { Console.WriteLine(w.Title); } finally { w.Dispose(); } } SPDisposeCheck Leaked SPSite / SPWeb objects are the #1 cause of memory pressure in custom farm code. Microsoft's old SPDisposeCheck tool and code reviews catch most of them. In event receivers, prefer properties.Web / properties.OpenWeb() per the documented ownership rules. # CRUD template CRUD on a custom list with SSOM C# Copy public static int CreateDeviation ( string siteUrl, string title, string area ) { using ( var site = new SPSite(siteUrl)) using ( var web = site.OpenWeb()) { SPList list = web.Lists.TryGetList( \"Deviations\" ) ?? throw new InvalidOperationException( \"List not found\" ); SPListItem item = list.AddItem(); item[ \"Title\" ] = title; item[ \"Area\" ] = area; // Choice column item[ \"Owner\" ] = web.EnsureUser( \"contoso\\\\jane\" ); // Person column item[ \"DueDate\" ] = DateTime.Today.AddDays( 14 ); item.Update(); // creates a version, fires events return item.ID; } } CRUD on a custom list with SSOM C# Copy public static IEnumerable<( int Id, string Title, string Status)> GetOpen( string siteUrl) { using ( var site = new SPSite(siteUrl)) using ( var web = site.OpenWeb()) { var list = web.Lists[ \"Devi"},{"t":"Farm Solutions (WSP) & Features – Packaging, Scopes, Receivers & Upgrades","c":"sharepoint-onprem","u":"articles/sharepoint-onprem/farm-solutions-features.html","s":"How full-trust customizations are packaged and deployed, covering WSP anatomy, feature scopes, element manifests, feature receivers, upgrade actions and a deployment pipeline, plus how to retire them before moving to the cloud.","k":"wsp, farm solution, features, feature receiver, deployment, visual studio","h":"Anatomy of a WSP · Elements: declarative provisioning · Feature receivers: imperative logic · Deployment lifecycle · Retiring farm solutions (cloud readiness) · Interview questions · What are the four feature scopes? · What is the difference between Add-SPSolution and Install-SPSolution? · How do you upgrade an already-activated feature?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Server-Side Development","b":"From MOSS 2007 onwards, customizations were shipped as solution packages ( .wsp ) containing one or more Features . A WSP is just a CAB file with a manifest.xml . A Feature is a unit of functionality that you activate at a specific scope . # Anatomy of a WSP Inside a .wsp solution package MANIFEST.XML Assemblies (GAC / bin) SafeControls entries Features to install Root files (TEMPLATE\\LAYOUTS,… deploys FEATURES (FEATURE.XML) Scope: Farm / WebApplication / Sit… Element manifests Feature receiver class Upgrade actions provisions ELEMENTS Fields & content types List instances & list templates Modules (files, master pages) Custom actions & ribbon Event receivers Web parts (.webpart) Inside a .wsp solution package Feature scope Activated on Typical contents Farm The whole farm Delegate controls, farm-wide timer jobs WebApplication One web application web.config mods, web-app timer jobs Site A site collection Site columns, content types, web parts gallery, master pages Web One site Lists, pages, custom actions # Elements: declarative provisioning Elements.xml – site column, content type and list XML Copy <?xml version= \"1.0\" encoding= \"utf-8\" ?> < Elements xmlns = \"http://schemas.microsoft.com/sharepoint/\" > < Field ID = \"{8A1C3D2E-5B4F-4E6A-9C7D-1E2F3A4B5C6D}\" Name = \"DeviationArea\" StaticName = \"DeviationArea\" DisplayName = \"Area\" Type = \"Choice\" Group = \"Contoso Quality\" Required = \"TRUE\" > < CHOICES > < CHOICE > Production </ CHOICE > < CHOICE > Warehouse </ CHOICE > < CHOICE > Lab </ CHOICE > </ CHOICES > </ Field > <!-- Content type ID: 0x01 (Item) + 00 + new GUID without braces/dashes --> < ContentType ID = \"0x0100A7B8C9D0E1F2435465768798A9B0C1D2\" Name = \"Deviation\" Group = \"Contoso Quality\" Inherits = \"TRUE\" > < FieldRefs > < FieldRef ID = \"{8A1C3D2E-5B4F-4E6A-9C7D-1E2F3A4B5C6D}\" Name = \"DeviationArea\" /> </ FieldRefs > </ ContentType > < ListInstance Title = \"Deviations\" Url = \"Lists/Deviations\" TemplateType = \"100\" FeatureId = \"00bfea71-de22-43b2-a848-c05709900100\" OnQuickLaunch = \"TRUE\" /> </ Elements > # Feature receivers: imperative logic Feature receiver: bind content type, add indexes, clean up on deactivate C# Copy [ Guid( \"2f1d0c4e-7a6b-4f55-9a7e-3c2b1a0d9e8f\" ) ] public class QualityFeatureEventReceiver : SPFeatureReceiver { public override void FeatureActivated ( SPFeatureReceiverProperties properties ) { var web = (SPWeb)properties.Feature.Pare"},{"t":"Event Receivers (Classic) – Synchronous & Asynchronous Handlers in C#","c":"sharepoint-onprem","u":"articles/sharepoint-onprem/event-receivers.html","s":"Everything about full-trust event receivers, covering receiver base classes, -ing vs -ed events, before and after properties, cancelling with errors, preventing recursion, registration in XML and code, the JavaScript-side equivalents, and the modern replacements.","k":"event receivers, csharp, ssom, itemadding, itemupdated, jsom, validation","h":"What a receiver can listen to · Synchronous (-ing) vs asynchronous (-ed) · A complete receiver · Binding to one specific list in code · The JavaScript side · Pitfalls and best practices · Modern replacements · Interview questions · Why is properties.ListItem null in ItemAdding ? · How do you stop an ItemUpdated receiver from triggering itself? · How can you make an \"-ed\" event run synchronously, and why would you? · What is the difference between registering with ListTemplateId and binding to a list in code?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Server-Side Development","b":"An event receiver is a class whose methods SharePoint calls when something happens: an item is added, a file is checked in, a list is deleted, a site is created. Classic receivers are full-trust C# code deployed in a farm solution. They run inside SharePoint (the w3wp worker process for synchronous events, and usually the same process on a background thread for asynchronous ones). # What a receiver can listen to Cheat sheet Receiver base classes and their events 01 SPItemEventReceiver ItemAdding/Added , ItemUpdating/Updated , ItemDeleting/Deleted , ItemCheckingIn/CheckedIn , ItemCheckedOut , ItemAttachmentAdding/Added , ItemFileMoving/Moved , ItemVersionDeleting/Deleted 02 SPListEventReceiver FieldAdding/Added , FieldUpdating/Updated , FieldDeleting/Deleted , ListAdding/Added , ListDeleting/Deleted 03 SPWebEventReceiver WebAdding/Provisioned , WebDeleting/Deleted , WebMoving/Moved , SiteDeleting/Deleted 04 SPEmailEventReceiver EmailReceived for incoming-email-enabled lists 05 SPWorkflowEventReceiver WorkflowStarting/Started , WorkflowCompleted , WorkflowPostponed 06 SPSecurityEventReceiver GroupAdded , GroupUserAdded , RoleAssignmentAdded , InheritanceBroken … (2013+) # Synchronous (-ing) vs asynchronous (-ed) sequenceDiagram autonumber actor U as User participant SP as SharePoint participant R as Receiver (-ing, sync) participant DB as Content DB participant A as Receiver (-ed, async) U->>SP: Save item SP->>R: ItemAdding(properties) alt cancel R-->>SP: properties.Status = CancelWithError SP-->>U: Error message, nothing saved else continue R-->>SP: (may change AfterProperties) SP->>DB: INSERT item SP-->>U: Saved SP-)A: ItemAdded(properties) on a background thread A->>DB: Further updates, notifications… end -ing (synchronous) -ed (asynchronous) Timing Before commit After commit Can cancel ✅ properties.Status = SPEventReceiverStatus.CancelWithError ❌ ListItem available Adding: ❌ null; Updating: ✅ (old values) ✅ AfterProperties New values (internal names) Values as saved Runs on Request thread (user waits) Background thread (can be made sync with <Synchronization> ) # A complete receiver DeviationReceiver.cs C# Copy public class DeviationReceiver : SPItemEventReceiver { // 1) Validate before saving public override void ItemAdding ( SPItemEventProperties properties ) { string title = properties.AfterProperties[ \"Title\" ] as string ; if ( string .IsNullOrWhiteSpa"},{"t":"Custom Timer Jobs – Scheduling Background Work on the Farm","c":"sharepoint-onprem","u":"articles/sharepoint-onprem/timer-jobs.html","s":"Build, schedule, deploy and debug SPJobDefinition timer jobs in C#, with lock types, schedules, persisted properties, and the cloud-era replacements based on Azure Functions and scheduled flows.","k":"timer jobs, spjobdefinition, owstimer, scheduling, csharp, azure functions","h":"How timer jobs run · The job class · Installing the job with a feature receiver · Running, monitoring and debugging · Cloud replacements · Interview questions · What is the difference between SPJobLockType.Job, ContentDatabase and None? · Your updated timer job code is not running after deployment. Why? · How do you store configuration for a timer job?","l":"Advanced","x":"Server-Side Development","b":"SharePoint has its own scheduler: the SharePoint Timer Service ( OWSTIMER.EXE ) runs on every server and executes timer jobs . Out of the box it handles hundreds of jobs (search, workflow, usage, content type hub). You can add your own by inheriting from SPJobDefinition . Typical custom jobs include nightly reminders for overdue tasks, archiving closed items, syncing a list from an ERP database, and generating reports. # How timer jobs run flowchart LR FR[Feature receiver<br/>creates job definition] --> CFG[(Config DB<br/>job definition + schedule)] CFG --> T1[OWSTIMER on SPAPP01] CFG --> T2[OWSTIMER on SPAPP02] T1 -->|lock acquired| RUN[\"Execute(targetInstanceId)\"] T2 -.->|lock busy, skip| RUN RUN --> CDB[(Content DBs)] RUN --> LOG[Job history + ULS] Lock type Behaviour Use for SPJobLockType.Job Runs on one server per schedule Most business jobs SPJobLockType.ContentDatabase Runs once per content DB (in parallel) Jobs that scan sites in each DB SPJobLockType.None Runs on every server where provisioned Server-local work (cleaning a local cache) # The job class OverdueReminderJob.cs C# Copy public class OverdueReminderJob : SPJobDefinition { public const string JobName = \"Contoso - Overdue Deviation Reminders\" ; // Required parameterless constructor (for serialization) public OverdueReminderJob () : base () { } public OverdueReminderJob ( SPWebApplication webApp ) : base ( JobName, webApp, null , SPJobLockType.Job ) { Title = JobName; } // Persisted settings are stored with the job definition in the config DB [ Persisted ] public string SiteUrl; public override void Execute ( Guid targetInstanceId ) { using ( var site = new SPSite(SiteUrl)) using ( var web = site.OpenWeb()) { var list = web.Lists[ \"Deviations\" ]; var q = new SPQuery { Query = \"<Where><And>\" + \"<Lt><FieldRef Name=&#x27;DueDate&#x27;/><Value Type=&#x27;DateTime&#x27;><Today/></Value></Lt>\" + \"<Neq><FieldRef Name=&#x27;Status&#x27;/><Value Type=&#x27;Choice&#x27;>Closed</Value></Neq>\" + \"</And></Where>\" , RowLimit = 2000 }; foreach (SPListItem item in list.GetItems(q)) { var owner = new SPFieldUserValue(web, item[ \"Owner\" ] as string ); if (owner.User == null || string .IsNullOrEmpty(owner.User.Email)) continue ; SPUtility.SendEmail(web, false , false , owner.User.Email, $\"Overdue deviation: {item.Title} \" , $\"Deviation <b> {item.Title} </b> was due on {item[ \"DueDate\" ]:d} . <a href=&#x27; {web"},{"t":"Sandboxed Solutions – What They Were, Why They Died, and No-Code Sandbox Today","c":"sharepoint-onprem","u":"articles/sharepoint-onprem/sandboxed-solutions.html","s":"The 2010 attempt at safe server-side code, covering the User Code Service architecture, resource points, what was allowed, the deprecation of code-based sandboxing, and the declarative sandbox solutions that still exist.","k":"sandboxed solutions, user code service, resource points, deprecation, history","h":"Architecture · Resource points: the throttle · Why it was deprecated · Migration mapping · Interview questions · What happened when a site collection exceeded its resource points? · Can you still use sandboxed solutions today?","l":"Beginner","x":"Server-Side Development","b":"SharePoint 2010 introduced sandboxed solutions to let site collection administrators upload customizations without farm admin involvement and without risking the farm . A sandboxed WSP was uploaded to the Solution Gallery of a site collection and activated there. # Architecture SharePoint 2010 sandbox execution REQUEST Web part render Event receiver Feature receiver proxied by SPUCWORKERPROCESSPROXY Runs in w3wp Marshals calls to USER CODE SERVICE (SPUCHOSTSERVICE) Load-balanced across servers Tier-based process pools executes in SPUCWORKERPROCESS.EXE Restricted CAS policy Subset of SSOM (site collection only) Resource point monitoring SharePoint 2010 sandbox execution Code ran in a separate, low-privilege worker process ( SPUCWorkerProcess.exe ) with a restricted subset of the server object model: Allowed Not allowed Lists, items, fields, content types within the site collection Anything above the site collection (farm, web app) Web parts (not visual web parts until VS add-ons), event receivers, feature receivers RunWithElevatedPrivileges Declarative workflows (SPD/VS), custom actions File system, registry, external network calls (without a full-trust proxy) Timer jobs, web.config , GAC deployment # Resource points: the throttle Each site collection had a daily quota of resource points (default 300). Metrics such as CPU time, SQL queries, exceptions and memory consumed points. When a site collection hit its quota, all sandboxed code in that site collection was disabled for the rest of the day , which meant an unpredictable user experience. # Why it was deprecated Too restrictive for real applications, while still running code on shared servers. Resource-point throttling made solutions randomly stop working. Did not fit the multi-tenant cloud (SharePoint Online) model. Microsoft deprecated code-based sandboxed solutions in 2014 and later blocked them in SharePoint Online. The replacement path was add-ins (2013) , then SPFx (2017) . Declarative (no-code) sandbox solutions still work A WSP that contains only declarative elements , such as fields, content types, list instances, modules and custom actions without an assembly, can still be uploaded to the solution gallery in SharePoint Server. SharePoint Online no longer supports adding them (Microsoft disabled sandbox solution activation in SPO). Use PnP provisioning or site scripts instead. # Migration mapping "},{"t":"Classic Branding – Master Pages, Page Layouts, Design Manager & JSLink","c":"sharepoint-onprem","u":"articles/sharepoint-onprem/classic-branding.html","s":"How classic SharePoint sites were branded and customized, covering master pages, page layouts, publishing, alternate CSS, Design Manager, Client-Side Rendering (JSLink) and Script Editor web parts, plus the risks they create in upgrades and how to modernize them.","k":"branding, master page, page layout, jslink, csr, publishing, design manager, classic","h":"The classic page model · JSLink / Client-Side Rendering example · Why classic branding hurts upgrades · Modernization map · Interview questions · What is the difference between a master page and a page layout? · What replaced JSLink in modern SharePoint? · Why are custom master pages discouraged even on-premises?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Server-Side Development","b":"Before modern pages, SharePoint looked the way its master page said it should. Brand-heavy intranets in 2007–2016 replaced master pages, built page layouts for publishing sites, and injected JavaScript everywhere. These techniques still exist on-premises and cause most of the \"why does my migrated site look broken?\" tickets. # The classic page model How a classic publishing page is assembled MASTER PAGE (.MASTER) Header / nav / footer ContentPlaceHolders CSS & JS references merged with PAGE LAYOUT (.ASPX) Field controls bound to page columns Web part zones Content type: Article / Welcome renders PUBLISHING PAGE (PAGES LIBRARY) Page content (HTML field) Web parts Metadata (columns) customized at runtime by CLIENT-SIDE TWEAKS Alternate CSS URL JSLink (CSR) on fields & views Script Editor / Content Editor web parts How a classic publishing page is assembled Technique Where Notes Custom master page _catalogs/masterpage Full control; must be re-applied/updated on every version upgrade Page layouts Master page gallery, tied to content types Publishing feature required Design Manager (2013) Convert HTML designs to master pages Removed from SharePoint 2019 modern experiences; deprecated Composed looks / themes Site settings → Change the look Lightweight; colour palette (.spcolor) + font scheme Alternate CSS URL Site settings → Master page Quick and dirty overrides JSLink / CSR Field, view, form web parts Render fields with JavaScript templates (2013+) Script/Content Editor web part Any page \"Inline\" JS and HTML; powerful but ungoverned # JSLink / Client-Side Rendering example CSR lets you replace how a field renders in views and forms, for example coloured status badges: status-badge.js (referenced via the field or list view web part JSLink property) JavaScript Copy ( function ( ) { var colors = { \"Submitted\" : \"#2563eb\" , \"In Review\" : \"#d97706\" , \"Closed\" : \"#059669\" , \"Rejected\" : \"#dc2626\" }; function renderStatus ( ctx ) { var value = ctx. CurrentItem . Status || \"\" ; var color = colors[value] || \"#64748b\" ; return \"<span style=&#x27;display:inline-block;padding:2px 10px;border-radius:12px;color:#fff;background:\" + color + \"&#x27;>\" + SPHttpUtility . HtmlEncode (value) + \"</span>\" ; } SPClientTemplates . TemplateManager . RegisterTemplateOverrides ({ Templates : { Fields : { \"Status\" : { View : renderStatus, DisplayForm : renderStatus } } } }); })(); Set it wit"},{"t":"CSOM, JSOM & REST – Client-Side CRUD in C# and JavaScript","c":"sharepoint-onprem","u":"articles/sharepoint-onprem/csom-jsom-rest.html","s":"The three classic client APIs side by side, with the same create, read, update and delete operations in C# CSOM, JavaScript JSOM and REST (fetch), plus request digests, ETags, batching and when to pick which.","k":"csom, jsom, rest, crud, javascript, csharp, request digest, odata","h":"The same CRUD in three APIs · Create · Read with filters · Update · Delete · Special column types cheat sheet · Which one should I use in 2026? · Interview questions · What does ExecuteQuery do in CSOM? · Why do REST POST requests need an X-RequestDigest? · How do you update a list item in REST without overwriting someone else's change?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Client-Side & Add-in Development","b":"SharePoint 2010 introduced the Client Object Model so code could run outside the server: in the browser ( JSOM ), in .NET apps ( CSOM ) and in Silverlight. SharePoint 2013 added the REST API at /_api . All three talk to the same server endpoint ( /_vti_bin/client.svc ) and are available in SharePoint Server and SharePoint Online. One server, three client APIs CLIENTS C# console / service (CSOM) Browser page (JSOM) Any HTTP client (REST) batched XML (CSOM/JSOM) or OData JSON (REST) SHAREPOINT ENDPOINTS /_vti_bin/client.svc/Process… /_api/web/... (OData v3) /_api/v2.0 (SPO, Graph-like) same server-side object model CONTENT Webs Lists Items Files Users & groups One server, three client APIs CSOM (.NET) JSOM (JavaScript) REST ( /_api ) Language C#, PowerShell JS in SharePoint pages Any Batching ExecuteQuery sends all queued ops executeQueryAsync $batch (multipart) Payload XML XML JSON (OData v3) Auth Windows, SharePointOnlineCredentials (legacy), access tokens Page cookie Cookie + digest, or bearer token Best for Back-end tools, migrations, PnP Legacy pages (2010–2016) SPFx, scripts, Power Automate HTTP # The same CRUD in three APIs # Create CSOM – create item C# Copy using ( var ctx = new ClientContext( \"https://sp.contoso.com/sites/quality\" )) { // On-prem Windows auth: ctx.Credentials = CredentialCache.DefaultNetworkCredentials; List list = ctx.Web.Lists.GetByTitle( \"Deviations\" ); ListItem item = list.AddItem( new ListItemCreationInformation()); item[ \"Title\" ] = \"Temperature excursion – Cold room 2\" ; item[ \"Area\" ] = \"Warehouse\" ; item[ \"DueDate\" ] = DateTime.Today.AddDays( 7 ); item.Update(); ctx.Load(item, i => i.Id); ctx.ExecuteQuery(); // one round trip Console.WriteLine( $\"Created {item.Id} \" ); } JSOM – create item JavaScript Copy var ctx = SP . ClientContext . get_current (); var list = ctx. get_web (). get_lists (). getByTitle ( \"Deviations\" ); var item = list. addItem ( new SP . ListItemCreationInformation ()); item. set_item ( \"Title\" , \"Temperature excursion – Cold room 2\" ); item. set_item ( \"Area\" , \"Warehouse\" ); item. update (); ctx. load (item); ctx. executeQueryAsync ( function ( ) { console . log ( \"Created\" , item. get_id ()); }, function ( sender, args ) { console . error (args. get_message ()); }); REST – create item JavaScript Copy async function getDigest ( siteUrl ) { const r = await fetch ( ` ${siteUrl} /_api/contextinfo` , { metho"},{"t":"SharePoint-Hosted Add-ins – App Web, Host Web & Cross-Domain Calls","c":"sharepoint-onprem","u":"articles/sharepoint-onprem/sharepoint-hosted-addins.html","s":"How SharePoint-hosted add-ins isolate JavaScript in an app web on its own domain, covering the app manifest, permissions, reading host-web data with the cross-domain library, app parts, and why they were retired in SharePoint Online.","k":"add-ins, sharepoint hosted, app web, host web, jsom, cross-domain, app part","h":"Host web vs app web · The app manifest · Reading host-web data from the app web · App parts (client web parts) · Limits and why they were retired · Interview questions · What is the purpose of the app web's separate domain? · How does a SharePoint-hosted add-in read data from the host web? · What replaces SharePoint-hosted add-ins?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Client-Side & Add-in Development","b":"A SharePoint-hosted add-in contains only client-side assets: HTML, JavaScript, CSS and declarative SharePoint artifacts (lists, content types). When installed, SharePoint creates a special subsite, the app web (add-in web), on an isolated domain , and serves the add-in's pages from there. # Host web vs app web SharePoint-hosted add-in topology HOST WEB https://sp.contoso.com/sites… Where the add-in is installed Business lists (Employees, Requests) install creates APP WEB (ADD-IN WEB) https://app-a1b2c3d4e5.conto… Pages/Default.aspx (your HTML/JS) Add-in lists (private) SP.RequestExecutor / SP.AppContextSite cross-domain calls PERMISSIONS AppManifest.xml requests (Read/Write/Manage/FullContr… Scope: Web / Site collection / List / Tenant Granted by installer (trust it) SharePoint-hosted add-in topology The host web is the site where the user installs the add-in. The app web is a hidden subsite with a unique domain ( app-<id>.<appdomain> ) that isolates the add-in's JavaScript from the host. That prevents an add-in from stealing the user's cookie session on the main site. On-premises this requires a dedicated app domain in DNS (wildcard *.contosoapps.com ), a wildcard certificate, and the App Management and Subscription Settings service applications. # The app manifest AppManifest.xml XML Copy <?xml version= \"1.0\" encoding= \"utf-8\" ?> < App xmlns = \"http://schemas.microsoft.com/sharepoint/2012/app/manifest\" Name = \"LeaveApp\" ProductID = \"{6c3f1e2a-9b7d-4e5f-8a1b-2c3d4e5f6a7b}\" Version = \"1.0.0.0\" SharePointMinVersion = \"15.0.0.0\" > < Properties > < Title > Leave Requests </ Title > < StartPage > ~appWebUrl/Pages/Default.aspx?{StandardTokens} </ StartPage > </ Properties > < AppPrincipal > < Internal /> <!-- SharePoint-hosted --> </ AppPrincipal > < AppPermissionRequests > < AppPermissionRequest Scope = \"http://sharepoint/content/sitecollection/web\" Right = \"Write\" /> </ AppPermissionRequests > </ App > # Reading host-web data from the app web Because the app web is on a different domain, JavaScript cannot simply call the host web. SharePoint provides the cross-domain library ( SP.RequestExecutor.js ), which uses a hidden iframe proxy ( AppWebProxy.aspx ): App.js – read the host web's Employees list via REST JavaScript Copy &#x27;use strict&#x27; ; var hostWebUrl = decodeURIComponent ( getQueryStringParameter ( &#x27;SPHostUrl&#x27; )); var appWebUrl = decodeURI"},{"t":"How Provider-Hosted Add-ins Work – Architecture, Tokens, Low-Trust vs High-Trust","c":"sharepoint-onprem","u":"articles/sharepoint-onprem/provider-hosted-addins.html","s":"A deep, diagram-first explanation of provider-hosted add-ins, covering the remote web, context and access tokens, Azure ACS (low-trust) vs S2S certificates (high-trust), app-only vs user+app policy, registration with AppRegNew and the install flow.","k":"provider-hosted, add-ins, oauth, acs, high trust, s2s, tokenhelper, csom, architecture","h":"Architecture at a glance · Two trust models · Low-trust flow (context token) · High-trust flow (server-to-server) · Registration and permissions · The project structure Visual Studio generates · Interview questions · What is the difference between low-trust and high-trust provider-hosted add-ins? · What is a context token and how is it used? · When would you use the app-only policy? · Why do effective permissions sometimes look \"lower\" than the user's?","l":"Advanced","x":"Client-Side & Add-in Development","b":"A provider-hosted add-in (PHA) is a SharePoint add-in whose logic runs on a web server you provide , the remote web . It is usually an ASP.NET MVC or Web Forms app on IIS or Azure App Service. SharePoint only knows the add-in's identity, its permissions and its start URL. When users open the add-in, they are redirected to your web app, which then calls back into SharePoint using CSOM or REST with an OAuth access token . This model let developers use any server-side technology (C#, Java, Node, SQL Server, external APIs) without installing code on SharePoint servers, which made it the 2013-era answer to \"no more farm solutions\". # Architecture at a glance Provider-hosted add-in architecture USER Browser Signed in to SharePoint clicks add-in tile / app part (iframe) SHAREPOINT (HOST WEB) Add-in instance & permissions AppRedirect.aspx (issues context token) Lists & libraries (data) POST SPAppToken + SPHostUrl to the remote web REMOTE WEB (IIS / AZURE APP SERVICE) ASP.NET MVC app TokenHelper.cs / SharePointContext.cs Business logic & own DB Remote event receivers (.svc) CSOM / REST with OAuth access token TRUST BROKER Low-trust: Azure ACS (SPO and hybrid on-prem) High-trust: S2S certificate (on-prem only) Provider-hosted add-in architecture # Two trust models Low-trust (ACS) High-trust (S2S) Where SharePoint Online (until Apr 2026); on-prem only if connected to ACS On-premises only Token issuer Azure Access Control Service (ACS) Your remote web, signing tokens with a certificate SharePoint trusts Setup Register client ID and secret ( AppRegNew.aspx ) New-SPTrustedSecurityTokenIssuer with a cert + Register-SPAppPrincipal User identity Context token carries user and context Remote web asserts the user identity (SharePoint trusts it) Typical use Store and tenant add-ins Enterprise on-prem line-of-business apps Status 2026 Retired in SPO (2 Apr 2026) Still supported on 2016/2019/SE # Low-trust flow (context token) sequenceDiagram autonumber actor U as User participant SP as SharePoint participant ACS as Azure ACS participant RW as Remote web (ASP.NET) U->>SP: Launch add-in (AppRedirect.aspx?client_id=…) SP->>ACS: Request context token for add-in + user ACS-->>SP: Context token (JWT, contains refresh token) SP-->>U: Auto-POST form → remote web (SPAppToken, SPHostUrl) U->>RW: POST /Home/Index (SPAppToken) RW->>RW: Validate context token with client secret RW->>ACS: Exc"},{"t":"Provider-Hosted Add-in CRUD – Read, Create, Update & Delete Code Templates (MVC + CSOM)","c":"sharepoint-onprem","u":"articles/sharepoint-onprem/provider-hosted-crud.html","s":"Copy-ready templates for a provider-hosted add-in, covering an MVC controller with SharePointContext, a repository with full CSOM CRUD, REST and app-only variants, Razor views, validation, paging and error handling.","k":"provider-hosted, crud, csom, mvc, csharp, rest, tokenhelper, app-only","h":"Request flow in the template · 1. The model · 2. The repository (all CSOM CRUD in one place) · 3. The controller · 4. A Razor view (Index) · 5. The same CRUD with REST from the remote web · 6. App-only context (no user), e.g. for a nightly job · Testing checklist · Interview questions · How does a provider-hosted MVC controller get a ClientContext for the host web? · Why don't REST calls from a provider-hosted add-in need X-RequestDigest? · How do you implement paging over a large list in CSOM?","l":"Advanced","x":"Client-Side & Add-in Development","b":"This article gives you a working CRUD template for a provider-hosted add-in built with ASP.NET MVC (.NET Framework 4.x) and CSOM . It manages a host-web list called Deviations ( Title , Area choice, Status choice, DueDate date, Owner person). Read how provider-hosted add-ins work first if the token flow is new to you. # Request flow in the template flowchart LR B[Browser] -->|GET /Deviations?SPHostUrl=...| F[SharePointContextFilter] F -->|no context → redirect to SharePoint<br/>to get a context token| SP[(SharePoint)] F -->|context OK| C[DeviationsController] C --> R[DeviationRepository] R -->|CSOM + Bearer token| SP C --> V[Razor views<br/>Index / Create / Edit / Delete] # 1. The model Models/Deviation.cs C# Copy using System; using System.ComponentModel.DataAnnotations; public class Deviation { public int Id { get ; set ; } [ Required, StringLength(255, MinimumLength = 5) ] public string Title { get ; set ; } [ Required ] public string Area { get ; set ; } // Production | Warehouse | Lab public string Status { get ; set ; } = \"Submitted\" ; [ DataType(DataType.Date) ] public DateTime? DueDate { get ; set ; } public string OwnerEmail { get ; set ; } // resolved to a SharePoint user public string OwnerName { get ; set ; } public DateTime Modified { get ; set ; } } # 2. The repository (all CSOM CRUD in one place) Data/DeviationRepository.cs C# Copy using System; using System.Collections.Generic; using System.Linq; using Microsoft.SharePoint.Client; public class DeviationRepository { private const string ListTitle = \"Deviations\" ; private readonly ClientContext _ctx; public DeviationRepository ( ClientContext ctx ) => _ctx = ctx; private List List => _ctx.Web.Lists.GetByTitle(ListTitle); // READ (paged) --------------------------------------------------------- public (IList<Deviation> Items, string NextPage) GetPage( string status, string pagingInfo, int pageSize = 30 ) { string where = string .IsNullOrEmpty(status) ? \"\" : $\"<Where><Eq><FieldRef Name=&#x27;Status&#x27;/><Value Type=&#x27;Choice&#x27;> {System.Security.SecurityElement.Escape(status)} </Value></Eq></Where>\" ; var q = new CamlQuery { ViewXml = $\"<View><Query> { where } <OrderBy><FieldRef Name=&#x27;ID&#x27; Ascending=&#x27;FALSE&#x27;/></OrderBy></Query>\" + $\"<ViewFields><FieldRef Name=&#x27;Title&#x27;/><FieldRef Name=&#x27;Area&#x27;/><FieldRef Name=&#x27;Status&#x27;/>\" + $\"<FieldRef Name=&#x27"},{"t":"Provider-Hosted Add-ins – Major Difficulties, Fixes & Migration to SPFx + Entra ID","c":"sharepoint-onprem","u":"articles/sharepoint-onprem/provider-hosted-challenges.html","s":"The real problems teams hit with provider-hosted add-ins (token errors, redirect loops, iframes, certificates, ACS retirement, hosting costs), how to fix each one, and a step-by-step plan to migrate add-ins to SPFx, Azure Functions and Entra ID apps.","k":"provider-hosted, troubleshooting, acs retirement, migration, spfx, entra id, add-ins","h":"Troubleshooting guide · Finding every add-in before you migrate · Migration: what each part becomes · Step-by-step plan · Replacing TokenHelper with MSAL + PnP Core SDK (.NET 8) · SPFx calling the new API · Interview questions · What are the most common causes of 401 errors in provider-hosted add-ins? · How would you migrate a provider-hosted add-in to modern SharePoint? · Why are app parts problematic in modern browsers?","l":"Advanced","x":"Client-Side & Add-in Development","b":"Provider-hosted add-ins (PHAs) solved the \"no server code in SharePoint\" problem, but they came with plenty of operational pain. If you support or migrate PHAs, these are the issues you will meet, roughly in order of how often they cause incidents. Cheat sheet The top 10 PHA pain points 01 1 · Token errors 401 Unauthorized , \"The remote server returned an error\", invalid context token, expired client secret. 02 2 · Redirect loops Lost SPHostUrl / session, cookies blocked in iframes, load-balanced servers without sticky sessions. 03 3 · Client secret expiry ACS secrets expired after 1 year (default) and everything broke on the same morning. 04 4 · Iframes App parts with double scroll bars, no responsive height, third-party cookie blocking in browsers. 05 5 · Certificates (high-trust) Expired signing certs, missing private-key permissions for the app pool. 06 6 · Hosting Separate IIS/Azure infrastructure, patching, scaling, DR and its own monitoring. 07 7 · Install & upgrade App web recreation, AppInstalled failures, versioning add-in instances across sites. 08 8 · Remote event receivers No retries, timeouts, unreachable endpoints, orphaned registrations. 09 9 · Performance Every page = redirect + token exchange + server-side CSOM round trips. 10 10 · Retirement ACS and SharePoint Add-ins retired in SPO on 2 April 2026 . # Troubleshooting guide Symptom Root cause Fix 401 on every CSOM call after a date Client secret expired Generate a new secret ( MsOnline /Graph for ACS-era apps), update web.config . Track expiry dates in a register \"Can't determine Security Token Service\" / invalid audience ClientId in web.config ≠ registered app; wrong realm Compare AppRegNew ID, AppManifest and config; check Get-SPAuthenticationRealm on-prem Endless redirects to AppRedirect.aspx Missing SPHostUrl on links/POSTs, session not shared across servers Persist SPHostUrl, use distributed session or sticky sessions, use SharePointContextProvider correctly Works in Chrome, fails in Safari or new Edge inside app part Third-party cookie blocking in iframes Avoid app parts, open full page, use SameSite=None; Secure cookies, or move the UI to SPFx High-trust: \"The token issuer is not trusted\" Cert mismatch, issuer ID not lowercase, missing IsTrustBroker Re-run New-SPTrustedSecurityTokenIssuer , iisreset on all servers High-trust: \"Keyset does not exist\" App pool identity cannot read the"},{"t":"Remote Event Receivers (RER) – How They Work, Code & Retirement","c":"sharepoint-onprem","u":"articles/sharepoint-onprem/remote-event-receivers.html","s":"How SharePoint calls out to a remote WCF service when list, item or add-in events fire, with sync vs async handling, registration, full C# code, and the modern webhook replacement.","k":"event receivers, remote event receivers, add-ins, wcf, csom, webhooks, provider-hosted","h":"How a remote event receiver works · Synchronous vs asynchronous events · Writing the receiver (C#) · Registering an RER on a host-web list · Classic vs remote vs webhooks vs Power Automate · The modern replacement: webhooks + Azure Functions · Common problems and fixes · Interview questions · What is the difference between ProcessEvent and ProcessOneWayEvent ? · How do you attach a remote event receiver to a list in the host web? · How do you prevent an infinite loop in an ItemUpdated RER? · Why were RERs replaced by webhooks in SharePoint Online? · Can a webhook cancel an item update like an -ing RER?","l":"Advanced","x":"Client-Side & Add-in Development","b":"A remote event receiver (RER) is an event handler whose code does not run inside SharePoint. When something happens in a list, library, web or add-in, SharePoint makes a SOAP call to a web service you host (on IIS or Azure App Service) and passes the event details. Your service reacts: it can validate data and cancel the change, update other items, call other systems, or log. RERs arrived with the SharePoint 2013 add-in (app) model . Microsoft wanted custom code out of the SharePoint process (farm solutions) so SharePoint Online could stay stable, and RERs were the answer for event-driven logic. Where RERs sit in the timeline 2007–2010: classic event receivers ran inside w3wp.exe as farm or sandboxed code. 2013: RERs moved that code to remote web services. 2016+: SharePoint webhooks became the recommended pattern for SharePoint Online. April 2, 2026: SharePoint Add-ins and Azure ACS were retired in SharePoint Online, so ACS-based RERs there stopped working. On-premises (2016, 2019, Subscription Edition), RERs still work with high-trust (S2S) add-ins. # How a remote event receiver works Remote event receiver – runtime architecture USER & CLIENT Browser Office client Power Automate CSOM/REST caller creates / updates / deletes an item SHAREPOINT (HOST WEB) List or library Event receiver registration (ReceiverUrl) Event pipeline SOAP POST: SPRemoteEventProperties + context token REMOTE WEB (IIS / AZURE APP SERVICE) WCF service (.svc) IRemoteEventService TokenHelper.cs CSOM / REST calls back with an OAuth access token IDENTITY Azure ACS (low-trust, SPO – retired) Certificate S2S (high-trust, on-prem) Remote event receiver – runtime architecture The flow: A user (or code) changes an item in a list that has an RER registered on it. SharePoint's event pipeline finds the registration and sees a ReceiverUrl pointing at your .svc endpoint. SharePoint serializes the event into SPRemoteEventProperties : event type, list ID, item ID, before and after properties, and a context token . It then POSTs that object to your service. Your service implements IRemoteEventService : ProcessEvent for synchronous (\"-ing\") events. SharePoint waits for your response, which can cancel the operation or change field values. ProcessOneWayEvent for asynchronous (\"-ed\") events. SharePoint fires and forgets. If you need to read or write SharePoint, TokenHelper.CreateRemoteEventReceiverClientCon"},{"t":"What is SharePoint Designer? Architecture, Workflows, Data Views & End of Support","c":"sharepoint-onprem","u":"articles/sharepoint-onprem/sharepoint-designer.html","s":"SharePoint Designer 2007–2013 explained, covering how it connects to sites, what it was used for (workflows, data views, external content types, page edits), the 2010 vs 2013 workflow platforms inside it, governance risks, and its July 2026 end of support.","k":"sharepoint designer, spd, workflows, data view web part, no-code, retirement","h":"Architecture: how SPD talks to SharePoint · What people used SharePoint Designer for · Workflow platforms inside SPD · Calling REST from a 2013 workflow · Governance risks · End of support and what replaces it · Interview questions · What is the difference between a list workflow, a reusable workflow and a site workflow in SPD? · What does the App Step do in a SharePoint 2013 workflow? · Why was SharePoint Designer considered a governance risk?","l":"Beginner","x":"Workflow & Forms","b":"SharePoint Designer (SPD) is a free desktop tool that connects directly to a SharePoint site and lets power users and developers build no-code workflows , data views , external content types and edit pages and master pages. It descends from FrontPage and shipped in three versions: 2007, 2010 and 2013 (2013 is the last; it works with SharePoint 2013, 2016, 2019, SE and SharePoint Online). # Architecture: how SPD talks to SharePoint SharePoint Designer connection architecture SHAREPOINT DESIGNER 2013 (DESKTOP) Workflow designer Site objects navigator Page & master page editor External content types FrontPage RPC (author.dll), SOAP web services (/_vti_bin/*.asmx), CSOM SHAREPOINT SITE Workflows library (XOML/XAML) Site Pages & Master Page Gallery Lists & content types BCS metadata store 2010 workflows run in SharePoint; 2013 workflows are published to WORKFLOW ENGINES SharePoint 2010 workflow engine (WF 3.5 in… Workflow Manager / SharePoint Workflow Manager… SharePoint Designer connection architecture SPD does not deploy anything to servers. Everything it creates is stored in the site (as files, list items or workflow definitions), so a site collection admin can use it without farm access. That convenience is also its biggest governance problem. # What people used SharePoint Designer for Cheat sheet SPD capabilities 01 Workflows List, reusable and site workflows. 2010 platform (stages via steps) and 2013 platform (stages, loops, dictionaries, HTTP calls). 02 Data View / Data Form web parts XSLT-based views over lists, SOAP/REST/XML and SQL data sources, with conditional formatting. 03 External content types Connect SQL / WCF / .NET data via BCS and generate external lists. 04 Custom list forms Replace NewForm/EditForm/DispForm with custom data form pages. 05 Page editing Edit .aspx pages, apply custom master pages, page layouts (publishing). 06 Site objects Manage lists, content types, columns, permissions, subsites from one UI. # Workflow platforms inside SPD SharePoint 2010 workflow SharePoint 2013 workflow Engine Windows Workflow Foundation 3.5, inside SharePoint WF 4.5 in Workflow Manager (separate service) Structure Steps, conditions, parallel blocks, impersonation step Stages , transitions (go to), loops, app step Data Variables, lookups Dictionaries, Call HTTP Web Service (REST) Approvals Rich \"Approval\" / \"Collect feedback\" task processes Task processes"},{"t":"Workflow Manager & SharePoint Workflow Manager – Installing the 2013 Workflow Platform","c":"sharepoint-onprem","u":"articles/sharepoint-onprem/workflow-manager.html","s":"How the SharePoint 2013 workflow platform runs outside SharePoint, covering the Workflow Manager and Service Bus architecture, installation and registration, the move to SharePoint Workflow Manager (2023), and troubleshooting stuck workflows.","k":"workflow manager, sharepoint workflow manager, service bus, 2013 workflows, installation","h":"Architecture · Installation outline (SharePoint Workflow Manager) · Troubleshooting stuck workflows · The future · Interview questions · Where do SharePoint 2013 workflows execute? · What is the role of the Workflow Manager client on SharePoint servers? · Why did Microsoft release SharePoint Workflow Manager in 2023?","l":"Advanced","x":"Workflow & Forms","b":"SharePoint 2013 moved the new workflow engine out of SharePoint . \"SharePoint 2013 workflows\" run in a separate farm, Workflow Manager , which talks to SharePoint over REST and OAuth. SharePoint 2010-style workflows still run inside SharePoint itself. In 2023 Microsoft released SharePoint Workflow Manager (SPWFM) , a supported successor to the ageing Workflow Manager 1.0 + Service Bus 1.1. It is built for SharePoint Server Subscription Edition , 2019 and 2016. # Architecture SharePoint 2013 workflow platform (on-premises) SHAREPOINT FARM SharePoint Designer / Visual Studio publish XAML Workflow Service Application Proxy Task lists & history lists HTTPS (port 12290) + OAuth S2S (SharePoint ↔ Workflow Manager trust) WORKFLOW MANAGER FARM Workflow Manager front end Workflow Manager backend (WF 4.5 engine) Service Bus / SPWFM messaging persistence SQL SERVER WFManagementDB WFInstanceManagementDB WFResourceManagementDB SB* databases (Service Bus) SharePoint 2013 workflow platform (on-premises) Workflow definitions (XAML) are published from SPD or Visual Studio into SharePoint, which pushes them to Workflow Manager. Workflow instances run in Workflow Manager. When they need SharePoint data they call SharePoint REST with an OAuth token (S2S trust). SharePoint calls Workflow Manager to start instances and deliver events (item changed, task completed). # Installation outline (SharePoint Workflow Manager) flowchart LR A[Install SPWFM binaries<br/>on 1 or 3 servers] --> B[Run Workflow Manager<br/>configuration wizard / PowerShell] B --> C[Create farm + DBs<br/>certificate for HTTPS] C --> D[Install SPWFM client<br/>on EVERY SharePoint server] D --> E[\"Register-SPWorkflowService<br/>-SPSite -WorkflowHostUri\"] E --> F[Test: SPD shows<br/>'SharePoint 2013 Workflow' platform] Register Workflow Manager with SharePoint (run on a SharePoint server) PowerShell Copy # HTTPS with a trusted certificate is strongly recommended Register-SPWorkflowService -SPSite \"https://sp.contoso.com\" ` -WorkflowHostUri \"https://wfm.contoso.com:12290\" ` -ScopeName \"SharePoint\" -Force # Verify the proxy is connected Get-SPWorkflowServiceApplicationProxy | Select-Object DisplayName, Status $proxy = Get-SPWorkflowServiceApplicationProxy $proxy .GetWorkflowServiceAddress(( Get-SPSite \"https://sp.contoso.com\" )) Health check on the Workflow Manager server PowerShell Copy Get-WFFarm | Select-Object End"},{"t":"Upgrade Paths – 2010 → 2013 → 2016 → 2019 → Subscription Edition (Database Attach)","c":"sharepoint-onprem","u":"articles/sharepoint-onprem/upgrade-paths.html","s":"How SharePoint version upgrades work, covering why there is no in-place upgrade, the database-attach method step by step, supported hops (including the 2016 → SE shortcut), service application upgrades, customizations and a project checklist.","k":"upgrade, database attach, subscription edition, test-spcontentdatabase, mount-spcontentdatabase, migration","h":"Supported hops · The database-attach process · Step by step · Planning checklist · Interview questions · Why does SharePoint use database attach instead of in-place upgrade? · What does Test-SPContentDatabase do? · What is the supported path from SharePoint 2013 to Subscription Edition? · Which service application databases can be upgraded with database attach?","l":"Advanced","x":"Upgrade & Lifecycle","b":"SharePoint Server does not support in-place upgrades between major versions. You build a new farm , then bring content across by database attach : restore the old databases into the new farm's SQL Server and mount them, and SharePoint upgrades the schema. Third-party migration tools such as ShareGate are the alternative when you want to restructure or skip versions. # Supported hops flowchart LR A[MOSS 2007] -->|DB attach| B[SP 2010] B -->|DB attach| C[SP 2013] C -->|DB attach| D[SP 2016] D -->|DB attach| E[SP 2019] D ==>|DB attach<br/>direct| F[Subscription Edition] E -->|DB attach| F C -.->|3rd-party tool<br/>e.g. ShareGate| F B -.->|3rd-party tool| F From To Native path 2010 SE 2010 → 2013 → 2016 → SE (three DB-attach hops) 2013 SE 2013 → 2016 → SE (two hops) 2016 SE Direct DB attach 2019 SE Direct DB attach Skipping versions with tools Each native hop needs a temporary farm, so 2010 → SE natively means building 2013 and 2016 farms just to upgrade databases. Migration tools (ShareGate, AvePoint, Quest) copy content directly from old to new and let you clean up as you go. See On-prem to on-prem . # The database-attach process Database-attach upgrade SOURCE FARM (E.G. 2016) Content DBs MMS DB Secure Store DB UPS Profile & Social DBs Search admin DB (optional) SQL backup (set read-only first for the final pass) TARGET SQL Restore DBs Correct permissions for farm account PowerShell on target farm TARGET FARM (SE) Service apps created from upgraded DBs Customizations deployed (WSPs) Test-SPContentDatabase Mount-SPContentDatabase validate POST-UPGRADE Upgrade-SPSite (if needed) Search crawl UAT & go-live DNS cut-over Database-attach upgrade # Step by step 1. On the SOURCE farm: inventory + set read-only for the final run PowerShell Copy Get-SPContentDatabase | Select-Object Name, WebApplication, CurrentSiteCount, @ {n= &#x27;SizeGB&#x27; ;e={[ math ]::Round( $_ .DiskSizeRequired/ 1 GB, 1 )}} | Export-Csv C:\\upg\\dbs.csv -NoTypeInformation # Final cut-over: make DBs read-only in SQL (users can still read) # ALTER DATABASE [WSS_Content_HR] SET READ_ONLY WITH NO_WAIT 2. On the TARGET farm: service apps from restored databases PowerShell Copy # Managed Metadata $pool = Get-SPServiceApplicationPool \"SharePoint Services App Pool\" $mms = New-SPMetadataServiceApplication -Name \"Managed Metadata Service\" -ApplicationPool $pool -DatabaseName \"SP2016_Service_MMS\" New-SPMet"},{"t":"Patching SharePoint – Cumulative Updates, PSConfig & Zero-Downtime Patching","c":"sharepoint-onprem","u":"articles/sharepoint-onprem/patching-cumulative-updates.html","s":"How SharePoint Server updates work, covering public updates vs CUs, the two-phase install (binaries + PSConfig), feature updates in Subscription Edition, zero-downtime patching with MinRole, and a monthly patch runbook.","k":"patching, cumulative update, psconfig, zero downtime, subscription edition, feature update","h":"Two phases: binaries, then configuration · Zero-downtime patching (2016+ with MinRole + HA) · Subscription Edition: feature updates and release rings · Monthly patch runbook · Interview questions · Why must PSConfig run on every server after installing an update? · How does zero-downtime patching work? · What is a feature update in Subscription Edition?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Upgrade & Lifecycle","b":"SharePoint Server receives monthly public updates (security and fixes) on Patch Tuesday. Since SharePoint 2016 these are packaged as a small number of cumulative packages per month. In Subscription Edition , the same channel also delivers feature updates (new capabilities) twice a year. # Two phases: binaries, then configuration flowchart LR A[Download monthly update<br/>sts + wssloc packages] --> B[Phase 1 · Install binaries<br/>on EVERY server] B --> C[Phase 2 · PSConfig<br/>on every server, one at a time] C --> D[Databases upgraded<br/>schema + build number] D --> E[Validate:<br/>Get-SPProduct, Health Analyzer,<br/>smoke tests] Install binaries ( .exe packages) on all SharePoint servers. Services keep running, but the farm is in a mixed state until phase 2. Run the configuration wizard / PSConfig on each server. The first server upgrades the databases, so expect downtime for content during that step unless you use the zero-downtime approach. PSConfig from the command line (run on each server) PowerShell Copy PSConfig.exe -cmd upgrade -inplace b2b -wait -cmd applicationcontent -install -cmd installfeatures -cmd secureresources -cmd services -install Check what still needs upgrading PowerShell Copy ( Get-SPFarm ).BuildVersion Get-SPProduct -Local # refresh local patch inventory Get-SPServer | Select-Object Name, NeedsUpgrade Get-SPContentDatabase | Where-Object NeedsUpgrade | Select-Object Name # Upgrade a content DB separately (allows controlling the window per DB) Upgrade -SPContentDatabase -Identity \"WSS_Content_HR\" -Confirm : $false # Zero-downtime patching (2016+ with MinRole + HA) Zero-downtime patching sequence PREREQUISITE Every role on ≥ 2 servers Load balancer with health probes SQL AlwaysOn Step 1 PATCH BINARIES – GROUP A Take FE1 App1 Search1 DC1 out of LB / rotation Install binaries Reboot return to rotation Step 2 PATCH BINARIES – GROUP B Repeat for FE2 App2 Search2 DC2 Step 3 PSCONFIG – ONE SERVER AT A TIME Upgrade databases (content DBs can be upgraded later… Users keep working (backwards-compatible mode) Zero-downtime patching sequence The trick is that SharePoint can serve content from databases at the old schema while binaries are newer ( backwards-compatibility mode ). That lets you postpone Upgrade-SPContentDatabase to quiet hours. # Subscription Edition: feature updates and release rings SE ships new features through the update channel"},{"t":"Backup, Restore & Disaster Recovery for SharePoint Server","c":"sharepoint-onprem","u":"articles/sharepoint-onprem/backup-dr.html","s":"What to back up (and what not), farm vs granular vs SQL backups, restoring a deleted site collection or document, unattached content database recovery, and designing DR with SQL AlwaysOn, with scripts.","k":"backup, restore, disaster recovery, sql alwayson, recycle bin, unattached content database","h":"Recovery layers, from fastest to slowest · What to back up · Restoring a single document from last week · Disaster recovery design · Interview questions · How do you restore a single deleted document when the recycle bins are empty? · Why don't you replicate the configuration database to a DR farm? · What is the difference between RPO and RTO?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Upgrade & Lifecycle","b":"A SharePoint farm is mostly SQL Server databases plus configuration on the servers. A good recovery plan protects both, and it is defined by two numbers: RPO (how much data you can lose) and RTO (how fast you must be back). # Recovery layers, from fastest to slowest flowchart TB A[User deletes a file] --> RB1[1 · Site recycle bin<br/>user self-service] RB1 --> RB2[2 · Site collection recycle bin<br/>admin, ~93 days total] RB2 --> V[3 · Versions<br/>restore previous version] V --> UDB[4 · Unattached content DB<br/>restore SQL backup to another name, export item] UDB --> SC[5 · Site collection backup<br/>Restore-SPSite] SC --> FARM[6 · Full SQL / farm restore<br/>disaster scenario] # What to back up Item How Notes Content databases SQL Server backups (full + diff + log) The heart of your data, so use full recovery model Service app DBs (MMS, Secure Store, UPS, BDC, Search admin) SQL backups Secure Store also needs the passphrase Configuration DB, Central Admin content DB SQL or farm backup Only useful for restoring the same farm; not for DR farms Search index Farm backup ( Backup-SPFarm ) or rebuild by crawling Index restore must match the topology Customizations Source control + WSP packages + scripts Do not rely on backups to recover code IIS, web.config, certificates Scripts, documentation, cert export Rebuild servers from scripts Farm configuration Backup-SPConfigurationDatabase + documentation For rebuilds Site collection and farm backups PowerShell Copy # Granular: one site collection (good up to ~100 GB) Backup-SPSite -Identity \"https://sp.contoso.com/sites/finance\" -Path \"\\\\backup\\sp\\finance_ $ (Get-Date -f yyyyMMdd).bak\" -UseSqlSnapshot # Restore it (to the same or another URL/content DB) Restore-SPSite -Identity \"https://sp.contoso.com/sites/finance-restored\" -Path \"\\\\backup\\sp\\finance_20261001.bak\" -DatabaseName \"WSS_Content_Restore\" -Force # Farm-level backup (config + content + service apps + search) Backup-SPFarm -Directory \"\\\\backup\\spfarm\" -BackupMethod Full # Restoring a single document from last week When the recycle bins are empty, restore last week's SQL backup of the content DB to a temporary database name (do not attach it to a web app), then export from it: Recover from an unattached content database PowerShell Copy $db = Get-SPContentDatabase -ConnectAsUnattachedDatabase -DatabaseName \"WSS_Content_HR_Restore\" -DatabaseServer \"SQL01\" # E"},{"t":"SharePoint Server Subscription Edition (SE) – What's New and How to Adopt It","c":"sharepoint-onprem","u":"articles/sharepoint-onprem/subscription-edition.html","s":"Everything that changed in Subscription Edition, including continuous feature updates, OIDC with Entra ID, TLS 1.3, modern PowerShell module, SSL certificate management, modern UI parity, licensing, and a readiness checklist.","k":"subscription edition, oidc, tls 1.3, feature updates, certificate management, sharepoint server","h":"What changed · Architecture: what is new around the farm · Certificate management (a real time-saver) · Modern SharePoint on-premises: what to expect · Readiness checklist for moving to SE · Interview questions · What is the main operational difference between SE and SharePoint 2019? · What authentication capability did SE add? · Can you upgrade from SharePoint 2013 directly to SE?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Upgrade & Lifecycle","b":"SharePoint Server Subscription Edition (SE) , released in November 2021 , is the on-premises SharePoint that no longer has version numbers. Instead of a new major release every three years, SE is updated continuously, and you stay supported by staying current. Since SharePoint 2016 and 2019 reached end of support on 14 July 2026 , SE is the only supported on-premises SharePoint. # What changed Cheat sheet Subscription Edition highlights 01 Continuous updates Monthly CUs + twice-yearly feature updates ; choose Standard or Early release ring. 02 Modern authentication OpenID Connect 1.0 with Microsoft Entra ID or ADFS; claims providers for people picker. 03 Security TLS 1.3 support, strong crypto by default, ADFS/SAML improvements, removal of legacy protocols. 04 SSL certificate management Manage certificates in Central Admin and PowerShell: import, assign to web apps, renew, notifications. 05 PowerShell SharePointServer module (PowerShell 5.1), no more snap-in requirement. 06 Modern experiences Modern sites, lists, pages, web parts and SPFx (versions aligned with what SE supports). 07 Windows Server 2019/2022/2025 & SQL 2019/2022 Newer platform support; .NET Framework 4.8. 08 Licensing Subscription licences (server + CAL) through volume licensing; no \"perpetual\" SE. # Architecture: what is new around the farm A modern SE farm IDENTITY Microsoft Entra ID (OIDC) or ADFS Conditional Access & MFA Claims provider for people picker OIDC sign-in SHAREPOINT SE FARM MinRole servers Central Admin certificate management Feature release ring (Standard / Early) TLS 1.3 / SQL PLATFORM Windows Server 2022 / 2025 SQL Server 2019 / 2022 AlwaysOn Office Online Server (separate) hybrid MICROSOFT 365 (OPTIONAL) Hybrid search / OneDrive redirection Power Automate via data gateway Microsoft 365 apps A modern SE farm # Certificate management (a real time-saver) SE: import, assign and renew certificates with PowerShell PowerShell Copy # Import a PFX into the SharePoint certificate store $pfxPwd = Read-Host -AsSecureString \"PFX password\" $cert = Import-SPCertificate -Path \"C:\\certs\\sp-contoso-2026.pfx\" -Password $pfxPwd -Exportable # Assign it to the web application&#x27;s HTTPS binding (all servers, handled by SharePoint) Set-SPWebApplication -Identity \"https://sp.contoso.com\" -Zone Default -Certificate $cert -SecureSocketsLayer -Port 443 # See expiring certificates Get-SPCertificate"},{"t":"SharePoint Hybrid – Search, OneDrive, Sites & App Launcher with Microsoft 365","c":"sharepoint-onprem","u":"articles/sharepoint-onprem/hybrid.html","s":"How to connect a SharePoint Server farm to Microsoft 365, covering hybrid OneDrive, cloud hybrid search, hybrid sites and the app launcher, the Hybrid Configuration Wizard, identity prerequisites, and hybrid as a migration bridge.","k":"hybrid, cloud hybrid search, onedrive, entra connect, hybrid configuration wizard, migration","h":"Hybrid features · Cloud hybrid search architecture · Prerequisites · Hybrid Configuration Wizard (HCW) · Hybrid as a migration bridge · Interview questions · Why must users be synchronized to Entra ID for cloud hybrid search? · What does the Hybrid Configuration Wizard set up? · What is usually the first hybrid workload, and why?","l":"Advanced","x":"Upgrade & Lifecycle","b":"Hybrid connects an on-premises SharePoint farm with SharePoint Online so users get one experience while content lives in both places. It is most useful during a long migration or when some content must stay on-premises permanently. # Hybrid features Cheat sheet Hybrid capabilities 01 Hybrid OneDrive Redirects \"OneDrive\"/My Site links on-prem to OneDrive in Microsoft 365. Usually the first workload moved. 02 Cloud hybrid search On-prem content is crawled by a cloud SSA and indexed in Microsoft 365, so one index and one result set across both. 03 Hybrid sites Followed sites and profile redirection: the user's profile lives in Microsoft 365. 04 Hybrid app launcher Microsoft 365 app launcher tiles appear on-prem. 05 Hybrid taxonomy & content types Shared term store and content type hub from SPO pushed to on-prem. 06 Hybrid auditing (preview era) Send on-prem audit data to Microsoft 365 (deprecated). 07 Inbound hybrid Search results from on-prem shown in SPO via a reverse proxy (older federated search model). # Cloud hybrid search architecture Cloud hybrid search ON-PREMISES SharePoint farm content File shares Cloud Search Service Application (crawler only) crawled items + ACLs pushed over HTTPS MICROSOFT 365 SEARCH INDEX Unified index Security trimming using synced identities queried from EXPERIENCES SharePoint Online search Microsoft Search in Bing/Office On-prem search center (redirect to SPO results) Cloud hybrid search The on-premises cloud SSA only crawls. Content processing and the index live in Microsoft 365. Because security trimming uses Entra ID identities , users must be synchronized with Entra Connect and on-prem ACLs must map to cloud identities. # Prerequisites Requirement Why Entra Connect (directory sync) of AD users Same identity on-prem and in the cloud UPN suffix routable (e.g. contoso.com , not contoso.local ) Matching users between environments User Profile + MMS service apps on-prem Hybrid OneDrive/sites, taxonomy App Management + Subscription Settings services Server-to-server trust SharePoint and Microsoft 365 global admin accounts Running the wizard Outbound HTTPS from the farm Cloud SSA, S2S # Hybrid Configuration Wizard (HCW) Run from Central Administration → Office 365 → Configure hybrid . It downloads the latest wizard, validates prerequisites, establishes the server-to-server trust (on-prem STS certificate registered with Entra ID),"},{"t":"What is SharePoint Online? The Content Platform of Microsoft 365","c":"sharepoint-online","u":"articles/sharepoint-online/overview.html","s":"SharePoint Online explained for 2026, covering what it is, how it relates to Teams, OneDrive, Lists, Loop and Copilot, the modern site model, what you can and cannot customize, and how it differs from SharePoint Server.","k":"sharepoint online, microsoft 365, overview, teams, onedrive, modern sharepoint","h":"Where SharePoint sits in Microsoft 365 · The modern site model · What you can customize · SharePoint Online vs SharePoint Server · What changed recently (2024–2026) · Interview questions · Why is SharePoint called the \"content backbone\" of Microsoft 365? · Why does modern SharePoint recommend flat site architecture? · What customization options are not available in SharePoint Online?","l":"Beginner","x":"Fundamentals","b":"SharePoint Online (SPO) is the cloud version of SharePoint, run by Microsoft as part of Microsoft 365 . It is much more than an intranet tool: it is the storage and content engine under Teams files, OneDrive, Microsoft Lists, Loop workspaces and Copilot's knowledge of your organization. If you use Microsoft 365, you use SharePoint, even if you never open a SharePoint site. 25 TB storage per site 250 GB maximum file size 30 M items per list or library 2 M sites per tenant # Where SharePoint sits in Microsoft 365 SharePoint as the content layer of Microsoft 365 EXPERIENCES Teams (files tab, channels) OneDrive (personal files) SharePoint sites & pages Microsoft Lists Loop Viva Connections all store content in SHAREPOINT ONLINE Site collections (team, communication… Libraries & lists Permissions & sharing Search index governed and enriched by PLATFORM SERVICES Microsoft Entra ID (identity) Microsoft Purview (labels, DLP,… Microsoft Graph (APIs) SharePoint Premium (AI content services) powers AI & APPS Microsoft 365 Copilot & agents Power Platform SPFx solutions SharePoint Embedded apps SharePoint as the content layer of Microsoft 365 # The modern site model Site type Purpose Backed by Microsoft 365 group? Team site Collaboration for a team or project Usually yes (Teams-connected) Communication site Broadcast: intranet, news, policies No Hub site A team or communication site registered as a hub to join others n/a (a role, not a template) Channel site Created for private/shared Teams channels Separate permissions OneDrive Personal storage per user No The key principle is a flat structure . Instead of deep subsites, create many site collections and connect them with hub sites . See Site types and Hubs & IA . # What you can customize flowchart LR A[No code] --> A1[Pages & web parts] A --> A2[Column/view/form formatting JSON] A --> A3[Site templates & themes] B[Low code] --> B1[Power Apps forms & apps] B --> B2[Power Automate flows] B --> B3[Copilot Studio / SharePoint agents] C[Pro code] --> C1[SPFx web parts & extensions] C --> C2[Graph / REST + Azure Functions] C --> C3[SharePoint Embedded apps] X[Not allowed] --> X1[Farm solutions / server code] X --> X2[Custom master pages on modern] X --> X3[Add-ins & ACS after Apr 2026] # SharePoint Online vs SharePoint Server SharePoint Online SharePoint Server (SE) Updates Continuous, automatic Monthly CUs you install Custom"},{"t":"Tenant Architecture – How SharePoint Online Is Organized Behind the Scenes","c":"sharepoint-online","u":"articles/sharepoint-online/tenant-architecture.html","s":"Tenants, the admin center, the root site, URLs and geos, how Microsoft 365 groups and Teams create sites, where identity and permissions come from, and the request path from browser to content.","k":"tenant, architecture, microsoft 365 groups, multi-geo, entra id, urls","h":"URLs you should know · The logical picture · How a Teams team creates a SharePoint site · Storage and quotas · Multi-Geo · Request path (simplified) · Interview questions · What is the difference between /sites/ and /teams/ URLs? · How are permissions managed for a Teams-connected site? · What determines the tenant's SharePoint storage?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Fundamentals","b":"A tenant is your organization's dedicated instance of Microsoft 365. For SharePoint, it means a set of URLs, a pool of storage, an admin center and policies that apply to every site. # URLs you should know URL What lives there https://contoso.sharepoint.com Root site (often the intranet home) https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/<name> Communication sites and most team sites https://contoso.sharepoint.com/teams/<name> Team sites (configurable managed path) https://contoso-my.sharepoint.com/personal/<user> OneDrive for each user https://contoso-admin.sharepoint.com SharePoint admin center https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/appcatalog Tenant app catalog (name varies) https://contoso.sharepoint.com/_layouts/15/sharepoint.aspx SharePoint start page # The logical picture SharePoint Online tenant – logical architecture IDENTITY – MICROSOFT ENTRA ID Users & guests Security groups Microsoft 365 groups Conditional Access & MFA membership drives permissions WORKLOADS CREATING SITES Teams (team + channel sites) Outlook / Planner / Viva Engage groups SharePoint admin & users Provisioning (PnP / Graph) site collections in SHAREPOINT ONLINE TENANT Root site Hub sites Team / communication… OneDrive sites App catalog governed by POLICIES Sharing & external access Purview labels / DLP / retention SharePoint Advanced Management Storage quotas SharePoint Online tenant – logical architecture # How a Teams team creates a SharePoint site sequenceDiagram autonumber actor U as Team owner participant T as Microsoft Teams participant G as Microsoft 365 Group (Entra ID) participant SP as SharePoint Online U->>T: Create team \"Quality Ops\" T->>G: Create M365 group (owners, members) G->>SP: Provision group-connected team site /sites/QualityOps SP-->>T: Default library \"Documents\" → General channel folder U->>T: Add private channel \"Audit\" T->>SP: Create separate channel site /sites/QualityOps-Audit Permissions on a group-connected team site come from the group : owners → Site Owners, members → Site Members. Adding someone to the team adds them to the site. Private and shared channels get their own site with separate membership. That matters for governance and migrations. # Storage and quotas Tenant storage pool = 1 TB + 10 GB per licensed user (plus purchased add-on storage). Each site can go up to 25 TB . Admins can switch between automatic and manual site quotas. OneDrive default sto"},{"t":"Modern vs Classic SharePoint – Differences, Why It Matters & How to Modernize","c":"sharepoint-online","u":"articles/sharepoint-online/modern-vs-classic.html","s":"A side-by-side comparison of the classic and modern experiences (pages, lists, customization, performance, mobile), how to detect classic sites in your tenant, and the modernization options from no-code to scripted transformation.","k":"modern, classic, modernization, pages, page transformation, experience","h":"Side by side · How the two experiences render · Modernization options · Interview questions · Name three reasons to move from classic to modern. · How do you convert classic pages to modern pages? · What does \"groupify\" mean?","l":"Beginner","x":"Fundamentals","b":"Classic SharePoint is the 2007–2013 user interface (ribbon, master pages, wiki and web part pages, server-rendered lists). Modern SharePoint (from 2016) is a client-side, responsive experience built with React. Both still exist in SharePoint Online, but every new investment, including Copilot, agents, the new web parts and SPFx extensions, targets modern . # Side by side Area Classic Modern Pages Wiki/web part/publishing pages, page layouts Site pages with sections and web parts, page templates Navigation Structural/managed navigation per site Hub navigation, global navigation, audience targeting Lists Server-rendered views, JSLink Client-rendered, column/view/form formatting JSON Branding Master pages, alternate CSS Themes, brand center, SPFx application customizer Customization Script Editor, JSLink, add-ins, farm (on-prem) SPFx (web parts, extensions, ACEs) Mobile Limited Fully responsive, SharePoint mobile app Performance Heavy server rendering Client-side, CDN-hosted assets Copilot / agents Limited value Full support # How the two experiences render flowchart LR subgraph Classic C1[Request] --> C2[ASP.NET renders master page<br/>+ page layout + web parts on server] C2 --> C3[HTML + inline scripts<br/>Script Editor, JSLink] end subgraph Modern M1[Request] --> M2[Lightweight shell] M2 --> M3[React app loads from CDN] M3 --> M4[Page JSON + web parts via API<br/>SPFx bundles from CDN] end # Modernization options Cheat sheet Ways to modernize 01 Lists & libraries Already modern by default; check \"List experience\" settings and remove JSLink dependencies. 02 Pages PnP page transformation : converts wiki/web part/publishing pages to modern pages with mapped web parts. 03 Team sites Connect to a Microsoft 365 group (\"groupify\") to add Teams, Planner, shared mailbox. 04 Navigation Replace subsites with hubs ; flatten structure with ShareGate/Move-SPOSite or rebuild. 05 Customizations Script Editor → SPFx web part; custom actions → command sets; master page → app customizer. 06 Root site Swap the classic root with a modern communication site ( Invoke-SPOSiteSwap ). Groupify a classic team site and swap the root site PowerShell Copy Connect-SPOService -Url https://contoso -admin .sharepoint.com # Attach a Microsoft 365 group to an existing team site Set-SPOSiteOffice365Group -Site https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/finance ` -DisplayName \"Finance\" -Alias \"finance"},{"t":"Team Sites vs Communication Sites vs Hub Sites vs Channel Sites","c":"sharepoint-online","u":"articles/sharepoint-online/site-types.html","s":"When to use each modern site type, what each one includes, permissions and Microsoft 365 group implications, templates you can pick, and how to create each one with PowerShell or Graph.","k":"team site, communication site, hub site, channel site, microsoft 365 groups, site templates","h":"Decision guide · Comparison · Templates you can choose · Creating sites with code · Interview questions · When would you choose a communication site over a team site? · Is a hub site a separate template? · Why does a private channel have its own SharePoint site?","l":"Beginner","x":"Sites & Information Architecture","b":"Choosing the right site type is the first information architecture decision. The rule of thumb: team sites are for working together, communication sites are for telling many people something , and hubs tie related sites together. # Decision guide flowchart TD Q1{Who creates the content?} -->|A small group, everyone edits| T[Team site] Q1 -->|A few authors, many readers| C[Communication site] T --> Q2{Need Teams chat,<br/>Planner, shared mailbox?} Q2 -->|yes| TG[Group-connected team site<br/>+ Microsoft Teams] Q2 -->|no| TN[Team site without a group<br/>STS#3] C --> Q3{Should related sites share<br/>navigation, theme, search?} T --> Q3 Q3 -->|yes| H[Register one site as a HUB<br/>and associate the others] TG --> Q4{Confidential sub-team?} Q4 -->|yes| P[Private / shared channel<br/>→ separate channel site] # Comparison Team site Communication site Hub site Channel site Purpose Collaboration Broadcast and publishing Connect & roll up Private/shared channel files Template GROUP#0 / STS#3 SITEPAGEPUBLISHING#0 Any site registered as hub TEAMCHANNEL#1 (auto) M365 group Usually yes No n/a No (channel membership) Permissions model Owners / Members / Visitors (group) Owners / Members / Visitors (SharePoint groups) Each site keeps its own Channel owners/members Default home page News, activity, documents Hero, news, events Hub navigation bar + roll-ups Library only Typical example Project X, HR team workspace Intranet home, HR policies portal \"Human Resources\" hub \"Audit\" private channel # Templates you can choose When creating a site, users can pick Microsoft-provided site templates : Project management, Event planning, Training and courses, Department, Leadership connection, Learning central, Crisis management, Human resources, Showcase, Topic, Blank and others. Organizations can add custom templates with site scripts. See Site templates & site scripts . # Creating sites with code PnP PowerShell PowerShell Copy Connect-PnPOnline -Url https://contoso -admin .sharepoint.com -Interactive -ClientId $clientId # Group-connected team site (also creates the M365 group) New-PnPSite -Type TeamSite -Title \"Quality Ops\" -Alias \"qualityops\" -IsPublic : $false -Owners \"jane@contoso.com\" # Team site without a Microsoft 365 group New-PnPSite -Type TeamSiteWithoutMicrosoft365 Group -Title \"Legacy Archive\" -Url https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/archive # Communication site New-PnPS"},{"t":"Hub Sites & Information Architecture – Designing a Flat, Scalable Intranet","c":"sharepoint-online","u":"articles/sharepoint-online/hub-sites-information-architecture.html","s":"How to design modern information architecture with hubs, global navigation, hub navigation, audience targeting, site naming and metadata, with a worked example of an intranet IA and the scripts to build it.","k":"hub sites, information architecture, navigation, audience targeting, intranet, flat architecture","h":"The three levels of navigation · Why hubs instead of subsites · Hub features · Worked example: intranet IA · Audience targeting · IA design checklist · Interview questions · What is the maximum depth of hub navigation, and how do associated hubs work? · Is audience targeting a security feature? · How would you restructure an intranet built with deep subsites?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Sites & Information Architecture","b":"Information architecture (IA) is how content is organized, labelled and navigated so people can find it. In modern SharePoint the IA building blocks are sites (one per topic, team or project), hubs (to group sites), navigation at three levels, and metadata inside sites. # The three levels of navigation Modern navigation layers GLOBAL NAVIGATION (APP BAR) Tenant-wide Set from the home site Links to top hubs & resources then HUB NAVIGATION Shared by all sites in a hub Up to 3 levels Audience targeting then SITE NAVIGATION Per site Quick launch or horizontal Pages libraries lists Modern navigation layers # Why hubs instead of subsites flowchart LR subgraph Old[\"Classic: hierarchy\"] R[Intranet] --> HR[HR] --> HRP[Policies] R --> FIN[Finance] --> AP[AP Team] end subgraph New[\"Modern: flat + hubs\"] HUB1((HR hub)) --- s1[HR Portal] HUB1 --- s2[HR Policies] HUB1 --- s3[Recruitment team] HUB2((Finance hub)) --- s4[Finance Portal] HUB2 --- s5[AP Team] HOME[Home site] -.global nav.-> HUB1 HOME -.global nav.-> HUB2 end Subsites (classic) Hubs (modern) Permissions inherit down the tree Each site keeps its own permissions Hard to move (re-parenting means a migration) Re-associate a site with another hub in seconds Navigation built per site collection Hub navigation shared automatically Fixed by org chart Flexible when the organization changes # Hub features Shared navigation bar and theme for associated sites. Search scope : searching from a hub site returns results from all associated sites. Roll-ups : the News, Events, Highlighted content and Sites web parts can show content from all hub sites. Hub permissions sync (optional): add hub visitors to associated sites. Associated hubs : link hubs together for search and roll-ups (up to 3 levels). # Worked example: intranet IA Hub Sites (examples) Owner Audience Home (home site) Intranet home, News center Internal Comms Everyone People & HR HR Portal (comm), Policies (comm), Learning (comm), HR Team (team) HR Everyone / HR Operations Quality (comm), Deviations app (team), Plant A (team), Plant B (team) COO office Ops staff Finance Finance Portal (comm), AP (team), Audit (team, private) CFO office Finance / Everyone IT IT Help (comm), Projects hub sites CIO office Everyone / IT Build the hub structure with PnP PowerShell PowerShell Copy $hubs = @ ( @ { Url = \"/sites/people\" ; Title = \"People & HR\" }, @ { Url = \"/sites/operatio"},{"t":"Modern Pages & News – Sections, Layouts, Page Templates and News Publishing","c":"sharepoint-online","u":"articles/sharepoint-online/pages-news.html","s":"How modern pages are built (sections, columns, vertical sections, backgrounds), page templates, news posts and boosting, organization news sites, scheduling, approvals and the page APIs for automation.","k":"pages, news, sections, page templates, organization news, boost, publishing","h":"Page anatomy · Page templates · News · Creating pages with code · Interview questions · What makes a page a \"news post\"? · What is an organization news site? · How do you standardize page layouts for authors?","l":"Beginner","x":"Sites & Information Architecture","b":"A modern page is a canvas of sections containing web parts . Pages are stored as list items in the Site Pages library with page content serialized as JSON, so they are fast, responsive and editable without code. # Page anatomy Title area · image, author, published date (or a full-width hero) Section 1 · full-width: Hero web part Section 2 · two-thirds column: News One-third: Events Section 3 · three columns: Quick links People Countdown timer Section 4 · two columns + vertical section: Text, Image Vertical section (right rail) · Highlighted content Sections stack vertically. Each has a column layout (1, 2, 3, ⅓-⅔, ⅔-⅓, full width) and an optional background. A vertical section can sit on the right. Element Options Section layouts One column, two columns, three columns, one-third left/right, full-width (comm sites), vertical section Section background None, neutral, soft, strong, or image (with overlays) Title area Image, plain, colour block, overlap; topic header; author & date Page details Page properties (metadata) shown with the Page Properties web part # Page templates Save any page as a template ( Save as template in the page command bar). Templates live in the Templates folder of Site Pages and appear in the New page dialog. They are perfect for standard layouts such as \"Department announcement\", \"Project status\" or \"Policy page\". # News News posts are pages with PromotedState = 2 . They roll up into: the site's News web part , the hub's News web part (for associated sites), the SharePoint start page and SharePoint app bar for followed sites, Viva Connections and the SharePoint mobile app , email digests. Cheat sheet News publishing options 01 Organization news sites Admin marks authoritative sites (e.g. Corporate Comms). Their posts get special styling and priority. 02 Boost Promote a post to appear at the top of news feeds and Viva Connections for a time window. 03 Scheduling Pick a future publish date (Site Pages scheduling must be enabled). 04 Approvals Page approval or Power Automate approval flows before publishing. 05 Audience targeting Show a post only to selected groups in News web parts. 06 Send by email Send a news post as an email digest to people or groups. Mark an organization news site PowerShell Copy Connect-SPOService -Url https://contoso -admin .sharepoint.com Set-SPOOrgNewsSite -OrgNewsSiteUrl https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/new"},{"t":"Multilingual Sites – Translation Pages, Language Settings & Global Intranets","c":"sharepoint-online","u":"articles/sharepoint-online/multilingual-sites.html","s":"How the modern multilingual feature works (source and translation pages, translators, notifications), language of the UI vs content, machine translation options, and patterns for global intranets.","k":"multilingual, translation, global intranet, languages, communication sites","h":"How it works · Patterns for global intranets · Interview questions · What happens if a user's language has no translation page? · Can you change a site's default language later?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Sites & Information Architecture","b":"Global organizations need intranet content in several languages. Modern SharePoint handles this with the multilingual pages feature on communication sites (and team sites created without a group): each source page can have translation pages in target languages, and users automatically see the version for their preferred language. # How it works flowchart LR A[Site default language<br/>e.g. English] --> B[Enable 'Multilingual'<br/>+ choose target languages] B --> C[Assign translators<br/>per language] C --> D[Author creates source page] D --> E[Translation → creates copies<br/>in /SitePages/de/, /fr/...] E --> F[Translators notified,<br/>translate & publish] F --> G[User with German preference<br/>sees the German page automatically] D -.source updated.-> H[Translators notified<br/>that source changed] Concept Detail UI language Site chrome (menus, buttons) follows the user's Microsoft 365 language if the site has that language enabled Content language Pages are separate items per language; the source page holds links to translations Fallback If no translation exists, users see the source page Navigation Site and hub navigation labels can be translated per language News Translated news posts roll up correctly for each language # Patterns for global intranets Cheat sheet Choosing a multilingual pattern 01 Translation pages (built-in) Best for curated content on comm sites; translators keep pages in sync. 02 Separate sites per region Use when content differs (local policies, local news). Hubs per region + global hub. 03 Machine translation Use SharePoint's built-in translate options or Microsoft Translator via Power Automate for drafts, then human review. 04 English-first with local news Common compromise: global content in English, regional hubs publish local-language news. Language settings that trip people up Users' preferred language comes from their Microsoft 365 profile (or Delve and profile settings). A site's language list must include it. Remember that the site default language cannot be changed after creation, so pick it carefully. # Interview questions Q1 What happens if a user's language has no translation page? They see the source (default language) page. The multilingual feature only redirects when a published translation exists for the user's preferred language. Q2 Can you change a site's default language later? No. The default language is set whe"},{"t":"The Modern Web Part Catalog – Every Out-of-the-Box Web Part, Grouped by Purpose","c":"sharepoint-online","u":"articles/sharepoint-online/webparts-catalog.html","s":"A field guide to modern SharePoint web parts, grouped into text and media, news and roll-ups, documents and lists, people and events, business data and connectors, and layout helpers, with when to use each and their gotchas.","k":"web parts, modern pages, hero, quick links, events, news, highlighted content, catalog","h":"1 · Text, media & storytelling · 2 · News & roll-ups (dynamic content) · 3 · Documents & lists · 4 · People & events · 5 · Business data & connected apps · 6 · Layout helpers · Choosing the right web part · Interview questions · What is the difference between the News and Highlighted content web parts? · How do you embed external content safely on modern pages? · Which web part would you use for a department's latest policy documents?","l":"Beginner","x":"Modern Web Parts","b":"Modern pages ship with dozens of web parts. Knowing them well is the cheapest way to build great intranets without code , and the best way to avoid writing an SPFx web part that already exists. 6 families text & media, roll-ups, documents, people & events, data, layout 0 code needed for most intranet pages 1 click to swap layouts on Hero, Quick links, News, Events # 1 · Text, media & storytelling Family 1 Text & media web parts 01 Text Rich text, headings, tables, links. Use heading styles for accessibility and page TOC. 02 Image Single image with caption, alt text, and link; supports cropping and aspect ratios. 03 Image gallery Brick, carousel or grid layouts from uploaded images or a library. 04 Hero Up to 5 tiles (or layers) with image, title, call-to-action. See the deep dive. 05 Banner Large headline banner with background image or colour. 06 Button / Call to action Single prominent action, with an optional background image. 07 File and media Shows PDF, Office docs, 3D, video files inline (formerly \"File viewer\"). 08 Stream / YouTube / Embed Videos and embeddable content (iframe allow-list governs Embed). 09 Markdown / Code snippet Technical content with syntax highlighting. # 2 · News & roll-ups (dynamic content) Web part What it rolls up Scope options Typical use News News posts This site, hub, selected sites, recommended for user Home page news carousel Highlighted content Documents, pages, news, videos, list items by filter/query Site, hub, all sites, a library \"Latest policies\", \"Training videos\" Sites Sites (frequent, hub sites, selected) Hub / user Department directory Recent documents Documents the user worked on recently Personal Personal dashboard Saved for later Items the user saved Personal Personal dashboard Site activity Recent changes in the site Site Team site home # 3 · Documents & lists Web part Notes Document library Embed a library view; pick a view, folder and size. Users can upload and edit in place. List Show a list view with formatting; supports filtering and Microsoft Lists features. List properties / Page properties Show selected fields of an item or of the page itself (connected web parts). Quick chart Simple bar/pie chart from manual data or a list. # 4 · People & events Web part Notes People Profile cards of chosen people (compact or descriptive). Organization chart Manager/peer view from Entra ID data. Events Upcoming event"},{"t":"The Hero Web Part – Tiles vs Layers, the 5-Tile Layout and Design Rules","c":"sharepoint-online","u":"articles/sharepoint-online/hero-webpart.html","s":"A deep dive into the Hero web part, covering tiles and layers layouts (1–5 items), image sizes, call-to-action links, dynamic content from a library, accessibility, and how to automate Hero configuration.","k":"hero web part, web parts, layout, intranet design, communication sites","h":"Tiles layout · Layers layout · Content sources per item · Design rules that make Hero look professional · Automating a Hero web part · Interview questions · What is the maximum number of items in a Hero web part, and what layouts exist? · How do you keep hero images from cropping faces on mobile?","l":"Beginner","x":"Modern Web Parts","b":"The Hero web part is the first thing most people see on a communication site: up to five visual items, each with an image, title, optional call to action and link. It has two layouts, Tiles and Layers . # Tiles layout Tile 1 · largest (primary story) Tile 2 Tile 3 Tile 4 Tile 5 Tiles with 5 items: one large tile + four smaller tiles. With 1–4 items, SharePoint rearranges the grid automatically. 1 2 3 Tiles with 3 items # Layers layout Layer 1 · image left, text right, call-to-action link Layer 2 · alternates image side Layer 3 Layers stack items as full-width bands. Better for longer descriptions and scrolling storytelling. Tiles Layers Items 1–5 1–5 Best for Visual home page \"front door\" Story-like sections, campaigns Text Title + optional call to action Title, description, call to action, topic heading Mobile Stacks to single column Stacks naturally Section Full-width section recommended on comm sites Any section # Content sources per item Manual : pick an image and link to any URL (page, document, external site). From the site : choose a page or document and the web part uses its thumbnail and title. Custom image : upload or pick from stock images, organization assets library or the web. # Design rules that make Hero look professional Cheat sheet Hero design checklist 01 Image size Use high-resolution landscape images. 16:9 works across tile sizes; the largest tile benefits from ~1920×1080. 02 Focal point Set the focal point on each image so cropping keeps faces and subjects visible on all devices. 03 Short titles 3–7 words. Tiles truncate long titles, especially on small tiles. 04 Contrast Text sits on images, so avoid busy backgrounds; the web part adds a gradient but it is not magic. 05 Alt text Add alternative text for every image (accessibility and Copilot understanding). 06 Rotation Refresh content regularly; stale hero tiles are ignored within weeks. 07 Org assets Store approved images in an organization assets library so authors pick consistent brand imagery. # Automating a Hero web part The Hero web part's configuration is JSON stored in the page canvas. You can add one with PnP PowerShell and set its properties: Add a Hero web part to a page PowerShell Copy Connect-PnPOnline -Url https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/home -Interactive -ClientId $clientId $page = Get-PnPPage -Identity \"Home.aspx\" Add-PnPPageSection -Page $page -SectionTemplate One"},{"t":"Events & Calendar Web Parts – Events Lists, Group Calendars and Migrating Classic Calendars","c":"sharepoint-online","u":"articles/sharepoint-online/events-calendar-webparts.html","s":"How the Events web part, events lists and Group calendar work, with filmstrip, compact and carousel layouts, hub roll-ups, category filters, the classic calendar list problem, and options to migrate or replace calendars.","k":"events web part, calendar, group calendar, events list, migration, roll-up","h":"Events web part layouts · Events web part vs Group calendar · How hub roll-up works · The classic calendar list problem · Interview questions · What is the difference between the Events and Group calendar web parts? · How do you show events from several department sites on the intranet home? · How should recurring events in classic calendars be handled during migration?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Modern Web Parts","b":"Most intranets need \"what's on\" calendars. Modern SharePoint offers two different tools that people often confuse: the Events web part (backed by a SharePoint events list ) and the Group calendar web part (backed by the Outlook calendar of a Microsoft 365 group ). # Events web part layouts Filmstrip · 12 OCT · Town hall 15 OCT · Safety week 20 OCT · Hackathon → more Filmstrip: date badges with images, scroll horizontally 12 OCT · Town hall · 10:00 · Teams 15 OCT · Safety week kickoff · Plant A 20 OCT · Hackathon registration closes Compact: dense list, ideal for one-third columns or vertical sections Carousel · one big event card at a time with image Carousel (full-width sections). A Calendar view lets users browse by month. # Events web part vs Group calendar Events web part Group calendar web part Data source SharePoint events list (site, hub or multiple sites) Exchange calendar of a Microsoft 365 group Who adds events Site members (or anyone with list permissions) Group members via Outlook/Teams Roll-ups across sites ✅ Hub, selected sites, all sites in a hub ❌ One group per web part Categories & filters ✅ Category column, date ranges, audience targeting ❌ Add to my calendar ✅ Download .ics Native Outlook Online meeting links ✅ Teams link field ✅ Best for Public intranet events, trainings Team-internal schedule # How hub roll-up works flowchart LR E1[Site A<br/>Events list] --> W[Events web part<br/>on hub home<br/>Source: all sites in the hub] E2[Site B<br/>Events list] --> W E3[Site C<br/>Events list] --> W W --> F[Filter: Category = Training<br/>Date: next 30 days<br/>Audience: Plant A] # The classic calendar list problem Classic Calendar lists (template 106) from SharePoint 2010–2016 still exist in many tenants. In modern SharePoint: The Events web part can use them as a source (events lists are calendar lists). Recurring events created in classic calendars render, but editing recurrence in the modern UI is limited. The classic calendar view (month grid with overlays) is classic-only. Modern lists offer a Calendar view (list view type) for any list with date columns. Migration options for classic calendars Keep as events list : works for simple calendars, so add an Events web part on the modern page. Modern list + calendar view : create a Microsoft List with start/end date columns and a calendar view; migrate items (ShareGate maps calendar → list). Gro"},{"t":"Quick Links & Highlighted Content – Layouts, Filters, KQL and Audience Targeting","c":"sharepoint-online","u":"articles/sharepoint-online/quick-links-highlighted-content.html","s":"The two most versatile modern web parts, with all six Quick links layouts, icons and audiences, and Highlighted content sources, filters, sort options, custom KQL queries and performance tips.","k":"quick links, highlighted content, kql, web parts, audience targeting, roll-up","h":"Quick links: six layouts · Highlighted content: dynamic roll-ups · Custom KQL examples · Performance and governance tips · Interview questions · How does Highlighted content get its data? · How do you filter Highlighted content by a custom column across sites? · What is the most compact Quick links layout and when would you use it?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Modern Web Parts","b":"# Quick links: six layouts 🔗 Payroll 🔗 IT help 🔗 Travel 🔗 Policies 🔗 Canteen 🔗 Learning 🔗 Benefits 🔗 Org chart Compact: icon + title chips, the densest layout Filmstrip · image card Card Card → Filmstrip: image cards in a scrolling strip Grid · image + title Grid Grid Grid Grid Grid Grid: image tiles in rows Button · Submit leave Button · Book a desk Button · Raise ticket Button: call-to-action buttons with optional description List · icon · Title · description List · icon · Title · description List: vertical list with descriptions Tiles · icon Tiles Tiles Tiles Tiles: square icon tiles Each link can have a title, description, thumbnail (auto, custom image or icon), alternative text , and an audience (Entra ID / M365 groups) if audience targeting is enabled on the web part. # Highlighted content: dynamic roll-ups Highlighted content query pipeline SOURCE This site A document library The page library Hub sites All sites Selected sites then FILTER MODE Type (documents, pages, news,… Title includes… Managed property = value Created/modified by Recently added/changed or CUSTOM QUERY KQL across the search index Managed properties (RefinableStringXX) Result source sort & present LAYOUT Cards List Carousel Filmstrip Show N items \"See all\" link Highlighted content query pipeline Because Highlighted content uses search under the hood (except when the source is a single library in \"document library\" mode), new content appears after crawling, typically within minutes, and security trimming is automatic. # Custom KQL examples Useful Highlighted content queries KQL Copy // Policies from any site, newest first (use with \"Custom query\" mode) ContentType:\"Policy Document\" IsDocument:true // Training videos tagged for Plant A, last 90 days FileExtension:mp4 RefinableString05:\"Plant A\" LastModifiedTime>={Today-90} // News posts from sites in the HR hub (DepartmentId = the hub site's ID) by the HR comms author DepartmentId:\"6f1c2a9e-3b4d-4e5f-8a7b-9c0d1e2f3a4b\" PromotedState:2 Author:\"HR Communications\" // Deviation SOPs in quality sites (path scope) path:\"https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/quality\" ContentType:SOP Managed properties for filters To filter on your own site columns (e.g. \"Plant\"), map their crawled property ( ows_Plant ) to a reusable RefinableString managed property in the SharePoint admin center search schema. Then use RefinableString05:\"Plant A\" in "},{"t":"Document Library, List, File & Embed Web Parts – Bringing Data onto Pages","c":"sharepoint-online","u":"articles/sharepoint-online/document-list-embed-webparts.html","s":"How to put libraries, lists, files, Power BI, Power Apps and external content on modern pages, with connected (dynamic data) web parts, view and folder selection, iframe allow-lists and common limitations.","k":"document library web part, list web part, embed, power bi, power apps, dynamic data, file viewer","h":"Connected web parts (dynamic data) · Document library & List web parts · File and media · Power BI & Power Apps · Embed web part & HTML field security · Interview questions · How can you build a dashboard where selecting a project filters its documents, without code? · Why might an iframe not render in the Embed web part?","l":"Beginner","x":"Modern Web Parts","b":"Pages become useful dashboards when they show live data. Modern SharePoint provides Document library , List , File and media , Power BI , Power Apps and Embed web parts. Several of them can be connected , so selecting an item in one filters another. # Connected web parts (dynamic data) flowchart LR L[List web part<br/>Projects] -->|selected project| D[Document library web part<br/>filtered by Project column] L -->|selected project| P[List properties web part<br/>shows project details] D -->|selected document| F[File and media web part<br/>previews the document] List · Projects (select a row) List properties · Manager, Budget, Status Document library · files of the selected project File and media · preview of the selected document A no-code project dashboard built from four connected web parts Supported connections include List → Document library, List → List properties, Document library → File and media, Page properties and search-box-like scenarios. Configure them in the target web part's property pane under Connect to source . # Document library & List web parts Setting Notes Library / list Any in the current site (cross-site requires the source site's web part or an SPFx solution) View Pick a view, including views with view formatting JSON Folder Show a specific folder Size Fixed height for consistent page design Hide command bar Cleaner read-only display Same-site limitation These web parts show libraries and lists from the current site . For cross-site roll-ups use Highlighted content (search-based) or an SPFx web part. # File and media Previews 270+ file types inline: Office documents, PDFs, images, 3D models, videos and Visio. Choose a start page for PDFs and toggle the download and print toolbar. # Power BI & Power Apps Power BI : paste the report link and choose the page and display options. Viewers need access to the report (and appropriate licences or Premium capacity). Use filters in the URL for context ( ?filter=Table/Column eq 'Value' ). Power Apps : paste the app's web link or ID. Pass context with URL parameters, then read them in the app with Param(\"project\") . Read a parameter passed from the SharePoint page into an embedded canvas app Power Fx Copy // App.OnStart Set(varProjectId, Value (Param( \"projectId\" ))); ClearCollect(colTasks, Filter (Tasks, ProjectId = varProjectId)); # Embed web part & HTML field security The Embed web part accept"},{"t":"Lists & Libraries – Columns, Views, Versioning, Approval and Settings That Matter","c":"sharepoint-online","u":"articles/sharepoint-online/lists-libraries.html","s":"The core data containers of SharePoint, covering column types, views and grouping, versioning and version limits, content approval, check-out, metadata vs folders, rules, and the settings to standardize.","k":"lists, libraries, columns, views, versioning, content approval, metadata","h":"Column types at a glance · Folders vs metadata · Views · Versioning, approval and check-out · Interview questions · Why is metadata preferred over folders? · What is the difference between major/minor versioning and content approval? · Why avoid \"Require check-out\"?","l":"Beginner","x":"Lists, Libraries & Formatting","b":"Everything in SharePoint ultimately lives in a list (rows of data) or a library (a list whose rows are files). Getting their configuration right is what makes search, automation, retention and Copilot work well. # Column types at a glance Column type Stores Notes / gotchas Single line of text ≤ 255 chars Indexable; most common filter column Multiple lines of text Plain, rich or enhanced rich text Cannot be indexed; \"Append changes\" requires versioning Choice One or many fixed values Great for status; formatting colours per value Number / Currency Numeric Set min/max and decimals Date and time Date or date+time Stored UTC; displayed in the site's/user's time zone Person or group Entra ID users/groups Lookup to the hidden User Information List; counts toward the lookup threshold Lookup Value from another list Same site only; counts toward the 12-lookup threshold Yes/No Boolean Use for flags Hyperlink or picture URL + description Picture mode shows images Managed metadata Term(s) from the term store Best for enterprise taxonomy & refiners Calculated Formula over other columns Recomputed on item save only; no [Today] live updates Image Image file Stored in a SiteAssets folder Location Address via Bing Rich location data # Folders vs metadata flowchart LR subgraph Folders F1[/Contracts/2026/Vendor A/] --> F2[Hard to view by vendor<br/>across years] end subgraph Metadata M1[Contracts library] --> M2[Columns: Year, Vendor, Type, Status] M2 --> M3[Views: by vendor, by year,<br/>expiring soon] M2 --> M4[Search refiners,<br/>retention by type,<br/>Copilot grounding] end Use metadata for classification and folders only for permission boundaries, very large volumes, or synced folder structures users really need. # Views Standard list, gallery/tiles , calendar , board (Kanban, in Microsoft Lists), datasheet (\"Edit in grid view\"). Filter, sort, group (2 levels), totals, item limit (paged). Personal vs public views. Default view per library. View formatting for row styling and card layouts. See View formatting . # Versioning, approval and check-out Cheat sheet Library settings decision guide 01 Versioning Major versions for most libraries; major+minor (drafts) when authors need private drafts before publishing. 02 Version limits Tenant/site/library version history limits : automatic (time + count based) or manual (e.g. keep 100 major). 03 Content approval Items/files are p"},{"t":"Content Types & Site Columns – Designing Reusable Metadata (and the Content Type Gallery)","c":"sharepoint-online","u":"articles/sharepoint-online/content-types-columns.html","s":"How content types and site columns model your business documents, the content type ID hierarchy, the modern content type gallery and hub publishing, document templates, and provisioning with PnP and Graph.","k":"content types, site columns, content type hub, metadata, information architecture, pnp","h":"Inheritance and IDs · Where to define them · Designing content types: a worked example · Provisioning with PnP PowerShell · Best practices · Interview questions · What is a content type ID and how is inheritance represented? · What replaced the content type hub in SharePoint Online? · Why should list-level content types not be edited directly?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Lists, Libraries & Formatting","b":"A site column is a reusable column definition (name, type, settings). A content type is a reusable schema for a kind of item or document: its columns, its template, and the policies that apply to it. Content types let one library hold \"Contracts\", \"Invoices\" and \"Policies\", each with the right fields. # Inheritance and IDs Content type inheritance SYSTEM Item (0x01) inherits BUILT-IN Document (0x0101) Event (0x0102) Task (0x0108) Folder (0x0120) inherits YOUR BASE TYPE Contoso Document (0x0101 00 + GUID) Columns: Department Confidentiality inherits SPECIFIC TYPES Contract Invoice Policy SOP Content type inheritance A content type ID encodes its parent: 0x0101 (Document) + 00 + a 32-hex GUID creates a child, for example 0x010100A1B2C3… . Short suffixes like 01 and 02 are used for built-in descendants. This is how SharePoint knows that a Contract is a Document. # Where to define them Location Scope Use Content type gallery (SharePoint admin center) Tenant-wide, published to all sites that use it or on demand Enterprise types (Contract, Policy) Hub site content types Pushed to associated sites Department-wide types Site content types One site collection Local needs List content types Copy of a site type attached to a list Local customizations (avoid diverging) The modern content type hub In SharePoint Online the classic content type hub site has been replaced by the content type gallery in the admin center. Published types are pulled to sites when needed, so you no longer wait for timer jobs. # Designing content types: a worked example Content type Parent Columns Template Retention label Contoso Document Document Department, Confidentiality – – Contract Contoso Document Vendor, ContractValue, StartDate, EndDate, Owner contract.dotx Contracts 7y Invoice Contoso Document Vendor, InvoiceNo, Amount, DueDate – Finance 10y SOP Contoso Document SOPNumber, Version, EffectiveDate, ReviewDate sop.dotx Quality 15y # Provisioning with PnP PowerShell Site columns + content type + add to library PowerShell Copy Connect-PnPOnline -Url https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/legal -Interactive -ClientId $clientId Add-PnPField -DisplayName \"Vendor\" -InternalName \"ContosoVendor\" -Type Text -Group \"Contoso Columns\" -AddToDefaultView Add-PnPField -DisplayName \"Contract value\" -InternalName \"ContosoContractValue\" -Type Currency -Group \"Contoso Columns\" Add-PnPFieldFromXml -FieldXml @\""},{"t":"Column Formatting JSON – Status Badges, Progress Bars, Icons, Buttons & Flows","c":"sharepoint-online","u":"articles/sharepoint-online/column-formatting.html","s":"Practical column formatting recipes with the JSON schema, expressions (Excel-style and AST), @currentField, @me, @now, conditional classes, launching Power Automate flows from a button, and debugging tips.","k":"column formatting, json, formatting, power automate, no-code, lists","h":"How it works · Recipe 1 · Status badge (Choice column) · Recipe 2 · Progress bar (Number 0–100) · Recipe 3 · Overdue due date with icon · Recipe 4 · \"Assigned to me\" highlight (Person column) · Recipe 5 · Button that starts a Power Automate flow · Useful tokens & functions · Applying formats with PowerShell · Interview questions · What is the difference between column formatting and JSLink? · How can a column format start a Power Automate flow? · Can column formatting change the stored value?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Lists, Libraries & Formatting","b":"Column formatting changes how a field is displayed in list views, using declarative JSON instead of code. It is safe (no script runs), fast, and works in SharePoint Online, Microsoft Lists, Teams and SharePoint 2019/SE. It does not change the stored data. # How it works flowchart LR J[JSON format<br/>stored on the column<br/>CustomFormatter property] --> R[List view renderer] D[(\"Item data<br/>@currentField, $Column values<br/>@me, @now\")] --> R R --> H[Safe HTML elements<br/>div, span, a, img, svg, button] The JSON describes an element tree: elmType , txtContent , style , attributes (including class ), children , and customRowAction . Values can be expressions . Expression styles JSON Copy // Excel-style string expression (most readable) \"=if(@currentField == &#x27;Closed&#x27;, &#x27;sp-field-severity--good&#x27;, &#x27;sp-field-severity--warning&#x27;)\" // AST style (older, verbose but equivalent) { \"operator\" : \"?\" , \"operands\" : [ { \"operator\" : \"==\" , \"operands\" : [ \"@currentField\" , \"Closed\" ] } , \"sp-field-severity--good\" , \"sp-field-severity--warning\" ] } # Recipe 1 · Status badge (Choice column) Status pill with colours JSON Copy { \"$schema\" : \"https://developer.microsoft.com/json-schemas/sp/v2/column-formatting.schema.json\" , \"elmType\" : \"div\" , \"style\" : { \"padding\" : \"2px 10px\" , \"border-radius\" : \"12px\" , \"color\" : \"white\" , \"font-weight\" : \"600\" , \"display\" : \"inline-block\" , \"background-color\" : \"=if(@currentField == &#x27;Closed&#x27;, &#x27;#059669&#x27;, if(@currentField == &#x27;In Review&#x27;, &#x27;#d97706&#x27;, if(@currentField == &#x27;Rejected&#x27;, &#x27;#dc2626&#x27;, &#x27;#2563eb&#x27;)))\" } , \"txtContent\" : \"@currentField\" } # Recipe 2 · Progress bar (Number 0–100) Progress bar JSON Copy { \"$schema\" : \"https://developer.microsoft.com/json-schemas/sp/v2/column-formatting.schema.json\" , \"elmType\" : \"div\" , \"style\" : { \"width\" : \"100%\" , \"background-color\" : \"#e5e7eb\" , \"border-radius\" : \"6px\" , \"height\" : \"16px\" , \"position\" : \"relative\" } , \"children\" : [ { \"elmType\" : \"div\" , \"style\" : { \"width\" : \"=@currentField + &#x27;%&#x27;\" , \"height\" : \"100%\" , \"border-radius\" : \"6px\" , \"background-color\" : \"=if(@currentField >= 80, &#x27;#059669&#x27;, if(@currentField >= 40, &#x27;#d97706&#x27;, &#x27;#dc2626&#x27;))\" } } , { \"elmType\" : \"span\" , \"txtContent\" : \"=@currentField + &#x27;%&#x27;\" , \"style\" : { \"position\" : \"absolute\" , "},{"t":"View Formatting JSON – Row Styling, Card Galleries and Grouped Headers","c":"sharepoint-online","u":"articles/sharepoint-online/view-formatting.html","s":"Customize entire list views with additionalRowClass, rowFormatter, tileProps gallery cards and groupProps headers, with complete JSON examples for a priority list, a people directory and a Kanban-like card layout.","k":"view formatting, json, gallery, row formatter, lists, no-code","h":"Example 1 · Highlight urgent rows · Example 2 · People directory cards (gallery view) · Example 3 · Card-style rows with rowFormatter · Example 4 · Custom group headers · Applying view formats in bulk · Interview questions · What is the difference between additionalRowClass and rowFormatter ? · How do you create card layouts for a list?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Lists, Libraries & Formatting","b":"View formatting applies to a whole view instead of one column. It has four hooks: Property What it does View type additionalRowClass Adds a CSS class to each row (keep default rendering) List rowFormatter Replaces each row with your own element tree List tileProps Defines card layout for the gallery (tiles) view Gallery groupProps / hideColumnHeader / hideSelection Custom group headers, hide header row and selection List # Example 1 · Highlight urgent rows additionalRowClass – red background for high-priority open items JSON Copy { \"$schema\" : \"https://developer.microsoft.com/json-schemas/sp/v2/row-formatting.schema.json\" , \"additionalRowClass\" : \"=if([$Priority] == &#x27;High&#x27; && [$Status] != &#x27;Closed&#x27;, &#x27;sp-field-severity--blocked&#x27;, &#x27;&#x27;)\" } # Example 2 · People directory cards (gallery view) 👤 Jane Doe · QA Lead · Plant A 👤 Ravi Kumar · Engineer 👤 Ana Silva · Auditor 👤 Li Wei · Manager Gallery view with tileProps cards tileProps for a gallery view JSON Copy { \"$schema\" : \"https://developer.microsoft.com/json-schemas/sp/v2/tile-formatting.schema.json\" , \"height\" : 160 , \"width\" : 240 , \"hideSelection\" : false , \"formatter\" : { \"elmType\" : \"div\" , \"style\" : { \"display\" : \"flex\" , \"flex-direction\" : \"column\" , \"align-items\" : \"center\" , \"padding\" : \"12px\" , \"border-radius\" : \"12px\" , \"box-shadow\" : \"0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,.12)\" , \"height\" : \"136px\" , \"width\" : \"216px\" } , \"children\" : [ { \"elmType\" : \"img\" , \"attributes\" : { \"src\" : \"=&#x27;/_layouts/15/userphoto.aspx?size=M&accountname=&#x27; + [$Person.email]\" , \"title\" : \"[$Person.title]\" } , \"style\" : { \"width\" : \"56px\" , \"height\" : \"56px\" , \"border-radius\" : \"50%\" } } , { \"elmType\" : \"div\" , \"txtContent\" : \"[$Person.title]\" , \"style\" : { \"font-weight\" : \"600\" , \"margin-top\" : \"8px\" } } , { \"elmType\" : \"div\" , \"txtContent\" : \"[$Role]\" , \"style\" : { \"font-size\" : \"12px\" , \"color\" : \"#64748b\" } } , { \"elmType\" : \"div\" , \"txtContent\" : \"[$Location]\" , \"style\" : { \"font-size\" : \"12px\" } } ] } } # Example 3 · Card-style rows with rowFormatter rowFormatter – compact task cards JSON Copy { \"$schema\" : \"https://developer.microsoft.com/json-schemas/sp/v2/row-formatting.schema.json\" , \"hideSelection\" : true , \"hideColumnHeader\" : true , \"rowFormatter\" : { \"elmType\" : \"div\" , \"attributes\" : { \"class\" : \"ms-bgColor-white ms-borderColor-neutralLight\" } , \"style\" : { \"display\" : \"flex\""},{"t":"List Form Configuration – Header, Body & Footer JSON and Conditional Show/Hide Formulas","c":"sharepoint-online","u":"articles/sharepoint-online/form-formatting.html","s":"Customize the out-of-the-box list form without Power Apps, covering conditional column formulas, header and footer formatting, body sections, and when to choose form formatting vs Power Apps vs an SPFx form customizer.","k":"form formatting, conditional formulas, list forms, json, power apps, form customizer","h":"Conditional show/hide · Header & footer · Body sections · Which form technology should you use? · Interview questions · How do you show a field only when another field has a certain value in a modern list form? · When would you choose an SPFx form customizer over form formatting?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Lists, Libraries & Formatting","b":"The modern list form (the side panel for new and edit) can be tailored without leaving SharePoint: Conditional formulas to show or hide columns. Header and footer formatting (JSON). Body sections : group fields into labelled sections. # Conditional show/hide In the form, choose Edit form → Edit columns → ⋮ → Edit conditional formula . Formulas use Excel-like syntax with [$InternalName] : Conditional formula examples Text Copy =if([$Category] == 'Hardware', 'true', 'false') → show \"Asset tag\" only for hardware =if([$Status] == 'Rejected', 'true', 'false') → show \"Rejection reason\" =if([$Amount] > 10000, 'true', 'false') → show \"CFO approver\" for big amounts =if(@me == [$Requester.email], 'false', 'true') → hide \"Internal notes\" from the requester Hiding is not security A hidden column is still visible in list views, through APIs and in exports. Use separate lists, item permissions or Power Apps with proper data design for confidential fields. # Header & footer Header: icon + dynamic title JSON Copy { \"elmType\" : \"div\" , \"attributes\" : { \"class\" : \"ms-borderColor-neutralTertiary\" } , \"style\" : { \"width\" : \"99%\" , \"border-top-width\" : \"0px\" , \"border-bottom-width\" : \"1px\" , \"border-left-width\" : \"0px\" , \"border-right-width\" : \"0px\" , \"border-style\" : \"solid\" , \"margin-bottom\" : \"16px\" } , \"children\" : [ { \"elmType\" : \"div\" , \"style\" : { \"display\" : \"flex\" , \"box-sizing\" : \"border-box\" , \"align-items\" : \"center\" } , \"children\" : [ { \"elmType\" : \"div\" , \"attributes\" : { \"iconName\" : \"ShieldAlert\" , \"class\" : \"ms-fontSize-42 ms-fontWeight-regular ms-fontColor-themePrimary\" , \"title\" : \"Deviation\" } , \"style\" : { \"flex\" : \"none\" , \"padding\" : \"0px\" , \"padding-left\" : \"0px\" , \"height\" : \"36px\" } } ] } , { \"elmType\" : \"div\" , \"attributes\" : { \"class\" : \"ms-fontColor-neutralSecondary ms-fontWeight-bold ms-fontSize-24\" } , \"style\" : { \"box-sizing\" : \"border-box\" , \"width\" : \"100%\" , \"text-align\" : \"left\" , \"padding\" : \"21px 12px\" , \"overflow\" : \"hidden\" } , \"children\" : [ { \"elmType\" : \"div\" , \"txtContent\" : \"=if([$Title] == &#x27;&#x27;, &#x27;New deviation&#x27;, &#x27;Deviation: &#x27; + [$Title])\" } ] } ] } Footer: help link JSON Copy { \"elmType\" : \"div\" , \"style\" : { \"width\" : \"100%\" , \"text-align\" : \"left\" , \"overflow\" : \"hidden\" , \"border-top-width\" : \"1px\" } , \"children\" : [ { \"elmType\" : \"a\" , \"txtContent\" : \"How to classify a deviation (SOP-QA-014)\" , \"attrib"},{"t":"Large Lists in SharePoint Online – The 5,000 Threshold, Indexes and Query Patterns","c":"sharepoint-online","u":"articles/sharepoint-online/large-lists-threshold.html","s":"What the list view threshold means in SharePoint Online, automatic and manual indexing, modern view behaviour, and safe patterns for REST, Graph, Power Automate, Power Apps and PnPjs against lists with millions of items.","k":"large lists, list view threshold, indexing, pagination, odata, performance","h":"What SharePoint Online does for you · Safe query patterns per technology · Design patterns for very large data · Interview questions · Can you increase the 5,000 list view threshold in SharePoint Online? · A Power Automate \"Get items\" returns only 100 items from a 20,000-item list. Why? · What is the maximum number of indexed columns per list?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Lists, Libraries & Formatting","b":"SharePoint Online lists can hold 30 million items, but any single query that must scan more than 5,000 items without an index fails with \"The attempted operation is prohibited because it exceeds the list view threshold\" . Unlike on-premises, the threshold cannot be changed in SharePoint Online. # What SharePoint Online does for you Cheat sheet Built-in help for large lists 01 Automatic indexing Modern views can auto-create indexes for columns used in sort/filter when the list is under a size limit (indexes can be added on lists up to 20,000 items through the UI). 02 Modern view fallback When a view would break the threshold, modern UI shows the first items and prompts to filter on indexed columns. 03 Up to 20 indexes Per list. Index the columns you filter or sort by most. 04 Files: folders help Library views scoped to a folder only scan that folder's items. 05 Search-driven roll-ups Highlighted content and search are not bound by the threshold. # Safe query patterns per technology Technology Pattern REST $filter on an indexed column first; $top ≤ 5000; follow odata.nextLink Graph /items?$filter=fields/Status eq 'Open' with indexed column, $top , @odata.nextLink ; header Prefer: HonorNonIndexedQueriesWarningMayFailRandomly only for small lists CSOM CamlQuery with RowLimit Paged=TRUE + ListItemCollectionPosition ; OrderBy ID PnPjs items.top(5000) + async iteration over pages, or getItemsByCAMLQuery with paging Power Automate Get items with Filter Query on indexed columns, Top Count , and Pagination threshold (settings) Power Apps Delegable Filter on indexed columns; delegation limit (500–2,000) for non-delegable functions PnPjs: iterate all items of a huge list safely TypeScript Copy import { spfi } from \"@pnp/sp\" ; import \"@pnp/sp/webs\" ; import \"@pnp/sp/lists\" ; import \"@pnp/sp/items\" ; const sp = spfi ( /* SPFx context or Node config */ ); const all : any [] = []; // async iterator pages through results (default page size 2000 here via top) for await ( const page of sp. web . lists . getByTitle ( \"Deviations\" ). items . select ( \"Id\" , \"Title\" , \"Status\" ) . filter ( \"Status eq &#x27;Open&#x27;\" ) // Status must be indexed! . top ( 2000 )) { all. push (...page); } console . log (all. length ); REST: indexed filter + paging HTTP Copy GET /_api/web/lists/getbytitle(&#x27;Deviations&#x27;)/items?$select=Id,Title,Status&$filter=Status eq &#x27;Open&#x27;&$top=5"},{"t":"Microsoft Lists – Templates, Rules, Board Views, Comments and Teams Integration","c":"sharepoint-online","u":"articles/sharepoint-online/microsoft-lists.html","s":"The modern Lists app explained, covering where lists live, ready-made templates, rules (the alerts successor), board/calendar/gallery views, comments, forms, Teams integration and when Lists is (not) enough.","k":"microsoft lists, rules, alerts, board view, teams, templates","h":"What Lists adds on top of classic lists · Where your list lives matters · Rules vs alerts vs Power Automate · Interview questions · Where is a list stored when created from the Lists app under \"My lists\"? · What replaced SharePoint alerts for most scenarios?","l":"Beginner","x":"Lists, Libraries & Formatting","b":"Microsoft Lists (launched in 2020) is a modern front end for SharePoint lists, available as a Microsoft 365 app, in Teams and on mobile. Every list is still a SharePoint list: either in a SharePoint site or, for \"My lists\", in the creator's OneDrive . # What Lists adds on top of classic lists Cheat sheet Microsoft Lists capabilities 01 Templates Issue tracker, Asset manager, Employee onboarding, Event itinerary, Content scheduler, Work progress tracker, Recruitment tracker, Travel requests… 02 Views List, Board (Kanban grouped by choice), Calendar , Gallery cards. 03 Rules \"When Status changes to Closed, send email to Owner\" with no flow needed; successor of classic alerts for many uses. 04 Comments Item-level comments with @mentions. 05 Forms Share a Lists form to collect new items from people who don't need to see the list. 06 Teams Add as a tab; collaborate in a channel with conversations. 07 Formatting Column, view and form formatting with the same JSON as SharePoint. 08 Sync & offline Edit lists offline in the Lists app (Windows / mobile). # Where your list lives matters flowchart LR C[Create a list] --> Q{Save to?} Q -->|My lists| OD[User's OneDrive<br/>personal ownership<br/>⚠ deleted with the user] Q -->|A SharePoint site / Team| SP[Team site<br/>shared ownership<br/>✅ governed, retained] Business lists belong in team sites Lists saved to My lists live in the creator's OneDrive. When the user leaves, the list follows OneDrive retention and deletion. Put shared business data in a team site owned by a group. # Rules vs alerts vs Power Automate Rules Classic alerts Power Automate Setup List → Automate → Create a rule Item/list \"Alert me\" Flow designer Conditions Column changes, item created/deleted Any change, filters Unlimited logic Actions Notify people, set a value Email/SMS digest Anything (approvals, integrations) Status Current Being retired in SPO (2025–2026) Current # Interview questions Q1 Where is a list stored when created from the Lists app under \"My lists\"? In the creator's OneDrive (a personal site). It is shared like a OneDrive file and is subject to the user's lifecycle, so business-critical lists should be created in team sites. Q2 What replaced SharePoint alerts for most scenarios? Rules in Microsoft Lists and libraries for simple notifications, and Power Automate flows for anything that needs conditions or workflow logic. Microsoft ha"},{"t":"Site Templates (Site Designs) & Site Scripts – Provision Sites with JSON","c":"sharepoint-online","u":"articles/sharepoint-online/site-templates-scripts.html","s":"How site scripts and site templates (formerly site designs) automate site setup, covering the JSON action schema, lists, columns, themes, hub association, triggering Power Automate, scoping templates to groups and applying them with PowerShell.","k":"site templates, site designs, site scripts, provisioning, json, power automate","h":"How it fits together · A complete site script · Registering and applying with PowerShell · Site scripts vs PnP provisioning · Interview questions · What is the difference between a site script and a site template? · How do you run provisioning steps that site scripts can't do?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Provisioning & Automation","b":"Site templates (called site designs until 2021) let users create a site that is already configured: lists, columns, theme, navigation, hub association and even a Power Automate flow for anything else. Each template is a bundle of one or more site scripts , which are JSON files describing actions . # How it fits together Site template provisioning SITE SCRIPT (JSON) createSPList addSiteColumn applyTheme joinHubSite setRegionalSetti… triggerFlow one or more scripts bundled into SITE TEMPLATE Title & thumbnail WebTemplate 64 (team) / 68 (comm) Scoped to groups (who can see it) applied when TRIGGER User creates a site Admin applies to existing site Hub association (hub's template) PowerShell / REST calls (optional) POWER AUTOMATE + AZURE HTTP trigger flow Azure Function / PnP for advanced setup Site template provisioning # A complete site script deviation-site.json JSON Copy { \"$schema\" : \"https://developer.microsoft.com/json-schemas/sp/site-design-script-actions.schema.json\" , \"actions\" : [ { \"verb\" : \"applyTheme\" , \"themeName\" : \"Contoso Quality\" } , { \"verb\" : \"setRegionalSettings\" , \"timeZone\" : 23 , \"locale\" : 16393 , \"sortOrder\" : 25 , \"hourFormat\" : \"24\" } , { \"verb\" : \"createSiteColumn\" , \"fieldType\" : \"Choice\" , \"internalName\" : \"ContosoSeverity\" , \"displayName\" : \"Severity\" , \"isRequired\" : true , \"group\" : \"Contoso Quality\" , \"choices\" : [ \"Minor\" , \"Major\" , \"Critical\" ] } , { \"verb\" : \"createSPList\" , \"listName\" : \"Deviations\" , \"templateType\" : 100 , \"subactions\" : [ { \"verb\" : \"setDescription\" , \"description\" : \"Quality deviations for this plant\" } , { \"verb\" : \"addSiteColumn\" , \"internalName\" : \"ContosoSeverity\" , \"addToDefaultView\" : true } , { \"verb\" : \"addSPField\" , \"fieldType\" : \"DateTime\" , \"displayName\" : \"Detected on\" , \"internalName\" : \"DetectedOn\" , \"isRequired\" : true , \"addToDefaultView\" : true } , { \"verb\" : \"addSPField\" , \"fieldType\" : \"User\" , \"displayName\" : \"Owner\" , \"internalName\" : \"Owner\" , \"addToDefaultView\" : true } , { \"verb\" : \"addSPView\" , \"name\" : \"Open critical\" , \"viewFields\" : [ \"LinkTitle\" , \"ContosoSeverity\" , \"Owner\" , \"DetectedOn\" ] , \"query\" : \"<Where><Eq><FieldRef Name=&#x27;ContosoSeverity&#x27;/><Value Type=&#x27;Choice&#x27;>Critical</Value></Eq></Where>\" , \"rowLimit\" : 100 } ] } , { \"verb\" : \"addNavLink\" , \"url\" : \"/Lists/Deviations\" , \"displayName\" : \"Deviations\" , \"isWebRelative\" : true } , { \"verb\" : \"join"},{"t":"PnP Provisioning Templates – Export, Edit and Apply Complete Site Blueprints","c":"sharepoint-online","u":"articles/sharepoint-online/pnp-provisioning.html","s":"How the PnP provisioning engine captures and applies sites (lists, content types, pages, navigation, security, files, term sets), template anatomy, tokens, tenant templates, and running provisioning from Azure Functions or Azure Automation.","k":"pnp, provisioning, templates, pnp powershell, automation, azure functions","h":"Lifecycle · What a template can contain · Exporting and applying · Template anatomy (trimmed) · Running provisioning unattended · Interview questions · How is PnP provisioning different from site scripts? · What are provisioning tokens and why do they matter?","l":"Advanced","x":"Provisioning & Automation","b":"The PnP provisioning engine (part of PnP Framework and PnP PowerShell) is the community's answer to \"make every new site look exactly like this one\". It can export a configured site to a template (XML or .pnp package) and apply it to other sites, idempotently. # Lifecycle flowchart LR G[Golden site<br/>configured by hand] -->|Get-PnPSiteTemplate| T[template.xml / .pnp] T -->|edit: remove noise,<br/>add tokens| T2[Clean template<br/>in Git] T2 -->|Invoke-PnPSiteTemplate| S1[New site A] T2 -->|Invoke-PnPSiteTemplate| S2[New site B] T2 -->|CI/CD pipeline| S3[Test → Prod] # What a template can contain Cheat sheet Template handlers 01 Site columns & content types Including choices, formulas, managed metadata bindings. 02 Lists & libraries Settings, views (incl. formatting), fields, data rows, folders. 03 Pages Modern client-side pages with sections and web parts (incl. SPFx with properties). 04 Navigation Quick launch and top navigation, hub navigation. 05 Files Upload assets (images, templates) to libraries. 06 Security Site groups, role definitions, membership, breaking inheritance. 07 Term groups Term sets and terms (with IDs). 08 Features & settings Site features, regional settings, theme, header/footer, property bag. 09 Tenant scope Sequences of sites, apps (sppkg), themes, site scripts (tenant templates). # Exporting and applying Capture a golden site PowerShell Copy Connect-PnPOnline -Url https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/golden -quality -Interactive -ClientId $clientId Get-PnPSiteTemplate -Out \".\\quality-site.xml\" ` -Handlers Lists, ContentTypes, Fields, Pages, Navigation, Theme, SiteSecurity ` -IncludeAllPages -PersistBrandingFiles -ExcludeHandlers Workflows Apply to a new site with parameters PowerShell Copy Connect-PnPOnline -Url https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/plant -b-quality -Interactive -ClientId $clientId Invoke-PnPSiteTemplate -Path \".\\quality-site.xml\" -Parameters @ { \"PlantName\" = \"Plant B\" ; \"QualityLead\" = \"ravi@contoso.com\" } # Template anatomy (trimmed) quality-site.xml XML Copy < pnp:Provisioning xmlns:pnp = \"http://schemas.dev.office.com/PnP/2022/09/ProvisioningSchema\" > < pnp:Preferences Generator = \"PnP.Framework\" > < pnp:Parameters > < pnp:Parameter Key = \"PlantName\" > Plant A </ pnp:Parameter > </ pnp:Parameters > </ pnp:Preferences > < pnp:Templates ID = \"CONTAINER-QUALITY\" > < pnp:ProvisioningTemplate ID = \"QUALITY\" Version "},{"t":"PnP PowerShell Essentials – Connect, Query, Bulk-Update and Report (2026 Edition)","c":"sharepoint-online","u":"articles/sharepoint-online/pnp-powershell.html","s":"Practical PnP PowerShell for SharePoint Online, covering installing on PowerShell 7, registering your own Entra app (required since 2024), interactive and certificate connections, and 15 copy-ready scripts for lists, files, permissions, sites and reporting.","k":"pnp powershell, powershell, automation, scripts, entra id, administration","h":"Setup (2026) · Connection options · 15 scripts you will reuse · PnP PowerShell vs SPO Management Shell vs CLI for Microsoft 365 · Interview questions · What changed for PnP PowerShell authentication in 2024? · How do you speed up adding thousands of items with PnP PowerShell? · How would you run PnP PowerShell unattended in Azure?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Provisioning & Automation","b":"PnP PowerShell is the open-source, cross-platform module with 700+ cmdlets for SharePoint Online, Teams, Planner, Entra ID and more. It is maintained by the Microsoft 365 PnP community and is the most productive way to script SharePoint. # Setup (2026) Install on PowerShell 7.4+ PowerShell Copy Install-Module PnP.PowerShell -Scope CurrentUser Import-Module PnP.PowerShell Get-Module PnP.PowerShell -ListAvailable | Select-Object Name, Version Bring your own Entra app Since September 2024 the shared multi-tenant \"PnP Management Shell\" app was removed. You must register your own Entra ID app for PnP PowerShell and pass its client ID when connecting. One-time: register an app for interactive use PowerShell Copy Register-PnPEntraIDAppForInteractiveLogin -ApplicationName \"PnP PowerShell - Contoso\" -Tenant contoso.onmicrosoft.com -Interactive # Note the returned Client ID; store it in $env:PNP_CLIENTID or your profile # Connection options flowchart TD A{Who runs the script?} -->|You, interactively| I[\"Connect-PnPOnline -Interactive -ClientId $id\"] A -->|Unattended job| C[\"Connect-PnPOnline -ClientId $id -Tenant contoso.onmicrosoft.com<br/>-CertificatePath cert.pfx (app-only)\"] A -->|Azure Automation / Function<br/>with managed identity| M[\"Connect-PnPOnline -ManagedIdentity\"] A -->|Device without browser| D[\"Connect-PnPOnline -DeviceLogin -ClientId $id\"] Connect PowerShell Copy $clientId = $env:PNP_CLIENTID Connect-PnPOnline -Url \"https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/quality\" -Interactive -ClientId $clientId # App-only with certificate (register with Register-PnPEntraIDApp, grant Sites.FullControl.All or Sites.Selected) Connect-PnPOnline -Url \"https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/quality\" -ClientId $clientId ` -Tenant \"contoso.onmicrosoft.com\" -CertificatePath \".\\pnp-app.pfx\" -CertificatePassword ( Read-Host -AsSecureString ) # 15 scripts you will reuse 1–5: lists and items PowerShell Copy # 1. List all lists with item counts (excluding hidden) Get-PnPList | Where-Object Hidden -eq $false | Select-Object Title, ItemCount, LastItemUserModifiedDate # 2. Export a list to CSV (paged, all items) Get-PnPListItem -List \"Deviations\" -PageSize 2000 -Fields \"Title\" , \"Status\" , \"Owner\" | ForEach-Object { [ pscustomobject ] @ { Id = $_ .Id; Title = $_ [ \"Title\" ]; Status = $_ [ \"Status\" ]; Owner = $_ [ \"Owner\" ].Email } } | Export-Csv .\\deviations.csv -NoTypeInformation # 3. B"},{"t":"Permissions & Sharing – Groups, Permission Levels, Sharing Links and Oversharing Control","c":"sharepoint-online","u":"articles/sharepoint-online/permissions-sharing.html","s":"How SharePoint Online permissions really work, covering site groups, Microsoft 365 group membership, permission levels, inheritance, sharing link types, external sharing settings at tenant and site level, and how to audit and fix oversharing.","k":"permissions, sharing, external sharing, security, oversharing, guest access","h":"The permission model · Inheritance · Sharing links · Checking who has access · Oversharing and Copilot · Interview questions · What is the difference between \"People in your organization\" and \"Specific people\" links? · How do Microsoft 365 group members get access to a team site? · Why is breaking inheritance on many items a problem? · What tools help you find oversharing before rolling out Copilot?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Security, Governance & Admin","b":"Permissions answer who can do what with which content . In SharePoint Online they come from three sources at once: SharePoint groups , the Microsoft 365 group (for group-connected sites), and sharing links . Understanding all three is the key to preventing oversharing, which matters more than ever now that Copilot can find anything a user has access to. # The permission model How access is granted IDENTITIES Users (members & guests) Entra security groups Microsoft 365 groups Everyone except external users added to SHAREPOINT GROUPS Site Owners Site Members Site Visitors Custom groups assigned PERMISSION LEVELS Full Control Edit Contribute Read View Only Custom levels on securable objects SCOPES Site List / library Folder Item / file plus direct grants via SHARING LINKS Anyone People in your organization People with existing access Specific people How access is granted Default group Group-connected team site Communication site Permission level Owners M365 group owners Added directly Full Control Members M365 group members Added directly Edit Visitors (added directly) Added directly Read # Inheritance By default everything inherits from its parent: site → library → folder → file. Breaking inheritance creates a unique permission scope. Each unique scope: adds complexity users cannot see, slows permission checks and search, counts toward limits (50,000 unique scopes per list; keep it far lower). Golden rules Grant to groups , not individuals. Break inheritance at site or library level, not on thousands of items. Use separate sites for genuinely different audiences. Document every exception. # Sharing links Link type Who can open Risk Anyone Anyone with the link, no sign-in Highest: forwardable, anonymous People in your organization Any signed-in employee with the link Medium: discoverable by Copilot/search once used People with existing access Only those who already have permission None: just a URL Specific people Named users (internal or guests) Lowest: explicit grant Sharing settings are configured at tenant level (the maximum) and can be more restrictive per site : Tenant and site sharing settings PowerShell Copy Connect-SPOService -Url https://contoso -admin .sharepoint.com # Tenant: allow guests (new and existing) but no anonymous links; default link = specific people Set-SPOTenant -SharingCapability ExternalUserSharingOnly ` -DefaultSharingLinkType Direct "},{"t":"Governance & Compliance – Retention, Sensitivity Labels, DLP and Site Lifecycle","c":"sharepoint-online","u":"articles/sharepoint-online/governance-compliance.html","s":"How Microsoft Purview protects SharePoint content, covering retention policies vs labels, preservation hold library, sensitivity labels for files and sites, DLP, eDiscovery, audit, and site lifecycle policies with SharePoint Advanced Management.","k":"governance, compliance, retention, sensitivity labels, dlp, purview, lifecycle","h":"The protection stack · Retention: policies vs labels · Sensitivity labels at two levels · DLP for SharePoint · Site lifecycle · A governance plan in one page · Interview questions · What is the Preservation Hold library? · How does a sensitivity label on a site differ from one on a file? · How would you stop users sharing credit card data externally from SharePoint?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Security, Governance & Admin","b":"Governance is policies + people + tools . For SharePoint the tools come mainly from Microsoft Purview (retention, labels, DLP, eDiscovery, audit) and SharePoint Advanced Management (lifecycle, access control). # The protection stack Information protection for SharePoint content CLASSIFY Sensitivity labels (Public → Highly Confidential) Auto-labeling (trainable classifiers, SITs) Content types & metadata protect PROTECT Encryption & usage rights… Site/container labels: privacy guest access unmanaged devices DLP policies (block sharing,… Conditional Access retain RETAIN & DISPOSE Retention policies (site-wide) Retention labels (item-level, records) Preservation Hold library Disposition reviews monitor AUDIT & DISCOVER Unified audit log eDiscovery (Standard/Premium) Activity explorer Data access governance reports Information protection for SharePoint content # Retention: policies vs labels Retention policy Retention label Scope Whole sites / locations Individual files and items Applied by Admin (static or adaptive scopes) Users, defaults on libraries, auto-apply rules Records management ❌ ✅ declare as record / regulatory record Disposition review ❌ ✅ Typical use \"Keep everything in Teams sites 3 years\" \"Contracts: keep 7 years after end date, then review\" When a retained file is edited or deleted, the original is kept in the site's Preservation Hold library until the retention period ends. Retention blocks deletion of sites A site under a retention policy cannot be fully deleted while retained content exists. Migration and cleanup projects must check retention first, or deletions silently fill the Preservation Hold library and storage. # Sensitivity labels at two levels flowchart LR L[Sensitivity label<br/>'Confidential – Finance'] --> F[On files & emails<br/>encryption, watermark,<br/>who can open] L --> S[On sites, groups, Teams<br/>privacy: private<br/>guests: blocked<br/>unmanaged devices: web-only] Enable sensitivity labels for SharePoint and apply a site label PowerShell Copy # Allow labels in Office for the web / co-authoring for encrypted files Set-SPOTenant -EnableAIPIntegration $true # Apply a container label to a site (label GUID from Purview) Set-SPOSite -Identity https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/finance -SensitivityLabel \"4f9b1e2a-7c6d-4e5f-9a0b-1c2d3e4f5a6b\" # DLP for SharePoint DLP policies detect sensitive information types (credit cards, A"},{"t":"SharePoint Admin Center & Tenant Settings – What to Configure on Day One","c":"sharepoint-online","u":"articles/sharepoint-online/admin-center-tenant-settings.html","s":"A guided tour of the SharePoint admin center, the tenant settings that matter (sharing, site creation, storage, access control, versioning, scripts, notifications), and the PowerShell equivalents with a day-one baseline script.","k":"admin center, tenant settings, set-spotenant, administration, baseline, custom script","h":"Admin center map · Day-one baseline · Site-level settings you will change often · Interview questions · What does Set-SPOTenant -ConditionalAccessPolicy AllowLimitedAccess do? · Why should custom script remain disabled?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Security, Governance & Admin","b":"The SharePoint admin center ( https://<tenant>-admin.sharepoint.com ) is where tenant-wide SharePoint and OneDrive behaviour is configured. Everything there has a PowerShell equivalent, mostly in Set-SPOTenant , which is better for documentation and repeatability. # Admin center map Cheat sheet SharePoint admin center sections 01 Sites Active sites, deleted sites, site details (owners, hub, sharing, storage), bulk actions. 02 Policies Sharing, access control (unmanaged devices, idle session sign-out, network location). 03 Settings Site creation, notifications, pages, site storage limits, version history limits, OneDrive defaults. 04 Content services Term store, content type gallery. 05 Migration Migration Manager for file shares, Box, Google, Dropbox. 06 Advanced management Data access governance, site lifecycle, restricted access (with SAM licence). 07 Reports Data access governance, usage. 08 More features Classic features: search, apps, BCS, Secure Store, user profiles, records management. # Day-one baseline Tenant baseline with SPO Management Shell PowerShell Copy Connect-SPOService -Url https://contoso -admin .sharepoint.com # Sharing: guests yes, anonymous no; default link = specific people, view Set-SPOTenant -SharingCapability ExternalUserSharingOnly -DefaultSharingLinkType Direct -DefaultLinkPermission View Set-SPOTenant -ExternalUserExpirationRequired $true -ExternalUserExpireInDays 90 # Where users land when a site is locked with LockState NoAccess (e.g. after migration) Set-SPOTenant -NoAccessRedirectUrl \"https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/help/SitePages/site-moved.aspx\" # Site creation: team sites under /teams/, default time zone Set-SPOTenant -DefaultTimezone \"(UTC+05:30) Chennai, Kolkata, Mumbai, New Delhi\" # Unmanaged devices: browser-only access (requires Entra Conditional Access integration) Set-SPOTenant -ConditionalAccessPolicy AllowLimitedAccess # Idle session sign-out for browsers Set-SPOBrowserIdleSignOut -Enabled $true -WarnAfter ( New-TimeSpan -Minutes 50 ) -SignOutAfter ( New-TimeSpan -Minutes 60 ) # Version history limits: automatic (Microsoft-optimized) for new libraries Set-SPOTenant -EnableAutoExpirationVersionTrim $true # Show the current state for documentation Get-SPOTenant | Select-Object SharingCapability, DefaultSharingLinkType, ConditionalAccessPolicy, EnableAutoExpirationVersionTrim | Format-List Custom script \"Custom "},{"t":"Search in SharePoint Online – Microsoft Search, Managed Properties, KQL & PnP Modern Search","c":"sharepoint-online","u":"articles/sharepoint-online/search-managed-properties.html","s":"How search works in Microsoft 365, covering Microsoft Search verticals, bookmarks and acronyms, the search schema (crawled vs managed properties, RefinableStrings), reindexing, KQL, the Search REST and Graph APIs, and PnP Modern Search web parts.","k":"search, microsoft search, managed properties, kql, pnp modern search, refiners","h":"The search flow · Managed property flags · KQL patterns · Microsoft Search admin features · Calling search from code · PnP Modern Search · Interview questions · Why can't you create a new refinable managed property in SharePoint Online? · A new column doesn't appear as a refiner. What do you check? · What are Microsoft Graph connectors?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Security, Governance & Admin","b":"Search in Microsoft 365 is powered by Microsoft Search : one index shared by SharePoint, OneDrive, Teams files and Graph connectors, which also grounds Copilot. Your job as a SharePoint professional is to make content findable : good metadata, a well-mapped schema and search experiences tuned for your users. # The search flow From column to refiner LIST COLUMN Department (site column) Value: \"Quality\" crawl CRAWLED PROPERTY ows_Department / ows_q_TEXT_Department mapped in search schema MANAGED PROPERTY RefinableString01 (refinable, queryable,… used in EXPERIENCES KQL: RefinableString01:\"Qu… Refiners in PnP Modern Search Highlighted content filters Copilot & Graph search API From column to refiner # Managed property flags Flag Enables Searchable Full-text match on the value Queryable Property:value in KQL Retrievable Return the value in results Refinable Use as a refiner/facet Sortable Sort results by it You cannot create new refinable managed properties in SharePoint Online. Instead, reuse the pre-created pool ( RefinableString00–199 , RefinableDate00–19 , RefinableInt00–49 , RefinableDecimal00–09 , RefinableDouble00–09 ) and map your crawled properties to them. Map at tenant level (admin center → More features → Search → Manage search schema) for consistency across sites. Remember to reindex Mapping changes apply to newly crawled content. Request a reindex of the list or library (List settings → Advanced → Reindex) or the site (Site settings → Search and offline availability → Reindex site). # KQL patterns KQL cheat sheet KQL Copy \"vendor agreement\" AND FileExtension:docx Author:\"Jane Doe\" LastModifiedTime>2026-01-01 ContentType:Contract RefinableString01:Quality path:\"https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/legal\" IsDocument:true Title:SOP* NOT Title:draft SiteTitle:\"Plant A\" (ContentClass:STS_ListItem_DocumentLibrary OR ContentClass:STS_ListItem) # Microsoft Search admin features Bookmarks : promoted results for queries (\"payslip\" → payroll portal). Q&As and Acronyms : answers in search results. Verticals and result types : custom tabs (e.g. \"Policies\") with Adaptive Card layouts. Graph connectors : index external systems (ServiceNow, Confluence, file shares, databases) into the same index. # Calling search from code Microsoft Graph Search API TypeScript Copy const body = { requests : [{ entityTypes : [ \"listItem\" ], query : { queryString : \"ContentType:Cont"},{"t":"The App Catalog & the 2026 Add-in Retirement – Deploying Apps the Modern Way","c":"sharepoint-online","u":"articles/sharepoint-online/app-catalog-addin-retirement.html","s":"Tenant and site collection app catalogs, how SPFx packages are deployed and approved, API access approvals, what the SharePoint Add-in and Azure ACS retirement (April 2026) changed, and how to clean up legacy app principals.","k":"app catalog, spfx, add-ins, acs, retirement, deployment, api access","h":"Tenant vs site collection app catalogs · Deploying an SPFx package · What the 2026 retirement changed · Interview questions · What is the difference between deploying an SPFx package tenant-wide and per site? · Where are SPFx API permission requests approved? · What replaced ACS app-only access for background jobs in 2026?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Security, Governance & Admin","b":"The app catalog is a special site where solution packages are uploaded and made available. Since the retirement of SharePoint Add-ins in SharePoint Online (2 April 2026), it is primarily the home of SPFx packages ( .sppkg ) and Teams/Viva apps built with SPFx . # Tenant vs site collection app catalogs App catalogs in SharePoint Online TENANT APP CATALOG https://contoso.share… Apps for SharePoint library (.sppkg) API access requests (via admin center) Tenant-wide deployment option available to ALL SITES Add an app from \"My apps\" Tenant-scoped extensions Teams / Viva via Sync to Teams optional SITE COLLECTION APP CATALOG Per-site Apps for SharePoint library Isolated testing or site-specific apps Enabled by tenant admin App catalogs in SharePoint Online Tenant catalog Site collection catalog Who manages SharePoint/global admins (app catalog owners) Site collection admins (after enablement) Scope Whole tenant Only that site collection Tenant-wide deployment ( skipFeatureDeployment ) ✅ ❌ API permission requests ✅ (approved in admin center) ❌ (must be in tenant catalog) Use for Production apps Pilots, isolated or site-specific solutions Create / enable app catalogs PowerShell Copy # Tenant app catalog (if none exists) Connect-SPOService -Url https://contoso -admin .sharepoint.com Register-SPOAppCatalogSite -Url https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/appcatalog -Owner admin@contoso.com -TimeZoneId 23 # Site collection app catalog Add-SPOSiteCollectionAppCatalog -Site https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/pilot # Deploying an SPFx package sequenceDiagram autonumber participant Dev as Developer / pipeline participant AC as App catalog participant Adm as SharePoint admin participant Site as Site Dev->>AC: Upload solution.sppkg AC-->>Adm: Trust dialog: deploy? (tenant-wide?) Adm->>AC: Deploy (enable) AC-->>Adm: API access requests pending (webApiPermissionRequests) Adm->>Adm: Approve in admin center → API access Site->>AC: Site owner adds the app (unless tenant-wide) Site-->>Site: Web parts available in toolbox Automate with PnP PowerShell PowerShell Copy Connect-PnPOnline -Url https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/appcatalog -Interactive -ClientId $clientId Add-PnPApp -Path .\\deviation -webparts .sppkg -Scope Tenant -Overwrite -Publish -SkipFeatureDeployment # Approve pending API permission requests Get-PnPTenantServicePrincipalPermissionRequests | ForEach-Object { Approv"},{"t":"SharePoint Embedded – Headless Document Storage for Your Own Apps","c":"sharepoint-online","u":"articles/sharepoint-online/sharepoint-embedded.html","s":"What SharePoint Embedded is, covering container types and containers, the owning app model, Graph APIs for files, permissions, billing, Copilot and Purview integration, and when to use it instead of a normal SharePoint site.","k":"sharepoint embedded, containers, graph api, isv, document management, headless","h":"Architecture · Key concepts · When to use SPE vs a SharePoint site · Interview questions · How is SharePoint Embedded different from using Graph against a normal document library? · How do you access files in an SPE container?","l":"Advanced","x":"Platform Features","b":"SharePoint Embedded (SPE) gives developers SharePoint's document engine without SharePoint sites : no UI, no site, no navigation, just containers of files accessed through Microsoft Graph . Your application owns the experience. SharePoint provides storage, versioning, co-authoring, search, Purview compliance and Copilot readiness inside the customer's tenant. # Architecture SharePoint Embedded architecture YOUR APPLICATION Web / mobile / desktop UI Entra app (owning application) Business logic Microsoft Graph (/storage/fileStorage/containers, /drives) CONTAINER TYPE Defined by the owning app Billing configuration (standard / pass-through) One per app (typically) instantiates CONTAINERS (IN THE CONSUMING TENANT) Container = a drive (like a library) Files & folders versions Container permissions (owner, manager,… governed by tenant MICROSOFT 365 PLATFORM Purview: retention DLP eDiscovery Office co-authoring Search & Copilot (where enabled) SharePoint Embedded architecture # Key concepts Concept Meaning Container type Template owned by one Entra app; defines billing and behaviour Container An instance (e.g. one per matter, case or customer project). Exposed as a drive in Graph Owning application The app that created the container type; controls access Consuming tenant The customer tenant where containers and data live Permissions Container-level roles: Owner, Manager, Writer, Reader Graph: create a container and upload a file HTTP Copy POST https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0/storage/fileStorage/containers Content-Type : application/json { \"displayName\": \"Matter 2026-0142\", \"description\": \"Contract dispute – Vendor A\", \"containerTypeId\": \"4a9c1e2b-3d4f-4a5b-8c6d-7e8f9a0b1c2d\" } → 201 { \"id\": \"b!Zx…containerId\", ... } PUT https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0/drives/{containerId}/root:/evidence/email-thread.pdf:/content Content-Type : application/pdf <binary> # When to use SPE vs a SharePoint site flowchart TD A{Do users need a SharePoint<br/>site UI, pages, lists?} -->|yes| S[Normal SharePoint site] A -->|no, my app is the UI| B{Must data stay in the<br/>customer's M365 tenant<br/>with Purview compliance?} B -->|yes| E[SharePoint Embedded ✅] B -->|no| C[Blob storage / your own DB] Typical scenarios: legal matter management, case files, ISV document features, line-of-business apps (claims, HR cases) and Loop/Designer-style experiences (Microsoft itself uses SPE for some"},{"t":"Viva Connections – Dashboard, Feed and Resources on Top of Your Intranet","c":"sharepoint-online","u":"articles/sharepoint-online/viva-connections.html","s":"How Viva Connections brings the SharePoint intranet into Teams and mobile, covering the home site, dashboard cards (Adaptive Card Extensions), feed, resources, audience targeting, and building custom cards with SPFx.","k":"viva connections, dashboard, adaptive card extensions, home site, teams, intranet","h":"The three building blocks · Built-in card types · Setup outline · Interview questions · What is a Viva Connections dashboard card technically? · What does Viva Connections reuse from SharePoint?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Platform Features","b":"Viva Connections is the employee-experience front door in Microsoft Teams (desktop and mobile) and the browser. It reuses your SharePoint home site and navigation and adds a personalized dashboard , feed and resources . # The three building blocks Viva Connections experience DASHBOARD Cards (ACEs): approvals tasks shifts links Power Apps Audience-targeted Mobile-first next to FEED News from org news sites & followed sites Viva Engage posts Boosted news and RESOURCES Global navigation links From the SharePoint app bar / home site powered by SHAREPOINT Home site / Viva Connections home Dashboard page (Dashboard for Viva Connections) SPFx Adaptive Card Extensions Viva Connections experience # Built-in card types Cheat sheet Dashboard card options 01 Approvals / Tasks / Shifts Integrations with Microsoft 365 apps (Approvals, Planner/To Do tasks, Shifts). 02 Card designer No-code card with title, image, link or Quick View built from JSON. 03 Assigned tasks & Top news Personalized content from Microsoft 365. 04 Teams app / Web link Deep links to Teams apps and websites. 05 Third-party cards Partner ACEs (ServiceNow, Workday and others) through the app store. 06 Custom ACEs (SPFx) Your own cards with a Card view and Quick view, data from Graph or your APIs. Custom cards are built with SPFx Adaptive Card Extensions . See Adaptive Card Extensions . # Setup outline Set a home site (or configure Viva Connections home) for the organization. Edit the Dashboard (created automatically): add cards, set audiences, preview desktop and mobile. Configure the feed sources (organization news sites, boosted news). Add Viva Connections to Teams (admins can pin it in setup policies). Set the home site PowerShell Copy Connect-SPOService -Url https://contoso -admin .sharepoint.com Set-SPOHomeSite -HomeSiteUrl https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/intranet # Interview questions Q1 What is a Viva Connections dashboard card technically? An Adaptive Card Extension (ACE) : an SPFx component that renders a Card view (on the dashboard) and optional Quick views (Adaptive Cards), with data from Graph, SharePoint or any API, and support for audience targeting and mobile. Q2 What does Viva Connections reuse from SharePoint? The home site (or Viva Connections home), global navigation (resources), news from SharePoint (feed), and the dashboard page stored in SharePoint. It is a layer on top of the "},{"t":"SharePoint & Microsoft Teams – How Files, Channels, Tabs and Sites Connect","c":"sharepoint-online","u":"articles/sharepoint-online/teams-integration.html","s":"Where Teams stores files, how standard, private and shared channels map to SharePoint, adding pages, lists and SPFx apps as tabs, permissions surprises, and governance and migration considerations.","k":"teams, sharepoint, channels, tabs, private channels, shared channels, files","h":"Where things live · Bringing SharePoint into Teams · Common surprises · Interview questions · Where are files shared in a Teams private channel stored? · Where are files shared in a 1:1 chat stored?","l":"Beginner","x":"Platform Features","b":"Every Microsoft Teams team is backed by a Microsoft 365 group and a SharePoint team site . Files you share in a channel are stored in SharePoint. Files shared in a 1:1 or group chat are stored in the sender's OneDrive . # Where things live Teams ↔ SharePoint storage map TEAM \"QUALITY OPS\" Microsoft 365 group Owners & members standard channels TEAM SITE /SITES/QUALITYOPS Documents library Folder per standard channel (General, Audits, CAPA) private channel \"Leadership\" PRIVATE CHANNEL SITE /SITES/QUALITYOPS-LEADERSHIP Own library & permissions (channel members only) shared channel \"Supplier X\" SHARED CHANNEL SITE /SITES/QUALITYOPS-SUPPLIERX Own site Members from other tenants via B2B direct connect chats ONEDRIVE OF THE SENDER Microsoft Teams Chat Files folder Shared with chat participants Teams ↔ SharePoint storage map Where you share Stored in Permissions Standard channel Team site → channel folder Team members Private channel Separate channel site Private channel members Shared channel Separate channel site Shared channel members (incl. external orgs) Chat Sender's OneDrive Chat participants (sharing link) Meeting recording Organizer's OneDrive (non-channel) or channel's SharePoint folder Meeting/channel participants # Bringing SharePoint into Teams Tabs : add a SharePoint page, a list or a document library as a channel tab. SPFx in Teams : SPFx web parts can be published as Teams tabs or personal apps . See SPFx in Teams . Viva Connections : the intranet in Teams. Lists app : Microsoft Lists as a tab with conversations. # Common surprises Renaming channels does not rename folders Renaming a standard channel in Teams renames the folder in SharePoint (modern behaviour), but old links, sync clients and migration tools can still point to the old path. Avoid frequent renames and do not move or rename channel folders in SharePoint directly. Permissions added in SharePoint (for example a visitor group) are invisible in Teams, which confuses owners. Private channel sites do not appear in the SharePoint admin center as normal team sites. Find them with Get-SPOSite -Template \"TEAMCHANNEL#1\" (or #0 ). Deleting a team deletes the group and soft-deletes the sites (restorable within 30 days through the group). # Interview questions Q1 Where are files shared in a Teams private channel stored? In a separate SharePoint site created for that private channel, whose permissio"},{"t":"SharePoint Premium (formerly Syntex) – Document Processing, Autofill Columns and Content AI","c":"sharepoint-online","u":"articles/sharepoint-online/sharepoint-premium.html","s":"The AI content services of SharePoint, covering prebuilt and custom document processing models, autofill columns, taxonomy tagging, content assembly, eSignature, translation, OCR, pay-as-you-go billing, and where it fits next to Copilot.","k":"sharepoint premium, syntex, document processing, autofill, ai, content assembly, esignature","h":"Capabilities · How document processing works · Premium vs Copilot · Interview questions · What is the difference between prebuilt and custom document processing models? · How are autofill columns different from traditional extraction models?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Platform Features","b":"SharePoint Premium (the evolution of Project Cortex → SharePoint Syntex , renamed in 2023) adds AI services that understand documents: extracting fields, classifying files, generating documents from templates and more. Most features are pay-as-you-go through an Azure subscription linked in the Microsoft 365 admin center. # Capabilities Cheat sheet SharePoint Premium services 01 Document processing Prebuilt models (invoices, receipts, contracts, sensitive info), custom models (unstructured, freeform, structured) to classify & extract into columns. 02 Autofill columns Describe in natural language what to extract or summarize (\"Extract the contract end date\") and a large language model fills the column. 03 Taxonomy tagging Auto-tag managed metadata columns from document content. 04 Content assembly Generate documents from modern templates bound to list data. 05 eSignature Send PDFs for signature from SharePoint (with partner options). 06 OCR & image tagging Extract text from images and scanned PDFs for search. 07 Document translation Translate documents into other languages, preserving format. 08 Archive & backup Microsoft 365 Archive and Microsoft 365 Backup (related offerings). # How document processing works flowchart LR U[Upload invoice.pdf<br/>to Invoices library] --> M{Model applied<br/>to the library} M --> X[Extract: Vendor, InvoiceNo,<br/>Total, DueDate] X --> C[Write to library columns<br/>+ apply content type] C --> F[Power Automate:<br/>if Total > 10k → approval] C --> S[Search & Copilot use<br/>the structured metadata] Model type Best for Training Prebuilt (invoice, receipt, contract, ID…) Common business documents None (ready to use) Unstructured (teaching method) Classification + extraction from varied text (contracts, letters) ~5 positive + 1 negative examples, explanations Freeform / Structured (AI Builder based) Forms with consistent layout or free text Label samples in AI Builder Autofill columns (LLM) Quick extraction or summaries with natural language prompts None, just a prompt per column # Premium vs Copilot SharePoint Premium Microsoft 365 Copilot Purpose Turn content into structured metadata , automate processing Help users ask, summarize and create Output Columns, content types, generated docs Answers, drafts, summaries Runs On upload/update (background) On demand, per user Licensing Pay-as-you-go Per-user licence They complement each "},{"t":"What is the SharePoint Framework (SPFx)? Architecture, Components & History","c":"spfx","u":"articles/spfx/overview.html","s":"SPFx explained end to end, covering how client-side components run in the page, the component types (web parts, extensions, ACEs, library components, form customizers), where code is hosted, the toolchain, versions from 2017 to 2026, and when to choose SPFx.","k":"spfx, sharepoint framework, architecture, web parts, extensions, overview","h":"How SPFx runs · Component types · SPFx vs earlier models · A short history · When to choose SPFx · Interview questions · Why can't you store an API key in an SPFx web part? · What are the main SPFx component types? · How does SPFx authenticate to SharePoint and Graph?","l":"Beginner","x":"Getting Started","b":"The SharePoint Framework (SPFx) is Microsoft's page and web part model for customizing SharePoint Online, Microsoft Teams, Viva Connections, Outlook and the Microsoft 365 app, and SharePoint Server 2016 FP2, 2019 and SE. You write TypeScript (usually with React ), package it as an .sppkg , and SharePoint loads your bundle directly into the page : no iframes, no server code. 2017 SPFx GA (Feb 2017) 0 servers to host: assets are served from the M365 CDN 6 component types 1 page context shared with SharePoint (user, site, theme) # How SPFx runs SPFx runtime architecture BROWSER – MODERN SHAREPOINT PAGE SharePoint page shell (React) Your web part / extension bundle Page context: user site list theme loads bundles from ASSET HOSTING Microsoft 365 public CDN (ClientSideAssets) or your own CDN / Azure Storage calls APIs as the signed-in user APIS SharePoint REST (SPHttpClient) Microsoft Graph (MSGraphClientV3) Your APIs via Entra ID (AadHttpClient) data & services BACK ENDS SharePoint lists & libraries Microsoft 365 (mail, Teams,… Azure Functions Dataverse Azure Storage SQL SPFx runtime architecture Key implications: Your code runs as the current user , with exactly their permissions. Nothing is elevated. It is client-side only . Secrets must never be placed in SPFx code. Call a protected API instead. It shares the page with other components, so be a good citizen: small bundles, no global CSS overrides, no jQuery conflicts. # Component types Cheat sheet What you can build with SPFx 01 Web parts Visual components users add to pages (React, Vue, Angular or vanilla). Also usable as Teams tabs. 02 Application customizer Scripts that run on every page: header/footer placeholders, analytics, banners. 03 Field customizer Custom rendering for a column in list views. 04 ListView command set Custom buttons in the list command bar and context menu. 05 Form customizer Replace the New/Edit/Display forms of a list or content type. 06 Adaptive Card Extensions (ACEs) Cards for the Viva Connections dashboard and SharePoint pages. 07 Library components Shared code (services, components) consumed by other SPFx solutions. 08 Search query modifier (retired) Older extension type. # SPFx vs earlier models Farm solution Add-in (SP/provider hosted) SPFx Runs On SharePoint servers In iframe / remote web In the page SPO support ❌ Retired Apr 2026 ✅ Hosting GAC on farm App web / your IIS M365"},{"t":"Setting Up the SPFx Development Environment (Node, Yeoman, gulp/Heft, Certificates)","c":"spfx","u":"articles/spfx/dev-environment.html","s":"Install the right Node.js LTS, the Yeoman SharePoint generator and build tools, trust the dev certificate, use nvm for multiple SPFx versions, configure the hosted workbench, and fix the most common setup errors.","k":"spfx, setup, node, yeoman, gulp, heft, nvm, workbench","h":"Version compatibility (key rows) · Install steps · Working with several SPFx versions · Recommended VS Code setup · Common setup errors · Interview questions · Why use nvm when developing SPFx? · What is the hosted workbench and why was the local workbench removed?","l":"Beginner","x":"Getting Started","b":"Most SPFx problems on day one are version mismatches : the wrong Node.js version for the SPFx version you are using. Set up carefully once, with a Node version manager, and everything else is smooth. # Version compatibility (key rows) SPFx Node.js Notes 1.1 6.x SharePoint 2016 Feature Pack 2 1.4.1 6.x / 8.x SharePoint 2019 & Subscription Edition baseline 1.12.1 10 / 12 / 14 1.15 12 / 14 / 16 Form customizer GA, MSGraphClientV3 1.16–1.17 16 1.18 16 / 18 1.19–1.20 18 1.21 22 Node 22 LTS 1.22+ 22 Heft-based build (gulp replaced in v1.22, December 2025); check release notes Always confirm with Microsoft&#39;s matrix The official compatibility matrix is the source of truth for Node, TypeScript, React and generator versions. Run m365 spfx doctor in a project to validate your machine. # Install steps 1. Install a Node version manager (Windows: nvm-windows) and Node LTS PowerShell Copy # Windows: install nvm-windows from its GitHub releases, then: nvm install 22 nvm use 22 node -v # v22.x # macOS/Linux: nvm # nvm install 22 && nvm use 22 2. Global tools PowerShell Copy # Yeoman + SharePoint generator (+ gulp CLI for gulp-based SPFx versions) npm install --global yo @microsoft/generator -sharepoint gulp -cli # Optional but recommended npm install --global @pnp/ cli -microsoft365 3. Scaffold a project and trust the dev certificate PowerShell Copy md hello -codex ; cd hello -codex yo @microsoft/sharepoint # choose WebPart, React, name \"HelloCodex\" gulp trust -dev-cert # gulp-based versions: trusts the localhost HTTPS cert gulp serve # opens the hosted workbench (set initialPage in serve.json) # Heft-based versions use npm scripts instead (e.g. npm run start / npm run build); see package.json config/serve.json – point serve at your tenant's hosted workbench JSON Copy { \"$schema\" : \"https://developer.microsoft.com/json-schemas/spfx-build/spfx-serve.schema.json\" , \"port\" : 4321 , \"https\" : true , \"initialPage\" : \"https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/dev/_layouts/15/workbench.aspx\" } # Working with several SPFx versions flowchart LR P1[Project A<br/>SPFx 1.4.1 for SP2019] -->|nvm use 8| N8[Node 8] P2[Project B<br/>SPFx 1.20] -->|nvm use 18| N18[Node 18] P3[Project C<br/>SPFx 1.21+] -->|nvm use 22| N22[Node 22] Tips: Add an .nvmrc file to every project with the Node version ( 22 ), then run nvm use when you open it. Install the generator version matching the target when you"},{"t":"Anatomy of an SPFx Project – Every Folder and Config File Explained","c":"spfx","u":"articles/spfx/project-structure.html","s":"What each file in a generated SPFx solution does (manifest, package-solution.json, config.json, serve.json, write-manifests, loc strings, sharepoint/assets) and how the build turns TypeScript into an .sppkg.","k":"spfx, project structure, manifest, package-solution, build, sppkg","h":"The files that matter most · Web part manifest · package-solution.json · From source to package · Interview questions · What does includeClientSideAssets do? · Why must the solution version be incremented? · What is the purpose of skipFeatureDeployment ?","l":"Beginner","x":"Getting Started","b":"A freshly scaffolded SPFx project contains a lot of files. Only a handful matter day to day, but knowing all of them makes debugging deployments much easier. Generated solution (React web part) Text Copy hello-codex/ ├─ config/ │ ├─ config.json ← bundles: which entry points go into which JS bundle; localized resources │ ├─ deploy-azure-storage.json (older versions: deploy assets to your own CDN) │ ├─ package-solution.json ← solution ID, version, features, API permissions, packaging flags │ ├─ serve.json ← local serve port + initialPage (hosted workbench URL) │ └─ write-manifests.json ← CDN base path if you host assets yourself ├─ sharepoint/ │ ├─ assets/ ← elements.xml, ClientSideInstance.xml (extensions), list schemas │ └─ solution/ ← build output: hello-codex.sppkg ├─ src/ │ ├─ index.ts │ └─ webparts/helloCodex/ │ ├─ HelloCodexWebPart.ts ← entry class (BaseClientSideWebPart) │ ├─ HelloCodexWebPart.manifest.json ← component ID, alias, preconfigured entries, supportedHosts │ ├─ components/ │ │ ├─ HelloCodex.tsx ← React component │ │ ├─ HelloCodex.module.scss ← CSS modules (scoped class names) │ │ └─ IHelloCodexProps.ts │ └─ loc/ │ ├─ en-us.js ← localized strings │ └─ mystrings.d.ts ├─ teams/ ← Teams app icons (manifest generated at package time) ├─ .yo-rc.json ← generator version & choices (used by upgrade tools) ├─ gulpfile.js / (Heft config in newer versions) ├─ package.json, tsconfig.json, .eslintrc.js └─ .nvmrc (add this yourself) # The files that matter most # Web part manifest HelloCodexWebPart.manifest.json JSON Copy { \"$schema\" : \"https://developer.microsoft.com/json-schemas/spfx/client-side-web-part-manifest.schema.json\" , \"id\" : \"8f2b7a51-0c3d-4e7f-9a1b-2c3d4e5f6a7b\" , \"alias\" : \"HelloCodexWebPart\" , \"componentType\" : \"WebPart\" , \"version\" : \"*\" , \"manifestVersion\" : 2 , \"requiresCustomScript\" : false , \"supportedHosts\" : [ \"SharePointWebPart\" , \"TeamsTab\" , \"TeamsPersonalApp\" , \"SharePointFullPage\" ] , \"supportsThemeVariants\" : true , \"preconfiguredEntries\" : [ { \"groupId\" : \"5c03119e-3074-46fd-976b-c60198311f70\" , \"group\" : { \"default\" : \"Contoso\" } , \"title\" : { \"default\" : \"Hello Codex\" } , \"description\" : { \"default\" : \"Demo web part\" } , \"officeFabricIconFontName\" : \"Code\" , \"properties\" : { \"description\" : \"Hello Codex\" } } ] } Never change the id The component ID identifies your web part on every page where it is used. Changing it makes exi"},{"t":"Build Your First SPFx Web Part – From yo to the Page in 15 Minutes","c":"spfx","u":"articles/spfx/first-webpart.html","s":"A guided build of a React web part that shows the current user and the site's lists, covering scaffolding, the web part class, the React component, calling SharePoint REST, testing in the hosted workbench, packaging and deploying.","k":"spfx, tutorial, web part, react, sphttpclient, beginner","h":"1. Scaffold · 2. The web part class: wiring context into React · 3. The React component: fetch lists with SPHttpClient · 4. Test in the hosted workbench · 5. Package and deploy · Interview questions · How does a web part pass SharePoint context to a React component? · Why do you need onDispose with unmountComponentAtNode ?","l":"Beginner","x":"Getting Started","b":"We will build \"Site Lists\" , a web part that greets the current user and lists the site's visible lists with item counts. It covers the full loop: scaffold → code → test → package → deploy. # 1. Scaffold Generate the project PowerShell Copy md site -lists ; cd site -lists yo @microsoft/sharepoint # ? What is your solution name? site-lists # ? Which type of client-side component to create? WebPart # ? What is your Web part name? SiteLists # ? Which template would you like to use? React # 2. The web part class: wiring context into React src/webparts/siteLists/SiteListsWebPart.ts TypeScript Copy import * as React from &#x27;react&#x27; ; import * as ReactDom from &#x27;react-dom&#x27; ; import { Version } from &#x27;@microsoft/sp-core-library&#x27; ; import { IPropertyPaneConfiguration , PropertyPaneTextField } from &#x27;@microsoft/sp-property-pane&#x27; ; import { BaseClientSideWebPart } from &#x27;@microsoft/sp-webpart-base&#x27; ; import SiteLists from &#x27;./components/SiteLists&#x27; ; import { ISiteListsProps } from &#x27;./components/ISiteListsProps&#x27; ; export interface ISiteListsWebPartProps { title : string ; } export default class SiteListsWebPart extends BaseClientSideWebPart < ISiteListsWebPartProps > { public render (): void { const element : React . ReactElement < ISiteListsProps > = React . createElement ( SiteLists , { title : this . properties . title , userDisplayName : this . context . pageContext . user . displayName , siteUrl : this . context . pageContext . web . absoluteUrl , spHttpClient : this . context . spHttpClient }); ReactDom . render (element, this . domElement ); } protected onDispose (): void { ReactDom . unmountComponentAtNode ( this . domElement ); } protected get dataVersion (): Version { return Version . parse ( &#x27;1.0&#x27; ); } protected getPropertyPaneConfiguration (): IPropertyPaneConfiguration { return { pages : [{ header : { description : &#x27;Settings&#x27; }, groups : [{ groupName : &#x27;General&#x27; , groupFields : [ PropertyPaneTextField ( &#x27;title&#x27; , { label : &#x27;Title&#x27; })] }] }] }; } } # 3. The React component: fetch lists with SPHttpClient src/webparts/siteLists/components/SiteLists.tsx TSX Copy import * as React from &#x27;react&#x27; ; import { SPHttpClient } from &#x27;@microsoft/sp-http&#x27; ; import styles from &#x27;./SiteLists.module.scss&#x27; ; import { ISiteListsProps } fro"},{"t":"SPFx Web Part Lifecycle – onInit, render, Property Changes, Display Modes & Dispose","c":"spfx","u":"articles/spfx/webpart-lifecycle.html","s":"The exact order in which SharePoint calls your web part methods, async initialization with onInit, reactive vs non-reactive property panes, display modes, theme changes, data versioning and disposal, with a lifecycle diagram and best practices.","k":"spfx, lifecycle, oninit, render, property pane, dispose, theme","h":"Lifecycle diagram · A robust skeleton · Interview questions · Where should you initialize services such as PnPjs or the Graph client? · How do you migrate saved web part properties after changing their structure? · What is the difference between reactive and non-reactive property panes?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Getting Started","b":"Knowing when SharePoint calls each method of your web part avoids double data loads, flicker and memory leaks. # Lifecycle diagram sequenceDiagram autonumber participant P as Page participant W as Your web part P->>W: constructor() P->>W: onInit() : Promise (await services, config, PnPjs setup) P->>W: render() Note over W: Page in Read mode P->>W: (Edit) getPropertyPaneConfiguration() P->>W: onPropertyPaneFieldChanged(path, old, new) P->>W: render() [reactive pane → after each change] P->>W: onDisplayModeChanged(oldMode) P->>W: onThemeChanged(theme) [section background changes] P->>W: onDispose() (removed / navigation) Method When Use it for onInit() Once, before first render; can return a Promise Load config, initialize PnPjs/Graph clients, theme provider render() First load and after property changes Render React tree (keep cheap and idempotent) getPropertyPaneConfiguration() When the pane opens Define pages/groups/fields onPropertyPaneConfigurationStart() Pane opens Lazy-load dropdown options onPropertyPaneFieldChanged() A field changes Cascading options, validation onAfterPropertyPaneChangesApplied() Non-reactive pane \"Apply\" Re-fetch data after batch changes onDisplayModeChanged() Read ↔ Edit Show placeholders/config UI in edit mode onThemeChanged() Theme/section background changes Pass semantic colors to components onDispose() Removal or navigation Unmount React, clear timers, abort fetches dataVersion / onAfterDeserialize Loading saved properties Migrate property shape between versions # A robust skeleton Lifecycle best practices TypeScript Copy export default class DeviationsWebPart extends BaseClientSideWebPart < IDeviationsWebPartProps > { private _theme : IReadonlyTheme | undefined ; private _abort = new AbortController (); protected async onInit (): Promise < void > { await super . onInit (); // initialize PnPjs once with the SPFx context this . _sp = spfi (). using ( SPFx ( this . context )); // theme for section backgrounds (requires supportsThemeVariants: true) const provider = this . context . serviceScope . consume ( ThemeProvider . serviceKey ); this . _theme = provider. tryGetTheme (); provider. themeChangedEvent . add ( this , ( args : ThemeChangedEventArgs ) => { this . _theme = args. theme ; this . render (); }); } public render (): void { ReactDom . render ( React . createElement ( Deviations , { sp : this . _sp , listTitle : this . pr"},{"t":"The SPFx Property Pane – Fields, Pages, Validation, Async Options & PnP Controls","c":"spfx","u":"articles/spfx/property-pane.html","s":"Configure web parts with the property pane, covering built-in fields, multi-page panes, validation, dynamic dropdowns loaded on demand, conditional fields, and the PnP property pane controls (list picker, people picker, collection data).","k":"spfx, property pane, configuration, pnp property controls, validation","h":"Built-in field types · Dynamic dropdown with lazy loading + validation · PnP property pane controls · Interview questions · How do you load dropdown options from SharePoint without slowing down page load? · Where are web part properties stored?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Web Parts","b":"The property pane is how page authors configure your web part without code. Properties are saved with the page, so design them like an API: stable names, sensible defaults and validation. # Built-in field types Field Function Text PropertyPaneTextField (single/multi-line, onGetErrorMessage validation) Toggle / Checkbox PropertyPaneToggle , PropertyPaneCheckbox Dropdown PropertyPaneDropdown (static or loaded options) Choice group PropertyPaneChoiceGroup (radio, with images/icons) Slider PropertyPaneSlider Link / Label / Button PropertyPaneLink , PropertyPaneLabel , PropertyPaneButton Horizontal rule PropertyPaneHorizontalRule Dynamic field PropertyPaneDynamicField (dynamic data connections) # Dynamic dropdown with lazy loading + validation Load lists only when the pane opens TypeScript Copy private _listOptions : IPropertyPaneDropdownOption [] = []; private _listsLoading = false ; protected async onPropertyPaneConfigurationStart (): Promise < void > { if ( this . _listOptions . length ) return ; this . _listsLoading = true ; this . context . propertyPane . refresh (); const res = await this . context . spHttpClient . get ( ` ${ this .context.pageContext.web.absoluteUrl} /_api/web/lists?$select=Title&$filter=Hidden eq false and BaseTemplate eq 100` , SPHttpClient . configurations . v1 ); const data = await res. json (); this . _listOptions = data. value . map ( ( l : { Title: string } ) => ({ key : l. Title , text : l. Title })); this . _listsLoading = false ; this . context . propertyPane . refresh (); } protected getPropertyPaneConfiguration (): IPropertyPaneConfiguration { return { pages : [ { header : { description : &#x27;Data source&#x27; }, groups : [{ groupName : &#x27;List&#x27; , groupFields : [ PropertyPaneDropdown ( &#x27;listTitle&#x27; , { label : &#x27;List&#x27; , options : this . _listOptions , disabled : this . _listsLoading }), PropertyPaneSlider ( &#x27;pageSize&#x27; , { label : &#x27;Items per page&#x27; , min : 5 , max : 50 , step : 5 }), PropertyPaneToggle ( &#x27;showClosed&#x27; , { label : &#x27;Include closed items&#x27; }) ] }] }, { header : { description : &#x27;Appearance&#x27; }, groups : [{ groupName : &#x27;Header&#x27; , groupFields : [ PropertyPaneTextField ( &#x27;title&#x27; , { label : &#x27;Title&#x27; , onGetErrorMessage : ( v : string ) => (v && v. length > 60 ? &#x27;Max 60 characters&#x27; : &#x27;&#x27; ) }), // con"},{"t":"React in SPFx – Components, Hooks, State, Services and Clean Architecture","c":"spfx","u":"articles/spfx/react-webpart.html","s":"Structuring real-world React web parts, covering functional components and hooks, a service layer for data, context providers for SPFx context, loading/error/empty states, memoization, error boundaries and folder conventions.","k":"spfx, react, hooks, architecture, typescript, services","h":"Recommended structure · Service layer · Custom hook with loading, error and refresh · Container component with states · Error boundary · Interview questions · Why separate a service layer from React components in SPFx? · How do you prevent \"Can't perform a React state update on an unmounted component\"?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Web Parts","b":"The generator gives you a single class component. Real solutions need a structure that keeps data access, state and UI separate so you can test and change each piece independently. # Recommended structure Feature-oriented layout Text Copy src/webparts/deviations/ ├─ DeviationsWebPart.ts ← SPFx glue only (context, properties, render) ├─ components/ │ ├─ Deviations.tsx ← container: uses hooks, composes UI │ ├─ DeviationList.tsx ← presentational │ ├─ DeviationForm.tsx │ └─ ErrorBoundary.tsx ├─ hooks/ │ └─ useDeviations.ts ← loading/error/data state ├─ services/ │ ├─ IDeviationService.ts ← interface (mockable) │ └─ DeviationService.ts ← PnPjs / SPHttpClient implementation ├─ models/Deviation.ts └─ context/SpfxContext.tsx ← React context for services & SPFx context Layers inside a React web part SPFX WEB PART CLASS Reads properties & context Creates services Renders <App/> provides via React context HOOKS useDeviations() useCurrentUser() useDebounce() call SERVICES DeviationService (PnPjs) GraphService (MSGraphClientV3) ApiService (AadHttpClient) render with COMPONENTS Container components Presentational components (Fluent UI) Layers inside a React web part # Service layer services/DeviationService.ts TypeScript Copy import { SPFI } from &#x27;@pnp/sp&#x27; ; import &#x27;@pnp/sp/webs&#x27; ; import &#x27;@pnp/sp/lists&#x27; ; import &#x27;@pnp/sp/items&#x27; ; import { IDeviation } from &#x27;../models/Deviation&#x27; ; export interface IDeviationService { getOpen ( top : number ): Promise < IDeviation []>; create ( d : Partial < IDeviation >): Promise < number >; close ( id : number ): Promise < void >; } export class DeviationService implements IDeviationService { constructor ( private sp : SPFI, private listTitle : string ) {} public async getOpen ( top : number ): Promise < IDeviation []> { return this . sp . web . lists . getByTitle ( this . listTitle ). items . select ( &#x27;Id&#x27; , &#x27;Title&#x27; , &#x27;Status&#x27; , &#x27;DueDate&#x27; , &#x27;Owner/Title&#x27; ). expand ( &#x27;Owner&#x27; ) . filter ( \"Status ne &#x27;Closed&#x27;\" ). orderBy ( &#x27;DueDate&#x27; , true ). top (top)(); } public async create ( d : Partial < IDeviation >): Promise < number > { const r = await this . sp . web . lists . getByTitle ( this . listTitle ). items . add ({ Title : d. Title , Status : &#x27;Submitted&#x27; }); return r. Id ?? r. data ?. Id ; // PnPjs v4"},{"t":"Fluent UI & Theming in SPFx – Theme-Aware Web Parts and Section Backgrounds","c":"spfx","u":"articles/spfx/fluent-ui-theming.html","s":"Use Fluent UI React (v8) and v9 in SPFx, consume the site theme and semantic colors, support section background variants, write SCSS that respects themes, and keep accessibility and dark backgrounds right.","k":"spfx, fluent ui, theming, section backgrounds, scss, accessibility","h":"Where theme information comes from · Consuming the theme · Theme-aware SCSS · Fluent UI v8 vs v9 · Accessibility checklist · Interview questions · How do you make an SPFx web part look correct on a dark section background? · What are semantic colors?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Web Parts","b":"Users can change a site's theme and set section backgrounds (neutral, soft, strong). A professional web part looks right on all of them. SPFx gives you the theme at runtime, and Fluent UI gives you components that already speak SharePoint's design language. # Where theme information comes from Theme flow into a web part SITE THEME Tenant / custom theme (palette) Brand center / Change the look section background variant THEMEPROVIDER SERVICE tryGetTheme() themeChangedEvent IReadonlyTheme: semanticColors palette fonts your code WEB PART Pass theme to React Fluent ThemeProvider / customizations CSS variables in SCSS Theme flow into a web part # Consuming the theme Manifest: opt in to section backgrounds JSON Copy { \"supportsThemeVariants\" : true } Web part: get the theme and react to changes TypeScript Copy import { ThemeProvider , ThemeChangedEventArgs , IReadonlyTheme } from &#x27;@microsoft/sp-component-base&#x27; ; private _themeProvider!: ThemeProvider ; private _themeVariant : IReadonlyTheme | undefined ; protected async onInit (): Promise < void > { this . _themeProvider = this . context . serviceScope . consume ( ThemeProvider . serviceKey ); this . _themeVariant = this . _themeProvider . tryGetTheme (); this . _themeProvider . themeChangedEvent . add ( this , ( args : ThemeChangedEventArgs ) => { this . _themeVariant = args. theme ; this . render (); }); return super . onInit (); } Component: use semantic colors (and Fluent v8 ThemeProvider) TSX Copy import { ThemeProvider as FluentThemeProvider , PartialTheme } from &#x27;@fluentui/react&#x27; ; export const Card : React . FC <{ theme ?: IReadonlyTheme ; title : string }> = ( { theme, title, children } ) => { const sc = theme?. semanticColors ; return ( < FluentThemeProvider theme = {theme as PartialTheme }> < div style = {{ background: sc ? .bodyBackground , color: sc ? .bodyText , border: ` 1px solid ${ sc ? .bodyDivider }`, borderRadius: 8 , padding: 16 }}> < h3 style = {{ color: sc ? .link }}> {title} </ h3 > {children} </ div > </ FluentThemeProvider > ); }; # Theme-aware SCSS Component.module.scss – theme tokens with fallbacks SCSS Copy // \"[theme:...]\" tokens are replaced at runtime by SharePoint&#x27;s theme loader .card { background-color : \"[theme:bodyBackground, default:#ffffff]\" ; color : \"[theme:bodyText, default:#323130]\" ; border : 1px solid \"[theme:bodyDivider, default:#edebe9]\" ; .tit"},{"t":"Connected Web Parts with Dynamic Data – Sources, Consumers and Page Filters","c":"spfx","u":"articles/spfx/dynamic-data.html","s":"Use SPFx dynamic data to connect web parts, covering implementing IDynamicDataCallables as a source, consuming with DynamicProperty and PropertyPaneDynamicField, connecting to out-of-the-box web parts, and the page context source.","k":"spfx, dynamic data, connected web parts, idynamicdatacallables, property pane","h":"Source web part · Consumer web part · Interview questions · What interface must a dynamic data source implement? · How does a consumer read a connected value?","l":"Advanced","x":"Web Parts","b":"Dynamic data lets one component publish values (a selected item, a filter) and others consume them, configured by page authors in the property pane. It is the same mechanism the out-of-the-box List, Document library and File viewer web parts use. flowchart LR S[Source web part<br/>Department picker<br/>implements IDynamicDataCallables] -- \"property: department\" --> DM[\"Dynamic data manager<br/>(page)\"] DM --> C1[Consumer web part<br/>Employee directory] DM --> C2[OOTB List web part<br/>filtered by Department] P[Page environment<br/>query string, search] --> DM # Source web part DepartmentPickerWebPart.ts (source) TypeScript Copy import { IDynamicDataCallables , IDynamicDataPropertyDefinition } from &#x27;@microsoft/sp-dynamic-data&#x27; ; export default class DepartmentPickerWebPart extends BaseClientSideWebPart < IProps > implements IDynamicDataCallables { private _selected : string = &#x27;&#x27; ; protected async onInit (): Promise < void > { this . context . dynamicDataSourceManager . initializeSource ( this ); return super . onInit (); } // 1) Declare what you publish public getPropertyDefinitions (): ReadonlyArray < IDynamicDataPropertyDefinition > { return [{ id : &#x27;department&#x27; , title : &#x27;Selected department&#x27; }]; } // 2) Return current values public getPropertyValue ( propertyId : string ): string { if (propertyId === &#x27;department&#x27; ) return this . _selected ; throw new Error ( &#x27;Bad property id&#x27; ); } // 3) Notify consumers when the value changes (called from your React component) private _onSelect = ( dept : string ): void => { this . _selected = dept; this . context . dynamicDataSourceManager . notifyPropertyChanged ( &#x27;department&#x27; ); }; public render (): void { ReactDom . render ( React . createElement ( DepartmentPicker , { onSelect : this . _onSelect }), this . domElement ); } } # Consumer web part EmployeeDirectoryWebPart.ts (consumer) TypeScript Copy import { DynamicProperty } from &#x27;@microsoft/sp-component-base&#x27; ; import { PropertyPaneDynamicField , PropertyPaneDynamicFieldSet , DynamicDataSharedDepth } from &#x27;@microsoft/sp-property-pane&#x27; ; export interface IDirectoryProps { department : DynamicProperty < string >; } export default class EmployeeDirectoryWebPart extends BaseClientSideWebPart < IDirectoryProps > { public render (): void { const dept : string | undefined = this . pro"},{"t":"SPFx Library Components – Sharing Code Across Solutions","c":"spfx","u":"articles/spfx/library-components.html","s":"Package shared services, models and UI as an SPFx library component, consume it from web parts with npm link or a private feed, deploy it tenant-wide, and manage versioning and the trade-offs vs a plain npm package.","k":"spfx, library component, shared code, npm, versioning","h":"Two ways to share code · Creating a library · Consuming it · Interview questions · What is the main advantage of an SPFx library component over a shared npm package? · What is the main risk?","l":"Advanced","x":"Web Parts","b":"When several SPFx solutions need the same code (a logging service, a Graph wrapper, a branded header component), you can either publish a normal npm package that each solution bundles, or build an SPFx library component that is deployed once to the app catalog and loaded at runtime by consumers. # Two ways to share code flowchart TB subgraph NPM[\"npm package (bundled)\"] L1[shared-lib v1.2] -->|bundled into| A1[Solution A bundle] L1 -->|bundled into| B1[Solution B bundle] end subgraph LIB[\"SPFx library component (runtime)\"] LC[library .sppkg<br/>deployed tenant-wide] -->|loaded at runtime| A2[Solution A] LC -->|loaded at runtime| B2[Solution B] end npm package SPFx library component Deployment Every consumer rebuilds to get updates Update the library once; consumers get it at runtime Bundle size Duplicated in each bundle Shared, loaded once per page Versioning risk Low (each app pins a version) Higher : a breaking change in the library affects all consumers Best for Utility code, UI kits Shared runtime services (telemetry, config, auth wrappers) # Creating a library Scaffold PowerShell Copy yo @microsoft/sharepoint # ? Which type of client-side component to create? Library # ? What is your Library name? contoso-core src/libraries/contosoCore/index.ts TypeScript Copy export { Logger } from &#x27;./Logger&#x27; ; export { ConfigService } from &#x27;./ConfigService&#x27; ; // Logger.ts export class Logger { constructor ( private source : string ) {} public info ( msg : string , data ?: unknown ): void { console . info ( `[ ${ this .source} ] ${msg} ` , data ?? &#x27;&#x27; ); } public error ( msg : string , err ?: unknown ): void { console . error ( `[ ${ this .source} ] ${msg} ` , err); /* + App Insights */ } } # Consuming it Local development with npm link PowerShell Copy # in the library folder gulp build; npm link # in the consumer web part folder npm link contoso -core Consumer package.json (version must match the deployed library) JSON Copy \"dependencies\" : { \"contoso-core\" : \"0.0.1\" } Use it in a web part TypeScript Copy import { Logger } from &#x27;contoso-core&#x27; ; const log = new Logger ( &#x27;DeviationsWebPart&#x27; ); log. info ( &#x27;Rendering&#x27; , { items : 12 }); Deploy the library .sppkg to the tenant app catalog (tenant-wide), then deploy consumers. In production, publish the library to a private npm feed (Azure Artifacts) so consumer bu"},{"t":"Application Customizer – Custom Headers, Footers, Banners and Page Scripts","c":"spfx","u":"articles/spfx/application-customizer.html","s":"Build an SPFx application customizer that renders into Top and Bottom placeholders, handles SPA navigation, reads properties from ClientSideComponentProperties, and deploys tenant-wide via the Tenant Wide Extensions list or per site.","k":"spfx, application customizer, extensions, header, footer, placeholders","h":"The code · Deploying and activating · Interview questions · Why must an application customizer subscribe to placeholder and navigation events? · How do you deploy an application customizer to every site in the tenant?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Extensions","b":"An application customizer runs on every modern page of the sites where it is activated. It can render UI in the Top and Bottom placeholders (below the suite bar and at the end of the page) or run non-visual code such as analytics, cookie banners or redirects. It is the modern replacement for custom master page headers and footers. Where an application customizer renders SUITE BAR (MICROSOFT 365) App launcher Search Settings PlaceholderName.Top YOUR HEADER Emergency banner Global mega-menu Classification notice page content MODERN PAGE Site header Sections & web parts PlaceholderName.Bottom YOUR FOOTER Links Contact Feedback button Where an application customizer renders # The code src/extensions/contosoChrome/ContosoChromeApplicationCustomizer.ts TypeScript Copy import { BaseApplicationCustomizer , PlaceholderContent , PlaceholderName } from &#x27;@microsoft/sp-application-base&#x27; ; import * as React from &#x27;react&#x27; ; import * as ReactDom from &#x27;react-dom&#x27; ; import { Banner } from &#x27;./components/Banner&#x27; ; export interface IContosoChromeProperties { message : string ; // passed via ClientSideComponentProperties JSON severity : &#x27;info&#x27; | &#x27;warning&#x27; | &#x27;critical&#x27; ; } export default class ContosoChromeApplicationCustomizer extends BaseApplicationCustomizer < IContosoChromeProperties > { private _top : PlaceholderContent | undefined ; private _bottom : PlaceholderContent | undefined ; public async onInit (): Promise < void > { // Placeholders can appear later and are re-created on SPA navigation this . context . placeholderProvider . changedEvent . add ( this , this . _renderPlaceholders ); this . context . application . navigatedEvent . add ( this , this . _renderPlaceholders ); this . _renderPlaceholders (); } private _renderPlaceholders (): void { if (! this . _top ) { this . _top = this . context . placeholderProvider . tryCreateContent ( PlaceholderName . Top , { onDispose : this . _onDispose }); if ( this . _top ) { ReactDom . render ( React . createElement ( Banner , { message : this . properties . message , severity : this . properties . severity , siteTitle : this . context . pageContext . web . title }), this . _top . domElement ); } } if (! this . _bottom ) { this . _bottom = this . context . placeholderProvider . tryCreateContent ( PlaceholderName . Bottom , { onDispose : this . _onDispose }); if "},{"t":"Field Customizer – Custom Rendering for List Columns with SPFx","c":"spfx","u":"articles/spfx/field-customizer.html","s":"Render list view cells with code (React or plain DOM) using a field customizer, covering onRenderCell, reading the item and field value, associating with a column, and when JSON column formatting is the better choice.","k":"spfx, field customizer, extensions, list views, column formatting","h":"Associating it with a column · Field customizer vs column formatting · Interview questions · When would you choose a field customizer over column formatting? · How does a field customizer read other columns of the same item?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Extensions","b":"A field customizer controls how one column's cells render in modern list views. Since JSON column formatting covers most visual needs declaratively, use a field customizer only when you need code : calling an API per cell, complex components, or third-party rendering libraries. flowchart LR V[Modern list view] -->|for each visible cell<br/>of the bound column| R[\"onRenderCell(event)\"] R --> D[event.fieldValue<br/>event.listItem] R --> E[Render into<br/>event.domElement] V -->|cell removed / scrolled out| X[\"onDisposeCell(event)\"] RiskScoreFieldCustomizer.ts TypeScript Copy import { BaseFieldCustomizer , IFieldCustomizerCellEventParameters } from &#x27;@microsoft/sp-listview-extensibility&#x27; ; import * as React from &#x27;react&#x27; ; import * as ReactDom from &#x27;react-dom&#x27; ; import { RiskBadge } from &#x27;./components/RiskBadge&#x27; ; export interface IRiskScoreProperties { highThreshold ?: number ; } export default class RiskScoreFieldCustomizer extends BaseFieldCustomizer < IRiskScoreProperties > { public onRenderCell ( event : IFieldCustomizerCellEventParameters ): void { const score = Number (event. fieldValue ?? 0 ); const severity = event. listItem . getValueByName ( &#x27;Severity&#x27; ) as string ; // other columns must be in the view ReactDom . render ( React . createElement ( RiskBadge , { score, severity, high : this . properties . highThreshold ?? 70 }), event. domElement ); } public onDisposeCell ( event : IFieldCustomizerCellEventParameters ): void { ReactDom . unmountComponentAtNode (event. domElement ); super . onDisposeCell (event); } } # Associating it with a column The customizer is bound through the field's ClientSideComponentId (and optional properties): elements.xml – a site column that uses the customizer XML Copy < Field ID = \"{7b8c9d0e-1f2a-4b3c-9d4e-5f6a7b8c9d0e}\" Name = \"RiskScore\" DisplayName = \"Risk score\" Type = \"Number\" Group = \"Contoso\" ClientSideComponentId = \"d1e2f3a4-b5c6-4d7e-8f90-a1b2c3d4e5f6\" ClientSideComponentProperties = \"{ &quot; highThreshold &quot; :75}\" /> Or attach to an existing column with PnP PowerShell Copy Set-PnPField -List \"Deviations\" -Identity \"RiskScore\" -Values @ { ClientSideComponentId = \"d1e2f3a4-b5c6-4d7e-8f90-a1b2c3d4e5f6\" ClientSideComponentProperties = &#x27;{\"highThreshold\":75}&#x27; } # Field customizer vs column formatting Column formatting (JSON) Field customizer (SPFx) Skills "},{"t":"ListView Command Set – Custom Buttons in the Command Bar and Context Menu","c":"spfx","u":"articles/spfx/command-set.html","s":"Add custom commands to modern lists and libraries, covering command visibility based on selection, listViewStateChangedEvent, opening dialogs, calling REST or Power Automate on selected items, and registering the command set on specific list templates.","k":"spfx, command set, extensions, command bar, context menu, dialog","h":"Interview questions · How do you show a command only when exactly one document is selected? · How do you target a command set to document libraries only?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Extensions","b":"A ListView Command Set adds buttons to a list or library's command bar and context menu (ECB) . It is the modern replacement for classic ribbon custom actions: \"Send for approval\", \"Generate PDF\", \"Copy to archive\", \"Export to Excel with template\". sequenceDiagram autonumber actor U as User participant L as List view participant C as Command set participant API as SharePoint REST / Flow / API U->>L: Select 2 items L->>C: listViewStateChangedEvent (selectedRows = 2) C->>C: command.visible = selectedRows ≥ 1 U->>C: Click \"Send for approval\" C->>U: Confirm dialog (BaseDialog) C->>API: POST for each selected item API-->>C: 200 OK C->>U: Toast / refresh view ApprovalCommandSet.ts TypeScript Copy import { BaseListViewCommandSet , Command , IListViewCommandSetExecuteEventParameters , ListViewStateChangedEventArgs } from &#x27;@microsoft/sp-listview-extensibility&#x27; ; import { Dialog } from &#x27;@microsoft/sp-dialog&#x27; ; import { SPHttpClient } from &#x27;@microsoft/sp-http&#x27; ; export interface IApprovalCommandSetProperties { flowUrl : string ; } export default class ApprovalCommandSet extends BaseListViewCommandSet < IApprovalCommandSetProperties > { public async onInit (): Promise < void > { const cmd : Command = this . tryGetCommand ( &#x27;SEND_APPROVAL&#x27; ); cmd. visible = false ; this . context . listView . listViewStateChangedEvent . add ( this , this . _onStateChanged ); } private _onStateChanged = ( args : ListViewStateChangedEventArgs ): void => { const cmd = this . tryGetCommand ( &#x27;SEND_APPROVAL&#x27; ); const rows = this . context . listView . selectedRows ?? []; // visible only when 1+ items selected and none already submitted cmd. visible = rows. length > 0 && rows. every ( r => r. getValueByName ( &#x27;Status&#x27; ) === &#x27;Draft&#x27; ); this . raiseOnChange (); }; public async onExecute ( event : IListViewCommandSetExecuteEventParameters ): Promise < void > { if (event. itemId !== &#x27;SEND_APPROVAL&#x27; ) return ; const ids = (event. selectedRows ?? []). map ( r => Number (r. getValueByName ( &#x27;ID&#x27; ))); const list = this . context . pageContext . list !; const web = this . context . pageContext . web . absoluteUrl ; for ( const id of ids) { await this . context . spHttpClient . post ( ` ${web} /_api/web/lists(guid&#x27; ${list.id} &#x27;)/items( ${id} )/ValidateUpdateListItem` , SPHttpClient . configurations . v1 ,"},{"t":"Form Customizer – Replace SharePoint List New/Edit/Display Forms with React","c":"spfx","u":"articles/spfx/form-customizer.html","s":"Build a custom list form with the SPFx form customizer, covering display modes, loading and saving the item, validation, formSaved/formClosed, and binding the component to a content type with PnP PowerShell, with a comparison against Power Apps forms.","k":"spfx, form customizer, list forms, react, validation, content type","h":"How it works · The customizer class · The form component (React + validation) · Binding the form to a content type · Form customizer vs Power Apps vs form formatting · Interview questions · How does SharePoint know to use your form customizer? · What must the form call after saving?","l":"Advanced","x":"Extensions","b":"The form customizer (GA in SPFx 1.15) lets developers replace the New , Edit and Display forms of a list or content type with their own React component. It is the pro-code alternative to Power Apps forms, with full control, native performance and Git-based ALM. # How it works sequenceDiagram autonumber actor U as User participant L as List participant F as Form customizer participant API as SharePoint REST / PnPjs U->>L: Click \"New\" (or open an item) L->>F: Load component bound to the content type<br/>displayMode = New / Edit / Display F->>API: (Edit/Display) GET item by context.itemId U->>F: Fill in fields → Save F->>F: Validate (cross-field rules, async checks) F->>API: POST / MERGE item F->>L: this.formSaved() L-->>U: Panel closes, view refreshes # The customizer class DeviationFormFormCustomizer.ts TypeScript Copy import { BaseFormCustomizer } from &#x27;@microsoft/sp-listview-extensibility&#x27; ; import { FormDisplayMode } from &#x27;@microsoft/sp-core-library&#x27; ; import * as React from &#x27;react&#x27; ; import * as ReactDOM from &#x27;react-dom&#x27; ; import { spfi, SPFx } from &#x27;@pnp/sp&#x27; ; import &#x27;@pnp/sp/webs&#x27; ; import &#x27;@pnp/sp/lists&#x27; ; import &#x27;@pnp/sp/items&#x27; ; import DeviationForm from &#x27;./components/DeviationForm&#x27; ; export default class DeviationFormFormCustomizer extends BaseFormCustomizer <{}> { private _sp = spfi (); public async onInit (): Promise < void > { this . _sp = spfi (). using ( SPFx ( this . context )); return super . onInit (); } public render (): void { ReactDOM . render ( React . createElement ( DeviationForm , { displayMode : this . displayMode , // New | Edit | Display itemId : this . context . itemId , // undefined for New listGuid : this . context . list . guid . toString (), sp : this . _sp , onSave : this . _onSave , onClose : this . _onClose }), this . domElement ); } public onDispose (): void { ReactDOM . unmountComponentAtNode ( this . domElement ); super . onDispose (); } private _onSave = (): void => { this . formSaved (); }; // tells SharePoint the item was saved private _onClose = (): void => { this . formClosed (); }; // cancel / close } # The form component (React + validation) components/DeviationForm.tsx TSX Copy import * as React from &#x27;react&#x27; ; import { FormDisplayMode } from &#x27;@microsoft/sp-core-library&#x27; ; import { TextField , Dropdown , D"},{"t":"Adaptive Card Extensions (ACEs) – Cards for Viva Connections and SharePoint Pages","c":"spfx","u":"articles/spfx/adaptive-card-extensions.html","s":"Build Viva Connections dashboard cards with SPFx, covering card view vs quick view, the component-based card views (SPFx 1.18+), state, loading data from SharePoint or Graph, card sizes, actions, and deployment.","k":"spfx, ace, adaptive card extensions, viva connections, dashboard, adaptive cards","h":"Anatomy · The ACE class · Card view (component-based, SPFx 1.18+) · Quick view (Adaptive Card template) · Interview questions · What is the difference between a card view and a quick view? · How does an ACE update after the user approves something in the quick view?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Extensions","b":"An Adaptive Card Extension (ACE) is an SPFx component that renders a card on the Viva Connections dashboard (desktop and mobile) or on a SharePoint page. Clicking it can open a Quick View , which is an Adaptive Card with more detail and actions, or navigate to a URL. # Anatomy ACE structure ACE CLASS BaseAdaptiveCardExten… State (e.g. pending approvals) onInit: load data Card size: Medium / Large renders CARD VIEW Component-based card (header,… Image icon title Shown on dashboard on click QUICK VIEW(S) Adaptive Card JSON template +… Actions: Submit OpenUrl ShowCard Can navigate between quick… data from DATA SharePoint list (SPHttpClient/PnPjs) Microsoft Graph Your API (AadHttpClient) ACE structure # The ACE class PendingApprovalsAdaptiveCardExtension.ts TypeScript Copy import { BaseAdaptiveCardExtension } from &#x27;@microsoft/sp-adaptive-card-extension-base&#x27; ; import { CardView } from &#x27;./cardView/CardView&#x27; ; import { QuickView } from &#x27;./quickView/QuickView&#x27; ; import { SPHttpClient } from &#x27;@microsoft/sp-http&#x27; ; export interface IPendingApprovalsState { items : { id : number ; title : string ; requester : string }[]; loading : boolean ; } const CARD_VIEW_REGISTRY_ID = &#x27;PendingApprovals_CARD_VIEW&#x27; ; export const QUICK_VIEW_REGISTRY_ID = &#x27;PendingApprovals_QUICK_VIEW&#x27; ; export default class PendingApprovalsAdaptiveCardExtension extends BaseAdaptiveCardExtension <{ title : string ; listTitle : string }, IPendingApprovalsState > { public async onInit (): Promise < void > { this . state = { items : [], loading : true }; this . cardNavigator . register ( CARD_VIEW_REGISTRY_ID , () => new CardView ()); this . quickViewNavigator . register ( QUICK_VIEW_REGISTRY_ID , () => new QuickView ()); await this . _load (); } private async _load (): Promise < void > { const web = this . context . pageContext . web . absoluteUrl ; const me = this . context . pageContext . user . email ; const url = ` ${web} /_api/web/lists/getbytitle(&#x27; ${ this .properties.listTitle} &#x27;)/items` + `?$select=Id,Title,Requester/Title,Approver/EMail&$expand=Requester,Approver` + `&$filter=Status eq &#x27;Pending&#x27; and Approver/EMail eq &#x27; ${me} &#x27;&$top=20` ; const res = await this . context . spHttpClient . get (url, SPHttpClient . configurations . v1 ); const data = await res. json (); this . setState ({ loading : false , ite"},{"t":"SPFx CRUD with SPHttpClient – Create, Read, Update, Delete via SharePoint REST","c":"spfx","u":"articles/spfx/sphttpclient-crud.html","s":"A complete, typed service for SharePoint list CRUD in SPFx using SPHttpClient, covering configurations, OData queries, ETags and concurrency, person and lookup fields, batching, error handling, and the full React usage.","k":"spfx, sphttpclient, rest, crud, typescript, odata, etag","h":"A reusable, typed list service · Using it from the web part · Special fields cheat sheet (nometadata) · Batching many operations · Interview questions · Why use SPHttpClient instead of fetch in SPFx? · What does SPHttpClient.configurations.v1 do? · How do you implement optimistic concurrency on updates?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Data & APIs","b":"SPHttpClient is SPFx's built-in HTTP client for SharePoint REST . It automatically adds authentication, the request digest for writes, and the right OData headers. No extra libraries are needed. flowchart LR C[React component] --> S[ListService<br/>typed CRUD] S -->|\"spHttpClient.get/post<br/>configurations.v1\"| R[\"/_api/web/lists/…/items\"] R --> SP[(SharePoint list)] S -->|errors → typed messages| C # A reusable, typed list service services/ListService.ts TypeScript Copy import { SPHttpClient , SPHttpClientResponse , ISPHttpClientOptions } from &#x27;@microsoft/sp-http&#x27; ; export interface IListItem { Id : number ; Title : string ; Status : string ; DueDate ?: string ; OwnerId ?: number ; &#x27;@odata.etag&#x27; ?: string ; } export class ListService { private readonly headers = { &#x27;Accept&#x27; : &#x27;application/json;odata=nometadata&#x27; , &#x27;Content-Type&#x27; : &#x27;application/json;odata=nometadata&#x27; , &#x27;odata-version&#x27; : &#x27;&#x27; }; constructor ( private client : SPHttpClient , private webUrl : string , private listTitle : string ) {} private get itemsUrl (): string { return ` ${ this .webUrl} /_api/web/lists/getbytitle(&#x27; ${ encodeURIComponent ( this .listTitle.replace(/ &#x27;/g, \"&#x27; &#x27;\"))}&#x27; )/items `; } // READ (filter + select + expand + paging) public async getItems(filter = \"Status ne &#x27;Closed&#x27;\", top = 50): Promise<IListItem[]> { const url = ` ${ this .itemsUrl}?$select=Id,Title,Status,DueDate,OwnerId,Owner/Title&$expand=Owner ` + ` &$filter=${ encodeURIComponent (filter)}&$orderby=DueDate asc&$top=${top} `; const res = await this.client.get(url, SPHttpClient.configurations.v1, { headers: this.headers }); const json = await this.ensureOk(res); return json.value as IListItem[]; } public async getItem(id: number): Promise<IListItem> { const res = await this.client.get(` ${ this .itemsUrl}(${id})?$select=Id,Title,Status,DueDate,OwnerId `, SPHttpClient.configurations.v1, { headers: this.headers }); return this.ensureOk(res); } // CREATE public async create(item: Partial<IListItem>): Promise<IListItem> { const res = await this.client.post(this.itemsUrl, SPHttpClient.configurations.v1, { headers: this.headers, body: JSON.stringify({ Title: item.Title, Status: item.Status ?? &#x27;Submitted&#x27;, DueDate: item.DueDate, OwnerId: item.OwnerId }) }); return this.ensureOk(res); } // UPDATE with opti"},{"t":"PnPjs in SPFx – Fluent CRUD, Batching, Caching, Files and Large Lists","c":"spfx","u":"articles/spfx/pnpjs-crud.html","s":"Set up PnPjs v3/v4 with SPFx behaviours and a context-aware factory, then cover CRUD, select/expand/filter, batching, file upload with chunking, caching, paging large lists, error handling and Graph access.","k":"spfx, pnpjs, crud, batching, caching, files, typescript","h":"Setup · CRUD · Batching · Large lists: async iteration · Files · Caching · Graph with PnPjs · Interview questions · Why use PnPjs instead of SPHttpClient? · What does spfi().using(SPFx(this.context)) do? · How do you avoid \"property does not exist on type\" errors after upgrading PnPjs?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Data & APIs","b":"PnPjs is the community's fluent TypeScript library for SharePoint REST and Microsoft Graph. It turns string-built URLs into discoverable, typed calls and adds batching, caching and paging. It is the most common data layer in production SPFx solutions. PnPjs inside SPFx WEB PART / EXTENSION onInit(): spfi().using(SPFx(this.conte… fluent API PNPJS CORE Behaviors: SPFx auth Caching InjectHeaders Timeline (observers) Batching HTTP ENDPOINTS SharePoint REST /_api Microsoft Graph (graphfi + GraphSPFx) PnPjs inside SPFx # Setup Install (match versions across @pnp packages) PowerShell Copy npm install @pnp/ sp @pnp/graph @pnp/logging --save pnpjsConfig.ts – a single place to get configured instances TypeScript Copy import { WebPartContext } from &#x27;@microsoft/sp-webpart-base&#x27; ; import { spfi, SPFI , SPFx } from &#x27;@pnp/sp&#x27; ; import { graphfi, GraphFI , SPFx as graphSPFx } from &#x27;@pnp/graph&#x27; ; import { LogLevel , PnPLogging } from &#x27;@pnp/logging&#x27; ; import &#x27;@pnp/sp/webs&#x27; ; import &#x27;@pnp/sp/lists&#x27; ; import &#x27;@pnp/sp/items&#x27; ; import &#x27;@pnp/sp/batching&#x27; ; import &#x27;@pnp/sp/files&#x27; ; import &#x27;@pnp/sp/folders&#x27; ; import &#x27;@pnp/sp/site-users/web&#x27; ; import &#x27;@pnp/graph/users&#x27; ; let _sp : SPFI | undefined ; let _graph : GraphFI | undefined ; export const getSP = ( context ?: WebPartContext ): SPFI => { if (context) _sp = spfi (). using ( SPFx (context)). using ( PnPLogging ( LogLevel . Warning )); return _sp!; }; export const getGraph = ( context ?: WebPartContext ): GraphFI => { if (context) _graph = graphfi (). using ( graphSPFx (context)). using ( PnPLogging ( LogLevel . Warning )); return _graph!; }; Web part onInit TypeScript Copy protected async onInit (): Promise < void > { await super . onInit (); getSP ( this . context ); getGraph ( this . context ); } # CRUD Create · Read · Update · Delete TypeScript Copy const sp = getSP (); const list = sp. web . lists . getByTitle ( &#x27;Deviations&#x27; ); // CREATE const created = await list. items . add ({ Title : &#x27;Cold room excursion&#x27; , Status : &#x27;Submitted&#x27; , OwnerId : 12 }); // READ – select / expand / filter / order / top const open = await list. items . select ( &#x27;Id&#x27; , &#x27;Title&#x27; , &#x27;Status&#x27; , &#x27;DueDate&#x27; , &#x27;Owner/Title&#x27; , &#x27;Owner/EMail&#x27; ) . expand"},{"t":"Calling Microsoft Graph from SPFx with MSGraphClientV3","c":"spfx","u":"articles/spfx/msgraph-client.html","s":"Use MSGraphClientV3 to read users, groups, mail, calendar, files and Teams data from SPFx, request permissions with webApiPermissionRequests, handle paging and batching, and avoid the common consent and throttling mistakes.","k":"spfx, microsoft graph, msgraphclient, permissions, users, calendar, teams","h":"Token flow · Request permissions · Examples · Paging and batching · Common errors · Interview questions · Which identity does MSGraphClientV3 use? · Why are approved Graph permissions a tenant-wide decision?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Data & APIs","b":"MSGraphClientV3 (SPFx 1.15+) wraps the Microsoft Graph JavaScript SDK v3 and authenticates as the current user with delegated permissions that a tenant admin approves once. It is the easiest way for a web part to show people, calendars, mail, Planner tasks, Teams or files across Microsoft 365. # Token flow sequenceDiagram autonumber participant WP as Web part participant F as msGraphClientFactory participant AAD as Microsoft Entra ID participant G as graph.microsoft.com WP->>F: getClient('3') F->>AAD: Token for Graph (SharePoint Online Client Extensibility principal, user context) AAD-->>F: Access token with approved delegated scopes WP->>G: GET /me/events (Bearer token) G-->>WP: JSON All SPFx solutions share one Entra service principal ( SharePoint Online Client Extensibility Web Application Principal ). Permissions approved for one solution are available to every SPFx solution in the tenant. Approve the minimum. # Request permissions config/package-solution.json JSON Copy \"webApiPermissionRequests\" : [ { \"resource\" : \"Microsoft Graph\" , \"scope\" : \"User.ReadBasic.All\" } , { \"resource\" : \"Microsoft Graph\" , \"scope\" : \"Calendars.Read\" } , { \"resource\" : \"Microsoft Graph\" , \"scope\" : \"Sites.Read.All\" } ] After deployment an admin approves them in SharePoint admin center → Advanced → API access (or with m365 spo serviceprincipal permissionrequest approve ). See API permissions . # Examples Users, calendar, files and Teams TypeScript Copy import { MSGraphClientV 3 } from &#x27;@microsoft/sp-http&#x27; ; const client : MSGraphClientV 3 = await this . context . msGraphClientFactory . getClient ( &#x27;3&#x27; ); // 1) Search people (User.ReadBasic.All) const people = await client. api ( &#x27;/users&#x27; ) . header ( &#x27;ConsistencyLevel&#x27; , &#x27;eventual&#x27; ) . search ( &#x27;\"displayName:jan\"&#x27; ). select ( &#x27;id,displayName,mail,jobTitle&#x27; ). top ( 10 ). get (); // 2) My next 5 meetings (Calendars.Read) const now = new Date (). toISOString (); const inAWeek = new Date ( Date . now () + 7 * 864e5 ). toISOString (); const events = await client. api ( &#x27;/me/calendarView&#x27; ) . query ({ startDateTime : now, endDateTime : inAWeek }) . select ( &#x27;subject,start,end,onlineMeeting,location&#x27; ). orderby ( &#x27;start/dateTime&#x27; ). top ( 5 ). get (); // 3) Recent files (Files.Read) const recent = await client. api ( &#x27;/me/drive/"},{"t":"SPFx + Azure Functions with AadHttpClient – Secure Custom APIs End to End","c":"spfx","u":"articles/spfx/aadhttpclient-azure-functions.html","s":"Protect an Azure Function with Entra ID (Easy Auth), expose an API scope, request it from SPFx, call it with AadHttpClient, read the user's identity server-side, and use on-behalf-of or managed identity for back-end resources, with full code and setup steps.","k":"spfx, aadhttpclient, azure functions, entra id, api, easy auth, on-behalf-of","h":"Architecture · Step 1 · Create the Function and its Entra app · Step 2 · The function · Step 3 · Request the permission in SPFx · Step 4 · Call it from the web part · End-to-end flow · Going further: acting as the user on Graph (on-behalf-of) · Interview questions · How does SPFx get a token for a custom API? · How does the Azure Function know who called it? · Why not call Azure SQL or Storage directly from SPFx?","l":"Advanced","x":"Data & APIs","b":"When a web part needs secrets, elevated access, heavy processing or non-SharePoint data , put that logic in an Azure Function and let SPFx call it with AadHttpClient . The function is protected by Entra ID , so only your tenant's users (through SPFx) can call it, and it knows exactly who the caller is. # Architecture SPFx → Entra-protected Azure Function SPFX WEB PART (BROWSER) aadHttpClientFactory.getClie… ID URI) Bearer token for scope user_impersonation HTTPS + Authorization: Bearer (aud = api://deviation-api) AZURE FUNCTION (APP SERVICE AUTHENTICATION / EASY AUTH) Validates token (issuer, audience) x-ms-client-principal header CORS: https://contoso.sharepoint.com calls downstream with BACK-END IDENTITY Managed identity (Key Vault, Storage, SQL) On-behalf-of token (Graph as user) App-only cert (SharePoint Sites.Selected) data RESOURCES Azure SQL / Table Storage Microsoft Graph External APIs with secrets SPFx → Entra-protected Azure Function # Step 1 · Create the Function and its Entra app Create a Function App (Node 20/22 or .NET 8 isolated). Authentication → Add identity provider → Microsoft (creates an app registration). Set Require authentication and Return HTTP 401 for unauthenticated requests. In the app registration → Expose an API : set the App ID URI (e.g. api://deviation-api or the default api://<client-id> ) and the scope user_impersonation . Under Authentication of the Function, add https://contoso.sharepoint.com (or the client principal) to allowed token audiences if you use a custom URI. CORS : allow https://contoso.sharepoint.com (and https://localhost:4321 for dev). # Step 2 · The function src/functions/deviations.ts (Azure Functions v4, Node) TypeScript Copy import { app, HttpRequest , HttpResponseInit , InvocationContext } from &#x27;@azure/functions&#x27; ; function getCaller ( req : HttpRequest ): { name : string ; oid : string } { // Easy Auth injects the validated principal as base64 JSON const raw = req. headers . get ( &#x27;x-ms-client-principal&#x27; ); if (!raw) throw new Error ( &#x27;unauthenticated&#x27; ); const p = JSON . parse ( Buffer . from (raw, &#x27;base64&#x27; ). toString ( &#x27;utf8&#x27; )); const claim = ( t : string ) => p. claims . find ( ( c : any ) => c. typ === t)?. val ; return { name : claim ( &#x27;name&#x27; ), oid : claim ( &#x27;http://schemas.microsoft.com/identity/claims/objectidentifier&#x27; ) }; }"},{"t":"SPFx API Permissions – webApiPermissionRequests, Approval and the Shared Service Principal","c":"spfx","u":"articles/spfx/api-permissions.html","s":"How SPFx API permissions work behind the scenes, covering the shared Entra principal, requesting Graph and custom API scopes, approving and revoking with the admin center, PowerShell and CLI, isolated web parts, and governance rules.","k":"spfx, permissions, api access, entra id, governance, isolated web parts","h":"Lifecycle of a permission request · Approving and auditing · Isolated web parts · Governance rules that work · Interview questions · Why can approving one SPFx solution's permission request be risky? · What does isDomainIsolated change?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Data & APIs","b":"When an SPFx solution needs a token for Microsoft Graph or another Entra-protected API, it does not get its own app registration by default. All SPFx code shares one Entra service principal, the SharePoint Online Client Extensibility Web Application Principal , and tenant admins decide which scopes that principal may use. # Lifecycle of a permission request flowchart LR D[Developer adds<br/>webApiPermissionRequests] --> P[.sppkg uploaded<br/>to tenant app catalog] P --> Q[Pending requests<br/>Admin center → API access] Q -->|Approve| G[OAuth2 grant on shared<br/>SPFx service principal] Q -->|Reject| X[Solution must handle<br/>missing permission] G --> ALL[Available to EVERY<br/>SPFx solution in tenant] Requesting permissions JSON Copy \"webApiPermissionRequests\" : [ { \"resource\" : \"Microsoft Graph\" , \"scope\" : \"Mail.Send\" } , { \"resource\" : \"Dataverse\" , \"scope\" : \"user_impersonation\" } , { \"resource\" : \"deviation-api\" , \"scope\" : \"user_impersonation\" } ] # Approving and auditing SPO Management Shell PowerShell Copy Get-SPOTenantServicePrincipalPermissionRequests Approve-SPOTenantServicePrincipalPermissionRequest -RequestId <guid> Get-SPOTenantServicePrincipalPermissionGrants # what is currently granted Revoke-SPOTenantServicePrincipalPermission -ObjectId < grant-object -id > CLI for Microsoft 365 Bash Copy m365 spo serviceprincipal permissionrequest list m365 spo serviceprincipal permissionrequest approve -- id <guid> m365 spo serviceprincipal grant list # Isolated web parts For high-risk scopes you can set \"isDomainIsolated\": true in package-solution.json . The solution's web parts then run in an iframe on a separate domain with their own Entra app , so their granted permissions are not shared with other SPFx solutions. The trade-off is iframe behaviour (sizing, performance, no page-level theming). Shared (default) Isolated Principal Shared SPFx principal Dedicated app per solution Scope sharing All solutions get all approved scopes Only this solution Rendering In page iframe Use for Low-risk scopes Sensitive scopes (Mail.Send, Files.ReadWrite.All…) # Governance rules that work Keep a register of approved scopes, why they were requested, and the solution owner. Approve least privilege ( User.ReadBasic.All instead of User.Read.All , Sites.Selected patterns in custom APIs). Prefer custom APIs for powerful operations, so the high privilege sits on a dedicated "},{"t":"SPFx Connected to Dataverse – Web API CRUD from a SharePoint Web Part","c":"spfx","u":"articles/spfx/spfx-dataverse.html","s":"Read and write Dataverse tables directly from SPFx with AadHttpClient, covering permission requests, environment URLs, OData queries, formatted values, lookups with @odata.bind, create/update/delete, security roles, CORS and when to put a custom API in the middle.","k":"spfx, dataverse, web api, aadhttpclient, crud, power platform, odata","h":"Architecture · Prerequisites · A typed Dataverse service · Using it in React · Request flow · Direct call vs custom API · Troubleshooting · Interview questions · How does an SPFx web part authenticate to Dataverse? · How do you set a lookup column when creating a Dataverse row through the Web API? · Why would you put an Azure Function between SPFx and Dataverse?","l":"Advanced","x":"Integrations: Azure & Dataverse","b":"Many organizations keep business data in Dataverse (model-driven apps, Dynamics 365) but want it on the SharePoint intranet or in Teams . An SPFx web part can call the Dataverse Web API directly as the signed-in user . Dataverse security roles still apply, so users only see and change what they are allowed to. # Architecture SPFx ↔ Dataverse (delegated, as the user) SPFX WEB PART aadHttpClientFactory.getClie… URL) React UI (list, form) Bearer token (aud = https://contoso.crm4.dynamics.com, scope user_impersonation) MICROSOFT ENTRA ID SharePoint Online Client Extensibility principal Approved: Dataverse / user_impersonation HTTPS OData v4 DATAVERSE WEB API /api/data/v9.2/cr8a1_deviati… Security roles & business units Business rules & plug-ins run server-side stores DATAVERSE TABLES Deviation (cr8a1_deviation) Account / Contact Plant (lookup) SPFx ↔ Dataverse (delegated, as the user) # Prerequisites Environment URL , e.g. https://contoso.crm4.dynamics.com (from Power Platform admin center → environment → Environment URL ). Users must exist in that environment (licensed, with a security role granting the table privileges). Request the Dataverse scope in package-solution.json : config/package-solution.json JSON Copy \"webApiPermissionRequests\" : [ { \"resource\" : \"Dataverse\" , \"scope\" : \"user_impersonation\" } ] Resource display name The Entra service principal for Dataverse appears as \"Dataverse\" in most tenants (older tenants may show Common Data Service or Dynamics CRM ). Use the name exactly as shown under Enterprise applications (app ID 00000007-0000-0000-c000-000000000000 ). Then approve the request in SharePoint admin center → API access . # A typed Dataverse service services/DataverseService.ts TypeScript Copy import { AadHttpClient , HttpClientResponse , IHttpClientOptions } from &#x27;@microsoft/sp-http&#x27; ; import { WebPartContext } from &#x27;@microsoft/sp-webpart-base&#x27; ; export interface IDeviation { cr8a1_deviationid ?: string ; cr8a1_name : string ; // primary name column cr8a1_severity ?: number ; // choice (option set) value &#x27;cr8a1_severity@OData.Community.Display.V1.FormattedValue&#x27; ?: string ; cr8a1_duedate ?: string ; _cr8a1_plant_value ?: string ; // lookup id (read) &#x27;_cr8a1_plant_value@OData.Community.Display.V1.FormattedValue&#x27; ?: string ; } export class DataverseService { private client!: AadHttpClient ; private read"},{"t":"SPFx Connected to Azure Table Storage – Two Secure Patterns with Full Code","c":"spfx","u":"articles/spfx/spfx-azure-table-storage.html","s":"Store and query data in Azure Table Storage from a SharePoint web part, either through an Entra-protected Azure Function using managed identity (recommended) or directly from the browser with Entra ID tokens and Azure RBAC, with key design, CRUD code, CORS and cost notes.","k":"spfx, azure table storage, azure functions, managed identity, aadhttpclient, rbac, nosql","h":"Pattern A (recommended): SPFx → Azure Function → Table Storage · The function (Node, @azure/data-tables) · The SPFx side · Pattern B: SPFx → Table Storage directly (Entra ID + RBAC) · Which pattern? · Designing keys · Interview questions · Why use Table Storage instead of a SharePoint list for some SPFx data? · How can SPFx write to Azure Tables without exposing an account key? · What is a good PartitionKey for a desk check-in app?","l":"Advanced","x":"Integrations: Azure & Dataverse","b":"Azure Table Storage is a cheap, massively scalable key-value (NoSQL) store , ideal for data that does not belong in a SharePoint list: high-volume logs, telemetry, user preferences, kiosk check-ins, survey responses, or anything that would blow past list thresholds. There are two secure ways to use it from SPFx. PartitionKey + RowKey the only index (design keys for your queries) ~$0.045/GB storage per month (ballpark, region-dependent) 1 MB max entity size, 252 custom properties 0 secrets in the browser (both patterns below) # Pattern A (recommended): SPFx → Azure Function → Table Storage Pattern A – API in the middle with managed identity SPFX WEB PART AadHttpClient → api://checkin-api Validates input in UI Bearer token (user) AZURE FUNCTION (EASY AUTH) Knows the caller (oid, name) Business rules & validation Managed identity (no keys) Entra token for Storage, RBAC: Storage Table Data Contributor AZURE TABLE STORAGE Table: CheckIns PartitionKey = site/date RowKey = inverted ticks + user Pattern A – API in the middle with managed identity # The function (Node, @azure/data-tables) src/functions/checkins.ts TypeScript Copy import { app, HttpRequest , HttpResponseInit } from &#x27;@azure/functions&#x27; ; import { TableClient , odata } from &#x27;@azure/data-tables&#x27; ; import { DefaultAzureCredential } from &#x27;@azure/identity&#x27; ; // Managed identity in Azure, your az login locally — no account keys anywhere const table = new TableClient ( `https:// ${process.env.STORAGE_ACCOUNT} .table.core.windows.net` , &#x27;CheckIns&#x27; , new DefaultAzureCredential ()); const inverted = ( ) => String ( 8640000000000000 - Date . now ()). padStart ( 16 , &#x27;0&#x27; ); // newest first function caller ( req : HttpRequest ) { const p = JSON . parse ( Buffer . from (req. headers . get ( &#x27;x-ms-client-principal&#x27; ) ?? &#x27;&#x27; , &#x27;base64&#x27; ). toString () || &#x27;{}&#x27; ); const c = ( t : string ) => p. claims ?. find ( ( x : any ) => x. typ === t)?. val ; return { oid : c ( &#x27;http://schemas.microsoft.com/identity/claims/objectidentifier&#x27; ), name : c ( &#x27;name&#x27; ) }; } app. http ( &#x27;checkins&#x27; , { methods : [ &#x27;GET&#x27; , &#x27;POST&#x27; , &#x27;PATCH&#x27; , &#x27;DELETE&#x27; ], authLevel : &#x27;anonymous&#x27; , // Easy Auth enforces Entra ID route : &#x27;checkins/{site}/{rowKey?}&#x27; , handler : async ( re"},{"t":"SPFx with Azure Blob Storage & Azure SQL – Large Files and Relational Data","c":"spfx","u":"articles/spfx/spfx-azure-blob-sql.html","s":"Upload large files from SPFx to Blob Storage with short-lived user-delegation SAS issued by an Azure Function, and query Azure SQL through a secure API with managed identity and parameterized queries, with architecture, code and security guidance.","k":"spfx, azure blob storage, sas, azure sql, managed identity, azure functions, api","h":"Part 1 · Large files to Blob Storage · Part 2 · Azure SQL through an API · Choosing where data should live · Interview questions · Why use a user-delegation SAS instead of an account-key SAS? · Why shouldn't SPFx connect to Azure SQL directly?","l":"Advanced","x":"Integrations: Azure & Dataverse","b":"Some data does not belong in SharePoint: huge media or engineering files with special retention, or relational data that already lives in SQL . SPFx can work with both safely, always through a small API you control (see AadHttpClient + Azure Functions ). # Part 1 · Large files to Blob Storage sequenceDiagram autonumber participant WP as SPFx web part participant FN as Azure Function (Entra-protected) participant ST as Blob Storage WP->>FN: POST /upload-url {fileName, size} (Bearer) FN->>FN: Authorize user, validate type/size, build blob path FN->>ST: getUserDelegationKey (managed identity) FN-->>WP: SAS URL (write-only, 15 min, single blob) WP->>ST: PUT blocks directly (browser → storage, no function bandwidth) WP->>FN: POST /uploaded {blobPath} → record metadata (SQL / list) Function: issue a user-delegation SAS for one blob TypeScript Copy import { BlobServiceClient , BlobSASPermissions , generateBlobSASQueryParameters, SASProtocol } from &#x27;@azure/storage-blob&#x27; ; import { DefaultAzureCredential } from &#x27;@azure/identity&#x27; ; const account = process. env . STORAGE_ACCOUNT !; const svc = new BlobServiceClient ( `https:// ${account} .blob.core.windows.net` , new DefaultAzureCredential ()); export async function createUploadUrl ( userOid : string , fileName : string ): Promise < string > { if (! /\\.(pdf|dwg|mp4|zip)$/i . test (fileName)) throw new Error ( &#x27;File type not allowed&#x27; ); const container = &#x27;engineering-drops&#x27; ; const blobName = ` ${userOid} / ${ Date .now()} - ${fileName.replace(/[^\\w.\\-]/g, &#x27;_&#x27; )} ` ; const starts = new Date ( Date . now () - 2 * 60 * 1000 ); const expires = new Date ( Date . now () + 15 * 60 * 1000 ); const key = await svc. getUserDelegationKey (starts, expires); // MI needs \"Storage Blob Delegator\" + data role const sas = generateBlobSASQueryParameters ({ containerName : container, blobName, permissions : BlobSASPermissions . parse ( &#x27;cw&#x27; ), // create + write only startsOn : starts, expiresOn : expires, protocol : SASProtocol . Https }, key, account). toString (); return `https:// ${account} .blob.core.windows.net/ ${container} / ${ encodeURIComponent (blobName)} ? ${sas} ` ; } SPFx: upload with progress using the Azure SDK in the browser TypeScript Copy import { BlockBlobClient } from &#x27;@azure/storage-blob&#x27; ; const { url } = await api. post ( &#x27;/upload-url&#x27; "},{"t":"SPFx in Microsoft Teams, Outlook and the Microsoft 365 App","c":"spfx","u":"articles/spfx/teams-apps-with-spfx.html","s":"Surface SPFx web parts as Teams tabs, personal apps, meeting apps and in Outlook and the Microsoft 365 app, covering supportedHosts, Sync to Teams, detecting the host with the TeamsJS SDK, Teams theming and SSO, plus packaging tips.","k":"spfx, teams, teams tab, personal app, outlook, microsoft 365 app, teamsjs","h":"Hosting surfaces · Detecting Teams and reading context · Deploying to Teams · Interview questions · How does an SPFx web part know it is running in Teams? · What does \"Sync to Teams\" do?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Integrations: Azure & Dataverse","b":"The same SPFx web part can run in SharePoint , as a Teams channel tab , a Teams personal app , a meeting app , and in Outlook and the Microsoft 365 app . You write it once and host it in many places, using SharePoint's CDN and authentication. # Hosting surfaces One SPFx bundle, many hosts SPFX SOLUTION (.SPPKG) supportedHosts in manifest Teams manifest (auto or custom) Tenant app catalog \"Sync to Teams\" / deploy HOSTS SharePoint page (SharePointWebPa… Teams channel tab (TeamsTab) Teams personal app… Meeting side panel… Outlook & Microsoft 365… runtime context CONTEXT AVAILABLE this.context.sdks.microsoftT… (TeamsJS) Team / channel / user IDs Theme (default, dark, contrast) One SPFx bundle, many hosts Manifest: declare the hosts JSON Copy \"supportedHosts\" : [ \"SharePointWebPart\" , \"TeamsTab\" , \"TeamsPersonalApp\" , \"TeamsMeetingApp\" , \"SharePointFullPage\" ] # Detecting Teams and reading context Host detection + Teams theme TypeScript Copy protected async onInit (): Promise < void > { await super . onInit (); const teams = this . context . sdks . microsoftTeams ; if (teams) { const ctx = await teams. teamsJs . app . getContext (); this . _host = &#x27;teams&#x27; ; this . _teamName = ctx. team ?. displayName ; this . _channelId = ctx. channel ?. id ; this . _applyTeamsTheme (ctx. app . theme ); // &#x27;default&#x27; | &#x27;dark&#x27; | &#x27;contrast&#x27; teams. teamsJs . app . registerOnThemeChangeHandler ( t => this . _applyTeamsTheme (t)); } else { this . _host = &#x27;sharepoint&#x27; ; } } private _applyTeamsTheme ( theme : string ): void { this . domElement . setAttribute ( &#x27;data-teams-theme&#x27; , theme); // switch CSS variables this . render (); } Channel-aware data In a channel tab, ctx.channel.id and ctx.team.groupId let you show channel-specific content, for example the documents of that channel's folder or tasks filtered by team. Store per-tab settings in web part properties: the tab's configuration page is the property pane. # Deploying to Teams Build and package normally, then upload the .sppkg to the tenant app catalog . Select the app and click Sync to Teams (or m365 spo app teamspackage download and upload a customized Teams package). Users add it as a tab or pin the personal app. Admins can pre-pin it with Teams setup policies. Custom icons and descriptions go in the teams/ folder (or a full Teams app manifest for multiple tabs and bo"},{"t":"Deploying SPFx Solutions – App Catalogs, Tenant-Wide Deployment, CDN and Upgrades","c":"spfx","u":"articles/spfx/deployment-app-catalog.html","s":"The complete deployment story for SPFx, covering ship builds, tenant vs site collection app catalogs, skipFeatureDeployment, asset hosting on the Microsoft 365 CDN or your own, versioning and upgrading installed apps, and rollback.","k":"spfx, deployment, app catalog, cdn, tenant-wide, versioning","h":"From build to page · Where the JavaScript is served from · Versioning and upgrades · Rollback strategy · Interview questions · What happens when you upload a new version of a tenant-wide deployed SPFx package? · How do you roll back a bad SPFx release?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Deployment & DevOps","b":"# From build to page flowchart LR B[\"Ship build<br/>bundle --ship\"] --> P[\"package-solution --ship<br/>→ .sppkg\"] P --> U[Upload to app catalog] U --> D{Deploy dialog} D -->|Make available to all sites| T[Tenant-wide<br/>skipFeatureDeployment] D -->|Only enable| S[Site owners add app<br/>per site] T & S --> W[Web parts in toolbox<br/>extensions activated] U --> A[API access requests<br/>→ admin approves] # Where the JavaScript is served from Option Setting Notes Microsoft 365 CDN via app catalog (default) includeClientSideAssets: true Assets go to ClientSideAssets in the app catalog, served by the public CDN. Simplest. Your own CDN / Azure Storage includeClientSideAssets: false + write-manifests.json cdnBasePath You upload temp/deploy/* yourself. Useful for very large assets or special caching. # Versioning and upgrades package-solution.json – bump for every release JSON Copy \"solution\" : { \"version\" : \"1.4.0.0\" , \"features\" : [ { \"id\" : \"…\" , \"version\" : \"1.4.0.0\" , \"title\" : \"Deviations feature\" } ] } Tenant-wide solutions: uploading a higher version updates everywhere instantly . There is nothing for site owners to do. Site-installed solutions: sites show Update available in Site contents. Use PnP or CLI to update in bulk ( Update-PnPApp ). Bundles are cached by file hash , so new builds produce new file names and users get them without cache issues. Update an app on all sites where it is installed PowerShell Copy Connect-PnPOnline -Url https://contoso -admin .sharepoint.com -Interactive -ClientId $clientId $app = Get-PnPApp -Identity \"deviation-webparts-client-side-solution\" Get-PnPTenantSite | ForEach-Object { Connect-PnPOnline -Url $_ .Url -Interactive -ClientId $clientId $installed = Get-PnPApp -Identity $app .Id -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue if ( $installed .InstalledVersion -and $installed .CanUpgrade) { Update-PnPApp -Identity $app .Id; \"Updated $ ( $_ .Url)\" } } # Rollback strategy Keep the previous .sppkg artifacts (pipeline artifacts or releases). To roll back, re-upload the previous package with a higher version number (for example rebuild 1.3 as 1.4.0.1). SharePoint does not support downgrading versions. Release checklist Version bumped · gulp bundle --ship (not debug) · no console.log noise · API permissions reviewed · tested in a site collection app catalog first · release notes · artifact archived for rollback. # Interview questions Q1 What"},{"t":"SPFx CI/CD with Azure DevOps – YAML Pipeline to Build, Test, Package and Deploy","c":"spfx","u":"articles/spfx/cicd-azure-devops.html","s":"A production-ready Azure DevOps multi-stage YAML pipeline for SPFx, covering Node setup, npm ci with caching, lint and Jest tests, ship build, versioning, artifacts, certificate-based CLI for Microsoft 365 login, deployment to test and prod app catalogs with approvals.","k":"spfx, azure devops, ci/cd, yaml, pipeline, cli for microsoft 365, devops","h":"Pipeline design · Prerequisites · The YAML · Branching model that fits · Interview questions · How does the pipeline authenticate to SharePoint without a user? · Why stamp the solution version in the pipeline? · How do you add an approval before production?","l":"Advanced","x":"Deployment & DevOps","b":"Manual \"build on my laptop and upload\" deployments do not scale and cannot be audited. A pipeline makes every release repeatable , tested , versioned and approved . # Pipeline design Multi-stage SPFx pipeline TRIGGER PR to main → CI only Merge to main → CI + deploy Test Tag v* → deploy Prod Stage 1 BUILD Node 22 + npm ci (cached) ESLint + Jest tests Version stamp from build… bundle --ship + package-solutio… Publish .sppkg artifact Stage 2 DEPLOY TEST Environment \"spfx-test\" CLI for Microsoft 365 (cert login) Site collection / test tenant app catalog Stage 3 (approval) DEPLOY PROD Environment \"spfx-prod\" with approvers Tenant app catalog skipFeatureDeployment Multi-stage SPFx pipeline # Prerequisites An Entra app registration for deployment with a certificate , granted SharePoint → Sites.FullControl.All (application) or, preferably, Sites.Selected with full control on the app catalog site. Upload the .pfx to Pipelines → Library → Secure files ( spfx-deploy.pfx ). A variable group spfx-deploy with TenantId , AppId , CertPassword (secret), AppCatalogUrl . Two Environments : spfx-test and spfx-prod (with approvers). # The YAML azure-pipelines.yml YAML Copy trigger: branches: { include: [ main ] } tags: { include: [ &#x27;v*&#x27; ] } pr: branches: { include: [ main ] } variables: - group: spfx-deploy - name: nodeVersion value: &#x27;22.x&#x27; - name: packageName value: &#x27;deviation-webparts.sppkg&#x27; stages: - stage: Build jobs: - job: build pool: { vmImage: &#x27;ubuntu-latest&#x27; } steps: - task: NodeTool@0 inputs: { versionSpec: &#x27;$(nodeVersion)&#x27; } displayName: &#x27;Use Node $(nodeVersion)&#x27; - task: Cache@2 inputs: key: &#x27;npm | \"$(Agent.OS)\" | package-lock.json&#x27; path: &#x27;$(Pipeline.Workspace)/.npm&#x27; displayName: &#x27;Cache npm&#x27; - script: npm ci --cache $(Pipeline.Workspace)/.npm displayName: &#x27;npm ci&#x27; - script: npm run lint && npm test -- --ci --reporters=default --reporters=jest-junit displayName: &#x27;Lint & unit tests&#x27; - task: PublishTestResults@2 condition: succeededOrFailed() inputs: { testResultsFiles: &#x27;**/junit.xml&#x27; } # Stamp the solution version from the pipeline run (e.g. 1.4.<run>.0) - pwsh: | $f = &#x27;config/package-solution.json&#x27; $j = Get-Content $f -Raw | ConvertFrom-Json $parts = $j.solution.version.Split(&#x27;.&#x27;) $j.solution.version = \"$($parts[0]).$($parts[1]).$("},{"t":"SPFx CI/CD with GitHub Actions – Build, Test and Deploy with OIDC or Certificates","c":"spfx","u":"articles/spfx/cicd-github-actions.html","s":"A GitHub Actions workflow for SPFx, covering caching, lint and tests, ship build, version stamping, artifact upload, deployment with CLI for Microsoft 365 using a certificate stored in secrets, environment protection rules and release tags.","k":"spfx, github actions, ci/cd, devops, cli for microsoft 365, deployment","h":"Secrets and environments · Workflow · Interview questions · How do you prevent a deployment to production without approval in GitHub Actions? · What is the advantage of federated credentials (OIDC) for deployments?","l":"Advanced","x":"Deployment & DevOps","b":"If your code lives on GitHub, GitHub Actions gives you the same CI/CD as Azure DevOps with less YAML. The flow is the same: build and test on every PR, deploy to test on merge, deploy to production on a release tag with a required reviewer. flowchart LR PR[Pull request] --> CI[CI job:<br/>lint · test · ship build] M[Push to main] --> CI --> T[Deploy job → test catalog<br/>environment: test] R[Release tag v*] --> CI --> P[Deploy job → tenant catalog<br/>environment: production<br/>required reviewers] # Secrets and environments Name Where Value SPO_APP_ID , SPO_TENANT_ID Repository secrets Entra app for deployment SPO_CERT_BASE64 , SPO_CERT_PASSWORD Environment secrets Base64 of the .pfx and its password APP_CATALOG_URL Environment variable Test: site collection catalog; Prod: tenant catalog Create environments test and production (Settings → Environments) and add required reviewers to production. # Workflow .github/workflows/spfx.yml YAML Copy name: spfx on: pull_request: branches: [ main ] push: branches: [ main ] tags: [ &#x27;v*&#x27; ] jobs: build: runs-on: ubuntu-latest steps: - uses: actions/checkout@v4 - uses: actions/setup-node@v4 with: { node-version: 22 , cache: npm } - run: npm ci - run: npm run lint && npm test -- --ci - name: Stamp version run: | node -e \"const f=&#x27;config/package-solution.json&#x27;;const j=require(&#x27;./&#x27;+f);const p=j.solution.version.split(&#x27;.&#x27;);j.solution.version=[p[0],p[1],process.env.GITHUB_RUN_NUMBER,0].join(&#x27;.&#x27;);require(&#x27;fs&#x27;).writeFileSync(f,JSON.stringify(j,null,2));console.log(j.solution.version)\" - run: npx gulp bundle --ship && npx gulp package-solution --ship # or the Heft npm scripts - uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4 with: { name: sppkg , path: sharepoint/solution/*.sppkg } deploy-test: if: github.event_name == &#x27;push&#x27; && github.ref == &#x27;refs/heads/main&#x27; needs: build runs-on: ubuntu-latest environment: test steps: - uses: actions/download-artifact@v4 with: { name: sppkg } - uses: actions/setup-node@v4 with: { node-version: 22 } - run: npm i -g @pnp/cli-microsoft365 - name: Deploy to site collection app catalog env: CERT: ${{ secrets.SPO_CERT_BASE64 }} run: | m365 login --authType certificate --certificateBase64Encoded \"$CERT\" --password \"${{ secrets.SPO_CERT_PASSWORD }}\" \\ --appId \"${{ secrets.SPO_APP_ID }}\" --tenant \"${{ secrets.SPO_TENANT_ID }}\" PKG=$(ls *."},{"t":"Upgrading SPFx Projects – CLI for Microsoft 365, Breaking Changes and the Heft Toolchain","c":"spfx","u":"articles/spfx/upgrade-spfx-versions.html","s":"A safe, repeatable process to upgrade SPFx solutions, covering m365 spfx project upgrade reports, Node and TypeScript changes, React and Fluent UI versions, deprecated APIs, toolchain modernization (gulp → Heft), testing and rollout.","k":"spfx, upgrade, cli for microsoft 365, heft, gulp, maintenance, node","h":"The upgrade loop · Things that commonly break · Interview questions · How do you upgrade an SPFx project safely? · Why can't you just bump all package versions in package.json?","l":"Advanced","x":"Deployment & DevOps","b":"SPFx releases several versions a year. Older projects slowly fall behind: unsupported Node versions, vulnerable dependencies, missing features. The community tool m365 spfx project upgrade turns upgrading from guesswork into a checklist. # The upgrade loop flowchart LR A[Branch: upgrade/1.2x] --> B[m365 spfx project upgrade<br/>--output md] B --> C[Apply steps:<br/>package.json, configs, code mods] C --> D[nvm use target Node<br/>rm -rf node_modules; npm i] D --> E[Build + lint + tests] E --> F{Works in workbench<br/>& test catalog?} F -- no --> C F -- yes --> G[PR, review, deploy test → prod] Generate an upgrade report Bash Copy cd my-spfx-project nvm use 22 # target Node for the new SPFx version m365 spfx doctor # validate environment for current project m365 spfx project upgrade --toVersion 1.21.1 --output md > upgrade-report.md # The report lists every file change, npm command and code mod (FN###### findings) Example findings in the report: Finding Typical action Update @microsoft/sp-* packages npm i -SE @microsoft/sp-core-library@1.21.1 … Update @microsoft/generator-sharepoint in .yo-rc.json Edit version Remove deprecated packages npm un -S @microsoft/sp-webpart-workbench … Update tsconfig.json / .eslintrc.js Replace with template contents Update React / TypeScript versions Align with the SPFx release Replace deprecated API usage e.g. onListViewUpdated → listViewStateChangedEvent # Things that commonly break Node version : each SPFx version supports specific Node LTS lines, so switch with nvm. TypeScript stricter checks : new compiler versions surface type errors. Fix them rather than suppressing. Third-party packages that pin old React or webpack versions: upgrade or replace them. Custom gulp tasks / webpack config tweaks : with the move to a Heft-based toolchain in newer SPFx releases, custom gulp extensions must be ported to Heft plugins or npm scripts. PnPjs major versions (v2 → v3/v4): selective imports and changed return types. Upgrade one major step at a time when far behind From very old versions (for example 1.4 → 1.21), upgrade in a few hops (1.4 → 1.11 → 1.16 → 1.18 → 1.21), building and testing between hops. Or scaffold a fresh project and move your src over, which is often faster for tiny solutions. # Interview questions Q1 How do you upgrade an SPFx project safely? On a branch, run m365 spfx project upgrade --toVersion x --output md to get"},{"t":"Debugging & Testing SPFx – Workbench, Source Maps, VS Code, Jest and Mocks","c":"spfx","u":"articles/spfx/debugging-testing.html","s":"Debug web parts and extensions in the hosted workbench and real pages with source maps and VS Code, read SPFx logs, and write Jest unit tests with mocked services and React Testing Library.","k":"spfx, debugging, testing, jest, vs code, source maps, react testing library","h":"Debugging options · Debugging an extension on a real page · Production debugging · Unit testing with Jest · Interview questions · How do you debug an SPFx extension? · How do you unit test a web part that calls SharePoint?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Quality & Troubleshooting","b":"# Debugging options Where to debug what WEB PARTS Hosted workbench (/_layouts/15/workbench.aspx) Real page + ?debugManifestsFile Browser DevTools + source maps extensions need real pages EXTENSIONS serve.json serveConfigurations… ?loadSPFX=true&debugManifest… IDE VS CODE launch.json (Edge/Chrome) Breakpoints in .ts/.tsx via source maps Debug toolbar Where to debug what .vscode/launch.json (hosted workbench) JSON Copy { \"version\" : \"0.2.0\" , \"configurations\" : [ { \"name\" : \"Hosted workbench (Edge)\" , \"type\" : \"msedge\" , \"request\" : \"launch\" , \"url\" : \"https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/dev/_layouts/15/workbench.aspx\" , \"webRoot\" : \"${workspaceRoot}\" , \"sourceMaps\" : true , \"sourceMapPathOverrides\" : { \"webpack:///.././src/*\" : \"${webRoot}/src/*\" } , \"runtimeArgs\" : [ \"--remote-debugging-port=9222\" , \"-inprivate\" ] } ] } Run gulp serve --nobrowser (or the npm start script), then press F5 . Breakpoints in your TypeScript files are hit through source maps. # Debugging an extension on a real page config/serve.json – serveConfigurations JSON Copy \"serveConfigurations\" : { \"default\" : { \"pageUrl\" : \"https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/dev/Lists/Deviations/AllItems.aspx\" , \"customActions\" : { \"a1b2c3d4-e5f6-4a7b-8c9d-0e1f2a3b4c5d\" : { \"location\" : \"ClientSideExtension.ListViewCommandSet.CommandBar\" , \"properties\" : { } } } } } # Production debugging Append ?debug=true&noredir=true&debugManifestsFile=https://localhost:4321/temp/manifests.js to load your local bundle on a production page, without deploying. Use Log.info/warn/error from @microsoft/sp-core-library with a source name, then filter in DevTools. Press Ctrl+F12 on a modern page to open the SharePoint developer dashboard (component load times, manifests, logs). # Unit testing with Jest Install test tooling Bash Copy npm i -D jest ts-jest @types/jest jest-environment-jsdom @testing-library/react@12 @testing-library/jest-dom identity-obj-proxy jest.config.js JavaScript Copy module . exports = { preset : &#x27;ts-jest&#x27; , testEnvironment : &#x27;jsdom&#x27; , roots : [ &#x27;<rootDir>/src&#x27; ], moduleNameMapper : { &#x27;\\\\.(css|scss)$&#x27; : &#x27;identity-obj-proxy&#x27; , &#x27;^@microsoft/sp-(.*)$&#x27; : &#x27;<rootDir>/__mocks__/sp-stub.js&#x27; }, reporters : [ &#x27;default&#x27; ], }; Deviations.test.tsx – component with a mocked service TSX Copy import * as React from &#x27;react&#x27; ; imp"},{"t":"SPFx Performance – Bundle Size, Lazy Loading, Caching and Fast Data Access","c":"spfx","u":"articles/spfx/performance.html","s":"Make SPFx solutions fast, covering analysing bundles, dynamic imports, avoiding duplicate libraries, externalizing dependencies, caching, debouncing, efficient REST and Graph queries, and measuring with the developer dashboard.","k":"spfx, performance, bundle size, lazy loading, caching, webpack","h":"Measure first · Shrink the bundle · Fewer, smarter requests · Render performance · Interview questions · How do you find what makes an SPFx bundle large? · How do dynamic imports help SPFx performance?","l":"Advanced","x":"Quality & Troubleshooting","b":"A modern page loads many components. If yours adds 2 MB of JavaScript and five sequential REST calls, users feel it on every visit. Performance is mostly about shipping less code and making fewer, smarter requests . < 100 KB target gzipped bundle per web part (rule of thumb) 1–2 data requests on first render 0 blocking calls in onInit that aren't needed for first paint # Measure first Bundle analysis : in gulp-based versions, gulp bundle --ship writes stats under temp/stats/ . Open the *.stats.html to see which modules dominate. Page Diagnostics tool (browser extension) for SharePoint flags slow web parts and large requests. Developer dashboard (Ctrl+F12 on modern pages): per-component load and render times. # Shrink the bundle Cheat sheet Bundle diet 01 Import only what you use import { Button } from '@fluentui/react/lib/Button' instead of the whole package root (older Fluent versions). 02 Dynamic imports Load heavy dialogs, charts, editors on demand with import(/* webpackChunkName: 'chart' */ './Chart') . 03 No duplicates Don't bundle a second React, moment + date-fns, two chart libs. Check stats. 04 Replace heavy libs dayjs instead of moment, native Intl instead of i18n libraries. 05 Externalize Very large shared libs can be loaded from a CDN via config.json externals (carefully). 06 Library components Share runtime code across many solutions instead of bundling it in each. Lazy-load a heavy component with React.lazy TSX Copy const ChartPanel = React . lazy ( () => import ( /* webpackChunkName: &#x27;deviation-chart&#x27; */ &#x27;./ChartPanel&#x27; )); export const Dashboard : React . FC < Props > = ( props ) => { const [showChart, setShowChart] = React . useState ( false ); return ( <> < DefaultButton text = \"Show trend\" onClick = {() => setShowChart(true)} /> {showChart && ( < React.Suspense fallback = { < Spinner label = \"Loading chart…\" /> }> < ChartPanel { ...props } /> </ React.Suspense > )} </> ); }; # Fewer, smarter requests Anti-pattern Better $select not used (returns all columns) $select only the fields you render N+1 calls (one per item for user details) $expand=Author or one batch Sequential awaits for independent calls Promise.all([...]) Re-fetching on every render useEffect with proper dependencies; cache Polling every few seconds Fetch on demand, refresh button, or webhooks/SignalR for real-time Search for exact list data Direct list quer"},{"t":"Common SPFx Errors and How to Fix Them (Build, Workbench, Deployment, Runtime)","c":"spfx","u":"articles/spfx/common-errors.html","s":"A troubleshooting catalogue of the SPFx errors developers hit most, from Node and certificate problems to \"web part not found\", API access denied, CORS, 5000-item threshold, stale bundles and Teams issues, each with cause and fix.","k":"spfx, troubleshooting, errors, debugging, deployment, cors","h":"Build & install · Workbench & serve · Deployment · Runtime · Interview questions · Users report \"web part not found\" on pages after a redeploy. What happened? · An SPFx web part gets CORS errors calling an Azure Function. How do you fix it?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Quality & Troubleshooting","b":"flowchart TD E{Where does it fail?} --> B[Build / install] E --> W[Workbench / local serve] E --> D[Deployment / app catalog] E --> R[Runtime on pages] B --> B1[Node version · peer deps · TS errors] W --> W1[Certificate · manifests not loaded] D --> D1[Package errors · API access · versions] R --> R1[403/401 · CORS · threshold · stale cache] # Build & install Error Cause Fix Your dev environment is running NodeJS version vXX which does not meet the requirements Wrong Node nvm use <supported> ; check compatibility matrix npm ERR! ERESOLVE unable to resolve dependency tree Peer dependency conflicts after adding a library Align versions; use the library version that supports your React; --legacy-peer-deps only as a last resort Cannot find module '@pnp/sp/items' / method missing PnPjs selective import missing Add import \"@pnp/sp/items\" TypeScript errors after upgrade Stricter compiler Fix types; don't disable strict Error - [webpack] 'dist': ... size limit warnings Large bundle See Performance # Workbench & serve Error Cause Fix \"Your web part will not appear in the toolbox. Please make sure 'gulp serve' is running\" Serve not running or cert not trusted Start serve, trust dev cert, open https://localhost:4321/temp/manifests.js once NET::ERR_CERT_AUTHORITY_INVALID Dev cert untrusted gulp trust-dev-cert (or equivalent), restart browser Changes not reflected Browser cache / old serve Hard refresh, restart serve # Deployment Error Cause Fix \"Something went wrong… invalid package\" on upload Debug build packaged, corrupted package bundle --ship then package-solution --ship Web part missing in toolbox after deploy App not added to site (not tenant-wide), or wrong supportedHosts Add the app or deploy tenant-wide; check manifest \"Web part not found\" on existing pages Component ID changed or solution removed Restore original ID; redeploy API permission not taking effect Request pending Approve in API access New version not picked up Same version number Increment solution.version # Runtime Symptom Cause Fix 403 from SharePoint REST User lacks permission Check permissions; handle gracefully AADSTS65001 / consent_required Scope not approved Approve in API access CORS error calling Azure Function Origin not allowed Add https://<tenant>.sharepoint.com to Function CORS \"exceeds the list view threshold\" Unindexed filter on large list Index columns, filter on them first, page Old"},{"t":"Localization & Configuration in SPFx – Strings, Tenant Properties and Environment Settings","c":"spfx","u":"articles/spfx/localization-configuration.html","s":"Ship SPFx solutions in multiple languages with loc files, and manage configuration without rebuilding through web part properties, tenant properties (storage entities), configuration lists, and per-environment settings in pipelines.","k":"spfx, localization, configuration, tenant properties, storage entities, environments","h":"Localization · Configuration without rebuilds · Interview questions · How do you change an API URL between test and production without separate code? · How does SPFx pick the language file?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Quality & Troubleshooting","b":"# Localization Every web part has a loc folder with one JavaScript file per language and a TypeScript declaration: loc/mystrings.d.ts TypeScript Copy declare interface IDeviationsWebPartStrings { Title : string ; NoItems : string ; ItemsCount : string ; // \"{0} open deviations\" } declare module &#x27;DeviationsWebPartStrings&#x27; { const strings : IDeviationsWebPartStrings ; export = strings; } loc/en-us.js and loc/de-de.js JavaScript Copy define ([], function ( ) { return { Title : \"Deviations\" , NoItems : \"Nothing open 🎉\" , ItemsCount : \"{0} open deviations\" }; }); define ([], function ( ) { return { Title : \"Abweichungen\" , NoItems : \"Nichts offen 🎉\" , ItemsCount : \"{0} offene Abweichungen\" }; }); Using strings TypeScript Copy import * as strings from &#x27;DeviationsWebPartStrings&#x27; ; const label = strings. ItemsCount . replace ( &#x27;{0}&#x27; , String (items. length )); SharePoint loads the file matching the site/user UI language . Test with ?locale=de-de on the workbench URL (local serve supports a --locale flag in gulp-based versions). Also translate the manifest's title , description and group per locale. # Configuration without rebuilds Configuration options PER INSTANCE Web part properties (property pane) Page authors decide per tenant / app TENANT PROPERTIES App catalog storage entities Set-PnPStorageEntity / m365 spo storageentity set Read: /_api/web/GetStorageEntity('… per site / business data CONFIGURATION LIST SharePoint list in a central site Editable by business admins Cached in session per environment BUILD-TIME Pipeline variables replacing tokens Separate packages for test/prod only if… Configuration options Set a tenant property (app catalog storage entity) PowerShell Copy Connect-PnPOnline -Url https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/appcatalog -Interactive -ClientId $clientId Set-PnPStorageEntity -Key \"DeviationApiUrl\" -Value \"https://fn-deviation-prod.azurewebsites.net/api\" -Description \"Deviation API base URL\" Read it in SPFx TypeScript Copy const res = await this . context . spHttpClient . get ( ` ${ this .context.pageContext.web.absoluteUrl} /_api/web/GetStorageEntity(&#x27;DeviationApiUrl&#x27;)` , SPHttpClient . configurations . v1 ); const apiUrl : string = ( await res. json ()). Value ; Tenant properties are readable by everyone Any authenticated user can read storage entities. Store URLs and feature flags , never secrets."},{"t":"What is Power Apps? Canvas, Model-Driven, Power Pages and How They Fit Together","c":"power-apps","u":"articles/power-apps/overview.html","s":"Power Apps explained for SharePoint and Microsoft 365 professionals, covering the app types, environments, data sources and connectors, licensing basics, the maker-to-pro-dev spectrum, and where Power Apps fits next to SPFx.","k":"power apps, canvas app, model-driven, power pages, low-code, power platform","h":"The three app types · How a canvas app talks to data · Where Power Apps fits vs SPFx · Learning path in this tab · Interview questions · What is the main difference between canvas and model-driven apps? · What is an environment in Power Platform? · Why does connector classification matter?","l":"Beginner","x":"Fundamentals","b":"Power Apps is Microsoft's low-code platform for building business applications. It sits in the Power Platform with Power Automate, Power BI, Copilot Studio and Power Pages, and runs on top of connectors (1,000+) and Dataverse . # The three app types Power Apps family CANVAS APPS Pixel-level UI design Power Fx formulas Any connector (SharePoint,… Phones tablets Teams SharePoint forms share data with MODEL-DRIVEN APPS Generated from Dataverse tables Forms views dashboards Business process flows & securit… external audiences POWER PAGES Websites for external users Dataverse back end Authentication providers (Entra External ID, local) all live in ENVIRONMENTS Dev / Test / Prod Solutions for ALM DLP policies & managed environments Power Apps family Canvas app Model-driven app Power Pages UI You design every screen Generated from data model Web pages and templates Data Any connector Dataverse only Dataverse Logic Power Fx formulas Business rules, flows, plug-ins Liquid, JS, Web API Best for Task-focused, mobile, SharePoint-based apps Process-heavy, data-rich back-office Partner and customer portals SharePoint list form ✅ ❌ ❌ # How a canvas app talks to data flowchart LR U[User on phone / browser / Teams] --> A[Canvas app<br/>Power Fx] A -->|delegable queries| C1[SharePoint connector] A --> C2[Dataverse] A --> C3[SQL Server / Azure SQL] A --> C4[Office 365 Users, Outlook] A --> C5[Custom connector → your API] C3 -.on-prem.-> GW[On-premises data gateway] Connectors are either standard (SharePoint, Outlook, Teams, OneDrive) or premium (Dataverse, SQL, HTTP, custom connectors). Premium connectors require Power Apps premium licensing for each user, so check the licensing guide before designing. # Where Power Apps fits vs SPFx Choose Power Apps when… Choose SPFx when… Makers or citizen developers will own and change it Developers own it with Git and CI/CD Forms, approvals, data entry, mobile/offline Pixel-perfect intranet UI, high performance on pages Many connectors are needed quickly Custom logic, Graph-heavy, deep SharePoint integration The audience is licensed appropriately Large audiences without extra licences They work together: SPFx pages can embed Power Apps, and Power Apps can call APIs that SPFx also uses. # Learning path in this tab Power Fx cheat sheet Formulas you will use every day. CRUD on SharePoint Patch, forms, attachments and people fields. Delegatio"},{"t":"Power Fx Cheat Sheet – The Formulas You Use Every Day","c":"power-apps","u":"articles/power-apps/power-fx-cheatsheet.html","s":"A practical Power Fx reference grouped by task, covering variables, collections, filtering and lookup, Patch and forms, text, dates, tables (ForAll, AddColumns, GroupBy), navigation, error handling, user info and behaviour formulas.","k":"power fx, formulas, cheat sheet, canvas app, patch, filter, lookup","h":"Variables & collections · Read: Filter, LookUp, Search, Sort · Write: Patch, forms, Remove · Text & numbers · Dates · Table shaping · Users, environment and navigation · Error handling · Interview questions · What is the difference between Set and UpdateContext? · Why prefer Patch with a table over ForAll + Patch? · How do you set a SharePoint Person column with Patch?","l":"Beginner","x":"Fundamentals","b":"Power Fx is the Excel-inspired, declarative language of Power Apps (and Copilot Studio, Dataverse formula columns and more). Formulas recalculate automatically when their inputs change. Behaviour properties ( OnSelect , OnStart , OnVisible ) run actions. Cheat sheet Power Fx mental model 01 Declarative Label.Text = \"Hi \" & User().FullName updates automatically, so there is no \"set label\" call. 02 Behaviour Buttons run actions: Patch , Navigate , Set , Collect chained with ; . 03 Tables everywhere Data sources, collections and galleries are tables. Use Filter , Sort , AddColumns . 04 Records { Title: \"A\", Status: \"Open\" } is a record; ThisItem is the current gallery record. 05 Delegation Some functions run on the server, others only on downloaded rows. See the delegation article. 06 Errors are values Use IfError , IsError , Errors(DataSource) and App.OnError . # Variables & collections Variables Power Fx Copy Set(varUser, User()); // global variable UpdateContext({ locShowPan el: true }); // screen-scoped (context) variable Navigate(DetailsScreen, ScreenTransition.Fade, { loc Id: ThisItem.ID }); // pass context to the next screen ClearCollect(colOpen, Filter (Deviations, Status.Value = \"Open\" )); // build a local table Collect(colCart, { Produ ct: \"Badge\" , Q ty: 1 }); Remove(colCart, LookUp (colCart, Product = \"Badge\" )); Clear(colCart); # Read: Filter, LookUp, Search, Sort Querying Power Fx Copy Filter (Deviations, Status.Value = \"Open\" && Severity.Value = \"Critical\" ) LookUp (Employees, Email = User().Email) // first matching record LookUp (Employees, Email = User().Email, Department) // a single field Search (Deviations, txtSearch.Text, \"Title\" ) // contains, NOT delegable on SharePoint StartsWith(Title, txtSearch.Text) // delegable alternative SortByColumns( Filter (Deviations, Owner.Email = User().Email), \"Modified\" , SortOrder.Descending) CountRows( Filter (Deviations, Status.Value = \"Open\" )) FirstN( Sort (Deviations, Created, SortOrder.Descending), 5 ) # Write: Patch, forms, Remove Create / update / delete Power Fx Copy // Create Patch(Deviations, Defaults(Deviations), { Tit le: txtTitle.Text, Severi ty: { Val ue: ddSeverity.Selected.Value }, // Choice column Own er: { Clai ms: \"i:0#.f|membership|\" & cbOwner.Selected.Mail, // Person column (SharePoint) DisplayNa me: cbOwner.Selected.DisplayName, Ema il: cbOwner.Selected.Mail, Departme nt: \"\" , JobTit"},{"t":"Canvas vs Model-Driven vs SPFx vs Power Pages – A Decision Guide","c":"power-apps","u":"articles/power-apps/canvas-vs-model-driven.html","s":"Choose the right app technology with a decision tree and comparison matrix covering audience, data source, UI control, offline, licensing, ALM and performance, with typical scenarios mapped to recommendations.","k":"canvas app, model-driven app, spfx, power pages, architecture, decision","h":"Decision tree · Comparison matrix · Typical scenarios · Interview questions · When is a model-driven app a better choice than a canvas app? · Can you combine app types?","l":"Beginner","x":"Fundamentals","b":"Most project failures with Power Platform start with the wrong app type. Use this guide before you build. # Decision tree flowchart TD A{Who are the users?} -->|External: customers, partners| PP[Power Pages] A -->|Internal employees| B{Where does the data live?} B -->|SharePoint lists,<br/>mixed connectors| C{Need pixel control,<br/>mobile, forms?} B -->|Dataverse, complex<br/>relational + security| D{Process / back-office<br/>with many tables?} C -->|yes| CA[Canvas app] C -->|intranet UI in pages,<br/>developer-owned| SPFX[SPFx] D -->|yes| MDA[Model-driven app<br/>+ custom pages] D -->|no, task-focused UI| CA2[Canvas app on Dataverse] # Comparison matrix Criteria Canvas Model-driven SPFx Power Pages Builder Maker / pro maker Maker / functional consultant Developer Maker + web dev UI flexibility ★★★★★ ★★ (consistent UI) ★★★★★ ★★★★ Data sources Any connector Dataverse Any API Dataverse Complex security (row/field) Depends on source ★★★★★ (Dataverse) Depends on API ★★★★ (table permissions) Offline / mobile ★★★★ ★★★ ★ ★ Business process Flows ★★★★★ (BPF, rules) Custom Flows ALM Solutions Solutions Git + CI/CD Solutions + PAC CLI Licensing for users Per app/user (premium if premium connectors) Premium M365 only Per site capacity Performance at scale Watch delegation Good Best Good # Typical scenarios Scenario Recommendation Leave/expense request with SharePoint list + approval Canvas app (or customized list form) + Power Automate Inventory management with items, warehouses, movements, audit Model-driven app on Dataverse Intranet dashboard on the home page SPFx web part Supplier onboarding portal for external vendors Power Pages Field inspection app with photos, offline Canvas app on Dataverse (offline) Complex form replacing InfoPath Canvas (SharePoint form or standalone) or SPFx form customizer # Interview questions Q1 When is a model-driven app a better choice than a canvas app? When the solution is data-heavy and process-driven on Dataverse (many related tables, role-based security, business process flows, auditing) and a consistent, responsive UI is acceptable. You configure the data model instead of building screens. Q2 Can you combine app types? Yes. Model-driven apps can include custom pages (canvas technology), canvas apps can be embedded in SharePoint and Teams, and SPFx pages can embed Power Apps. Choose per user journey, not per project."},{"t":"Environments, Connectors, Data Gateways & Licensing Basics","c":"power-apps","u":"articles/power-apps/environments-connectors-licensing.html","s":"How Power Platform environments, environment types and regions work, standard vs premium vs custom connectors, connection references, the on-premises data gateway, DLP policies, and a practical overview of licensing models.","k":"environments, connectors, data gateway, licensing, dlp, managed environments","h":"Environments · Connectors · On-premises data gateway · DLP policies · Licensing overview (check current terms) · Interview questions · Why shouldn't business apps live in the default environment? · What does the on-premises data gateway do? · What is a connection reference?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Fundamentals","b":"# Environments A typical environment strategy DEFAULT ENVIRONMENT Everyone is a maker Personal productivity only Strict DLP separate from DEVELOPER / SANDBOX Per developer or team Unmanaged solutions Experiments solution export / pipelines TEST (SANDBOX) Managed solutions UAT users Copy of prod config approved release PRODUCTION Managed solutions only Managed environment features Restricted makers A typical environment strategy Environment type Use Default Every tenant has one; limit to personal productivity apps Sandbox Dev/test; can be reset and copied Production Live apps; backups and full support Developer Free individual environment for learning and dev Dataverse for Teams Lightweight environment inside a team Trial Temporary evaluation Managed Environments add governance features such as sharing limits, weekly usage digests, solution checker enforcement, IP firewall and maker welcome content. # Connectors Type Examples Licence implication Standard SharePoint, Outlook, Teams, OneDrive, Excel Online, Approvals Included with Microsoft 365 Power Apps use rights Premium Dataverse (outside Teams), SQL Server, HTTP, Azure services, Salesforce Requires Power Apps premium licence for users Custom Your OpenAPI-described APIs Premium On-premises via gateway SQL Server, file system, SharePoint Server Gateway required; usually premium Connection references (in solutions) decouple flows and apps from a specific user's connection, so you can map them per environment during deployment. # On-premises data gateway flowchart LR APP[Power Apps / Power Automate<br/>/ Power BI in the cloud] --> AZ[Azure Relay<br/>gateway cloud service] AZ <-->|outbound HTTPS only| GW[Gateway service<br/>on a Windows server<br/>in your network] GW --> SQL[(SQL Server)] GW --> FS[File shares] GW --> SPS[SharePoint Server] The gateway opens outbound connections only, so no inbound firewall ports are needed. Install it on an always-on server (not a laptop), configure a cluster of two or more gateways for high availability, and keep it updated monthly. # DLP policies Data Loss Prevention policies classify connectors as Business , Non-business or Blocked per environment. An app or flow cannot combine Business and Non-business connectors. For example, you can stop someone wiring SharePoint data to a personal Dropbox. # Licensing overview (check current terms) Model What it covers Microsoft 365 / O"},{"t":"Canvas App Architecture – Screens, Controls, Data Flow and App Lifecycle Events","c":"power-apps","u":"articles/power-apps/canvas-architecture.html","s":"How a canvas app is structured and executed, covering the player, App.OnStart vs App.Formulas vs StartScreen, screen lifecycle, controls and properties, data sources and caching, and a reference architecture for a SharePoint-backed request app.","k":"canvas app, architecture, onstart, app formulas, screens, performance","h":"Runtime architecture · Startup: OnStart vs Formulas vs StartScreen · Reference architecture: SharePoint request app · Interview questions · Why are named formulas (App.Formulas) preferred over variables set in OnStart? · What does Concurrent() do?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Canvas Apps","b":"A canvas app is a set of screens containing controls whose properties are formulas. The Power Apps player (browser, mobile app, Teams or SharePoint embed) loads the app package, evaluates formulas and talks to connectors . # Runtime architecture Canvas app at runtime PLAYER Web browser / Power Apps mobile / Teams / SharePoint… Downloads .msapp package evaluates APP OBJECT App.Formulas (named formulas) App.OnStart (legacy init) App.StartScreen App.OnError renders SCREENS & CONTROLS OnVisible / OnHidden Galleries forms inputs Property formulas… through CONNECTORS Delegated queries (server-side) Non-delegable ops (client-side, row limit) API Hub / gateway data SOURCES SharePoint lists Dataverse SQL Office 365 Users Custom APIs Canvas app at runtime # Startup: OnStart vs Formulas vs StartScreen sequenceDiagram participant P as Player participant A as App participant S as First screen P->>A: Load app A->>A: App.Formulas evaluated lazily when used (no blocking) A->>A: App.OnStart runs (blocking if Delay OnStart = off) A->>A: App.StartScreen decides first screen (deep links) A->>S: Screen.OnVisible S-->>P: Render Property Use for Tip App.Formulas Named formulas: fxUser = User(); fxIsAdmin = ...; Lazy, cached, auto-updating; preferred over OnStart variables App.OnStart Imperative init: ClearCollect reference data Keep it short; it delays the first screen App.StartScreen Choose the first screen by Param(\"screen\") Replaces Navigate in OnStart Screen.OnVisible Load screen-specific data Use Concurrent() for parallel loads App.OnError Global error handling and logging Combine with IfError locally App.Formulas (named formulas) Power Fx Copy fxUserEmail = Lower (User().Email); fxIsQualityLead = ! IsBlank ( LookUp (QualityLeads, Lower (Email) = fxUserEmail)); fxOpenCount = CountRows( Filter (Deviations, Status.Value <> \"Closed\" )); Screen.OnVisible – parallel loading Power Fx Copy Concurrent( ClearCollect(colAreas, Areas ), ClearCollect(colMyOpen, Filter (Deviations, Owner.Email = fxUserEmail && Status.Value <> \"Closed\" )), Set(varProfile, Office365Users.MyProfileV2()) ) # Reference architecture: SharePoint request app Deviation reporting app (canvas + SharePoint + Power Automate) CANVAS APP Home: my items (gallery) New/Edit form Approver screen Offline-friendly local cache standard connectors SHAREPOINT SITE Deviations list (indexed: Status, Owner, Created) Areas & Approve"},{"t":"Canvas App CRUD with SharePoint – Galleries, Forms, Patch, People, Lookups & Attachments","c":"power-apps","u":"articles/power-apps/canvas-sharepoint-crud.html","s":"Build a complete create, read, update and delete app on a SharePoint list, covering gallery with delegable filters, edit form vs Patch, choice, person, lookup and managed metadata columns, attachments and document library uploads, plus validation and error handling.","k":"canvas app, sharepoint, crud, patch, gallery, forms, attachments, person column","h":"READ: a delegable gallery · CREATE & UPDATE: two approaches · A) Edit form (fast to build) · B) Patch (full control, custom UI) · Attachments and documents · DELETE · Interview questions · When would you use Patch instead of an edit form? · How do you set a lookup column with Patch? · Why is Search() a problem with large SharePoint lists?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Canvas Apps","b":"The most common Power Apps scenario is a SharePoint list behind a canvas app . Here is a complete, production-minded pattern for a Deviations list with Title, Area (choice), Severity (choice), Owner (person), Plant (lookup), DueDate (date), Description (multi-line) and attachments. flowchart LR H[Home screen<br/>gallery + search + filters] -->|select| D[Detail screen<br/>view form] H -->|+ New| E[Edit screen<br/>form or Patch] D -->|Edit| E E -->|Save → Patch / SubmitForm| L[(SharePoint list)] D -->|Delete → Remove| L H -->|delegable Filter| L # READ: a delegable gallery galDeviations.Items Power Fx Copy SortByColumns( Filter ( Deviations, (ddStatus.Selected.Value = \"All\" || Status.Value = ddStatus.Selected.Value), ( IsBlank (txtSearch.Text) || StartsWith(Title, txtSearch.Text)), // StartsWith is delegable, Search is not Owner.Email = fxUserEmail || chkAll.Value ), \"Created\" , SortOrder.Descending ) Delegation rules matter: Filter on text with = / StartsWith , choice .Value , numbers, dates and person .Email is delegable to SharePoint. Search , in (substring) and many functions are not. See Delegation . # CREATE & UPDATE: two approaches # A) Edit form (fast to build) Form & buttons Power Fx Copy frmDeviation.DataSource = Deviations frmDeviation.Item = galDeviations.Selected // ignored in New mode btnNew.OnSelect = NewForm(frmDeviation); Navigate(EditScreen) btnEdit.OnSelect = EditForm(frmDeviation); Navigate(EditScreen) btnSave.OnSelect = SubmitForm(frmDeviation) frmDeviation.OnSuccess = Notify( \"Saved\" , NotificationType.Success); Back() frmDeviation.OnFailure = Notify( \"Save failed: \" & frmDeviation.Error, NotificationType.Error) # B) Patch (full control, custom UI) btnSave.OnSelect with validation Power Fx Copy If ( Len ( Trim (txtTitle.Text)) < 5 , Notify( \"Title must have at least 5 characters\" , NotificationType.Warning), IsBlank (ddArea.Selected), Notify( \"Choose an area\" , NotificationType.Warning), // el se: save IfError ( Set(varSaved, Patch( Deviations, If ( IsBlank (locRecord), Defaults(Deviations), locRecord), // create or update { Tit le: Trim (txtTitle.Text), Ar ea: { Val ue: ddArea.Selected.Value }, // Choice Severi ty: { Val ue: rdSeverity.Selected.Value }, Own er: { // Person Clai ms: \"i:0#.f|membership|\" & Lower (cbOwner.Selected.Mail), DisplayNa me: cbOwner.Selected.DisplayName, Ema il: cbOwner.Selected.Mail, Departme nt: \"\" , JobTit le: "},{"t":"Canvas App CRUD with Dataverse – Relationships, Choices, Owners and Bulk Updates","c":"power-apps","u":"articles/power-apps/canvas-dataverse-crud.html","s":"Working with Dataverse in canvas apps, covering table and column naming, lookups and polymorphic lookups, choices, the Owner field, related records with Relate/Unrelate, delegation advantages, bulk Patch, and security roles.","k":"canvas app, dataverse, crud, patch, relationships, choices, relate","h":"Read with rich delegation · Create with lookups, choices and owner · Polymorphic lookups (Customer, Regarding, Owner) · One-to-many and many-to-many · Bulk update · Security is enforced by Dataverse · Interview questions · Why is Dataverse often better than SharePoint for canvas apps? · How do you set a lookup column in Dataverse with Patch? · What do Relate and Unrelate do?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Canvas Apps","b":"Dataverse is the best data source for canvas apps: rich delegation, real relationships, row-level security and no list thresholds. The formulas look similar to SharePoint, but a few patterns differ. Canvas app over Dataverse CANVAS APP Galleries & forms Power Fx (Patch, Relate) Respects user's security role Dataverse connector (premium) DATAVERSE Deviation (custom table) Plant (lookup) Account / Contact Choices: Severity Status server-side LOGIC Business rules Calculated & formula columns Plug-ins / flows Auditing Canvas app over Dataverse # Read with rich delegation Delegable even with 1M+ rows Power Fx Copy Filter ( Deviations, Severity = &#x27;Severity (Deviations)&#x27;.Critical, // choice comparison uses the enum &#x27;Due Date &#x27; < Today (), Plant.Name = \"Bangalore\" , // filter through lookup (delegable) StartsWith(&#x27;Deviation Name&#x27;, txtSearch.Text) ) Dataverse delegates far more than SharePoint: in on text, CountRows , Sum , Average , filtering through lookups, Sort on most columns, and Search (on Dataverse, with search configured). # Create with lookups, choices and owner Patch a new deviation Power Fx Copy Patch( Deviations, Defaults(Deviations), { &#x27;Deviation Name&#x27; : txtTitle.Text, Severi ty: &#x27;Severity (Deviations)&#x27;.Major, // choice Pla nt: ddPlant.Selected, // lookup = the related record &#x27;Due Date &#x27; : dpDue.SelectedDate, Own er: LookUp (Users, &#x27;Primary Email&#x27; = cbOwner.Selected.Mail) // assign owner (needs privileges) } ) Display names vs logical names Power Fx uses display names ( 'Due Date' ) with single quotes when names contain spaces. Logical names ( cr8a1_duedate ) also work. Pick one style consistently. # Polymorphic lookups (Customer, Regarding, Owner) Set a Customer (Account OR Contact) lookup Power Fx Copy Patch(Cases, Defaults(Cases), { Tit le: \"Leak at Plant A\" , Custom er: LookUp (Accounts, &#x27;Account Name&#x27; = \"Contoso Ltd\" ) }) // Readi ng: check the type with IsType / AsType If (IsType(ThisItem.Customer, Accounts), AsType(ThisItem.Customer, Accounts).&#x27;Account Name&#x27;, AsType(ThisItem.Customer, Contacts).&#x27;Full Name&#x27;) # One-to-many and many-to-many Relate / Unrelate (N:N or 1:N) Power Fx Copy // Add a CAPA action to a deviation ( 1:N relationship &#x27;CAPA Actions&#x27;) Relate(galDeviations.Selected.&#x27;CAPA Actions&#x27;, galActions.Selected); // Remove "},{"t":"Delegation in Power Apps – The 500/2000 Row Limit Explained (and How to Beat It)","c":"power-apps","u":"articles/power-apps/delegation.html","s":"What delegation is, why non-delegable formulas silently return wrong results, delegable functions per data source (SharePoint, Dataverse, SQL), the data row limit, warning signs, and proven patterns to build apps over large lists.","k":"delegation, power apps, performance, sharepoint, dataverse, row limit","h":"Delegable vs non-delegable · Delegable functions by source (common cases) · Patterns that work at scale · Interview questions · What happens when a formula isn't delegable? · How would you implement search over a 100,000-item SharePoint list? · Is CountRows delegable on SharePoint?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Canvas Apps","b":"Delegation means Power Apps asks the data source to do the work (filter, sort, count) and only downloads the matching rows. When a formula is not delegable , Power Apps downloads only the first N rows (the data row limit : 500 by default, up to 2,000) and processes them locally. Anything beyond N is silently ignored . 500 default data row limit (non-delegable queries) 2,000 maximum configurable limit 0 errors shown to users when results are incomplete # Delegable vs non-delegable flowchart LR subgraph D[\"Delegable ✅\"] A1[App: Filter Status = 'Open'] -->|OData $filter| S1[(SharePoint<br/>50,000 rows)] S1 -->|returns 3,400 matches<br/>paged as you scroll| A2[Gallery shows all matches] end subgraph N[\"Not delegable ⚠\"] B1[App: Search Title contains 'pump'] -->|download first 500 rows only| S2[(SharePoint<br/>50,000 rows)] B1 --> B2[Searches 500 rows locally<br/>→ misses 49,500] end # Delegable functions by source (common cases) Function / operator SharePoint Dataverse SQL Server Filter with = , <> , < , > , && , ` ` ✅ (most column types) StartsWith ✅ ✅ ✅ Search , in (substring) ❌ ✅ ( in ) ✅ Sort / SortByColumns ✅ (single column, not complex types) ✅ ✅ LookUp ✅ ✅ ✅ CountRows , Sum , Average , Min , Max ❌ (counts are limited) ✅ ✅ Lower , Upper , Len , Left , Year inside filter ❌ ❌ (mostly) ❌ Filter on Person .Email , Choice .Value , Lookup .Id ✅ ✅ (lookups) n/a AddColumns , GroupBy , Distinct , ForAll ❌ ❌ (Distinct partially) ❌ Always look for the blue underline and delegation warning in Power Apps Studio. It is the only signal you get. # Patterns that work at scale Cheat sheet Large-data patterns 01 Filter first, shape later Do the delegable Filter on the source, then AddColumns/GroupBy on the (small) result. 02 StartsWith over Search For SharePoint, use StartsWith on indexed text columns, or a \"SearchKey\" column populated by a flow. 03 Index + narrow Index filter columns in SharePoint; always filter by owner, status or date range. 04 Pre-compute Use calculated/rollup columns (Dataverse) or flows to maintain counts and totals. 05 Views & Dataverse Move to Dataverse when you need complex queries, aggregates or relational filters. 06 Server-side search Call a flow or API (Graph search, SQL stored procedure) and return a result table. 07 Paging UX Galleries load in pages as users scroll (delegated), so don't ClearCollect everything. Bad vs good Power Fx Copy // ❌ N"},{"t":"Variables, Collections & State in Canvas Apps – When to Use Which","c":"power-apps","u":"articles/power-apps/variables-collections.html","s":"Global variables, context variables, named formulas, collections and component properties compared, with patterns for app state, navigation parameters, shopping-cart style editing, caching and avoiding stale data.","k":"power apps, variables, collections, state, named formulas, set, updatecontext","h":"The options · Pattern: edit a batch, then save once · Common mistakes · Interview questions · What is the difference between a variable and a named formula? · When should you use a collection?","l":"Beginner","x":"Canvas Apps","b":"# The options Kind Create with Scope Typical use Named formula App.Formulas: fxX = …; App Derived values (current user, permissions) that should stay up to date Global variable Set(varX, …) App App state: selected record, mode flags Context variable UpdateContext({ locX: … }) Screen Screen UI state: dialog open, tab selected Navigation params Navigate(Scr, None, { locId: 5 }) Target screen Passing values to a screen Collection ClearCollect(colX, …) App Small local tables: cart, edits, offline cache Component properties Custom input/output properties Component Reusable controls' API flowchart TD Q{What do you need?} -->|Value derived from other data| NF[Named formula] Q -->|Remember something app-wide| GV[Set global variable] Q -->|UI state on one screen| CV[UpdateContext] Q -->|Edit many rows before saving| COL[Collection → Patch table] Q -->|Data from source to display| DS[Bind directly to the data source<br/>no copy needed] # Pattern: edit a batch, then save once Shopping-cart style editing Power Fx Copy // Load the rows to edit ( small , filtered) ClearCollect(colEdit, ShowColumns( Filter (Inspections, Batch = locBatch), \"ID\" , \"Result\" , \"Notes\" )); // In the galle ry: update a row locally Patch(colEdit, ThisItem, { Resu lt: ddResult.Selected.Value }); // Save everything in one batched call Patch(Inspections, colEdit); Notify( \"Saved \" & CountRows(colEdit) & \" results\" , NotificationType.Success) # Common mistakes Copying whole lists into collections to \"make them faster\". This hits row limits and shows stale data. Bind galleries directly to the source with delegable filters. Global variables for everything . Use context variables for screen-only state to avoid side effects. Stale variables . A variable set in OnStart does not update when data changes, so use named formulas or re-query. Record vs table confusion . galX.Selected is a record, while Filter(...) returns a table. First() converts a table to a record. # Interview questions Q1 What is the difference between a variable and a named formula? A variable stores a value at the time Set runs and changes only when you set it again. A named formula is a declaration that Power Apps keeps up to date automatically whenever its dependencies change, and it is evaluated only when used. Q2 When should you use a collection? For small, local tables: temporary edits before a batch save, an offline cache with Save"},{"t":"Canvas Components & Component Libraries – Reusable Headers, Dialogs and Controls","c":"power-apps","u":"articles/power-apps/components.html","s":"Build reusable canvas components with custom input, output, event and function properties, share them through component libraries, version and update them, and design a small internal design system.","k":"canvas components, component library, reuse, design system, custom properties","h":"Component anatomy · Component libraries · Design system tips · Interview questions · What is the difference between an output property and an event property? · How do component library updates reach apps?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Canvas Apps","b":"Copy-pasting the same header, dialog or navigation into every screen and app is the fastest route to inconsistent apps. Canvas components package UI and behaviour with a clean property interface . Component libraries share them across apps with versioned updates. # Component anatomy A reusable confirm dialog component INPUT PROPERTIES Title (Text) Message (Text) Visible (Boolean) ConfirmText (Text) used by INSIDE THE COMPONENT Container + labels + buttons Theme from input record raises OUTPUT & EVENTS OnConfirm (behaviour/event) OnCancel (event) Result (output Text) A reusable confirm dialog component Property kind Direction Example Input (data) App → component Title , Items , Theme Output (data) Component → app SelectedValue , IsValid Event (behaviour) Component raises, app handles OnConfirm , OnSelectItem Function (behaviour/output with parameters) App calls into component Format(value) Using the dialog in a screen Power Fx Copy // Delete button UpdateContext({ locConfi rm: true }) // cmpConfirm properties cmpConfirm.Visible = locConfirm cmpConfirm.Title = \"Delete deviation?\" cmpConfirm.Message = \"This moves &#x27;\" & galDeviations.Selected.Title & \"&#x27; to the recycle bin.\" cmpConfirm.OnConfirm = Remove(Deviations, galDeviations.Selected); UpdateContext({ locConfi rm: false }) cmpConfirm.OnCancel = UpdateContext({ locConfi rm: false }) # Component libraries flowchart LR LIB[Component library app<br/>Header · Dialog · NavRail · Toast] -->|publish v3| ENV[(Environment)] ENV -->|\"Update available\" prompt| A1[App A] ENV --> A2[App B] ENV --> A3[App C] Create a component library (Apps → Component libraries), build components, publish . Apps import components from the library. When a new version is published, makers see Updates available when editing an app. Ship libraries with solutions to move them between environments. # Design system tips A Theme input property (record of colours and fonts) passed to every component makes rebranding a one-line change. Keep components stateless where possible: the app owns data, the component renders and raises events. Document each component's properties in a description and in a \"storybook\" screen in the library. Avoid accessing global variables or data sources inside components unless the \"Access app scope\" setting is intended. # Interview questions Q1 What is the difference between an output property and an event prope"},{"t":"Responsive Canvas Apps with Containers – Phone, Tablet and Desktop from One App","c":"power-apps","u":"articles/power-apps/responsive-layout.html","s":"Build responsive layouts with horizontal and vertical containers, flexible width and height, breakpoints with Parent.Width and App.Size, screen templates, and modern controls, plus a layout recipe for a list/detail screen.","k":"responsive, containers, layout, canvas app, modern controls, ux","h":"Layout building blocks · Practical rules · Interview questions · How do you make a canvas app responsive? · What does App.SizeBreakpoints control?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Canvas Apps","b":"Canvas apps started as fixed-size phone or tablet layouts. With auto-layout containers (horizontal and vertical), Scale to fit off, and breakpoints, one app can adapt like a web page. # Layout building blocks Responsive screen skeleton SCREEN Scale to fit = Off Lock aspect ratio = Off contains VERTICAL CONTAINER (ROOT) Header (fixed height 64) Body (Fill portions = 1) Footer (fixed, optional) body is HORIZONTAL CONTAINER Left: list panel (min 320, hide on phone) Right: details panel (fill) Wrap = true for phones Responsive screen skeleton Breakpoints Power Fx Copy // App.SizeBreakpoints = [ 600 , 900 , 1200 ] → App.Si ze: 1 (phone) .. 4 ( large ) conLeftPanel.Visible = App.Size >= ScreenSize.Medium || IsBlank (locSelected) conRightPanel.Visible = App.Size >= ScreenSize.Medium || ! IsBlank (locSelected) conLeftPanel.Width = If (App.Size >= ScreenSize.Medium, 360 , Parent.Width) lblTitle.Size = If (App.Size = ScreenSize.Small, 18 , 24 ) Header · logo · search · user List panel (360px) Details panel (fills) Desktop / tablet: list and details side by side Header List (full width) → tap → Details replaces list Phone: one panel at a time # Practical rules Turn off Scale to fit and Lock aspect ratio in Settings → Display. Use containers everywhere , and avoid absolute X/Y positions except inside small groups. Use Fill portions for flexible children and fixed sizes for headers and toolbars. Prefer modern controls (Fluent-based) for consistent theming and accessibility. Test in Studio with different preview sizes and on a real phone. # Interview questions Q1 How do you make a canvas app responsive? Disable scale-to-fit, build screens from nested horizontal and vertical containers with flexible sizing (fill portions, min and max widths), and adapt visibility and sizes with breakpoints ( App.Size , Parent.Width ) for phone, tablet and desktop. Q2 What does App.SizeBreakpoints control? It defines the width thresholds that map the screen width to App.Size values (Small, Medium, Large, ExtraLarge), which formulas use to switch layouts."},{"t":"Customizing SharePoint List Forms with Power Apps (SharePointIntegration Deep Dive)","c":"power-apps","u":"articles/power-apps/sharepoint-list-forms.html","s":"Replace a SharePoint list's default form with a canvas app, covering how SharePointIntegration works (OnNew, OnEdit, OnView, OnSave, OnCancel), cascading dropdowns, conditional sections, multi-screen forms, attachments, performance, and when to revert or choose an alternative.","k":"power apps, sharepoint list form, sharepointintegration, customized form, infopath replacement","h":"How the integration works · Recipe: cascading dropdowns and conditional sections · Recipe: validate before save, multi-screen form · Performance tips · Limitations and alternatives · Interview questions · What does SharePointIntegration.OnSave do? · How do you close the form panel after saving? · How do you build a multi-screen customized list form?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Canvas Apps","b":"From a SharePoint list choose Integrate → Power Apps → Customize forms . SharePoint creates a special canvas app whose SharePointIntegration control connects the list's New/Edit/Display actions to your form. It is the most common InfoPath replacement for list forms. # How the integration works sequenceDiagram actor U as User participant L as SharePoint list participant SI as SharePointIntegration (in the app) participant F as SharePointForm1 U->>L: Click \"+ New\" L->>SI: OnNew SI->>F: NewForm(SharePointForm1) U->>L: Click \"Save\" (SharePoint panel) L->>SI: OnSave SI->>F: SubmitForm(SharePointForm1) F-->>SI: OnSuccess → RequestHide() SI-->>L: Panel closes, view refreshes U->>L: Open existing item L->>SI: OnView (Selected = the item) SI->>F: ViewForm(SharePointForm1) SharePointIntegration property Default formula Customize to… OnNew NewForm(SharePointForm1) Reset variables, set default screen OnEdit EditForm(SharePointForm1) Navigate to an edit screen OnView ViewForm(SharePointForm1) Show a read-only summary screen OnSave SubmitForm(SharePointForm1) Validate first, submit several forms, or Patch OnCancel ResetForm(SharePointForm1) Clear variables Selected (the current item) Use in Item properties # Recipe: cascading dropdowns and conditional sections Cascading Area → Equipment Power Fx Copy ddArea.Items = Choices(Deviations.Area) ddEquipment.Items = Filter (Equipment, Area.Value = ddArea.Selected.Value) // Equipment list (indexed Area) // Show the &#x27;Impact&#x27; section only for Major/Critical DataCard_Impact.Visible = ddSeverity.Selected.Value in [ \"Major\" , \"Critical\" ] # Recipe: validate before save, multi-screen form SharePointIntegration.OnSave Power Fx Copy If ( !SharePointForm1.Valid, Notify( \"Please complete the required fields\" , NotificationType.Warning), ddSeverity.Selected.Value = \"Critical\" && IsBlank (dpDue.SelectedDate), Notify( \"Critical deviations need a due date\" , NotificationType.Warning), SubmitForm(SharePointForm1) ) // SharePointForm1.OnSuccess ResetForm(SharePointForm1); RequestHide() For long forms, split fields across screens using several forms bound to the same item (each with a subset of cards), and on save use Patch(Deviations, SharePointIntegration.Selected, Form1.Updates, Form2.Updates, Form3.Updates) . # Performance tips Remove unused data cards and data sources. Every connector adds load time. Avoid heavy OnStart logic: the "},{"t":"Offline Canvas Apps – Dataverse Offline vs SaveData/LoadData Patterns","c":"power-apps","u":"articles/power-apps/offline-apps.html","s":"Make canvas apps work without connectivity, covering the built-in Dataverse offline profile, the classic SaveData/LoadData pattern with a sync queue for SharePoint, conflict handling, photos, and testing tips for field workers.","k":"offline, canvas app, savedata, loaddata, dataverse, mobile, field service","h":"Option 1 · Dataverse offline (recommended for new apps) · Option 2 · SaveData / LoadData (SharePoint or any source) · Conflict handling · Interview questions · What are the two main ways to build offline canvas apps? · How do you detect connectivity in Power Apps?","l":"Advanced","x":"Canvas Apps","b":"Inspectors in basements, technicians in plants and drivers on highways need apps that keep working without signal. There are two approaches. # Option 1 · Dataverse offline (recommended for new apps) Canvas apps on Dataverse can use the built-in offline profile . You enable offline in the app settings, define which tables and filters (for example \"my open work orders\") sync to the device, and Power Apps handles local storage, sync and conflicts. Your formulas stay the same. Dataverse offline architecture DEVICE (POWER APPS MOBILE) Local Dataverse cache App works against local data Connection status indicator background sync when online DATAVERSE Offline profile (tables + filters) Conflict detection Security still enforced on sync Dataverse offline architecture # Option 2 · SaveData / LoadData (SharePoint or any source) For SharePoint-backed apps, you implement offline yourself: cache reference data, queue changes locally, and sync when Connection.Connected becomes true. flowchart LR A[User saves inspection] --> B{Connection.Connected?} B -- yes --> P[Patch to SharePoint] B -- no --> Q[Collect into colQueue<br/>SaveData colQueue 'queue'] T[Timer every 30s / App OnStart] --> C{Online and queue not empty?} C -- yes --> S[ForAll queue → Patch<br/>remove synced rows<br/>SaveData] App.OnStart – load cache and queue Power Fx Copy LoadData(colInspections, \"inspections\" , true ); LoadData(colQueue, \"queue\" , true ); If (Connection.Connected, ClearCollect(colInspections, Filter (Inspections, Inspector.Email = User().Email && Status.Value = \"Planned\" )); SaveData(colInspections, \"inspections\" ) ) btnSave.OnSelect – online or queue Power Fx Copy If (Connection.Connected, Patch(Inspections, LookUp (Inspections, ID = locItem.ID), { Resu lt: ddResult.Selected.Value, Not es: txtNotes.Text }), Collect(colQueue, { ID: locItem.ID, Resu lt: ddResult.Selected.Value, Not es: txtNotes.Text, Queued At: Now () }); SaveData(colQueue, \"queue\" ); Notify( \"Saved offline, will sync when online\" , NotificationType.Information) ) tmrSync.OnTimerEnd – flush the queue Power Fx Copy If (Connection.Connected && CountRows(colQueue) > 0 , ForAll(colQueue As q, Patch(Inspections, LookUp (Inspections, ID = q.ID), { Resu lt: { Val ue: q.Result }, Not es: q.Notes }) ); Clear(colQueue); SaveData(colQueue, \"queue\" ); Notify( \"Offline changes synced\" , NotificationType.Success) ) Limits of SaveData Save"},{"t":"Modern Power Fx – Named Formulas, User-Defined Functions and Types","c":"power-apps","u":"articles/power-apps/named-formulas-udf.html","s":"The modern way to structure canvas apps with App.Formulas, user-defined functions (UDFs), behaviour UDFs, user-defined types and With(), replacing large OnStart blocks and duplicated formulas.","k":"power fx, named formulas, user defined functions, udf, app formulas, refactoring","h":"Interview questions · What problem do user-defined functions solve in canvas apps? · How are named formulas different from OnStart?","l":"Advanced","x":"Canvas Apps","b":"Large canvas apps used to suffer from huge OnStart formulas and the same logic copy-pasted into many controls. Modern Power Fx adds named formulas , user-defined functions (UDFs) and user-defined types so you can structure apps like code. Cheat sheet Modern Power Fx toolbox 01 Named formulas fxToday = Today(); declarative, cached, auto-updating values in App.Formulas. 02 UDFs fnSeverityColor(sev: Text): Color = Switch(sev, \"Critical\", Color.Red, …); reusable pure logic. 03 Behaviour UDFs Functions that run actions ( { … } block) such as Patch or Notify. 04 User-defined types Typed records/tables for UDF parameters and JSON parsing ( ParseJSON + Type ). 05 With() Local named values inside a formula, avoiding repeated sub-expressions. App.Formulas Power Fx Copy // Named formulas fxUserEmail = Lower (User().Email); fxIsAdmin = fxUserEmail in [ \"jane@contoso.com\" , \"ravi@contoso.com\" ]; // U DF: pure function fnSeverityColor(severi ty: Text ) : Color = Switch (severity, \"Critical\" , RGBA( 220 , 38 , 38 , 1 ), \"Major\" , RGBA( 217 , 119 , 6 , 1 ), RGBA( 5 , 150 , 105 , 1 )); // U DF: days overdue fnDaysOverdue(d ue: Date ) : Number = Max ( 0 , DateDiff(due, Today (), TimeUnit.Days)); // Behaviour U DF: shared save logic with notifications fnCloseDeviation( id: Number) : Void = { Patch(Deviations, LookUp (Deviations, ID = id), { Stat us: { Val ue: \"Closed\" } }); Notify( \"Deviation \" & id & \" closed\" , NotificationType.Success) }; Usage in controls Power Fx Copy lblSeverity.Fill = fnSeverityColor(ThisItem.Severity.Value) lblOverdue.Text = If (fnDaysOverdue(ThisItem.DueDate) > 0 , fnDaysOverdue(ThisItem.DueDate) & \" days overdue\" , \"\" ) btnClose.OnSelect = fnCloseDeviation(ThisItem.ID) Feature availability UDFs, behaviour UDFs and user-defined types became available progressively (some started as experimental or preview settings). Check Settings → Updates in Power Apps Studio for your environment. # Interview questions Q1 What problem do user-defined functions solve in canvas apps? They remove duplicated formula logic across controls and screens. Logic is defined once in App.Formulas with typed parameters and reused everywhere, which improves maintainability and testability. Q2 How are named formulas different from OnStart? Named formulas are declarations evaluated on demand and kept up to date automatically. OnStart is imperative code that runs once at startup, bloc"},{"t":"Error Handling & Monitoring in Power Apps – IfError, Errors(), App.OnError and Monitor","c":"power-apps","u":"articles/power-apps/error-handling.html","s":"Build robust canvas apps with formula-level error handling, data source errors, global App.OnError logging to Application Insights, user-friendly messages, and troubleshooting with the Monitor tool.","k":"error handling, iferror, monitor, application insights, debugging, power apps","h":"Layers of error handling · Monitor · Interview questions · What is the difference between IfError and App.OnError? · How would you find out why a Patch fails only for some users?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Canvas Apps","b":"# Layers of error handling Defence in depth for canvas apps PREVENT Validation before save Disable buttons while saving DataSourceInfo permission checks handle locally FORMULA LEVEL IfError(…, fallback) IsError / IsBlankOrError Form.OnFailure + Form.Error catch everything else APP LEVEL App.OnError (FirstError, AllErrors) Trace() to Application Insights investigate TOOLS Monitor (live session) Application Insights queries Solution checker Defence in depth for canvas apps Local handling with useful messages Power Fx Copy IfError ( Patch(Deviations, Defaults(Deviations), { Tit le: txtTitle.Text, Ar ea: { Val ue: ddArea.Selected.Value } }), Notify( Switch (FirstError.Kind, ErrorKind.Validation, \"Please check the highlighted fields.\" , ErrorKind.Permission, \"You don&#x27;t have permission to create deviations.\" , ErrorKind.Network, \"No connection. Try again when online.\" , \"Unexpected error: \" & FirstError.Message), NotificationType.Error), Notify( \"Saved\" , NotificationType.Success); Back() ) App.OnError – global safety net + telemetry Power Fx Copy Trace( \"Unhandled error\" , TraceSeverity.Error, { messa ge: FirstError.Message, sour ce: FirstError.Source, ki nd: FirstError.Kind, scre en: App.ActiveScreen.Name, us er: User().Email } ); Notify( \"Something went wrong. The support team has been informed.\" , NotificationType.Error) Connect the app to Application Insights (App settings → Instrumentation key/connection string) and Trace calls plus automatic telemetry flow there. # Monitor Monitor (Advanced tools → Monitor) records every formula evaluation and network call in a live session: request URL, response, duration and errors. It is the fastest way to see why a Patch failed (for example 400: Column 'X' does not exist ) or which call is slow. Make errors actionable Show users what to do next , log technical details for support, and include a correlation (screen, record ID, time). Never show raw JSON. # Interview questions Q1 What is the difference between IfError and App.OnError? IfError handles errors in one formula , with a fallback and success branch. App.OnError runs for errors that were not handled anywhere, which makes it ideal for logging (Trace to Application Insights) and a generic user message. Q2 How would you find out why a Patch fails only for some users? Run Monitor with the affected user (or have them share a session) to see the exact request and "},{"t":"Model-Driven Apps – Architecture, Building Blocks and Building Your First App","c":"power-apps","u":"articles/power-apps/model-driven-overview.html","s":"How model-driven apps are composed from Dataverse metadata (tables, forms, views, charts, dashboards, site map), how security roles shape the experience, and a step-by-step build of an inventory management app.","k":"model-driven app, dataverse, site map, forms, views, inventory","h":"Architecture · Example: Inventory management app · Steps · What users get for free · Interview questions · What makes a model-driven app \"model-driven\"? · How do security roles affect a model-driven app?","l":"Beginner","x":"Model-Driven Apps","b":"A model-driven app is generated from your data model . You define tables, relationships, forms, views and business logic in Dataverse , then compose an app that exposes the right components to the right people. The UI is consistent, responsive and accessible by default. It is the same technology behind Dynamics 365. # Architecture Model-driven app composition APP MODULE Site map (navigation) Selected tables & components Security roles that can access it exposes UI COMPONENTS Main / quick create / quick… Views (public, personal) Charts & dashboards Custom pages (canvas) Command bar buttons on top of LOGIC Business rules Business process flows Power Automate & plug-ins Formula / rollup columns stored in DATAVERSE Tables & relationships Security roles & teams Auditing & solutions Model-driven app composition # Example: Inventory management app erDiagram WAREHOUSE ||--o{ STOCK_LEVEL : holds PRODUCT ||--o{ STOCK_LEVEL : \"stocked as\" PRODUCT ||--o{ STOCK_MOVEMENT : moves WAREHOUSE ||--o{ STOCK_MOVEMENT : \"from/to\" SUPPLIER ||--o{ PRODUCT : supplies PRODUCT { string name string sku money unitCost int reorderLevel } STOCK_MOVEMENT { choice type \"In, Out, Transfer\" int quantity datetime movedOn } # Steps Create a solution (Contoso Inventory) with a publisher prefix ( ci_ ). Create tables: Product, Warehouse, Stock Level, Stock Movement and Supplier, with lookups for the relationships above. Configure main forms (sections, tabs, sub-grids for related records) and views (Active products, Below reorder level). Add a rollup or flow to maintain stock levels from movements. Build the app in the app designer : add tables to the site map (groups such as Inventory and Purchasing), and add a dashboard. Create security roles (Warehouse Clerk: create movements; Inventory Manager: everything) and share the app with those roles. Publish and test as each role. # What users get for free Grid views with sorting, filtering, inline edit (editable grid), export to Excel. Forms with tabs, related records, a timeline (notes, activities) and an audit trail. Advanced Find / modern filtering, saved personal views. Responsive UI on web, tablet and phone (Power Apps mobile). Accessibility and keyboard support. # Interview questions Q1 What makes a model-driven app \"model-driven\"? The UI is generated from Dataverse metadata (tables, relationships, forms, views) rather than designed screen by sc"},{"t":"Model-Driven Forms, Views, Charts & Dashboards – Design Guide","c":"power-apps","u":"articles/power-apps/forms-views-dashboards.html","s":"Design main, quick create and quick view forms, sub-grids, form scripts with the Client API, public and personal views, editable grids, charts and interactive dashboards for model-driven apps.","k":"model-driven, forms, views, dashboards, client api, javascript, editable grid","h":"Form types · Form scripts (Client API) · Views · Dashboards · Interview questions · When should you use JavaScript on a model-driven form instead of a business rule? · What is a quick view form?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Model-Driven Apps","b":"# Form types Form Purpose Main Full record view and edit, with tabs, sections, sub-grids and timeline Quick create Lightweight fly-out for fast entry from anywhere Quick view Read-only panel showing a related record's fields on another form Card Compact layout for views on small screens and in components Header · Name · Status · Owner · Priority Tabs: Summary | Details | Related Section: General (fields) Timeline (notes, emails, activities) Sub-grid: Stock movements Quick view: Supplier contact A typical main form layout # Form scripts (Client API) Most logic belongs in business rules, formula columns or flows. When you need interactive client logic, use a JavaScript web resource with the Client API: ci_product.form.js JavaScript Copy var Contoso = Contoso || {}; Contoso . Product = { onLoad : function ( executionContext ) { var formContext = executionContext. getFormContext (); formContext. getAttribute ( \"ci_category\" ). addOnChange ( Contoso . Product . onCategoryChange ); Contoso . Product . onCategoryChange (executionContext); }, onCategoryChange : function ( executionContext ) { var formContext = executionContext. getFormContext (); var category = formContext. getAttribute ( \"ci_category\" ). getValue (); // choice value var isHazardous = category === 100000002 ; formContext. ui . tabs . get ( \"tab_safety\" ). setVisible (isHazardous); formContext. getAttribute ( \"ci_msdsurl\" ). setRequiredLevel (isHazardous ? \"required\" : \"none\" ); }, onSave : function ( executionContext ) { var formContext = executionContext. getFormContext (); var cost = formContext. getAttribute ( \"ci_unitcost\" ). getValue (); if (cost !== null && cost <= 0 ) { formContext. ui . setFormNotification ( \"Unit cost must be greater than zero.\" , \"ERROR\" , \"cost\" ); executionContext. getEventArgs (). preventDefault (); // cancel save } else { formContext. ui . clearFormNotification ( \"cost\" ); } } }; Register Contoso.Product.onLoad on the form's OnLoad event and onSave on OnSave, passing the execution context. # Views Public views for everyone (Active products, Low stock), personal views created by users. Use filters on related tables (for example products whose supplier country is India). Enable the editable grid control for bulk updates in place. Add charts to views for quick visual summaries. # Dashboards flowchart LR D[Inventory dashboard] --> C1[Chart: stock by warehouse] D --> C2[Cha"},{"t":"Business Rules & Business Process Flows in Dataverse","c":"power-apps","u":"articles/power-apps/business-rules-bpf.html","s":"No-code logic with business rules (scope, conditions, actions) and guided processes with business process flows (stages, steps, branching, BPF tables), with a deviation-management example and the limits of each.","k":"business rules, business process flow, dataverse, model-driven, no-code","h":"Business rules · Business process flows (BPF) · Business rule vs BPF vs Power Automate vs plug-in · Interview questions · Does a business rule run when data is created through the Web API? · Where is business process flow progress stored?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Model-Driven Apps","b":"# Business rules Business rules apply conditions and actions without code: set field values, set requirement levels, show or hide fields, enable or lock fields, show error messages, set defaults and recommendations. Scope Runs Entity (table) Server-side and on all forms (also applies to imports, API and flows for value-setting actions) All forms Client-side on every form of the table Specific form Client-side on one form flowchart LR C{Severity = Critical} -->|true| A1[Set Due Date required] C -->|true| A2[Show 'QA Head' field] C -->|true| A3[Set Priority = High] C -->|false| B1[Due Date optional] C -->|false| B2[Hide 'QA Head'] Server-side execution With scope Entity , \"set field value\" and \"show error message\" actions are enforced server-side too, which makes them a lightweight alternative to a plug-in for simple validation. UI actions (show/hide, lock) only affect forms. # Business process flows (BPF) A BPF guides users through stages (Identify → Investigate → CAPA → Close), each with steps (fields to complete). It shows a process bar at the top of the form. flowchart LR S1[Identify<br/>• Title • Area • Severity] --> S2[Investigate<br/>• Root cause • Investigator] S2 --> D{Severity = Critical?} D -->|yes| S3a[QA Review<br/>• QA Head approval] D -->|no| S3b[CAPA<br/>• Actions • Due date] S3a --> S3b S3b --> S4[Close<br/>• Effectiveness check] Key facts: Each BPF is stored in its own table (one row per process instance), so you can report on stage durations and bottlenecks. Stages can span multiple tables (for example Lead → Opportunity). Branching on conditions, plus required steps that block moving to the next stage. Automate on stage change with Power Automate (trigger on the BPF table) or call workflows and action steps inside stages. # Business rule vs BPF vs Power Automate vs plug-in Need Tool Field defaults, required logic, simple validation Business rule Guided multi-stage process with visible progress Business process flow Notifications, approvals, integration after save Power Automate Complex synchronous validation or calculations in transaction Plug-in # Interview questions Q1 Does a business rule run when data is created through the Web API? If the rule's scope is Entity , value-setting and error actions run server-side, so yes for those actions. Rules scoped to forms only run in the form UI. Q2 Where is business process flow progress stored? In"},{"t":"Custom Pages – Canvas Experiences Inside Model-Driven Apps","c":"power-apps","u":"articles/power-apps/custom-pages.html","s":"Use custom pages to bring canvas flexibility into model-driven apps, covering full pages, dialogs and side panes, opening them with navigateTo and Power Fx, passing record context, and when to use them over forms.","k":"custom pages, model-driven, canvas, dialog, navigateto, power fx","h":"Opening a custom page as a dialog from a command button · When to use custom pages · Interview questions · How do you pass the current record to a custom page? · What are the targets for opening a custom page?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Model-Driven Apps","b":"Custom pages are canvas-based pages that live inside a model-driven app. They let you build a wizard, a dashboard, a scanning screen or a specialized editor with canvas freedom, while keeping the model-driven shell, navigation and security. Where custom pages appear MODEL-DRIVEN APP Site map entry (full page) Command bar button → dialog Side pane next to a record hosts CUSTOM PAGE (CANVAS) Power Fx Modern controls Responsive containers Param(\"recordId\") context data via DATAVERSE + CONNECTORS Same security as the user Other connectors (premium rules apply) Where custom pages appear # Opening a custom page as a dialog from a command button Command button JavaScript (Client API) JavaScript Copy function openCloseDialog ( primaryControl ) { var recordId = primaryControl. data . entity . getId (). replace ( /[{}]/g , \"\" ); Xrm . Navigation . navigateTo ( { pageType : \"custom\" , name : \"ci_closedeviationdialog_8a1b2\" , entityName : \"ci_deviation\" , recordId : recordId }, { target : 2 , position : 1 , width : { value : 480 , unit : \"px\" }, title : \"Close deviation\" } // target 2 = dialog ). then ( function ( ) { primaryControl. data . refresh (); }); } Inside the custom page Power Fx Copy // App.OnStart / screen OnVisib le: read the record passed from the form Set(varRecord, LookUp (Deviations, Deviation = GUID(Param( \"recordId\" )))); // Close button Patch(Deviations, varRecord, { Stat us: &#x27;Status (Deviations)&#x27;.Closed, &#x27;Closure Comment&#x27; : txtComment.Text }); Back() // closes the dialog and returns to the form With the modern command bar , buttons can also open custom pages with Power Fx: Navigate(Self.Selected.Item) style formulas in command designer, or Navigate('Close Deviation Page', { ... }) in supported scenarios. # When to use custom pages Use a form when… Use a custom page when… Standard create/edit of one record Multi-step wizard or guided task Business rules suffice Rich layout: maps, galleries, camera, barcode Data is one table + related grids Aggregated views across tables or connectors # Interview questions Q1 How do you pass the current record to a custom page? Open it with Xrm.Navigation.navigateTo({ pageType: \"custom\", name, entityName, recordId }) and read the ID in the page with Param(\"recordId\") , then LookUp the record. Q2 What are the targets for opening a custom page? Inline full page ( target: 1 ), centred or side dialog ("},{"t":"Modern Command Bar – Custom Buttons with Power Fx and JavaScript","c":"power-apps","u":"articles/power-apps/command-bar.html","s":"Customize model-driven app command bars with the modern command designer, covering Power Fx actions and visibility, JavaScript commands, form vs grid locations, and replacing classic Ribbon Workbench customizations.","k":"command bar, ribbon, power fx, model-driven, javascript, command designer","h":"Command locations · Power Fx command example: \"Mark as Critical\" · When to use JavaScript instead · Interview questions · What replaced the Ribbon Workbench for most customizations? · How do you hide a command for closed records?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Model-Driven Apps","b":"The command bar (formerly the ribbon ) holds buttons such as New, Save and Deactivate. The modern command designer lets makers add buttons with Power Fx for both action and visibility, without the old Ribbon Workbench XML. # Command locations Location Context Main form One record ( Self.Selected.Item ) Main grid View of many records ( Self.Selected.AllItems ) Sub-grid Related records on a form Associated view Related records in a separate view # Power Fx command example: \"Mark as Critical\" Action (OnSelect) Power Fx Copy Patch(Deviations, Self.Selected.Item, { Severi ty: &#x27;Severity (Deviations)&#x27;.Critical, Priori ty: &#x27;Priority (Deviations)&#x27;.High }); Notify( \"Marked as critical\" , NotificationType.Warning) Visible Power Fx Copy Self.Selected.Item.Status <> &#x27;Status (Deviations)&#x27;.Closed && User().Email in Concat ( Filter (&#x27;QA Leads&#x27;, Active = true ), Email & \";\" ) For grids, use ForAll(Self.Selected.AllItems, Patch(...)) and set visibility to CountRows(Self.Selected.AllItems) > 0 . # When to use JavaScript instead Use a JavaScript action (web resource + function name + parameters such as PrimaryControl or SelectedControlSelectedItemIds ) when you need the Client API : opening dialogs or custom pages with navigateTo , calling custom APIs, or complex UI interactions. flowchart LR B[Command button] --> Q{Logic type} Q -->|Data change, simple checks| FX[Power Fx action<br/>+ Power Fx visibility] Q -->|Dialogs, custom API, Client API| JS[JavaScript web resource] Q -->|Long-running / integration| FL[Run a flow / custom API] # Interview questions Q1 What replaced the Ribbon Workbench for most customizations? The modern command designer in the app designer, which supports Power Fx for actions and visibility, plus JavaScript actions, all stored in solutions. Q2 How do you hide a command for closed records? Set the command's Visible property to a Power Fx formula such as Self.Selected.Item.Status <> 'Status (Deviations)'.Closed ."},{"t":"PowerApps Component Framework (PCF) – Build Code Components in TypeScript","c":"power-apps","u":"articles/power-apps/pcf-controls.html","s":"Build a PCF code component from scratch, covering pac pcf init, manifest, init/updateView/getOutputs/destroy lifecycle, React (virtual) controls, dataset vs field components, testing in the harness, packaging in a solution and using it in canvas and model-driven apps.","k":"pcf, component framework, typescript, react, pac cli, code components","h":"Lifecycle · Build a \"StarRating\" field component · Package and deploy · Field vs dataset components · Interview questions · What is the difference between a standard and a virtual (React) PCF control? · How does a PCF control tell the host its value changed?","l":"Advanced","x":"Pro-Dev & Extensibility","b":"PCF code components let professional developers build custom controls (TypeScript, optionally React) that makers then use like built-in controls in model-driven forms and views , canvas apps and Power Pages . Examples: a star rating, a signature pad, a colour-coded timeline, a barcode scanner or an interactive Gantt grid. # Lifecycle sequenceDiagram participant H as Host (form/canvas) participant C as Your component H->>C: init(context, notifyOutputChanged, state, container) H->>C: updateView(context) (initial render) Note over C: user interacts C->>H: notifyOutputChanged() H->>C: getOutputs() → { value: 4 } H->>C: updateView(context) (whenever inputs/size change) H->>C: destroy() # Build a \"StarRating\" field component Scaffold (Power Platform CLI) Bash Copy pac pcf init --namespace Contoso --name StarRating --template field --framework react --run-npm-install npm start watch # test harness at http://localhost:8181 ControlManifest.Input.xml XML Copy < manifest > < control namespace = \"Contoso\" constructor = \"StarRating\" version = \"1.0.0\" display-name-key = \"Star rating\" description-key = \"Rate 1–5\" control-type = \"virtual\" > < property name = \"value\" display-name-key = \"Rating\" of-type = \"Whole.None\" usage = \"bound\" required = \"true\" /> < property name = \"maxStars\" display-name-key = \"Max stars\" of-type = \"Whole.None\" usage = \"input\" default-value = \"5\" /> < resources > < code path = \"index.ts\" order = \"1\" /> < platform-library name = \"React\" version = \"16.14.0\" /> < platform-library name = \"Fluent\" version = \"9.46.2\" /> </ resources > </ control > </ manifest > index.ts (virtual/React control) TypeScript Copy import { IInputs , IOutputs } from &#x27;./generated/ManifestTypes&#x27; ; import * as React from &#x27;react&#x27; ; import { Stars } from &#x27;./Stars&#x27; ; export class StarRating implements ComponentFramework . ReactControl < IInputs , IOutputs > { private notify!: () => void ; private current = 0 ; public init ( context : ComponentFramework . Context < IInputs >, notifyOutputChanged : () => void ): void { this . notify = notifyOutputChanged; } public updateView ( context : ComponentFramework . Context < IInputs >): React . ReactElement { this . current = context. parameters . value . raw ?? 0 ; return React . createElement ( Stars , { value : this . current , max : context. parameters . maxStars . raw ?? 5 , disabled : context. mode . isControl"},{"t":"Power Pages – External Websites on Dataverse (formerly Power Apps Portals)","c":"power-apps","u":"articles/power-apps/power-pages.html","s":"What Power Pages is, covering the site architecture, authentication for external users, table permissions and web roles, lists and forms, Liquid templates, the portals Web API, the design studio, and security pitfalls.","k":"power pages, portals, external users, table permissions, liquid, web api","h":"Architecture · Security model in one table · Liquid example · Interview questions · How are external users represented in Power Pages? · What is the most important security configuration in Power Pages?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Pro-Dev & Extensibility","b":"Power Pages (renamed from Power Apps portals in 2022) builds secure, low-code websites for external audiences (customers, suppliers, citizens, partners) backed by Dataverse . # Architecture Power Pages site VISITORS Anonymous Authenticated (Entra External ID, Microsoft,… HTTPS POWER PAGES SITE Pages & templates (design studio) Lists & forms (Dataverse) Liquid + JS + CSS Web API (/_api) authorization SECURITY Web roles Table permissions (global, contact, account, parent,… Page permissions data DATAVERSE Site metadata tables Business tables (Cases, Applications) Contacts = portal users Power Pages site # Security model in one table Concept Meaning Contact Every authenticated site user is a Dataverse contact Web role Group of users (Authenticated Users, Suppliers, Admins) Table permission What a web role can do with a table: Read/Create/Write/Delete/Append, scoped by Global, Contact, Account, Parent, Self Page permission Who can see which pages Table permissions are the security boundary If a list or the Web API exposes a table, table permissions decide what external users can read. Overly broad Global read permissions have caused real data leaks. Review them like firewall rules and test as an anonymous user. # Liquid example Show the signed-in supplier's open applications LIQUID Copy {% if user %} {% fetchxml apps %} <fetch top=\"20\"> <entity name=\"ci_application\"> <attribute name=\"ci_name\" /><attribute name=\"statuscode\" /><attribute name=\"createdon\" /> <filter><condition attribute=\"ci_supplier\" operator=\"eq\" value=\"{{ user.parentcustomerid.id }}\" /></filter> <order attribute=\"createdon\" descending=\"true\" /> </entity> </fetch> {% endfetchxml %} <ul> {% for a in apps.results.entities %} <li>{{ a.ci_name }} · {{ a.statuscode.label }} · {{ a.createdon | date: \"dd MMM yyyy\" }}</li> {% endfor %} </ul> {% else %} <a href=\"/SignIn\">Sign in to see your applications</a> {% endif %} # Interview questions Q1 How are external users represented in Power Pages? As contacts in Dataverse, linked to identity providers. Authorization uses web roles assigned to contacts and table permissions attached to those roles. Q2 What is the most important security configuration in Power Pages? Table permissions with the right scope (Contact, Account, Parent, Self rather than Global), because lists, forms, Liquid and the Web API all respect them, and misconfiguration exposes data to externa"},{"t":"Custom Connectors – Wrap Your Own APIs for Power Apps & Power Automate","c":"power-apps","u":"articles/power-apps/custom-connectors.html","s":"Create custom connectors from OpenAPI definitions or from scratch, covering authentication types (Entra ID OAuth, API key), actions and triggers, policies, code in connectors, solution-aware deployment, and certification basics.","k":"custom connector, openapi, api, power automate, power apps, entra id","h":"OpenAPI definition (excerpt) · Entra ID (OAuth 2.0) setup checklist · Using it · Interview questions · Why use a custom connector instead of the HTTP action? · Which OpenAPI version does the custom connector wizard use?","l":"Advanced","x":"Pro-Dev & Extensibility","b":"A custom connector turns any REST API into something makers can drag into Power Apps and Power Automate, with typed actions, inputs and outputs and managed authentication. It is the standard way to expose your Azure Functions or line-of-business APIs to the Power Platform. Custom connector architecture MAKER Power Apps (DeviationApi.CreateDeviatio… Power Automate action connection (user or service identity) CUSTOM CONNECTOR OpenAPI definition Auth: OAuth 2.0 (Entra ID) / API key… Policies (set header, route) Optional C# code HTTPS via Power Platform connector infrastructure YOUR API Azure Function / App Service / APIM Validates Entra token Business logic Custom connector architecture # OpenAPI definition (excerpt) deviation-api.yaml (Swagger 2.0 is required by the connector designer) YAML Copy swagger: &#x27;2.0&#x27; info: { title: Deviation API , version: &#x27;1.0&#x27; } host: fn-deviation.azurewebsites.net basePath: /api schemes: [ https ] paths: /deviations: get: operationId: ListDeviations summary: List deviations parameters: - { name: status , in: query , type: string , enum: [ Open , Closed ], default: Open } responses: &#x27;200&#x27;: description: OK schema: { type: array , items: { $ref: &#x27;#/definitions/Deviation&#x27; } } post: operationId: CreateDeviation summary: Create a deviation parameters: - { name: body , in: body , required: true , schema: { $ref: &#x27;#/definitions/NewDeviation&#x27; } } responses: { &#x27;201&#x27;: { description: Created , schema: { $ref: &#x27;#/definitions/Deviation&#x27; } } } definitions: NewDeviation: { type: object , required: [ title ], properties: { title: { type: string }, severity: { type: string } } } Deviation: { type: object , properties: { id: { type: string }, title: { type: string }, status: { type: string } } } securityDefinitions: oauth2: { type: oauth2 , flow: accessCode , authorizationUrl: &#x27;https://login.microsoftonline.com/common/oauth2/authorize&#x27; , tokenUrl: &#x27;https://login.windows.net/common/oauth2/authorize&#x27; , scopes: {} } # Entra ID (OAuth 2.0) setup checklist API app registration: expose scope user_impersonation (as for SPFx). Connector client app registration with a secret, delegated permission to the API scope, and redirect URI https://global.consent.azure-apim.net/redirect (or the connector-specific redirect shown in the designer). In the connector Security tab: Ide"},{"t":"ALM for Power Platform – Solutions, Environment Variables, Pipelines and Source Control","c":"power-apps","u":"articles/power-apps/alm-solutions.html","s":"How application lifecycle management works on Power Platform, covering managed vs unmanaged solutions, publishers, layering, connection references and environment variables, Power Platform pipelines, PAC CLI with GitHub or Azure DevOps, and Git integration.","k":"alm, solutions, managed solutions, pipelines, pac cli, environment variables, devops","h":"Managed vs unmanaged · The release flow · Environment variables & connection references · Power Platform pipelines (in-product) · Solution layering · Interview questions · Why deploy managed solutions to test and production? · What are environment variables and connection references for? · Your managed solution update didn't change a form in production. Why?","l":"Advanced","x":"ALM, Security & Performance","b":"Solutions are how Power Platform packages apps, flows, tables, connectors and configuration, and moves them between environments. Doing ALM properly is the difference between \"it works in my environment\" and a governed release process. # Managed vs unmanaged Unmanaged Managed Where Development environments Test and production Editable ✅ ❌ (locked, unless customization allowed) Delete solution Removes the container, not the components Removes components and data (tables) Purpose Building Deploying # The release flow Power Platform ALM pipeline DEV ENVIRONMENT Unmanaged solution Publisher prefix (ci_) Connection references & environment variables export (unmanaged → source control, managed → artifact) SOURCE CONTROL pac solution unpack Git PRs & history Build pipeline packs managed zip deploy managed TEST ENVIRONMENT Import managed Set env variable values & connections Automated + UAT tests approval PRODUCTION Import managed (upgrade) Post-deploy smoke tests Power Platform ALM pipeline PAC CLI essentials Bash Copy pac auth create --name dev --url https://contoso-dev.crm4.dynamics.com pac solution export --name ContosoQuality --path ./out/ContosoQuality.zip --managed false pac solution unpack --zipfile ./out/ContosoQuality.zip --folder ./src/ContosoQuality --processCanvasApps # CI: pack managed from source pac solution pack --zipfile ./out/ContosoQuality_managed.zip --folder ./src/ContosoQuality --packagetype Managed # CD: import to test with a settings file for env variables & connection references pac auth create --name test --url https://contoso-test.crm4.dynamics.com pac solution create-settings --solution-zip ./out/ContosoQuality_managed.zip --settings-file ./deploy/test.settings.json pac solution import --path ./out/ContosoQuality_managed.zip --settings-file ./deploy/test.settings.json --activate-plugins # Environment variables & connection references deploy/test.settings.json (generated, then filled in) JSON Copy { \"EnvironmentVariables\" : [ { \"SchemaName\" : \"ci_SharePointSiteUrl\" , \"Value\" : \"https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/quality-test\" } , { \"SchemaName\" : \"ci_DeviationApiUrl\" , \"Value\" : \"https://fn-deviation-test.azurewebsites.net/api\" } ] , \"ConnectionReferences\" : [ { \"LogicalName\" : \"ci_sharedsharepointonline_6f1c2\" , \"ConnectionId\" : \"a1b2c3…\" , \"ConnectorId\" : \"/providers/Microsoft.PowerApps/apis/shared_sharepointonline\" } ] } # Power Plat"},{"t":"Securing Power Apps – Sharing, Data Source Permissions, DLP and Managed Environments","c":"power-apps","u":"articles/power-apps/security-sharing-dlp.html","s":"How app sharing differs from data permissions, securing SharePoint and Dataverse back ends, service accounts vs user connections, DLP policies, tenant isolation, Managed Environments controls and a security review checklist.","k":"security, sharing, dlp, managed environments, power apps, governance","h":"Patterns for hiding data users shouldn't edit · DLP policies in practice · Security review checklist · Interview questions · If a user can open your canvas app, can they see all data in the SharePoint list behind it? · How do you let users trigger an action they don't have permission to perform?","l":"Intermediate","x":"ALM, Security & Performance","b":"Sharing an app ≠ securing data Sharing a canvas app only lets people open the app . What they can read or change is decided by their permissions on the data source (SharePoint list, Dataverse role, SQL). Users can reach the same data outside your app, through SharePoint UI, Excel or the API, with the same permissions. Three layers of Power Apps security APP ACCESS Share with users / Entra groups Co-owner vs user Managed Environment sharing limits app opens, then DATA ACCESS (PER USER) SharePoint permissions Dataverse security roles (row/column) SQL / API authorization governed by TENANT & ENVIRONMENT POLICIES DLP connector policies Tenant isolation (cross-tenant) Conditional Access IP firewall / cookie binding Three layers of Power Apps security # Patterns for hiding data users shouldn't edit Need Approach Users submit, but must not edit after submission Item-level permission change by a flow after submit, or Dataverse role logic Users must not see others' salary data Separate list with restricted permissions, or Dataverse column security Users write data they don't have rights to Flow with a service account connection (run-only users) performs the write Admin-only screens Check role ( fxIsAdmin ) and enforce on the data source; UI hiding alone is not security # DLP policies in practice flowchart LR subgraph Business SP[SharePoint] --- DV[Dataverse] --- O365[Office 365 Outlook] end subgraph NonBusiness[\"Non-business\"] TW[Twitter / X] --- DB[Dropbox] end subgraph Blocked HTTPX[HTTP to unknown domains] end Business -. cannot be combined .- NonBusiness # Security review checklist Data source permissions reviewed: least privilege, no \"Everyone\" edit on business lists. No secrets in app formulas (they are visible in the app package). Privileged actions run in flows or APIs with service identities, not in the app. App shared with groups , not individuals; co-owners limited. DLP policy for the environment classifies all used connectors. Connection references owned by a service account in production solutions. Audit: Dataverse auditing enabled, or SharePoint version history for lists. # Interview questions Q1 If a user can open your canvas app, can they see all data in the SharePoint list behind it? They can see whatever their SharePoint permissions allow, both in the app and directly in SharePoint. App sharing does not restrict data. Security must be enforced on th"},{"t":"Canvas App Performance – From 20 Seconds to 2 Seconds","c":"power-apps","u":"articles/power-apps/performance.html","s":"A practical performance playbook for canvas apps, covering fast startup with named formulas and deferred loading, Concurrent, delegation, control count, gallery tuning, caching, image optimization, Monitor and App Checker.","k":"performance, canvas app, concurrent, onstart, delegation, monitor, optimization","h":"The N+1 trap in galleries · Startup timeline · Interview questions · Name three ways to make a slow canvas app start faster. · What tools help diagnose canvas app performance?","l":"Advanced","x":"ALM, Security & Performance","b":"Cheat sheet Canvas performance playbook 01 Startup Move OnStart logic to named formulas; enable \"Delayed load\"; set StartScreen; load per screen with Concurrent(). 02 Data Delegable filters on indexed columns; select fewer columns (ShowColumns / explicit column selection setting). 03 Controls Fewer controls per screen (< 300 ideal); galleries over repeated controls; avoid nested galleries on large data. 04 Galleries Use DelayItemLoading, LoadingSpinner; limit rows; avoid LookUp inside gallery rows (N+1). 05 Media Compress images; use SVG icons; avoid large embedded media in the app package. 06 Connectors Fewer data sources per screen; cache reference data in collections once. 07 Measure Monitor, App Checker (Performance rules), browser network tab, Live monitor with users. # The N+1 trap in galleries Slow vs fast Power Fx Copy // ❌ One LookUp per row → N extra calls lblAreaName.Text = LookUp ( Areas , ID = ThisItem.AreaId).Title // ✅ Use a lookup column (expanded by the connector) or cache reference data once // OnVisib le: ClearCollect(colAreas, Areas ) lblAreaName.Text = LookUp (colAreas, ID = ThisItem.AreaId).Title # Startup timeline flowchart LR subgraph Before[\"Before · ~12 s to first screen\"] B1[\"OnStart: 6 ClearCollects<br/>one after another (9 s)\"] --> B2[\"Home screen loads<br/>its data (3 s)\"] end subgraph After[\"After · ~3 s to first screen\"] A1[\"Named formulas<br/>(lazy, ~0 s)\"] --> A2[\"Home screen OnVisible:<br/>Concurrent loads (2–3 s)\"] end # Interview questions Q1 Name three ways to make a slow canvas app start faster. Replace OnStart variable loading with named formulas (lazy evaluation), load data per screen with Concurrent() instead of everything at startup, and reduce the data loaded: delegable filters, fewer columns, and caching only small reference tables. Q2 What tools help diagnose canvas app performance? Monitor (network calls, durations, formula evaluations), App Checker performance rules, browser developer tools, and Application Insights telemetry for production usage."},{"t":"What is Power Automate? Cloud Flows, Desktop Flows and How Flows Run","c":"power-automate","u":"articles/power-automate/overview.html","s":"Power Automate explained, covering flow types (automated, instant, scheduled, desktop, business process), how the engine executes runs, connectors and licensing tiers, run history, and where Power Automate fits next to Logic Apps, Nintex and SharePoint workflows.","k":"power automate, cloud flows, desktop flows, workflow, automation, overview","h":"Flow types · How a cloud flow runs · Your first useful flow: notify the owner of a new deviation · Power Automate vs alternatives · Interview questions · What is the difference between an automated and an instant cloud flow? · How is Power Automate related to Azure Logic Apps? · How long is run history kept?","l":"Beginner","x":"Fundamentals","b":"Power Automate (called Microsoft Flow until November 2019) is Microsoft's automation service. It is the successor to SharePoint 2010 and 2013 workflows and the default choice for automation in Microsoft 365. Under the hood, cloud flows run on the same engine as Azure Logic Apps . # Flow types Cheat sheet Power Automate flow types 01 Automated cloud flow Starts on an event: item created in SharePoint, email arrives, Dataverse row changes. 02 Instant cloud flow Starts manually: button in the mobile app, \"For a selected item\" in SharePoint, from Power Apps or Teams. 03 Scheduled cloud flow Runs on a recurrence: every weekday at 08:00, first day of the month. 04 Desktop flow (RPA) Automates Windows/web UIs with Power Automate Desktop (attended or unattended). 05 Business process flow Guided stages in model-driven apps (Dataverse). 06 Process mining Analyse event logs to discover bottlenecks and automation candidates. # How a cloud flow runs Cloud flow execution TRIGGER Polling (checks every N min) or webhook (push) Trigger conditions filter starts creates a run FLOW ENGINE (LOGIC APPS RUNTIME) Actions executed as a DAG Retries & timeouts per action Concurrency control via CONNECTORS Standard (SharePoint, Outlook, Teams) Premium (HTTP, Dataverse, SQL) Custom connectors recorded in RUN HISTORY Inputs/outputs per action (28 days) Resubmit cancel Analytics Cloud flow execution # Your first useful flow: notify the owner of a new deviation flowchart LR T[\"When an item is created<br/>(SharePoint: Deviations)\"] --> C{Severity = Critical?} C -- yes --> A1[\"Post adaptive card in Teams<br/>Quality channel\"] C -- yes --> A2[Send email to QA Head<br/>high importance] C -- no --> A3[Send email to Owner] A1 & A2 & A3 --> U[\"Update item<br/>Status = Notified\"] # Power Automate vs alternatives Power Automate Azure Logic Apps Nintex Automation Cloud SharePoint 2013 workflows Audience Makers & pros Developers / integration Process owners Legacy Hosting Microsoft 365 / Power Platform Azure subscription Nintex cloud Workflow Manager ALM Solutions ARM/Bicep, DevOps Nintex tools WSP / SPD Billing Per user / per flow / PAYG Per execution / Standard plan Nintex licensing Included (retired in SPO) Status Strategic Strategic Strategic (Nintex) Retired in SPO (Apr 2026) # Interview questions Q1 What is the difference between an automated and an instant cloud flow? An automated flow starts "},{"t":"Triggers & Trigger Conditions – Start Flows Only When They Should","c":"power-automate","u":"articles/power-automate/triggers-trigger-conditions.html","s":"SharePoint and other triggers explained (polling vs webhook, created vs created or modified), avoiding infinite loops, trigger conditions with expressions, splitOn and concurrency, and when to use 'For a selected item'.","k":"power automate, triggers, trigger conditions, sharepoint, infinite loop, concurrency","h":"SharePoint triggers you will use · The infinite loop problem · Concurrency and splitOn · Interview questions · How do you prevent a \"created or modified\" flow from triggering itself when it updates the item? · Where are trigger conditions evaluated, and why does that matter?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Fundamentals","b":"# SharePoint triggers you will use Trigger Fires when Notes When an item is created New list item Polling; good for intake When an item is created or modified Any change Most used; watch for loops When a file is created (properties only) New document Metadata available When an item or file is deleted Deletion Limited data (ID, name) For a selected item / file User clicks in list (or column formatting button) Instant flow with item context When an item or file is modified Changes only Newer trigger SharePoint triggers are polling triggers: Power Automate checks for changes at an interval (often around a minute for premium, longer on some plans), and runs once per changed item. # The infinite loop problem flowchart LR T[Trigger: item created or modified] --> U[Update item: Status = 'Notified'] U -->|modification fires trigger again| T Fix it with a trigger condition so the flow only starts when it needs to: Trigger conditions (Settings → Trigger conditions) Text Copy @equals(triggerOutputs()?['body/Status/Value'], 'Submitted') @not(equals(triggerOutputs()?['body/Editor/Email'], 'svc-flow@contoso.com')) @greater(triggerOutputs()?['body/{VersionNumber}'], '1.0') @and(equals(triggerOutputs()?['body/Severity/Value'], 'Critical'), empty(triggerOutputs()?['body/NotifiedOn'])) All conditions in the list must be true (AND). Use @or(...) inside a single condition for OR logic. Runs that fail the condition do not start , so they don't appear in history or consume runs. Loop-proof patterns Have the flow write with a service account connection, then exclude edits by that account. Use a flag column ( NotifiedOn ) that the trigger condition checks. Prefer When an item is created when you only care about new items. # Concurrency and splitOn Concurrency control (trigger settings) limits parallel runs, for example 1 to process items strictly in order and avoid race conditions on counters. Split on (for array-based triggers) creates one run per item, which is on by default for SharePoint triggers. # Interview questions Q1 How do you prevent a \"created or modified\" flow from triggering itself when it updates the item? Add trigger conditions that exclude the flow's own update, for example when the editor is the service account, or check a status or flag column the flow sets. Alternatively, use a different trigger such as When an item is created . Q2 Where are trigger conditions e"},{"t":"Power Automate Expressions Cheat Sheet (Workflow Definition Language)","c":"power-automate","u":"articles/power-automate/expressions-cheatsheet.html","s":"The expressions you will actually use, covering triggerOutputs, body and outputs, string, date and time zone, logical, collection and JSON functions, null-safe access with ?, formatting numbers and dates, and common recipes.","k":"power automate, expressions, wdl, formatdatetime, coalesce, json, cheat sheet","h":"Referencing data · Strings · Dates and time zones · Logic and nulls · Collections and JSON · Numbers · Recipes · Interview questions · Why do dates in flows often appear \"wrong\" by several hours? · What is the difference between body('X') and outputs('X') ? · How do you avoid errors when a field might be empty?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Fundamentals","b":"Expressions are written in the Workflow Definition Language (WDL) , the same as Azure Logic Apps. Enter them in the expression editor (fx), or prefix them with @ in raw JSON. # Referencing data Getting values Text Copy triggerOutputs()?['body/Title'] // SharePoint trigger field triggerOutputs()?['body/Owner/Email'] // person column email triggerOutputs()?['body/Severity/Value'] // choice column value triggerBody()?['text'] // instant flow input (Power Apps V2 / manual) body('Get_item')?['DueDate'] // output of a named action outputs('Compose_total') // Compose output items('Apply_to_each')?['ID'] // current loop item variables('varApprovers') // variable item()?['Title'] // inside Select / Filter array Always use ? for safe navigation ?['field'] returns null instead of failing when the property is missing. It is essential for optional fields and empty people columns. # Strings Text Text Copy concat('DEV-', formatNumber(triggerOutputs()?['body/ID'], '00000')) // DEV-00042 toUpper(…) / toLower(…) / trim(…) substring('ABCDEF', 1, 3) // BCD replace(variables('s'), ';', ',') split('a;b;c', ';') // [\"a\",\"b\",\"c\"] join(body('Select_emails'), ';') // array → \"x@y;z@y\" indexOf('hello world', 'world') // 6 startsWith(…), endsWith(…), contains(collection or string, value) length('text'), length(body('Get_items')?['value']) uriComponent('a b&c') // URL-encode for HTTP calls # Dates and time zones Dates (UTC by default!) Text Copy utcNow() // 2026-10-08T07:42:10.1234567Z formatDateTime(utcNow(), 'dd-MMM-yyyy HH:mm') // 08-Oct-2026 07:42 convertFromUtc(utcNow(), 'India Standard Time', 'dd/MM/yyyy hh:mm tt') addDays(utcNow(), 7, 'yyyy-MM-dd') addHours(…), addMinutes(…), startOfDay(utcNow()), startOfMonth(utcNow()) dayOfWeek(utcNow()) // 0 = Sunday ticks(…) // for date differences div(sub(ticks(utcNow()), ticks(triggerOutputs()?['body/Created'])), 864000000000) // days between formatDateTime(triggerOutputs()?['body/DueDate'], 'yyyy-MM-dd') // for OData filters # Logic and nulls Conditions Text Copy if(equals(triggerOutputs()?['body/Severity/Value'], 'Critical'), 'P1', 'P2') and(greater(…, 10000), not(empty(…))) or(equals(…), equals(…)) coalesce(triggerOutputs()?['body/Comments'], 'No comments') // first non-null empty(body('Get_items')?['value']) // true if no items equals(triggerOutputs()?['body/IsUrgent'], true) # Collections and JSON Arrays & objects Text Copy first(body("},{"t":"The SharePoint Connector in Depth – Get Items, OData Filters, Files, Folders & Permissions","c":"power-automate","u":"articles/power-automate/sharepoint-connector-odata.html","s":"Master the SharePoint connector actions, covering Get items with OData filter queries for every column type, views and row limits, create/update with people and lookups, file and folder actions, copy/move, and permission-related actions.","k":"power automate, sharepoint connector, odata, get items, filter query, files","h":"Get items: the action you use most · OData filter cheat sheet (use internal names) · Create & update with complex columns · Files and folders · Interview questions · How do you filter Get items by a person column? · Why should you avoid filtering with a Condition or Filter array after Get items?","l":"Intermediate","x":"SharePoint Automation","b":"# Get items: the action you use most Setting Purpose Filter Query OData $filter evaluated server-side , so always use it instead of filtering after Order By Created desc Top Count Rows per call (default 100, max 5,000) Limit Columns by View Only return a view's columns (faster, smaller payloads) Settings → Pagination Fetch beyond Top Count (threshold up to 100,000 on premium plans) # OData filter cheat sheet (use internal names) Filter Query examples OData Copy Status eq 'Open' // choice / text Status ne 'Closed' and Severity eq 'Critical' Amount gt 10000 // number DueDate lt '2026-10-08' // date (yyyy-MM-dd) DueDate le '@{formatDateTime(utcNow(),'yyyy-MM-dd')}' // dynamic date via expression startswith(Title, 'DEV-') // text starts with substringof('pump', Title) // contains (not efficient on big lists) Owner/EMail eq 'jane@contoso.com' // person (EMail, Title) Plant/Id eq 3 // lookup by id (also: Plant/Title eq 'Bangalore') IsActive eq 1 // yes/no Department eq null // empty Created ge datetime'2026-01-01T00:00:00Z' // datetime literal form Internal names and the 5,000 threshold Filter columns by internal name ( Due_x0020_Date if created with a space). On lists with more than 5,000 items, the first filtered column must be indexed or the action fails with a threshold error. # Create & update with complex columns Column In \"Create item\" / \"Update item\" Person Provide the email/claims in the field (e.g. jane@contoso.com ) Multi-person Switch to array input: `[{\"Claims\":\"i:0#.f Lookup Provide the ID of the target item Choice / multi-choice Value text or array of { \"Value\": \"A\" } Managed metadata `Label # Files and folders flowchart LR A[Create file<br/>Folder path + name + content] --> B[Update file properties<br/>by ItemId] C[Get file content] --> D[Convert / send / attach] E[Copy file / Move file<br/>across sites] --> F[Conflict: replace / rename / fail] G[Create new folder] --> H[Grant access to an item or folder] Useful file actions: Get file metadata , Get file content (using path) , Check out/in , Copy file , Move file , Create sharing link for a file or folder , Grant access to an item or a folder , Stop sharing an item or a file . # Interview questions Q1 How do you filter Get items by a person column? Use the person field's sub-property in the OData filter, for example Owner/EMail eq 'jane@contoso.com' (note the capital E in EMail ). The column should"},{"t":"\"Send an HTTP Request to SharePoint\" – REST Recipes for What Standard Actions Can't Do","c":"power-automate","u":"articles/power-automate/send-http-sharepoint.html","s":"Use the standard SharePoint HTTP action to call the REST API, covering break and set permissions, create sites and lists, batch updates, set Created and Modified, copy files across sites, get item version history, and handle responses.","k":"power automate, sharepoint rest, http request, permissions, batch, versioning","h":"Recipe 1 · Break inheritance and grant a group Contribute on an item · Recipe 2 · Set Created By / Modified (migration fix-ups) · Recipe 3 · Create a list with columns · Recipe 4 · Version history of an item · Recipe 5 · Batch update many items in one call · Interview questions · Is \"Send an HTTP request to SharePoint\" a premium action? · How do you grant item-level permissions from a flow?","l":"Advanced","x":"SharePoint Automation","b":"The Send an HTTP request to SharePoint action is a standard (non-premium) connector action that calls the SharePoint REST API as the flow's connection user. It unlocks almost anything the UI can do. How the action works FLOW Method (GET/POST/PATCH/DELETE) Uri relative to site (_api/...) Headers + body (JSON) uses the SharePoint connection (user or service account) SHAREPOINT REST API /_api/web/... No request digest needed (handled) Returns JSON parse NEXT ACTIONS body('Send_an_HTTP_request')?['d'] (verbose) or ?['value']… Parse JSON for typed tokens How the action works # Recipe 1 · Break inheritance and grant a group Contribute on an item 1) Break role inheritance (keep no existing permissions) Text Copy POST _api/web/lists/getbytitle('Deviations')/items(@{triggerOutputs()?['body/ID']})/breakroleinheritance(copyRoleAssignments=false,clearSubscopes=true) Headers: Accept: application/json;odata=nometadata 2) Look up principal and role definition IDs Text Copy GET _api/web/sitegroups/getbyname('Quality Members')?$select=Id GET _api/web/roledefinitions/getbyname('Contribute')?$select=Id 3) Add the role assignment Text Copy POST _api/web/lists/getbytitle('Deviations')/items(@{triggerOutputs()?['body/ID']})/roleassignments/addroleassignment(principalid=@{body('Get_group')?['Id']},roledefid=@{body('Get_role')?['Id']}) # Recipe 2 · Set Created By / Modified (migration fix-ups) ValidateUpdateListItem Text Copy POST _api/web/lists/getbytitle('Deviations')/items(42)/ValidateUpdateListItem Body: { \"formValues\": [ { \"FieldName\": \"Author\", \"FieldValue\": \"[{'Key':'i:0#.f|membership|jane@contoso.com'}]\" }, { \"FieldName\": \"Created\", \"FieldValue\": \"15/03/2024 10:30\" }, { \"FieldName\": \"Editor\", \"FieldValue\": \"[{'Key':'i:0#.f|membership|ravi@contoso.com'}]\" }, { \"FieldName\": \"Modified\", \"FieldValue\": \"20/03/2024 16:05\" } ], \"bNewDocumentUpdate\": true } Dates use the site's regional format . bNewDocumentUpdate: true avoids creating a new version. # Recipe 3 · Create a list with columns Create list + add a choice field Text Copy POST _api/web/lists Body: { \"Title\": \"Audit Findings\", \"BaseTemplate\": 100, \"Description\": \"Created by flow\" } POST _api/web/lists/getbytitle('Audit Findings')/fields/CreateFieldAsXml Body: { \"parameters\": { \"SchemaXml\": \"<Field Type='Choice' DisplayName='Severity' Name='Severity' Format='Dropdown'><CHOICES><CHOICE>Minor</CHOICE><CHOICE>Major</CHOICE></C"},{"t":"Approvals in Power Automate – Sequential, Parallel, Custom Responses and Escalations","c":"power-automate","u":"articles/power-automate/approvals.html","s":"Build real approval processes, covering approval types (first to respond, everyone must approve, custom responses), sequential multi-level approvals, parallel branches, reminders and timeouts, delegation, approvals in Teams, and audit trail patterns.","k":"power automate, approvals, multi-level approval, teams, escalation, timeout","h":"Approval types · Multi-level sequential approval (deviation example) · Reminders, escalation and timeouts · Audit trail · Interview questions · How do you implement a reminder for a pending approval? · What happens if an approval waits longer than 30 days? · Where are approval requests stored?","l":"Intermediate","x":"SharePoint Automation","b":"The Approvals connector creates approval requests that appear in Teams (Approvals app) , Outlook actionable messages , the Power Automate portal and the mobile app. Approval data is stored in Dataverse in the environment. # Approval types Type Behaviour Approve/Reject – Everyone must approve All assigned approvers respond; any reject → rejected Approve/Reject – First to respond One response completes it Custom responses – Wait for all Your own options (Approve, Reject, Need info), all respond Custom responses – Wait for one Your options, first response wins # Multi-level sequential approval (deviation example) flowchart TD T[Deviation submitted] --> L1[Level 1: Line manager<br/>Start and wait for an approval] L1 -->|Reject| R[Update item: Rejected<br/>notify requester] L1 -->|Approve| C{Severity = Critical?} C -->|No| Q[Level 2: QA reviewer] C -->|Yes| P[\"Parallel:<br/>QA Head + Plant Head<br/>(everyone must approve)\"] Q -->|Approve| A[Update item: Approved<br/>stamp approvers & dates] P -->|All approve| A Q -->|Reject| R P -->|Any reject| R Key expressions Text Copy // Outcome of an approval outputs('Start_and_wait_for_an_approval')?['body/outcome'] // \"Approve\" | \"Reject\" | custom // Comments of all responders join(xpath(xml(json(concat('{\"r\":{\"c\":', string(outputs('Start_and_wait_for_an_approval')?['body/responses']), '}}'))), '/r/c/comments/text()'), ' | ') // Simpler: Select action over body/responses → item()?['responder/displayName'] & ': ' & item()?['comments'] # Reminders, escalation and timeouts flowchart LR S[\"Create an approval<br/>(not 'start and wait')\"] --> P{{Parallel branches}} P --> W[Wait for an approval] P --> D[Do until approved or 3 reminders:<br/>Delay 2 days → send reminder] W --> X[Approval completed] D -->|after 3 reminders| E[Escalate: reassign / notify manager] Use Create an approval + Wait for an approval (instead of Start and wait ) when you need reminders in parallel. Configure a timeout on the wait action (Settings → Timeout, ISO 8601 such as P7D ), then handle the timeout with Configure run after → has timed out . Cloud flow runs have a 30-day maximum duration . Approvals waiting longer need a re-start design, for example state stored in the list and the flow re-triggered. # Audit trail Write every decision back to the item: L1Approver , L1Outcome , L1Date , L1Comments , or append to a multi-line \"Approval history\" field. App"},{"t":"Processing Large SharePoint Lists in Power Automate – Pagination, Batching and Throttling","c":"power-automate","u":"articles/power-automate/pagination-large-lists.html","s":"Reliable patterns for lists with tens of thousands of items, covering Top Count vs pagination threshold, Do-until with ID-based paging, Select instead of Apply to each, $batch updates, concurrency, throttling limits and run-duration constraints.","k":"power automate, pagination, large lists, batch, throttling, performance","h":"Option 1 · Built-in pagination · Option 2 · ID-based paging loop (scales indefinitely) · Process without Apply to each · Throttling and limits · Interview questions · How do you retrieve more than 5,000 items from SharePoint in a flow? · Why replace Apply to each with Select?","l":"Advanced","x":"SharePoint Automation","b":"100 default Top Count of Get items 5,000 max items per Get items call 100,000 pagination threshold (premium); lower on some plans 30 days maximum flow run duration # Option 1 · Built-in pagination Get items → Settings → Pagination: On, Threshold: 20000 . Simple, but the whole result lands in memory, and plan limits apply to the threshold. # Option 2 · ID-based paging loop (scales indefinitely) flowchart TD I[Initialize lastId = 0] --> L{Do until<br/>batch is empty} L --> G[\"Get items<br/>Filter: ID gt lastId<br/>Order by: ID asc<br/>Top: 5000\"] G --> P[Process batch<br/>Select / batch update] P --> S[Set lastId = last item ID] S --> L Expressions Text Copy Filter Query: ID gt @{variables('lastId')} Order By: ID asc Top Count: 5000 Set lastId: last(body('Get_items')?['value'])?['ID'] Do until: empty(body('Get_items')?['value']) ID is always indexed, so this works on lists of any size without hitting the threshold. # Process without Apply to each Apply to each over thousands of items is slow (each iteration is billed as actions and executes sequentially by default). Prefer data operations: Select → shape data in one action Text Copy From: body('Get_items')?['value'] Map: { \"Id\": item()?['ID'], \"Title\": item()?['Title'], \"Owner\": item()?['Owner']?['Email'] } Filter array → keep only overdue items: @less(item()?['DueDate'], utcNow()) Create HTML table / CSV table → report in one step When you must write back, use the SharePoint $batch API (see Send HTTP request recipes ) to update up to ~1,000 items per request, or Apply to each with concurrency 20–50 for independent updates. # Throttling and limits Limit Mitigation Power Platform request limits per user/licence per 24 h Use Select/Filter instead of loops; batch; spread over time SharePoint throttling (429) Built-in retry policy (exponential); lower concurrency; run off-hours 30-day run duration, 100,000 loop iterations (Do until) Chunk work; child flows; scheduled incremental processing Action payload size (~100 MB) Limit columns (view), page smaller batches Incremental processing Instead of reprocessing everything nightly, store a watermark (last processed Modified date or ID) in a config list and only query newer items: Modified gt '@{variables('lastRun')}' . # Interview questions Q1 How do you retrieve more than 5,000 items from SharePoint in a flow? Either enable pagination on Get items with a higher thresh"},{"t":"Document Generation with Power Automate – Word Templates, PDF Conversion and Filing","c":"power-automate","u":"articles/power-automate/document-generation-pdf.html","s":"Generate documents from SharePoint data, covering Word Online (Business) Populate a Microsoft Word template with content controls, repeating sections, PDF conversion with OneDrive or Word actions, naming and filing in libraries, and signature options.","k":"power automate, document generation, word template, pdf, content controls","h":"Prepare the template · The flow · Interview questions · How do you fill a table with multiple rows in a Word template via Power Automate? · How do you convert a generated Word file to PDF?","l":"Intermediate","x":"SharePoint Automation","b":"Generating certificates, deviation reports, offer letters or purchase orders is a classic workflow need (previously Nintex DocGen or InfoPath print views). Power Automate does it with a Word template + content controls and a PDF conversion . Document generation pipeline TEMPLATE Word .docx with Plain Text / Picture / Repeating Section… Stored in a Templates library Populate a Microsoft Word template (premium) DATA SharePoint item fields Related items (repeating rows) Images (signatures, logos) Create file → Convert to PDF OUTPUT PDF in the deviation's folder / Reports library Metadata set on the file Email / Teams notification Document generation pipeline # Prepare the template In Word: Developer tab → insert Plain Text Content Controls and set each one's Title/Tag ( DeviationNo , Title , Area , RootCause ). For tables (CAPA actions), wrap a table row in a Repeating Section Content Control containing plain text controls ( Action , Owner , Due ). Save the .docx to a SharePoint library (Templates). # The flow flowchart LR T[For a selected item<br/>Deviations] --> G[Get item + Get CAPA items<br/>filter DeviationId eq ID] G --> S[\"Select → array of<br/>{Action, Owner, Due}\"] S --> W[Populate a Microsoft Word template<br/>fields + repeating section array] W --> C1[Create file: DEV-00042.docx<br/>temp folder] C1 --> P[Convert file → PDF<br/>OneDrive / Word Online] P --> C2[Create file: DEV-00042.pdf<br/>Reports library] C2 --> U[Update file properties<br/>+ delete temp docx] U --> N[Email PDF to QA] Select for the repeating section Text Copy From: body('Get_CAPA_items')?['value'] Map: { \"Action\": item()?['Title'], \"Owner\": item()?['Owner']?['DisplayName'], \"Due\": formatDateTime(item()?['DueDate'],'dd MMM yyyy') } Licensing and alternatives Populate a Microsoft Word template is a premium action. Alternatives: SharePoint Premium content assembly (modern templates from list data), Nintex DocGen, Encodian/Plumsail connectors, or an Azure Function using OpenXML for full control. # Interview questions Q1 How do you fill a table with multiple rows in a Word template via Power Automate? Wrap the table row in a repeating section content control in the template, then pass an array of objects (built with Select) whose property names match the inner content control titles to the Populate a Microsoft Word template action. Q2 How do you convert a generated Word file to PDF? Sav"},{"t":"15 Common SharePoint Flow Recipes – Reminders, Archiving, Folders, Permissions & More","c":"power-automate","u":"articles/power-automate/common-sharepoint-recipes.html","s":"Ready-made patterns for everyday SharePoint automation, covering overdue reminders, weekly digests, archiving old items, auto-creating folders, unique reference numbers, permissions on submit, copying files, syncing lists, and notifying on metadata changes.","k":"power automate, sharepoint, recipes, reminders, archiving, folders, patterns","h":"Recipe deep dive: overdue reminders grouped by owner · Recipe deep dive: unique reference numbers without race conditions · Interview questions · How do you send one reminder email per owner instead of one per item? · What's the safest way to generate unique sequential reference numbers?","l":"Intermediate","x":"SharePoint Automation","b":"1 · Overdue reminders Daily recurrence → Get items (DueDate lt today, Status ne Closed) → group by owner → one email each. 2 · Weekly digest Recurrence Monday 08:00 → items created last 7 days → HTML table → email/Teams. 3 · Archive old items Monthly → items Closed and Modified lt -12 months → copy to Archive list → delete. 4 · Folder per project Item created in Projects → Create new folder in Documents/{Title} → set permissions. 5 · Reference number Item created → compose DEV-yyyy-00000 from ID → update item (trigger condition: RefNo empty). 6 · Lock after approval Approved → break inheritance → readers only + approver group edit. 7 · Copy approved doc Library item Approved → Copy file to Published site library → set metadata. 8 · Field change alert Created or modified → Get changes for an item → if Status changed → notify. 9 · Expiring contracts Daily → EndDate within 30 days → reminder to owner, escalation at 7 days. 10 · Sync two lists Item modified in master → find by key in replica → update or create. 11 · Teams post on news Page published in Site Pages (PromotedState = 2) → post to channel. 12 · Form to list Microsoft Forms response → Get response details → create item (+ attachments to folder). 13 · Email to library Email arrives with attachment in shared mailbox → save attachment → set metadata. 14 · New user onboarding Item in Onboarding list → create tasks in Planner + Teams welcome + add to groups (Graph). 15 · Version cleanup Scheduled → for large libraries trim versions via REST (or use version limits). # Recipe deep dive: overdue reminders grouped by owner flowchart TD R[\"Recurrence: weekdays 08:00<br/>(time zone: India Standard Time)\"] --> G[\"Get items<br/>DueDate lt today and Status ne 'Closed'\"] G --> S1[\"Select: owner emails\"] S1 --> U[\"Compose: union(owners, owners)<br/>→ distinct list\"] U --> A[Apply to each owner] A --> F[Filter array: items for this owner] F --> H[Create HTML table<br/>Title · DueDate · Link] H --> E[Send email V2<br/>'You have N overdue deviations'] Key expressions Text Copy Get items filter: DueDate lt '@{formatDateTime(utcNow(),'yyyy-MM-dd')}' and Status ne 'Closed' Select (owners): item()?['Owner']?['Email'] Distinct owners: union(body('Select_owners'), body('Select_owners')) Filter array: @equals(item()?['Owner']?['Email'], items('Apply_to_each')) Count in subject: length(body('Filter_array')) Link column: concat("},{"t":"Error Handling in Power Automate – Try/Catch/Finally Scopes, Run After and Retry Policies","c":"power-automate","u":"articles/power-automate/error-handling.html","s":"Production-grade error handling for cloud flows, covering configure run after, the Try/Catch/Finally scope pattern, extracting the failed action's error with result(), retry policies, timeouts, terminate, notifications and logging to a list or Teams.","k":"power automate, error handling, try catch, scope, run after, retry policy","h":"Try / Catch / Finally · Getting the real error message · Retry policies and timeouts · Logging pattern · Interview questions · How do you implement try/catch in Power Automate? · Why should a flow end with Terminate: Failed after handling an error?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Advanced Patterns","b":"Flows that fail silently are worse than flows that fail loudly. The standard pattern uses three Scopes wired with Configure run after . # Try / Catch / Finally Scope-based error handling SCOPE: TRY All business actions (Get item, approvals, updates) Catch runs after Try \"has failed\" or \"has timed out\" SCOPE: CATCH Filter failed actions with result('Try') Log error (list / Teams / email) Optional compensation (revert status) Finally runs after Catch \"is successful, has failed, is skipped\" SCOPE: FINALLY Cleanup Terminate: Failed (if catch ran) or Succeeded Scope-based error handling flowchart LR T[Scope: Try] -->|is successful| F[Scope: Finally] T -->|has failed / has timed out| C[Scope: Catch] C -->|any outcome| F F --> X{Catch ran?} X -->|yes| TF[Terminate: Failed<br/>with error message] X -->|no| TS[Terminate: Succeeded] # Getting the real error message Inside Catch Text Copy Filter array: From: result('Try') Where: @equals(item()?['status'], 'Failed') Compose error message: first(body('Filter_failed'))?['error']?['message'] Compose failing action name: first(body('Filter_failed'))?['name'] Link to the run (for the support email): concat('https://make.powerautomate.com/environments/', workflow()?['tags']?['environmentName'], '/flows/', workflow()?['name'], '/runs/', workflow()?['run']?['name']) Nested scopes result() only returns the direct children of the scope. If the failure is inside a nested scope or loop, filter on that inner scope too (or flatten your Try scope). # Retry policies and timeouts Setting (action → Settings) Use Retry policy : default (exponential, 4 retries), fixed, none Transient errors (429, 5xx). Set none for non-idempotent calls Timeout (ISO 8601, e.g. PT10M ) Stop waiting on slow actions and approvals Configure run after Run on success, failure, skipped or timed out Secure inputs/outputs Hide sensitive data in run history # Logging pattern Create item in 'Flow Errors' list Text Copy Title: workflow()?['tags']?['flowDisplayName'] Action: first(body('Filter_failed'))?['name'] Message: first(body('Filter_failed'))?['error']?['message'] RunUrl: outputs('Compose_run_link') ItemId: triggerOutputs()?['body/ID'] A Power BI or list view on the errors list gives operations a single place to monitor every flow. # Interview questions Q1 How do you implement try/catch in Power Automate? Put the main actions in a Try scope, add a Catch scope con"},{"t":"Loops & Performance – Apply to Each, Concurrency, Do Until and Faster Alternatives","c":"power-automate","u":"articles/power-automate/loops-performance.html","s":"How loops consume time and API requests, using degree of parallelism safely, variables inside parallel loops, Do until limits, and replacing loops with Select, Filter array, Join, xpath and batch calls for 10–100× faster flows.","k":"power automate, performance, apply to each, concurrency, select, do until","h":"Apply to each with concurrency · Replace loops with data operations · Do until limits · Interview questions · How can you speed up an Apply to each that updates 2,000 SharePoint items? · Why is appending to a variable inside a parallel Apply to each a problem?","l":"Advanced","x":"Advanced Patterns","b":"Cheat sheet Flow performance rules 01 Filter at the source OData Filter Query, view columns, Top Count, not Condition inside loops. 02 Select instead of loop Reshape arrays in one action (no per-item cost). 03 Concurrency Apply to each → Settings → Degree of parallelism up to 50 for independent iterations. 04 No variables in parallel loops Append to array variable inside a parallel loop is unsafe (race); use Compose + Select after. 05 Batch writes SharePoint $batch / Dataverse batch (ExecuteMultiple-style) instead of one call per row. 06 Child flows Split long processes; run parts in parallel. # Apply to each with concurrency flowchart LR subgraph Sequential[\"Sequential (default)\"] a1[item 1] --> a2[item 2] --> a3[item 3] --> a4[...] end subgraph Parallel[\"Degree of parallelism = 20\"] direction TB b1[item 1] b2[item 2] b3[item 3] b4[... 20 at once] end Variables and parallelism Set variable and Increment variable are not thread-safe inside parallel Apply to each. Results become unpredictable. Use Compose inside the loop and collect outputs afterwards with a Select over outputs('Compose') , or keep that loop sequential. # Replace loops with data operations Goal Loop version Loop-free version Build an email list Apply to each + Append to string Select emails → join(body('Select'), ';') Keep matching rows Apply to each + Condition Filter array Sum a column Apply to each + Increment xpath(xml(json(...)), 'sum(/root/item/Amount)') or Select + add() chain Distinct values Loop + contains check union(arr, arr) Lookup between two arrays Nested loops Select with body('Filter') per item, or JSON object dictionary # Do until limits Default limits: 60 iterations and 1 hour ( PT1H ). Change them in the loop's Change limits (up to 5,000 iterations and the 30-day run limit). Always add a safety exit condition to prevent endless loops. # Interview questions Q1 How can you speed up an Apply to each that updates 2,000 SharePoint items? Filter the items server-side first, enable concurrency (for example 20–50) if updates are independent, avoid variables inside the loop, or replace per-item updates with a SharePoint $batch request built with Select and join. Q2 Why is appending to a variable inside a parallel Apply to each a problem? Iterations run simultaneously and variable actions are not atomic, so updates can be lost or interleaved. Use Compose inside the loop and aggregate"},{"t":"Working with JSON in Power Automate – Parse JSON, Select, Filter Array, Compose and Schemas","c":"power-automate","u":"articles/power-automate/json-parse-select.html","s":"Handle API responses and arrays confidently, covering Parse JSON schemas (and making them null-tolerant), Select for reshaping, Filter array, building objects with Compose, json(), string(), and common data-operation recipes.","k":"power automate, json, parse json, select, filter array, compose, data operations","h":"Parse JSON with a forgiving schema · Select: reshape arrays · Filter array · Compose objects for HTTP calls · Interview questions · Why does Parse JSON fail with \"Invalid type. Expected String but got Null\"? · What is the difference between Select and Apply to each?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Advanced Patterns","b":"The data operations toolkit INPUT HTTP / connector response body Trigger payload Arrays from Get items type it PARSE JSON Schema → dynamic content tokens Validation of shape reshape SELECT / FILTER ARRAY / JOIN Map arrays Filter rows Array → text build COMPOSE Objects & arrays Expressions Debug output The data operations toolkit # Parse JSON with a forgiving schema Generate the schema from a sample payload (\"Use sample payload\"), then make it null-tolerant . Generated schemas mark types strictly and fail when a field is null : Null-tolerant schema snippet JSON Copy { \"type\" : \"object\" , \"properties\" : { \"id\" : { \"type\" : \"integer\" } , \"title\" : { \"type\" : [ \"string\" , \"null\" ] } , \"owner\" : { \"type\" : [ \"object\" , \"null\" ] , \"properties\" : { \"email\" : { \"type\" : [ \"string\" , \"null\" ] } } } , \"tags\" : { \"type\" : [ \"array\" , \"null\" ] , \"items\" : { \"type\" : \"string\" } } } , \"required\" : [ \"id\" ] } # Select: reshape arrays Select examples Text Copy From: body('Get_items')?['value'] Map (key-value mode): Id → item()?['ID'] Title → item()?['Title'] Owner → item()?['Owner']?['Email'] DueText → formatDateTime(item()?['DueDate'], 'dd MMM yyyy') Map (text mode – produce a simple array of strings): item()?['Owner']?['Email'] # Filter array Advanced mode conditions Text Copy @and(equals(item()?['Status']?['Value'], 'Open'), less(item()?['DueDate'], utcNow())) @contains(createArray('Critical','Major'), item()?['Severity']?['Value']) # Compose objects for HTTP calls Compose (object) JSON Copy { \"title\" : \"@{triggerOutputs()?[&#x27;body/Title&#x27;]}\" , \"severity\" : \"@{triggerOutputs()?[&#x27;body/Severity/Value&#x27;]}\" , \"approvers\" : @ { body( &#x27;Select_approver_emails&#x27; ) } , \"dueDate\" : \"@{formatDateTime(addDays(utcNow(), 14), &#x27;yyyy-MM-dd&#x27;)}\" } Debug with Compose Drop a Compose with an expression to inspect intermediate values in run history. It is the flow equivalent of console.log . # Interview questions Q1 Why does Parse JSON fail with \"Invalid type. Expected String but got Null\"? The schema generated from a sample marks the property as string , but the real payload contains null . Change the type to [\"string\",\"null\"] (or remove the type constraint) for optional fields. Q2 What is the difference between Select and Apply to each? Select maps every element of an array into a new shape in a single action, without per-item actions. Apply to each execut"},{"t":"Child Flows – Reusable Building Blocks with Run a Child Flow","c":"power-automate","u":"articles/power-automate/child-flows.html","s":"Structure complex automation with child flows, covering requirements (solutions, manual trigger, response), passing inputs and returning outputs, connection usage, error propagation, and patterns such as a shared notification or logging service.","k":"power automate, child flows, reuse, solutions, modular design","h":"Requirements · Designing child flows like functions · Interview questions · What must a child flow contain to be callable and return data? · What happens if the child flow fails before responding?","l":"Advanced","x":"Advanced Patterns","b":"A child flow is a flow called by another flow with Run a Child Flow . It keeps parent flows short and readable and centralizes shared logic, such as \"send a branded notification\", \"write an audit log\" or \"resolve approvers for a department\". sequenceDiagram participant P as Parent flow (Deviation process) participant C1 as Child: Resolve approvers participant C2 as Child: Send notification participant C3 as Child: Write audit log P->>C1: Run a Child Flow (department, severity) C1-->>P: Respond to a PowerApp or flow (approverEmails) P->>C2: Run a Child Flow (to, subject, html) C2-->>P: {status: \"sent\"} P->>C3: Run a Child Flow (itemId, action, actor) C3-->>P: {logId} # Requirements Parent and child must be in the same solution . The child uses the Manually trigger a flow trigger (with inputs) and ends with Respond to a PowerApp or flow (outputs). In the child flow's Run only users settings, set connections to use a specific connection (not \"provided by run-only user\") so it runs with its own connection. # Designing child flows like functions Rule Why Small, single-purpose Easier testing and reuse Explicit inputs/outputs Clear contract (like a function signature) Always respond (also in Catch) Parent otherwise waits until timeout Return a status + message Parent can branch on failure Version through solutions Changes deploy consistently # Interview questions Q1 What must a child flow contain to be callable and return data? A Manually trigger a flow trigger with defined inputs, and a Respond to a PowerApp or flow action with outputs. Both flows must be in the same solution, and the child must use embedded connections rather than run-only user connections. Q2 What happens if the child flow fails before responding? The parent's Run a Child Flow action fails (or waits until timeout if the child hangs). Handle errors inside the child with a Try/Catch pattern and always send a response with a status."},{"t":"Professional Email Reports – HTML Tables, Styling, Teams Messages and CSV Attachments","c":"power-automate","u":"articles/power-automate/html-table-reports.html","s":"Turn list data into polished scheduled reports, covering Create HTML table with custom columns, injecting CSS, conditional formatting with replace(), Teams posts, CSV/Excel attachments and accessibility tips.","k":"power automate, html table, email, reports, csv, teams","h":"Interview questions · How do you add hyperlinks to a Create HTML table output? · How do you style an HTML table in an email from a flow?","l":"Beginner","x":"Advanced Patterns","b":"flowchart LR R[Recurrence Mon 08:00] --> G[Get items: open deviations] G --> S[Select: Title, Owner, Due, Link] S --> H[Create HTML table] H --> C[Compose: CSS + table + summary] C --> E[Send email V2] S --> V[Create CSV table → attachment] C --> T[Post in Teams channel] Select with a clickable link column Text Copy Deviation → item()?['Title'] Owner → item()?['Owner']?['DisplayName'] Due → formatDateTime(item()?['DueDate'], 'dd MMM yyyy') Link → concat('LINK_START', item()?['{Link}'], 'LINK_END') Compose: styled email body HTML Copy < style > table { border-collapse : collapse; font-family : Segoe UI, Arial, sans-serif; font-size : 13px ; width : 100% ; } th { background : #0f6cbd ; color : #fff ; text-align : left; padding : 8px 10px ; } td { border-bottom : 1px solid #e2e8f0 ; padding : 8px 10px ; } tr :nth-child (even) td { background : #f6f8fb ; } </ style > < p > Hello team, here are the < b > @{length(body(&#x27;Select&#x27;))} </ b > open deviations as of @{convertFromUtc(utcNow(),&#x27;India Standard Time&#x27;,&#x27;dd MMM yyyy&#x27;)}. </ p > @{replace(replace(replace(replace(body(&#x27;Create_HTML_table&#x27;), &#x27;LINK_START&#x27;, &#x27; < a href = \"&#x27;), &#x27;LINK_END&#x27;, &#x27;\" > Open </ a > &#x27;), &#x27; &lt; &#x27;, &#x27;<&#x27;), &#x27; &gt; &#x27;, &#x27;>&#x27;)} < p style = \"color:#6b7280\" > Automated report · Quality Operations </ p > Create HTML table HTML-encodes cell content, so links are injected with placeholders that are replaced afterwards, as shown above. Excel instead of CSV For formatted Excel output, write rows to a table in an Excel template stored in SharePoint (Excel Online connector), or generate CSV with Create CSV table for a quick attachment ( name: deviations.csv , content: body('Create_CSV_table') ). # Interview questions Q1 How do you add hyperlinks to a Create HTML table output? Use placeholder text in the Select output (for example LINK_START…LINK_END ), then replace() the placeholders and the HTML-encoded characters in the table output with real <a href=\"…\"> markup before sending the email. Q2 How do you style an HTML table in an email from a flow? Wrap the table in a Compose that includes a <style> block (or inline styles) and use that Compose as the email body. Most email clients render basic CSS such as borders, padding and header colours."},{"t":"Calling Microsoft Graph from Power Automate – HTTP with Entra ID, Office 365 Groups Action and Custom Connectors","c":"power-automate","u":"articles/power-automate/graph-http.html","s":"Three ways to call Graph from flows (the premium HTTP action with app credentials, the 'Send an HTTP request' actions in Office 365 connectors, and a custom connector), with app registration, certificates in Key Vault, examples for users, groups, Teams and Planner, and paging.","k":"power automate, microsoft graph, http, entra id, app registration, teams, planner","h":"Three options · HTTP action with an app registration · Useful Graph calls from flows · Paging Graph results · Interview questions · What's the difference between delegated and application permissions when calling Graph from a flow? · How do you keep the client secret out of the flow definition?","l":"Advanced","x":"Integration","b":"# Three options Option Auth Licence When Office 365 Groups / Outlook \"Send an HTTP request\" Delegated (connection user), limited Graph segments Standard Group, calendar and mail calls the connector allows HTTP action + Entra app (client credentials) Application permissions Premium Admin automation: users, groups, Teams, Planner, reports Custom connector for Graph Delegated OAuth Premium Reusable maker-friendly actions # HTTP action with an app registration Flow → Graph with application permissions FLOW HTTP action (Active Directory OAuth) Secrets from Azure Key Vault connector or environment… client credentials token for https://graph.microsoft.com ENTRA APP REGISTRATION Application permissions (e.g. Group.ReadWrite.All) Admin consent Certificate or secret calls MICROSOFT GRAPH /users /groups /teams /planner /sites Flow → Graph with application permissions HTTP action configuration Text Copy Method: POST URI: https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0/teams/@{variables('teamId')}/channels Headers: Content-Type: application/json Body: { \"displayName\": \"@{triggerOutputs()?['body/Title']}\", \"membershipType\": \"standard\" } Authentication: Active Directory OAuth Authority: https://login.microsoftonline.com Tenant: <tenant id> Audience: https://graph.microsoft.com Client ID: <app id> Credential: Secret (from Key Vault 'Get secret') or Certificate (pfx base64 + password) # Useful Graph calls from flows Examples HTTP Copy GET https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0/users/jane@contoso.com?$select=displayName,department,manager GET https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0/users/jane@contoso.com/manager?$select=mail,displayName POST https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0/groups/{group-id}/members/$ref { \"@odata.id\": \"https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0/directoryObjects/{user-id}\" } POST https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0/planner/tasks { \"planId\": \"{plan}\", \"bucketId\": \"{bucket}\", \"title\": \"Investigate DEV-00042\", \"assignments\": { \"{user-id}\": { \"@odata.type\": \"#microsoft.graph.plannerAssignment\", \"orderHint\": \" !\" } } } GET https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0/sites/{site-id}/lists/{list-id}/items?$expand=fields&$top=200 # Paging Graph results Graph returns @odata.nextLink . Loop with Do until empty(variables('nextLink')) , call HTTP with the link, append body('HTTP')?['value'] to an array variable, and set nextLink to body('HTTP')?['@odata.nextLink'] . Least privilege Application permissions such as Group.Re"},{"t":"Adaptive Cards in Microsoft Teams with Power Automate – Post, Wait for Response, Update","c":"power-automate","u":"articles/power-automate/adaptive-cards-teams.html","s":"Design adaptive cards for Teams, post them as the Flow bot, wait for user responses with inputs, update the card after action, and build a full in-Teams deviation triage experience.","k":"power automate, adaptive cards, teams, flow bot, notifications, approvals","h":"Interview questions · How does a flow wait for a user's input in Teams? · Approvals connector or adaptive card?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Integration","b":"Adaptive Cards are JSON-defined UI cards. With the Teams connector, a flow can post a card to a user or channel and wait for a response (buttons and inputs) without anyone leaving Teams. sequenceDiagram participant F as Flow participant T as Teams (Flow bot) actor Q as QA Lead F->>T: Post adaptive card and wait for a response (channel) T-->>Q: Card: DEV-00042 · Critical · [Accept] [Reassign ▾] [Comment] Q->>T: Selects \"Accept\", adds comment T-->>F: Response body (action, inputs, responder) F->>F: Update SharePoint item (Owner, Status) F->>T: Update card / reply \"Accepted by Q\" Card JSON (paste into the action; dynamic content via @{...}) JSON Copy { \"type\" : \"AdaptiveCard\" , \"$schema\" : \"http://adaptivecards.io/schemas/adaptive-card.json\" , \"version\" : \"1.4\" , \"body\" : [ { \"type\" : \"TextBlock\" , \"text\" : \"New critical deviation\" , \"weight\" : \"Bolder\" , \"size\" : \"Large\" , \"color\" : \"Attention\" } , { \"type\" : \"FactSet\" , \"facts\" : [ { \"title\" : \"ID\" , \"value\" : \"@{triggerOutputs()?[&#x27;body/RefNo&#x27;]}\" } , { \"title\" : \"Title\" , \"value\" : \"@{triggerOutputs()?[&#x27;body/Title&#x27;]}\" } , { \"title\" : \"Area\" , \"value\" : \"@{triggerOutputs()?[&#x27;body/Area/Value&#x27;]}\" } , { \"title\" : \"Reported by\" , \"value\" : \"@{triggerOutputs()?[&#x27;body/Author/DisplayName&#x27;]}\" } ] } , { \"type\" : \"Input.ChoiceSet\" , \"id\" : \"owner\" , \"label\" : \"Assign investigator\" , \"style\" : \"compact\" , \"choices\" : [ { \"title\" : \"Ravi Kumar\" , \"value\" : \"ravi@contoso.com\" } , { \"title\" : \"Ana Silva\" , \"value\" : \"ana@contoso.com\" } ] } , { \"type\" : \"Input.Text\" , \"id\" : \"comment\" , \"label\" : \"Comment\" , \"isMultiline\" : true } ] , \"actions\" : [ { \"type\" : \"Action.Submit\" , \"title\" : \"Accept & assign\" , \"data\" : { \"decision\" : \"accept\" } } , { \"type\" : \"Action.OpenUrl\" , \"title\" : \"Open in SharePoint\" , \"url\" : \"@{triggerOutputs()?[&#x27;body/{Link}&#x27;]}\" } ] } Reading the response Text Copy body('Post_adaptive_card_and_wait_for_a_response')?['data']?['decision'] body('Post_adaptive_card_and_wait_for_a_response')?['data']?['owner'] body('Post_adaptive_card_and_wait_for_a_response')?['responder']?['userPrincipalName'] Design with the Adaptive Cards Designer Build and preview the card at adaptivecards.io/designer (host app: Microsoft Teams), then paste the JSON. Keep to Adaptive Card version 1.4 or lower for broad Teams support, and test on mobile. # Interview questions Q1 How does"},{"t":"Dataverse in Power Automate – Triggers, Filtering Rows, Actions and Bound/Unbound Actions","c":"power-automate","u":"articles/power-automate/dataverse-triggers.html","s":"Automate Dataverse with 'When a row is added, modified or deleted' (scope, select columns, filter rows), list rows with FetchXML and OData, relate rows, perform bound and unbound actions, and compare flows with plug-ins.","k":"power automate, dataverse, triggers, fetchxml, odata, plug-ins","h":"The trigger, configured well · List rows: OData or FetchXML · Flows vs plug-ins · Interview questions · How do you stop a Dataverse-triggered flow from looping when it updates the same row? · When should you use a plug-in instead of a flow?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Integration","b":"# The trigger, configured well Setting Use Change type Added, Modified, Deleted (or combinations) Table e.g. Deviations Scope User / Business unit / Parent-child BU / Organization Select columns ci_status,ci_severity , which fires only when these columns change (prevents loops) Filter rows OData: ci_severity eq 100000002 and statecode eq 0 Run as (in solutions) Flow owner / modifying user / row owner flowchart LR E[Row modified in Dataverse] --> SC{Changed columns<br/>∩ Select columns?} SC -- no --> X[No run] SC -- yes --> FR{Filter rows<br/>matches?} FR -- no --> X FR -- yes --> RUN[Flow run] # List rows: OData or FetchXML OData in 'List rows' Text Copy Select columns: ci_name,ci_duedate,_ci_plant_value Filter rows: statecode eq 0 and ci_duedate lt @{utcNow()} Sort by: ci_duedate asc Expand Query: ci_Plant($select=ci_name) Row count: 5000 (+ pagination for more) FetchXML (aggregates, linked entities) XML Copy < fetch aggregate = \"true\" > < entity name = \"ci_deviation\" > < attribute name = \"ci_deviationid\" alias = \"count\" aggregate = \"count\" /> < attribute name = \"ci_severity\" alias = \"severity\" groupby = \"true\" /> < filter > < condition attribute = \"statecode\" operator = \"eq\" value = \"0\" /> </ filter > </ entity > </ fetch > # Flows vs plug-ins Power Automate Plug-in (C#) Timing Asynchronous (seconds later) Synchronous or async, in transaction Can block the save ❌ ✅ (throw InvalidPluginExecutionException) Skill Maker Developer Integration with other services ✅ connectors Limited (HTTP from sandbox) Best for Notifications, integrations, approvals Validation, calculations that must be atomic # Interview questions Q1 How do you stop a Dataverse-triggered flow from looping when it updates the same row? Set Select columns on the trigger to only the columns that should start the flow, excluding the columns the flow updates, and/or add Filter rows so the flow ignores rows already processed. Q2 When should you use a plug-in instead of a flow? When logic must run synchronously inside the transaction , for example to block an invalid save, guarantee consistency across rows, or calculate values before commit. Flows run asynchronously after the change."},{"t":"Desktop Flows (RPA) – Power Automate Desktop, Attended vs Unattended and Machine Setup","c":"power-automate","u":"articles/power-automate/desktop-flows-rpa.html","s":"Automate legacy Windows and web applications with Power Automate Desktop, covering UI elements and selectors, recorder, error handling, calling desktop flows from cloud flows, machines and machine groups, attended vs unattended runs, and governance.","k":"power automate desktop, rpa, desktop flows, unattended, attended, machine groups","h":"Good RPA practices · Interview questions · What is the difference between attended and unattended desktop flows? · When is RPA the right choice?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Integration","b":"Desktop flows automate applications that have no API : an old ERP client, a mainframe terminal, a web portal of a supplier, or Excel macros. Power Automate Desktop (PAD) is free on Windows for building and running attended flows. Running them from cloud flows and unattended requires premium licensing. RPA architecture CLOUD FLOW Trigger: SharePoint item / schedule Run a flow built with Power Automate… Pass inputs receive outputs via on-premises machine registration (no inbound ports) MACHINE / MACHINE GROUP Windows VM with PAD agent Attended (user signed in) or unattended (headless… Queue & load balancing drives APPLICATIONS Legacy desktop app (UI elements) Web portal (browser automation) Excel / files RPA architecture Attended Unattended User present ✅ (runs in the user's session) ❌ (VM signs in with a machine account) Typical use Assist a user (copy data while they work) Back-office batch processing Licence Premium per user (RPA attended) Process / hosted RPA capacity Scale One user Machine groups, hosted machines # Good RPA practices Prefer APIs or connectors when they exist. RPA is the fallback for systems without APIs. Use robust selectors (avoid indexes and window titles that change), and add waits on UI elements rather than fixed delays. Wrap steps in On block error handlers, take screenshots on failure, and return a status to the cloud flow. Store credentials in Azure Key Vault or credential assets, never in flows. Run unattended flows on dedicated VMs with locked-down service accounts. # Interview questions Q1 What is the difference between attended and unattended desktop flows? Attended flows run in an active user session (the user is present and can watch or interact). Unattended flows run on a machine without a signed-in user. The agent signs in with configured credentials, which suits scheduled back-office automation at scale. Q2 When is RPA the right choice? When the target system has no usable API or connector and the process is rules-based and stable. If an API exists, a cloud flow or custom connector is more reliable and cheaper to maintain."},{"t":"Solution-Aware Flows – Connection References, Environment Variables and Deployment","c":"power-automate","u":"articles/power-automate/solutions-env-variables.html","s":"Build flows that deploy cleanly across dev, test and prod, covering creating flows inside solutions, connection references, environment variables for site URLs and list names, run-only users and service accounts, and post-deployment checks.","k":"power automate, solutions, environment variables, connection references, alm, deployment","h":"Using environment variables in SharePoint actions · Service accounts and ownership · Post-deployment checklist · Interview questions · Why use environment variables for SharePoint site and list in flows? · What's the risk of building production flows under a personal account?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Enterprise & Governance","b":"Flows created outside solutions (\"My flows\") are tied to a person and cannot be promoted cleanly. Production flows belong in solutions with connection references and environment variables . A deployable SharePoint flow SOLUTION: CONTOSO QUALITY Cloud flow Connection reference: SharePoint (svc… Environment variables: ci_SiteUrl ci_DeviationsList deploy managed TEST ENVIRONMENT ci_SiteUrl = .../sites/quality-test ci_DeviationsList = list GUID (test) Connection → svc-flow-test deploy managed PRODUCTION ci_SiteUrl = .../sites/quality ci_DeviationsList = list GUID (prod) Connection → svc-flow-prod A deployable SharePoint flow # Using environment variables in SharePoint actions In the solution: New → More → Environment variable , type Data source → SharePoint → Site (and another for List ). In SharePoint actions, choose Enter custom value for Site Address and List Name and select the environment variables from dynamic content. On import, the deployment asks for (or the settings file supplies) the values for that environment. Expressions Text Copy parameters('Site URL (ci_SiteUrl)') // in the flow, env variables appear as parameters # Service accounts and ownership Production connection references should use a service account (licensed appropriately, MFA-protected, with conditional access), not a developer's personal account. Add a co-owner group to every production flow so it survives people leaving. Turn on flow run notifications for failures, or centralize via the CoE kit. # Post-deployment checklist Connection references bound and connections healthy Environment variable values set (no dev URLs in prod) Flows turned on (imports may leave them off) Run a smoke test item, verify run history Owners and run-only users set correctly # Interview questions Q1 Why use environment variables for SharePoint site and list in flows? Because site URLs and list IDs differ between dev, test and prod. With environment variables, the same managed solution deploys to every environment and you only set values at import time, with no flow edits. Q2 What's the risk of building production flows under a personal account? When that person leaves or their licence or password changes, connections break and flows stop. Ownership, connection and run history are tied to one user. Use solutions, service-account connection references and co-owner groups."},{"t":"Power Platform Governance – DLP, Environments, the CoE Starter Kit and Monitoring","c":"power-automate","u":"articles/power-automate/governance-coe.html","s":"Govern Power Automate and Power Apps at scale with an environment strategy, DLP policies, Managed Environments, the Center of Excellence (CoE) Starter Kit inventory and nudges, maker onboarding, and operational monitoring.","k":"governance, coe starter kit, dlp, managed environments, power platform admin","h":"Environment strategy (recommended baseline) · CoE Starter Kit highlights · Monitoring flows in production · Interview questions · What is the CoE Starter Kit? · How would you secure the default environment?","l":"Advanced","x":"Enterprise & Governance","b":"Governance operating model POLICY Environment strategy DLP policies Licensing rules Naming & ownership enforced by PLATFORM CONTROLS Managed Environments Tenant isolation Conditional Access Sharing limits observed by COE STARTER KIT Inventory of apps/flows/connectors Orphaned resources Compliance nudges Power BI dashboards nurtured by COMMUNITY Maker onboarding & training Champions network Templates & reusable components Governance operating model # Environment strategy (recommended baseline) Environment Who DLP Default (renamed \"Personal Productivity\") All makers Strict: only Microsoft 365 business connectors Team / department dev Requested by teams Moderate Shared test & prod per solution area Admins + pro devs Business connectors needed by the solutions Developer environments Individual pro devs As needed # CoE Starter Kit highlights Core : sync of all environments, apps, flows, connectors and makers into Dataverse; Power BI dashboard. Governance : compliance process (makers justify business-critical apps), orphaned object reassignment, inactivity cleanup. Nurture : welcome emails, training in a day, maker assessments, templates. # Monitoring flows in production Centralized error logging (see Error handling ) plus failure alerts. Power Platform admin center analytics for runs and errors. Application Insights integration for Dataverse and cloud flow telemetry (where available) for enterprise monitoring. Regular review of connections owned by leavers and of flows with high failure rates. # Interview questions Q1 What is the CoE Starter Kit? A Microsoft-provided set of solutions (apps, flows, Dataverse tables, Power BI reports) that inventories Power Platform resources across the tenant and supports governance processes: compliance reviews, orphan cleanup, maker nudges and adoption. Q2 How would you secure the default environment? Rename it to signal personal productivity, apply a strict DLP policy (Microsoft 365 connectors only as business, block risky connectors), restrict environment creation, and route business-critical solutions to dedicated managed environments."},{"t":"Power Automate Limits, Throttling and Licensing – What Really Constrains Your Flows","c":"power-automate","u":"articles/power-automate/limits-licensing.html","s":"The limits that matter in design, covering run duration, action and loop limits, request allocations per licence, connector throttling, payload sizes, retention, and an overview of licensing models (Microsoft 365, Premium per user, Process, pay-as-you-go).","k":"power automate, limits, throttling, licensing, request limits, api calls","h":"Limits that drive design · Licensing (orientation only) · Interview questions · What happens to a flow waiting on an approval for 35 days? · How do request limits affect flow design?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Enterprise & Governance","b":"30 days max run duration 500 max actions per flow definition 100,000 max Apply to each items (default 5,000 on some plans) 28 days run history retention # Limits that drive design Limit Impact Design response Run duration 30 days Long approvals time out Persist state, restart flows, set approval timeouts Power Platform request limits (per licence per 24h) Heavy flows get slowed down Fewer actions: Select, batch, filters Connector throttling (e.g. SharePoint calls per connection per minute) 429 errors, retries Concurrency limits, spread load, multiple service connections Message size (~100 MB) Large files fail Chunking, work with links not content Apply to each concurrency (max 50) Throughput ceiling Batch APIs, child flows in parallel Do until defaults (60 / 1h) Loops stop early Change limits explicitly # Licensing (orientation only) Model Covers Microsoft 365 / Office 365 seeded rights Cloud flows with standard connectors in M365 context Power Automate Premium (per user) Premium connectors, attended RPA, process mining (limited) Power Automate Process (per bot/flow) Unattended RPA or a business-critical flow licensed independently of users Pay-as-you-go Metered via Azure subscription per run or per flow Power Apps licences Flows in the context of a licensed app Validate against the current licensing guide Microsoft revises Power Platform licences and request limits periodically. Check the current Power Platform Licensing Guide and admin center capacity reports before finalizing an architecture. # Interview questions Q1 What happens to a flow waiting on an approval for 35 days? It fails at the 30-day maximum run duration. Design long-running processes with persisted state, escalation timeouts and restartable flows. Q2 How do request limits affect flow design? Every action, including each iteration of a loop, counts toward the user's or flow's daily request allocation. Chatty designs get throttled, so minimize actions with server-side filters, Select, Filter array and batch requests."},{"t":"Migrating SharePoint 2010/2013 & Nintex Workflows to Power Automate","c":"power-automate","u":"articles/power-automate/migrate-legacy-workflows.html","s":"A practical method to move legacy workflows, covering inventory with the Microsoft 365 Assessment tool, classifying by complexity, mapping SPD and Nintex actions to Power Automate, handling tasks and history lists, approvals, impersonation steps, and the cut-over sequence.","k":"power automate, migration, sharepoint designer, 2013 workflows, nintex, legacy","h":"Method · Classify by complexity · Action mapping (SPD 2013 → Power Automate) · Data that needs special care · Cut-over checklist · Interview questions · How do you replace a SharePoint Designer \"impersonation step\" in Power Automate? · How do you migrate a state-machine workflow? · How do you find all classic workflows in a tenant?","l":"Advanced","x":"Enterprise & Governance","b":"SharePoint 2010 workflows were retired in SharePoint Online in 2020 and 2013 workflows on 2 April 2026 . On-premises they still run on Workflow Manager, but they are frozen technology. Here is a repeatable method to move them, including Nintex workflows when the target is Power Automate. # Method flowchart LR I[1 · Inventory<br/>Assessment tool / Nintex reports] --> C[2 · Classify<br/>retire · rebuild · redesign] C --> M[3 · Map actions<br/>& data] M --> B[4 · Build in solutions<br/>env vars, service account] B --> T[5 · Test with<br/>real scenarios] T --> X[6 · Cut over<br/>stop old, start new] X --> D[7 · Decommission<br/>remove old workflows] # Classify by complexity Class Typical content Effort Retire Unused (no runs in 6–12 months), duplicates Remove Simple Notify on create/change, set a field Hours, or use list rules Medium One or two approvals, a few conditions Days Complex State machines, loops, impersonation, web service calls, document generation Redesign; consider Nintex NAC or custom code # Action mapping (SPD 2013 → Power Automate) SharePoint Designer action Power Automate equivalent Send an email Send an email (V2) (Outlook) / Send email notification (V3) Set field in current item / Update list item Update item Create list item Create item Assign a task / Start a task process Start and wait for an approval (or Create approval + Wait) Wait for field change in current item Separate flow triggered on modification + trigger condition Pause for duration / until date Delay / Delay until Call HTTP web service Send an HTTP request to SharePoint / HTTP (premium) Build dictionary / Get item from dictionary Compose (JSON object) / expressions Loop n times / Loop with condition Apply to each / Do until Go to stage (state machine) Switch on a Status column + Do until, or split into several flows App step / Impersonation step Use a service account connection Log to history list Create item in a log list / Application Insights Set workflow status Update a status column on the item For Nintex actions, see Nintex to Power Automate comparison . # Data that needs special care In-flight instances : decide whether to let old workflows finish, or migrate status into list columns and let the new flow continue from there. Task lists and history lists : export what you must keep for audit (CSV or archive list) before removing workflows. Approvers in lookups/groups : re"},{"t":"What is Microsoft Dataverse? Architecture, Capabilities and When to Use It","c":"dataverse","u":"articles/dataverse/overview.html","s":"Dataverse explained, covering what it stores, the services behind it (relational, file, search, logs), tables and metadata, the security model at a glance, APIs, solutions and how it powers Power Apps, Power Automate, Copilot Studio and Dynamics 365.","k":"dataverse, common data service, architecture, power platform, dynamics 365, overview","h":"Architecture · Core concepts · Dataverse vs a plain database · Interview questions · What was Dataverse called before and when did it change? · What storage capacity types does Dataverse have? · Name three ways to access Dataverse data programmatically.","l":"Beginner","x":"Fundamentals","b":"Microsoft Dataverse (called the Common Data Service until November 2020) is the managed data platform of the Power Platform and Dynamics 365. It is far more than a database: it bundles storage, metadata, security, business logic, auditing, APIs and integration as a service. # Architecture Inside Dataverse CLIENTS Model-driven & canvas apps Power Automate Copilot Studio agents Power Pages Custom code (Web API / SDK) APIs DATAVERSE SERVICE LAYER Web API (OData v4) Organization service (.NET SDK) TDS endpoint (read-only SQL) Search (Dataverse search) enforces PLATFORM FEATURES Security roles & business units Business rules & plug-ins Auditing Duplicate detection Solutions (ALM) stores in POLYGLOT STORAGE Relational (Azure SQL) for rows Azure Blob for files & images Search index Log storage (audit, plug-in traces) Inside Dataverse 3 capacity types: Database, File, Log 4,000+ standard tables incl. Dynamics 365 (when installed) OData v4 Web API standard Row + column level security # Core concepts Concept What it is Environment Container with one Dataverse database Table (formerly entity) Standard (Account, Contact, User) or custom ( cr8a1_deviation ) Column (formerly field/attribute) Typed: text, number, choice, lookup, file, image, formula… Relationship 1:N, N:1, N:N with cascade behaviour Row (formerly record) A data item with a GUID primary key Publisher prefix Your namespace for custom components ( cr8a1_ , ci_ ) Solution Package of components for ALM # Dataverse vs a plain database Need Dataverse gives you Security Roles, BUs, teams, column security, hierarchy security without writing code UI Model-driven apps and forms generated from metadata Logic Business rules, plug-ins, flows, formula columns Integration Connectors, Web API, events (webhooks, Service Bus), Synapse/Fabric Link Governance Auditing, solutions, managed environments AI Copilot features, Dataverse search, Copilot Studio knowledge # Interview questions Q1 What was Dataverse called before and when did it change? It was the Common Data Service (CDS) , renamed Microsoft Dataverse in November 2020. (Dataverse for Teams was announced in 2020 as well.) Q2 What storage capacity types does Dataverse have? Database (table rows), File (attachments, file and image columns), and Log (audit logs and plug-in traces). Each is tracked separately against the tenant's capacity entitlement. Q3 Name three ways to "},{"t":"Dataverse vs SharePoint Lists – Choosing the Right Data Store for Power Apps","c":"dataverse","u":"articles/dataverse/dataverse-vs-sharepoint.html","s":"An honest comparison of SharePoint lists and Dataverse, covering scale, relationships, security, delegation, business logic, ALM, licensing and cost, with a decision tree and migration notes for apps that outgrow lists.","k":"dataverse, sharepoint lists, architecture, decision, power apps, licensing","h":"Decision tree · Comparison · Moving from lists to Dataverse later · Interview questions · When would you choose SharePoint lists over Dataverse? · What are the main technical limits of SharePoint lists for apps?","l":"Beginner","x":"Fundamentals","b":"The most common architecture question in Power Platform projects: SharePoint list or Dataverse? Both are valid. They solve different problems. # Decision tree flowchart TD A{Relational data?<br/>several related tables} -->|no, 1–3 simple lists| B{More than ~100k rows<br/>or heavy querying?} A -->|yes| DV[Dataverse] B -->|no| C{Row/column-level security<br/>or complex business logic?} B -->|yes| DV C -->|no| D{Users licensed only<br/>with Microsoft 365?} C -->|yes| DV D -->|yes| SP[SharePoint lists ✅] D -->|premium available| E{Model-driven app,<br/>auditing, ALM needed?} E -->|yes| DV E -->|no| SP # Comparison Criteria SharePoint lists Dataverse Licensing (Power Apps) Standard connector, so M365 is enough Premium (except Dataverse for Teams) Scale 30M items/list; 5,000 view threshold Millions of rows, no view threshold Relationships Lookup columns (limited, no cascade rules) True relationships, cascade, N:N Delegation in canvas apps Limited (no in , CountRows ) Broad Security Site/list/item permissions Roles, BUs, teams, column-level , hierarchy Business logic Validation formulas, flows Business rules, plug-ins, formula/rollup columns, BPF Auditing Version history Field-level audit log UI options List UI, Power Apps, SPFx Model-driven, canvas, Power Pages ALM PnP templates Solutions (managed) Documents ✅ native libraries File columns or SharePoint integration Search & Copilot Microsoft Search Dataverse search, Copilot Studio Hybrid is normal Many solutions keep documents in SharePoint and structured process data in Dataverse . Model-driven apps can show SharePoint document locations for a row. # Moving from lists to Dataverse later Model tables and relationships properly (don't copy the list one-to-one). Load data with dataflows (see SharePoint to Dataverse with dataflows ). Switch the canvas app's data source. Formulas change (choices, lookups, people → users). Replace SharePoint-triggered flows with Dataverse triggers. # Interview questions Q1 When would you choose SharePoint lists over Dataverse? For simple, low-volume data (a few lists, limited relationships), when users only have Microsoft 365 licences, and when collaboration features such as documents, sharing and versioning in a SharePoint site matter more than relational integrity and advanced security. Q2 What are the main technical limits of SharePoint lists for apps? The 5,000-item list view thres"},{"t":"Dataverse for Teams – Low-Code Apps Inside Microsoft Teams Without Premium Licences","c":"dataverse","u":"articles/dataverse/dataverse-for-teams.html","s":"What Dataverse for Teams includes and excludes, capacity limits, security tied to team membership, building apps and bots in Teams, and when and how to upgrade a Teams environment to full Dataverse.","k":"dataverse for teams, teams, low-code, environment, licensing","h":"Interview questions · What decides who can access data in Dataverse for Teams? · When should you upgrade from Dataverse for Teams?","l":"Beginner","x":"Fundamentals","b":"Dataverse for Teams is a lightweight Dataverse environment created per team when someone builds an app, flow or bot in Teams. It is included with many Microsoft 365 licences, so team-scoped apps can use relational tables without premium licences . Dataverse for Teams environment MICROSOFT TEAMS TEAM Owners members guests Apps / bots added as tabs provisions TEAMS ENVIRONMENT Dataverse for Teams database (≤ 2 GB /… Power Apps (Teams) Power Automate Copilot Studio (Teams) security mapped from TEAM MEMBERSHIP Owners → full control Members → edit Guests → limited Dataverse for Teams environment Dataverse for Teams Full Dataverse Licensing Included in eligible M365 Premium Capacity Up to 2 GB per team environment Tenant capacity, expandable Access Inside Teams only (and Teams mobile) Any client, Web API Security Owner/member/guest roles Full role model APIs / Web API / plug-ins ❌ ✅ Model-driven apps, Power Pages ❌ ✅ ALM Limited export/import Solutions, pipelines Upgrade path : an admin can upgrade a Teams environment to a full Dataverse environment in the Power Platform admin center. After that, premium licensing applies. # Interview questions Q1 What decides who can access data in Dataverse for Teams? Team membership. Owners, members and guests map to predefined roles (owners get full control, members can create and edit, guests are restricted), so no separate security role design is needed. Q2 When should you upgrade from Dataverse for Teams? When the app must be used outside Teams, needs the Web API or plug-ins, exceeds the 2 GB capacity, requires model-driven apps or Power Pages, or needs enterprise ALM and security."},{"t":"Tables & Columns in Dataverse – Types, Choices, Keys and Naming Conventions","c":"dataverse","u":"articles/dataverse/tables-columns.html","s":"Design Dataverse tables properly, covering standard/activity/virtual/elastic table types, ownership (user/team vs organization), every column type with tips, global vs local choices, alternate keys, autonumber and naming conventions.","k":"dataverse, tables, columns, choices, alternate keys, autonumber, data modeling","h":"Table types and ownership · Column types cheat sheet · Alternate keys · Naming conventions · Interview questions · What is the difference between user-owned and organization-owned tables? · Why define alternate keys? · What should you watch out for with Date and time columns?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Data Modeling","b":"# Table types and ownership Table type Use Standard Most business data (Deviation, Product) Activity Time-based interactions (email, task, appointment); appear in timelines Virtual Data stays in an external system, surfaced as a table Elastic Massive volume, NoSQL-backed (Cosmos DB) for logs/IoT-like data Ownership Meaning Choose when User or team Each row has an Owner; security can be user/BU-scoped Data belongs to people or teams Organization No owner; access is all-or-nothing per role Reference data (Plants, Categories) Ownership can&#39;t be changed later Decide ownership when creating the table. Switching from organization to user-owned later means recreating the table. # Column types cheat sheet Type Notes Text / Multiline text / Rich text Max lengths configurable; email, URL, phone formats Whole number / Decimal / Float / Currency Currency adds base-currency conversion fields Date and time / Date only Behaviour: User local , Date only , Time-zone independent (set carefully) Choice / Choices Single or multi-select option sets; local or global (reusable) Yes/No Two options with labels Lookup N:1 relationship to another table; Customer and Owner are polymorphic File / Image Stored in file capacity (blob) Formula Power Fx expression computed on read Calculated / Rollup Classic calculated and aggregated columns Autonumber DEV-{SEQNUM:5}-{DATETIMEUTC:yyyy} style numbering # Alternate keys An alternate key (one or more columns) uniquely identifies rows besides the GUID. It is essential for integrations and upserts : Upsert by alternate key (Web API) HTTP Copy PATCH [org]/api/data/v9.2/cr8a1_products(cr8a1_sku=&#x27;SKU-10042&#x27;) Content-Type : application/json { \"cr8a1_name\": \"Pressure sensor X2\", \"cr8a1_unitcost\": 42.5 } # Naming conventions Publisher prefix for every custom component ( ci_ for Contoso Inventory), set in the publisher before creating anything. Singular table names ( Deviation ), plural display collection names ( Deviations ). Schema names in PascalCase ( ci_DueDate ); logical names are lowercase automatically. Prefer global choices for values shared across tables (Severity, Status). # Interview questions Q1 What is the difference between user-owned and organization-owned tables? User- or team-owned tables have an Owner column, and security roles can grant access at user, business unit, parent-child BU or organization depth. Organization-"},{"t":"Dataverse Relationships – 1:N, N:1, N:N, Cascade Behaviours and Polymorphic Lookups","c":"dataverse","u":"articles/dataverse/relationships.html","s":"Model relationships correctly, covering one-to-many and many-to-many (native and manual intersect tables), cascade behaviours for assign, share, delete and reparent, polymorphic lookups, hierarchical relationships, and common design mistakes.","k":"dataverse, relationships, cascade, many-to-many, lookup, data modeling","h":"Relationship types · Cascade behaviours · Design tips · Interview questions · What is the difference between a native and a manual many-to-many relationship? · What does \"Restrict\" delete behaviour do?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Data Modeling","b":"# Relationship types erDiagram PLANT ||--o{ DEVIATION : \"1:N (Plant has many Deviations)\" DEVIATION ||--o{ CAPA_ACTION : \"1:N parental (cascade delete)\" DEVIATION }o--o{ PRODUCT : \"N:N affected products\" DEVIATION }o--|| USER : \"Owner (N:1)\" Type Implementation 1:N / N:1 Lookup column on the \"many\" side N:N (native) Hidden intersect table managed by Dataverse; no extra columns N:N (manual) Your own intersect table with two lookups, so you can add columns (e.g. Quantity affected ) Self-referential / hierarchical Lookup to the same table (Parent deviation, Manager) Polymorphic Lookup that can point to several tables (Customer → Account/Contact; Regarding) # Cascade behaviours For each relationship you configure how actions on the parent cascade to children: Action Options Assign Cascade All / Active / User-owned / None Share / Unshare Cascade All / Active / User-owned / None Reparent Cascade All / … / None Delete Cascade All (delete children), Remove Link (clear lookup), Restrict (block deletion) Merge Cascade All / None Behaviour preset Meaning Parental All actions cascade, so children live and die with the parent (Deviation → CAPA actions) Referential Delete removes the link only; others don't cascade (Plant → Deviation) Referential, Restrict Delete Can't delete the parent while children exist Custom Choose per action Parental cascade on huge child sets Assigning or deleting a parent with thousands of children triggers asynchronous cascade jobs that can run long and lock rows. Use referential relationships for large or shared child sets. # Design tips Use manual N:N whenever the relationship itself has data or will need it later. Avoid deep chains of parental relationships (only one parental relationship per child table is allowed). Name lookups by role, not by table ( Reported By , Approved By , both pointing to User). # Interview questions Q1 What is the difference between a native and a manual many-to-many relationship? A native N:N uses a hidden intersect table managed by Dataverse that only stores the two keys. A manual N:N is a custom table with two lookups, which allows extra columns (quantities, dates, roles) and full form, view and security support. Q2 What does \"Restrict\" delete behaviour do? It prevents deleting a parent row while related child rows exist, which protects referential integrity (for example you cannot delete a Plant that still has D"},{"t":"Formula, Calculated and Rollup Columns – Server-Side Calculations Without Code","c":"dataverse","u":"articles/dataverse/calculated-formula-columns.html","s":"Compare Power Fx formula columns, classic calculated columns and rollup columns, with examples (days open, SLA status, totals from child rows), limitations, recalculation timing, and when to use plug-ins or flows instead.","k":"dataverse, formula columns, calculated columns, rollup columns, power fx","h":"Interview questions · What is the difference between a formula column and a rollup column? · How do you get an up-to-date rollup immediately after adding a child row?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Data Modeling","b":"Formula column Calculated column (classic) Rollup column Language Power Fx Designer conditions/actions Aggregate definition Computed On read (real-time) On read Asynchronously (system job every hour by default, or on demand) Related data Parent lookups Parent lookups Child rows (sum, count, min, max, avg) Example DateDiff('Created On', UTCNow(), TimeUnit.Days) Concatenate fields Total cost of CAPA actions Filterable/sortable Yes (with limits) Yes Yes (stored) Formula column examples Power Fx Copy // Days open DateDiff(&#x27;Created On&#x27;, UTCNow(), TimeUnit.Days) // SLA status ( text ) If (&#x27;Due Date &#x27; < UTCToday() && Status <> &#x27;Status (Deviations)&#x27;.Closed, \"Breached\" , &#x27;Due Date &#x27; - UTCToday() <= 2 , \"At risk\" , \"On track\" ) // Display label from lookup Plant.Name & \" · \" & Text (Severity) flowchart LR C1[CAPA action 1<br/>Cost 1,200] --> R[(Rollup on Deviation:<br/>Total CAPA cost = 3,450)] C2[CAPA action 2<br/>Cost 2,000] --> R C3[CAPA action 3<br/>Cost 250] --> R R -.recalculated by system job<br/>or 'Recalculate' button.-> R Rollups are not real-time Rollup columns are recalculated by a recurring system job (hourly by default) or on demand (API CalculateRollupField or the form's recalculate button). For real-time totals use a plug-in or a flow that maintains the value. # Interview questions Q1 What is the difference between a formula column and a rollup column? A formula column computes a value from the row and its parent lookups with Power Fx at read time. A rollup column aggregates child rows (sum, count and so on) and is refreshed asynchronously by a system job or on demand. Q2 How do you get an up-to-date rollup immediately after adding a child row? Call the CalculateRollupField function (Web API or SDK) for that row and column, use the form's recalculate option, or maintain the total with a synchronous plug-in or a flow instead of a rollup."},{"t":"Virtual Tables & Elastic Tables – External Data and Massive Volumes in Dataverse","c":"dataverse","u":"articles/dataverse/virtual-elastic-tables.html","s":"When to use virtual tables (SQL, SharePoint, OData, custom providers) to surface external data without copying, and elastic tables for very high-volume, schema-flexible data, with limitations of each.","k":"dataverse, virtual tables, elastic tables, external data, cosmos db","h":"Virtual tables · Elastic tables · Interview questions · When would you choose a virtual table over importing data with a dataflow? · What is special about elastic tables?","l":"Advanced","x":"Data Modeling","b":"# Virtual tables A virtual table looks like a normal Dataverse table to apps, but rows are fetched live from an external source : SQL Server, SharePoint lists, Excel, OData services or a custom provider. Nothing is copied. Virtual table MODEL-DRIVEN / CANVAS APP Views forms lookups to virtual rows Dataverse query VIRTUAL TABLE (METADATA ONLY) Column mapping Primary key mapping data provider PROVIDER Virtual connector provider (SQL, SharePoint, Excel) OData v4 provider Custom plug-in provider live call EXTERNAL SOURCE Azure SQL / SQL Server (gateway) SharePoint list ERP OData API Virtual table Limitation Note No offline, limited auditing Data isn't stored in Dataverse Performance depends on the source Each view/form query calls the source No rollups/calculations across virtual data Limited business rules Security Dataverse table privileges + the provider's connection identity # Elastic tables Elastic tables are backed by Azure Cosmos DB and designed for very high volume (tens of millions of rows) with horizontal scale, optional time-to-live (automatic deletion) and JSON columns . Typical use: telemetry, audit-like event logs, IoT readings, bulk import staging. Standard table Elastic table Store Azure SQL Azure Cosmos DB Scale Large Massive, high write throughput Transactions & relationships Full Limited (no N:N, limited relationship support) TTL auto-delete ❌ ✅ Best for Business entities High-volume event data # Interview questions Q1 When would you choose a virtual table over importing data with a dataflow? When data must stay in the source system (single source of truth, frequently changing, or too large to copy) and users need to see it inside Dataverse apps, and when real-time accuracy matters more than offline access and advanced Dataverse features. Q2 What is special about elastic tables? They are stored in Azure Cosmos DB for horizontal scale and high write throughput, support time-to-live and JSON columns, but have limited relationship and transaction features compared with standard tables."},{"t":"The Dataverse Security Model – Business Units, Security Roles, Teams, Column & Hierarchy Security","c":"dataverse","u":"articles/dataverse/security-model.html","s":"How Dataverse decides who can see and do what, covering business units, privileges and access levels, owner and access teams, Entra group teams, sharing, column-level security, hierarchy security, and a worked design for a multi-plant quality system.","k":"dataverse, security, security roles, business units, teams, column security, hierarchy security","h":"The building blocks · Access levels · Worked design: multi-plant deviations · Column-level security · Hierarchy security · Interview questions · What are the access levels in a security role, and what does Business Unit level mean? · What's the difference between owner teams and access teams? · How do you hide a column from some users who can otherwise read the row?","l":"Advanced","x":"Security","b":"Dataverse security is role-based , row-aware and column-aware . Getting the model right up front saves months of rework. # The building blocks Dataverse security model BUSINESS UNITS (BU TREE) Root BU (Contoso) Child BUs: Plant Bangalore / Plant Pune / QA Central users & teams belong to a BU PRINCIPALS Users (licensed) Owner teams (own rows) Access teams (share rows) Entra ID group teams receive SECURITY ROLES Privileges per table: Create / Read / Write / Delete /… Access level: User / BU / Parent-child BU /… refined by EXTRA CONTROLS Column security profiles Hierarchy security (manager / position) Sharing (row-level grants) Dataverse security model # Access levels Level Icon (classic) User can access rows owned by… None ○ Nobody User ◔ Themselves (and teams they belong to, plus rows shared with them) Business Unit ◑ Anyone in their BU Parent: Child BUs ◕ Their BU and all child BUs Organization ● Everyone flowchart TB ROOT[Contoso root BU] --> QA[QA Central BU] ROOT --> BLR[Plant Bangalore BU] ROOT --> PUN[Plant Pune BU] BLR --> U1[Ravi: Plant user role<br/>Deviation: Read BU, Write User] QA --> U2[Jane: QA reviewer role<br/>Deviation: Read Org, Write Parent-Child] # Worked design: multi-plant deviations Role Deviation privileges Notes Plant User Create (User), Read (BU), Write (User), Append (BU) Sees own plant only, edits own rows Plant Quality Lead Read/Write (BU), Assign (BU) Manages all deviations of the plant QA Central Reviewer Read (Org), Write (Org) via team Reviews across plants Auditor Read (Org) only Read-only everywhere Use Entra ID security-group teams (team type Microsoft Entra ID Security Group ) to assign roles by group membership instead of user by user. # Column-level security Mark sensitive columns (e.g. Root cause, legal notes , Salary ) as secured , then grant column security profiles (Read / Update / Create per column) to users or teams. Even users with full row access can't see secured columns without a profile. # Hierarchy security Manager hierarchy gives managers access to their reports' rows (down N levels). Position hierarchy does the same based on positions, independent of BUs. Use it for sales-like ownership models. Matrix BU model (modern) Dataverse supports owning records in a BU different from the owner's BU (\"matrix data access structure\"). That lets a user in QA Central own rows that belong to Plant Bangalore's BU, which si"},{"t":"Dataverse Web API CRUD – OData Queries, Create, Update, Upsert, Delete and Batch","c":"dataverse","u":"articles/dataverse/web-api-crud.html","s":"Hands-on Web API reference, covering authentication, entity sets, $select/$filter/$expand/$orderby, formatted values, create with @odata.bind, update, upsert with alternate keys, delete, associate/disassociate, $batch, and calling functions and actions, with HTTP and JavaScript examples.","k":"dataverse, web api, odata, crud, rest, javascript, batch","h":"Headers you always send · READ · CREATE · UPDATE · UPSERT · DELETE · JavaScript (fetch) wrapper · $batch (transactional changesets) · Interview questions · How do you set a lookup when creating a row with the Web API? · How do you prevent PATCH from creating a new row? · How do you get choice labels instead of numbers?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Development","b":"The Dataverse Web API is an OData v4 REST endpoint: https://<org>.<region>.dynamics.com/api/data/v9.2/ . Any language can call it with an Entra ID token for the environment URL. # Headers you always send Standard headers HTTP Copy Authorization : Bearer <token for https://contoso.crm4.dynamics.com> Accept : application/json Content-Type : application/json; charset=utf-8 OData-MaxVersion : 4.0 OData-Version : 4.0 Prefer : odata.include-annotations=\"OData.Community.Display.V1.FormattedValue\" # READ Query with select, filter, expand, order, paging HTTP Copy GET /api/data/v9.2/cr8a1_deviations?$select=cr8a1_name,cr8a1_severity,cr8a1_duedate &$filter=statecode eq 0 and cr8a1_severity eq 100000002 and cr8a1_duedate lt 2026-11-01 &$expand=cr8a1_Plant($select=cr8a1_name) &$orderby=cr8a1_duedate asc &$top=50 Prefer : odata.maxpagesize=50 → { \"value\": [ ... ], \"@odata.nextLink\": \"…$skiptoken=…\" } Function Example contains / startswith / endswith contains(cr8a1_name,'pump') Lookup filter _cr8a1_plant_value eq 6f1c2a9e-… Query functions Microsoft.Dynamics.CRM.LastXDays(PropertyName='createdon',PropertyValue=7) Count $count=true (max 5,000 returned in count) Aggregation $apply=groupby((cr8a1_severity),aggregate($count as total)) # CREATE · UPDATE · UPSERT · DELETE Create (return the row) with a lookup HTTP Copy POST /api/data/v9.2/cr8a1_deviations Prefer : return=representation { \"cr8a1_name\": \"Cold room excursion\", \"cr8a1_severity\": 100000001, \"cr8a1_Plant@odata.bind\": \"/cr8a1_plants(6f1c2a9e-3b4d-4e5f-8a7b-9c0d1e2f3a4b)\" } Update (PATCH) — If-Match prevents accidental create HTTP Copy PATCH /api/data/v9.2/cr8a1_deviations(0a1b2c3d-…) If-Match : * { \"statecode\": 1, \"statuscode\": 2, \"cr8a1_closurecomment\": \"Validated\" } Upsert by alternate key HTTP Copy PATCH /api/data/v9.2/cr8a1_products(cr8a1_sku=&#x27;SKU-10042&#x27;) { \"cr8a1_name\": \"Pressure sensor X2\" } Delete / disassociate HTTP Copy DELETE /api/data/v9.2/cr8a1_deviations(0a1b2c3d-…) DELETE /api/data/v9.2/cr8a1_deviations(0a1b2c3d-…)/cr8a1_deviation_product/$ref?$id=[org]/api/data/v9.2/cr8a1_products(…) # JavaScript (fetch) wrapper Minimal client (token from MSAL or AadHttpClient) JavaScript Copy async function dv ( method, path, body, extraHeaders = {} ) { const res = await fetch ( ` ${ORG} /api/data/v9.2/ ${path} ` , { method, headers : { Authorization : `Bearer ${ await getToken()} ` , Accept : &#x27;applicatio"},{"t":"Dataverse SDK for .NET (ServiceClient) – Connect, CRUD, Queries and Bulk Operations","c":"dataverse","u":"articles/dataverse/sdk-dotnet.html","s":"Use Microsoft.PowerPlatform.Dataverse.Client from .NET 8 apps and Azure Functions, covering connection strings (client secret, certificate, managed identity), late- vs early-bound entities, QueryExpression and FetchXML, ExecuteMultiple and CreateMultiple, and retry handling.","k":"dataverse, sdk, dotnet, serviceclient, csharp, queryexpression, bulk","h":"Connect · CRUD (late-bound) · Queries · Bulk operations · Interview questions · How does a background service authenticate to Dataverse? · How do you page through more than 5,000 rows with QueryExpression?","l":"Advanced","x":"Development","b":"Package PowerShell Copy dotnet add package Microsoft.PowerPlatform.Dataverse.Client # Connect Connection options C# Copy // 1) App registration + client secret (application user in Dataverse) var cs = $\"AuthType=ClientSecret;Url= {url} ;ClientId= {appId} ;ClientSecret= {secret} \" ; using var svc = new ServiceClient(cs); // 2) Certificate var csCert = $\"AuthType=Certificate;Url= {url} ;ClientId= {appId} ;Thumbprint= {thumb} \" ; // 3) Managed identity / any TokenCredential (Azure Function) var credential = new DefaultAzureCredential(); using var svcMi = new ServiceClient( new Uri(url), async ( string resource) => ( await credential.GetTokenAsync( new TokenRequestContext( new [] { $\" {resource} /.default\" }), default )).Token); if (!svc.IsReady) throw new Exception(svc.LastError); Application users Server-to-server apps authenticate as an application user created in the environment (Power Platform admin center → Users → App users) and assigned a security role. # CRUD (late-bound) Create, retrieve, update, delete C# Copy var dev = new Entity( \"cr8a1_deviation\" ) { [ \"cr8a1_name\" ] = \"Cold room excursion\" , [ \"cr8a1_severity\" ] = new OptionSetValue( 100000001 ), [ \"cr8a1_plant\" ] = new EntityReference( \"cr8a1_plant\" , plantId), [ \"cr8a1_duedate\" ] = DateTime.UtcNow.AddDays( 14 ) }; Guid id = await svc.CreateAsync(dev); Entity row = await svc.RetrieveAsync( \"cr8a1_deviation\" , id, new ColumnSet( \"cr8a1_name\" , \"cr8a1_severity\" )); var upd = new Entity( \"cr8a1_deviation\" , id) { [ \"cr8a1_name\" ] = \"Cold room excursion – validated\" }; await svc.UpdateAsync(upd); await svc.DeleteAsync( \"cr8a1_deviation\" , id); # Queries QueryExpression with link-entity and paging C# Copy var q = new QueryExpression( \"cr8a1_deviation\" ) { ColumnSet = new ColumnSet( \"cr8a1_name\" , \"cr8a1_duedate\" ), Criteria = { Conditions = { new ConditionExpression( \"statecode\" , ConditionOperator.Equal, 0 ) } }, Orders = { new OrderExpression( \"cr8a1_duedate\" , OrderType.Ascending) }, PageInfo = new PagingInfo { Count = 5000 , PageNumber = 1 } }; var plant = q.AddLink( \"cr8a1_plant\" , \"cr8a1_plant\" , \"cr8a1_plantid\" ); plant.Columns = new ColumnSet( \"cr8a1_name\" ); plant.EntityAlias = \"p\" ; EntityCollection page; do { page = await svc.RetrieveMultipleAsync(q); foreach ( var e in page.Entities) Console.WriteLine( $\" {e[ \"cr8a1_name\" ]} @ {((AliasedValue)e[ \"p.cr8a1_name\" ]).Value} \" ); q.PageInfo.Pag"},{"t":"Dataverse Plug-ins – Event Pipeline, Pre/Post Images, Sync vs Async and a Complete C# Example","c":"dataverse","u":"articles/dataverse/plugins.html","s":"Write, register and debug plug-ins, covering the event execution pipeline stages (pre-validation, pre-operation, post-operation), IPluginExecutionContext, images, depth checks, throwing user-friendly errors, the Plug-in Registration Tool, tracing and best practices.","k":"dataverse, plug-ins, csharp, event pipeline, pre-image, post-image, server-side","h":"The event pipeline · Complete example: validate and stamp a deviation · Registering · Best practices · Interview questions · What is the difference between pre-operation and post-operation stages? · What are pre- and post-images? · How do you show a friendly error to the user from a plug-in?","l":"Advanced","x":"Development","b":"A plug-in is a .NET class ( IPlugin ) that Dataverse runs inside the event pipeline when a message (Create, Update, Delete, Assign, custom API…) is processed. It is the Dataverse equivalent of a classic SharePoint event receiver, with transactional behaviour. # The event pipeline flowchart LR R[Request: Update deviation] --> S10[Stage 10<br/>Pre-validation<br/>outside transaction] S10 --> S20[Stage 20<br/>Pre-operation<br/>in transaction<br/>modify Target] S20 --> CORE[Core operation<br/>write to DB] CORE --> S40[\"Stage 40<br/>Post-operation<br/>in transaction (sync)<br/>or queued (async)\"] S40 --> RESP[Response] S20 -.throw InvalidPluginExecutionException.-> RB[Rollback + error to user] Stage Transaction Typical use Pre-validation (10) Outside Early validation, cancel before locks Pre-operation (20) Inside Modify the Target (set defaults, compute fields) Post-operation (40) sync Inside Create related rows; failure rolls back everything Post-operation async Background (system job) Integrations, slow work, non-critical # Complete example: validate and stamp a deviation DeviationPlugin.cs C# Copy using System; using Microsoft.Xrm.Sdk; public class DeviationPlugin : IPlugin { public void Execute ( IServiceProvider serviceProvider ) { var context = (IPluginExecutionContext)serviceProvider.GetService( typeof (IPluginExecutionContext)); var tracing = (ITracingService)serviceProvider.GetService( typeof (ITracingService)); var factory = (IOrganizationServiceFactory)serviceProvider.GetService( typeof (IOrganizationServiceFactory)); var service = factory.CreateOrganizationService(context.UserId); if (context.Depth > 2 ) return ; // guard against recursion if (!(context.InputParameters[ \"Target\" ] is Entity target) || target.LogicalName != \"cr8a1_deviation\" ) return ; tracing.Trace( $\" {context.MessageName} stage {context.Stage} for {target.Id} \" ); // Pre-image holds the values BEFORE the update (registered on the step with alias \"pre\") Entity pre = context.PreEntityImages.Contains( \"pre\" ) ? context.PreEntityImages[ \"pre\" ] : null ; int ? severity = target.GetAttributeValue<OptionSetValue>( \"cr8a1_severity\" )?.Value ?? pre?.GetAttributeValue<OptionSetValue>( \"cr8a1_severity\" )?.Value; DateTime? due = target.Contains( \"cr8a1_duedate\" ) ? target.GetAttributeValue<DateTime?>( \"cr8a1_duedate\" ) : pre?.GetAttributeValue<DateTime?>( \"cr8a1_duedate\" ); // 1) Validation (Pre"},{"t":"Querying Dataverse – FetchXML, OData, QueryExpression and the SQL (TDS) Endpoint","c":"dataverse","u":"articles/dataverse/fetchxml-queries.html","s":"Choose the right query language and write efficient queries, covering FetchXML with link-entities and aggregates, OData equivalents, paging cookies, the read-only SQL endpoint for reporting, and performance tips.","k":"dataverse, fetchxml, odata, tds endpoint, sql, queries, aggregation","h":"FetchXML examples · SQL endpoint for reporting · Interview questions · When would you use FetchXML instead of OData? · What is the TDS endpoint?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Development","b":"Language Where Strengths OData ($filter…) Web API, Power Automate REST-friendly, simple FetchXML Web API ( ?fetchXml= ), SDK, Power Automate, reports, views Joins, aggregates, complex filters; what views use QueryExpression .NET SDK Typed, composable in code SQL (TDS endpoint) SSMS, Power BI, read-only Familiar SQL for reporting (respecting security) # FetchXML examples Open deviations per plant with severity count XML Copy < fetch aggregate = \"true\" > < entity name = \"cr8a1_deviation\" > < attribute name = \"cr8a1_deviationid\" alias = \"total\" aggregate = \"count\" /> < attribute name = \"cr8a1_severity\" alias = \"severity\" groupby = \"true\" /> < link-entity name = \"cr8a1_plant\" from = \"cr8a1_plantid\" to = \"cr8a1_plant\" alias = \"p\" > < attribute name = \"cr8a1_name\" alias = \"plant\" groupby = \"true\" /> </ link-entity > < filter > < condition attribute = \"statecode\" operator = \"eq\" value = \"0\" /> < condition attribute = \"createdon\" operator = \"last-x-months\" value = \"6\" /> </ filter > </ entity > </ fetch > Rows WITHOUT related rows (outer join + null) XML Copy < fetch > < entity name = \"cr8a1_deviation\" > < attribute name = \"cr8a1_name\" /> < link-entity name = \"cr8a1_capaaction\" from = \"cr8a1_deviation\" to = \"cr8a1_deviationid\" link-type = \"outer\" alias = \"c\" /> < filter > < condition entityname = \"c\" attribute = \"cr8a1_capaactionid\" operator = \"null\" /> </ filter > </ entity > </ fetch > Run FetchXML through the Web API HTTP Copy GET /api/data/v9.2/cr8a1_deviations?fetchXml=<URL-encoded FetchXML> Get FetchXML for free Build the query in a model-driven view editor / advanced find , then download the FetchXML, or use the XrmToolBox FetchXML Builder . # SQL endpoint for reporting Read-only T-SQL via the TDS endpoint (port 5558, Entra auth) SQL Copy SELECT p.cr8a1_name AS Plant, d.cr8a1_severityname AS Severity, COUNT ( * ) AS OpenCount FROM cr8a1_deviation d JOIN cr8a1_plant p ON p.cr8a1_plantid = d.cr8a1_plant WHERE d.statecode = 0 GROUP BY p.cr8a1_name, d.cr8a1_severityname ORDER BY OpenCount DESC ; The TDS endpoint is read-only , respects Dataverse security, and must be enabled in the environment settings. Use it for ad-hoc analysis and Power BI DirectQuery, not for integrations. # Interview questions Q1 When would you use FetchXML instead of OData? For aggregates (count, sum, avg with group by), complex joins and filters across related tables, outer-join patterns ("},{"t":"Dataverse Custom APIs – Your Own Messages with Plug-in Logic","c":"dataverse","u":"articles/dataverse/custom-apis.html","s":"Define custom API messages (bound and unbound, functions and actions), request and response parameters, implement them with a plug-in, and call them from the Web API, Power Automate and Power Apps.","k":"dataverse, custom api, plug-ins, web api, power automate, messages","h":"Interview questions · Why use a custom API instead of letting clients update columns directly? · What is the difference between a function and an action custom API?","l":"Advanced","x":"Development","b":"A custom API creates a new Dataverse message (like Create or Update), for example ci_CloseDeviation with inputs and outputs, implemented by a plug-in . It is the modern replacement for custom actions (process-based). Custom API anatomy CUSTOM API DEFINITION Unique name: ci_CloseDeviation Bound to: cr8a1_deviation (or… Function (GET) or Action (POST) Allowed custom processing step type parameters REQUEST / RESPONSE Request: Comment (string) NotifyOwner (bool) Response: ClosedOn (datetime) implemented by PLUG-IN Reads InputParameters Performs logic in transaction Sets OutputParameters callable from CALLERS Web API .NET SDK Power Automate (Perform a… Power Apps (Power Fx via Environment) Custom API anatomy Plug-in implementing ci_CloseDeviation C# Copy public class CloseDeviationApi : IPlugin { public void Execute ( IServiceProvider sp ) { var ctx = (IPluginExecutionContext)sp.GetService( typeof (IPluginExecutionContext)); var svc = ((IOrganizationServiceFactory)sp.GetService( typeof (IOrganizationServiceFactory))).CreateOrganizationService(ctx.UserId); var target = (EntityReference)ctx.InputParameters[ \"Target\" ]; // bound API var comment = ( string )ctx.InputParameters[ \"Comment\" ]; svc.Update( new Entity(target.LogicalName, target.Id) { [ \"cr8a1_closurecomment\" ] = comment, [ \"statecode\" ] = new OptionSetValue( 1 ), [ \"statuscode\" ] = new OptionSetValue( 2 ) }); ctx.OutputParameters[ \"ClosedOn\" ] = DateTime.UtcNow; } } Call it through the Web API (bound action) HTTP Copy POST /api/data/v9.2/cr8a1_deviations(0a1b2c3d-…)/Microsoft.Dynamics.CRM.ci_CloseDeviation Content-Type : application/json { \"Comment\": \"Root cause fixed and verified\", \"NotifyOwner\": true } → { \"ClosedOn\": \"2026-10-08T09:15:22Z\" } # Interview questions Q1 Why use a custom API instead of letting clients update columns directly? It encapsulates a business operation (validation, multiple updates, side effects) behind one transactional message with a clear contract and dedicated privileges, callable consistently from apps, flows and integrations. Q2 What is the difference between a function and an action custom API? A function ( IsFunction = true ) is invoked with GET and must not change data. An action is invoked with POST and can change data. Both can be bound to a table or unbound."},{"t":"Auditing & Logging in Dataverse – Audit History, Plug-in Trace Logs and Retention","c":"dataverse","u":"articles/dataverse/auditing.html","s":"Turn on and use Dataverse auditing (environment, table, column), read audit history in apps and through the API, configure retention, combine with Purview, and use plug-in trace logs for troubleshooting.","k":"dataverse, auditing, audit history, trace logs, compliance, retention","h":"Plug-in trace logs · Interview questions · At which three levels must auditing be enabled to record a column change? · Where is audit data stored and how is it billed?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Administration & Integration","b":"Auditing answers who changed what, when, from which value to which value . In regulated industries (pharma deviations, finance) it is often mandatory. flowchart LR E[Environment: Start auditing ✓] --> T[Table: Audit changes ✓] T --> C[Column: Enable auditing ✓<br/>default on for new columns] C --> L[(Audit log<br/>Log capacity)] L --> UI[Audit history tab<br/>on the form] L --> API[Web API /audits,<br/>RetrieveRecordChangeHistory] L --> PV[Microsoft Purview<br/>activity logging] Setting Where Start auditing, log access, read logs Power Platform admin center → Environment → Settings → Auditing Retention period Same page (e.g. 1 year, 7 years, forever) Table-level audit Table properties → Audit changes to its data Column-level audit Column properties → Enable auditing Change history for one row (Web API function) HTTP Copy GET /api/data/v9.2/RetrieveRecordChangeHistory(Target=@target)?@target={&#x27;@odata.id&#x27;:&#x27;cr8a1_deviations(0a1b2c3d-…)&#x27;} Audit uses Log capacity Audit data counts against log capacity . Audit only the tables and columns you need, set a retention period, and avoid auditing very chatty integration tables. # Plug-in trace logs Enable Plug-in and custom workflow activity tracing (All or Exception) in environment settings, then read the Plug-in Trace Log table for ITracingService output and exceptions. Turn it off or set it to Exception after troubleshooting. # Interview questions Q1 At which three levels must auditing be enabled to record a column change? At the environment level (start auditing), the table level (audit changes) and the column level (enable auditing for the column). Q2 Where is audit data stored and how is it billed? In Dataverse audit storage, which counts against the environment's log capacity . Retention settings and selective auditing control its size."},{"t":"Integrating Dataverse – Webhooks, Service Bus, Synapse/Fabric Link, Dual-write and Events","c":"dataverse","u":"articles/dataverse/integration-options.html","s":"An architecture guide to getting data in and out of Dataverse, covering connectors and flows, Web API/SDK, webhooks and Azure Service Bus events, Azure Synapse Link and Microsoft Fabric link for analytics, virtual tables, dataflows and choosing between them.","k":"dataverse, integration, service bus, webhooks, synapse link, fabric, events","h":"Choosing a pattern · Interview questions · How would you send Dataverse changes to an external system reliably? · What is the difference between Synapse Link and Fabric link?","l":"Advanced","x":"Administration & Integration","b":"Dataverse integration landscape REAL-TIME EVENTS OUT Service endpoints: Azure Service Bus… Webhooks (HTTPS) Event Hub Registered on message steps like plug-ins consumers PROCESSING Azure Functions Logic Apps Power Automate External systems analytics copy ANALYTICS Azure Synapse Link (Data Lake / Synapse) Microsoft Fabric link (OneLake, no-copy) Power BI data in INBOUND Web API / SDK (CreateMultiple) Dataflows (Power Query) Power Automate connectors Virtual tables (no copy) Dataverse integration landscape # Choosing a pattern Requirement Pattern React to changes in near real time, reliably Service Bus endpoint step (async) → Azure Function consumer Simple push to an HTTPS endpoint Webhook step Low-code reactions Power Automate Dataverse trigger Analytics / data warehouse Fabric link or Synapse Link (incremental, no custom ETL) Bulk import or scheduled sync Dataflows , or SDK CreateMultiple/UpsertMultiple in a function Show external data without copying Virtual tables Finance & Operations ↔ Dataverse Dual-write / virtual entities sequenceDiagram participant U as User / app participant DV as Dataverse participant SB as Azure Service Bus participant F as Azure Function participant ERP as ERP system U->>DV: Update deviation (status = Closed) DV-)SB: Async step posts RemoteExecutionContext (JSON) SB-)F: Queue trigger F->>ERP: Post quality notification F->>DV: Write back ERP reference (SDK) Prefer async service endpoints for integrations Async steps don't slow down user saves, retry automatically, and Service Bus gives durable delivery with dead-lettering, which is far more robust than calling external APIs from synchronous plug-ins. # Interview questions Q1 How would you send Dataverse changes to an external system reliably? Register an asynchronous step with a Service Bus service endpoint for the message and table (filtered columns). Dataverse posts the execution context to a queue or topic, and an Azure Function processes it with retries and dead-lettering, then optionally writes results back. Q2 What is the difference between Synapse Link and Fabric link? Both deliver Dataverse data for analytics without custom ETL. Synapse Link exports to Azure Data Lake/Synapse in your subscription. Fabric link makes Dataverse data available in Microsoft Fabric's OneLake (via shortcuts) for Spark, SQL and Power BI, without managing the copy pipeline yourself."},{"t":"Dataverse Capacity, Service Protection Limits and API Throughput","c":"dataverse","u":"articles/dataverse/capacity-api-limits.html","s":"Understand database, file and log capacity, service protection API limits (requests, execution time, concurrent requests), 429 handling with Retry-After, bulk APIs and designing high-volume integrations that stay within limits.","k":"dataverse, capacity, service protection limits, throttling, 429, api limits","h":"Capacity · Service protection limits · Designing high-volume integrations · Interview questions · What happens when an integration exceeds Dataverse service protection limits? · How do you load millions of rows into Dataverse efficiently?","l":"Advanced","x":"Administration & Integration","b":"# Capacity Capacity Contains Tips Database Table rows, metadata Archive old data, avoid storing files as text/base64 File File/image columns, attachments, notes attachments Store large documents in SharePoint instead Log Audit, plug-in traces Retention policies, selective auditing Capacity is pooled at tenant level (base entitlement + per-licence accruals + add-ons) and reported in the Power Platform admin center. # Service protection limits Dataverse protects itself per user (or app user) per web server , within a 5-minute sliding window: Measure Approximate limit Number of requests 6,000 per 5 minutes Combined execution time 20 minutes per 5 minutes Concurrent requests 52 Exceeding them returns HTTP 429 (Web API) or a FaultException (SDK) with a Retry-After duration. The ServiceClient retries automatically. Custom code must honour Retry-After. Honour Retry-After with HttpClient (Web API) C# Copy for ( int attempt = 0 ; attempt < 5 ; attempt++) { var res = await http.SendAsync(Clone(request)); if (( int )res.StatusCode != 429 ) return res; var wait = res.Headers.RetryAfter?.Delta ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(Math.Pow( 2 , attempt)); await Task.Delay(wait); } # Designing high-volume integrations Cheat sheet Throughput rules 01 Bulk messages CreateMultiple / UpdateMultiple / UpsertMultiple beat one-by-one calls by far. 02 Parallelism with care Several threads (respect concurrency limit 52) and multiple app users if needed. 03 Only changed columns Update only modified attributes to reduce plug-in and audit work. 04 Filtering attributes Keep plug-ins from firing on irrelevant updates. 05 Off-peak windows Schedule large loads outside business hours. 06 Measure Track 429s and Retry-After; tune batch size and threads. # Interview questions Q1 What happens when an integration exceeds Dataverse service protection limits? Requests are rejected with 429 Too Many Requests (or an SDK fault) including a Retry-After value. Clients must wait that long and retry. Persistent overload means reducing request rate, batching, or spreading load across app users. Q2 How do you load millions of rows into Dataverse efficiently? Use bulk operations (CreateMultiple/UpsertMultiple) in batches with controlled parallelism, minimize plug-in and audit overhead (filtering attributes, disable non-essential steps during the load if acceptable), handle 429 Retry-After, and run off-peak. Consider el"},{"t":"What are Dataflows? Standard vs Analytical Dataflows, Power Query Online and Dataflow Gen2","c":"dataflows","u":"articles/dataflows/overview.html","s":"Dataflows explained, covering Power Query Online as the engine, standard dataflows that load into Dataverse, analytical dataflows that land in a data lake for Power BI, Dataflow Gen2 in Microsoft Fabric, refresh, gateways and typical use cases.","k":"dataflows, power query, dataverse, fabric, etl, overview","h":"Three flavours · A typical standard dataflow · When to use dataflows · Interview questions · What is the difference between a standard and an analytical dataflow? · Which engine do dataflows use?","l":"Beginner","x":"Fundamentals","b":"A dataflow is a cloud ETL (extract, transform, load) job built with Power Query Online , the same query engine as Excel and Power BI. It connects to sources (SharePoint, SQL, Excel, OData, files, Salesforce…), shapes the data, and loads it into a destination on a schedule. # Three flavours Dataflow types SOURCES SharePoint lists & files SQL Server (via gateway) Excel / CSV OData / REST 100+ connectors Power Query Online (M) STANDARD DATAFLOW (POWER APPS) Loads into Dataverse tables Upsert by alternate key Delete rows not in source (optional) ANALYTICAL DATAFLOW (POWER BI) Stores in Azure Data Lake (CDM folders) Reused by many Power BI datasets DATAFLOW GEN2 (MICROSOFT FABRIC) Outputs to Lakehouse / Warehouse / KQL / Azure SQL Pipelines orchestration Staging & fast copy Dataflow types Standard Analytical Dataflow Gen2 Built in Power Apps maker portal Power BI service Microsoft Fabric Destination Dataverse Data lake (Power BI storage / ADLS) Lakehouse, Warehouse, SQL, more Typical use App data migration and sync Reusable BI data prep Enterprise data engineering Licence Power Apps / Power Automate Power BI Pro/Premium Fabric capacity # A typical standard dataflow flowchart LR S[(SharePoint list<br/>Products — 12k items)] --> PQ[Power Query Online<br/>clean · rename · type · dedupe] X[(Excel on SharePoint<br/>Supplier master)] --> PQ PQ --> MAP[Map to Dataverse table<br/>alternate key = SKU] MAP --> DV[(Dataverse<br/>ci_product)] SCH[Schedule: daily 02:00<br/>or incremental] -.-> PQ # When to use dataflows Migrate list data into Dataverse when an app outgrows SharePoint. Keep reference data (products, cost centres) in Dataverse synchronized from ERP exports or SQL. Shared, governed data preparation for Power BI. Replace hand-written ETL scripts for simple to moderate transformations. # Interview questions Q1 What is the difference between a standard and an analytical dataflow? A standard dataflow loads its output into Dataverse tables (for apps). An analytical dataflow stores output in a data lake (Power BI or your ADLS Gen2) as reusable entities for Power BI datasets and analytics. Q2 Which engine do dataflows use? Power Query Online , the same M-language engine as Power BI Desktop and Excel's Get & Transform, running in the cloud with refresh scheduling and gateway support."},{"t":"Power Query M Essentials – The Formula Language Behind Dataflows and Power BI","c":"dataflows","u":"articles/dataflows/power-query-m.html","s":"Learn M through practical examples, covering let/in, steps and types, table transformations, custom columns, merges and appends, functions, parameters, error handling with try/otherwise, and reading SharePoint and REST sources.","k":"power query, m language, transformations, functions, dataflows, power bi","h":"Anatomy of a query · Most-used functions · Custom function + REST API paging · Error handling · Interview questions · What does each mean in M? · Why might a dataflow that works in the editor fail on scheduled refresh?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Fundamentals","b":"Every click in the Power Query editor writes M code. Reading and writing M directly (Advanced editor) unlocks transformations the UI can't do and makes queries maintainable. # Anatomy of a query let … in, one step per line Power Query M Copy let Source = SharePoint.Tables(\"https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/quality\", [Implementation = \"2.0\", ViewMode = \"All\"]), Deviations = Source{[Title = \"Deviations\"]}[Items], Selected = Table.SelectColumns(Deviations, {\"ID\", \"Title\", \"Severity\", \"DueDate\", \"Owner\", \"Plant\"}), Typed = Table.TransformColumnTypes(Selected, {{\"ID\", Int64.Type}, {\"Title\", type text}, {\"DueDate\", type date}}), OwnerEmail = Table.AddColumn(Typed, \"OwnerEmail\", each try [Owner][EMail] otherwise null, type text), PlantName = Table.AddColumn(OwnerEmail, \"PlantName\", each [Plant][Title], type text), OpenOnly = Table.SelectRows(PlantName, each [Severity] <> null and [DueDate] <> null), Removed = Table.RemoveColumns(OpenOnly, {\"Owner\", \"Plant\"}) in Removed # Most-used functions Task M Filter rows Table.SelectRows(t, each [Status] = \"Open\") Add column Table.AddColumn(t, \"DaysOpen\", each Duration.Days(DateTime.LocalNow() - [Created]), Int64.Type) Rename Table.RenameColumns(t, {{\"Title\", \"Name\"}}) Replace values Table.ReplaceValue(t, \"\", null, Replacer.ReplaceValue, {\"Plant\"}) Merge (join) Table.NestedJoin(a, {\"PlantId\"}, b, {\"ID\"}, \"P\", JoinKind.LeftOuter) then Table.ExpandTableColumn Append (union) Table.Combine({t2024, t2025, t2026}) Group Table.Group(t, {\"Plant\"}, {{\"Count\", each Table.RowCount(_), Int64.Type}}) Pivot / unpivot Table.Pivot , Table.UnpivotOtherColumns Dedupe Table.Distinct(t, {\"SKU\"}) Text Text.Trim , Text.Upper , Text.Split , Text.Contains Dates Date.From , DateTime.LocalNow() , Date.AddDays , Date.StartOfMonth # Custom function + REST API paging Call a paged REST API and combine pages Power Query M Copy let GetPage = (url as text) as record => let resp = Json.Document(Web.Contents(url, [Headers = [Accept = \"application/json\"]])) in resp, Pages = List.Generate( () => GetPage(\"https://api.contoso.com/products?page=1\"), each _ <> null, each if Record.HasFields(_, \"next\") and _[next] <> null then GetPage(_[next]) else null, each _[items] ), Rows = List.Combine(Pages), AsTable = Table.FromRecords(Rows) in AsTable Use relative paths with Web.Contents For scheduled refresh, keep the base URL static and pass the variable part via Relative"},{"t":"Dataflows vs Power Automate vs Azure Data Factory vs Synapse/Fabric Link – Choosing an Integration Tool","c":"dataflows","u":"articles/dataflows/dataflows-vs-alternatives.html","s":"A decision guide for moving data in the Microsoft ecosystem, comparing volume, latency, transformations, cost, skills and monitoring, with recommended patterns for common SharePoint and Dataverse scenarios.","k":"dataflows, power automate, azure data factory, fabric, integration, architecture","h":"Interview questions · When is Power Automate the wrong tool for data integration? · How would you get Dataverse data into a data warehouse for Power BI?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Fundamentals","b":"Tool Best at Latency Volume Skill Power Automate Event-driven, record-by-record, approvals Seconds–minutes Low–medium Maker Dataflows (standard) Scheduled bulk loads into Dataverse with transformations Hours (schedule) / incremental Medium–high Maker / analyst Dataflow Gen2 + pipelines (Fabric) Lakehouse/warehouse ETL, orchestration Scheduled High Data engineer Azure Data Factory Enterprise ETL/ELT, hybrid, 90+ connectors, control flow Scheduled / triggered Very high Data engineer Synapse Link / Fabric link Continuous Dataverse → analytics replication Near real-time Very high Admin Custom code (Functions + SDK) Complex logic, bulk APIs, special protocols Any Any Developer flowchart TD Q{What triggers the movement?} -->|An event on one record| PA[Power Automate] Q -->|A schedule / bulk| V{Destination?} V -->|Dataverse| DF[Standard dataflow] V -->|Lakehouse / warehouse / BI| F[Fabric Dataflow Gen2 / pipelines] V -->|Many systems, complex orchestration| ADF[Azure Data Factory] Q -->|Dataverse → analytics continuously| L[Synapse Link / Fabric link] Don&#39;t sync bulk data with Apply to each Copying 50,000 rows nightly with Power Automate loops is slow, burns API request limits and is fragile. Use a dataflow, ADF or bulk APIs instead. # Interview questions Q1 When is Power Automate the wrong tool for data integration? For scheduled bulk synchronization of large datasets: loops are slow, consume request allocations and are hard to make idempotent. Dataflows, ADF or Fabric pipelines handle bulk loads, transformations and retries far better. Q2 How would you get Dataverse data into a data warehouse for Power BI? Use Fabric link or Azure Synapse Link for continuous, incremental replication without custom ETL, rather than exporting with flows or scheduled API scripts."},{"t":"Recipe – Load a SharePoint List into Dataverse with a Dataflow (Step by Step)","c":"dataflows","u":"articles/dataflows/sharepoint-to-dataverse.html","s":"Migrate or continuously sync SharePoint list data into a Dataverse table, covering source connection, transforming person, lookup and choice columns, alternate keys for upsert, mapping to choices and lookups, deleting rows not in source, scheduling and validation.","k":"dataflows, sharepoint, dataverse, migration, upsert, alternate key, power query","h":"Step 1 · Prepare Dataverse · Step 2 · Create the dataflow and connect · Step 3 · Transform (Advanced editor) · Step 4 · Map to Dataverse · Step 5 · Refresh, validate, cut over · Interview questions · How do you avoid duplicates when re-running a dataflow into Dataverse? · How do you set a lookup column in a standard dataflow?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Recipes","b":"Scenario: the Deviations app outgrew its SharePoint list (40,000 items, delegation problems, complex security). We will move the data into the Dataverse table ci_deviation , keep it in sync during a transition period, then switch the app. flowchart LR SP[(SharePoint<br/>Deviations list)] --> Q1[Query: Deviations<br/>select · type · flatten] SPP[(SharePoint<br/>Plants list)] --> Q2[Query: Plants] Q2 --> L1[Load → ci_plant<br/>key: ci_code] Q1 --> L2[Load → ci_deviation<br/>key: ci_legacyid] L1 -. lookup by alternate key .-> L2 SCH[Refresh daily 01:00] -.-> Q1 # Step 1 · Prepare Dataverse Create tables ci_plant (alternate key ci_code ) and ci_deviation (alternate key ci_legacyid = SharePoint ID). Create choice columns with the same labels as SharePoint choices (Minor/Major/Critical). Alternate keys make the load an upsert : reruns update existing rows instead of creating duplicates. # Step 2 · Create the dataflow and connect Power Apps → Tables → Import → Import data (dataflow) → SharePoint Online list → site URL → select Deviations and Plants . # Step 3 · Transform (Advanced editor) Deviations query Power Query M Copy let Source = SharePoint.Tables(\"https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/quality\", [Implementation = \"2.0\", ViewMode = \"All\"]), Items = Source{[Title = \"Deviations\"]}[Items], Keep = Table.SelectColumns(Items, {\"ID\", \"Title\", \"Severity\", \"Status\", \"DueDate\", \"Owner\", \"Plant\", \"Created\"}), // person → email (used to resolve owner later), lookup → plant code OwnerEmail = Table.AddColumn(Keep, \"OwnerEmail\", each try Text.Lower([Owner][EMail]) otherwise null, type text), PlantCode = Table.AddColumn(OwnerEmail, \"PlantCode\", each try [Plant][Code] otherwise null, type text), Typed = Table.TransformColumnTypes(PlantCode, {{\"ID\", type text}, {\"DueDate\", type date}, {\"Created\", type datetime}}), Clean = Table.RemoveColumns(Typed, {\"Owner\", \"Plant\"}), Rename = Table.RenameColumns(Clean, {{\"ID\", \"LegacyId\"}}) in Rename Lookup columns from SharePoint With Implementation = \"2.0\" , lookup and person columns arrive as records. Expand only the fields you need ( Title , EMail , or a custom field such as Code if the lookup projects it), or merge with the Plants query on the lookup ID. # Step 4 · Map to Dataverse Source column Destination Notes LegacyId ci_legacyid Alternate key → upsert Title ci_name Primary name Severity (text) ci_severity (choice) Labels must match "},{"t":"On-Premises Data Gateway for Dataflows – SQL Server, File Shares and SharePoint Server","c":"dataflows","u":"articles/dataflows/onprem-gateway.html","s":"Install and operate the on-premises data gateway, covering architecture, clusters for HA, connections and credentials, privacy levels, performance tuning, monitoring and common errors when refreshing dataflows from on-prem sources.","k":"data gateway, on-premises, sql server, dataflows, hybrid, power platform","h":"Setup checklist · Troubleshooting · Interview questions · Why doesn't the gateway need inbound firewall rules? · How do you make a gateway highly available?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Recipes","b":"Gateway architecture CLOUD SERVICES Dataflows Power BI Power Apps & Power Automate (SQL… Logic Apps request via AZURE RELAY / SERVICE BUS Gateway cloud service Encrypted credentials outbound only (443 / 5671-5672) GATEWAY CLUSTER (ON-PREM) Gateway VM 1 (primary) Gateway VM 2 (HA) Credentials decrypted locally LAN SOURCES SQL Server Oracle / DB2 File shares (CSV, Excel) SharePoint Server 2016/2019/SE Gateway architecture # Setup checklist Dedicated Windows Server VMs (not laptops), close to data sources, 8+ cores, 16+ GB RAM for heavy refresh. Service account with network access to sources; outbound HTTPS to Azure allowed. Install, sign in with a work account, register a recovery key (store it in your vault). Add a second gateway to the cluster for high availability. Create connections (data sources) with credentials; share with makers who need them. Update monthly (new gateway version each month). # Troubleshooting Symptom Cause / fix \"Unable to connect to the data source\" Name resolution from the gateway VM, firewall, wrong credentials Refresh slow Non-folding transformations executed on the gateway, so push filters to source and increase VM size Privacy level error combining sources Set privacy levels consistently (Organizational) or restructure queries Gateway offline Service stopped, proxy changes, expired account, so check the logs in GatewayLogs # Interview questions Q1 Why doesn't the gateway need inbound firewall rules? It establishes outbound connections to Azure Relay/Service Bus and listens for requests over those connections. Cloud services never connect inbound to your network. Q2 How do you make a gateway highly available? Install additional gateways on separate VMs and add them to the same gateway cluster . Requests are distributed and fail over automatically if one member is offline."},{"t":"Dataflow Gen2 in Microsoft Fabric – Destinations, Staging, Pipelines and When to Use It","c":"dataflows","u":"articles/dataflows/dataflow-gen2-fabric.html","s":"What's new in Dataflow Gen2, covering output destinations (Lakehouse, Warehouse, Azure SQL, KQL), staging and fast copy, orchestration with data pipelines, monitoring, CI/CD with Git, and moving Power BI dataflows (Gen1) to Gen2.","k":"dataflow gen2, microsoft fabric, lakehouse, warehouse, pipelines, power query","h":"Interview questions · What is the main architectural difference between Dataflow Gen1 and Gen2? · When would you choose Fabric over a standard Power Platform dataflow?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Recipes","b":"Microsoft Fabric (GA 2023) brings data engineering, warehousing and Power BI onto one SaaS platform with OneLake storage. Dataflow Gen2 is the Power Query-based ingestion tool inside Fabric's Data Factory experience. Dataflow Gen2 in Fabric SOURCES SharePoint / Excel / SQL / APIs On-prem via gateway Power Query Online (Gen2) DATAFLOW GEN2 Staging lakehouse (optional) Fast copy for large volumes Incremental refresh output destinations DESTINATIONS Lakehouse (Delta tables) Warehouse Azure SQL Database KQL database orchestrated by DATA PIPELINE Schedules & triggers Dependencies Notebooks & copy activities consumed by POWER BI (DIRECT LAKE) Semantic models Reports Dataflow Gen2 in Fabric Power BI dataflow (Gen1) Dataflow Gen2 Output Dataflow storage (CDM) Explicit destinations (Lakehouse, Warehouse, SQL…) Orchestration Refresh schedule Data pipelines Scale Moderate Staging + fast copy ALM Limited Git integration, deployment pipelines SharePoint list analytics A common pattern: Dataflow Gen2 reads SharePoint lists nightly into a Lakehouse , and a Direct Lake Power BI model gives instant, threshold-free reporting across 30 sites, with no load on SharePoint during report views. # Interview questions Q1 What is the main architectural difference between Dataflow Gen1 and Gen2? Gen2 writes to explicit output destinations (Lakehouse, Warehouse, Azure SQL, KQL) in Fabric and is orchestrated by data pipelines , with staging and fast copy for scale. Gen1 stores entities in dataflow-managed storage for Power BI. Q2 When would you choose Fabric over a standard Power Platform dataflow? When the destination is analytics (lakehouse, warehouse, Power BI at scale) rather than Dataverse for apps, or when you need orchestration, large volumes and data engineering tooling."},{"t":"Incremental Refresh & Scheduling for Dataflows","c":"dataflows","u":"articles/dataflows/incremental-refresh.html","s":"Refresh only new and changed data, covering incremental refresh settings (store vs refresh windows, change detection), requirements on the source (date columns, query folding), schedules, refresh history and failure notifications.","k":"dataflows, incremental refresh, scheduling, refresh history, performance","h":"Scheduling tips · Interview questions · What does \"Detect data changes\" do in incremental refresh? · Why must the incremental filter fold?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Operations","b":"Full refreshes reload everything every time, which is slow and expensive for large tables. Incremental refresh partitions data by a DateTime column and only refreshes recent partitions. flowchart LR subgraph Stored[\"Store rows from the last 5 years\"] P1[2022] --- P2[2023] --- P3[2024] --- P4[2025] end subgraph Refresh[\"Refresh rows from the last 30 days\"] P5[Sep 2026] --- P6[Oct 2026] end P4 --- P5 CD[Optional: detect changes<br/>by Modified column] -.-> Refresh Setting Meaning Column DateTime column used to partition (e.g. Created ) Store rows from the past History kept (older partitions are dropped) Refresh rows from the past Partitions re-queried on each refresh Detect data changes Only refresh a partition if max(Modified) changed Only refresh complete periods Skip the current incomplete day/month Requirements The date filter must fold to the source (SQL, OData/Dataverse, SharePoint with filters) so only the needed range is fetched. Otherwise incremental refresh brings no benefit, or it fails. # Scheduling tips Stagger schedules so several dataflows don't hit the same source simultaneously. Respect source maintenance windows (SQL backups, SharePoint off-hours). Turn on failure notifications to the owner and a shared mailbox. Review refresh history (per-table duration and rows) after changes. # Interview questions Q1 What does \"Detect data changes\" do in incremental refresh? For each partition in the refresh window, the service first checks the maximum value of a change-tracking column (such as Modified). It only re-queries partitions whose maximum changed, which saves time and load. Q2 Why must the incremental filter fold? Because the goal is to fetch only the rows in the refresh window from the source. If the filter doesn't fold, the engine pulls all rows and filters locally, which defeats the purpose and may time out."},{"t":"Query Folding & Dataflow Performance – Make the Source Do the Work","c":"dataflows","u":"articles/dataflows/query-folding-performance.html","s":"Understand query folding, how to check it (View native query, folding indicators), which steps break folding, ordering steps for performance, handling SharePoint and API sources that don't fold, and troubleshooting slow or failing refreshes.","k":"query folding, power query, performance, dataflows, troubleshooting","h":"Steps that usually fold vs break folding (relational sources) · Checking folding · Sources that don't fold well · Interview questions · What is query folding and why does it matter? · How can you tell whether a step folds?","l":"Advanced","x":"Operations","b":"Query folding means Power Query translates your steps into the source's native query language (SQL, OData), so filtering, column selection and aggregation happen at the source. Folding queries are fast and light. Non-folding ones pull everything and process it in the mashup engine. flowchart LR subgraph Folding[\"✅ Folding\"] A1[Filter Year = 2026] --> A2[Select 5 columns] --> A3[Group by Plant] A3 --> SQL1[\"SELECT Plant, COUNT(*)<br/>FROM Deviations WHERE Year=2026<br/>GROUP BY Plant\"] end subgraph Broken[\"⚠ Folding broken\"] B1[Add index column] --> B2[Filter Year = 2026] B2 --> X[Engine downloads ALL rows,<br/>then filters locally] end # Steps that usually fold vs break folding (relational sources) Usually fold Often break folding Filter rows, remove/select columns Index column, Table.Buffer Sort, group by, simple joins Custom M functions per row, Text.Proper style text ops (source-dependent) Rename, change type (simple) Merging queries from different sources Top N Pivot/unpivot (source-dependent), Table.AddColumn with complex logic Rule of thumb: put filters and column selection first , and non-folding steps as late as possible. # Checking folding Right-click a step → View native query (enabled = folding up to that step). Folding indicators in Power Query Online show which steps fold. Query diagnostics for detailed timing. # Sources that don't fold well SharePoint lists (folding is limited, with large lists downloaded in pages), Excel/CSV files, and most REST APIs. Mitigations: filter with source options (SharePoint views, API query parameters), reduce columns early, and stage into Dataverse or a Lakehouse once, then transform from there. # Interview questions Q1 What is query folding and why does it matter? Folding pushes transformation steps back to the data source as native queries, so only the needed data is transferred and processed by the source engine. Without folding, Power Query downloads more data and processes it locally, which is slower, heavier and can time out. Q2 How can you tell whether a step folds? Use View native query on the step (if available, it folds), or check step folding indicators in Power Query Online."},{"t":"Nintex Explained – History and Product Family from Workflow 2007 to Nintex Automation Cloud","c":"nintex","u":"articles/nintex/history-platforms.html","s":"The Nintex story and product map, covering Nintex Workflow and Forms for SharePoint (2007–SE), Nintex for Office 365, Nintex Workflow Cloud becoming Nintex Automation Cloud, K2, RPA and Process Manager, plus how the pieces fit today.","k":"nintex, history, nintex workflow, nintex forms, nac, k2, product family","h":"Product map (2026) · Interview questions · What is the difference between Nintex for Office 365 and Nintex Automation Cloud? · Why did Nintex become popular in the SharePoint world?","l":"Beginner","x":"Platform & History","b":"Nintex (founded in Melbourne, Australia) became famous for one thing: letting business users build SharePoint workflows in a browser with drag-and-drop , years before Microsoft offered anything comparable. Today it is a broader process automation platform centred on Nintex Automation Cloud (NAC) . 2007–08 On-prem Nintex Workflow 2007 Browser-based drag-and-drop designer for MOSS 2007 on Windows Workflow Foundation. A huge hit with SharePoint teams. 2010 On-prem Nintex Workflow 2010 & Nintex Forms Workflow for SharePoint 2010; Nintex Forms replaced many InfoPath forms with a friendlier designer. 2013 Cloud Nintex Workflow & Forms 2013 · Nintex for Office 365 On-prem 2013 versions, plus an app-model product for SharePoint Online running in Nintex's cloud. 2016 Cloud Nintex Workflow Cloud A cloud-native workflow platform independent of SharePoint, with connectors. 2016–19 On-prem SharePoint 2016 / 2019 versions Continued on-prem releases (later Subscription Edition support). 2019 Portfolio Promapp & EnableSoft Process mapping (Nintex Process Manager) and RPA (Nintex RPA, Foxtrot) join the portfolio. 2020 Portfolio K2 acquisition K2 low-code app and workflow platform becomes Nintex K2 . 2022 Cloud Nintex Automation Cloud Nintex Workflow Cloud is rebranded NAC : workflows, forms, DocGen, e-sign, RPA, process intelligence in one platform. 2023–26 Cloud Migration era Customers move Nintex for Office 365 and on-prem workflows to NAC; AI features and connectors expand. # Product map (2026) The Nintex platform DISCOVER & DESIGN Nintex Process Manager (process maps) Process discovery automate NINTEX AUTOMATION CLOUD Workflows & connectors Forms (responsive) DocGen & e-sign Xtensions (custom… Hybrid gateway to on-prem extend SPECIALISED Nintex RPA (desktop bots) Nintex K2 (apps, on-prem & cloud) Nintex for SharePoint (on-prem, legacy) integrates with SYSTEMS SharePoint Online & on-prem Microsoft 365 / Teams Salesforce ServiceNow SAP Any REST API The Nintex platform Product Where it runs Status in 2026 Nintex Workflow & Forms for SharePoint (2013–SE) Your SharePoint farm Supported per Nintex lifecycle; tied to your SharePoint version Nintex for Office 365 Nintex cloud + SPO app Legacy, with customers migrating to NAC Nintex Automation Cloud Nintex cloud (multi-region) Strategic Nintex K2 On-prem / cloud Supported Nintex RPA, Process Manager Desktop / cloud Supported # In"},{"t":"Nintex K2, Nintex RPA and Nintex Process Manager – The Rest of the Portfolio","c":"nintex","u":"articles/nintex/nintex-k2-rpa-process-manager.html","s":"Where Nintex K2 (low-code apps and workflows), Nintex RPA (desktop bots) and Nintex Process Manager (process maps) fit, how they integrate with NAC and SharePoint, and when each is appropriate.","k":"nintex k2, nintex rpa, process manager, promapp, portfolio","h":"Interview questions · How do Process Manager and NAC complement each other? · What is a SmartObject in K2?","l":"Beginner","x":"Platform & History","b":"From process map to automation NINTEX PROCESS MANAGER Map processes with owners & steps Process health & variation Improvement suggestions identify automation candidates NINTEX AUTOMATION CLOUD Workflows forms DocGen Connectors to SharePoint & M365 UI automation where no API NINTEX RPA Desktop bots Attended / unattended Called from NAC workflows complex apps & on-prem NINTEX K2 Model-driven style apps (SmartObjects) Complex forms (SmartForms) On-prem or cloud From process map to automation Product Origin Best for Nintex Process Manager Promapp (acquired 2019) Documenting processes so people follow one version of the truth; finding automation candidates Nintex RPA EnableSoft Foxtrot (2019) and later RPA acquisitions Automating legacy desktop/web apps without APIs Nintex K2 K2 (acquired 2020) Complex line-of-business apps with data integration layers (SmartObjects), long-lived on-prem estates # Interview questions Q1 How do Process Manager and NAC complement each other? Process Manager documents and governs how a process works (steps, roles, variations), which highlights pain points and automation candidates. NAC then automates those steps with workflows, forms and documents, and process maps can link to the automations. Q2 What is a SmartObject in K2? K2's data abstraction layer: a reusable business object (for example Customer) that maps to one or more back-end systems (SQL, SharePoint, web services) and is used by K2 SmartForms and workflows without dealing with each source directly."},{"t":"Nintex Workflow for SharePoint (On-Premises) – Architecture, Designer and Administration","c":"nintex","u":"articles/nintex/nintex-workflow-onprem.html","s":"How Nintex Workflow runs on a SharePoint farm, covering solution and features, configuration and content databases, the browser designer, workflow constants, user-defined actions, snippets, LazyApproval, NWAdmin commands, history purging and performance.","k":"nintex workflow, sharepoint on-premises, nwadmin, lazyapproval, workflow constants, administration","h":"Architecture · Building blocks · Administration essentials · Performance tips · Interview questions · What is LazyApproval? · What are workflow constants used for? · How do you keep a Nintex on-prem farm healthy?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Nintex for SharePoint (On-Prem)","b":"Nintex Workflow for SharePoint (2007, 2010, 2013, 2016, 2019 and Subscription Edition) installs as farm solutions and runs on top of SharePoint's own workflow engine (Windows Workflow Foundation in SharePoint 2010-style workflows), with its own databases for tracking. # Architecture Nintex Workflow on a SharePoint farm BROWSER Nintex Workflow Designer (drag & drop) Task forms & LazyApproval emails farm solution features SHAREPOINT WEB & APP SERVERS Nintex workflow actions (activities) SharePoint workflow host (WF 3.5) Timer jobs (scheduled workflows, notifications) stores SQL SERVER Nintex configuration DB Nintex content DBs (workflow progress, history) SharePoint content DBs (workflow definitions, task… integrates EXTERNAL SMTP / Exchange (LazyApproval inboun… Web services SQL LDAP / AD Nintex Workflow on a SharePoint farm # Building blocks Feature What it is Workflow designer Browser canvas with action toolbox, configured per list, library, site or reusable Workflow variables Text, number, date, person, collection, list item ID, Yes/No Workflow constants Farm/site-level values and credentials (service accounts) used by actions User Defined Actions (UDAs) Reusable sub-workflows with parameters, a library of custom actions Snippets Saved groups of actions to drag into other workflows LazyApproval Approve or reject by replying to the email (\"Approved\", \"Yes\", \"Declined\") Scheduled workflows Run on site workflows or items by schedule Workflow history Nintex DB + SharePoint history list, viewable per instance with the visual progress map # Administration essentials NWAdmin.exe examples (run on a SharePoint server) PowerShell Copy # Purge old workflow progress data (keeps databases small) – test first! NWAdmin.exe -o PurgeWorkflowData -state Completed -lastActivityBefore 2025 -01-01 -siteUrl https://sp.contoso.com/sites/quality # Purge history list items for a list NWAdmin.exe -o PurgeHistoryListData -siteUrl https://sp.contoso.com/sites/quality -state Completed -lastActivityBeforeDate 2025 -01-01 # Find all workflows in a web application (inventory for migration) NWAdmin.exe -o FindWorkflows -url https://sp.contoso.com -scope WebApplication > C:\\reports\\nintex -workflows .txt # Export / import a workflow definition NWAdmin.exe -o ExportWorkflow -siteUrl https://sp.contoso.com/sites/quality -list \"Deviations\" -workflowName \"Deviation approval\" -fileName C:\\expor"},{"t":"Nintex Workflow Actions Reference – The 40 Actions You Actually Use (On-Prem & Office 365)","c":"nintex","u":"articles/nintex/workflow-actions-reference.html","s":"A categorized reference of Nintex Workflow actions, covering user interaction (request approval, flexi task, assign to-do), list operations, logic and flow, operations (regex, collections, build string, math), integration (web request, call web service, query XML) and their NAC equivalents.","k":"nintex, workflow actions, flexi task, request approval, regular expression, collection operation, reference","h":"The approval actions compared · Inline functions you'll use in Build string / text fields · Regular expression and collections · Web request (calling REST) · NAC equivalents (for migration) · Interview questions · When do you use Flexi task instead of Request approval? · How do you call a REST API from a Nintex on-prem workflow?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Nintex for SharePoint (On-Prem)","b":"Reference Nintex actions by category 01 User interaction Request approval , Request review , Flexi task (custom outcomes), Assign to-do task, Request data, Send notification, Task reminder, Delegate/escalate. 02 List operations Create item, Update item, Delete item, Query list (CAML), Copy item, Check in/out, Set item permissions, Create list, Update multiple items. 03 Logic & flow Set a condition , Run if, Run parallel actions, Loop / For each , State machine , Switch, Pause for/until, Wait for item update, End workflow, Start workflow, Action set. 04 Operations Regular expression , Collection operation , Build string (inline functions), Math operation, Convert value, Set variable, Calculate date, Log in history list. 05 Integration Web request , Call web service, Query XML / JSON, Execute SQL, Query LDAP, Send document to repository, Create site, Provision user (AD). 06 Documents Generate document (DocGen), Convert document, Read document, Update document. # The approval actions compared Action Outcomes Use when Request approval Approve / Reject Classic single or group approval; LazyApproval support Request review Continue only Someone must look and acknowledge Flexi task Custom outcomes (Approve, Reject, Need more info, Escalate) Most flexible; branch per outcome Assign to-do task Complete Non-decision work item Task options include behaviour for multiple assignees (first response applies, all must respond, majority) and escalation (delegate or auto-complete after a time). # Inline functions you'll use in Build string / text fields Common inline functions Nintex Copy fn-FormatDate({ItemProperty:DueDate}, \"dd MMM yyyy\") fn-DateDiffDays({Common:CurrentDate}, {ItemProperty:DueDate}) fn-Substring({ItemProperty:Title}, 0, 20) fn-Replace({Variable:Body}, \";\", \", \") fn-ToUpper({ItemProperty:Plant}) fn-Length({Variable:Approvers}) fn-NewGuid() Available inline functions differ slightly between Nintex versions (2010, 2013+, Office 365). Use the Insert reference → Inline functions tab in the designer to see the exact list and signatures for your version. # Regular expression and collections Split approvers 'a@x.com;b@x.com' into a collection Text Copy Regular expression: Pattern: ; Operation: Split Input text: {Variable:ApproverEmails} Store result in: colApprovers (Collection) For each (colApprovers) → Request approval to {Variable:CurrentApprover} Collection oper"},{"t":"Building a Multi-Level Approval with a Nintex State Machine (Worked Example)","c":"nintex","u":"articles/nintex/state-machine-approvals.html","s":"Design a deviation approval process as a Nintex state machine, covering states, transitions, flexi tasks with custom outcomes, rework loops, escalation, status updates and audit logging, plus how the same design maps to NAC Branch by stage and Power Automate.","k":"nintex, state machine, approval, flexi task, workflow design, branch by stage","h":"The process · Workflow design (Nintex for SharePoint) · Same design in NAC and Power Automate · Interview questions · Why use a state machine instead of nested conditions? · How do you handle \"send back for rework\" without restarting the workflow?","l":"Advanced","x":"Nintex for SharePoint (On-Prem)","b":"Real approval processes are not straight lines: reviewers send items back, critical items need extra sign-off, and some requests are cancelled. A state machine models this cleanly. Each state does its work, then sets the next state . # The process stateDiagram-v2 [*] --> Submitted Submitted --> ManagerReview ManagerReview --> QAReview: Approve ManagerReview --> Rework: Need changes ManagerReview --> Rejected: Reject Rework --> ManagerReview: Resubmitted QAReview --> QAHeadApproval: Approve & Critical QAReview --> Closed: Approve & not Critical QAReview --> Rework: Need changes QAHeadApproval --> Closed: Approve QAHeadApproval --> Rework: Need changes Rejected --> [*] Closed --> [*] # Workflow design (Nintex for SharePoint) Outline Text Copy Set variable varNextState = \"Manager Review\" State machine (states: Manager Review, Rework, QA Review, QA Head Approval, Closed, Rejected) ┌ State: Manager Review │ Update item: Status = \"Manager Review\" │ Flexi task → {ItemProperty:Manager} │ Outcomes: Approve | Need changes | Reject │ Escalation: reminder after 2 days, delegate to {WorkflowConstant:QAInbox} after 5 days │ Switch on {Variable:TaskOutcome} │ Approve → Change state: QA Review │ Need changes → Set varReturnTo = \"Manager Review\"; Change state: Rework │ Reject → Change state: Rejected │ ├ State: Rework │ Update item: Status = \"Rework\" │ Send notification → {Common:Initiator} with comments │ Wait for item update: ReworkDone = Yes │ Update item: ReworkDone = No │ Change state: {Variable:varReturnTo} │ ├ State: QA Review │ Flexi task → {WorkflowConstant:QAReviewersGroup} (first response applies) │ Approve & Severity = Critical → Change state: QA Head Approval │ Approve → Change state: Closed │ Need changes → varReturnTo = \"QA Review\"; Change state: Rework │ ├ State: QA Head Approval (similar) ├ State: Closed → Update item: Status = Closed, ClosedOn = Now; Log; (exit state machine) └ State: Rejected → Update item: Status = Rejected; notify initiator; (exit) Store the decision trail on the item After each task, append to a multi-line \"Approval history\" column: {Common:CurrentDate} – {Variable:Approver} – {Variable:TaskOutcome} – {Variable:Comments} . Business users and auditors read that, not workflow history. # Same design in NAC and Power Automate Concept Nintex (SharePoint) NAC Power Automate State container State machine Branch by stage Do until + Switch on St"},{"t":"Nintex Forms (Classic) – Controls, Rules, Calculated Values, Repeating Sections and Layouts","c":"nintex","u":"articles/nintex/nintex-forms-onprem.html","s":"Build Nintex Forms for SharePoint lists, covering layouts for desktop, tablet and phone, controls, formatting and validation rules, calculated values with form functions, list lookups and cascading, repeating sections stored as XML, and publishing.","k":"nintex forms, forms, rules, calculated value, repeating section, list lookup","h":"Building blocks · Rules examples · Calculated values & lookups · Repeating sections · Interview questions · What is the difference between a formatting rule and a validation rule in Nintex Forms? · How is repeating section data stored and consumed?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Nintex for SharePoint (On-Prem)","b":"Nintex Forms replaced the default SharePoint list forms (and many InfoPath forms) with a drag-and-drop designer. Forms are stored with the list, render in the browser, and support multiple layouts per device. # Building blocks Cheat sheet Nintex Forms toolbox 01 Layouts Default (desktop) plus tablet and phone layouts, each with its own control arrangement. 02 Controls Single/multi line text, Choice, Date/time, People, List lookup , Yes/No, Attachments, Image, Label, Panel, Button, Repeating section , Calculated value , Signature (later versions). 03 Rules Formatting rules (hide/disable when condition is true) and Validation rules (invalid when condition is true + message). 04 Form variables Calculated values not stored in list columns; reusable in rules. 05 Functions lookup() , userProfileLookup() , isNullOrEmpty() , formatDate() , sum() , count() , If() . 06 JavaScript Custom JavaScript + CSS via form settings; NWF$ (jQuery) and form events. # Rules examples Formatting rule: hide 'QA Head' panel unless Critical Text Copy Rule type: Formatting Condition: Severity != \"Critical\" Action: Hide (and disable) Applied to: pnlQAHead Validation rule: due date within 7 days for critical Text Copy Rule type: Validation Condition: and(equals(Severity, \"Critical\"), greaterThan(DueDate, dateAdd(Current Date, 7, \"d\"))) Message: \"Critical deviations must be due within 7 days.\" # Calculated values & lookups Calculated value formulas Text Copy Total cost (repeating section): sum(CostPerItem) Days open: dateDiffDays(Created, Current Date) Manager email: userProfileLookup(Current User, \"Manager\") Plant code: lookup(\"Plants\", \"Title\", PlantName, \"Code\") Cascading lookups : a List lookup control (Area) filters the second List lookup (Equipment) using Filter available selections → Filtered by control value . # Repeating sections Repeating section data is stored as XML in a multi-line text column (with Connected to the column), for example multiple line items in a purchase request. Workflows read it with Query XML : Stored XML (simplified) XML Copy < RepeaterData > < Items > < Item > < Product type = \"System.String\" > Sensor X2 </ Product > < Qty type = \"System.Double\" > 4 </ Qty > </ Item > < Item > < Product type = \"System.String\" > Cable 5m </ Product > < Qty type = \"System.Double\" > 10 </ Qty > </ Item > </ Items > </ RepeaterData > Query XML XPath to get all products Text Copy"},{"t":"Nintex Forms JavaScript Recipes – Classic NWF$ and NAC Custom Plugins","c":"nintex","u":"articles/nintex/nintex-forms-javascript.html","s":"Extend Nintex forms with code, covering client IDs and JavaScript variables, NWF$ jQuery events, setting and reading control values, repeating section scripts, calling REST from a form, and building NAC form plugins as web components.","k":"nintex forms, javascript, nwf, jquery, nac plugins, web components","h":"Classic Nintex Forms (SharePoint & Office 365) · NAC forms: custom plugins (web components) · Interview questions · How do you access a Nintex classic form control in JavaScript? · How do you extend NAC forms with custom UI?","l":"Advanced","x":"Nintex for SharePoint (On-Prem)","b":"# Classic Nintex Forms (SharePoint & Office 365) Give a control a Client ID JavaScript variable name in its Advanced settings (e.g. jsSeverity ). Nintex then emits a global variable containing the control's client ID. Use NWF$ (Nintex's jQuery) to work with it. Form settings → Custom JavaScript JavaScript Copy NWF . FormFiller . Events . RegisterAfterReady ( function ( ) { var $sev = NWF$ ( &#x27;#&#x27; + jsSeverity); var $due = NWF$ ( &#x27;#&#x27; + jsDueDate); // React to changes $sev. change ( function ( ) { var isCritical = NWF$ ( this ). find ( &#x27;input:checked&#x27; ). val () === &#x27;Critical&#x27; || NWF$ ( this ). val () === &#x27;Critical&#x27; ; if (isCritical && !$due. val ()) { var d = new Date (); d. setDate (d. getDate () + 7 ); $due. val ((d. getMonth () + 1 ) + &#x27;/&#x27; + d. getDate () + &#x27;/&#x27; + d. getFullYear ()); // match site locale format } }); // Read a value before submit and block it NWF$ ( &#x27;#&#x27; + jsSaveButton). click ( function ( e ) { if ( NWF$ ( &#x27;#&#x27; + jsTitle). val (). length < 5 ) { alert ( &#x27;Title must have at least 5 characters.&#x27; ); e. preventDefault (); return false ; } }); }); Repeating section: react to new rows JavaScript Copy NWF . FormFiller . Events . RegisterRepeaterRowAdded ( function ( ) { var $row = NWF$ ( arguments [ 0 ][ 0 ]); // the new row $row. find ( &#x27;.qty-input input&#x27; ). val ( &#x27;1&#x27; ); // CSS class set on the control }); Call SharePoint REST from the form (on-prem / O365 classic) JavaScript Copy NWF $. ajax ({ url : _spPageContextInfo. webAbsoluteUrl + \"/_api/web/lists/getbytitle(&#x27;Plants&#x27;)/items?$select=Title,Code&$top=200\" , headers : { Accept : &#x27;application/json;odata=nometadata&#x27; } }). done ( function ( data ) { var $dd = NWF$ ( &#x27;#&#x27; + jsPlant); data. value . forEach ( function ( p ) { $dd. append ( NWF$ ( &#x27;<option>&#x27; ). val (p. Code ). text (p. Title )); }); }); Prefer rules over scripts JavaScript in forms breaks more easily on upgrades and migrations (DOM changes, new form engines). Use rules, calculated values and lookups first, and keep scripts small, documented and tested. # NAC forms: custom plugins (web components) NAC forms don't run arbitrary page scripts. Instead you build custom plugins as standard web components (often with Lit) that declare a contract of properties and events, then register the"},{"t":"Nintex Best Practices & Troubleshooting – Design, Performance, Governance and Common Errors","c":"nintex","u":"articles/nintex/nintex-best-practices.html","s":"Field-tested guidance for Nintex on-prem, Office 365 and NAC, covering naming and documentation, error handling, avoiding chatty loops, task and history hygiene, service accounts, versioning, and a troubleshooting table of frequent errors.","k":"nintex, best practices, troubleshooting, performance, governance, errors","h":"Troubleshooting · Governance · Interview questions · What are the first things you check when a Nintex workflow is stuck? · How do you make Nintex workflows maintainable?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Nintex for SharePoint (On-Prem)","b":"Cheat sheet Nintex design rules 01 Name & label Rename every action (\"Request approval – QA\") and add action labels/comments. 02 One responsibility Split big workflows into child/component workflows. 03 No secrets inline Credentials in workflow constants (on-prem) or connections (NAC). 04 Error branches Capture errors (Web request status, \"Capture errors\" options) and notify an owner. 05 Status on the item Update a Status column at each stage; don't rely on workflow history. 06 Version & export Export .nwf / NAC workflow files before every change; keep in source control. 07 Test data Test with real permission levels, not as an admin. # Troubleshooting Symptom Likely cause Fix Workflow stuck \"In progress\" at a Pause Timer job / workflow timer service not running Check Workflow timer jobs; on-prem restart SPTimerV4 \"Failed on start (retrying)\" Permissions, missing columns, corrupted definition Check history, republish, verify initiator rights LazyApproval replies ignored Incoming email not configured, reply words not recognized Configure inbound email; check approved/declined vocabulary Slow designer / site Huge history lists, many running instances Purge with NWAdmin; terminate orphans Web request 401 Wrong credentials / auth type Use workflow constant credentials; check NTLM vs claims NAC: connection errors Expired connection or permissions on SharePoint Re-authorize connection; verify site permissions of the connection account NAC: workflow not starting from SharePoint event Event subscription missing or site not registered Re-publish workflow; check start event configuration # Governance Maintain an inventory of workflows: owner, purpose, list or site, last run, dependencies. Use service accounts for connections and constants, never personal accounts. Define who may publish to production sites (on-prem: Nintex permissions per site). Review long-running instances monthly and terminate abandoned ones. # Interview questions Q1 What are the first things you check when a Nintex workflow is stuck? The workflow history and status page for the last executed action, timer services and workflow timer jobs (on-prem), permissions of the initiator or connection account, and whether the workflow is waiting for an event (pause, item update, task) that never happens. Q2 How do you make Nintex workflows maintainable? Descriptive action names and comments, small single-purp"},{"t":"Nintex for Office 365 – Architecture, Workflows, Forms and Limitations","c":"nintex","u":"articles/nintex/nintex-o365.html","s":"How Nintex for Office 365 works with SharePoint Online (app model, Nintex-hosted execution), the workflow designer and actions, classic and responsive forms, external start, licensing by workflow, limits, and why organizations are moving to NAC.","k":"nintex for office 365, sharepoint online, workflows, forms, app model, migration","h":"Why move to NAC · Interview questions · Where do Nintex for Office 365 workflows execute? · What are typical limitations of Nintex for Office 365 compared with on-prem Nintex?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Nintex for Office 365","b":"Nintex for Office 365 (O365) brought the Nintex designer to SharePoint Online through the SharePoint app (add-in) model . Workflows are designed per list or library inside SharePoint but execute in Nintex's cloud , calling back into SharePoint. Nintex for Office 365 architecture SHAREPOINT ONLINE SITE Nintex Workflow & Forms apps installed List / library with workflows & forms Task list history designer & runtime in NINTEX CLOUD (AZURE) Workflow designer service Workflow execution engine Form rendering (classic / responsive) SharePoint APIs (app permissions) MICROSOFT 365 SharePoint Online Exchange (notifications) optional NINTEX WORKFLOW CLOUD / NAC External start Component workflows (hybrid scenarios) Nintex for Office 365 architecture Aspect Notes Design Per list/library/site in SharePoint; similar actions to on-prem (fewer) Execution Nintex cloud, so long-running workflows don't load SharePoint servers Forms Classic Nintex Forms for O365, then responsive forms (new designer) Licensing Typically by number of workflows (Standard/Enterprise) and forms Limits Some on-prem actions unavailable (SQL, LDAP); throttling by SharePoint APIs # Why move to NAC The SharePoint add-in model and ACS were retired in SharePoint Online in April 2026 , so add-in-based products must use modern integration. Nintex's strategic path for customers is Nintex Automation Cloud . NAC workflows are not bound to one list : one workflow can span SharePoint, Teams, Salesforce and email. Better forms, DocGen, e-signature, Xtensions, API and analytics. See Migrating Nintex for Office 365 to NAC . # Interview questions Q1 Where do Nintex for Office 365 workflows execute? In Nintex's cloud infrastructure, not inside SharePoint Online. They interact with SharePoint through its APIs using the permissions granted to the Nintex app. Q2 What are typical limitations of Nintex for Office 365 compared with on-prem Nintex? Fewer actions (no direct SQL, LDAP or farm-level operations), dependency on the app model, SharePoint API throttling, list-bound design, and licensing by workflow counts."},{"t":"Nintex Automation Cloud (NAC) – Architecture, Building Blocks and How It Connects to Microsoft 365","c":"nintex","u":"articles/nintex/nac-overview.html","s":"NAC explained end to end, covering tenants and regions, workflows, start events, connections and connectors, forms, data tables (Nintex tables), Xtensions, Gateway for on-prem, instance management, permissions and the API.","k":"nintex automation cloud, nac, architecture, connectors, start events, workflows","h":"Building blocks · A SharePoint-centred NAC workflow · Interview questions · How does NAC connect to SharePoint Online? · What is a component workflow in NAC?","l":"Beginner","x":"Nintex Automation Cloud","b":"Nintex Automation Cloud (NAC) , formerly Nintex Workflow Cloud , is a SaaS automation platform. Workflows are independent of any single system: SharePoint is one connector among many. NAC architecture USERS & TRIGGERS NAC forms (web, mobile) SharePoint Online events Email / schedule / API calls Component workflow calls start events NAC TENANT (REGIONAL CLOUD) Workflow designer &… Variables & data tables Tasks (express approvals) Forms service DocGen & e-sign connections (OAuth / service accounts) CONNECTORS SharePoint Online Microsoft 365 (Outlook, Teams… Salesforce ServiceNow Box DocuSign Xtensions (custom REST) hybrid ON-PREMISES Nintex Gateway SharePoint Server SQL / file shares NAC architecture # Building blocks Concept Description Workflow Designed in the browser; published versions; owners & participants Start event Nintex form, SharePoint (item created/modified, file events), Scheduled, Component workflow, External (email, Salesforce…), Public web form Actions Logic (branch by condition/stage, loops, parallel paths), Tasks (assign task, express approval), Connectors, Data (collections, text, date), DocGen Variables Workflow variables, start event variables, output variables of actions Connections Authenticated links to systems (per connector), shareable with permissions Xtensions Custom connectors from OpenAPI (Swagger 2.0) definitions Component workflows Reusable sub-workflows with inputs and outputs Instances Run history: view, resubmit, terminate; analytics # A SharePoint-centred NAC workflow flowchart LR F[NAC form<br/>Deviation report] --> W[Workflow] W --> SP1[SharePoint: Create item<br/>Deviations list] W --> T[Assign a task: QA review<br/>Approve / Reject / Rework] T -->|Approve| D[Generate document<br/>Deviation report PDF] D --> SP2[SharePoint: Store file<br/>in Reports library] T -->|Reject| N[Send email to submitter] SP2 --> TE[Teams: post message] # Interview questions Q1 How does NAC connect to SharePoint Online? Through the SharePoint Online connector with a connection (OAuth with a user or service account). Workflows can start on SharePoint events and perform actions such as create, update and query items, and file operations, using that connection's permissions. Q2 What is a component workflow in NAC? A reusable workflow with defined input and output variables that other workflows call (Call a workflow / Start a component workflow), s"},{"t":"NAC Workflow Designer – Start Events, Variables, Branching, Loops, Tasks and Error Handling","c":"nintex","u":"articles/nintex/nac-workflow-designer.html","s":"A practical walkthrough of designing workflows in Nintex Automation Cloud, covering start events, variable types and collections, branch by condition and stage, loops, parallel paths, tasks and express approvals, call a web service, error handling and publishing.","k":"nac, workflow designer, branch by stage, variables, tasks, express approval","h":"Anatomy of a NAC workflow · Variables and collections · Branch by stage (state machine) · Tasks and approvals · Calling REST APIs · Error handling · Interview questions · What is the difference between Express approval and Assign a task in NAC? · How do you build a state machine in NAC?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Nintex Automation Cloud","b":"# Anatomy of a NAC workflow NAC workflow structure START EVENT Form / SharePoint / Schedule / Component / External Start event variables (form fields, item properties) then VARIABLES Text Integer Decimal Boolean DateTime Collection Object (JSON-like) File actions LOGIC Branch by condition Branch by stage (state machine) Run parallel paths Loop for each / Loop n times / Loop… people TASKS Assign a task (custom outcomes, form) Express approval (email/one-click) Reminders & escalation systems CONNECTORS & DATA SharePoint Microsoft 365 Call a web service Xtensions Data tables NAC workflow structure # Variables and collections Typical patterns Text Copy Collection: approverEmails (Text collection) - Add item to collection ← each approver from SharePoint query Loop for each item in approverEmails - Assign a task → current item Object variable: deviation { id, title, severity, plant } - Parse JSON (Call a web service response) → object # Branch by stage (state machine) Use Branch by stage for processes with loops and rework, like the Nintex state machine pattern. Each stage ends with a Go to stage action. A stage can be revisited any number of times. # Tasks and approvals Action Use Express approval Fast approve/reject from email or mobile; minimal form Assign a task Custom outcomes, a task form with extra fields (comments, choices), multiple assignees (first/all/majority), reminders, escalation, expiry Assign a task to multiple users Parallel reviews with completion criteria # Calling REST APIs Call a web service supports GET/POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE with headers, body and authentication. Store the response in a variable and parse it with Query JSON . For repeated API use, wrap the API as an Xtension so makers get typed actions. # Error handling Many connector actions offer \"Continue on error\" / error output variables. Check them with Branch by condition. Use a dedicated error-handling component workflow to log and notify. Use instance details ( Resubmit , Terminate ) for operations. # Interview questions Q1 What is the difference between Express approval and Assign a task in NAC? Express approval is a lightweight approve/reject decision answered by email or one click. Assign a task supports custom outcomes, a full task form with fields, multiple assignees with completion rules, reminders, escalation and expiry. Q2 How do you build a state machine in NAC? With Branch"},{"t":"NAC Forms – Responsive Forms with Rules, Variables, Data Lookups and Repeating Sections","c":"nintex","u":"articles/nintex/nac-forms.html","s":"Design Nintex Automation Cloud forms, covering the responsive designer, controls, rules (show/hide, required, validation), form variables and expressions, data lookup control from SharePoint, repeating sections, file upload and signatures, themes, and public vs authenticated forms.","k":"nac forms, nintex forms, rules, data lookup, repeating section, responsive","h":"Data lookup from SharePoint · Rules examples · Repeating sections · Interview questions · How do NAC forms pass data to the workflow? · How do you show live SharePoint data in a NAC form dropdown?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Nintex Automation Cloud","b":"NAC forms are responsive , built in the browser and typically used as the start event of a workflow (or as task forms). They can be public (anonymous, e.g. supplier registration) or require sign-in. NAC form anatomy CONTROLS Text Choice Date Email Number File upload Signature Data lookup Repeating section Label / Image Group behaviour RULES Show / hide Required Read-only Validation with message Set value (variables) data FORM VARIABLES Expressions (concat, sum, if) Values passed to the workflow start WORKFLOW Start event variables = form fields Files & signatures as file variables NAC form anatomy # Data lookup from SharePoint The Data lookup control reads from a connector (SharePoint list, Salesforce…) using a connection, so users pick from live data (e.g. Plants list). You can filter it by another control's value for cascading choices. # Rules examples Rule Condition Action Show QA head section Severity equals Critical Show group QA Head Require due date Severity is Critical or Major Required: Due date Validate amount Amount > 10,000 and CFO approval is empty Validation message # Repeating sections Repeating sections produce collections of objects in the workflow start event (e.g. line items). Loop over them with Loop for each to create child SharePoint items. Migrating classic repeating sections Classic Nintex Forms stored repeating data as XML in a list column. In NAC the same data arrives as a collection, so model it as a child list in SharePoint (parent ID lookup) rather than XML text. # Interview questions Q1 How do NAC forms pass data to the workflow? The form is the workflow's start event : every form control becomes a start event variable (repeating sections become collections, file uploads and signatures become file variables), available to all workflow actions. Q2 How do you show live SharePoint data in a NAC form dropdown? Use the Data lookup control with a SharePoint Online connection, choose the list and display column, and optionally filter by another control's value for cascading lookups."},{"t":"NAC Connectors, Connections and Xtensions – Integrate SharePoint, Microsoft 365 and Any REST API","c":"nintex","u":"articles/nintex/nac-connectors-xtensions.html","s":"Work with NAC connectors (SharePoint Online, Microsoft 365, Teams, Salesforce, DocuSign), manage connections and permissions, connect on-prem with Nintex Gateway, and build Xtensions from OpenAPI with OAuth 2.0 or API keys, including an Azure Function example.","k":"nac, connectors, xtensions, openapi, sharepoint online, gateway, oauth","h":"Connections · Xtensions: your API as Nintex actions · Interview questions · What is an Xtension in Nintex Automation Cloud? · How does NAC reach on-premises systems?","l":"Advanced","x":"Nintex Automation Cloud","b":"# Connections A connection stores authentication for a connector (for example a SharePoint Online service account via OAuth). Workflow actions use a connection, and connection permissions control which designers can use it. flowchart LR D[Workflow designer] -->|uses| C[Connection: SPO – svc-nac@contoso.com] C -->|OAuth tokens| SPO[(SharePoint Online)] A[NAC admin] -->|grants use/manage| C G[Nintex Gateway<br/>on-prem agent] --> SPS[(SharePoint Server / SQL)] D -->|hybrid actions| G Service accounts for production connections Use a dedicated, licensed service account with only the needed SharePoint permissions (site members or specific lists) for production connections, so workflows don't break when employees leave. # Xtensions: your API as Nintex actions An Xtension is a custom connector defined by an OpenAPI 2.0 (Swagger) file. Each operation becomes a workflow action (and optionally a start event via webhooks). deviation-api.swagger.json (excerpt) JSON Copy { \"swagger\" : \"2.0\" , \"info\" : { \"title\" : \"Contoso Deviation API\" , \"version\" : \"1.0\" , \"description\" : \"Deviation operations\" } , \"host\" : \"fn-deviation.azurewebsites.net\" , \"basePath\" : \"/api\" , \"schemes\" : [ \"https\" ] , \"securityDefinitions\" : { \"oauth2\" : { \"type\" : \"oauth2\" , \"flow\" : \"accessCode\" , \"authorizationUrl\" : \"https://login.microsoftonline.com/<tenant-id>/oauth2/v2.0/authorize\" , \"tokenUrl\" : \"https://login.microsoftonline.com/<tenant-id>/oauth2/v2.0/token\" , \"scopes\" : { \"api://deviation-api/user_impersonation\" : \"Access the API\" } } } , \"security\" : [ { \"oauth2\" : [ \"api://deviation-api/user_impersonation\" ] } ] , \"paths\" : { \"/deviations\" : { \"post\" : { \"operationId\" : \"createDeviation\" , \"summary\" : \"Create a deviation\" , \"parameters\" : [ { \"in\" : \"body\" , \"name\" : \"body\" , \"required\" : true , \"schema\" : { \"type\" : \"object\" , \"properties\" : { \"title\" : { \"type\" : \"string\" } , \"severity\" : { \"type\" : \"string\" , \"enum\" : [ \"Minor\" , \"Major\" , \"Critical\" ] } } } } ] , \"responses\" : { \"201\" : { \"description\" : \"Created\" , \"schema\" : { \"type\" : \"object\" , \"properties\" : { \"id\" : { \"type\" : \"string\" } } } } } } } } } Import it in Xtensions → Add custom connector , provide client ID and secret for OAuth (an Entra app with the NAC redirect URI), create a connection, and the actions appear in the designer. # Interview questions Q1 What is an Xtension in Nintex Automation Cloud? A custom conn"},{"t":"NAC Component Workflows, Instance Management and the Nintex REST API","c":"nintex","u":"articles/nintex/nac-component-workflows-api.html","s":"Build reusable component workflows with inputs and outputs, manage running instances (resubmit, terminate), monitor with analytics, and automate NAC with its REST API (start workflows, query instances) from Power Automate, Azure or scripts.","k":"nac, component workflows, api, instances, monitoring, automation","h":"Component workflows · Instance management · Starting workflows via API · Interview questions · How do component workflows return data to their caller? · How would you start a NAC workflow from an external system?","l":"Advanced","x":"Nintex Automation Cloud","b":"# Component workflows sequenceDiagram participant P as Parent workflow (Deviation) participant C as Component: Resolve approvers participant L as Component: Audit log P->>C: Start a component workflow (plant, severity) C-->>P: Output: approverEmails (collection) P->>L: Start (itemId, action, actor) — don't wait Create a workflow with the Component workflow start event and define input variables . End with output variables (returned to the caller when it waits for completion). Callers use Call a workflow (wait or fire-and-forget). Permissions: callers need permission to the component workflow. # Instance management Task Where See running/failed instances Instances page (filter by workflow, status, date) Inspect a failure Instance details → action-level status and messages Resubmit / terminate Instance actions Track volumes & durations Insights / analytics dashboards # Starting workflows via API NAC workflows can be started from other systems through the Nintex Automation Cloud API (or a workflow's external start URL with a token). A common pattern: a Power Automate flow or Azure Function calls NAC when a Dataverse row changes. Start a workflow (illustrative) HTTP Copy POST https://<region>.nintex.io/workflows/v1/designs/<workflow-id>/instances Authorization : Bearer <api-token> Content-Type : application/json { \"startData\": { \"se_title\": \"Leak at Plant A\", \"se_severity\": \"Critical\" } } Check the current API reference Endpoint paths, regions and authentication (API keys / OAuth client credentials) evolve. Use the Nintex developer portal for the exact contract of your tenant region. # Interview questions Q1 How do component workflows return data to their caller? Through output variables defined in the component workflow. When the caller waits for completion, the outputs become available as variables in the parent workflow. Q2 How would you start a NAC workflow from an external system? Use the workflow's external start (start URL with key) or the NAC REST API with an API token or client credentials, passing start data that maps to the workflow's start event variables."},{"t":"Nintex DocGen & e-Signature – Generate Documents and Collect Signatures in NAC","c":"nintex","u":"articles/nintex/nac-docgen-esign.html","s":"Generate Word, PDF and PowerPoint documents from workflow data with Nintex DocGen tags, repeating tables and conditional content, store them in SharePoint, and send them for signature with Nintex eSign or DocuSign/Adobe connectors.","k":"nintex, docgen, document generation, e-signature, docusign, pdf","h":"Template tags · Signature step · Interview questions · How do you generate a document with a variable number of table rows in Nintex? · Where should generated and signed documents be stored?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Nintex Automation Cloud","b":"flowchart LR T[Template in SharePoint<br/>Word with DocGen tags] --> G[Generate document<br/>action] D[Workflow data<br/>item fields, collections] --> G G --> P[PDF / DOCX] P --> S[Store in SharePoint<br/>Reports library] P --> E[Send for signature<br/>Nintex eSign / DocuSign] E --> W[Wait for signing<br/>→ store signed copy] # Template tags DocGen uses tags in Word, PowerPoint or PDF templates bound to workflow variables (inserted with the Nintex DocGen tagger in the designer): Purpose Example (illustrative) Simple value {{DeviationTitle}} Date formatting {{DueDate:dd MMM yyyy}} Repeating table rows A table row bound to a collection (CAPA actions) Conditional section Show the \"Critical escalation\" paragraph only if Severity = Critical Images Signature or logo from file variables # Signature step Generate the PDF. Nintex eSign (or DocuSign / Adobe Sign connector): create an envelope with signers, routing order and fields. Wait for completion (event or polling), then download the signed PDF and certificate of completion. Store both in SharePoint with metadata ( SignedOn , Signers ). # Interview questions Q1 How do you generate a document with a variable number of table rows in Nintex? Bind a table row in the template to a collection variable (for example CAPA actions queried from SharePoint) using DocGen repeating tags. The row repeats for each item. Q2 Where should generated and signed documents be stored? In a SharePoint library (or a DMS) with metadata linking them to the source item, so they inherit retention, permissions and search, rather than only being emailed."},{"t":"Migrating Nintex for Office 365 to Nintex Automation Cloud – A Complete Playbook","c":"nintex","u":"articles/nintex/migrate-o365-to-nac.html","s":"A field-tested approach to moving Nintex for Office 365 workflows and forms to NAC, covering inventory, the Nintex migration tool, action mapping, redesigning list-bound workflows, rebuilding forms, connections, in-flight instances, testing, cut-over and lessons learned.","k":"nintex, migration, nintex for office 365, nac, workflows, forms, playbook","h":"The playbook · 1 · Inventory and rationalize · 2 · Prepare NAC · 3 · Convert with the migration tool, then fix · 4 · Rebuild forms · 5 · In-flight instances and cut-over · Interview questions · What is the biggest design difference when migrating from Nintex for Office 365 to NAC? · How do you handle long-running in-flight instances during migration? · What would you put in the migration inventory?","l":"Advanced","x":"Migration & Comparison","b":"Moving from Nintex for Office 365 (O365) to Nintex Automation Cloud (NAC) is not a copy-paste job. O365 workflows are bound to a list, and NAC workflows are independent and connector-based. The best migrations combine tool-assisted conversion with targeted redesign . # The playbook flowchart LR A[1 · Inventory<br/>sites, lists, workflows,<br/>forms, run counts] --> B[2 · Rationalize<br/>retire · merge · redesign] B --> C[3 · Prepare NAC<br/>connections, service account,<br/>permissions, naming] C --> D[4 · Convert<br/>migration tool +<br/>manual rebuild] D --> E[5 · Rebuild forms<br/>NAC responsive forms] E --> F[6 · Test<br/>UAT with real data,<br/>permission levels] F --> G[7 · Cut-over<br/>stop O365 starts,<br/>drain in-flight] G --> H[8 · Decommission<br/>remove O365 apps] # 1 · Inventory and rationalize Capture for each workflow: site, list, start type (manual, on create, on change), actions used, forms, last run date, run frequency, owner and business criticality. Category Typical share Action Not run in 12 months 20–40% Retire after owner confirmation Simple notifications 20–30% Rebuild quickly in NAC (or SharePoint rules / Power Automate, if strategy allows) Standard approvals 30–40% Tool conversion + adjust tasks and forms Complex (state machines, loops, web requests, DocGen) 10–20% Redesign using NAC patterns (branch by stage, component workflows, Xtensions) # 2 · Prepare NAC Connections : SharePoint Online connection on a service account with access to the target sites. Office 365 / Teams connections for notifications. Naming convention : [Dept] – [Process] – [Trigger] , for example QA – Deviation approval – Form . Permissions : workflow owners and designers per department; connection usage rights. Component workflows for shared pieces: approver resolution, audit logging, notifications. # 3 · Convert with the migration tool, then fix Nintex provides migration tooling to export O365 workflows and import them into NAC, producing a conversion report that lists unsupported actions. Expect to fix: O365 element NAC approach Start on item created/modified SharePoint Online start event (watch for duplicate triggers), or a NAC form as start Workflow context (Current item) Start event variables; Get item action to refresh fields Request approval / Flexi task Assign a task (custom outcomes) / Express approval Task forms (O365) NAC task forms Set a condition "},{"t":"Migrating Nintex for SharePoint (On-Prem) to NAC or Hybrid","c":"nintex","u":"articles/nintex/migrate-onprem-to-nac.html","s":"Paths for Nintex Workflow and Forms on SharePoint 2013–SE, covering moving to NAC with SharePoint Online, hybrid with Nintex Gateway while SharePoint stays on-prem, inventory with NWAdmin, mapping farm-only actions, forms and UDAs, and sequencing with the SharePoint content migration.","k":"nintex, migration, on-premises, nac, hybrid, nintex gateway, sharegate","h":"Choose the target · Sequencing with a SharePoint migration · On-prem only features to redesign · Interview questions · Do Nintex on-prem workflows run in SharePoint Online after migrating content? · What does Nintex Gateway enable?","l":"Advanced","x":"Migration & Comparison","b":"# Choose the target flowchart TD A{Is SharePoint content<br/>moving to SharePoint Online?} -->|yes| B[Content: ShareGate / SPMT<br/>Workflows: rebuild in NAC<br/>against SPO connector] A -->|no, stays on SE| C{Keep Nintex on-prem?} C -->|yes, short term| D[Upgrade Nintex for SE<br/>reduce & clean up] C -->|no| E[NAC + Nintex Gateway<br/>hybrid actions to on-prem SharePoint] # Sequencing with a SharePoint migration Inventory workflows ( NWAdmin -o FindWorkflows ), forms, UDAs, constants and scheduled workflows. Migrate content (ShareGate copies lists and libraries; Nintex definitions don't execute in SPO). Rebuild workflows in NAC pointing at the new SPO lists (connections, start events). Rebuild forms as NAC forms (or Power Apps, depending on strategy). Cut over per site/process, and archive history (export Nintex history to CSV or a list if needed for audit). # On-prem only features to redesign On-prem feature Cloud approach Execute SQL / Query LDAP Xtension over an API, or Nintex Gateway hybrid actions Workflow constants (credentials) NAC connections UDAs Component workflows Farm-level actions (create site collection, provision AD user) Graph/Entra via Xtension or Power Automate Site workflows on schedules Scheduled start event LazyApproval Express approvals Nintex history stays behind Workflow history and Nintex databases don't migrate to NAC. If you need an audit trail, export it before decommissioning, or rely on status and approval history columns maintained on the items. # Interview questions Q1 Do Nintex on-prem workflows run in SharePoint Online after migrating content? No. Nintex for SharePoint is a farm product. After content moves to SPO, workflows must be rebuilt in NAC (or another platform) using the SharePoint Online connector. Q2 What does Nintex Gateway enable? Hybrid scenarios where NAC workflows interact with on-premises systems (SharePoint Server, SQL, file shares) through an outbound-connected agent in your network, which is useful when SharePoint stays on-prem but you want cloud workflow capabilities."},{"t":"Nintex vs Power Automate – An Honest Comparison for SharePoint-Centric Organizations","c":"nintex","u":"articles/nintex/nintex-vs-power-automate.html","s":"Compare Nintex Automation Cloud and Power Automate on capabilities, forms, document generation, approvals, connectors, governance, ALM, licensing model and skills, with a decision guide and an action mapping table for teams moving between them.","k":"nintex, power automate, comparison, decision, workflow, migration","h":"Capability comparison · Decision guide · Action mapping (for teams switching) · Interview questions · When would you recommend Nintex over Power Automate? · When would you recommend Power Automate?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Migration & Comparison","b":"Both platforms automate business processes around SharePoint and Microsoft 365. The right choice depends on existing investment, process complexity, users and licensing , not on \"which is better\" in general. # Capability comparison Area Nintex Automation Cloud Power Automate Workflow designer Process-owner friendly, clear stage/branch visuals Maker friendly; very flexible; expressions-heavy for complex logic State machines Branch by stage (native) Pattern-based (Do until + Switch) Forms Built-in responsive forms (public & internal) Power Apps / Microsoft Forms (separate tools) Document generation Built-in DocGen Word template action (premium) / Premium content assembly / third-party e-Signature Nintex eSign + connectors Connectors (Adobe, DocuSign) Approvals Assign a task, express approval Approvals connector, Teams adaptive cards Connectors Strong core set + Xtensions 1,000+ connectors + custom connectors RPA Nintex RPA Power Automate Desktop Process mapping Nintex Process Manager Process mining / advisor Microsoft 365 integration Via connectors Native (same platform, Dataverse, Teams, Copilot) ALM Nintex export/import, environments Solutions , pipelines, PAC CLI Governance Nintex admin, workflow permissions DLP, CoE kit, Managed Environments Licensing Nintex subscription (workflows / usage) Seeded M365 rights + Premium / Process / PAYG # Decision guide flowchart TD A{Large existing Nintex estate<br/>and skilled Nintex team?} -->|yes| N1[NAC: migrate & standardize] A -->|no| B{Need built-in forms + DocGen +<br/>process mapping in one tool?} B -->|yes| N2[Nintex is a strong fit] B -->|no| C{Microsoft-first strategy,<br/>Dataverse / Copilot / Teams?} C -->|yes| PA[Power Automate] C -->|mixed| H[Hybrid: Nintex for document-heavy<br/>processes, Power Automate for<br/>M365 automations] # Action mapping (for teams switching) Nintex Power Automate Start event: SharePoint item created Trigger: When an item is created Assign a task (custom outcomes) Approvals: custom responses Express approval Approvals: Approve/Reject Branch by condition Condition / Switch Branch by stage Do until + Switch on status Loop for each Apply to each Run parallel paths Parallel branches Call a web service HTTP (premium) / Send an HTTP request to SharePoint Generate document Populate a Microsoft Word template + Convert to PDF Component workflow Child flow Connections Connection references "},{"t":"What is InfoPath? Architecture, Form Types and Why It Mattered (2003–2026)","c":"infopath-legacy","u":"articles/infopath-legacy/infopath-overview.html","s":"InfoPath explained for people who inherit it, covering InfoPath Designer and Filler, XML-based form templates (XSN), browser forms via InfoPath Forms Services, list forms vs form libraries, how data is stored, and its retirement timeline.","k":"infopath, forms, xml, xsn, infopath forms services, legacy","h":"The pieces · Two kinds of SharePoint InfoPath forms · Promoted properties · Timeline · Interview questions · What is an XSN file? · What is the difference between a form library form and a list form?","l":"Beginner","x":"InfoPath","b":"Microsoft InfoPath (2003–2013) is an XML-based electronic forms tool. For a decade it was the way to build complex SharePoint forms: rules, repeating tables, data connections to lists and web services, and code-behind. Thousands of organizations still have InfoPath forms in production, which is exactly why its retirement matters. # The pieces InfoPath architecture DESIGN InfoPath Designer 2013 Form template (.xsn = cabinet of XSD, XSL views,… publish to SHAREPOINT Form library (stores XML documents) List form (customizes list New/Edit/Display) Data connection library (.udcx) rendered by CLIENTS InfoPath Filler (desktop client) Browser via InfoPath Forms Services (SharePoint… data DATA CONNECTIONS SharePoint lists (lookup) Web services (SOAP / REST) SQL Server Submit to library / list / web service /… InfoPath architecture # Two kinds of SharePoint InfoPath forms Form library form List form Data stored as XML file per form in a library (promoted fields become columns) List item columns Repeating data Native (repeating tables in XML) Limited (no repeating tables to columns) Typical use Complex documents: purchase requests with line items, audit checklists Customized list New/Edit forms Migration difficulty Higher (XML must be transformed) Lower # Promoted properties Form library forms promote selected XML fields to library columns, so views, search and workflows can use them. Fields not promoted exist only inside the XML file. They are invisible to SharePoint and must be extracted during migration. # Timeline 2003 InfoPath 2003 Ships with Office 2003 Professional/Enterprise; XML forms for desktop. 2007 InfoPath Forms Services Browser-enabled forms in MOSS 2007 Enterprise. 2010 InfoPath 2010 Designer/Filler split; list form customization; tighter SharePoint integration. 2013 InfoPath 2013 The last version . Microsoft announced in 2014 that no new version would follow. 2026 End of support InfoPath 2013 reaches end of support (July 2026) and InfoPath Forms Services in SharePoint Online is retired. On-prem SE still includes Forms Services for now. Confirm dates for your environment Microsoft announced the InfoPath 2013 end of support and the InfoPath Forms Services retirement in SharePoint Online for 2026. On-premises behaviour follows the SharePoint Server version's lifecycle. Check Microsoft's lifecycle pages and Message Center for your tenant before planning. #"},{"t":"Designing InfoPath Forms – Data Source, Views, Controls, Rules and Repeating Tables","c":"infopath-legacy","u":"articles/infopath-legacy/infopath-form-design.html","s":"How InfoPath forms are built, covering the main data source (XML schema), groups and fields, views, controls (repeating table, optional section, people picker), rules (validation, formatting, action), formulas and XPath, and browser compatibility, so you can read and migrate existing forms.","k":"infopath, rules, views, repeating table, xpath, form design","h":"Main data source · Views · Rule types · Browser compatibility · Migration reading checklist · Interview questions · Where does most InfoPath business logic hide? · What is a repeating table in InfoPath and how should it be migrated?","l":"Intermediate","x":"InfoPath","b":"Understanding how an InfoPath form is built is essential to migrating it correctly. Most hidden business logic lives in rules and views . # Main data source Example main data source (purchase request) Text Copy myFields ├─ RequestId (text) ├─ Requester (person, group) ├─ Department (text) ├─ Status (text) ├─ Items (repeating group) │ └─ Item │ ├─ Product (text) │ ├─ Qty (whole number) │ └─ UnitPrice (decimal) ├─ Total (decimal, formula: sum(../my:Items/my:Item/my:Qty * ...)) └─ Approvals (repeating group: Approver, Decision, Date, Comment) # Views Forms often have several views (Request, Manager approval, Finance, Print). Switch view rules show the right one by status or user role, which is effectively a lightweight UI state machine. # Rule types Cheat sheet InfoPath rules 01 Validation Mark a field invalid with a message: \"Qty must be > 0\". 02 Formatting Hide, disable or style controls: hide Finance section unless Status = \"Manager Approved\". 03 Action Set field value, query/submit data connection, switch view, close form, send data to web part. 04 Form Load rules Set defaults (current user, today), switch to the right view by role. 05 Submit rules Custom submit sequences: set status, submit to library, email notification. Typical formulas (XPath functions) Text Copy userName() current user (account name) today() / now() dates concat(my:Department, \"-\", my:RequestId) sum(my:Items/my:Item/my:LineTotal) count(my:Items/my:Item) substring-before(my:Email, \"@\") # Browser compatibility Browser forms (InfoPath Forms Services) support a subset of desktop features. Look for the Browser compatibility check (Design Checker). Many organizations designed \"browser-enabled\" forms, which simplifies migration because they avoid desktop-only features. # Migration reading checklist Export the XSN ( File → Publish → Export Source Files ) to read manifest.xsf and schema. List all views and the rules that switch them. List all rules per control (validation, formatting, action) and form load / submit rules. List data connections (lookup lists, web services, submit targets). Note promoted fields vs XML-only fields. Note code-behind (if any) and what it does. # Interview questions Q1 Where does most InfoPath business logic hide? In rules (validation, formatting, action, form load and submit rules), view switching , formulas on fields, and data connections , plus occasional code-beh"},{"t":"InfoPath Data Connections – SharePoint Lists, Web Services, SQL and UDCX Files","c":"infopath-legacy","u":"articles/infopath-legacy/infopath-data-connections.html","s":"How InfoPath forms retrieve and submit data, covering receive and submit connections, SharePoint list lookups, SOAP and REST web services, SQL, the data connection library and UDCX files with authentication, and the cross-domain and admin-approval considerations.","k":"infopath, data connections, udcx, web services, sql, sharepoint","h":"Interview questions · What is a UDCX file used for? · How do you replace an InfoPath web service connection in a modern solution?","l":"Intermediate","x":"InfoPath","b":"InfoPath data connections FORM TEMPLATE Main data connection (submit) Secondary connections (receive) Rules that query/submit receive data SOURCES SharePoint list/library… SOAP web service (asmx/svc) REST (XML) SQL Server (desktop only o… XML file submit data TARGETS SharePoint form library SharePoint list (list forms) Web service Email Hosting environment centrally managed DATA CONNECTION LIBRARY .udcx files Credentials via Secure Store Admin-approved changes without republishing InfoPath data connections Connection type Notes for migration SharePoint list (receive) Lookups for dropdowns (Departments, Approvers). Rebuild with Power Apps data sources or NAC data lookup Web service (receive/submit) Business logic often lives there. Expose via custom connector / Xtension / Azure Function SQL Server Direct DB access. Move behind an API; never connect forms directly to SQL in the cloud Submit to library Creates XML files. The target becomes a list item (+ child list) or Dataverse row Email submit Replace with Power Automate / NAC notifications UDCX in a data connection library Admin-managed connection files (URL, auth). Document them, because they hide endpoints and credentials A UDCX file (simplified) XML Copy < udc:DataSource MajorVersion = \"2\" MinorVersion = \"0\" xmlns:udc = \"http://schemas.microsoft.com/office/infopath/2006/udc\" > < udc:Name > GetCostCentres </ udc:Name > < udc:Type MajorVersion = \"2\" MinorVersion = \"0\" Type = \"WebService\" > < udc:SubType MajorVersion = \"0\" MinorVersion = \"0\" Type = \"\" /> </ udc:Type > < udc:ConnectionInfo Purpose = \"ReadOnly\" AltDataSource = \"\" > < udc:WsdlUrl > https://erp.contoso.local/CostCentreService.asmx?WSDL </ udc:WsdlUrl > < udc:SelectCommand > < udc:ServiceUrl > https://erp.contoso.local/CostCentreService.asmx </ udc:ServiceUrl > </ udc:SelectCommand > < udc:Authentication > < udc:SSO AppId = \"ERPReadOnly\" CredentialType = \"NTLM\" /> </ udc:Authentication > </ udc:ConnectionInfo > </ udc:DataSource > Inventory hidden endpoints UDCX files and web service connections reveal integrations nobody documented . List every endpoint and its owner early in a migration, because those systems need APIs or connectors in the new design. # Interview questions Q1 What is a UDCX file used for? A Universal Data Connection file stored in a SharePoint data connection library . It centralizes connection details (URLs, authentication suc"},{"t":"InfoPath Code-Behind & Administrator-Approved Templates","c":"infopath-legacy","u":"articles/infopath-legacy/infopath-code-behind.html","s":"How InfoPath forms with C# or VB code (VSTA) work, covering security levels (restricted, domain, full trust), administrator-approved form templates deployed through Central Administration, common code patterns, and what to do with code during migration.","k":"infopath, code-behind, vsta, csharp, full trust, administrator-approved","h":"Security levels · Typical code patterns you'll find · Migration guidance · Interview questions · What is an administrator-approved form template? · Why can't InfoPath forms with code-behind be moved as-is to modern platforms?","l":"Advanced","x":"InfoPath","b":"Some InfoPath forms contain managed code (C# or VB.NET) written in VSTA (Visual Studio Tools for Applications). The code compiles into an assembly inside the XSN and runs in the InfoPath client or on the SharePoint server (Forms Services). flowchart LR D[InfoPath Designer<br/>+ VSTA C# code] --> X[XSN with assembly] X -->|Full trust / needs code on server| CA[Central Admin:<br/>Upload form template] CA --> AP[Administrator-approved<br/>template, farm feature] AP --> ACT[Activate to site collection] ACT --> FS[InfoPath Forms Services<br/>runs code server-side] # Security levels Level Can do Deployment Restricted No access outside the form Any Domain Access to resources in the form's domain Normal publish Full trust Anything the user can (files, registry, network) Administrator-approved template (signed, uploaded by admin) # Typical code patterns you'll find FormCode.cs (typical InfoPath code-behind) C# Copy public partial class FormCode { public void InternalStartup () { EventManager.FormEvents.Loading += new LoadingEventHandler(FormEvents_Loading); EventManager.XmlEvents[ \"/my:myFields/my:Department\" ].Changed += new XmlChangedEventHandler(Department_Changed); EventManager.FormEvents.Submit += new SubmitEventHandler(FormEvents_Submit); } public void FormEvents_Loading ( object sender, LoadingEventArgs e ) { XPathNavigator root = MainDataSource.CreateNavigator(); root.SelectSingleNode( \"/my:myFields/my:RequestId\" , NamespaceManager) .SetValue( \"REQ-\" + DateTime.Now.ToString( \"yyyyMMddHHmmss\" )); } public void Department_Changed ( object sender, XmlEventArgs e ) { // query a secondary data source filtered by department DataSources[ \"CostCentres\" ].QueryConnection.Execute(); } public void FormEvents_Submit ( object sender, SubmitEventArgs e ) { // custom submit: set status then submit through the main connection MainDataSource.CreateNavigator().SelectSingleNode( \"/my:myFields/my:Status\" , NamespaceManager).SetValue( \"Submitted\" ); DataConnections[ \"SharePoint Library Submit\" ].Execute(); e.CancelableArgs.Cancel = false ; } } # Migration guidance Code does… Modern replacement Generate IDs / defaults Power Fx defaults, SharePoint ID-based numbering, Dataverse autonumber Cascading lookups Delegable filters in Power Apps / NAC data lookup Complex validation Power Fx validation, SPFx form customizer, Dataverse plug-in Custom submit sequences Power Automate / NAC wor"},{"t":"InfoPath Forms Services Administration (On-Premises) – Configuration, Throttles and State","c":"infopath-legacy","u":"articles/infopath-legacy/infopath-forms-services.html","s":"Administer browser-based InfoPath on SharePoint Server, covering enabling Forms Services, the State Service, data connection timeouts and throttles, user browser-enabled templates, upgrades, and monitoring before retirement.","k":"infopath forms services, administration, state service, sharepoint server, configuration","h":"Configuration (Central Administration → General Application Settings → InfoPath Forms Services) · Inventory before retirement · Interview questions · Which service application does InfoPath Forms Services depend on? · How do you find all InfoPath forms in a farm or tenant?","l":"Intermediate","x":"InfoPath","b":"InfoPath Forms Services (IFS) renders InfoPath forms in the browser. It is part of SharePoint Server Enterprise (2007–SE). InfoPath Forms Services in a farm BROWSER Form filling (no InfoPath client needed) request SHAREPOINT WEB SERVERS InfoPath Forms Services (FormServer.aspx) Session state via State Service uses SERVICES State Service (SQL DB) Secure Store (UDCX credentials) Data connection library data TARGETS Form libraries Lists Web services SQL InfoPath Forms Services in a farm # Configuration (Central Administration → General Application Settings → InfoPath Forms Services) Setting Purpose Allow users to browser-enable form templates Designers can publish browser forms Render form templates that are browser-enabled by users Actually render them in the browser Data connection timeouts (default/max) Protect servers from slow back ends Data connection response size Limit large responses HTTP data connections / cross-domain Allow authentication for embedded SQL / cross-domain UDCX Session state thresholds Max postbacks per session, max actions per postback PowerShell equivalents PowerShell Copy Get-SPInfoPathFormsService | Select-Object AllowUserFormBrowserEnabling, AllowUserFormBrowserRendering, DefaultDataConnectionTimeout, MaxDataConnectionTimeout Set-SPInfoPathFormsService -AllowUserFormBrowserEnabling $true -AllowUserFormBrowserRendering $true -DefaultDataConnectionTimeout 10000 # State Service (required by IFS) New-SPStateServiceDatabase -Name \"SP_StateService\" | New-SPStateServiceApplication -Name \"State Service\" | New-SPStateServiceApplicationProxy -DefaultProxyGroup # Inventory before retirement Find InfoPath-enabled libraries and lists PowerShell Copy Get-SPSite -Limit All | ForEach-Object { foreach ( $web in $_ .AllWebs) { foreach ( $list in $web .Lists) { $isFormLib = $list .BaseTemplate -eq \"XMLForm\" $hasIPForm = $list .ContentTypes | Where-Object { $_ .DocumentTemplateUrl -like \"*.xsn\" } if ( $isFormLib -or $hasIPForm ) { [ pscustomobject ] @ { Web = $web .Url; List = $list .Title; Items = $list .ItemCount; FormLibrary = $isFormLib ; Modified = $list .LastItemModifiedDate } } } $web .Dispose() } $_ .Dispose() } | Export-Csv C:\\reports\\infopath -inventory .csv -NoTypeInformation For SharePoint Online use the Microsoft 365 Assessment tool (InfoPath mode). # Interview questions Q1 Which service application does InfoPath Forms Services depend on?"},{"t":"SharePoint 2010 Workflows – Out-of-the-Box Approvals, SPD 2010 and Visual Studio Workflows","c":"infopath-legacy","u":"articles/infopath-legacy/sp2010-workflows.html","s":"The SharePoint 2010 workflow platform (Windows Workflow Foundation 3.5 inside SharePoint), covering out-of-the-box Approval, Collect Feedback, Three-state workflows, SharePoint Designer 2010 reusable and site workflows, Visual Studio workflows, task and history lists, and the 2020 SPO retirement.","k":"sharepoint 2010 workflow, wf 3.5, approval workflow, three-state, sharepoint designer, legacy","h":"Out-of-the-box workflows · SharePoint Designer 2010 workflows · Interview questions · Where do SharePoint 2010 workflows execute? · What is the three-state workflow?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Legacy Workflows","b":"The SharePoint 2010 workflow platform (also used by MOSS 2007) runs Windows Workflow Foundation 3.5 inside SharePoint (w3wp for interactive steps, OWSTIMER for paused and delayed work). It powered out-of-the-box approvals and SharePoint Designer workflows for a decade. SharePoint 2010 workflow platform AUTHORING Out-of-the-box templates (Approval, Collect Feedback… SharePoint Designer 2010 (list, reusable, site) Visual Studio 2010 (code workflows) runs on SHAREPOINT SERVER WF 3.5 runtime in SharePoint Workflow timer jobs (OWSTIMER) Workflow associations on lists / content types stores CONTENT DB Task list Workflow history list Workflow state (persisted instances) SharePoint 2010 workflow platform # Out-of-the-box workflows Workflow Use Approval Route a document or item to approvers (serial/parallel stages) Collect Feedback Gather comments Collect Signatures Office documents with signature lines Three-state Track issues through three states of a choice column (e.g. Active → Resolved → Closed) Disposition approval Records management Publishing approval Publishing pages # SharePoint Designer 2010 workflows List workflows : bound to a list. Reusable workflows : bound to a content type, exportable as WSP and usable in Visual Studio. Site workflows : not bound to an item. Impersonation step : run actions as the workflow author. Task processes : highly customizable approval behaviour (the most powerful SPD 2010 feature). Retired in SharePoint Online SharePoint 2010 workflows were turned off for new SharePoint Online tenants on 1 August 2020 and removed from existing tenants from 1 November 2020 . On-premises they remain available in SharePoint 2013 through SE but are deprecated, so migrate them. # Interview questions Q1 Where do SharePoint 2010 workflows execute? Inside the SharePoint server processes on Windows Workflow Foundation 3.5. Interactive steps run in the web application process, and delayed or paused work resumes in the SharePoint Timer service (OWSTIMER). Q2 What is the three-state workflow? An out-of-the-box workflow that tracks an item through three values of a choice column (for example Active → Resolved → Closed), creating tasks and notifications at each transition. It was popular for issue tracking."},{"t":"SharePoint 2013 Workflows – Stages, Loops, Dictionaries, REST Calls and App Step","c":"infopath-legacy","u":"articles/infopath-legacy/sp2013-workflows.html","s":"The SharePoint 2013 workflow platform (Workflow Manager), covering SPD 2013 stage-based designer, loops, dictionaries and Call HTTP Web Service, app step permissions, task processes, Visual Studio declarative workflows, and the April 2026 SPO retirement.","k":"sharepoint 2013 workflow, workflow manager, sharepoint designer 2013, app step, legacy","h":"What was new compared with 2010 · Retirement · Interview questions · What is the biggest functional difference between 2010 and 2013 workflows? · What happened to SharePoint 2013 workflows in SharePoint Online?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Legacy Workflows","b":"The SharePoint 2013 workflow platform runs in Workflow Manager (a separate service, see Workflow Manager ) on Windows Workflow Foundation 4.5. SharePoint Designer 2013 introduced a stage-based designer that made more complex logic possible without code. # What was new compared with 2010 Cheat sheet SharePoint 2013 workflow capabilities 01 Stages Workflows are composed of stages with explicit \"Go to\" transitions, enabling loops between stages. 02 Loops \"Loop n times\" and \"Loop with condition\" blocks. 03 Dictionaries Build Dictionary / Get item from dictionary, working with JSON from REST. 04 Call HTTP Web Service REST calls to SharePoint or external services. 05 App step Run enclosed actions with the workflow app's permissions. 06 Start a task process Approval-style tasks with outcomes. 07 Workflow Manager Scalable, out-of-SharePoint execution with its own databases. flowchart LR S1[Stage: Submit<br/>set status, notify] --> S2[Stage: Manager approval<br/>start task process] S2 -->|Approved| S3[Stage: Finance check<br/>Call HTTP Web Service → ERP] S2 -->|Rejected| S5[Stage: Rejected] S3 -->|Budget OK| S4[Stage: Completed] S3 -->|Over budget| S2 # Retirement Nov 2023 Retirement announced Microsoft announced the retirement of SharePoint 2013 workflows in SharePoint Online. 2 Apr 2024 New tenants SharePoint 2013 workflows turned off for new tenants. 2 Apr 2026 Retired SharePoint 2013 workflows retired for all SharePoint Online tenants. On-premises (2016/2019/SE) the platform keeps running with SharePoint Workflow Manager , but no new investment is expected. Plan migration to Power Automate or Nintex. See Mapping legacy workflow actions to Power Automate . # Interview questions Q1 What is the biggest functional difference between 2010 and 2013 workflows? 2013 workflows are stage-based with transitions (allowing loops back to earlier stages), have loops , dictionaries and HTTP web service calls , and run in Workflow Manager outside SharePoint. 2010 workflows run inside SharePoint with linear steps and richer task processes. Q2 What happened to SharePoint 2013 workflows in SharePoint Online? They were disabled for new tenants in April 2024 and retired for all tenants on 2 April 2026 . Existing workflows must be rebuilt in Power Automate or another platform."},{"t":"InfoPath Retirement (2026) – What Changes and Your Migration Options","c":"infopath-legacy","u":"articles/infopath-legacy/infopath-retirement.html","s":"What the InfoPath 2013 end of support and the InfoPath Forms Services retirement in SharePoint Online mean, how to find every form, a decision matrix for Power Apps, list form formatting, Nintex/NAC, SPFx form customizer and Microsoft Forms, and a phased migration plan.","k":"infopath, retirement, migration, power apps, nintex, spfx, end of support","h":"Step 1 · Find every form · Step 2 · Choose a target per form · Step 3 · Plan the data · Step 4 · Phased plan · Interview questions · How would you plan an InfoPath migration for 300 forms? · What makes form-library InfoPath forms harder to migrate than list forms?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Retirement & Migration","b":"Microsoft announced in 2014 that InfoPath 2013 would be the last version . In 2026, InfoPath 2013 reached end of support and InfoPath Forms Services in SharePoint Online is being retired . Forms that still depend on it must move to a modern platform. 2013 last InfoPath version 2014 \"no new version\" announcement Jul 2026 InfoPath 2013 end of support 5 realistic modern targets Confirm exact dates and behaviour for your tenant Microsoft communicated the retirement through its lifecycle site and Message Center. Check the current notices for SharePoint Online behaviour (rendering and editing of existing forms) and for your SharePoint Server version's lifecycle. # Step 1 · Find every form flowchart LR A[Microsoft 365 Assessment tool<br/>InfoPath mode] --> R[(Inventory:<br/>site, list, form type,<br/>last used, item count)] B[On-prem PowerShell scan] --> R R --> C[Classify:<br/>retire · simple · complex] # Step 2 · Choose a target per form Target Best for Notes Out-of-box list form + form formatting Simple list forms with show/hide Fastest; no licences; limited logic Power Apps (customized list form or standalone) Most business forms with rules, lookups, multiple views Standard connectors → M365 licence; premium if Dataverse/SQL Nintex Automation Cloud forms Organizations on Nintex; public forms; DocGen Built-in workflow + documents SPFx form customizer Complex validation, developer-owned, high performance Requires developers & ALM Microsoft Forms + Power Automate Surveys, simple intake (no edit after submit) Very simple, limited data model flowchart TD F{Form complexity} -->|show/hide only| FF[List form + formatting] F -->|rules, lookups, views,<br/>repeating data| PA{Strategy} PA -->|Power Platform| P[Power Apps + child list / Dataverse] PA -->|Nintex| N[NAC form + workflow] F -->|heavy code-behind,<br/>custom UI| S[SPFx form customizer<br/>or Power Apps + API] F -->|one-way survey| MF[Microsoft Forms + flow] # Step 3 · Plan the data List forms : data is already in columns, so the new form points to the same list. Form libraries (XML) : extract data from XML files (promoted and non-promoted fields) into a list or Dataverse table , with repeating tables as child lists . Keep the original XML/PDF for records if required. Extract fields from InfoPath XML files (PnP PowerShell sketch) PowerShell Copy $items = Get-PnPListItem -List \"Purchase Requests\" -PageSize 500 fo"},{"t":"InfoPath to Power Apps – Feature Mapping and a Worked Conversion","c":"infopath-legacy","u":"articles/infopath-legacy/infopath-to-power-apps.html","s":"Map every InfoPath concept to Power Apps (views, rules, repeating tables, data connections, submit, code-behind), then follow a worked conversion of a purchase-request form with line items into a canvas app with SharePoint parent/child lists and a Power Automate approval.","k":"infopath, power apps, migration, mapping, repeating table, canvas app","h":"Concept mapping · Worked example: purchase request with line items · Interview questions · How do you convert an InfoPath repeating table to Power Apps? · What replaces InfoPath views in Power Apps?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Retirement & Migration","b":"# Concept mapping InfoPath Power Apps equivalent Form template (XSN) Canvas app / customized list form Main data source (XML schema) SharePoint list(s) or Dataverse table(s) Views Screens (or containers shown by variables) Switch view rule Navigate() / visibility by locView Validation rule If(...) checks before SubmitForm / Patch , DataCard.Required , Valid Formatting rule (hide/disable) Visible / DisplayMode formulas Action rule (set field value) UpdateContext / Set + control Default , or Patch Form load rule App.OnStart /named formulas/ Screen.OnVisible Default values (userName(), today()) User().Email , Today() in Default Repeating table Gallery + collection saved to a child list Optional section Container with visibility toggle Secondary data connection (list lookup) Data source + Filter / LookUp (delegable) Web service connection Custom connector / flow Submit to library SubmitForm / Patch to list(s) Email submit Power Automate notification Code-behind Power Fx, Power Automate, or an API Print view Power Automate DocGen / Word template → PDF # Worked example: purchase request with line items Target design CANVAS APP Request screen (header) Lines gallery (editable collection) Review & submit screen Patch header, then Patch lines SHAREPOINT LISTS PR Requests (header) PR Lines (child: RequestId lookup) Approvers (config) trigger POWER AUTOMATE Approval: manager → finance (> 50k) Status updates PDF generation Target design Lines gallery: edit a collection, then save header + lines Power Fx Copy // Screen OnVisible (new request) ClearCollect(colLines, { Produ ct: \"\" , Q ty: 1 , UnitPri ce: 0 }); // Add line button Collect(colLines, { Produ ct: \"\" , Q ty: 1 , UnitPri ce: 0 }); // In galle ry: txtProduct.OnChange Patch(colLines, ThisItem, { Produ ct: txtProduct.Text }); // Total label Sum (colLines, Qty * UnitPrice) // Submit button If ( CountRows( Filter (colLines, IsBlank ( Product ) || Qty <= 0 )) > 0 , Notify( \"Every line needs a product and quantity > 0\" , NotificationType.Warning), With( { h dr: Patch(&#x27;PR Requests&#x27;, Defaults(&#x27;PR Requests&#x27;), { Tit le: \"PR-\" & Text ( Now (), \"yyyymmddhhmmss\" ), Departme nt: ddDept.Selected.Value, Tot al: Sum (colLines, Qty * UnitPrice), Stat us: { Val ue: \"Submitted\" } }) }, Patch(&#x27;PR Lines&#x27;, ForAll(colLines As l, { Tit le: l.Product, Q ty: l.Qty, UnitPri ce: l.UnitPrice, Reque st: { Id: hdr.I"},{"t":"Mapping SharePoint Designer & Legacy Workflow Actions to Power Automate","c":"infopath-legacy","u":"articles/infopath-legacy/legacy-workflow-to-power-automate.html","s":"A complete action-by-action mapping from SharePoint 2010 and 2013 workflows (SharePoint Designer) to Power Automate, including conditions, loops, tasks, dictionaries, HTTP calls, impersonation, history logging and workflow status, with patterns for the tricky parts.","k":"sharepoint designer, legacy workflows, power automate, mapping, migration","h":"Triggers · Actions · Tricky patterns · Interview questions · How do you migrate \"Wait for field change in current item\"? · What replaces the App Step or impersonation step?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Retirement & Migration","b":"# Triggers SPD workflow start option Power Automate Start manually For a selected item (instant flow) Start when item created When an item is created Start when item changed When an item is created or modified + trigger conditions Site workflow (manual) Instant flow (manual) or scheduled flow Retention/policy-based start Scheduled flow with OData filter # Actions SharePoint Designer Power Automate Send an Email Send an email (V2) / Send an email notification (V3) Log to History List Create item in a log list / Compose (debug) Set Field in Current Item Update item (current ID) Update List Item / Create List Item / Delete Item Update item / Create item / Delete item Copy Document Copy file Set Workflow Variable Initialize / Set variable Do Calculation Expressions ( add , mul , div ) Add Time to Date addDays() , addHours() Extract Substring substring() Find Interval Between Dates ticks() difference / dateDifference() Pause for Duration / Until Date Delay / Delay until Wait for Field Change in Current Item Separate flow on modify + trigger condition Assign a Task / Start a Task Process Start and wait for an approval (custom responses) Assign a Form to a Group Approvals / Microsoft Forms + adaptive card Set Content Approval Status Set content approval status (SharePoint action) Check In / Check Out / Discard Check Out Check in / Check out / Discard check out Call HTTP Web Service Send an HTTP request to SharePoint / HTTP Build Dictionary / Get Item from Dictionary Compose JSON / Parse JSON / expressions Count Items in Dictionary length() Loop n times / Loop with condition Do until / Apply to each Go to stage Status variable + Switch, or split flows App Step Service account connection Impersonation Step (2010) Service account connection Set Workflow Status Update a Status column Stop Workflow Terminate Start another workflow (2013 → 2010) Run a child flow # Tricky patterns flowchart LR A[\"SPD: 'Wait for field change<br/>Status = Approved'\"] --> B[\"Flow 1 (creation):<br/>set Status = Pending, notify\"] B --> C[\"Flow 2 (modification):<br/>trigger condition Status eq 'Approved'\"] C --> D[Continue process] Long waits : avoid one flow waiting for weeks (30-day limit). Split into event-driven flows. History lists : replace with an audit list or a multi-line \"Process history\" column. Task lists with custom content types : use Approvals (data in Dataverse) and write decisi"},{"t":"Retired & Deprecated SharePoint Features – What Disappeared and What Replaced It","c":"infopath-legacy","u":"articles/infopath-legacy/retired-classic-features.html","s":"A reference of classic SharePoint features that were retired or deprecated between 2014 and 2026, including sandbox code, Access Services, Excel and Visio Services changes, 2010/2013 workflows, InfoPath Forms Services, add-ins and ACS, alerts, classic web parts, and their modern replacements.","k":"retired features, deprecated, classic sharepoint, replacement, roadmap","h":"Interview questions · Which SharePoint Online retirements took effect on 2 April 2026? · What should replace Access Services web apps?","l":"Beginner","x":"Retirement & Migration","b":"Feature Retired / deprecated Replace with Code-based sandboxed solutions (SPO) Deprecated 2014, blocked later SPFx Access Services (web apps) Retired in SPO (2018) Power Apps + Dataverse SharePoint 2010 workflows (SPO) Aug–Nov 2020 Power Automate SharePoint 2013 workflows (SPO) 2 Apr 2026 Power Automate / NAC SharePoint Add-ins (SPO) 2 Apr 2026 SPFx + Entra apps Azure ACS for SPO 2 Apr 2026 Entra app registrations (certs, Sites.Selected) InfoPath Forms Services (SPO) 2026 Power Apps, NAC forms, SPFx, form formatting InfoPath 2013, SharePoint Designer 2013 End of support Jul 2026 Power Apps / Power Automate SharePoint Server 2016 & 2019 End of support 14 Jul 2026 Subscription Edition or SPO SharePoint alerts (SPO) Being retired (announced 2025) List rules, Power Automate Classic publishing / Design Manager Deprecated Modern pages, themes, SPFx Script Editor / Content Editor on modern Not available SPFx web parts Yammer web part / classic conversations Rebranded / replaced Viva Engage web parts Microsoft Stream (Classic) Retired 2024 Stream on SharePoint Classic Search Center customizations Legacy Microsoft Search, PnP Modern Search flowchart LR subgraph Retired R1[Farm & sandbox code] --- R2[Add-ins & ACS] R2 --- R3[2010/2013 workflows] R3 --- R4[InfoPath & SPD] end subgraph Modern M1[SPFx] --- M2[Entra apps + Graph] M2 --- M3[Power Automate / NAC] M3 --- M4[Power Apps / form formatting] end R1 --> M1 R2 --> M2 R3 --> M3 R4 --> M4 Keep a retirement register For each legacy dependency, track sites, owners, replacement design, target date and status. The Microsoft 365 Assessment tool produces most of the inventory automatically. # Interview questions Q1 Which SharePoint Online retirements took effect on 2 April 2026? SharePoint Add-ins (SharePoint-hosted and provider-hosted), Azure ACS-based app authentication for SharePoint Online, and SharePoint 2013 workflows. Q2 What should replace Access Services web apps? Power Apps with Dataverse (model-driven or canvas apps), which provide the data model, forms, security and business logic that Access web apps offered, with modern ALM."},{"t":"SharePoint Migration Strategy – Assess, Plan, Migrate, Validate, Adopt","c":"migration","u":"articles/migration/migration-strategy.html","s":"A complete migration framework for SharePoint and file content, covering goals and scope, lift-and-shift vs restructure, wave planning, tool selection, identity and permissions, customizations, communication, cut-over models and success metrics, with a phase diagram and RACI.","k":"migration, strategy, planning, waves, cutover, sharepoint online","h":"The five phases · Key decisions · Wave planning · RACI (simplified) · Success metrics · Interview questions · What is the difference between lift-and-shift and restructuring in a SharePoint migration? · Why migrate in waves?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Strategy & Assessment","b":"A migration is a business change project with a technical core . Content moves in days. Getting decisions, people and customizations right takes months. This framework works for on-prem → SharePoint Online, version upgrades and tenant-to-tenant moves. # The five phases flowchart LR A[1 · Assess<br/>inventory, risks,<br/>customizations] --> P[2 · Plan<br/>target IA, mappings,<br/>waves, tools] P --> M[3 · Migrate<br/>pilot → waves<br/>→ incremental → cut-over] M --> V[4 · Validate<br/>counts, permissions,<br/>functional UAT] V --> AD[5 · Adopt<br/>training, support,<br/>decommission] V -. issues .-> M # Key decisions Decision Options Guidance Approach Lift-and-shift vs restructure Lift-and-shift for speed; restructure where subsites/IA are broken (flatten into hubs) Scope All content vs active content Archive stale content (> 2–3 years untouched) instead of migrating it Tooling ShareGate, SPMT, Migration Manager, AvePoint, Quest ShareGate for SharePoint-to-SharePoint flexibility; Microsoft tools for file shares/cloud drives Identity Map on-prem accounts → Entra ID Entra Connect sync first; user/group mapping files Customizations Rebuild, replace, retire Farm solutions, workflows, InfoPath, add-ins all need modern equivalents Cut-over Big bang vs waves vs per site Waves by department, with incremental syncs and short freezes # Wave planning Wave-based migration plan WAVE 0 · PILOT 3–5 representative… IT & champions Validate tools timings mappings lessons learned WAVE 1 · SIMPLE Team sites without customizations Document-heavy content then WAVE 2 · MODERATE Lists with workflows/forms Rebuilt flows & Power Apps go live then WAVE 3 · COMPLEX Custom apps large libraries (> 1 M items) Integrations finally DECOMMISSION Old farm read-only Redirects Archive & shut down Wave-based migration plan # RACI (simplified) Activity Migration team Site owners IT security Business sponsor Inventory & assessment R/A C C I Target IA & site mapping R A C C Permissions model R C A I Customization rebuild R/A C C I UAT & sign-off C R I A Communication & training R C I A # Success metrics 100% of in-scope items migrated or consciously excluded (with reports). Permission parity verified for sampled sites. Zero P1 incidents after cut-over, < 2% support tickets per migrated user. Old platform decommissioned on schedule. # Interview questions Q1 What is the difference between lift-and-shi"},{"t":"Pre-Migration Assessment – Inventory Scripts, ShareGate Reports, SMAT and the Microsoft 365 Assessment Tool","c":"migration","u":"articles/migration/pre-migration-assessment.html","s":"What to measure before migrating, covering sites and sizes, item counts, long paths, file types, versions, unique permissions, customizations (WSPs, workflows, InfoPath, add-ins), orphaned users, checked-out files, with PowerShell scripts and tool outputs.","k":"assessment, inventory, smat, sharegate, powershell, migration","h":"What to inventory · Tools · Scripts · Turning findings into decisions · Interview questions · What are the top technical risks you look for during assessment? · What is SMAT?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Strategy & Assessment","b":"You can&#39;t plan what you haven&#39;t measured Assessment surfaces the 10% of content that causes 90% of migration problems: huge lists, long paths, unique permissions, customizations and orphaned users. # What to inventory Cheat sheet Assessment checklist 01 Structure Web apps, site collections, subsites (depth), templates, languages. 02 Volume Size per site/library, item counts, largest lists (> 5k, > 100k, > 1M). 03 Files Path length (> 400 chars), blocked file types, very large files (> 15 GB on-prem / 250 GB SPO), file count per folder. 04 Versions Versions per file, total version storage. 05 Permissions Unique scopes per list, item-level permissions, direct user grants, \"Everyone\" grants. 06 Identity Orphaned/disabled users, groups not synced to Entra ID. 07 Customizations WSPs/features, event receivers, timer jobs, custom master pages, web parts. 08 Workflows & forms 2010/2013 workflows, Nintex, InfoPath (list & library forms). 09 Apps & integrations Add-ins, BCS models, web services, alerts, RSS, search customizations. 10 Activity Last modified/accessed dates to find stale content. # Tools Tool Scope Output SMAT (SharePoint Migration Assessment Tool) SharePoint 2010/2013/2016 farms CSV per risk (long paths, large lists, workflows, InfoPath, customizations) ShareGate Migrate Explore / pre-migration check reports Interactive reports of issues per object, with fix suggestions Microsoft 365 Assessment tool SharePoint Online Add-ins/ACS, workflows, InfoPath, classic pages, with Power BI reports PowerShell (SSOM / PnP) Anything custom Your own CSVs # Scripts On-prem: large lists and unique permissions per list (SSOM) PowerShell Copy Add-PSSnapin Microsoft.SharePoint.PowerShell -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue Get-SPSite -Limit All | ForEach-Object { foreach ( $web in $_ .AllWebs) { foreach ( $list in $web .Lists | Where-Object { -not $_ .Hidden }) { [ pscustomobject ] @ { Web = $web .Url; List = $list .Title; Items = $list .ItemCount UniqueScopes = ( $list .RoleAssignments.Count -gt 0 -and $list .HasUniqueRoleAssignments) ItemLevelUnique = ( $list .Items | Where-Object HasUniqueRoleAssignments | Measure-Object ).Count # slow on huge lists Workflows = $list .WorkflowAssociations.Count LastModified = $list .LastItemModifiedDate } } $web .Dispose() } $_ .Dispose() } | Export-Csv C:\\assess\\lists.csv -NoTypeInformation On-prem: file paths longer than 400 charact"},{"t":"Migration Scenarios Matrix – On-Prem→Online, On-Prem→On-Prem, Online→Online, File Shares & Cloud Drives","c":"migration","u":"articles/migration/migration-scenarios.html","s":"Every common migration scenario side by side, covering recommended tools, identity considerations, what moves and what doesn't, typical blockers and a link to the detailed playbook for each.","k":"migration, scenarios, tenant to tenant, on-premises, file share, google drive","h":"What usually does not migrate automatically · Interview questions · Which tools would you choose for a file share migration vs a SharePoint 2013 migration? · What is special about tenant-to-tenant migrations?","l":"Beginner","x":"Strategy & Assessment","b":"Migration scenarios at a glance SOURCES SharePoint Server 2010–SE SharePoint Online tenant A File shares (NAS) Box / Dropbox / Google Drive Other ECM (Documentum,… tools TOOLS ShareGate Migrate SPMT / Migration Manager Cross-tenant migration… AvePoint / Quest / others Database attach (upgrade) targets TARGETS SharePoint Online SharePoint Server SE OneDrive Teams Migration scenarios at a glance Scenario Primary tools Identity Watch out for Playbook On-prem → SharePoint Online ShareGate, SPMT, Migration Manager (file shares) AD → Entra mapping Customizations, workflows, InfoPath, long paths On-prem to Online On-prem → On-prem (version upgrade) Database attach, ShareGate Same AD Hops (2013 → 2016 → SE), WSP rebuilds On-prem to On-prem Online → Online (tenant to tenant: M&A, divestiture) ShareGate, Microsoft cross-tenant migration Map users between tenants Domain changes, Teams, OneDrive, licences Tenant to tenant File share → SPO Migration Manager, SPMT, ShareGate NTFS ACLs → Entra groups Path length, folder sprawl, permissions File share migration Box / Dropbox / Google Drive → M365 Migration Manager (cloud), ShareGate (some) Map accounts Google Docs conversion, sharing links SPMT & Migration Manager Within a tenant (restructure) ShareGate, PnP PowerShell, site rename/swap Same Subsites to sites, hub associations Classic to modern # What usually does not migrate automatically Farm solutions, master pages, timer jobs, event receivers. SharePoint 2010/2013 workflows (SPO retired them) and Nintex workflows (rebuild). InfoPath forms in SPO (retired) → rebuild. Alerts (recreate or replace with rules), RSS feeds, search configuration. Running workflow instances and task history (archive). # Interview questions Q1 Which tools would you choose for a file share migration vs a SharePoint 2013 migration? For file shares , Microsoft Migration Manager (or SPMT) is usually enough and free, with ShareGate as an alternative for richer mapping. For SharePoint 2013 → SPO , a SharePoint-aware tool such as ShareGate (or SPMT for simpler sites) preserves metadata, versions, permissions, content types and structure. Q2 What is special about tenant-to-tenant migrations? Identities change (users exist in both tenants with different UPNs or domains), so you need user and group mapping, licences in the target, Teams and OneDrive coordination, and often domain moves. Microsoft offers na"},{"t":"Migration Tools Compared – ShareGate vs SPMT vs Migration Manager vs AvePoint vs Quest","c":"migration","u":"articles/migration/migration-tools-comparison.html","s":"An objective comparison of SharePoint migration tools by sources and targets, fidelity (metadata, versions, permissions, workflows), scale (Migration API), automation (PowerShell), reporting, cost and support model, with a selection guide.","k":"migration tools, sharegate, spmt, migration manager, avepoint, quest, comparison","h":"Interview questions · Why do many consultants prefer ShareGate for SharePoint-to-SharePoint migrations? · When are Microsoft's free tools enough?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Strategy & Assessment","b":"Capability ShareGate Migrate SPMT Migration Manager AvePoint FLY Quest (Metalogix/On Demand) Cost Licensed (per user/year) Free Free Licensed Licensed SharePoint 2010–SE → SPO ✅ ✅ (2010/2013/2016) Partial (focus on files) ✅ ✅ File shares → SPO ✅ ✅ ✅ (agents, centralized) ✅ ✅ Box/Dropbox/Google → M365 Limited ❌ ✅ ✅ ✅ Tenant → tenant ✅ ❌ ❌ ✅ ✅ Restructure (copy/merge sites, lists) ✅ very flexible Basic ❌ ✅ ✅ Metadata, versions, authors/dates ✅ ✅ ✅ ✅ ✅ Permissions & mappings (users, templates, content types) ✅ rich Basic Basic ✅ ✅ Incremental passes ✅ ✅ ✅ ✅ ✅ Migration API (Azure) at scale ✅ Insane mode ✅ ✅ ✅ ✅ PowerShell automation ✅ ShareGate module ✅ SPMT module Admin center + PowerShell ✅ ✅ Pre-migration analysis ✅ reports Scan Scan ✅ ✅ Workflows/forms conversion Partial (some workflow migration to Power Automate in newer versions) ❌ ❌ Partial Partial Capabilities change Vendors release frequently. Verify current features (especially workflow and Teams support) against vendor documentation for your version. flowchart TD A{Source?} -->|File shares / cloud drives| MM[Migration Manager<br/>or SPMT] A -->|SharePoint Server, simple lift & shift,<br/>tight budget| SPMT[SPMT] A -->|SharePoint with restructuring,<br/>permissions & mappings, T2T| SG[ShareGate] A -->|Very large enterprise,<br/>multi-workload, managed service| EN[AvePoint / Quest] # Interview questions Q1 Why do many consultants prefer ShareGate for SharePoint-to-SharePoint migrations? It combines an easy UI with powerful restructuring (copy/merge sites, lists, content), rich mappings (users, permissions, templates, content types, metadata), incremental copies, pre-migration reports, Insane mode for speed, and a full PowerShell module for repeatable, scheduled migrations. Q2 When are Microsoft's free tools enough? For file share and cloud-drive migrations (Migration Manager), and for straightforward lift-and-shift of SharePoint Server sites to SPO without restructuring (SPMT), especially when budgets are tight and customizations are limited."},{"t":"ShareGate Migrate – How It Works, Architecture, Licensing and Your First Migration","c":"migration","u":"articles/migration/sharegate-overview.html","s":"ShareGate explained for migration engineers, covering the desktop architecture, connection methods (browser/modern auth, app-only), Explore and reports, Copy (structure, content, permissions), Insane mode via Azure, mappings, incremental copies, logs and PowerShell, with a first-site walkthrough.","k":"sharegate, migration, insane mode, architecture, sharepoint online, tool","h":"Architecture · Core features · Your first site migration (UI) · Interview questions · What is ShareGate Insane mode? · What does an incremental copy do in ShareGate?","l":"Beginner","x":"ShareGate","b":"ShareGate Migrate (often still called \"ShareGate Desktop\") is a Windows application, developed in Montreal, that migrates and reorganizes SharePoint, OneDrive, Teams and file share content. It runs on your machine or a migration VM , reads from the source and writes to the target using the platforms' APIs. # Architecture ShareGate migration architecture MIGRATION WORKSTATION / VM ShareGate Migrate app Local task database & logs PowerShell module reads via (CSOM / REST / SSOM-free) SOURCE SharePoint Server 2010–SE (on-prem) SharePoint Online tenant File shares Exported packages writes via TARGET APIS CSOM / REST (normal mode) SharePoint Migration API (Insane mode) Insane mode stages packages in AZURE STORAGE (MICROSOFT-PROVIDED OR YOURS) Encrypted packages Microsoft import queue lands in TARGET SharePoint Online / OneDrive / Teams SharePoint Server (normal mode) ShareGate migration architecture Mode How When Normal Item-by-item through CSOM/REST Smaller copies, on-prem targets, metadata-heavy fixes Insane mode Packages uploaded to Azure and imported by the SharePoint Migration API Large SPO migrations; much faster and less throttled # Core features Cheat sheet What ShareGate does 01 Explore Browse sources, run reports: size, permissions, workflows, checked-out files, customizations. 02 Pre-check Simulate a copy and list blocking issues before you migrate. 03 Copy structure Sites, lists, libraries, content types, columns, views, permissions, navigation. 04 Copy content Items/files with versions, metadata, authors, created/modified dates. 05 Mappings Users & groups, permission levels, site templates, content types, columns, values. 06 Incremental Copy only new/changed items since last run (cut-over friendly). 07 Permissions & groups Copy, merge, clean up permissions; manage groups. 08 PowerShell Script and schedule everything for repeatable waves. # Your first site migration (UI) flowchart LR C1[Connect source<br/>SP2016 site] --> C2[Connect target<br/>SPO tenant] C2 --> S[Copy structure<br/>site → new site collection] S --> M[Set mappings<br/>users, templates] M --> CC[Copy content<br/>libraries & lists + versions] CC --> R[Review report<br/>warnings / errors] R --> I[Incremental copy<br/>before cut-over] Connect : source site URL (Windows/forms/claims auth) and target tenant (browser sign-in/modern auth, or app-only with a registered app). Copy structure → ch"},{"t":"ShareGate Copy Structure vs Copy Content – Options That Make or Break a Migration","c":"migration","u":"articles/migration/sharegate-copy-structure-content.html","s":"Deep dive into ShareGate copy operations, covering structure copy with merge and subsites, content copy with versions and metadata, preserving authors and dates, folder restructuring, filters, content type and property mappings, and recommended settings per scenario.","k":"sharegate, copy structure, copy content, versions, metadata, mappings","h":"Copy structure options · Copy content options · Interview questions · When would you use Copy content instead of Copy structure? · How do you turn folder structures into metadata during a ShareGate migration?","l":"Intermediate","x":"ShareGate","b":"Copy structure Copy content What Sites, lists, libraries, columns, content types, views, (optionally) content Items, files, folders into existing lists Typical use Recreate a site or list in the target Move documents, merge libraries, restructure folders Granularity Site/list level Item/folder level, with filters Mappings Templates, users, permissions Content types, columns (property mapping), values # Copy structure options Cheat sheet Structure copy settings to decide 01 Target New site collection, existing site (merge), or subsite → site collection (flatten). 02 Merge vs overwrite Merge keeps existing target objects; avoids duplicates on re-runs. 03 Subsites Include or exclude; flatten selected subsites into separate site collections instead. 04 Permissions Copy permissions & groups, or rebuild with clean model in target. 05 Navigation & features Copy navigation, site features (validate on modern). 06 Template mapping Map classic templates (e.g. STS#0) to modern targets. 07 Content Optionally copy content in the same run (or separate content copy for control). # Copy content options Option Recommendation Versions Copy all versions for records-relevant libraries. Otherwise limit (e.g. last 10) to save time and storage Preserve authorship & timestamps Yes (needs user mapping) Content type mapping Map legacy content types to new ones; map columns with property mapping Filters Exclude old items (Modified < 2019), file types, checked-out files Folder restructuring Use property-based folders or flatten folders into metadata Incremental Use for delta passes flowchart LR subgraph Source L1[Shared Documents<br/>/Contracts/2024/VendorA/*.pdf] end subgraph Target L2[Contracts library<br/>flat, with columns:<br/>Year, Vendor, Type] end L1 -->|Copy content +<br/>property mapping:<br/>folder path → Year, Vendor| L2 Re-runs and duplicates Use merge for structure and incremental update / skip existing for content so re-running a task never creates duplicates. Always test a re-run in the pilot. # Interview questions Q1 When would you use Copy content instead of Copy structure? When the target list or library already exists (created by provisioning or a structure copy), and you need fine control: filtering items, restructuring folders, mapping content types and properties, merging several sources into one target, or delta passes. Q2 How do you turn folder structures into m"},{"t":"ShareGate Mappings – Users & Groups, Permission Levels, Site Templates, Content Types and Properties","c":"migration","u":"articles/migration/sharegate-mappings.html","s":"Get identities and metadata right with ShareGate mappings, covering automatic vs explicit user mapping, mapping files (.sgum/.sgpm), departed users, AD to Entra ID claims, permission level mapping, template and content type mapping, and property and value mapping.","k":"sharegate, mappings, user mapping, permissions, content types, property mapping","h":"Users and groups · Content types and properties · Interview questions · How do you handle users who left the company during a migration? · Why map content types instead of copying them as-is?","l":"Advanced","x":"ShareGate","b":"Mappings tell ShareGate how source objects translate to target objects. Most post-migration complaints (\"Created By shows System Account\", \"my permissions are missing\", \"the Category column is empty\") are mapping problems . ShareGate mapping types USERS & GROUPS Auto-match by email/UPN Explicit mapping file Departed users → placeholder AD groups → Entra / M365 groups plus PERMISSION LEVELS Custom levels → standard ones e.g. \"Approve\" → \"Edit\" plus SITE TEMPLATES Classic templates → modern (STS#0 → STS#3 / group site) plus CONTENT TYPES & PROPERTIES Old CT → new CT Column → column Value → value (choices, terms) ShareGate mapping types # Users and groups Situation Mapping approach Same users synced to Entra ID Automatic (by email/UPN) Domain change (contoso.local → contoso.com, mergers) Explicit user mapping file (CSV/Excel import) Departed users Map to a placeholder account (e.g. former.employee@contoso.com ) to keep Created/Modified meaningful AD security groups Map to Entra security groups (synced) or Microsoft 365 groups SharePoint groups Recreated in target (structure copy) ShareGate PowerShell: user mapping from CSV PowerShell Copy Import-Module Sharegate $map = New-MappingSettings Import-Csv .\\user -map .csv | ForEach-Object { Set-UserAndGroupMapping -MappingSettings $map -Source $_ .SourceAccount -Destination $_ .TargetUpn | Out-Null } Export-UserAndGroupMapping -MappingSettings $map -Path .\\mappings\\users.sgum # Content types and properties Example mapping plan Text Copy Source CT \"Contoso Document\" → Target CT \"Document\" Source CT \"Contract 2013\" → Target CT \"Contract\" Column \"Vendor Name\" (text) → \"Vendor\" (lookup to Vendors list by Title) Column \"Dept\" (choice: FIN, HR) → \"Department\" (managed metadata: Finance, Human Resources) Value mapping: FIN → Finance, HR → Human Resources Managed metadata must exist first Copy or create the term store (with term IDs where possible) before content, otherwise managed metadata values can't resolve. # Interview questions Q1 How do you handle users who left the company during a migration? Map them explicitly to a placeholder account (or a designated owner) so authorship metadata and permissions don't fail or fall back to the migration account. Optionally record the original name in a text column for audit. Q2 Why map content types instead of copying them as-is? To modernize the information architecture: consolida"},{"t":"ShareGate PowerShell – Scripted, Repeatable Migrations (Copy-Site, Copy-List, Copy-Content, Reports)","c":"migration","u":"articles/migration/sharegate-powershell.html","s":"Automate migrations with the ShareGate PowerShell module, covering connecting to source and target, copy settings and mappings, site, list and content copies, Insane mode, incremental runs from a CSV wave plan, exporting reports, and running parallel batches.","k":"sharegate, powershell, automation, copy-site, copy-content, incremental, waves","h":"Building blocks · A wave script driven by CSV · Copying a single library with restructuring · Interview questions · How do you make ShareGate re-runs safe for a delta (incremental) pass? · How do you scale a ShareGate migration to hundreds of sites?","l":"Advanced","x":"ShareGate","b":"The UI is perfect for pilots. For hundreds of sites you need scripts : repeatable, logged and schedulable. ShareGate ships a PowerShell module that exposes the same engine. # Building blocks Cmdlet Purpose Connect-Site Connect to a source or target site ( -Browser , -UseCredentialsFrom , -Credential ) Get-Site , Get-List Enumerate objects New-CopySettings Behaviour on existing items ( -OnContentItemExists IncrementalUpdate / Skip / Overwrite ) New-MappingSettings , Set-UserAndGroupMapping , Import-UserAndGroupMapping Identity mappings Copy-Site Structure (+content) copy, -Merge , -Subsites , -InsaneMode Copy-List One list/library, with options to exclude workflows or customized forms Copy-Content Items/files into existing lists, filters, templates, property mapping Copy-ObjectPermissions Copy permissions between objects Export-Report Save the migration report (xlsx) # A wave script driven by CSV Run-Wave.ps1 PowerShell Copy param ([ string ] $WaveFile = \".\\wave-03.csv\" , [ switch ] $Incremental ) Import-Module Sharegate $ErrorActionPreference = \"Stop\" $log = \".\\logs\\wave- $ (Get-Date -f yyyyMMdd-HHmm)\" New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force $log | Out-Null # Mappings shared by all sites in the wave $mapping = New-MappingSettings $mapping = Import-UserAndGroupMapping -MappingSettings $mapping -Path \".\\mappings\\users.sgum\" # Incremental re-runs only bring new/changed items $copySettings = if ( $Incremental ) { New-CopySettings -OnContentItemExists IncrementalUpdate } else { New-CopySettings -OnContentItemExists Skip } # One connection per tenant, reused (modern auth via browser the first time) $tenantConnection = Connect-Site -Url \"https://contoso-admin.sharepoint.com\" -Browser Import-Csv $WaveFile | ForEach-Object { $row = $_ try { Write-Host \"▶ $ ( $row .SourceUrl) → $ ( $row .TargetUrl)\" -ForegroundColor Cyan $src = Connect-Site -Url $row .SourceUrl -UseCredentialsFrom $row .SourceAuthSite # or -Credential (Get-Credential) $dst = Connect-Site -Url $row .TargetUrl -UseCredentialsFrom $tenantConnection $result = Copy-Site -Site $src -DestinationSite $dst -Merge -Subsites : $ ([ bool ]::Parse( $row .IncludeSubsites)) ` -MappingSettings $mapping -CopySettings $copySettings -InsaneMode Export-Report $result -Path \" $log \\ $ ( $row .Name).xlsx\" | Out-Null \"{0},{1},{2},{3}\" -f $row .Name, $result .Result, $result .Errors, $result .Warnings | Add-Content \" $log \\summa"},{"t":"Incremental Migration & Cut-Over Planning – Minimizing Downtime with Delta Passes","c":"migration","u":"articles/migration/sharegate-incremental-cutover.html","s":"Plan the move from source to target with minimal disruption, covering the bulk + delta + final pass model, read-only freezes, DNS and link redirection, communication, hypercare, rollback, and a timeline template for a weekend cut-over.","k":"cutover, incremental, migration, downtime, sharegate, planning","h":"The three-pass model · Weekend cut-over timeline (example) · Redirecting old links · Rollback plan · Interview questions · How do you migrate a busy site with minimal downtime? · Why set the source read-only instead of just telling users to stop?","l":"Advanced","x":"ShareGate","b":"# The three-pass model flowchart LR B[\"Pass 1 · Bulk copy<br/>weeks before<br/>(users keep working on source)\"] --> D[\"Pass 2..n · Delta copies<br/>nightly/weekly<br/>(only changes)\"] D --> F[\"Final pass · Freeze<br/>source read-only →<br/>last delta (hours)\"] F --> G[\"Go-live<br/>redirects, comms,<br/>hypercare\"] Pass Duration Users Bulk Days–weeks (large volume) Working in source Delta(s) Minutes–hours Working in source Final Short window (evening/weekend) Source read-only Go-live Instant switch Working in target # Weekend cut-over timeline (example) Fri 17:00 Freeze Set source sites read-only ( Set-SPSite -LockState ReadOnly on-prem / Set-SPOSite -LockState ReadOnly ). Banner on source pages. Fri 18:00 Final delta Run incremental script for wave sites; export reports. Sat 09:00 Validation Counts, spot checks, permissions sampling, key business tests by champions. Sat 14:00 Switch Update navigation, DNS/redirects (old URLs → new), Teams tabs, bookmarks communication. Sun Buffer Fix issues; rerun targeted copies. Mon 08:00 Go-live + hypercare Floor walkers, Teams support channel, daily triage for 2 weeks. # Redirecting old links On-prem: an IIS URL Rewrite rule or a simple redirect page on the old web application ( /sites/finance/* → new URL). In SPO tenant moves: site rename creates automatic redirects. For other moves, use redirect pages or link mapping lists. Fix hard-coded links in documents (Office files) with link-fix tools, or accept a redirect layer. # Rollback plan Keep the source read-only but available until sign-off. If a blocker appears, unlock the source and communicate. That is why you don't decommission immediately. # Interview questions Q1 How do you migrate a busy site with minimal downtime? Do a bulk copy while users continue working, run incremental delta passes to keep the target close, then freeze the source (read-only) for a short final delta and validation window, switch users with redirects and communication, and keep the source read-only as a fallback. Q2 Why set the source read-only instead of just telling users to stop? Read-only guarantees no new changes are made after the final pass, which would otherwise be lost or create conflicts, while still letting people read content during the cut-over."},{"t":"ShareGate Troubleshooting – Common Errors, Warnings and How to Fix Them","c":"migration","u":"articles/migration/sharegate-troubleshooting.html","s":"A practical catalogue of ShareGate migration issues, covering throttling, long paths, blocked files, unresolved users, missing content types or terms, version limits, checked-out files, large files, Insane mode failures, authentication problems and performance tuning.","k":"sharegate, troubleshooting, errors, throttling, migration, long paths","h":"Performance tuning checklist · Interview questions · What are the most common causes of ShareGate migration errors to SharePoint Online? · How do you deal with throttling during large migrations?","l":"Intermediate","x":"ShareGate","b":"Issue in report Cause Fix Throttled / 429 / slow copies SharePoint Online throttling (too many requests from one account/app) Use Insane mode , run off-peak, spread across accounts/apps, reduce parallel sessions Path too long Decoded URL > 400 characters Shorten folder names, flatten folders, map to a shorter library/site URL Blocked file type / invalid characters .exe / .dll policies, characters like \"*:<>?/| (older targets) Rename via pre-migration script, or exclude User not found Departed user, unsynced account, domain change Add user mapping (placeholder), sync accounts Content type / column missing Target lacks schema Copy structure first, or create schema via provisioning; map content types Managed metadata term not found Term store not migrated / different IDs Migrate term store with IDs before content File checked out Checked-out files have no published version for others Check in on source (or migrate with \"include checked-out\" options where possible) File too large Above target limit (SPO 250 GB) or slow uploads Exclude, archive elsewhere, or move manually Version count reduced Target library version limit lower Raise version limit temporarily or accept the trimmed history Insane mode error (packages) Azure upload issues, proxy/firewall, unsupported objects Check connectivity to Azure endpoints; fallback to normal mode for problematic items Access denied on target Migration account not site collection admin Add the account as SCA on target sites (and source) Workflows / InfoPath warnings Not migratable to SPO Rebuild (Power Automate, Power Apps, NAC) Read the report, not just the summary A \"Completed with warnings\" summary can hide thousands of skipped items. Filter the Excel report by Errors , then Warnings , group by message, and fix root causes in bulk before the next pass. # Performance tuning checklist Migration VM near the source, 8+ vCPU, 32 GB RAM, SSD, stable bandwidth to Microsoft 365. Exclude ShareGate folders from antivirus real-time scanning (per vendor guidance). Limit versions where business allows. Run large libraries alone, with small sites in parallel. Avoid business hours for SPO targets (throttling is stricter at peak). # Interview questions Q1 What are the most common causes of ShareGate migration errors to SharePoint Online? Throttling, long paths, unresolved users (departed or unsynced), missing schema (content types, column"},{"t":"SharePoint Migration Tool (SPMT) & Migration Manager – Microsoft's Free Migration Tools","c":"migration","u":"articles/migration/spmt-migration-manager.html","s":"When and how to use SPMT (desktop, SharePoint Server and file shares) and Migration Manager (centralized agents for file shares, Box, Dropbox, Google), with scanning, bulk CSV/JSON tasks, PowerShell, user mapping, incremental runs and limitations.","k":"spmt, migration manager, microsoft tools, file share, google drive, box","h":"SPMT bulk migration with PowerShell · Migration Manager workflow (file shares) · Interview questions · What's the difference between SPMT and Migration Manager? · Do Microsoft's tools use the Migration API?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Microsoft Tools","b":"Microsoft migration tooling SPMT (DESKTOP) Sources: SharePoint Server 2010/2013/2016 file shares Runs on one machine PowerShell module uses SHAREPOINT MIGRATION API Azure-backed import Fast less throttling uses MIGRATION MANAGER (ADMIN CENTER) Centralized in SharePoint admi… Agents on multiple server… Cloud connectors: Box Dropbox Google Drive Egnyte Scans bulk tasks reports lands in TARGETS SharePoint Online OneDrive Teams Microsoft migration tooling SPMT Migration Manager Interface Desktop app + PowerShell SharePoint admin center (web) Sources SharePoint Server, file shares File shares (agents), Box, Dropbox, Google Drive, Egnyte Scale One machine (multiple instances possible) Many agents, central queue Scans & reports Basic Pre-scan, dashboards Best for Straightforward SharePoint Server → SPO Large file share and cloud-drive programs # SPMT bulk migration with PowerShell SPMT PowerShell PowerShell Copy Import-Module Microsoft.SharePoint.MigrationTool.PowerShell $cred = Get-Credential # target M365 admin (or use modern auth per docs) Register-SPMTMigration -SPOCredential $cred -Force -MigrateFileVersionHistory $true -KeepAllVersions $false -MigrateAllSiteFieldsAndContentTypes $true # Add tasks from JSON/CSV or one by one Add-SPMTTask -FileShareSource \"\\\\fs01\\finance\\Contracts\" -TargetSiteUrl \"https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/finance\" -TargetList \"Contracts\" Add-SPMTTask -SharePointSourceCredential ( Get-Credential ) -SharePointSourceSiteUrl \"https://sp2013.contoso.local/sites/hr\" ` -TargetSiteUrl \"https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/hr\" -MigrateAll Start-SPMTMigration Get-SPMTMigration | Select-Object Status, ReportFolderPath # Migration Manager workflow (file shares) Admin center → Migration → File shares → download and install agents on servers that can read the shares. Scan sources (paths, sizes, issues). Upload a CSV of tasks (source path, target site, library, folder). Configure settings (versions, permissions preservation, filters). Run, monitor and re-run for incremental passes; download reports. Permissions from NTFS When preserving file share permissions, NTFS ACLs map to Entra identities. Clean up ACL sprawl first. Migrating chaotic folder permissions as-is recreates the same chaos with item-level permissions in SharePoint. # Interview questions Q1 What's the difference between SPMT and Migration Manager? SPMT is a desktop tool (with PowerSh"},{"t":"SharePoint 2013/2016/2019 to SharePoint Online – Step-by-Step Migration Playbook","c":"migration","u":"articles/migration/onprem-to-online.html","s":"An end-to-end playbook for moving on-premises SharePoint to Microsoft 365, covering identity sync, target architecture, assessment, customizations rebuild, pilot, ShareGate waves, permissions, navigation, search, cut-over, validation and decommissioning, with timeline and checklists.","k":"migration, sharepoint online, on-premises, playbook, sharegate, entra connect","h":"Target architecture first · Phase checklist · Typical customization mapping · Timeline example (≈ 2 TB, 400 sites) · Interview questions · What must be in place before you migrate content to SharePoint Online? · How do you handle subsites when moving to SharePoint Online?","l":"Advanced","x":"Scenarios","b":"This is the most common migration in the SharePoint world. It's also the one I've run end to end on real projects: SharePoint 2013/2016 farms with documents, lists, InfoPath forms and Nintex/SPD workflows moving to SharePoint Online. # Target architecture first From farm to tenant SOURCE: SHAREPOINT 2013/2016 FARM Web apps & path-based site… Deep subsites Farm solutions SPD & Nintex workflows InfoPath forms assess, rationalize, map MAPPING Site collection → site Large subsites → own sites (flatten) Department sites → hubs Customizations → modern equivalents ShareGate waves TARGET: SHAREPOINT ONLINE Hub sites per department Modern team/communicat… Power Automate / NAC workflows Power Apps forms SPFx web parts From farm to tenant # Phase checklist Cheat sheet On-prem → SPO playbook 01 Identity Entra Connect syncing all users/groups; UPNs routable; user mapping file for exceptions. 02 Inventory SMAT + ShareGate reports: sizes, customizations, workflows, InfoPath, long paths, permissions. 03 Target IA Site mapping sheet (source URL → target URL, template, hub, owners). 04 Customizations Decide per item: retire / OOTB / Power Platform / SPFx / NAC; build before content waves. 05 Pilot 3–5 sites incl. one complex; measure throughput; refine mappings & scripts. 06 Waves Bulk → deltas → freeze → final; communications 2 weeks / 2 days / day-of. 07 Validation Counts, versions, metadata, permissions sampling, functional tests by owners. 08 Cut-over Read-only source, redirects, update Teams/links, hypercare. 09 Decommission Keep read-only N months, archive DBs, shut down farm. # Typical customization mapping On-prem SharePoint Online Custom master page & branding Modern theme + SPFx application customizer Visual web parts SPFx web parts SPD 2010/2013 workflows Power Automate Nintex Workflow (on-prem) NAC or Power Automate InfoPath forms Power Apps / form formatting / NAC forms Event receivers / timer jobs Webhooks + Azure Functions / scheduled flows BCS external lists Power Apps connectors / SPFx + API / Graph connectors Search center customizations Microsoft Search + PnP Modern Search Publishing sites Communication sites + page templates # Timeline example (≈ 2 TB, 400 sites) flowchart LR W1[\"Weeks 1–4<br/>Assessment & design\"] --> W2[\"Weeks 5–10<br/>Build: hubs, provisioning,<br/>flows, apps, SPFx\"] W2 --> W3[\"Weeks 9–10<br/>Pilot wave\"] W3 --> W4[\"Weeks 11–20<br/>Wav"},{"t":"On-Prem to On-Prem Migrations – Database Attach Chains vs Tool-Based Copies (to Subscription Edition)","c":"migration","u":"articles/migration/onprem-to-onprem.html","s":"Moving between SharePoint Server versions or farms, covering database-attach hops (2010→2013→2016→SE), when a migration tool is better, consolidating farms, moving between domains, rebuilding customizations for SE and a decision matrix.","k":"migration, subscription edition, database attach, sharegate, farm consolidation, upgrade","h":"Typical plan to SE · Interview questions · When would you use a migration tool instead of database attach for an on-prem upgrade? · What is the supported native path from SharePoint 2010 to Subscription Edition?","l":"Advanced","x":"Scenarios","b":"flowchart TD A{Source version} -->|2016 / 2019| DB1[Direct DB attach → SE<br/>fast, full fidelity] A -->|2013| C{Need restructuring<br/>or cleanup?} A -->|2010| D[2010 → 2013 → 2016 → SE<br/>or tool-based copy] C -->|no| DB2[\"2013 → 2016 (temp farm) → SE\"] C -->|yes / skip hops| T[ShareGate / AvePoint<br/>2013 → SE directly] Database attach Tool-based copy (ShareGate etc.) Fidelity Exact (IDs, workflows state, everything) High, but object-level (IDs may change) Version hops One major version at a time (2016 → SE direct) Any source → any target Restructure/clean-up ❌ (as-is) ✅ Customizations Must be redeployed/compatible Must be redeployed/compatible Speed Very fast for large DBs Slower, item by item Domain/identity change Needs Move-SPUser / claims migration Mapping during copy Farm consolidation Possible (attach DBs from several farms) Easy, with merges # Typical plan to SE Build the SE farm (MinRole, Windows Server 2022+, SQL 2019/2022). Rebuild and test WSPs for SE (.NET 4.8), and replace deprecated features. Restore service application DBs (MMS, Secure Store, UPS) and create service apps from them. Test-SPContentDatabase , then mount content DBs (or ShareGate copy for restructured parts). Re-register workflows: SharePoint Workflow Manager for 2013-style workflows. Nintex upgrades per Nintex guidance. Configure authentication (Windows or OIDC ), certificates (SE cert management), search crawl, and DNS cut-over. See Upgrade paths for the DB-attach details. # Interview questions Q1 When would you use a migration tool instead of database attach for an on-prem upgrade? When you need to skip version hops (2013 → SE directly), restructure sites, clean up or consolidate content, migrate across domains with mapping, or move only part of a database's content. Database attach is best for fast, full-fidelity, as-is upgrades. Q2 What is the supported native path from SharePoint 2010 to Subscription Edition? 2010 → 2013 → 2016 → SE via database attach (three hops, each requiring a temporary farm), or a third-party tool to copy directly."},{"t":"Tenant-to-Tenant (Cross-Tenant) Migrations – Mergers, Divestitures and Rebranding","c":"migration","u":"articles/migration/tenant-to-tenant.html","s":"Plan Microsoft 365 tenant-to-tenant moves for SharePoint, OneDrive and Teams, covering identity mapping, Microsoft's cross-tenant SharePoint and OneDrive migration, third-party tools, domain moves, Teams and Planner, sharing links, licences and a phased runbook.","k":"tenant to tenant, cross-tenant migration, mergers, onedrive, teams, identity mapping","h":"Microsoft's native cross-tenant migration · What needs extra care · Interview questions · What's the hardest part of a tenant-to-tenant migration? · How does Microsoft's native cross-tenant SharePoint migration preserve links?","l":"Advanced","x":"Scenarios","b":"Mergers, acquisitions, divestitures and rebrands force content to move between tenants . The hard part is not the files but identity : the same person has different accounts (and domains) in source and target. Tenant-to-tenant migration SOURCE TENANT (CONTOSO.COM) SharePoint sites OneDrive accounts Teams (channels, files, chats*) M365 groups trust + identity mapping MAPPING User map: jane@contoso.com → jane@fabrikam.com Group map: source groups → target groups Site map: URLs tools MIGRATION TOOLS Microsoft cross-tenant SharePoint & OneDrive… ShareGate / AvePoint / Quest (Teams, chats, Planner) lands in TARGET TENANT (FABRIKAM.COM) Sites OneDrives Teams Redirects from source Tenant-to-tenant migration # Microsoft's native cross-tenant migration Outline (SharePoint Online Management Shell, both tenants) PowerShell Copy # Establish trust (run in source and target with the partner&#x27;s host URL) Set-SPOCrossTenantRelationship -Scenario MnA -PartnerRole Target -PartnerCrossTenantHostUrl \"https://fabrikam-my.sharepoint.com\" # in source Set-SPOCrossTenantRelationship -Scenario MnA -PartnerRole Source -PartnerCrossTenantHostUrl \"https://contoso-my.sharepoint.com\" # in target # Upload identity mapping CSV in the target, then verify compatibility Set-SPOCrossTenantIdentityMapping -Path .\\identity -map .csv # (command names per current docs) # Start a site move (source tenant) Start-SPOCrossTenantSiteContentMove -SourceSiteUrl https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/finance ` -TargetSiteUrl https://fabrikam.sharepoint.com/sites/contoso -finance -TargetCrossTenantHostUrl \"https://fabrikam-my.sharepoint.com\" # OneDrive move Start-SPOCrossTenantUserContentMove -SourceUserPrincipalName jane@contoso.com -TargetUserPrincipalName jane@fabrikam.com ` -TargetCrossTenantHostUrl \"https://fabrikam-my.sharepoint.com\" Check prerequisites and licensing Native cross-tenant migration requires specific licences (cross-tenant user data migration add-ons) and has prerequisites (trust, identity mapping, no blocking policies). Cmdlet names and parameters evolve, so follow Microsoft's current guide. # What needs extra care Item Consideration Teams Channels, tabs, chats, meeting recordings → third-party tools; recreate teams and map members Sharing links Links to old tenant break → re-share or rely on redirects (native move creates redirects) Domain move Verified domains move only when removed"},{"t":"File Share to SharePoint Online – Clean-Up, Permissions, Metadata and Where Files Should Land","c":"migration","u":"articles/migration/file-share-migration.html","s":"Migrate network drives without recreating the mess, covering ROT analysis (redundant, obsolete, trivial), mapping shares to Teams, sites and OneDrive, NTFS permission simplification, path and character limits, sync client considerations and tools.","k":"file share, migration, permissions, onedrive, teams, migration manager","h":"Where should each folder go? · Permission simplification · Limits to check · Interview questions · How do you decide whether file share content goes to OneDrive, Teams or SharePoint sites? · Why shouldn't you copy NTFS permissions one-to-one?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Scenarios","b":"flowchart LR FS[(\"File share: fs01/departments<br/>4 TB, 3M files,<br/>15 years of folders\")] --> ROT{ROT analysis} ROT -->|Obsolete > 5 yrs| AR[Archive storage<br/>or delete per policy] ROT -->|Personal folders| OD[OneDrive] ROT -->|Team working files| T[Teams channels /<br/>team sites] ROT -->|Published / reference| C[Communication sites<br/>with metadata] # Where should each folder go? Content Target Personal home drives (H:) OneDrive of the user Team working folders Teams (team site libraries, channel folders) Department reference material Communication site libraries with metadata Old project archives Archive site (read-only) or cold storage, or delete per retention Application data (exports, logs) Keep on file servers / Azure Files, not SharePoint # Permission simplification File shares usually have thousands of unique ACLs . Recreating them in SharePoint creates item-level permission chaos. Map share-level groups to site-level access (owners/members/visitors). Break inheritance only at library or top-level folder , never per file. Replace user-specific ACLs with groups . # Limits to check Limit Value / note Path length (decoded URL) 400 characters Invalid characters (older clients) `\" * : < > ? / \\ Blocked names .lock , CON , PRN , AUX , _vti_ , desktop.ini patterns File size 250 GB Sync Large libraries (> 300k files) sync poorly, so use shortcuts selectively Don&#39;t migrate a mess Run a ROT analysis (reports by last modified date, duplicates, file types). Typically 30–50% of file share data does not need to move, and migrating less is the best performance optimization. # Interview questions Q1 How do you decide whether file share content goes to OneDrive, Teams or SharePoint sites? Personal files go to OneDrive . Collaborative team work goes to Teams-connected sites . Curated, broadly shared reference content goes to communication sites with metadata. Stale content is archived or deleted per retention policy. Q2 Why shouldn't you copy NTFS permissions one-to-one? File shares often have deeply nested, file-level ACLs. In SharePoint that becomes thousands of unique permission scopes, which are slow, hard to manage and confusing for users. Simplify to site and library-level access with groups."},{"t":"Migrating Workflows During a SharePoint Migration – SPD, Nintex and Visual Studio Workflows","c":"migration","u":"articles/migration/workflow-migration.html","s":"Strategy for workflows when moving to SharePoint Online or SE, covering inventory, triage, choosing Power Automate or NAC, rebuilding before content cut-over, handling running instances and history, testing, and the most common pitfalls.","k":"migration, workflows, power automate, nintex, sharepoint designer, history","h":"Sequencing with content waves · History and audit · Interview questions · Why must new flows be off during content migration? · How do you preserve workflow history when migrating?","l":"Advanced","x":"Workflows, Forms & Customizations","b":"Content tools copy workflow definitions at best. In SharePoint Online, 2010/2013 workflows cannot run (retired), and Nintex on-prem workflows never ran there. Workflows are always a rebuild project running in parallel with content migration. flowchart LR I[Inventory<br/>SPD 2010/2013, Nintex,<br/>VS workflows, receivers] --> T{Triage} T -->|unused| R[Retire] T -->|simple notify| L[List rules /<br/>simple flows] T -->|approvals & logic| P[Power Automate] T -->|Nintex estate,<br/>forms+DocGen| N[NAC] T -->|complex/integration| A[Azure Functions /<br/>Logic Apps] P & N & A --> B[Build & test<br/>against TARGET lists] B --> C[Activate at content<br/>cut-over] # Sequencing with content waves Rebuild workflows against the target site schema (created by the structure copy or provisioning). Keep new flows turned off during bulk and delta passes (otherwise every migrated item triggers them). At cut-over: final delta → turn flows on → smoke test → go-live. For in-flight business items, set status columns so new flows pick up from the right stage. Migration storms Automated triggers (created or modified) fire for every migrated item if flows are on during copying, generating thousands of emails and approvals. Always disable, or use trigger conditions that exclude the migration account. # History and audit Export SPD/Nintex workflow history and task lists to Excel/CSV or an archive list before decommissioning. Keep the approval outcome columns on items (Approver, Date, Outcome) so business history survives. # Interview questions Q1 Why must new flows be off during content migration? Because \"item created/modified\" triggers fire for every item written by the migration tool, which floods users with notifications, starts thousands of approvals and consumes flow quotas. Enable flows only after the final pass, or exclude the migration account with trigger conditions. Q2 How do you preserve workflow history when migrating? Workflow history and tasks don't migrate as running instances. Export history and task lists (or copy them as plain lists) to an archive, and keep key decisions as columns on the business items."},{"t":"Classic to Modern Transformation – Pages, Sites, Navigation and Customizations in SPO","c":"migration","u":"articles/migration/classic-to-modern.html","s":"Modernize classic SharePoint Online (and migrated) sites, covering the Microsoft 365 Assessment tool, page transformation (wiki, web part, publishing pages), web part mapping, groupify, hub association, root site swap, and fixing classic customizations.","k":"classic to modern, page transformation, modernization, pnp, groupify, hub sites","h":"Interview questions · What does PnP page transformation do? · What can't be transformed automatically?","l":"Advanced","x":"Workflows, Forms & Customizations","b":"Migrating classic sites \"as-is\" gives you classic sites in the cloud. Modernization turns them into modern experiences that work with hubs, Viva, mobile and Copilot. Modernization pipeline ASSESS Microsoft 365 Assessment tool Classic pages web parts customizations per site transform PAGES Wiki & web part pages → modern site pages Publishing pages → modern pages (layout mapping) Web part mapping file modernize site SITE Groupify (connect M365 group) Associate to hub Modern theme & navigation Disable classic features replace CUSTOMIZATIONS Script Editor → SPFx Custom actions → command sets JSLink → formatting JSON Master page → app customizer Modernization pipeline Transform pages in a site PowerShell Copy Connect-PnPOnline -Url https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/legacy -hr -Interactive -ClientId $clientId # Wiki and web part pages in Site Pages Get-PnPListItem -List \"Site Pages\" | ForEach-Object { $name = $_ .FieldValues[ \"FileLeafRef\" ] ConvertTo-PnPPage -Identity $name -Overwrite -TakeSourcePageName -CopyPageMetadata -AddPageAcceptBanner } # Publishing pages into a target modern site ConvertTo-PnPPage -Identity \"Policies.aspx\" -PublishingPage -TargetWebUrl https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/hr -Overwrite Classic web part Modern mapping Content Editor (HTML) Text web part (or SPFx for scripts) Content Query / Content Search Highlighted content List View (XSLT) List / Document library web part Image viewer Image web part Script Editor No equivalent , so build SPFx Summary links Quick links # Interview questions Q1 What does PnP page transformation do? It reads a classic page (wiki, web part or publishing), maps its web parts to modern equivalents using a mapping file, recreates the content as a modern client-side page (optionally in another site), and can keep the page name and metadata. Q2 What can't be transformed automatically? Script Editor and other custom script web parts, custom classic web parts without modern equivalents, and master page or JSLink customizations. These need SPFx, formatting JSON or a redesign."},{"t":"Migration Performance & Throttling – Throughput Planning, the Migration API and Decorated Traffic","c":"migration","u":"articles/migration/throttling-performance.html","s":"Estimate and maximize migration throughput, covering how SharePoint Online throttles, the Azure-backed Migration API, realistic GB-per-hour numbers, parallelism, off-peak windows, decorating traffic, versions and file count effects, and a capacity planning worksheet.","k":"migration, throttling, performance, migration api, throughput, planning","h":"What affects speed · Capacity planning worksheet · Throttling hygiene · Interview questions · Why is migrating 1 TB of small files slower than 1 TB of large files? · What is the SharePoint Migration API?","l":"Advanced","x":"Validation & Operations","b":"Two ways content reaches SharePoint Online CSOM / REST (ITEM BY ITEM) One request per item/file/property Subject to per-user & per-app throttling Slower simple vs MIGRATION API (BULK IMPORT) Tool builds packages Upload to Azure Blob (encrypted) Import job queue in SPO Much higher throughput Two ways content reaches SharePoint Online # What affects speed Factor Effect File count vs size Many small files are much slower per GB than few large files Versions Each version is a file, so 10 versions ≈ 10× the work Metadata & permissions Unique permissions and rich metadata add processing Time of day Off-peak (nights, weekends in the tenant region) is faster Network Upload bandwidth and latency from the migration VM Parallelism More jobs help until throttling kicks in Planning numbers Microsoft's guidance gives indicative ranges (from a few hundred GB per day for small-file content to several TB per day for large-file content via the Migration API). Measure your pilot throughput and extrapolate rather than relying on published numbers. # Capacity planning worksheet Input Example In-scope data (after ROT cleanup) 2.4 TB Average file size 1.8 MB (≈ 1.4M files) Versions migrated Last 5 Pilot throughput (measured) 45 GB/hour (Insane mode, off-peak, 4 parallel jobs) Hours available per week (off-peak) 60 Bulk duration 2,400 / 45 ≈ 53 hours ≈ 1 week Delta passes ~5% of bulk each # Throttling hygiene Use tools that honour Retry-After and back off automatically. Decorate traffic with a user agent identifying the app (tools do this), so Microsoft can classify traffic. Avoid running heavy scripts and migrations under the same account simultaneously. Prefer app-only identities registered for migration where the tool supports it. # Interview questions Q1 Why is migrating 1 TB of small files slower than 1 TB of large files? Per-file overhead dominates: each file (and each version) needs metadata processing, permission and property updates, and API calls or package entries. Thousands of small files mean many more operations per gigabyte. Q2 What is the SharePoint Migration API? A bulk import API where tools package content and metadata, upload them to Azure Blob storage (encrypted), and submit an import job that SharePoint processes asynchronously. It offers far higher throughput and less throttling than item-by-item CSOM."},{"t":"Post-Migration Validation – Counts, Metadata, Versions, Permissions and Functional Testing","c":"migration","u":"articles/migration/post-migration-validation.html","s":"A rigorous validation approach after each wave, covering automated count comparisons with PowerShell, sampling metadata and versions, permission parity checks, broken links, search and navigation, functional UAT by owners, and a sign-off template.","k":"validation, migration, uat, permissions, powershell, sign-off","h":"Automated count comparison · Validation checklist · Sign-off template (per site) · Interview questions · How do you prove a migration was complete? · Why is owner UAT necessary if counts match?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Validation & Operations","b":"flowchart LR A[Automated checks<br/>counts, sizes, errors] --> B[Sampling<br/>metadata, versions,<br/>authors, dates] B --> C[Permissions parity<br/>key sites & libraries] C --> D[Functional UAT<br/>owners test real tasks] D --> E{Sign-off?} E -- yes --> F[Close wave] E -- no --> G[Fix & re-run<br/>targeted copies] G --> A # Automated count comparison Compare item counts per list: source (on-prem SSOM) vs target (PnP) PowerShell Copy # 1) Source counts (on-prem server) $src = Get-SPWeb \"https://sp2016.contoso.local/sites/finance\" $src .Lists | Where-Object { -not $_ .Hidden } | Select-Object Title, ItemCount | Export-Csv .\\counts -source .csv -NoTypeInformation # 2) Target counts (PnP PowerShell) Connect-PnPOnline -Url \"https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/finance\" -Interactive -ClientId $clientId Get-PnPList | Where-Object { -not $_ .Hidden } | Select-Object Title, ItemCount | Export-Csv .\\counts -target .csv -NoTypeInformation # 3) Compare $s = Import-Csv .\\counts -source .csv; $t = Import-Csv .\\counts -target .csv $s | ForEach-Object { $tt = $t | Where-Object Title -eq $_ .Title [ pscustomobject ] @ { List = $_ .Title; Source = [ int ] $_ .ItemCount; Target = [ int ]( $tt .ItemCount); Diff = [ int ] $_ .ItemCount - [ int ]( $tt .ItemCount) } } | Where-Object Diff -ne 0 | Format-Table # Validation checklist Cheat sheet Wave validation checklist 01 Counts Items/files per list match (or differences explained by exclusions). 02 Errors Migration report errors = 0 or accepted with reasons. 03 Metadata 20 random items per key library: columns, content types, managed metadata. 04 Authorship Created/Modified/By preserved (not migration account). 05 Versions Version counts as configured. 06 Permissions Unique permissions on sampled libraries/folders match the design. 07 Navigation & links Quick launch, hub nav, key links work; redirects in place. 08 Search Content appears in search after crawl; key queries return expected results. 09 Functional Owners run their scenarios (submit form, approval flow, report). # Sign-off template (per site) Item Result Notes Counts reconciled ✅ / ❌ Sample checks (20 items) ✅ / ❌ Permissions verified ✅ / ❌ Business scenarios tested ✅ / ❌ Owner sign-off Name, date # Interview questions Q1 How do you prove a migration was complete? Reconcile counts (items and files per list, sizes) between source and target, review the migration error r"},{"t":"Migration Runbook Template – Roles, Timeline, Commands, Go/No-Go and Rollback","c":"migration","u":"articles/migration/migration-runbook-template.html","s":"A ready-to-use runbook structure for a SharePoint migration wave, covering scope, roles and contacts, pre-checks, step-by-step timeline with commands, go/no-go criteria, communication templates, rollback steps and hypercare.","k":"runbook, migration, cutover, go/no-go, rollback, template","h":"1 · Scope · 2 · Roles & contacts · 3 · Timeline with commands · 4 · Go / No-Go criteria · 5 · Rollback · Interview questions · What belongs in a migration runbook? · What are typical go/no-go criteria?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Validation & Operations","b":"Copy this structure for each wave. # 1 · Scope Field Value Wave Wave 3 – Finance & Procurement Sites 42 (list in appendix A) Volume 380 GB, 410k items Freeze window Fri 18:00 → Sat 14:00 IST Go-live Mon 08:00 IST # 2 · Roles & contacts Role Name Responsibility Migration lead — Owns runbook, go/no-go call Migration engineer(s) — Runs scripts, monitors reports SharePoint admin — Locks/unlocks sites, permissions, redirects Business champions — Validation & UAT Service desk lead — Hypercare queue # 3 · Timeline with commands Cut-over steps Text Copy T-14d Comms #1: what's changing, dates, training links T-2d Comms #2: freeze reminder; verify final delta script on 2 sites T-0 17:30 Pre-checks: target sites exist, flows OFF, mapping files current T-0 18:00 Lock source: Set-SPSite -Identity <url> -LockState ReadOnly (per site, scripted) T-0 18:10 Final delta: .\\Run-Wave.ps1 -WaveFile .\\wave-03.csv -Incremental T+1 09:00 Validation: .\\Compare-Counts.ps1 ; sampling by champions T+1 12:00 GO / NO-GO meeting T+1 13:00 Enable flows, set redirects, update hub navigation, Teams tabs T+1 14:00 Comms #3: \"You're live\" + support channel T+2..T+14 Hypercare: daily stand-up, ticket triage # 4 · Go / No-Go criteria Final delta completed with 0 blocking errors Counts reconciled for all 42 sites (differences explained) Permissions verified on 10 sampled libraries Critical business scenarios passed (list in appendix B) Flows/apps tested on target Support team briefed # 5 · Rollback Announce rollback, then unlock source sites ( -LockState Unlock ). Remove redirects and revert navigation. Disable target flows; set target sites read-only to avoid split-brain edits. Root-cause analysis, then reschedule. Rehearse Do a dress rehearsal of the runbook on the pilot or one small site, timing each step. The real cut-over then becomes predictable. # Interview questions Q1 What belongs in a migration runbook? Scope, roles and contacts, a timed sequence of steps with exact commands, pre-checks, go/no-go criteria, communication templates, a rollback procedure, and hypercare arrangements, rehearsed before the real cut-over. Q2 What are typical go/no-go criteria? No blocking migration errors, reconciled counts, verified permissions on samples, passed critical business scenarios, working automation (flows and apps) on the target, and support readiness."},{"t":"Microsoft Entra ID App Registrations – Delegated vs Application Permissions, Certificates and Consent","c":"azure","u":"articles/azure/entra-app-registration.html","s":"Everything a Microsoft 365 developer needs about app registrations, covering app objects vs service principals, delegated vs application permissions, admin consent, client secrets vs certificates vs federated credentials, exposing APIs and scopes, app roles, and secure defaults with scripts.","k":"entra id, azure ad, app registration, oauth, permissions, certificates, consent","h":"Objects · Delegated vs application permissions · Credentials: choose wisely · Create an app with a certificate (PowerShell) · Exposing your own API · Interview questions · What is the difference between an app registration and an enterprise application? · Why can't you call SharePoint REST app-only with a client secret? · When should you use delegated permissions?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Identity","b":"An app registration defines an application's identity in Microsoft Entra ID (formerly Azure AD): who it is, how it authenticates, which APIs it may call, and which APIs it exposes. Since ACS retired for SharePoint Online in 2026, every non-interactive integration with SharePoint uses Entra apps. # Objects App registration anatomy APPLICATION OBJECT (HOME TENANT) Client ID Credentials: certificate /… Redirect URIs API permissions requested Expose an API (scopes) App roles instantiated per tenant as SERVICE PRINCIPAL (ENTERPRISE APPLICATION) Consent grants Assignment (users/groups) Conditional Access target tokens issued by MICROSOFT IDENTITY PLATFORM Delegated tokens (user + app) Application tokens (app only) App registration anatomy # Delegated vs application permissions Delegated Application Acts as Signed-in user + app App alone (daemon) Effective access Intersection of user's rights and scope Everything the permission grants (tenant-wide) Consent User or admin (depending on scope) Admin consent always Flows Auth code (+PKCE), on-behalf-of Client credentials Example SPFx web part reading my calendar Nightly job updating a list Application permissions are tenant-wide Sites.ReadWrite.All (application) lets the app write every site in the tenant. Prefer Sites.Selected with explicit per-site grants. See Sites.Selected . # Credentials: choose wisely Credential Use Notes Managed identity Azure-hosted code (Functions, App Service, Automation) No secret at all, which is best Federated identity credential GitHub Actions, Kubernetes, other IdPs No stored secret Certificate Daemons outside Azure, SharePoint app-only (CSOM requires certs for SPO app-only) Store in Key Vault; rotate Client secret Prototypes, some SaaS integrations Expires, so avoid for production SharePoint access SharePoint app-only needs certificates SharePoint Online REST and CSOM app-only calls with Entra apps require certificate authentication. Tokens obtained with a client secret are rejected by SharePoint APIs (Microsoft Graph accepts both). # Create an app with a certificate (PowerShell) Self-signed cert + app registration with PnP PowerShell PowerShell Copy # Creates the app, a certificate (pfx/cer), and requests permissions; prompts for admin consent Register-PnPEntraIDApp -ApplicationName \"Contoso Deviation Sync\" -Tenant contoso.onmicrosoft.com ` -OutPath .\\certs -DeviceLogin ` -GraphApplica"},{"t":"Sites.Selected – Least-Privilege App Access to Specific SharePoint Sites","c":"azure","u":"articles/azure/sites-selected.html","s":"Grant applications access to only the SharePoint sites they need, covering how Sites.Selected works (Graph and SharePoint), granting read, write, manage or fullcontrol per site with Graph or PnP, list-level and item-level scopes, and auditing grants.","k":"sites.selected, least privilege, entra id, sharepoint, graph, security","h":"Granting access · Interview questions · What does an app with only Sites.Selected consented have access to? · Why is Sites.Selected preferred over Sites.ReadWrite.All?","l":"Advanced","x":"Identity","b":"Sites.Selected is an application permission with zero access by default . After admin consent, the app can access nothing until a site admin explicitly grants it a role on specific sites . It is the recommended replacement for broad Sites.ReadWrite.All and for the old ACS app-only grants. sequenceDiagram autonumber participant Admin as Tenant admin participant App as Entra app (Sites.Selected) participant SP as SharePoint site /sites/quality Admin->>App: Admin consent: Sites.Selected (Graph and/or SharePoint) Note over App,SP: App has NO site access yet Admin->>SP: POST /sites/{id}/permissions { roles:[\"write\"], app } App->>SP: App-only call (cert) → allowed on /sites/quality only App-xSP: Call to /sites/finance → 403 # Granting access Microsoft Graph (caller needs Sites.FullControl.All or site admin rights) HTTP Copy POST https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0/sites/contoso.sharepoint.com:/sites/quality:/permissions Content-Type : application/json { \"roles\": [\"write\"], \"grantedToIdentities\": [{ \"application\": { \"id\": \"<client-id>\", \"displayName\": \"Contoso Deviation Sync\" } }] } PnP PowerShell PowerShell Copy Connect-PnPOnline -Url https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/quality -Interactive -ClientId $adminAppId Grant-PnPAzureADAppSitePermission -AppId \"<client-id>\" -DisplayName \"Contoso Deviation Sync\" -Permissions Write Get-PnPAzureADAppSitePermission # audit what is granted on this site Role Allows read Read site content write Read and write items and files manage Write plus manage lists (create and alter lists) fullcontrol Full control of the site (including permissions) Narrower than sites Graph also supports Lists.SelectedOperations.Selected , ListItems.SelectedOperations.Selected and Files.SelectedOperations.Selected for list-, item- and file-level grants, for even tighter scopes where supported. # Interview questions Q1 What does an app with only Sites.Selected consented have access to? Nothing, until a role (read, write, manage or fullcontrol) is granted to the app on specific sites through the Graph site permissions API or PnP/SPO cmdlets. Q2 Why is Sites.Selected preferred over Sites.ReadWrite.All? It enforces least privilege : a compromised or misbehaving app can only touch the sites explicitly granted, grants are auditable per site, and site owners can be involved in approvals."},{"t":"Key Vault & Managed Identities – Secret-Free Azure Code for Microsoft 365 Integrations","c":"azure","u":"articles/azure/key-vault-managed-identity.html","s":"Eliminate secrets in code, covering system vs user-assigned managed identities, Key Vault for certificates and secrets, Key Vault references in app settings, getting tokens for Graph and SharePoint with managed identity, and certificate rotation.","k":"key vault, managed identity, secrets, certificates, azure functions, security","h":"Key Vault references (no code changes) · Managed identity → Microsoft Graph (application permissions) · Interview questions · What problem do managed identities solve? · How do you give a managed identity Microsoft Graph permissions?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Identity","b":"Secret-free pattern AZURE FUNCTION (MANAGED IDENTITY) No secrets in code or config RBAC: Key Vault Secrets User / Certificate User KEY VAULT Certificates (SharePoint app-only) API keys for third parties Rotation & access logs MI tokens directly for AZURE RESOURCES & GRAPH Storage SQL Service Bus (RBAC) Microsoft Graph (app role assignment to… Secret-free pattern System-assigned User-assigned Lifecycle Tied to one resource Independent resource Share across apps ❌ ✅ Use when One app, simple Several apps share access, or pre-provisioned grants # Key Vault references (no code changes) Function App → Configuration → Application settings Text Copy PartnerApiKey = @Microsoft.KeyVault(SecretUri=https://kv-codex.vault.azure.net/secrets/PartnerApiKey/) # Managed identity → Microsoft Graph (application permissions) Managed identities can receive Graph app roles (Sites.Selected, User.Read.All) via PowerShell, with no app registration secret needed: Grant Graph Sites.Selected to a managed identity PowerShell Copy Connect-MgGraph -Scopes \"AppRoleAssignment.ReadWrite.All\" , \"Application.Read.All\" $mi = Get-MgServicePrincipal -Filter \"displayName eq &#x27;fn-deviation-sync&#x27;\" $graph = Get-MgServicePrincipal -Filter \"appId eq &#x27;00000003-0000-0000-c000-000000000000&#x27;\" $role = $graph .AppRoles | Where-Object { $_ .Value -eq \"Sites.Selected\" } New-MgServicePrincipalAppRoleAssignment -ServicePrincipalId $mi .Id -PrincipalId $mi .Id -ResourceId $graph .Id -AppRoleId $role .Id # then grant the MI&#x27;s app ID write on specific sites (see Sites.Selected) Use it in code C# Copy var credential = new DefaultAzureCredential(); // MI in Azure, developer login locally var graph = new GraphServiceClient(credential, new [] { \"https://graph.microsoft.com/.default\" }); var site = await graph.Sites[ \"contoso.sharepoint.com:/sites/quality\" ].GetAsync(); SharePoint REST/CSOM with managed identity Graph accepts managed identity tokens. For SharePoint REST and CSOM app-only calls, managed identity tokens for https://<tenant>.sharepoint.com work when the MI has SharePoint app roles assigned (for example Sites.Selected for the Office 365 SharePoint Online resource), which avoids certificate management. # Interview questions Q1 What problem do managed identities solve? They give Azure resources an Entra identity whose credentials are managed by Azure, so code obtains tokens for Key Vaul"},{"t":"Azure Services Map for SharePoint & Power Platform Developers","c":"azure","u":"articles/azure/azure-services-map.html","s":"Which Azure services matter for Microsoft 365 solutions and why, covering Entra ID, Functions, App Service and Static Web Apps, Storage, SQL and Cosmos DB, Key Vault, Service Bus and Event Grid, API Management, Logic Apps, Application Insights and Azure OpenAI, with a reference architecture.","k":"azure, architecture, microsoft 365, functions, storage, overview","h":"Reference architecture · Service cheat sheet · Interview questions · Which Azure services do you typically pair with SPFx? · Why put an API between Power Platform or SPFx and back-end systems?","l":"Beginner","x":"Compute & Integration","b":"SharePoint, SPFx and the Power Platform cover the front end and low-code logic . Azure supplies everything else: secure APIs, background processing, storage at scale, integration and AI. # Reference architecture Microsoft 365 + Azure reference architecture EXPERIENCES SharePoint pages (SPFx) Teams / Viva (SPFx, ACEs) Power Apps & Power Automate Copilot agents Entra ID tokens API LAYER Azure API Management (optional) Azure Functions / App Service APIs Custom connectors identity & secrets SECURITY Microsoft Entra ID (apps, Sites.Selected) Managed identities Key Vault async & integration MESSAGING Service Bus (queues/topics) Event Grid (events) Logic Apps (integration workflows) data DATA SharePoint / Dataverse Azure SQL Cosmos DB Storage (Tables, Blobs, Queues) Azure AI Search / Azure OpenAI observe OPERATIONS Application Insights / Log Analytics Azure Monitor alerts DevOps pipelines Microsoft 365 + Azure reference architecture # Service cheat sheet Need Azure service Article App identity, permissions Entra ID app registrations Entra app registration Least-privilege SharePoint access Sites.Selected Sites.Selected Serverless APIs & jobs Azure Functions Functions with SharePoint React to list changes Webhooks + Functions + Queues SharePoint webhooks Key-value data Table Storage Table Storage Large files Blob Storage Blob Storage Relational / document DB Azure SQL / Cosmos DB SQL & Cosmos Secrets, certificates Key Vault + managed identity Key Vault & MI Expose APIs to Power Platform API Management + custom connectors APIM Reliable async messaging Service Bus / Event Grid Messaging Monitoring Application Insights App Insights Host web apps App Service / Static Web Apps Hosting AI over SharePoint content Azure OpenAI + AI Search RAG # Interview questions Q1 Which Azure services do you typically pair with SPFx? Entra ID (an app registration exposing an API), Azure Functions or App Service for the API, Key Vault and managed identities for secrets, a data store (SQL, Cosmos DB, Table or Blob storage), and Application Insights for monitoring. Q2 Why put an API between Power Platform or SPFx and back-end systems? To keep secrets and privileged access server-side, enforce validation and business rules, shape and aggregate data, apply caching and throttling, and present a stable contract to many clients."},{"t":"Azure Functions with SharePoint – HTTP APIs, Timers and Queue Workers using Graph and PnP Core","c":"azure","u":"articles/azure/azure-functions-sharepoint.html","s":"Build Azure Functions that work with SharePoint Online, covering hosting plans, isolated .NET and Node v4 models, authentication with certificates or managed identity, PnP Core SDK and Graph SDK examples, timer and queue triggers, retries, and deployment.","k":"azure functions, sharepoint, pnp core sdk, microsoft graph, timer trigger, queue","h":".NET isolated + PnP Core SDK (certificate or MI) · Node (v4 model) + Graph SDK with managed identity · Interview questions · How do Azure Functions replace SharePoint timer jobs? · Which SDK would you use to work with SharePoint lists from .NET in Azure?","l":"Advanced","x":"Compute & Integration","b":"Azure Functions are the default back end for Microsoft 365 solutions: HTTP APIs for SPFx and Power Platform, timers that replace SharePoint timer jobs, and queue workers for webhooks and long-running tasks. Functions in a SharePoint solution TRIGGERS HTTP (SPFx / connector) Timer (CRON) Storage / Service Bus queue Event Grid code FUNCTION APP (.NET 8 ISOLATED / NODE 20+) Managed identity Key Vault references Application Insights SharePoint access SDKS PnP Core SDK (CSOM-like, batching) Microsoft Graph SDK PnP Framework (CSOM legacy) data SHAREPOINT ONLINE Lists & libraries (Sites.Selected) Search Taxonomy Functions in a SharePoint solution Hosting plan Use Flex Consumption / Consumption Spiky, event-driven workloads; pay per execution Premium VNet, no cold start, long runs Dedicated (App Service) Predictable load, existing plans # .NET isolated + PnP Core SDK (certificate or MI) Program.cs C# Copy var host = new HostBuilder() .ConfigureFunctionsWorkerDefaults() .ConfigureServices((ctx, services) => { services.AddApplicationInsightsTelemetryWorkerService(); services.AddPnPCore(o => { o.PnPContext.GraphFirst = true ; o.Sites.Add( \"Quality\" , new PnPCoreSiteOptions { SiteUrl = ctx.Configuration[ \"QualitySiteUrl\" ] }); }); services.AddPnPCoreAuthentication(a => { a.Credentials.Configurations.Add( \"cert\" , new PnPCoreAuthenticationCredentialConfigurationOptions { ClientId = ctx.Configuration[ \"ClientId\" ], TenantId = ctx.Configuration[ \"TenantId\" ], X509Certificate = new PnPCoreAuthenticationX509CertificateOptions { StoreName = \"My\" , StoreLocation = \"CurrentUser\" , Thumbprint = ctx.Configuration[ \"CertThumbprint\" ] } }); a.Credentials.DefaultConfiguration = \"cert\" ; a.Sites.Add( \"Quality\" , new PnPCoreAuthenticationSiteOptions { AuthenticationProviderName = \"cert\" }); }); }) .Build(); host.Run(); Timer function: close stale drafts nightly C# Copy public class CloseStaleDrafts ( IPnPContextFactory factory, ILogger<CloseStaleDrafts> log ) { [ Function( \"CloseStaleDrafts\" ) ] public async Task Run ( [TimerTrigger( \"0 30 1 * * *\" )] TimerInfo timer) // 01:30 UTC daily { using var ctx = await factory.CreateAsync( \"Quality\" ); var list = await ctx.Web.Lists.GetByTitleAsync( \"Deviations\" ); string cutoff = DateTime.UtcNow.AddDays( -30 ).ToString( \"yyyy-MM-ddTHH:mm:ssZ\" ); string caml = $@\"<View><Query><Where><And> <Eq><FieldRef Name=&#x27;Status&#x27;/><Value Type=&#x2"},{"t":"SharePoint Webhooks with Azure Functions – Subscriptions, Validation, GetChanges and Renewal","c":"azure","u":"articles/azure/sharepoint-webhooks.html","s":"The complete, production-ready pattern for reacting to SharePoint list changes, covering webhook subscription creation, the validation handshake, queue-based processing, GetChanges with change tokens, renewing subscriptions before 180 days, security with clientState, and monitoring.","k":"sharepoint webhooks, azure functions, getchanges, change token, queue, event-driven","h":"Architecture · 1 · Create the subscription · 2 · Receiver (validation + enqueue) · 3 · Processor with GetChanges · 4 · Renew before expiry · Interview questions · Why do SharePoint webhook notifications not include the changed item data? · What happens if your webhook endpoint is down? · What is the maximum lifetime of a webhook subscription?","l":"Advanced","x":"Compute & Integration","b":"SharePoint webhooks notify your HTTPS endpoint when items in a list or library change. They are the modern replacement for remote event receivers in SharePoint Online. Notifications are deliberately minimal (\"something changed in list X\"), so your code asks SharePoint what changed with GetChanges . # Architecture Production webhook pipeline SHAREPOINT ONLINE LIST Webhook subscription (expires ≤ 180 days) Sends POST on changes (batched) HTTPS POST (respond < 5 s) FUNCTION: RECEIVER Validation handshake clientState check Enqueue notification Storage queue / Service Bus FUNCTION: PROCESSOR Load last change token (Table storage) GetChanges(token) → changed item IDs Business logic (Graph / PnP) Save new change token scheduled FUNCTION: RENEWER (TIMER) Extend subscriptions before expiry Recreate if missing Production webhook pipeline sequenceDiagram autonumber participant SP as SharePoint list participant R as Receiver function participant Q as Queue participant P as Processor function participant T as Token store (Table) Note over SP,R: Subscription creation R->>SP: POST /_api/web/lists('{id}')/subscriptions { notificationUrl, expirationDateTime, clientState } SP->>R: POST ?validationtoken=abc R-->>SP: 200 text/plain \"abc\" (within 5 s) SP-->>R: 201 subscription created Note over SP,P: Changes SP->>R: POST { value:[{ subscriptionId, resource(listId), siteUrl, clientState }] } R->>Q: enqueue R-->>SP: 200 (fast) Q->>P: message P->>T: read lastChangeToken P->>SP: POST list/GetChanges { query: { ChangeTokenStart, Item:true, Add, Update, DeleteObject } } SP-->>P: changes (ItemId, ChangeType, ChangeToken) P->>P: process items (idempotent) P->>T: save newest ChangeToken # 1 · Create the subscription REST (app-only token or user with list permissions) HTTP Copy POST https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/quality/_api/web/lists/getbytitle(&#x27;Deviations&#x27;)/subscriptions Accept : application/json;odata=nometadata Content-Type : application/json { \"resource\": \"https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/quality/_api/web/lists/getbytitle(&#x27;Deviations&#x27;)\", \"notificationUrl\": \"https://fn-codex-webhooks.azurewebsites.net/api/spWebhook?code=<function-key>\", \"expirationDateTime\": \"2027-03-31T00:00:00Z\", \"clientState\": \"f6c2…secret-shared-with-receiver\" } Or with PnP PowerShell PowerShell Copy Add-PnPWebhookSubscription -List \"Deviations\" -NotificationUrl \"https://fn-codex-web"},{"t":"Logic Apps vs Power Automate – Same Engine, Different Audiences","c":"azure","u":"articles/azure/logic-apps-vs-power-automate.html","s":"Compare Azure Logic Apps (Consumption and Standard) with Power Automate cloud flows, covering ownership, ALM, networking, pricing, connectors, monitoring and identity, with guidance on when integration teams should use Logic Apps.","k":"logic apps, power automate, integration, azure, comparison","h":"Interview questions · What do Logic Apps and Power Automate have in common? · When would you choose Logic Apps Standard?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Compute & Integration","b":"Power Automate Logic Apps Owners Makers, business teams Developers, integration/IT Where Power Platform environments Azure subscriptions (resource groups) ALM Solutions, pipelines ARM/Bicep/Terraform, Azure DevOps/GitHub Networking Public (gateway for on-prem) VNet integration (Standard), private endpoints Pricing Per user / per flow / PAYG Per action (Consumption) or plan-based (Standard) Identity Connections (users/service accounts) Managed identity , connections Monitoring Run history (28 days), analytics Azure Monitor, Log Analytics, alerts Expressions & connectors Same WDL & largely same connectors Same + built-in high-performance connectors (Standard) Best for Human-centric automation in M365 System-to-system integration, B2B, high volume flowchart TD A{Who builds and runs it?} -->|Business makers| PA[Power Automate] A -->|IT integration team| B{Needs VNet, IaC,<br/>Azure monitoring?} B -->|yes| LA[Logic Apps Standard] B -->|no, low volume| LC[Logic Apps Consumption<br/>or Power Automate] # Interview questions Q1 What do Logic Apps and Power Automate have in common? The same workflow engine and Workflow Definition Language , a largely shared connector ecosystem, and similar designers. Differences are in hosting, ALM, networking, billing and audience. Q2 When would you choose Logic Apps Standard? For enterprise integrations that need VNet access or private endpoints, infrastructure-as-code deployment, managed identity, high throughput, and centralized Azure monitoring under IT ownership."},{"t":"Azure API Management for Microsoft 365 Solutions – Securing and Publishing APIs to Power Platform","c":"azure","u":"articles/azure/apim-custom-connectors.html","s":"Use APIM as the front door for your APIs, covering products and subscriptions, validate-jwt for Entra tokens, rate limits, caching, transforming legacy SOAP to REST, exporting APIs directly to Power Platform as custom connectors, and versioning.","k":"api management, apim, custom connectors, power platform, security, rate limiting","h":"Publishing to Power Platform · Interview questions · Why put APIM in front of Azure Functions used by Power Platform? · What does the validate-jwt policy do?","l":"Advanced","x":"Compute & Integration","b":"APIM as the API front door CONSUMERS SPFx (AadHttpClient) Power Apps / Power Automate (custom… Nintex Xtensions Partners HTTPS + Entra token / subscription key AZURE API MANAGEMENT validate-jwt rate-limit-by-key caching Transformations (SOAP→REST,… Versioning & revisions Developer portal backends APIS Azure Functions App Service APIs Legacy SOAP / on-prem via VNet SaaS APIs APIM as the API front door APIM inbound policy: require Entra token with a role, throttle per caller XML Copy < inbound > < base /> < validate-jwt header-name = \"Authorization\" failed-validation-httpcode = \"401\" require-scheme = \"Bearer\" > < openid-config url = \"https://login.microsoftonline.com/{tenant-id}/v2.0/.well-known/openid-configuration\" /> < audiences > < audience > api://deviation-api </ audience > </ audiences > < required-claims > < claim name = \"roles\" match = \"any\" > < value > Deviations.Read </ value > < value > Deviations.ReadWrite </ value > </ claim > </ required-claims > </ validate-jwt > < rate-limit-by-key calls = \"120\" renewal-period = \"60\" counter-key = \"@(context.Request.Headers.GetValueOrDefault(\" Authorization \",\"\") .AsJwt ()? .Subject )\" /> < cache-lookup vary-by-developer = \"false\" vary-by-developer-groups = \"false\" /> </ inbound > # Publishing to Power Platform APIM can export an API to a Power Platform environment as a custom connector (APIM → API → Create Power Connector ), with authentication settings. Makers then use typed actions without knowing the backend. # Interview questions Q1 Why put APIM in front of Azure Functions used by Power Platform? Central security (JWT validation, IP filters), rate limiting and quotas per consumer, caching, transformation, versioning, monitoring and a developer portal, plus one-click export to Power Platform as a custom connector. Q2 What does the validate-jwt policy do? It validates the bearer token's signature, issuer, audience, expiry and required claims (scopes or roles) against Entra ID's OpenID configuration before the request reaches the backend, and rejects invalid calls with 401."},{"t":"Service Bus vs Event Grid vs Storage Queues – Messaging Patterns for M365 Integrations","c":"azure","u":"articles/azure/service-bus-event-grid.html","s":"Choose the right messaging service, covering queues vs topics, Event Grid events and system topics, Storage queues for simple buffering, retries, dead-lettering, idempotency and ordering, with SharePoint, Dataverse and Graph examples.","k":"service bus, event grid, storage queues, messaging, integration, idempotency","h":"Reliability rules · Interview questions · When would you use Service Bus instead of a Storage queue? · What is the difference between Event Grid and Service Bus?","l":"Advanced","x":"Compute & Integration","b":"Service Model Best for Storage Queues Simple queue Cheap buffering (webhook → processor), < 64 KB messages Service Bus queues Brokered messaging Commands, ordered processing (sessions), transactions, dead-letter Service Bus topics Pub/sub One event, many subscribers with filters (Dataverse → ERP + DWH) Event Grid Reactive events \"Something happened\" (blob created, Graph/partner events) fan-out via push Event Hubs Streaming Telemetry at massive scale flowchart LR DV[Dataverse async step] -->|RemoteExecutionContext| SBT[(Service Bus topic<br/>deviation-events)] SBT --> S1[Subscription: ERP<br/>filter severity = Critical] SBT --> S2[Subscription: Data warehouse] SPW[SharePoint webhook] --> SQ[(Storage queue)] BLOB[Blob created] --> EG[Event Grid] --> F[Function: OCR / thumbnail] S1 --> F2[Function → ERP API] S2 --> F3[Function → SQL] # Reliability rules Idempotent consumers : messages can be delivered more than once (at-least-once). Dead-letter queues : inspect and replay poison messages; alert on DLQ depth. Retries with backoff : Functions bindings retry, so set max delivery counts. Ordering : use Service Bus sessions when per-entity order matters. # Interview questions Q1 When would you use Service Bus instead of a Storage queue? When you need topics/subscriptions (pub/sub), ordered processing (sessions), duplicate detection, transactions, scheduled messages, larger messages or richer dead-letter handling. Storage queues are fine for simple, cheap buffering. Q2 What is the difference between Event Grid and Service Bus? Event Grid distributes lightweight events (notifications that something happened) to many handlers with push delivery. Service Bus is a message broker for commands and business messages with queues, ordering, sessions and transactional guarantees."},{"t":"Azure Table Storage Fundamentals – Keys, Queries, Batches and the @azure/data-tables SDK","c":"azure","u":"articles/azure/table-storage.html","s":"Learn Table Storage properly, covering entities, PartitionKey/RowKey design, query patterns and costs, entity group transactions, optimistic concurrency with ETags, Entra ID RBAC access, and Node and .NET SDK examples, plus when to choose Cosmos DB for Table.","k":"azure table storage, nosql, partition key, data-tables, cosmos db, storage","h":"Query cost ladder · Designing keys: examples · .NET SDK · Interview questions · What is the only index in Azure Table Storage? · How do you get newest-first ordering in Table Storage?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Storage & Data","b":"Azure Table Storage stores entities (schemaless property bags up to 1 MB) in tables. Each entity is addressed by PartitionKey + RowKey , which is the only index. Design keys around your queries and it's extremely fast and cheap. 2 keys PartitionKey + RowKey (unique pair) 1 MB max entity, 252 custom properties 100 operations per batch (same partition) RBAC Entra ID data roles supported # Query cost ladder Query Efficiency PartitionKey eq 'A' and RowKey eq '1' (point read) ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐ PartitionKey eq 'A' and RowKey ge 'x' and RowKey lt 'y' (range) ⭐⭐⭐⭐ PartitionKey eq 'A' and Status eq 'Open' (partition scan) ⭐⭐⭐ Status eq 'Open' (table scan) ⭐ (avoid on big tables) # Designing keys: examples Scenario PartitionKey RowKey Desk check-ins per site per day `site yyyy-MM-dd` User preferences userOid settingName Audit log per list `listId yyyy-MM` Second access pattern (by user) Duplicate entity in table CheckInsByUser : userOid `yyyy-MM-dd # .NET SDK Azure.Data.Tables with managed identity C# Copy var table = new TableClient( new Uri( \"https://stcodexdata.table.core.windows.net\" ), \"CheckIns\" , new DefaultAzureCredential()); await table.CreateIfNotExistsAsync(); var e = new TableEntity( $\"blr| {DateTime.UtcNow:yyyy-MM-dd} \" , $\" {DateTime.MaxValue.Ticks - DateTime.UtcNow.Ticks:D19} _ {userOid} \" ) { [ \"Desk\" ] = \"B-12\" , [ \"UserName\" ] = \"Jane Doe\" }; await table.AddEntityAsync(e); await foreach ( var row in table.QueryAsync<TableEntity>(x => x.PartitionKey == $\"blr| {DateTime.UtcNow:yyyy-MM-dd} \" )) Console.WriteLine( $\" {row[ \"UserName\" ]} → {row[ \"Desk\" ]} \" ); // Optimistic concurrency with ETag var existing = await table.GetEntityAsync<TableEntity>(e.PartitionKey, e.RowKey); existing.Value[ \"Desk\" ] = \"B-14\" ; await table.UpdateEntityAsync(existing.Value, existing.Value.ETag, TableUpdateMode.Merge); // 412 if changed meanwhile // Batch (same partition) var actions = new List<TableTransactionAction> { new (TableTransactionActionType.UpsertMerge, e) }; await table.SubmitTransactionAsync(actions); Cosmos DB for Table Need global distribution, secondary indexes on all properties or guaranteed low latency at scale? Azure Cosmos DB for Table speaks the same API (same SDK) with richer capabilities and higher cost. See also SPFx + Table Storage for the end-to-end web part integration. # Interview questions Q1 What is the only index in Azure Table Storage? The combination of"},{"t":"Azure Blob Storage for Microsoft 365 Solutions – SAS, Access Tiers, Lifecycle and Events","c":"azure","u":"articles/azure/blob-storage.html","s":"When to store files in Blob Storage instead of SharePoint, covering containers and access tiers, user-delegation SAS, RBAC, lifecycle management, immutability for records, Event Grid triggers and integration with SPFx and Power Automate.","k":"azure blob storage, sas, lifecycle, access tiers, event grid, archive","h":"Interview questions · What is a user-delegation SAS and why prefer it? · How do you reduce storage costs for old files automatically?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Storage & Data","b":"Store in SharePoint when… Store in Blob when… People collaborate, co-author, need versions, metadata, search, Copilot Files are machine-generated, huge, or app-owned (media, backups, IoT, exports) Retention & sensitivity labels via Purview Cheap tiered storage, immutability (WORM), lifecycle rules Sharing with colleagues and guests Delivered through your app (SAS / CDN) Blob storage capabilities CONTAINERS Private by default RBAC (Storage Blob Data Reader/Contributor) SAS (user delegation preferred) tiers ACCESS TIERS Hot Cool Cold Archive (offline, hours to rehydrate) automation LIFECYCLE & EVENTS Lifecycle rules (move to Cool after 30d, Archive… Event Grid: BlobCreated → Function Immutability policies (WORM) Blob storage capabilities Lifecycle management rule JSON Copy { \"rules\" : [ { \"name\" : \"age-out-reports\" , \"enabled\" : true , \"type\" : \"Lifecycle\" , \"definition\" : { \"filters\" : { \"blobTypes\" : [ \"blockBlob\" ] , \"prefixMatch\" : [ \"reports/\" ] } , \"actions\" : { \"baseBlob\" : { \"tierToCool\" : { \"daysAfterModificationGreaterThan\" : 30 } , \"tierToArchive\" : { \"daysAfterModificationGreaterThan\" : 180 } , \"delete\" : { \"daysAfterModificationGreaterThan\" : 2555 } } } } } ] } For browser uploads with short-lived SAS from SPFx, see SPFx with Blob & SQL . # Interview questions Q1 What is a user-delegation SAS and why prefer it? A SAS signed with a key obtained from Entra ID (by a managed identity or user) instead of the storage account key. It avoids distributing account keys, can be revoked by revoking delegation keys, and is scoped and time-limited. Q2 How do you reduce storage costs for old files automatically? Use lifecycle management rules to move blobs to Cool, Cold or Archive tiers after periods of inactivity and delete them after the retention period, optionally scoped by prefix or tags."},{"t":"Azure SQL vs Cosmos DB vs Dataverse – Choosing a Data Store for Apps Around Microsoft 365","c":"azure","u":"articles/azure/azure-sql-cosmos.html","s":"Compare Azure SQL Database, Azure Cosmos DB and Dataverse for SPFx, Power Apps and integration back ends, covering data model, scale, security, cost, Power Platform connectivity and typical architectures.","k":"azure sql, cosmos db, dataverse, database, architecture, comparison","h":"Interview questions · When is Azure SQL a better choice than Dataverse for an app? · What is a Request Unit in Cosmos DB?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Storage & Data","b":"Azure SQL Database Azure Cosmos DB Dataverse Model Relational (T-SQL) Document/NoSQL (JSON), multi-API Relational + metadata platform Strength Joins, transactions, reporting, existing LOB Massive scale, global distribution, low latency Security model, low-code apps, business logic Access from Power Apps SQL connector (premium, delegation) Custom connector/API Native Access from SPFx Via API Via API Web API (delegated) Security SQL roles, RLS, Entra auth RBAC, keys, resource tokens Roles, BUs, column security Cost model vCore/DTU or serverless RU/s (provisioned or serverless) Capacity + Power Platform licences Best for Existing or reporting-heavy LOB data High-volume, flexible-schema, global apps Business apps built on Power Platform flowchart TD A{Built mainly with Power Platform?} -->|yes| DV[Dataverse] A -->|no| B{Relational, joins,<br/>reporting, existing SQL?} B -->|yes| SQL[Azure SQL] B -->|no| C{Massive scale, flexible schema,<br/>global low latency?} C -->|yes| COS[Cosmos DB] C -->|no| SQL # Interview questions Q1 When is Azure SQL a better choice than Dataverse for an app? When the data already lives in SQL or is shared with other systems, when complex reporting and joins dominate, when developers own the solution with code-first APIs, or when Power Platform premium licensing for every user is not justified. Q2 What is a Request Unit in Cosmos DB? A normalized measure of the cost of database operations (CPU, memory, IO). You provision RU/s (or use serverless). Reads by id are cheap, and complex queries and writes consume more RUs."},{"t":"Monitoring SPFx, Azure Functions and Power Platform with Application Insights","c":"azure","u":"articles/azure/application-insights.html","s":"End-to-end observability for Microsoft 365 solutions, covering instrumenting SPFx with the Application Insights JavaScript SDK, Functions telemetry, correlation across tiers, custom events and metrics, KQL queries, alerts, workbooks and privacy considerations.","k":"application insights, monitoring, kql, spfx, azure functions, observability","h":"Interview questions · How do you correlate a failing SPFx action with the backend error? · What would you alert on for an Azure Functions integration?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Operations & AI","b":"Observability across tiers SPFX WEB PART App Insights JS SDK Page views custom events exceptions Correlation headers on API… traceparent AZURE FUNCTIONS / APIS Requests dependencies logs Same operation ID dependencies BACK ENDS SharePoint / Graph calls SQL Storage all into APPLICATION INSIGHTS / LOG ANALYTICS KQL queries Workbooks Alerts → Teams / email Observability across tiers SPFx: initialize once (application customizer or web part onInit) TypeScript Copy import { ApplicationInsights } from &#x27;@microsoft/applicationinsights-web&#x27; ; const ai = new ApplicationInsights ({ config : { connectionString : &#x27;<from tenant property, not hard-coded>&#x27; , enableAutoRouteTracking : false , disableFetchTracking : false , enableCorsCorrelation : true , correlationHeaderDomains : [ &#x27;fn-deviation.azurewebsites.net&#x27; ] }}); ai. loadAppInsights (); ai. trackPageView ({ name : &#x27;Deviations web part&#x27; }); ai. trackEvent ({ name : &#x27;DeviationCreated&#x27; }, { severity : &#x27;Critical&#x27; , site : this . context . pageContext . web . serverRelativeUrl }); KQL: failures by operation in the last 24h KQL Copy requests | where timestamp > ago(24h) and success == false | summarize failures = count() by operation_Name, resultCode | order by failures desc // slow SharePoint/Graph dependencies from Functions dependencies | where timestamp > ago(1h) and (target has \"sharepoint.com\" or target has \"graph.microsoft.com\") | summarize p95 = percentile(duration, 95), calls = count() by name | order by p95 desc Privacy Don't send personal data (names, emails, document titles) in telemetry unless your privacy assessment allows it. Prefer IDs or hashed values, and set retention appropriately. # Interview questions Q1 How do you correlate a failing SPFx action with the backend error? Use Application Insights in both tiers with distributed tracing enabled (correlation headers on calls to your API domain). The SPFx dependency and the Function request share an operation ID , so a KQL query on that ID shows the full chain. Q2 What would you alert on for an Azure Functions integration? Failure rate per function, dependency failures (Graph/SharePoint 429/5xx), queue length or dead-letter counts, execution duration percentiles, and absence of expected executions (for example a timer didn't run)."},{"t":"Hosting Web Apps Next to Microsoft 365 – App Service, Static Web Apps and Entra Sign-In","c":"azure","u":"articles/azure/hosting-app-service-swa.html","s":"Where to host custom web apps, APIs and former provider-hosted add-in remote webs, covering App Service vs Static Web Apps vs Container Apps, Entra ID authentication (Easy Auth), deployment slots, custom domains, and calling SharePoint and Graph from the server.","k":"app service, static web apps, hosting, easy auth, provider-hosted, azure","h":"Interview questions · Where would you host the remote web of a migrated provider-hosted add-in? · What are deployment slots good for?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Operations & AI","b":"Option Use App Service Server apps (ASP.NET Core, Node), APIs, former provider-hosted remote webs, deployment slots Static Web Apps SPA/static sites (like this one!) with optional managed Functions APIs, free tier, global CDN Container Apps Containerized microservices, background workers, scale to zero Functions Event-driven APIs and jobs flowchart LR U[User] -->|Entra sign-in<br/>Easy Auth| AS[App Service<br/>ASP.NET Core] AS -->|OBO / app-only| G[Graph & SharePoint] U --> SWA[Static Web Apps<br/>docs / SPA] SWA --> API[Managed Functions API] Hosting this knowledge site The site you're reading is static HTML. It can be hosted on GitHub Pages , Azure Static Web Apps (free tier, custom domain, optional Entra sign-in for an internal knowledge base) or even a SharePoint document library behind a CDN. # Interview questions Q1 Where would you host the remote web of a migrated provider-hosted add-in? On Azure App Service (or Container Apps), with Entra ID authentication (Easy Auth or MSAL) instead of ACS tokens, calling SharePoint through Graph or PnP Core SDK with an Entra app (Sites.Selected) or on-behalf-of the user. Often the UI moves to SPFx and the server part becomes an API. Q2 What are deployment slots good for? Zero-downtime releases: deploy to a staging slot, warm it up and test it, then swap with production (and swap back to roll back)."},{"t":"Azure OpenAI over SharePoint Content – Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) Done Securely","c":"azure","u":"articles/azure/azure-openai-rag.html","s":"Build a custom AI assistant over SharePoint documents with Azure OpenAI and Azure AI Search, covering ingestion and chunking, embeddings, security trimming by permissions, prompt design, citations, evaluation, and when to use Microsoft 365 Copilot or Copilot Studio instead.","k":"azure openai, rag, ai search, sharepoint, embeddings, security trimming, copilot","h":"Security trimming is non-negotiable · Quality checklist · Interview questions · What is retrieval-augmented generation (RAG)? · How do you prevent a custom RAG app from exposing documents users can't access?","l":"Advanced","x":"Operations & AI","b":"Before building custom AI, check whether Microsoft 365 Copilot , SharePoint agents or Copilot Studio with SharePoint knowledge already meet the need. They respect permissions out of the box. Build custom RAG when you need a specific UX, special data sources, custom models or full control. RAG over SharePoint INGESTION Graph / SharePoint connector reads files Extract text (PDF, Office) Chunk (500–1,000 tokens, overlap) Capture ACLs (users/groups) embeddings AZURE AI SEARCH INDEX Chunks + vector embeddings Metadata (site, library, modified) Security fields (allowed groups) query time ORCHESTRATOR (FUNCTION / APP) User asks question Hybrid search (keyword + vector) filtered by user's… Top-k chunks prompt AZURE OPENAI (GPT MODEL) System prompt + retrieved chunks + question Answer with citations response UI SPFx web part / Teams app Citations link back to SharePoint RAG over SharePoint # Security trimming is non-negotiable flowchart LR Q[User question] --> T[Get user's group IDs<br/>Graph /me/transitiveMemberOf] T --> S[\"AI Search query with filter:<br/>allowedGroups/any(g: search.in(g, 'id1,id2,...'))\"] S --> C[Only chunks the user may see] C --> L[LLM answer + citations] If you index content without ACLs, the assistant can leak confidential documents to anyone. Store permitted principals with each chunk and filter every query, or use Copilot, which enforces permissions natively. # Quality checklist Chunking that respects headings, with titles and paths in each chunk. Hybrid search + semantic ranker for better retrieval than vectors alone. System prompt: answer only from sources, cite them, say \"I don't know\". Evaluation set of real questions, measuring groundedness, relevance and citation accuracy. Content hygiene: remove outdated versions, because garbage in, confident garbage out . # Interview questions Q1 What is retrieval-augmented generation (RAG)? A pattern where a search step retrieves relevant passages from your content (often hybrid keyword and vector search) and passes them to the language model with the question, so answers are grounded in your data, with citations, instead of relying only on the model's training. Q2 How do you prevent a custom RAG app from exposing documents users can't access? Index permission information (users and groups allowed) with every chunk and filter queries by the current user's group memberships (security trimming). Or u"},{"t":"SSOM vs CSOM vs JSOM vs REST vs Microsoft Graph – Which SharePoint API Should You Use?","c":"apis","u":"articles/apis/api-landscape.html","s":"A clear map of every SharePoint API generation, covering where each runs, authentication, capabilities, performance, support status in 2026, and a decision tree with examples for SPFx, Azure Functions, PowerShell and Power Platform.","k":"api, ssom, csom, jsom, rest, microsoft graph, decision","h":"Comparison · Graph vs SharePoint REST: feature gaps (typical) · Interview questions · Should new SharePoint Online integrations use Graph or REST? · What is the main difference between CSOM and REST?","l":"Beginner","x":"API Landscape","b":"2007 SSOM + ASMX web services Server object model in .NET on the farm; SOAP services ( /_vti_bin/Lists.asmx ) for remote calls. 2010 CSOM, JSOM, Silverlight OM, ListData.svc Client object models batched over client.svc ; first REST (OData v2 over lists). 2013 REST /_api Broad OData v3 REST API mirroring CSOM. 2015 Microsoft Graph Unified API across Microsoft 365 ( /sites , /drives , /lists ). 2020s Graph-first + PnP libraries PnPjs, PnP Core SDK, CLI for M365 wrap REST/Graph; SharePoint-specific gaps still filled by REST/CSOM. # Comparison API Runs Auth SharePoint Online Best for (2026) SSOM On farm servers Windows / farm ❌ On-prem farm solutions, admin scripts ASMX web services Anywhere Windows / cookie Legacy Avoid (old integrations only) CSOM (.NET) Anywhere Tokens (MSAL), Windows on-prem ✅ Back-end code needing SharePoint-specific features (via PnP Framework/Core) JSOM SharePoint pages Page cookie Legacy Classic pages only REST /_api Anywhere Cookie+digest or bearer token ✅ SPFx ( SPHttpClient /PnPjs), flows, SharePoint-specific features Microsoft Graph Anywhere Entra tokens ✅ Cross-M365 scenarios, files (drives), sites, lists, search, Teams flowchart TD A{Where does your code run?} -->|On a SharePoint Server farm| SSOM[SSOM / PowerShell snap-in] A -->|Browser in SharePoint| B{SPFx?} B -->|yes| C[REST via SPHttpClient / PnPjs<br/>+ Graph via MSGraphClientV3] A -->|Server / Azure / script| D{Does Graph cover it?<br/>files, sites, lists, search, users} D -->|yes| G[Microsoft Graph SDK] D -->|no: views, content types,<br/>permissions details, taxonomy,<br/>site designs, ...| R[SharePoint REST / CSOM<br/>via PnP Core SDK or PnP PowerShell] # Graph vs SharePoint REST: feature gaps (typical) Capability Graph SharePoint REST/CSOM Read/write list items & files ✅ ✅ Large file upload sessions ✅ ✅ Sites, lists metadata ✅ ✅ Views, view XML, formatting JSON Limited ✅ Fine-grained permissions on items Partial ✅ Taxonomy (term store) ✅ (termStore API) ✅ Site designs/scripts, features, navigation ❌ ✅ Search ✅ (Microsoft Search) ✅ (SharePoint search) Change notifications ✅ subscriptions ✅ webhooks # Interview questions Q1 Should new SharePoint Online integrations use Graph or REST? Prefer Microsoft Graph for anything it covers (files, sites, lists, items, search, users, Teams), because it is the strategic, unified API with consistent auth and SDKs. Use SharePoint REST/CS"},{"t":"Microsoft Graph for SharePoint – Sites, Lists, Items, Drives and Files CRUD","c":"apis","u":"articles/apis/graph-sharepoint.html","s":"Work with SharePoint through Microsoft Graph, covering resolving sites by path, lists and columns, list item CRUD with fields, filtering with indexed columns, drives and driveItems, large file upload sessions, permissions and sharing links, with HTTP, JavaScript and C# examples.","k":"microsoft graph, sharepoint, lists, drives, files, crud, sdk","h":"Resolve a site and list · List item CRUD · Files with drives · Interview questions · How do you set a lookup or person column with Graph? · When must you use an upload session in Graph?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Microsoft Graph","b":"SharePoint resources in Graph SITE /sites/{hostname… /sites/{site-id} Subsites columns contentTypes has LISTS /sites/{id}/lists /lists/{list-id}/columns /lists/{list-id}/items (+ fields) and DRIVES (DOCUMENT LIBRARIES) /sites/{id}/drives /drives/{drive-id}/root/chil… driveItem = file or folder plus CROSS-CUTTING permissions & sharing links search (/search/query) subscriptions (change notifications) SharePoint resources in Graph # Resolve a site and list Get IDs once, cache them HTTP Copy GET https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0/sites/contoso.sharepoint.com:/sites/quality?$select=id,displayName,webUrl → \"id\": \"contoso.sharepoint.com,6f1c…,9a2b…\" GET https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0/sites/{site-id}/lists?$filter=displayName eq &#x27;Deviations&#x27;&$select=id,displayName # List item CRUD Create, read, update, delete HTTP Copy POST /v1.0/sites/{site-id}/lists/{list-id}/items { \"fields\": { \"Title\": \"Cold room excursion\", \"Status\": \"Open\", \"Severity\": \"Major\", \"PlantLookupId\": \"3\" } } GET /v1.0/sites/{site-id}/lists/{list-id}/items?$expand=fields($select=Title,Status,DueDate)&$filter=fields/Status eq &#x27;Open&#x27;&$top=200 Prefer: HonorNonIndexedQueriesWarningMayFailRandomly (only if Status isn&#x27;t indexed, so index it instead!) PATCH /v1.0/sites/{site-id}/lists/{list-id}/items/{item-id}/fields { \"Status\": \"Closed\" } DELETE /v1.0/sites/{site-id}/lists/{list-id}/items/{item-id} Column type Graph field format Lookup \"PlantLookupId\": \"3\" Person (single) \"OwnerLookupId\": \"12\" (user's ID in the site User Information List) Multi-person / multi-lookup \"ReviewersLookupId@odata.type\": \"Collection(Edm.String)\" , \"ReviewersLookupId\": [\"12\",\"15\"] Choice (multi) \"Tags@odata.type\": \"Collection(Edm.String)\" , \"Tags\": [\"A\",\"B\"] Date ISO 8601 string # Files with drives Graph JS SDK: list, upload small file, create sharing link JavaScript Copy const drive = await client. api ( `/sites/ ${siteId} /drives` ). filter ( \"name eq &#x27;Documents&#x27;\" ). get (); const driveId = drive. value [ 0 ]. id ; const children = await client. api ( `/drives/ ${driveId} /root:/SOPs:/children` ). select ( &#x27;name,size,lastModifiedDateTime&#x27; ). get (); await client. api ( `/drives/ ${driveId} /root:/SOPs/SOP-001.pdf:/content` ). put (fileBlob); // < 250 MB → simple PUT (small files) const link = await client. api ( `/drives/ ${driveId} /root:/SOPs/SOP-001.pdf:/createLink` ) . post ({ typ"},{"t":"Graph Batching, Delta Queries and Graph Explorer – Efficient Data Access Patterns","c":"apis","u":"articles/apis/graph-batching-delta.html","s":"Reduce round trips with JSON $batch (20 requests, dependencies), sync changes with delta queries for drives, lists, users and groups, and prototype quickly with Graph Explorer, with code and pitfalls.","k":"microsoft graph, batching, delta query, graph explorer, sync, performance","h":"JSON batching · Delta queries · Interview questions · What's the limit and behaviour of Graph JSON batching? · How does a delta query work?","l":"Advanced","x":"Microsoft Graph","b":"# JSON batching Up to 20 requests in one call, with dependencies HTTP Copy POST https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0/$batch Content-Type : application/json { \"requests\": [ { \"id\": \"1\", \"method\": \"GET\", \"url\": \"/sites/contoso.sharepoint.com:/sites/quality?$select=id\" }, { \"id\": \"2\", \"method\": \"GET\", \"url\": \"/me/memberOf?$select=displayName&$top=50\" }, { \"id\": \"3\", \"dependsOn\": [\"1\"], \"method\": \"POST\", \"url\": \"/sites/{site-id}/lists/{list-id}/items\", \"headers\": { \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\" }, \"body\": { \"fields\": { \"Title\": \"Batched\" } } } ] } Each response carries its own status. Check every item, retry individual 429s, and keep in mind a batch is not transactional . # Delta queries sequenceDiagram participant App participant Graph App->>Graph: GET /drives/{id}/root/delta Graph-->>App: items… + @odata.nextLink (page) App->>Graph: follow nextLink until @odata.deltaLink Note over App: store deltaLink App->>Graph: (later) GET deltaLink Graph-->>App: only changed/deleted items + new deltaLink Resource Delta endpoint Files in a library /drives/{drive-id}/root/delta List items /sites/{site-id}/lists/{list-id}/items/delta Users / groups /users/delta , /groups/delta Calendar events /me/calendarView/delta Drive delta sync loop (Graph JS SDK) TypeScript Copy let url = savedDeltaLink ?? `/drives/ ${driveId} /root/delta?$select=id,name,file,deleted,lastModifiedDateTime,parentReference` ; do { const page = await client. api (url). get (); for ( const item of page. value ) item. deleted ? await removeFromIndex (item. id ) : await upsertIndex (item); url = page[ &#x27;@odata.nextLink&#x27; ] ?? null ; if (page[ &#x27;@odata.deltaLink&#x27; ]) savedDeltaLink = page[ &#x27;@odata.deltaLink&#x27; ]; } while (url); await persist (savedDeltaLink); Prototype in Graph Explorer Graph Explorer lets you sign in, run queries, consent to scopes, view code snippets (C#, JS, PowerShell) and see required permissions, which is the fastest way to design calls before coding. # Interview questions Q1 What's the limit and behaviour of Graph JSON batching? Up to 20 requests per batch, executed in any order unless you specify dependsOn . Each request returns its own status code (some can fail or be throttled while others succeed), and batches are not atomic. Q2 How does a delta query work? The first call returns all items (paged) and ends with a @odata.deltaLink . Calling that link later retu"},{"t":"Microsoft Search API (Graph) – Searching SharePoint, OneDrive and Connectors from Code","c":"apis","u":"articles/apis/graph-search-api.html","s":"Query the Microsoft Search index with POST /search/query, covering entity types (driveItem, listItem, site, externalItem), KQL, fields and managed properties, paging, aggregations (refiners), sorting, and comparing with the SharePoint search REST API.","k":"microsoft graph, search api, kql, sharepoint search, refiners, connectors","h":"Graph Search vs SharePoint Search REST · Interview questions · How do you get refiners from the Graph Search API? · Is Graph search security-trimmed?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Microsoft Graph","b":"Search documents with KQL, fields, sorting and refiners HTTP Copy POST https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0/search/query Content-Type : application/json { \"requests\": [{ \"entityTypes\": [\"driveItem\"], \"query\": { \"queryString\": \"contract AND FileType:pdf path:\\\"https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/legal\\\"\" }, \"fields\": [\"name\", \"webUrl\", \"lastModifiedDateTime\", \"RefinableString01\"], \"sortProperties\": [{ \"name\": \"lastModifiedDateTime\", \"isDescending\": true }], \"aggregations\": [{ \"field\": \"RefinableString01\", \"size\": 10, \"bucketDefinition\": { \"sortBy\": \"count\", \"isDescending\": true } }], \"from\": 0, \"size\": 25 }] } entityTypes Returns driveItem Files and folders listItem List items (and files as items) site , list Sites, lists externalItem Graph connector content message , event , chatMessage Mail, calendar, Teams messages (delegated) flowchart LR APP[App / SPFx / Agent] -->|POST /search/query| MS[Microsoft Search index] MS --> SP[(SharePoint & OneDrive)] MS --> GC[(Graph connectors:<br/>ServiceNow, Confluence…)] MS -->|security-trimmed results| APP # Graph Search vs SharePoint Search REST Graph /search/query SharePoint /_api/search Scope All M365 + connectors SharePoint/OneDrive Auth Graph tokens (delegated; app-only for some entity types) SharePoint auth Result shape Hits with resources Table rows with cells Features Aggregations, KQL, verticals Result sources, query rules, refiners Best for New apps, SPFx with Graph, agents Legacy and SharePoint-specific search features # Interview questions Q1 How do you get refiners from the Graph Search API? Add aggregations to the request with fields that are refinable managed properties (e.g. RefinableString01 , FileType ). The response contains buckets with counts and tokens you can use to filter subsequent queries. Q2 Is Graph search security-trimmed? Yes. Results are trimmed to the calling user's permissions for delegated calls, the same index that powers Microsoft Search and Copilot."},{"t":"Microsoft Graph Change Notifications – Subscriptions, Validation, Rich Notifications and Lifecycle Events","c":"apis","u":"articles/apis/graph-change-notifications.html","s":"React to changes in drives, lists, users, groups, Teams messages and mail with Graph subscriptions, covering creating subscriptions, validation tokens, clientState, expiry and renewal, lifecycle notifications, rich notifications with encrypted resource data, and Event Hubs delivery.","k":"microsoft graph, change notifications, subscriptions, webhooks, event hubs, teams","h":"Interview questions · How do Graph change notifications and SharePoint webhooks differ? · What are lifecycle notifications?","l":"Advanced","x":"Microsoft Graph","b":"Graph subscriptions push notifications to your HTTPS endpoint (or Azure Event Hubs / Event Grid) when resources change. For SharePoint libraries, use driveItem subscriptions on drive roots, or list subscriptions where supported, alongside SharePoint webhooks . Create a subscription HTTP Copy POST https://graph.microsoft.com/v1.0/subscriptions Content-Type : application/json { \"changeType\": \"updated\", \"notificationUrl\": \"https://fn-codex.azurewebsites.net/api/graphNotify\", \"lifecycleNotificationUrl\": \"https://fn-codex.azurewebsites.net/api/graphLifecycle\", \"resource\": \"/drives/{drive-id}/root\", \"expirationDateTime\": \"2026-11-05T11:00:00Z\", \"clientState\": \"secret-value-validated-by-receiver\" } sequenceDiagram participant App participant Graph participant EP as Your endpoint App->>Graph: POST /subscriptions Graph->>EP: POST ?validationToken=xyz EP-->>Graph: 200 text/plain xyz (≤ 10 s) Graph-->>App: 201 subscription (id, expiry) Graph->>EP: notifications (batched) EP-->>Graph: 202 Accepted Graph->>EP: lifecycle: reauthorizationRequired / subscriptionRemoved App->>Graph: PATCH /subscriptions/{id} (renew) Resource Max subscription lifetime (approx.) Notes driveItem (root) ~30 days Use delta query to fetch changes list ~30 days Item-level detail via delta/GetChanges users / groups ~29 days Teams chatMessage 60 minutes (rich data) Encrypted resource data, licensing/metering applies Outlook messages / events ~7 days Pair notifications with delta Notifications say something changed . Use delta queries to get exactly what changed, and store the deltaLink for the next run. # Interview questions Q1 How do Graph change notifications and SharePoint webhooks differ? Both validate an endpoint and send lightweight notifications. Graph subscriptions cover many Microsoft 365 resources (users, groups, mail, Teams, drives) with lifecycle notifications and optional Event Hubs delivery. SharePoint webhooks are specific to SharePoint lists and libraries and work with GetChanges change tokens. Q2 What are lifecycle notifications? Messages sent to a lifecycleNotificationUrl when a subscription needs attention (for example reauthorizationRequired or subscriptionRemoved ), so you can renew or recreate it before missing changes."},{"t":"SharePoint REST API Cheat Sheet – Endpoints, OData, Headers and Recipes","c":"apis","u":"articles/apis/rest-api-cheatsheet.html","s":"A dense reference for the SharePoint REST API, covering headers and digests, webs, lists, items, files and folders, users and groups, permissions, search, OData query options, paging, batch, and the most useful special endpoints.","k":"sharepoint rest, odata, cheat sheet, api, endpoints, http","h":"Headers · Endpoints · OData query options · Interview questions · How do you update a list item with REST? · How do you copy a file across site collections with REST?","l":"Intermediate","x":"SharePoint REST & CSOM","b":"# Headers Header Value Accept application/json;odata=nometadata (or verbose / minimalmetadata ) Content-Type application/json;odata=nometadata X-RequestDigest From POST /_api/contextinfo (cookie auth only) IF-MATCH * or ETag (update/delete) X-HTTP-Method MERGE (update), DELETE (with POST) Authorization Bearer <token> (apps) # Endpoints Web, lists, items HTTP Copy GET /_api/web?$select=Title,Url,Created GET /_api/web/lists?$filter=Hidden eq false&$select=Title,ItemCount,Id GET /_api/web/lists/getbytitle(&#x27;Deviations&#x27;)/fields?$filter=Hidden eq false&$select=InternalName,TypeAsString GET /_api/web/lists/getbytitle(&#x27;Deviations&#x27;)/items?$select=Id,Title,Owner/EMail&$expand=Owner&$top=100 GET /_api/web/lists/getbytitle(&#x27;Deviations&#x27;)/items(42)/FieldValuesAsText POST /_api/web/lists/getbytitle(&#x27;Deviations&#x27;)/items { \"Title\": \"New\" } POST /_api/web/lists/getbytitle(&#x27;Deviations&#x27;)/items(42) [MERGE] { \"Status\": \"Closed\" } POST /_api/web/lists/getbytitle(&#x27;Deviations&#x27;)/items(42)/recycle() POST /_api/web/lists/getbytitle(&#x27;Deviations&#x27;)/GetItems { \"query\": { \"ViewXml\": \"<View>…CAML…</View>\" } } POST /_api/web/lists/getbytitle(&#x27;Deviations&#x27;)/RenderListDataAsStream { \"parameters\": { \"RenderOptions\": 2, \"ViewXml\": \"…\" } } Files & folders HTTP Copy GET /_api/web/GetFolderByServerRelativeUrl(&#x27;/sites/q/Shared Documents/SOPs&#x27;)/Files?$select=Name,TimeLastModified GET /_api/web/GetFileByServerRelativeUrl(&#x27;/sites/q/Shared Documents/a.pdf&#x27;)/$value POST /_api/web/GetFolderByServerRelativeUrl(&#x27;/sites/q/Shared Documents&#x27;)/Files/add(url=&#x27;a.pdf&#x27;,overwrite=true) <binary> POST /_api/web/folders/add(&#x27;/sites/q/Shared Documents/2026&#x27;) GET /_api/web/GetFileByServerRelativeUrl(&#x27;/sites/q/Shared Documents/a.pdf&#x27;)/ListItemAllFields POST /_api/web/GetFileByServerRelativeUrl(&#x27;…/a.pdf&#x27;)/CheckOut() /CheckIn(comment=&#x27;x&#x27;,checkintype=1) POST /_api/web/GetFileByServerRelativeUrl(&#x27;…/a.pdf&#x27;)/copyTo(strNewUrl=&#x27;/sites/q/Archive/a.pdf&#x27;,bOverWrite=true) POST /_api/SP.MoveCopyUtil.CopyFileByPath (cross-site copy) Users, groups, permissions HTTP Copy GET /_api/web/currentuser?$select=Id,Title,Email,IsSiteAdmin POST /_api/web/ensureuser { \"logonName\": \"i:0#.f|membership|jane@contoso.com\" } GET /_api/web/sitegroups/getbyname(&#x27;Quality Member"},{"t":"CSOM for .NET with Modern Authentication (MSAL) – After SharePointOnlineCredentials and ACS","c":"apis","u":"articles/apis/csom-modern-auth.html","s":"Use CSOM with Microsoft.SharePointOnline.CSOM on .NET 6/8, acquiring tokens with MSAL (interactive, certificate app-only, managed identity), the ExecutingWebRequest pattern, PnP Framework AuthenticationManager, and migrating old code that used SharePointOnlineCredentials or ACS.","k":"csom, msal, modern authentication, dotnet, pnp framework, app-only","h":"Migrating legacy code · Interview questions · How do you authenticate CSOM on .NET 8 against SharePoint Online? · Why does old code using SharePointOnlineCredentials fail?","l":"Advanced","x":"SharePoint REST & CSOM","b":"Old CSOM samples used SharePointOnlineCredentials (username/password, legacy auth) or ACS app-only. Both are blocked or retired . Modern CSOM uses access tokens from Entra ID. flowchart LR APP[.NET 8 app] --> MSAL[MSAL / Azure.Identity<br/>interactive · certificate · MI] MSAL -->|token for https://contoso.sharepoint.com/.default| CTX[ClientContext] CTX -->|ExecutingWebRequest adds<br/>Authorization: Bearer| SPO[(SharePoint Online)] App-only CSOM with a certificate (MSAL) C# Copy using Microsoft.Identity.Client; using Microsoft.SharePoint.Client; using System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates; var cert = new X509Certificate2( \"deviation-sync.pfx\" , Environment.GetEnvironmentVariable( \"PFX_PASSWORD\" )); var msal = ConfidentialClientApplicationBuilder.Create(clientId) .WithCertificate(cert).WithTenantId(tenantId).Build(); async Task< string > GetTokenAsync () => ( await msal.AcquireTokenForClient( new [] { \"https://contoso.sharepoint.com/.default\" }).ExecuteAsync()).AccessToken; using var ctx = new ClientContext( \"https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/quality\" ); // The event is synchronous: resolve the (MSAL-cached) token synchronously, never with an async void lambda ctx.ExecutingWebRequest += (s, e) => e.WebRequestExecutor.RequestHeaders[ \"Authorization\" ] = \"Bearer \" + GetTokenAsync().GetAwaiter().GetResult(); var list = ctx.Web.Lists.GetByTitle( \"Deviations\" ); ctx.Load(list, l => l.ItemCount); await ctx.ExecuteQueryAsync(); Console.WriteLine(list.ItemCount); Shortcut: PnP Framework AuthenticationManager C# Copy var am = new PnP.Framework.AuthenticationManager(clientId, \"deviation-sync.pfx\" , pfxPassword, \"contoso.onmicrosoft.com\" ); using var ctx = await am.GetContextAsync( \"https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/quality\" ); App-only = certificate SharePoint app-only CSOM/REST requires certificate -based tokens (or managed identity), and the app needs SharePoint permissions ( Sites.Selected + site grant, or Sites.FullControl.All ). # Migrating legacy code Old New SharePointOnlineCredentials(user, pwd) MSAL interactive / device code, or app-only certificate ACS app-only ( TokenHelper.GetAppOnlyAccessToken ) Entra app + certificate (MSAL / PnP Framework) .NET Framework CSOM 16 Microsoft.SharePointOnline.CSOM for .NET Standard on .NET 6/8 # Interview questions Q1 How do you authenticate CSOM on .NET 8 against SharePoint Online? Acquire an access token for "},{"t":"The Microsoft 365 PnP Ecosystem – PnPjs, PnP Core SDK, PnP Framework, PnP PowerShell, CLI and Samples","c":"apis","u":"articles/apis/pnp-ecosystem.html","s":"A guided map of the community Patterns and Practices (PnP) projects, explaining what each library or tool is for, how they relate to Microsoft APIs, support model, community calls, sample galleries and how to contribute.","k":"pnp, pnpjs, pnp core sdk, pnp powershell, cli for microsoft 365, community","h":"Interview questions · What is the difference between PnP Framework and PnP Core SDK? · Is PnP officially supported by Microsoft?","l":"Beginner","x":"PnP Ecosystem","b":"PnP (Patterns and Practices) started inside Microsoft (2013, \"Office 365 Developer PnP\") and became the Microsoft 365 & Power Platform Community : open-source libraries, tools, samples and weekly community calls, maintained by Microsoft employees and MVPs. PnP project map JAVASCRIPT / TYPESCRIPT PnPjs (SharePoint & Graph fluent API) SPFx reusable controls (React) SPFx property controls PnP Modern Search web parts .NET .NET PnP Core SDK (modern, Graph-first) PnP Framework (CSOM extensions, provisioning… PnP Provisioning schema scripting SCRIPTING & CLI PnP PowerShell (cross-platform) CLI for Microsoft 365 (Node, bash/PowerShell) learning SAMPLES & COMMUNITY sp-dev-fx-webparts / extensions samples Script samples List formatting samples Weekly community calls & videos PnP project map Project Use it for PnPjs SPFx/Node data access to SharePoint and Graph with batching, caching SPFx React controls ListView, PeoplePicker, TaxonomyPicker, FilePicker, Charts… SPFx property controls List picker, people picker, collection data in the property pane PnP Core SDK Modern .NET access to SharePoint/Teams (Azure Functions, apps) PnP Framework CSOM-based extensions, provisioning engine, page transformation PnP PowerShell 700+ cmdlets for admins and developers CLI for Microsoft 365 Cross-platform CLI, CI/CD, SPFx doctor and upgrade PnP Modern Search Configurable search pages on modern SharePoint List Formatting samples Ready JSON for columns, views and forms Support model PnP projects are open source and community-supported , not covered by Microsoft Support SLAs. Pin versions, test upgrades, and contribute fixes back when you can. # Interview questions Q1 What is the difference between PnP Framework and PnP Core SDK? PnP Framework extends classic CSOM (and contains the provisioning engine and page transformation). It is the successor of the old PnP Sites Core. PnP Core SDK is a newer, modern .NET SDK built Graph-first with REST fallback, batching and dependency injection, designed for new development. Q2 Is PnP officially supported by Microsoft? PnP is a Microsoft-led community initiative, but its libraries and tools are open source and supported by the community through GitHub issues, not by Microsoft Support. The underlying Microsoft APIs they call remain supported."},{"t":"CLI for Microsoft 365 – Cross-Platform Automation for SharePoint, Teams, Entra and SPFx","c":"apis","u":"articles/apis/cli-for-m365.html","s":"Use the CLI for Microsoft 365 from bash or PowerShell, covering installation, login methods (device code, certificate, managed identity, federated), output formats and JMESPath queries, common SharePoint, Teams and Entra commands, SPFx doctor and upgrade, and scripting in pipelines.","k":"cli for microsoft 365, m365 cli, automation, bash, spfx, ci/cd","h":"Everyday commands · Interview questions · Why choose the CLI for Microsoft 365 over PnP PowerShell? · How does the CLI authenticate in a CI/CD pipeline?","l":"Intermediate","x":"PnP Ecosystem","b":"The CLI for Microsoft 365 ( m365 ) is a Node.js-based, cross-platform command line from the PnP community. It works the same on Windows, macOS, Linux and in CI pipelines. Install, set up an app, log in Bash Copy npm install -g @pnp/cli-microsoft365 m365 setup # guided: creates/uses your own Entra app registration m365 login # device code / browser m365 login --authType certificate --certificateFile ./ci.pfx --password \" $PFX_PWD \" --appId \" $APP_ID \" --tenant \" $TENANT_ID \" m365 status # Everyday commands SharePoint Bash Copy m365 spo site list -- type CommunicationSite --output text m365 spo list get --webUrl https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/quality --title Deviations --output json --query \"{Title:Title,Items:ItemCount}\" m365 spo listitem list --webUrl https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/quality --listTitle Deviations --filter \"Status eq &#x27;Open&#x27;\" --fields \"Id,Title\" m365 spo listitem add --webUrl https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/quality --listTitle Deviations --Title \"From CLI\" --Status Open m365 spo file add --webUrl https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/quality --folder \"Shared Documents/Reports\" --path ./report.pdf m365 spo hubsite register --siteUrl https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/operations Teams & Entra Bash Copy m365 teams team list --joined m365 teams channel add --teamName \"Quality Ops\" --name \"Audits\" m365 entra user list --properties \"displayName,mail,department\" --output csv > users.csv m365 entra group member list --groupDisplayName \"Quality Leads\" SPFx helpers Bash Copy m365 spfx doctor # validate env vs project m365 spfx project upgrade --toVersion 1.21.1 --output md > upgrade.md m365 spo app add --filePath ./sharepoint/solution/app.sppkg --overwrite m365 spo app deploy --name app.sppkg --skipFeatureDeployment JMESPath queries Every command supports --query with JMESPath to filter and reshape JSON output, for example --query \"[?ItemCount > \\ 5000`].{List:Title,Count:ItemCount}\"` to find large lists across a site. # Interview questions Q1 Why choose the CLI for Microsoft 365 over PnP PowerShell? When you work in bash/Linux/macOS or Node-based pipelines, want one tool across SharePoint, Teams, Entra, Planner and SPFx (doctor/upgrade), and like JSON plus JMESPath output for scripting. PnP PowerShell is often preferred by Windows-centric PowerShell teams. Both are PnP projects. Q2 How does the CLI authenticate in a CI/CD pip"},{"t":"PnP Core SDK – Modern .NET Access to SharePoint, Teams and Graph","c":"apis","u":"articles/apis/pnp-core-sdk.html","s":"Get productive with PnP Core SDK, covering DI setup, authentication providers, the PnPContext, loading properties with expressions, LINQ queries over list items, batching, paging large lists, Teams operations, and when to use it vs CSOM or the Graph SDK.","k":"pnp core sdk, dotnet, csharp, sharepoint, teams, batching","h":"Interview questions · What does \"Graph-first\" mean in PnP Core SDK? · How do you send many updates efficiently with PnP Core SDK?","l":"Advanced","x":"PnP Ecosystem","b":"PnP Core SDK is a modern .NET library for SharePoint Online and Teams. It routes calls through Microsoft Graph when possible and falls back to SharePoint REST , with built-in batching , paging , retries and a clean async API. PnP Core SDK architecture YOUR .NET APP / AZURE FUNCTION DI: AddPnPCore() IPnPContextFactory PnPContext per site PNP CORE SDK Domain model (Web, List,… Graph-first with REST fallback Batching throttling retries paging auth providers AUTHENTICATION Interactive X509 certificate Managed identity (custom provider) On-behalf-of APIs MICROSOFT 365 Microsoft Graph SharePoint REST PnP Core SDK architecture Read, filter (LINQ), batch update C# Copy using var ctx = await pnpContextFactory.CreateAsync( new Uri( \"https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/quality\" )); // Load only what you need with expressions await ctx.Web.LoadAsync(w => w.Title, w => w.Lists.QueryProperties(l => l.Title, l => l.ItemCount)); Console.WriteLine( $\" {ctx.Web.Title} : {ctx.Web.Lists.Length} lists\" ); var list = await ctx.Web.Lists.GetByTitleAsync( \"Deviations\" , l => l.Fields); // LINQ is translated to OData var open = await list.Items .Where(i => i.Title.StartsWith( \"DEV\" )) .QueryProperties(i => i.Title, i => i.FieldValuesAsText) .ToListAsync(); // Batch updates foreach ( var item in open) { item[ \"Status\" ] = \"In Review\" ; await item.UpdateBatchAsync(); } await ctx.ExecuteAsync(); // sends the batch (splits automatically) // Large lists: paging with CAML await foreach ( var row in list.Items.QueryProperties(i => i.Title).AsAsyncEnumerable()) { // pages fetched under the hood } Teams: create a channel and post a message C# Copy var team = await ctx.Team.GetAsync(t => t.Channels); var channel = await team.Channels.AddAsync( \"Audits\" , \"Audit coordination\" ); await channel.LoadAsync(c => c.Messages); await channel.Messages.AddAsync( \"Audit kickoff on Monday 10:00 🚀\" ); Choose When PnP Core SDK New .NET SharePoint/Teams code, batching, mixed Graph/REST needs Microsoft Graph SDK Graph-only scenarios across M365 (mail, users, Teams, drives) CSOM / PnP Framework Legacy code, provisioning engine, APIs not yet in PnP Core # Interview questions Q1 What does \"Graph-first\" mean in PnP Core SDK? For each operation, the SDK prefers a Microsoft Graph call when Graph supports it, and falls back to SharePoint REST otherwise. Developers use one consistent API without deciding per call. "},{"t":"OAuth 2.0 & OpenID Connect Flows for Microsoft 365 – Which Flow for Which App?","c":"apis","u":"articles/apis/oauth-flows.html","s":"The authorization flows you'll use with Entra ID, covering authorization code with PKCE, client credentials, on-behalf-of, device code, implicit (legacy), and SPFx's built-in token handling, with token anatomy (aud, scp, roles), scopes vs roles, and common errors.","k":"oauth, openid connect, entra id, msal, on-behalf-of, client credentials, pkce","h":"Token anatomy (decoded JWT) · Common errors · Interview questions · When do you use the on-behalf-of flow? · What is the difference between scp and roles claims?","l":"Advanced","x":"Auth & Resilience","b":"flowchart TD A{Who is the caller?} -->|User in a browser SPA / SPFx| B[Auth code + PKCE<br/>SPFx handles it for you] A -->|User in a server web app| C[Auth code<br/>with client secret/cert] A -->|Daemon / job, no user| D[\"Client credentials<br/>(app permissions)\"] A -->|API calling another API<br/>as the user| E[On-behalf-of] A -->|CLI / device without browser| F[Device code] Flow Token represents Example Authorization code + PKCE User + app (delegated) SPA, mobile app, SPFx (via platform) Client credentials App only Azure Function updating lists nightly On-behalf-of (OBO) User, through a middle-tier API SPFx → Function → Graph as the user Device code User + app CLI tools, PnP PowerShell -DeviceLogin Implicit (legacy) User Old SPAs, so avoid ROPC (username/password) User Avoid (no MFA) # Token anatomy (decoded JWT) Access token claims you check in APIs JSON Copy { \"aud\" : \"api://deviation-api\" , // audience = your API \"iss\" : \"https://login.microsoftonline.com/<tenant-id>/v2.0\" , \"tid\" : \"<tenant-id>\" , \"oid\" : \"<user-object-id>\" , \"scp\" : \"user_impersonation\" , // delegated scopes (space-separated) \"roles\" : [ \"Deviations.ReadWrite\" ] , // app roles (application or assigned to user) \"azp\" : \"<client app id>\" , // calling app \"exp\" : 1791460489 } # Common errors Error Meaning AADSTS65001 Consent missing for the requested scope AADSTS50011 Redirect URI mismatch AADSTS700016 App not found in tenant (wrong client ID or tenant) AADSTS7000215 Invalid client secret (expired or wrong) 401 from API with valid token Wrong aud (token requested for a different resource) 403 from Graph Scope or role missing, or the user lacks access # Interview questions Q1 When do you use the on-behalf-of flow? When a middle-tier API receives a user's token (e.g. from SPFx) and must call another API (Graph, SharePoint) as that user . It exchanges the incoming token for a new token for the downstream resource, preserving the user's identity and permissions. Q2 What is the difference between scp and roles claims? scp lists delegated scopes granted to the app for the signed-in user. roles lists app roles assigned to the application (application permissions) or to the user/group for the API. APIs authorize based on the appropriate claim."},{"t":"Throttling & Resilience for SharePoint and Graph – 429s, Retry-After and Decorated Traffic","c":"apis","u":"articles/apis/throttling-resilience.html","s":"Build integrations that survive throttling, covering how SharePoint Online and Graph throttle (per user, per app, per tenant, resource units), handling 429/503 with Retry-After, exponential backoff, batching and delta to reduce calls, decorating traffic with a user agent, and monitoring.","k":"throttling, 429, retry-after, resilience, graph, sharepoint online","h":"Limits to know (conceptually) · Resilient client (C#) · Reduce load in the first place · Interview questions · What should your code do when it receives HTTP 429 from SharePoint Online? · What is \"decorating traffic\" and why do it?","l":"Advanced","x":"Auth & Resilience","b":"SharePoint Online and Graph protect shared infrastructure by throttling : when a caller exceeds limits, they respond 429 Too Many Requests (or 503 Server Too Busy ) with a Retry-After header. Well-behaved code waits and retries. Badly behaved code retries immediately and gets blocked longer. flowchart LR R[Request] --> S{Throttled?} S -- no --> OK[200] S -- yes --> W[429/503 + Retry-After: 30] W --> D[\"Wait Retry-After<br/>(or exponential backoff)\"] D --> R D -.ignore & hammer.-> B[Longer throttling /<br/>temporary block] # Limits to know (conceptually) Scope What's measured Per user Requests per time window Per app (per tenant) Resource units per minute/day (SharePoint app throttling, weighted by operation cost) Per tenant Overall load on the tenant's farm Graph workloads Service-specific limits (e.g. Teams, Outlook, SharePoint via Graph) Operations cost differently: a delta query or a single-item GET is cheap, while full-list scans, permission-heavy calls and searches are expensive. # Resilient client (C#) HttpClient handler honouring Retry-After with backoff and jitter C# Copy public class ThrottleRetryHandler : DelegatingHandler { protected override async Task<HttpResponseMessage> SendAsync ( HttpRequestMessage req, CancellationToken ct ) { for ( int attempt = 1 ; ; attempt++) { var res = await base .SendAsync(req, ct); if (( int )res. StatusCode is not ( 429 or 503 ) || attempt == 6 ) return res; TimeSpan wait = res.Headers.RetryAfter?.Delta ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(Math.Pow( 2 , attempt) + Random.Shared.NextDouble()); await Task.Delay(wait, ct); req = await CloneAsync(req); // HttpRequestMessage can&#x27;t be resent as-is } } } Decorate your traffic (helps Microsoft classify and prioritize) HTTP Copy User-Agent : NONISV|Contoso|DeviationSync/2.3 # Reduce load in the first place Cheat sheet Fewer, cheaper calls 01 Delta & change tokens Sync changes, not full lists. 02 Batch Graph $batch (20), SharePoint $batch, PnP batching. 03 Select only needed fields $select / ViewFields, avoid expanding everything. 04 Cache Reference data, site/list IDs, user lookups. 05 Off-peak Schedule heavy jobs at night in the tenant's region. 06 App-only for jobs Separate background jobs from user-facing traffic. # Interview questions Q1 What should your code do when it receives HTTP 429 from SharePoint Online? Read the Retry-After header and wait at least that long before retr"},{"t":"How Microsoft 365 Copilot Works – Grounding, the Semantic Index, Permissions and Extensibility","c":"ai-copilot","u":"articles/ai-copilot/copilot-architecture.html","s":"A clear architecture of Microsoft 365 Copilot, covering the orchestrator, grounding through Microsoft Graph and the semantic index, permission-trimmed retrieval, responsible AI and data boundaries, Copilot Chat vs licensed Copilot, and the extensibility model (agents, connectors, plugins).","k":"microsoft 365 copilot, architecture, semantic index, grounding, agents, ai","h":"Architecture · Key facts · Copilot Chat vs Microsoft 365 Copilot · Extensibility · Interview questions · How does Copilot avoid showing users data they shouldn't see? · What is the semantic index?","l":"Beginner","x":"Foundations","b":"Microsoft 365 Copilot combines large language models with your organization's data in Microsoft 365, retrieved through Microsoft Graph and only what the user can already access . SharePoint is its largest knowledge source, so SharePoint quality and permissions directly determine Copilot quality and safety. # Architecture Copilot request flow USER PROMPT Word / Teams / Outlook / Copilot app / SharePoint pre-processing COPILOT ORCHESTRATOR Understand intent Plan retrieval (grounding) Choose agents / plugins retrieve (permission-trimmed) GROUNDING SOURCES Microsoft Graph (mail, chats,… SharePoint & OneDrive (semantic index) Graph connectors (external) Web (if enabled) prompt + grounded context LLM (AZURE OPENAI IN MICROSOFT SERVICE BOUNDARY) Generate response No training on your data post-processing RESPONSIBLE AI CHECKS Citations to sources Sensitivity labels honoured Audit & compliance logging (Purview) Copilot request flow sequenceDiagram autonumber actor U as User participant C as Copilot orchestrator participant G as Graph + semantic index participant L as LLM U->>C: \"Summarize open critical deviations at Plant A\" C->>G: Retrieve relevant content the user can access G-->>C: Snippets from SharePoint lists, docs, emails (trimmed) C->>L: Prompt + grounding data L-->>C: Draft answer C->>C: Compliance checks, citations, labels C-->>U: Answer with references # Key facts Topic Fact Permissions Copilot can only use content the user can already access (no new permissions) Training Prompts, responses and your data are not used to train foundation models Data boundary Processing happens within the Microsoft 365 service boundary, with EU Data Boundary support Labels Sensitivity labels and encryption are respected; outputs inherit labels where applicable Auditing Copilot interactions are captured for Purview audit, eDiscovery and retention # Copilot Chat vs Microsoft 365 Copilot Microsoft 365 Copilot Chat Microsoft 365 Copilot (licensed) Grounding Web (+ agents with org data via pay-as-you-go) Work data via Graph + web In apps Chat experience In Word, Excel, PowerPoint, Outlook, Teams, SharePoint Licence Included for Entra users Per-user add-on # Extensibility SharePoint agents Scoped agents over sites and files. Copilot Studio Low-code agents with knowledge, actions and topics. Declarative agents Instructions + knowledge + API actions, built with the Agents Toolkit. G"},{"t":"AI Builder in Power Platform – Prompts, Document Processing and Prediction in Apps and Flows","c":"ai-copilot","u":"articles/ai-copilot/ai-builder.html","s":"Use AI Builder for practical automation, covering custom prompts (GPT) in flows and apps, document processing (invoices, forms), text and image models, credits and licensing, accuracy and human review patterns, with a deviation triage example.","k":"ai builder, prompts, document processing, power automate, power apps, ai","h":"Example: auto-triage new deviations · Interview questions · How do you use a GPT prompt inside a Power Automate flow? · What does AI Builder consume, licensing-wise?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Foundations","b":"AI Builder brings prebuilt and custom AI models into Power Automate and Power Apps (and Copilot Studio as prompts and tools). Cheat sheet AI Builder model types 01 Prompts Reusable GPT prompts with inputs/outputs (classify, extract, summarize, draft replies). 02 Document processing Extract fields/tables from invoices, receipts, IDs, custom forms. 03 Text Sentiment, category classification, entity extraction, language detection, translation. 04 Image Object detection, text recognition (OCR). 05 Prediction Binary/numeric prediction from Dataverse history. # Example: auto-triage new deviations flowchart LR T[When a deviation is created<br/>SharePoint] --> P[\"AI Builder prompt:<br/>classify severity + area,<br/>suggest root-cause category<br/>(JSON output)\"] P --> C{Confidence /<br/>severity} C -->|Critical| H[Teams card to QA head<br/>for confirmation] C -->|Other| U[Update item with suggestions<br/>Status = Triage] Prompt (with JSON output) Text Copy You classify manufacturing deviations. Input: {Title} and {Description}. Return JSON: {\"severity\": \"Minor|Major|Critical\", \"area\": \"Production|Warehouse|Lab\", \"category\": \"Equipment|Process|Material|People|Environment\", \"reason\": \"<one sentence>\"} Only use the information provided. If unsure, choose the more severe option and explain why. Keep a human in the loop Treat AI output as suggestions for regulated decisions. Store the suggestion, let a person confirm, and log both for audit. # Interview questions Q1 How do you use a GPT prompt inside a Power Automate flow? Create an AI Builder prompt with input variables and (optionally) JSON output, then call it with the Run a prompt action in the flow, passing dynamic values and parsing the response for subsequent actions. Q2 What does AI Builder consume, licensing-wise? AI Builder usage consumes credits (from capacity add-ons or included entitlements, with evolving licensing models). Plan volumes and monitor consumption in the Power Platform admin center."},{"t":"SharePoint Agents – AI Assistants Scoped to Your Sites and Files","c":"ai-copilot","u":"articles/ai-copilot/sharepoint-agents.html","s":"What SharePoint agents are, covering the default site agent, creating custom agents from sites, libraries and files, instructions and starter prompts, where .agent files live, sharing in Teams chats, governance and approval, and design tips for accurate answers.","k":"sharepoint agents, copilot, agents, ai, knowledge, governance","h":"Creating a good agent · Governance · Interview questions · How does a SharePoint agent differ from asking Microsoft 365 Copilot directly? · What makes SharePoint agents give wrong answers?","l":"Beginner","x":"Building Agents","b":"SharePoint agents (introduced in late 2024) are Copilot experiences scoped to specific SharePoint content : a site, a library, a folder or selected files. Instead of asking Copilot about \"everything\", users ask the HR Policies agent or the Deviation SOP agent and get answers grounded only in that content. SharePoint agent anatomy KNOWLEDGE (SCOPE) Site(s) Libraries / folders Specific files Only content the user can access plus BEHAVIOUR Name & description Instructions (tone, rules) Starter prompts Icon stored as .AGENT FILE Saved in a site library Shareable link Versioned like a document used in SURFACES SharePoint (Copilot pane) Teams chats & meetings (@mention) Microsoft 365 Copilot SharePoint agent anatomy # Creating a good agent Open the site → Copilot → Create an agent , or select files/folders → Create an agent . Choose sources narrowly (the Policies library, not the whole intranet). Write instructions : \"Answer only from the provided SOPs. Quote the SOP number. If unsure, say so and suggest contacting QA.\" Add starter prompts users will actually ask. Test with real questions, then share the agent link or add it to a Teams chat. Example instructions Text Copy You are the Deviation SOP assistant for Contoso Quality. - Answer only using the SOPs in the \"SOPs\" library. - Always cite the SOP number and section. - If a question is about an open deviation case, tell the user to check the Deviations app. - Use short, numbered steps for procedures. An agent is only as good as its content Outdated duplicates, draft versions and conflicting documents produce confident but wrong answers. Curate the library (approve and publish, archive old versions, use clear titles) before creating the agent. See Content readiness . # Governance Site owners create approved agents for their sites. Admins can manage agent creation and usage through SharePoint and Copilot settings and pay-as-you-go controls for unlicensed usage. Agents respect permissions: users without access to a source file get no answers from it. Track agent files like documents (owners, review dates). # Interview questions Q1 How does a SharePoint agent differ from asking Microsoft 365 Copilot directly? A SharePoint agent is scoped to selected sites, libraries or files and has custom instructions and starter prompts, so answers are focused and consistent for a specific purpose. General Copilot grounds on every"},{"t":"Copilot Studio – Building Agents with Knowledge, Topics, Actions and Autonomous Triggers","c":"ai-copilot","u":"articles/ai-copilot/copilot-studio.html","s":"Build agents with Copilot Studio (formerly Power Virtual Agents), covering generative orchestration, knowledge sources (SharePoint, Dataverse, websites, files), topics with Power Fx, tools and actions (connectors, flows, REST), autonomous triggers, channels (Teams, M365 Copilot, web), ALM and governance.","k":"copilot studio, power virtual agents, agents, knowledge, actions, power platform","h":"Example: Deviation helpdesk agent · Topics (when you need deterministic logic) · Autonomous agents · Interview questions · What is the difference between knowledge and topics in Copilot Studio? · How does a Copilot Studio agent perform actions such as creating a SharePoint item?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Building Agents","b":"Copilot Studio (Power Virtual Agents until 2023) is the low-code tool for building agents : conversational or autonomous assistants that use your knowledge and take actions through connectors. Copilot Studio agent architecture CHANNELS Microsoft Teams Microsoft 365 Copilot SharePoint / web site Custom apps conversation GENERATIVE ORCHESTRATION Interprets intent Selects knowledge topics tools Asks clarifying questions knowledge KNOWLEDGE SOURCES SharePoint sites & libraries Dataverse tables Public websites Uploaded files Graph connectors actions TOOLS Power Automate flows Connectors (1,000+) REST APIs (custom connectors) Prompts (AI Builder) data & governance PLATFORM Dataverse environment Solutions (ALM) DLP policies Analytics & transcripts Copilot Studio agent architecture # Example: Deviation helpdesk agent flowchart LR U[Operator in Teams] -->|\"How do I report a temperature excursion?\"| K[\"Knowledge: SOP library<br/>(SharePoint)\"] U -->|\"Create a deviation for cold room 2\"| T[Tool: Power Automate flow<br/>→ create list item] U -->|\"Status of DEV-2026-00142?\"| A[Tool: SharePoint connector<br/>get item] T --> R[Confirmation card with link] # Topics (when you need deterministic logic) Topic outline: Report a deviation Text Copy Trigger phrases: \"report a deviation\", \"log a deviation\", \"quality issue\" 1. Question: Which area? (Choice: Production / Warehouse / Lab) → Topic.Area 2. Question: Describe the issue (text) → Topic.Description 3. Condition: if Topic.Description contains \"temperature\" → set Topic.Severity = \"Major\" 4. Call action: flow \"Create deviation\" (Area, Description, Severity, User email) 5. Message: \"Created {Topic.RefNo}. QA will review within 24 hours.\" + link Knowledge first, topics where precision matters Let generative answers handle FAQ-style questions from curated knowledge, and build topics for transactions and regulated flows where wording and steps must be exact. # Autonomous agents Agents can be triggered by events (a new email, a Dataverse row, a schedule) and act without a chat, for example triaging incoming supplier emails into SharePoint and notifying owners. Govern them carefully: restrict tools, add approvals for impactful actions, and review activity logs. # Interview questions Q1 What is the difference between knowledge and topics in Copilot Studio? Knowledge sources (SharePoint, websites, Dataverse, files) let the agent gene"},{"t":"Declarative Agents & API Plugins – Extending Microsoft 365 Copilot with the Agents Toolkit","c":"ai-copilot","u":"articles/ai-copilot/declarative-agents.html","s":"Build declarative agents for Microsoft 365 Copilot with the Microsoft 365 Agents Toolkit (formerly Teams Toolkit), covering the manifest (instructions, conversation starters, capabilities), SharePoint knowledge, API plugins from OpenAPI with authentication, testing and publishing, and when to choose custom engine agents.","k":"declarative agents, api plugins, agents toolkit, microsoft 365 copilot, openapi, extensibility","h":"Interview questions · What are the three main ingredients of a declarative agent? · How does a declarative agent call your API securely?","l":"Advanced","x":"Building Agents","b":"A declarative agent customizes Microsoft 365 Copilot with instructions, knowledge and actions , without hosting your own model. Copilot's orchestrator and LLM do the work, and you declare what the agent knows and can do. Declarative agent package APP PACKAGE (ZIP) manifest.json (Teams/M365 app) declarativeAgent.json ai-plugin.json + OpenAPI (actions) Icons declares AGENT DEFINITION Name description Instructions Conversation starters Capabilities: OneDriveAndShare… WebSearch GraphConnectors CodeInterpreter actions via API PLUGIN OpenAPI description of your API Auth: OAuth (Entra) / API key Response semantics (adaptive cards) runs inside MICROSOFT 365 COPILOT Copilot orchestrator & LLM User permissions Teams / Copilot app Declarative agent package declarativeAgent.json (simplified) JSON Copy { \"$schema\" : \"https://developer.microsoft.com/json-schemas/copilot/declarative-agent/v1.3/schema.json\" , \"version\" : \"v1.3\" , \"name\" : \"Quality Assistant\" , \"description\" : \"Answers SOP questions and creates deviations\" , \"instructions\" : \"You help Contoso operators. Use only the Quality SOP library for procedures and always cite the SOP number. Use the createDeviation action when the user asks to report an issue; confirm details before calling it.\" , \"conversation_starters\" : [ { \"title\" : \"Report a deviation\" , \"text\" : \"I need to report a temperature excursion in cold room 2\" } , { \"title\" : \"SOP lookup\" , \"text\" : \"What is the procedure for line clearance?\" } ] , \"capabilities\" : [ { \"name\" : \"OneDriveAndSharePoint\" , \"items_by_url\" : [ { \"url\" : \"https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/quality/SOPs\" } ] } ] , \"actions\" : [ { \"id\" : \"deviationApi\" , \"file\" : \"ai-plugin.json\" } ] } OpenAPI for the action (excerpt) YAML Copy openapi: 3.0 .1 info: { title: Deviation API , version: 1.0 .0 } servers: [ { url: https://fn-deviation.azurewebsites.net/api } ] paths: /deviations: post: operationId: createDeviation summary: Create a deviation record requestBody: required: true content: application/json: schema: type: object required: [ title , area ] properties: title: { type: string , description: Short description of the issue } area: { type: string , enum: [ Production , Warehouse , Lab ] } responses: &#x27;201&#x27;: { description: Created } Declarative vs custom engine agents Declarative agents reuse Copilot's orchestrator and model, so they are fastest to build and inherit co"},{"t":"Getting SharePoint Ready for Copilot – Oversharing, SharePoint Advanced Management and Content Hygiene","c":"ai-copilot","u":"articles/ai-copilot/copilot-content-readiness.html","s":"A practical readiness program before rolling out Microsoft 365 Copilot and agents, covering finding oversharing with data access governance reports, restricted access control, restricted content discovery, sensitivity labels, site lifecycle, content quality, and a phased rollout plan.","k":"copilot readiness, oversharing, sharepoint advanced management, governance, sensitivity labels, content hygiene","h":"Discover · Protect · Content hygiene (quality of answers) · Phased rollout · Interview questions · What is the biggest SharePoint risk when introducing Copilot? · What is the difference between restricted access control and restricted content discovery?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Readiness & Governance","b":"Copilot doesn't create new access. It reveals existing access . Years of \"Everyone except external users\" grants, org-wide sharing links and public Teams suddenly become visible when anyone can ask \"show me salary documents\" . Readiness is about fixing that, and about content quality. flowchart LR D[1 · Discover<br/>DAG reports, label coverage,<br/>sensitive content] --> P[2 · Protect<br/>fix permissions, labels,<br/>restrict access/discovery] P --> G[3 · Govern<br/>lifecycle, ownership,<br/>access reviews] G --> R[4 · Roll out<br/>pilot → waves,<br/>feedback loop] R --> D # Discover Tool Finds Data access governance (DAG) reports (SharePoint Advanced Management) Sites with \"Everyone\"/EEEU grants, anyone and org-wide links, sensitive labels exposure Purview content explorer / DSPM for AI Sensitive information types, labelled content, AI interactions risk Site permissions reports / PnP scripts Unique permissions and broad grants per site Microsoft 365 usage Inactive sites, ownerless sites # Protect Cheat sheet Protection controls 01 Fix the root cause Remove \"Everyone except external users\" from sensitive sites, convert public Teams to private where needed. 02 Restricted access control (RAC) Limit a site to members of a specific group, even if items were overshared. 03 Restricted content discovery (RCD) Hide a site from Copilot and org-wide search results while keeping access for members. 04 Sensitivity labels Classify sites and files; encryption limits who can open; Copilot honours labels. 05 Default sharing links Switch default to \"Specific people\"; expire anyone links. 06 Site access reviews Ask owners to attest who should have access. Example controls with SharePoint Online Management Shell PowerShell Copy # Hide a sensitive site from Copilot & org-wide search (members keep access) Set-SPOSite -Identity https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/hr -cases -RestrictContentOrgWideSearch $true # Restrict access to the site to one group (restricted access control) Set-SPOTenant -EnableRestrictedAccessControl $true Set-SPOSite -Identity https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/hr -cases -RestrictedAccessControl $true Set-SPOSite -Identity https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/hr -cases -AddRestrictedAccessControlGroups \"<entra-group-object-id>\" # Default sharing link: specific people Set-SPOTenant -DefaultSharingLinkType Direct # Content hygiene (quality of answers) Arch"},{"t":"SDLC for SharePoint & Power Platform Projects – Phases, Documents and Who Owns What","c":"architecture-docs","u":"articles/architecture-docs/sdlc-overview.html","s":"The delivery lifecycle that works for Microsoft 365 solutions, covering discovery, requirements, design (HLD/LLD), build, test, deploy and support, with the documents produced at each phase, Agile vs waterfall hybrids, and quality gates.","k":"sdlc, delivery, documentation, agile, project management, process","h":"Phases and artifacts · Agile, but with design · Quality gates · Interview questions · What documents would you produce for a Power Apps + SharePoint project? · Why is an HLD still needed in Agile low-code projects?","l":"Beginner","x":"Process","b":"SharePoint and Power Platform projects fail for the same reasons as any software project: unclear requirements, missing design decisions and late testing. Low-code makes building faster, so it makes skipping the thinking easier too. This tab gives you the documents and templates that keep projects on track. # Phases and artifacts Delivery lifecycle and documents 1 · DISCOVERY Stakeholder interviews Current process (as-is) Business case produces 2 · REQUIREMENTS BRD (business requirements) FRD / user stories + acceptance criteria NFRs (security, performance) produces 3 · DESIGN HLD (architecture, components, integrations) LLD (lists/tables, flows, screens, APIs) IA & governance produces 4 · BUILD Backlog code & solutions in Git Unit tests Code reviews produces 5 · TEST & DEPLOY Test strategy & cases UAT sign-off Deployment runbook Release notes produces 6 · SUPPORT & HANDOVER System Understanding Document (SUD) Support runbook KT sessions Delivery lifecycle and documents Phase Key document Owner Article Requirements BRD Business analyst + product owner Writing a requirements document Requirements FRD / user stories BA + tech lead Functional spec & stories Design HLD Solution architect / tech lead How to write an HLD Design LLD Tech lead / developers How to write an LLD Test Test strategy, UAT QA lead, business Test strategy & UAT Deploy Runbook, release notes DevOps / tech lead Deployment runbook Support SUD , KT Delivery team System Understanding Document # Agile, but with design flowchart LR D[\"Discovery &<br/>BRD (2–3 weeks)\"] --> H[\"HLD + backlog<br/>(1–2 weeks)\"] H --> S1[Sprint 1<br/>LLD for sprint scope<br/>build · test · demo] S1 --> S2[Sprint 2] --> S3[Sprint n] S3 --> U[UAT & hardening] --> R[Release & hypercare] S1 -.update HLD/LLD as decisions change.-> H Most successful Microsoft 365 projects use a hybrid : upfront discovery and HLD to fix architecture decisions (data store, licensing, security, integrations), then sprints where LLD is written just in time for each feature. # Quality gates Requirements gate : BRD signed, NFRs captured, licensing implications known. Design gate : HLD reviewed by architecture, security and the platform owner. Build gate : code reviewed, solution checker/app checker clean, ALM pipeline in place. Release gate : UAT sign-off, runbook rehearsed, support trained, SUD drafted. # Interview questions Q1 What documents woul"},{"t":"How to Write a Requirements Document (BRD) – Template and Worked Example","c":"architecture-docs","u":"articles/architecture-docs/requirements-brd.html","s":"A practical guide to business requirements documents for SharePoint and Power Platform solutions, covering structure, how to elicit requirements, writing testable requirements with MoSCoW, process diagrams, NFRs, assumptions and risks, plus a complete worked example for a Deviation Management System.","k":"requirements, brd, business analysis, template, moscow, documentation","h":"BRD template · Writing good requirements · Worked example: Deviation Management System (excerpt) · Interview questions · What is the difference between a BRD and an FRD? · How do you make requirements testable?","l":"Beginner","x":"Requirements","b":"A Business Requirements Document (BRD) answers why the solution is needed and what the business needs, not how it will be built. It is the contract between the business and the delivery team, and the basis for design, estimation and UAT. # BRD template Template BRD structure 01 1 · Document control Version, authors, reviewers, approval sign-off, change history. 02 2 · Executive summary Problem, objective, expected benefits in 5–6 sentences. 03 3 · Background & as-is Current process, systems, pain points (with numbers). 04 4 · Objectives & KPIs Measurable goals: \"Reduce deviation closure time from 45 to 20 days\". 05 5 · Scope In scope / out of scope / future phases. 06 6 · Stakeholders & users Roles, volumes, locations, languages, devices. 07 7 · Business process (to-be) Process map with roles, decisions, SLAs. 08 8 · Business requirements Numbered, testable, prioritized (MoSCoW). 09 9 · Non-functional requirements Security, performance, availability, compliance, accessibility, retention. 10 10 · Reporting Dashboards, KPIs, audit needs. 11 11 · Assumptions, constraints, dependencies Licences, platforms, integrations, timelines. 12 12 · Risks Risk, impact, likelihood, mitigation. 13 13 · Glossary & appendix Terms, sample forms, current reports. # Writing good requirements ❌ Vague ✅ Testable \"The system should be fast.\" \"The deviation list loads within 3 seconds for 95% of requests with 50,000 records.\" \"Managers approve deviations.\" \"BR-07: A deviation with severity Major or Critical must be approved by the area manager before QA review; the manager has 2 business days; after that, an escalation is sent to the plant head.\" \"Users can report things.\" \"BR-01: Any employee can report a deviation via web or mobile in under 3 minutes, entering title, area, description, date detected and optional photos (max 5, 10 MB each).\" Use MoSCoW priorities: M ust, S hould, C ould, W on't (this time). # Worked example: Deviation Management System (excerpt) Executive summary Deviations at three plants are reported on paper and tracked in Excel. Closure takes 45 days on average and audit findings cite missing approvals. The Deviation Management System (DMS) will digitize reporting, approvals, CAPA tracking and reporting on Microsoft 365. Objectives ID Objective KPI / target OBJ-1 Faster closure Average closure ≤ 20 days within 6 months OBJ-2 Audit compliance 100% of Major/Critic"},{"t":"Functional Specifications & User Stories – From Business Requirements to Buildable Backlog","c":"architecture-docs","u":"articles/architecture-docs/functional-spec-user-stories.html","s":"Turn a BRD into an FRD and a backlog, covering epics, features and user stories, INVEST, Gherkin acceptance criteria, business rules tables, screen specs, traceability matrices and Azure DevOps setup, with examples from the Deviation Management System.","k":"functional specification, user stories, acceptance criteria, gherkin, backlog, traceability","h":"From BRD to backlog · User story format · Acceptance criteria (Gherkin) · Business rules table · Screen specification (excerpt) · Traceability · Interview questions · What makes good acceptance criteria? · Why maintain a traceability matrix?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Requirements","b":"# From BRD to backlog flowchart TD BRD[BR-07: Major/Critical need manager approval] --> E[Epic: Deviation approvals] E --> F1[Feature: Manager approval] E --> F2[Feature: Escalations] F1 --> S1[Story: Approve from Teams] F1 --> S2[Story: Reject with mandatory comment] F2 --> S3[Story: Escalate after 2 business days] # User story format As a area manager, I want to approve or reject a Major deviation from a Teams notification, so that investigations start without delay. Check INVEST : Independent, Negotiable, Valuable, Estimable, Small, Testable. # Acceptance criteria (Gherkin) Story: Reject with mandatory comment Text Copy Scenario: Manager rejects a deviation Given a deviation DEV-2026-00142 with severity \"Major\" in status \"Pending manager approval\" And I am the area manager for \"Warehouse\" When I choose \"Reject\" without entering a comment Then I see the message \"A comment is required to reject\" And the deviation status remains \"Pending manager approval\" Scenario: Rejection with comment When I choose \"Reject\" and enter \"Duplicate of DEV-2026-00139\" Then the status becomes \"Rejected\" And the reporter receives a notification with my comment within 5 minutes And the approval history records my name, decision, comment and timestamp # Business rules table Rule Condition Outcome R1 Severity = Critical Due date ≤ 7 days, QA head notified immediately R2 Severity = Major Manager approval required; escalation after 2 business days R3 Status = Closed Item read-only except for QA admins R4 Reporter = Owner Not allowed (segregation of duties) # Screen specification (excerpt) Field Type Required Rules Title Text (255) Yes ≥ 5 characters Area Choice Yes Production / Warehouse / Lab Severity Choice QA only Visible to QA role; default empty Due date Date If Major/Critical Must be ≥ today; ≤ 7 days for Critical Photos Attachments No ≤ 5 files, ≤ 10 MB each, jpg/png # Traceability BR Stories Test cases Status BR-07 US-31, US-32, US-33 TC-70…TC-78 ✅ Passed UAT BR-10 US-40…US-44 TC-90…TC-104 🔄 In test # Interview questions Q1 What makes good acceptance criteria? They are specific, testable and written from the user's perspective (often in Given/When/Then form), covering the happy path and key alternative or error paths, with measurable outcomes (status, messages, timings, data recorded). Q2 Why maintain a traceability matrix? To prove every business requirement is implemented "},{"t":"How to Write a High-Level Design (HLD) – Template and Full Example for a SharePoint + Power Platform Solution","c":"architecture-docs","u":"articles/architecture-docs/hld-template.html","s":"A complete HLD guide, covering purpose and audience, architecture overview diagrams, component inventory, data architecture and platform choice, integration, security and identity, licensing, ALM and environments, NFRs, risks and decisions (ADRs), with a worked Deviation Management System HLD.","k":"hld, high level design, architecture, template, solution design, documentation","h":"HLD template · Worked example: Deviation Management System (DMS) · 2 · Architecture overview · 3 · Component inventory · 4 · Data architecture & key decision · 6 · Security model · 8 · Environments & ALM · 10 · Risks · Reviewer checklist · Interview questions · What belongs in an HLD vs an LLD? · What is an ADR and why include it in an HLD?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Design","b":"The High-Level Design (HLD) explains how the solution will be built at the architecture level : the main components, where data lives, how pieces communicate, and how security, licensing and deployment work. Its audience is architects, security, platform owners, the delivery team and technical stakeholders. # HLD template Template HLD structure 01 1 · Introduction Purpose, scope, references (BRD), audience, glossary. 02 2 · Architecture overview One diagram: users, channels, components, data stores, integrations. 03 3 · Component inventory Each component: technology, responsibility, owner. 04 4 · Data architecture Data stores & rationale, entities, volumes, retention, data classification. 05 5 · Integration Systems, direction, method (API, connector, flow), frequency, error handling. 06 6 · Security & identity AuthN/AuthZ, roles, data access model, secrets, compliance controls. 07 7 · Licensing Required licences per user group, premium connectors, capacity. 08 8 · Environments & ALM Dev/Test/Prod, solutions, pipelines, release process. 09 9 · Non-functional design Performance, availability, scalability, accessibility, monitoring. 10 10 · Key decisions (ADRs) Options considered, decision, consequences. 11 11 · Risks & open points With owners and dates. # Worked example: Deviation Management System (DMS) # 2 · Architecture overview DMS – solution architecture (HLD) USERS & CHANNELS Reporters (web, mobile) QA & managers (Teams) Leadership (Power BI) Entra ID SSO EXPERIENCE LAYER Canvas app \"DMS\" (Power Apps) Model-driven app \"DMS Admin\" SharePoint intranet page (SPFx dashboard web part) data DATA LAYER (DATAVERSE – ENV DMS-PROD) Deviation CAPA action Plant Approval history SharePoint document librar… automation PROCESS LAYER Power Automate: approvals & escalations AI Builder prompt: triage suggestion Azure Function: monthly PDF reports insights & ops REPORTING & OPERATIONS Power BI (Fabric link) Application Insights CoE kit monitoring DMS – solution architecture (HLD) # 3 · Component inventory ID Component Technology Responsibility C1 DMS app Power Apps canvas (responsive) Report, view, update deviations C2 DMS Admin Model-driven app QA administration, CAPA, master data C3 Approval flows Power Automate (solution) Manager/QA approvals, reminders, escalations C4 Evidence library SharePoint Online Photos, documents per deviation (folder per record) C5 Report gener"},{"t":"How to Write a Low-Level Design (LLD) – Template and Example (Lists/Tables, Flows, Screens, APIs)","c":"architecture-docs","u":"articles/architecture-docs/lld-template.html","s":"A detailed LLD guide for SharePoint and Power Platform builds, covering naming conventions, data schemas with column specs, security mapping, screen and control specs, Power Fx logic, flow designs step by step, SPFx component and API contracts, configuration and environment variables, error handling and logging.","k":"lld, low level design, technical design, template, schema, flows, documentation","h":"LLD template · Worked example (DMS) – excerpts · 1 · Naming conventions · 2 · Data model: dms_deviation · 4 · Screen spec: scrReport · 6 · Flow design: Manager approval · 7 · API contract (Azure Function: monthly report) · 8 · Configuration per environment · Interview questions · What level of detail should an LLD have? · How do you keep the LLD up to date in Agile projects?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Design","b":"The Low-Level Design (LLD) is the developer's blueprint. Someone who did not attend design meetings should be able to build or maintain the solution from it. It turns HLD components into exact schemas, screens, logic, flows and APIs. # LLD template Template LLD structure 01 1 · Conventions Naming (publisher prefix, flows, controls), coding standards, versioning. 02 2 · Data model Each table/list: columns, types, required, defaults, indexes, relationships, choices. 03 3 · Security mapping Roles/groups → privileges per table/list, column security, sharing. 04 4 · UI design Screens/pages, controls, visibility & validation formulas, navigation. 05 5 · Business logic Rules, formula columns, plug-ins/business rules, calculations. 06 6 · Automation Each flow: trigger, conditions, actions, error handling, connections. 07 7 · Integration & APIs Endpoints, request/response contracts, auth, retries. 08 8 · Configuration Environment variables, settings lists, feature flags per environment. 09 9 · Logging & monitoring What is logged where, alerts. 10 10 · Deployment notes Solution components, dependencies, post-deploy steps. # Worked example (DMS) – excerpts # 1 · Naming conventions Item Convention Example Publisher prefix dms_ dms_deviation Canvas controls type prefix galDeviations , frmDeviation , btnSubmit Flows DMS – [Area] – [Trigger] – [Purpose] DMS – Approval – Row modified – Manager approval Environment variables dms_[Name] dms_EvidenceSiteUrl # 2 · Data model: dms_deviation Column Schema name Type Req. Default / rule Index/notes Reference dms_reference Autonumber ✓ DEV-{DATETIMEUTC:yyyy}-{SEQNUM:5} Alternate key Title dms_name Text 200 ✓ ≥ 5 chars (form) Primary name Plant dms_plant Lookup → dms_plant ✓ From user profile Owning BU set by plug-in Area dms_area Choice (global) ✓ — Production/Warehouse/Lab Severity dms_severity Choice QA — Minor/Major/Critical Status statuscode Status reason ✓ Submitted Draft, Submitted, Pending approval, Investigation, CAPA, Verification, Closed, Rejected Due date dms_duedate Date only Cond. Critical ≤ 7 days Business rule Owner ownerid Owner ✓ QA assigns Days open dms_daysopen Formula — DateDiff('Created On', UTCNow(), TimeUnit.Days) erDiagram DMS_PLANT ||--o{ DMS_DEVIATION : has DMS_DEVIATION ||--o{ DMS_CAPAACTION : \"parental\" DMS_DEVIATION ||--o{ DMS_APPROVAL : \"audit trail\" DMS_DEVIATION { string dms_reference string dms_name "},{"t":"Reference Architectures – Intranet, Document Management, Approval Apps and Inventory on Microsoft 365","c":"architecture-docs","u":"articles/architecture-docs/reference-architectures.html","s":"Four proven solution architectures with diagrams and component choices, covering a modern intranet (hubs + Viva + SPFx), a document management system (content types, retention, approvals), a request and approval app (Power Apps + flows), and an inventory management system (Dataverse + model-driven + integrations).","k":"reference architecture, intranet, document management, approvals, inventory, solution design","h":"1 · Modern intranet · 2 · Document management system (DMS) · 3 · Request & approval app · 4 · Inventory management · Interview questions · How would you architect a modern intranet for 20,000 employees? · When does an approval app need Dataverse instead of SharePoint lists?","l":"Advanced","x":"Design","b":"# 1 · Modern intranet Modern intranet on SharePoint Online FRONT DOORS SharePoint start page Viva Connections (Teams & mobile) Home site with global navigation organised by HUBS Corporate hub (news) Department hubs (HR, IT, Finance, Operations) Regional hubs (multilingual) content SITES Communication sites (policies, news) Team sites (collaboration) Org assets library page templates extensions EXTENSIBILITY SPFx app customizer (header/footer) SPFx web parts (dashboards) ACEs (approvals, tasks) PnP Modern Search governance GOVERNANCE Owners & publishing model Audience targeting Analytics Content lifecycle Modern intranet on SharePoint Online # 2 · Document management system (DMS) Controlled document management (SOPs, policies) AUTHORING Team site \"Quality… Content types: SOP Policy Work instruction Major/minor versioning review & approval WORKFLOW Power Automate approvals (author → reviewer → QA… Effective date & review date publish PUBLISHING Communication site \"Quality Library\" (read-only) Copy approved PDF + metadata Audience-targeted news compliance COMPLIANCE Retention labels (record on approval) Sensitivity labels Periodic review reminders Audit log Controlled document management (SOPs, policies) # 3 · Request & approval app flowchart LR U[Employee<br/>Power Apps canvas] --> L[(SharePoint list<br/>Requests)] L --> F[Power Automate<br/>multi-level approval] F --> T[Teams adaptive cards<br/>approvers] F --> L L --> R[Power BI report<br/>SLA, volumes] Best when users have Microsoft 365 licences only, volume is moderate, and security is per site or list. # 4 · Inventory management Inventory management on Power Platform USERS Warehouse staff (canvas app, barcode scanning, offline) Managers (model-driven) Suppliers (Power Pages) Dataverse DATA Product Warehouse Stock level Stock movement Purchase order logic LOGIC Plug-in: stock recalculation… Flows: reorder alerts PO approvals Business rules integrate INTEGRATION ERP via Service Bus / custom connector Power BI (Fabric link) Inventory management on Power Platform # Interview questions Q1 How would you architect a modern intranet for 20,000 employees? A home site with global navigation, department and regional hubs, communication sites for publishing and team sites for collaboration, Viva Connections for Teams and mobile, audience targeting, PnP Modern Search where needed, SPFx only for gaps (branding header, "},{"t":"The Information Architecture (IA) Document – Sites, Hubs, Navigation, Metadata and Search","c":"architecture-docs","u":"articles/architecture-docs/information-architecture-document.html","s":"How to document SharePoint information architecture, covering site inventory and hub mapping, navigation design, content types and site columns, taxonomy (term sets), naming and URL standards, search configuration and audience targeting, with templates.","k":"information architecture, ia, navigation, taxonomy, content types, documentation","h":"IA document sections · Site inventory template · Content types & metadata map · Naming standards (example) · Interview questions · What goes into an information architecture document for SharePoint Online? · Why prefer metadata over folders in IA?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Design","b":"# IA document sections Section Content Principles Flat structure, one site per topic/team, metadata over folders Site inventory Every site: purpose, template, hub, owners, audience, URL Hub model Hubs, associated sites, hub navigation (3 levels) Global navigation Home site links, mega menu structure Content model Content types, site columns, document templates Taxonomy Term groups/sets, owners, change process Naming & URLs Rules, reserved words, examples Search Promoted results, managed properties mapping, verticals Audience targeting Groups used for targeting, rules Governance Who changes what, review cycle # Site inventory template URL Title Type Hub Owners (2+) Audience Sensitivity /sites/hr HR Portal Communication People & HR hr-comms@ All employees General /sites/hr-cases HR Cases Team (private) People & HR hr-leads@ HR only Confidential /sites/quality Quality Communication Operations qa-comms@ Operations General # Content types & metadata map flowchart TB Base[Contoso Document<br/>Department · Confidentiality] --> SOP[SOP<br/>SOP number · Effective date · Review date] Base --> POL[Policy<br/>Policy owner · Applies to] Base --> CON[Contract<br/>Vendor · Value · End date] TS[(Term store:<br/>Departments, Locations,<br/>Document types)] -.feeds.-> Base # Naming standards (example) URLs: lowercase, hyphens, no dates or people names: /sites/finance-ap , not /sites/Finance AP 2026 . Titles: human-friendly and unique (\"Finance – Accounts Payable\"). Libraries: plural nouns (\"Contracts\", \"SOPs\"). Avoid generic \"Documents\" for curated content. # Interview questions Q1 What goes into an information architecture document for SharePoint Online? Principles, the site inventory and hub model, navigation (global, hub, site), the content model (content types, columns, templates), the taxonomy, naming and URL standards, search configuration, audience targeting and IA governance (who can change what). Q2 Why prefer metadata over folders in IA? Metadata enables multiple views, filtering, search refiners, consistent retention and automation, plus better Copilot grounding. Folders impose a single rigid hierarchy and hide content."},{"t":"Test Strategy & UAT for SharePoint and Power Platform Solutions","c":"architecture-docs","u":"articles/architecture-docs/test-strategy-uat.html","s":"Plan testing that catches real problems, covering test levels (unit, integration, system, UAT, performance, security, accessibility), role-based test accounts, test data, test case templates, Power Apps Test Studio and Power Automate testing, defect triage and UAT sign-off.","k":"testing, uat, test strategy, test cases, quality assurance, power apps test studio","h":"Test with real roles · Test case template · Tools · Defect triage · UAT sign-off · Interview questions · Why must you test SharePoint and Power Platform solutions with non-admin accounts? · What is the difference between system testing and UAT?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Delivery & Operations","b":"Test levels UNIT SPFx Jest tests Plug-in unit tests Power Fx UDF checks then INTEGRATION Flows end-to-end with test data API contract tests Connector auth then SYSTEM Full scenarios per role Negative paths Large data (> 5k items) then NON-FUNCTIONAL Performance Security & permissions Accessibility (WCAG) Mobile finally UAT Business users Real scenarios Sign-off Test levels # Test with real roles Most SharePoint and Power Platform defects are permission defects , invisible when testing as an admin. Create test accounts per persona (Reporter, Plant QA, Manager, Auditor, External) and test every story as each relevant role. # Test case template ID Story Role Preconditions Steps Expected result Result TC-71 US-32 Reject with comment Area manager DEV in \"Pending approval\" 1. Open Teams approval 2. Reject without comment Error \"comment required\"; status unchanged ✅ TC-72 US-32 Area manager same Reject with comment Status Rejected; reporter notified ≤ 5 min; history recorded ✅ TC-80 NFR-01 Plant QA (Pune) DEV from Bangalore exists Search for it Not visible ❌ → BUG-114 # Tools Power Apps Test Studio / Test Engine for automated canvas app tests. Flow test feature with previous trigger data; separate test environment with seeded data. Jest for SPFx; Playwright for end-to-end UI tests on SharePoint pages. Accessibility Insights for WCAG checks. # Defect triage Severity Definition Release impact S1 Critical Blocks core process, data loss, security breach Blocks go-live S2 Major Important function broken, workaround hard Fix before go-live (or approved exception) S3 Minor Workaround exists Can go live with plan S4 Cosmetic UI text/layout Backlog # UAT sign-off UAT is complete when agreed scenarios pass, there are no open S1/S2 defects (or exceptions are formally accepted), and the business owner signs off, with the date and the version tested. # Interview questions Q1 Why must you test SharePoint and Power Platform solutions with non-admin accounts? Admins bypass most permission checks, so security trimming, item permissions, Dataverse role access, flow connection issues and hidden UI elements only show up when testing as real personas. Q2 What is the difference between system testing and UAT? System testing is done by the delivery/QA team to verify the solution meets specifications. UAT is done by business users to confirm the solution supports their real work and is ac"},{"t":"Deployment Runbooks & Release Notes for Microsoft 365 Solutions","c":"architecture-docs","u":"articles/architecture-docs/deployment-runbook.html","s":"Write deployment runbooks that anyone can execute, covering pre-deployment checks, ordered steps for SPFx, Power Platform solutions, SharePoint provisioning and Azure resources, post-deployment validation, rollback, release notes templates and change management approvals.","k":"deployment, runbook, release notes, change management, alm, devops","h":"Deployment order matters · Runbook template · Release notes template · Interview questions · In what order would you deploy a solution with Azure, SharePoint, Power Platform and SPFx components? · What's the difference between release notes and a runbook?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Delivery & Operations","b":"# Deployment order matters flowchart LR A[Azure resources<br/>Bicep: Function, Key Vault,<br/>Storage, App Insights] --> B[Entra apps & permissions<br/>consent, Sites.Selected grants] B --> C[SharePoint provisioning<br/>PnP template: lists, columns,<br/>libraries, pages] C --> D[Power Platform solution<br/>managed import + env vars<br/>+ connection refs] D --> E[SPFx package<br/>app catalog + API approval] E --> F[Post-deploy config<br/>turn on flows, security roles,<br/>seed data] F --> G[Smoke tests] # Runbook template # Step Command / action Owner Expected ✔ 0 Change approved (CAB), comms sent CR-2026-0412 Release manager Approved 1 Backup Export current solution (managed+unmanaged), PnP site template DevOps Files stored 2 Azure az deployment group create -f main.bicep -p prod.bicepparam DevOps Succeeded 3 SharePoint Invoke-PnPSiteTemplate -Path dms.xml DevOps No errors 4 Solution Pipeline \"DMS release\" stage Prod DevOps Imported v1.4.0 5 SPFx m365 spo app add/deploy DevOps Deployed 6 Flows Turn on 9 flows, verify connections Tech lead All on 7 Smoke tests Create test deviation, approve, close QA Pass 8 Comms Release notes to users Release manager Sent # Release notes template Release notes (user-facing) Markdown Copy ## Deviation Management – Release 1.4 (12 Oct 2026) ### What&#x27;s new - Approve deviations directly in Teams (Adaptive Cards). - Critical deviations now require a due date within 7 days. ### Improvements - Faster loading of \"My deviations\" (≈ 2× faster). ### Fixed - Plant QA users could not see deviations reassigned from another plant (BUG-114). ### Known issues - PDF report may take up to 10 minutes to appear after month end. # Interview questions Q1 In what order would you deploy a solution with Azure, SharePoint, Power Platform and SPFx components? Infrastructure and identity first (Azure resources, Entra apps and consents), then SharePoint structures the other parts depend on, then the Power Platform solution with environment variables and connection references, then SPFx packages and API approvals, then configuration (flows on, roles, seed data) and smoke tests. Q2 What's the difference between release notes and a runbook? A runbook is an operational, step-by-step procedure for the people deploying (commands, owners, checks, rollback). Release notes inform users and stakeholders about what changed, what's new, fixed issues and known i"},{"t":"System Understanding Document (SUD) – Knowledge Transfer for Support Teams","c":"architecture-docs","u":"articles/architecture-docs/system-understanding-document.html","s":"How to write a System Understanding Document during project transition or application takeover, covering application overview, architecture, components and data, integrations, jobs and flows, environments and access, known issues, runbooks for common incidents and a KT plan.","k":"sud, knowledge transfer, support, transition, documentation, application support","h":"SUD template · Application inventory row (for portfolios of 50+ apps) · Runbook example · KT plan · Interview questions · What would you include in a System Understanding Document? · How do you structure a knowledge transfer for 50+ SharePoint applications?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Delivery & Operations","b":"A System Understanding Document (SUD) captures everything a support team needs to run an application they didn't build. It is typically created during transition (vendor change, handover to AMS) or when taking over a portfolio of SharePoint applications. # SUD template Template SUD structure 01 Application overview Purpose, business owners, users, criticality, SLAs, business calendar. 02 Architecture Diagram, components, technologies, environments, URLs. 03 Data Lists/libraries/tables, volumes, retention, data owners. 04 Integrations Systems, direction, method, schedules, credentials owner (not the secret!). 05 Automations Flows/workflows/timer jobs: trigger, purpose, owner, run frequency. 06 Security Groups, roles, who grants access, service accounts. 07 Operations Monitoring, alerts, backups, known maintenance tasks. 08 Known issues & workarounds With ticket references. 09 Runbooks Step-by-step fixes for frequent incidents. 10 Contacts & escalation Business, IT, vendors. # Application inventory row (for portfolios of 50+ apps) App Platform Components Criticality Users Owner Last change Deviation Mgmt Power Apps + Dataverse 2 apps, 9 flows, 1 function High 1,500 QA Head 2026-08 Seat Booking SPFx + SharePoint list 1 web part, 2 flows Medium 4,000 Facilities 2025-11 Visitor Mgmt SharePoint 2016 + Nintex 3 lists, 4 workflows Medium 300 Security 2024-02 # Runbook example Incident: Approval emails not received Text Copy 1. Check flow \"DMS – Approval – …\" run history in DMS-PROD (failed/suspended runs?). 2. If connection error → re-authenticate connection reference (service account svc-dms-flow). 3. If runs succeeded → check Approvals in Teams for the approver; check spam/quarantine. 4. If flow turned off → verify licence of service account; turn on; resubmit failed runs (last 24h). 5. Update ticket with root cause; if recurring, raise problem record. # KT plan flowchart LR A[Week 1<br/>Docs review<br/>SUD draft] --> B[Week 2<br/>Shadowing:<br/>incumbent fixes,<br/>we observe] B --> C[Week 3<br/>Reverse shadow:<br/>we fix,<br/>incumbent observes] C --> D[Week 4<br/>Independent support<br/>sign-off] # Interview questions Q1 What would you include in a System Understanding Document? Application purpose and owners, architecture and components, data stores and volumes, integrations, automations (flows, workflows, jobs), security and access processes, environments and"},{"t":"Writing a Microsoft 365 / SharePoint Governance Plan – Template and Decisions","c":"architecture-docs","u":"articles/architecture-docs/governance-plan.html","s":"A practical governance plan template covering vision and principles, roles (owners, champions, CoE), site and Teams provisioning, naming and classification, sharing and external access, lifecycle, Power Platform governance, training, and metrics, with decision tables you can adopt.","k":"governance, governance plan, policy, teams, sharepoint, power platform, template","h":"Decision tables (examples) · Roles · Interview questions · What are the essential decisions in a SharePoint/Teams governance plan? · How do you make governance effective rather than a document nobody reads?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Delivery & Operations","b":"Governance is the set of decisions, roles and controls that let people use Microsoft 365 productively without chaos or risk. A good plan is short, decided, communicated and enforced through settings and automation , not just documents. Governance pillars PEOPLE Executive sponsor Platform owner / CoE Site & Team owners (2+) Champions network decide POLICIES Provisioning Naming & classification Sharing & external access Retention & lifecycle Power Platform DLP enforce CONTROLS Admin center settings Sensitivity & retention labels SAM policies Entra group policies DLP / Managed Environments measure METRICS Adoption Ownerless & inactive sites Oversharing reports Incidents Governance pillars # Decision tables (examples) Topic Decision Who can create Teams/sites Everyone via self-service with naming policy; communication sites via request Owners Minimum 2 owners; ownerless policy notifies members Naming Prefix by type: PRJ- , DEP- , COM- (Entra group naming policy) Classification Labels: Public, General, Confidential, Highly Confidential (default General) External sharing Allowed on PRJ- and EXT- sites only; guest expiry 90 days; no anonymous links Retention 3 years for collaboration; records by label Lifecycle Inactive 12 months → owner attestation → read-only → archive Power Platform Default env personal productivity; DLP per environment; solutions in managed envs Custom code SPFx via CoE review; app catalog controlled by IT # Roles Role Responsibilities Platform owner / CoE Policies, settings, roadmap, approvals of exceptions Site/Team owner Membership, content quality, attestation, sharing decisions Champions Local help, feedback, training Security & compliance Labels, DLP, audits, investigations Service desk L1 support, access requests routing See Governance & compliance features for the platform controls. # Interview questions Q1 What are the essential decisions in a SharePoint/Teams governance plan? Who can create sites and teams and how, ownership requirements, naming and classification, external sharing rules, retention and lifecycle (inactive or ownerless sites), Power Platform environment and DLP strategy, custom code policy, training and support, and how compliance is monitored. Q2 How do you make governance effective rather than a document nobody reads? Turn decisions into settings and automation (naming policies, labels, sharing defaults, lifecycle po"},{"t":"Estimating SharePoint, SPFx and Power Platform Work – Techniques and Reference Numbers","c":"architecture-docs","u":"articles/architecture-docs/estimation.html","s":"How to estimate Microsoft 365 projects credibly, covering work breakdown, complexity-based sizing for lists, flows, apps, web parts and migrations, three-point estimates, contingency, non-development effort (testing, documentation, deployment, hypercare), and a sample estimate sheet.","k":"estimation, planning, effort, project management, wbs, sizing","h":"Approach · Indicative baselines (calibrate with your own history) · Non-development effort (typical share of build) · Interview questions · How would you estimate a Power Platform project quickly but defensibly? · What's commonly forgotten in Microsoft 365 estimates?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Delivery & Operations","b":"# Approach flowchart LR W[Work breakdown<br/>features → components] --> C[Classify complexity<br/>S / M / L / XL] C --> B[Apply baseline hours<br/>per component type] B --> T[\"Three-point adjust<br/>(O + 4M + P) / 6\"] T --> N[Add non-dev effort<br/>test, docs, deploy, PM] N --> R[Contingency 15–25%<br/>by risk] # Indicative baselines (calibrate with your own history) Component Simple Medium Complex SharePoint list/library with views & formatting 2–4 h 4–8 h 1–2 d Power Automate flow 0.5–1 d 2–3 d 5–8 d (state machine, integrations) Canvas app screen 0.5–1 d 1–2 d 3–5 d Model-driven table + forms/views 0.5 d 1–2 d 3 d SPFx web part 2–3 d 5–8 d 10–15 d SPFx extension 1–2 d 3–5 d 8 d Azure Function API 1–2 d 3–5 d 8–10 d InfoPath form conversion 1–2 d 3–5 d 8–15 d Workflow migration (SPD/Nintex) 0.5–1 d 2–4 d 5–10 d Site migration (ShareGate, per site) 1–2 h 0.5–1 d 2–5 d (restructure, issues) # Non-development effort (typical share of build) Activity % of build Requirements & design (BRD, HLD, LLD) 15–20% Testing (system + UAT support) 25–35% Documentation & KT (SUD) 5–10% Deployment & ALM setup 5–10% Project management 10–15% Hypercare 5–10% Estimate risks explicitly List the assumptions (licences available, data quality, SME availability, no premium connectors) in the estimate. When an assumption breaks, you have a basis for a change request. # Interview questions Q1 How would you estimate a Power Platform project quickly but defensibly? Break it into components (lists/tables, apps and screens, flows, integrations, reports), classify complexity, apply calibrated baselines with three-point estimates, add non-development activities (design, testing, documentation, deployment, PM, hypercare) and contingency based on risks, and document all assumptions. Q2 What's commonly forgotten in Microsoft 365 estimates? Testing with real roles and permissions, data migration and cleansing, licensing and approval lead times, environment and ALM setup, documentation and KT, training and change management, and hypercare after go-live."},{"t":"Case Study – Building a Deviation Management System on Power Platform and SharePoint (End to End)","c":"architecture-docs","u":"articles/architecture-docs/case-study-deviation-management.html","s":"A complete worked case study from problem to production, covering requirements, architecture options and decisions, data model, security, apps and flows, document handling, reporting, migration of legacy data, testing, deployment, results and lessons learned.","k":"case study, deviation management, power apps, dataverse, power automate, quality, pharma","h":"1 · Problem · 2 · Requirements highlights · 3 · Options & decision · 4 · Architecture · 5 · Lifecycle (state machine) · 6 · Key implementation details · 7 · Results (after 6 months) · 8 · Lessons learned · Interview questions · Walk me through how you would design a deviation management solution on Microsoft 365. · Why use a state machine for deviation status?","l":"Advanced","x":"Case Studies","b":"A composite, anonymized case This case study combines patterns from real deviation management implementations in regulated manufacturing. Names and numbers are illustrative. # 1 · Problem Three plants tracked deviations in Excel and email. Closure averaged 45 days , approvals were inconsistent, and the last audit raised findings about missing records and late CAPAs. # 2 · Requirements highlights Report from any device in under 3 minutes, with photos. Triage by QA, with manager approval for Major/Critical, escalation after 2 business days. CAPA tracking with owners and due dates. Effectiveness verification. Plant-level data segregation, full audit trail, 10-year retention. Dashboards by plant, severity and ageing. # 3 · Options & decision Option Pros Cons Verdict SharePoint lists + canvas app No premium licences Thresholds, weak row security, audit gaps ❌ Dataverse + canvas + model-driven Security model, audit, scale, model-driven admin Premium licences ✅ Custom .NET + SQL Full control Cost, time, maintenance ❌ # 4 · Architecture Deviation Management – production architecture USERS Operators (mobile canvas app) QA & managers (Teams + model-driven) Leadership (Power BI on intranet) Entra SSO APPS Canvas: report & track Model-driven: QA workbench CAPA admin Custom page: closure wizard Dataverse (BU per plant) DATA Deviation CAPA action Approval Plant Area-manager mapping automation FLOWS & AI Approvals + escalations Teams adaptive cards AI Builder triage suggestions Monthly PDF via Azure Function documents & reporting SHAREPOINT & BI Evidence library (folder per deviation) Power BI via Fabric link SPFx KPI web part on intranet Deviation Management – production architecture # 5 · Lifecycle (state machine) stateDiagram-v2 [*] --> Submitted Submitted --> Triage: QA picks up Triage --> PendingApproval: Major/Critical Triage --> Investigation: Minor PendingApproval --> Investigation: Approved PendingApproval --> Rejected: Rejected Investigation --> CAPA: Root cause found CAPA --> Verification: All actions done Verification --> Closed: Effective Verification --> CAPA: Not effective Rejected --> [*] Closed --> [*] # 6 · Key implementation details Security : BU per plant; Entra group teams map roles; column security on investigation notes. Audit : Dataverse auditing on status, severity and owner; approvals written to an Approval table; retention 10 years. Documents : S"},{"t":"Case Study – Inventory Management with Dataverse, Canvas Barcode Scanning and ERP Integration","c":"architecture-docs","u":"articles/architecture-docs/case-study-inventory-management.html","s":"Design an inventory solution end to end, covering requirements, data model (products, warehouses, stock levels, movements), transactional stock updates with a plug-in, a barcode-scanning offline canvas app, a model-driven back office, reorder automation, ERP sync via Service Bus, and reporting.","k":"case study, inventory management, dataverse, barcode, offline, plug-in, erp integration","h":"Goals · Data model · Architecture · Why a plug-in for stock updates? · Results · Interview questions · How do you prevent negative stock when many users issue items at the same time? · How would you support scanning in warehouses with poor connectivity?","l":"Advanced","x":"Case Studies","b":"A composite case Based on common patterns in inventory projects on Power Platform. Details are illustrative. # Goals Real-time stock per warehouse and bin, with accurate movements (receipts, issues, transfers). Mobile scanning in warehouses with poor connectivity. Automatic reorder alerts and purchase requests. Sync stock with the ERP nightly and on demand. # Data model erDiagram PRODUCT ||--o{ STOCK_LEVEL : \"per warehouse/bin\" WAREHOUSE ||--o{ BIN : contains BIN ||--o{ STOCK_LEVEL : holds PRODUCT ||--o{ STOCK_MOVEMENT : moves BIN ||--o{ STOCK_MOVEMENT : \"from / to\" PRODUCT { string sku string name int reorderLevel money unitCost } STOCK_MOVEMENT { choice type int quantity datetime movedOn string reference } # Architecture Inventory solution WAREHOUSE STAFF Canvas app (barcode scanner, offline profile) Receive / issue / transfer / count screens Dataverse CORE DATA Product Warehouse Bin Stock level Stock movement transactional logic SERVER LOGIC Plug-in on Stock movement create (Pre-op): validate… Post-op: update Stock level atomically automation FLOWS Reorder alert when level < reorder point Purchase request approval Daily discrepancy report integration & BI ERP & REPORTING Async step → Service Bus → Function → ERP Nightly reconciliation Power BI stock dashboard Inventory solution # Why a plug-in for stock updates? Stock levels must change atomically with movements. If two operators issue the last item simultaneously, a flow (asynchronous) could let both succeed. A synchronous plug-in in the same transaction validates availability and updates the stock level, or rolls back. Post-operation plug-in (simplified) C# Copy var mv = (Entity)ctx.InputParameters[ \"Target\" ]; int qty = mv.GetAttributeValue< int >( \"ci_quantity\" ); var type = mv.GetAttributeValue<OptionSetValue>( \"ci_type\" ).Value; // 1 receipt, 2 issue, 3 transfer var level = GetStockLevel(svc, mv.GetAttributeValue<EntityReference>( \"ci_product\" ), mv.GetAttributeValue<EntityReference>( \"ci_frombin\" )); int current = level.GetAttributeValue< int >( \"ci_onhand\" ); if (type == 2 && current < qty) throw new InvalidPluginExecutionException( $\"Only {current} available.\" ); level[ \"ci_onhand\" ] = type == 1 ? current + qty : current - qty; svc.Update(level); // same transaction as the movement # Results 99.2% inventory accuracy (cycle counts) −35% stock-outs in 4 months 2 min average receipt processing (from"},{"t":"SharePoint On-Premises Interview Questions","c":"interview","u":"articles/interview/sharepoint-onprem-qa.html","s":"Farm architecture, service applications, SSOM and CSOM, event receivers, add-ins, workflows, search, upgrades and administration, with crisp answers from basics to architect level.","k":"interview, sharepoint server, on-premises, ssom, farm, questions","h":"Fundamentals · What is the hierarchy of objects in SharePoint Server? · What is a web application? · Difference between a site collection and a subsite? · What are managed paths? · What are Alternate Access Mappings? · What is stored in the configuration database? · Standard vs Enterprise edition? · Architecture & administration · What is MinRole? · Why should Distributed Cache have 1 or 3+ hosts, never 2? · Name important service applications. · What is a service application proxy group? · How do you troubleshoot \"Sorry, something went wrong\"? · What are the six search components? · Crawled vs managed properties? · Why does the list view threshold exist? · Kerberos vs NTLM? · What does i:0#.w|contoso\\jane mean? · Development · SPSite/SPWeb disposal rules? · Update() vs SystemUpdate()? · Why create a new SPSite inside RunWithElevatedPrivileges? · Synchronous vs asynchronous event receivers? · How do you prevent event receiver recursion? · Why is ListItem null in ItemAdding? · What are feature scopes? · Add-SPSolution vs Install-SPSolution? · What is a timer job lock type? · CSOM ExecuteQuery? · Why is X-RequestDigest needed in REST? · Provider-hosted vs SharePoint-hosted add-ins? · Low-trust vs high-trust add-ins? · What are remote event receivers? · Upgrade & lifecycle · Why database attach instead of in-place upgrade? · Supported path 2013 → SE? · What does Test-SPContentDatabase report? · What is zero-downtime patching? · What changed in Subscription Edition? · Which on-prem versions are supported after July 2026? · What is hybrid's first workload usually? · How do you restore a single document after the recycle bin is emptied? · What is the Workflow Manager client?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Technology Rounds","b":"Click a question to reveal the answer. Use Expand all answers for revision mode. Deep dives are linked in the SharePoint On-Premises tab . # Fundamentals Q1 What is the hierarchy of objects in SharePoint Server? Farm → web applications → content databases → site collections → sites (webs) → lists/libraries → items/documents. Service applications are shared across web applications via proxies. Q2 What is a web application? An IIS website plus SharePoint configuration (URLs per zone, authentication provider, app pool, managed paths, policies). It hosts site collections stored in its content databases. Q3 Difference between a site collection and a subsite? A site collection is the top-level container with its own quota, admins, features and recycle bin stage 2, stored in one content database. Subsites live inside it and can inherit permissions and navigation. Q4 What are managed paths? URL prefixes under a web application where site collections are created: wildcard ( /sites/* ) or explicit ( /search ). Q5 What are Alternate Access Mappings? Mappings between public URLs and internal URLs per zone, so SharePoint renders correct links behind proxies and load balancers. Q6 What is stored in the configuration database? Farm-wide settings: servers, services, web applications, solutions, timer job definitions and topology. Q7 Standard vs Enterprise edition? Enterprise adds features such as InfoPath Forms Services, advanced search and BI capabilities (varying by version). Both share the same platform. # Architecture & administration Q8 What is MinRole? SharePoint 2016+ server roles (Front-end, Application, Distributed Cache, Search, Custom, shared roles) that start only the required services and check compliance. Q9 Why should Distributed Cache have 1 or 3+ hosts, never 2? The cache cluster relies on quorum, so two hosts don't provide reliable HA. Use one (small farms) or three or more. Q10 Name important service applications. Search, Managed Metadata, User Profile, Secure Store, Business Data Connectivity, App Management, Subscription Settings, State Service, Usage and Health, Word Automation, Visio. Q11 What is a service application proxy group? A set of service application proxies associated with web applications, used to give different web applications different services. Q12 How do you troubleshoot \"Sorry, something went wrong\"? Take the correlation ID and run Me"},{"t":"SharePoint Online Interview Questions","c":"interview","u":"articles/interview/sharepoint-online-qa.html","s":"Modern sites and hubs, web parts, lists and formatting JSON, permissions and sharing, search, provisioning, governance, Copilot readiness and administration in SharePoint Online.","k":"interview, sharepoint online, modern sharepoint, hubs, formatting, questions","h":"Sites & information architecture · Team site vs communication site? · What is a hub site? · Why flat architecture? · What is a home site? · What does audience targeting do? · Private channel storage? · What is the site storage limit and tenant pool? · Pages & web parts · News vs Highlighted content web part? · Hero web part layouts? · How do you connect web parts? · What controls which iframes can be embedded? · What is an organization news site? · Lists, libraries & formatting · Column formatting vs JSLink? · How do you start a flow from a list column button? · What can view formatting do? · How do you show a field conditionally in the list form? · Can you change the 5,000-item threshold in SPO? · Maximum indexed columns per list? · Content type gallery vs content type hub? · Major/minor versions and content approval? · Security & governance · Sharing link types? · How do M365 group members get site access? · Why avoid item-level unique permissions? · What tools find oversharing before Copilot? · Retention policy vs retention label? · What is the Preservation Hold library? · What does ConditionalAccessPolicy AllowLimitedAccess do? · Why keep custom script disabled? · Provisioning & automation · Site script vs PnP template? · What changed in PnP PowerShell auth in 2024? · How do you speed up bulk item creation in PnP PowerShell? · How do you make a new column usable as a search refiner? · What are Graph connectors? · Platform & modernization · What is SharePoint Embedded? · What retired in SPO on 2 April 2026? · How do you convert classic pages to modern? · What does \"groupify\" mean? · Where are files shared in a 1:1 Teams chat stored? · What are SharePoint agents? · What is SharePoint Premium?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Technology Rounds","b":"Deep dives: SharePoint Online tab . # Sites & information architecture Q1 Team site vs communication site? Team sites are for collaboration (usually M365-group connected). Communication sites are for broadcasting to many readers (no group, publishing layout). Q2 What is a hub site? A site registered as a hub so associated sites share navigation, theme and search scope, and roll up news and events. It doesn't inherit permissions. Q3 Why flat architecture? Independent site collections are easier to secure, move and govern, and hubs replace hierarchy for navigation. Q4 What is a home site? The organization's landing site that powers global navigation (app bar) and Viva Connections. Q5 What does audience targeting do? It filters navigation links, news and web part content by Entra or M365 groups. It is not a security feature. Q6 Private channel storage? Each private or shared channel has its own SharePoint site with separate membership. Q7 What is the site storage limit and tenant pool? Up to 25 TB per site. The tenant pool is 1 TB + 10 GB per licence, plus add-ons. # Pages & web parts Q8 News vs Highlighted content web part? News shows only news posts with news features. Highlighted content shows any content via filters or KQL across scopes. Q9 Hero web part layouts? Tiles (up to 5 in a grid) and Layers (stacked bands). Q10 How do you connect web parts? Configure the target web part's \"Connect to source\" (dynamic data), for example a List web part filtering a Document library web part. Q11 What controls which iframes can be embedded? HTML Field Security settings (allowed domains) at site and tenant level. Q12 What is an organization news site? An admin-designated authoritative news source whose posts get priority and distinct styling. # Lists, libraries & formatting Q13 Column formatting vs JSLink? Column formatting is declarative JSON rendered safely in modern UI. JSLink was classic JavaScript rendering and isn't supported in modern views. Q14 How do you start a flow from a list column button? Use column formatting customRowAction with action: executeFlow and the flow ID (the flow uses the \"For a selected item\" trigger). Q15 What can view formatting do? Add row classes, replace row rendering ( rowFormatter ), define gallery cards ( tileProps ) and custom group headers. Q16 How do you show a field conditionally in the list form? Use a conditional formula on the"},{"t":"SPFx Interview Questions","c":"interview","u":"articles/interview/spfx-qa.html","s":"SharePoint Framework fundamentals, lifecycle, React, property pane, extensions, ACEs, data access with SPHttpClient, PnPjs, Graph and AadHttpClient, permissions, deployment, CI/CD, performance and troubleshooting.","k":"interview, spfx, sharepoint framework, react, questions","h":"Fundamentals · What is SPFx? · What component types exist? · Why can't SPFx hold secrets? · What does the web part manifest id do and why keep it stable? · What is supportedHosts ? · What is the hosted workbench? · Why use nvm? · Lifecycle & UI · Where do you initialize PnPjs or Graph clients? · Reactive vs non-reactive property pane? · How do you load property pane dropdown options lazily? · How do you migrate saved properties after a schema change? · How do you support section backgrounds? · What is dynamic data? · Why unmount React in onDispose? · Extensions · What placeholders can an application customizer use? · Why handle navigation events in application customizers? · How do you show a command only for one selected item? · How is a form customizer bound? · Field customizer vs column formatting? · What are ACE card view and quick view? · Data & APIs · SPHttpClient vs fetch? · PnPjs selective imports? · How do you batch with PnPjs? · What client calls Graph? · How do you call your own API? · Why are approved API permissions tenant-wide? · What is isDomainIsolated ? · How does SPFx call Dataverse? · Two secure ways to use Azure Table Storage? · Deployment & DevOps · includeClientSideAssets ? · skipFeatureDeployment ? · Why bump the solution version? · How do you deploy from a pipeline? · How do you roll back a bad release? · How do you upgrade SPFx versions? · What changed in recent toolchains? · Quality & troubleshooting · How do you debug extensions? · How do you unit test SPFx? · How do you reduce bundle size? · CORS error calling a Function? · What is m365 spfx doctor ?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Technology Rounds","b":"Deep dives: SPFx tab . # Fundamentals Q1 What is SPFx? A client-side development model for SharePoint, Teams, Viva and Outlook. TypeScript and React components run in the page under the user's context and are deployed via .sppkg packages. Q2 What component types exist? Web parts, application customizers, field customizers, ListView command sets, form customizers, Adaptive Card Extensions and library components. Q3 Why can't SPFx hold secrets? The code runs in the browser, so anything in the bundle is downloadable. Put secrets behind an Entra-protected API. Q4 What does the web part manifest id do and why keep it stable? It identifies the component on every page. Changing it breaks existing instances (\"web part not found\"). Q5 What is supportedHosts ? It declares where a web part can run: SharePointWebPart, TeamsTab, TeamsPersonalApp, TeamsMeetingApp, SharePointFullPage. Q6 What is the hosted workbench? /_layouts/15/workbench.aspx on a real site, loading locally served bundles with a real page context. Q7 Why use nvm? Each SPFx version supports specific Node versions, so you switch per project. # Lifecycle & UI Q8 Where do you initialize PnPjs or Graph clients? In onInit() , which runs once and can be async. Q9 Reactive vs non-reactive property pane? Reactive applies each change immediately and re-renders. Non-reactive ( disableReactivePropertyChanges ) uses an Apply button. Q10 How do you load property pane dropdown options lazily? In onPropertyPaneConfigurationStart() , then this.context.propertyPane.refresh() . Q11 How do you migrate saved properties after a schema change? Bump dataVersion and transform in onAfterDeserialize . Q12 How do you support section backgrounds? Set supportsThemeVariants: true , consume the ThemeProvider service, and use semantic colors. Q13 What is dynamic data? A mechanism for connected components: sources implement IDynamicDataCallables and consumers use DynamicProperty . Q14 Why unmount React in onDispose? To avoid memory leaks and stale listeners during SPA navigation. # Extensions Q15 What placeholders can an application customizer use? PlaceholderName.Top and PlaceholderName.Bottom . Q16 Why handle navigation events in application customizers? Modern SharePoint is a single-page app, so placeholders are recreated on navigation. Q17 How do you show a command only for one selected item? In listViewStateChangedEvent , set comman"},{"t":"Power Apps Interview Questions","c":"interview","u":"articles/interview/power-apps-qa.html","s":"Canvas and model-driven apps, Power Fx, delegation, variables and collections, SharePoint and Dataverse CRUD, forms, components, PCF, custom pages, ALM, security and performance.","k":"interview, power apps, canvas, model-driven, power fx, questions","h":"Fundamentals · Canvas vs model-driven apps? · What is an environment? · Standard vs premium connectors? · What is a connection reference? · What is DLP in Power Platform? · Power Fx & state · Set vs UpdateContext? · Named formulas vs OnStart? · What is Concurrent()? · What is a collection? · How do you handle errors in Patch? · What are UDFs? · Data · What is delegation? · Is Search delegable on SharePoint? · How do you set a SharePoint person column with Patch? · How do you set a lookup with Patch on SharePoint? · How do you set a lookup in Dataverse? · Relate and Unrelate? · How do you bulk update efficiently? · How do you build an offline app? · Forms & components · SharePointIntegration OnSave? · How do you close the SharePoint form panel? · Output vs event properties in components? · Component library updates? · How do you make apps responsive? · Model-driven & pro-dev · Business rule scope \"Entity\"? · Where is BPF progress stored? · What are custom pages? · Power Fx in the command bar? · What is PCF? · How does a PCF control notify value changes? · Table permissions in Power Pages? · ALM, security & performance · Managed vs unmanaged solutions? · What causes \"my update didn't apply\" in prod? · Does sharing an app grant data access? · How do you let users perform actions they lack rights for? · Top performance tips?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Technology Rounds","b":"Deep dives: Power Apps tab . # Fundamentals Q1 Canvas vs model-driven apps? Canvas: you design screens and use any connector. Model-driven: the UI is generated from Dataverse metadata (forms, views, site map). Q2 What is an environment? A container for apps, flows, connections and a Dataverse database, with its own security and region (dev, test, prod). Q3 Standard vs premium connectors? Standard (SharePoint, Outlook, Teams) are covered by M365 rights. Premium (Dataverse, SQL, HTTP, custom) require premium licences. Q4 What is a connection reference? A solution component that abstracts the connection used by flows and apps, so you can bind different connections per environment. Q5 What is DLP in Power Platform? Policies grouping connectors as Business, Non-business or Blocked to prevent data exfiltration combinations. # Power Fx & state Q6 Set vs UpdateContext? Set creates global variables. UpdateContext creates screen-scoped context variables. Q7 Named formulas vs OnStart? Named formulas (App.Formulas) are lazy, auto-updating and non-blocking. OnStart runs imperatively before the first screen. Q8 What is Concurrent()? It runs several behaviour formulas in parallel. Q9 What is a collection? A local in-memory table ( ClearCollect , Collect ), for small data, batch edits and offline caches. Q10 How do you handle errors in Patch? IfError(Patch(...), Notify(FirstError.Message, NotificationType.Error)) , plus App.OnError for global logging. Q11 What are UDFs? User-defined functions in App.Formulas with typed parameters for reusable logic. # Data Q12 What is delegation? Pushing query operations to the data source. Non-delegable formulas only process the first N rows (500 by default, up to 2,000). Q13 Is Search delegable on SharePoint? No. Use StartsWith on indexed columns or server-side search. Q14 How do you set a SharePoint person column with Patch? A record with Claims: \"i:0#.f|membership|\" & email plus DisplayName, Email and the other properties. Q15 How do you set a lookup with Patch on SharePoint? { Id: x, Value: \"text\" } . Q16 How do you set a lookup in Dataverse? Assign the related record, e.g. Plant: ddPlant.Selected . Q17 Relate and Unrelate? They manage 1:N or N:N associations in Dataverse. Q18 How do you bulk update efficiently? Patch(DataSource, tableOfRecords) instead of ForAll + Patch. Q19 How do you build an offline app? Use the Dataverse offline p"},{"t":"Power Automate Interview Questions","c":"interview","u":"articles/interview/power-automate-qa.html","s":"Flow types, triggers and conditions, expressions, SharePoint connector and HTTP, approvals, error handling, loops and performance, large lists, child flows, ALM, limits, Dataverse, Teams cards and legacy workflow migration.","k":"interview, power automate, flows, approvals, expressions, questions","h":"Fundamentals · Types of flows? · How is Power Automate related to Logic Apps? · Run history retention? · Maximum run duration? · Triggers & expressions · How do you prevent a \"created or modified\" flow from looping? · Where are trigger conditions evaluated? · body() vs outputs() ? · Why are dates \"wrong\" in flows? · What does ? do in triggerOutputs()?['body/Title'] ? · How do you give a default for empty values? · SharePoint · Filter Get items by a person column? · Get more than 5,000 items? · Is \"Send an HTTP request to SharePoint\" premium? · How do you set item-level permissions from a flow? · How do you set Created/Modified during migration? · Approvals & Teams · Approval types? · How do you add reminders? · Where is approval data stored? · Adaptive card vs approval? · Patterns & performance · Implement try/catch? · Why end with Terminate Failed after handling? · Apply to each performance? · Select vs Apply to each? · Parse JSON \"expected string but got null\"? · Child flow requirements? · How do you call Graph with app permissions? · Enterprise · Why build flows in solutions? · What is the CoE Starter Kit? · What happens with approvals waiting more than 30 days? · Dataverse trigger loop prevention? · Flow vs plug-in? · Attended vs unattended desktop flows? · Replace SPD \"Wait for field change\"? · Replace App Step or impersonation?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Technology Rounds","b":"Deep dives: Power Automate tab . # Fundamentals Q1 Types of flows? Automated cloud, instant cloud, scheduled cloud, desktop flows (RPA), business process flows, and process mining. Q2 How is Power Automate related to Logic Apps? Same engine and Workflow Definition Language. Logic Apps targets Azure developers and integration (ARM, VNet, Azure billing). Q3 Run history retention? 28 days. Q4 Maximum run duration? 30 days. # Triggers & expressions Q5 How do you prevent a \"created or modified\" flow from looping? Trigger conditions excluding the service account editor or checking a flag or status column. Q6 Where are trigger conditions evaluated? At the trigger, before a run is created (no run or quota consumed). Q7 body() vs outputs() ? outputs() returns the full output (status, headers, body). body() returns only the body. Q8 Why are dates \"wrong\" in flows? They are UTC. Convert with convertFromUtc(value, 'India Standard Time', format) . Q9 What does ? do in triggerOutputs()?['body/Title'] ? Null-safe navigation: it returns null instead of failing when the property is missing. Q10 How do you give a default for empty values? coalesce(value, 'default') . # SharePoint Q11 Filter Get items by a person column? Owner/EMail eq 'jane@contoso.com' in Filter Query. Q12 Get more than 5,000 items? Enable pagination with a threshold, or loop with ID gt lastId , ordered by ID, Top 5000. Q13 Is \"Send an HTTP request to SharePoint\" premium? No. It's part of the standard SharePoint connector (the generic HTTP action is premium). Q14 How do you set item-level permissions from a flow? SharePoint HTTP: break inheritance, resolve principal and role IDs, then addroleassignment . Q15 How do you set Created/Modified during migration? ValidateUpdateListItem with Author, Editor and date form values, with bNewDocumentUpdate: true . # Approvals & Teams Q16 Approval types? Everyone must approve, first to respond, and custom responses (wait for all or one). Q17 How do you add reminders? Create an approval, then in parallel branches run Wait for an approval and a Do until loop with Delay plus reminders. Q18 Where is approval data stored? In Dataverse (Approvals tables) in the environment. Q19 Adaptive card vs approval? Approvals for standard decisions with tracking. Adaptive cards for custom inputs and layouts. # Patterns & performance Q20 Implement try/catch? Try, Catch and Finally scopes w"},{"t":"Dataverse Interview Questions","c":"interview","u":"articles/interview/dataverse-qa.html","s":"Tables and columns, relationships and cascade, the security model, Web API and SDK, plug-ins, formula and rollup columns, auditing, integration, capacity and limits.","k":"interview, dataverse, security roles, plug-ins, web api, questions","h":"Data modeling · What was Dataverse previously called? · User-owned vs organization-owned tables? · What are alternate keys for? · Native vs manual N:N? · What does Restrict delete do? · Formula vs rollup column? · Virtual vs elastic tables? · Security · Access levels in security roles? · Owner team vs access team? · How do you hide a column from some users? · What is an Entra ID group team? · Development · How do you set a lookup in the Web API? · How do you prevent PATCH from creating rows? · How do you get choice labels? · Pipeline stages? · Pre/post images? · How do you show a friendly error from a plug-in? · Why use filtering attributes? · What are custom APIs? · FetchXML vs OData? · Administration & integration · Three levels for auditing? · Capacity types? · Service protection limits response? · Reliable outbound integration? · Synapse Link vs Fabric link?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Technology Rounds","b":"Deep dives: Dataverse tab . # Data modeling Q1 What was Dataverse previously called? Common Data Service (renamed November 2020). Q2 User-owned vs organization-owned tables? User/team-owned tables have an Owner and support depth-based access. Organization-owned tables are all-or-nothing access. Q3 What are alternate keys for? Uniquely identifying rows by business keys for integrations, upserts and lookup binding. Q4 Native vs manual N:N? Native uses a hidden intersect table with no extra columns. Manual uses your own table, so you can add columns. Q5 What does Restrict delete do? It blocks deleting a parent while children exist. Q6 Formula vs rollup column? Formula: Power Fx computed at read time from the row and parents. Rollup: aggregates children asynchronously (hourly or on demand). Q7 Virtual vs elastic tables? Virtual tables surface external data without copying. Elastic tables are Cosmos DB–backed for massive volumes with TTL. # Security Q8 Access levels in security roles? None, User, Business Unit, Parent: Child BUs, Organization. Q9 Owner team vs access team? Owner teams own rows and have roles. Access teams share specific rows with a dynamic set of users. Q10 How do you hide a column from some users? Column-level security with column security profiles. Q11 What is an Entra ID group team? A team linked to an Entra group, so membership and roles flow from the group. # Development Q12 How do you set a lookup in the Web API? \"nav@odata.bind\": \"/entityset(guid)\" using the navigation property name. Q13 How do you prevent PATCH from creating rows? Add If-Match: * . Q14 How do you get choice labels? Prefer: odata.include-annotations=\"OData.Community.Display.V1.FormattedValue\" . Q15 Pipeline stages? Pre-validation (10), pre-operation (20, in transaction), post-operation (40, sync in transaction or async). Q16 Pre/post images? Snapshots of column values before or after the operation, registered on steps. Q17 How do you show a friendly error from a plug-in? Throw InvalidPluginExecutionException in a synchronous step. Q18 Why use filtering attributes? So update steps run only when relevant columns change, which improves performance and prevents loops. Q19 What are custom APIs? Custom messages with typed parameters implemented by plug-ins, callable from the Web API, SDK and flows. Q20 FetchXML vs OData? FetchXML supports aggregates, complex joins and outer join"},{"t":"Nintex Interview Questions","c":"interview","u":"articles/interview/nintex-qa.html","s":"Nintex Workflow and Forms on-premises, Nintex for Office 365, Nintex Automation Cloud (start events, tasks, branch by stage, connections, Xtensions, component workflows), DocGen and migration to NAC.","k":"interview, nintex, nac, nintex forms, workflow, questions","h":"Platform · What is Nintex Automation Cloud? · Nintex for Office 365 vs NAC? · What did Nintex acquire around 2019–2020? · Nintex for SharePoint (on-prem) · What is LazyApproval? · What are workflow constants? · What are UDAs? · Request approval vs Flexi task? · Why use a state machine? · How do you call REST from Nintex on-prem? · How do you keep a Nintex farm healthy? · How do you find all Nintex workflows? · Nintex Forms · Formatting vs validation rules? · Where is repeating section data stored? · How do you access controls in JavaScript (classic)? · How do you extend NAC forms with custom UI? · Nintex Automation Cloud · NAC start events? · Express approval vs Assign a task? · State machine in NAC? · What is a connection? · What is an Xtension? · Component workflows? · How does NAC reach on-prem systems? · DocGen repeating tables? · Migration · Biggest difference migrating O365 → NAC? · How do you handle in-flight instances? · What do you inventory? · Common unsupported items? · Nintex vs Power Automate? · Do Nintex on-prem workflows run in SPO after content migration?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Technology Rounds","b":"Deep dives: Nintex tab . # Platform Q1 What is Nintex Automation Cloud? Nintex's SaaS automation platform (formerly Nintex Workflow Cloud) with workflows, forms, connectors, DocGen, e-sign and Xtensions, independent of SharePoint. Q2 Nintex for Office 365 vs NAC? O365 is list-bound via the SharePoint app model and runs in Nintex's cloud. NAC is independent, with connectors and richer features. Q3 What did Nintex acquire around 2019–2020? Promapp (Process Manager), EnableSoft (RPA) and K2. # Nintex for SharePoint (on-prem) Q4 What is LazyApproval? Approving or rejecting by replying to the task email with keywords. Q5 What are workflow constants? Reusable values and credentials at farm, web app, site collection or site scope. Q6 What are UDAs? User Defined Actions: reusable parameterized sub-workflows. Q7 Request approval vs Flexi task? Request approval offers approve/reject. Flexi task has custom outcomes and branching. Q8 Why use a state machine? For processes with loops and rework where each state sets the next. Q9 How do you call REST from Nintex on-prem? The Web request action with headers, body and constant credentials, then Query JSON/XML on the response. Q10 How do you keep a Nintex farm healthy? Purge history and progress data with NWAdmin, avoid pauses in loops, and monitor timer jobs. Q11 How do you find all Nintex workflows? NWAdmin.exe -o FindWorkflows with a scope (web application or farm). # Nintex Forms Q12 Formatting vs validation rules? Formatting rules hide or disable when the condition is true. Validation rules mark the control invalid with a message. Q13 Where is repeating section data stored? As XML in a connected multi-line text column, parsed with Query XML. Q14 How do you access controls in JavaScript (classic)? Set a Client ID JavaScript variable name and use NWF$('#' + varName) inside RegisterAfterReady . Q15 How do you extend NAC forms with custom UI? Custom plugins as web components with getMetaConfig() and ntx-value-change events. # Nintex Automation Cloud Q16 NAC start events? Nintex form, SharePoint events, schedule, component workflow, external systems and public forms. Q17 Express approval vs Assign a task? Express: quick approve/reject by email. Assign a task: custom outcomes, a form, multiple assignees and escalation. Q18 State machine in NAC? Branch by stage with Go to stage actions. Q19 What is a connection? Stored authent"},{"t":"SharePoint Migration & ShareGate Interview Questions","c":"interview","u":"articles/interview/migration-qa.html","s":"Migration strategy, assessment, ShareGate features and PowerShell, mappings, incremental cut-over, SPMT and Migration Manager, scenarios (on-prem, tenant-to-tenant, file shares), throttling, workflows and validation.","k":"interview, migration, sharegate, spmt, cutover, questions","h":"Strategy & assessment · What are the phases of a migration? · Lift-and-shift vs restructure? · Top risks found in assessment? · What is SMAT? · Why migrate in waves? · ShareGate · Copy structure vs copy content? · What is Insane mode? · How do you make re-runs safe? · How do you handle departed users? · How do you turn folders into metadata? · How do you script hundreds of sites? · Why migrate the term store first? · Microsoft tools & scenarios · SPMT vs Migration Manager? · Supported native path 2010 → SE? · What's hard about tenant-to-tenant? · What does native cross-tenant SharePoint migration leave behind? · File share permissions approach? · Where should H: drives go? · Execution & validation · Three-pass model? · Why set the source read-only at cut-over? · Why turn flows off during migration? · What slows migrations? · How do you handle throttling? · How do you prove completeness? · What doesn't migrate automatically? · Classic pages to modern? · Key contents of a runbook? · What do you do with workflow history? · What if counts don't match? · How do you decommission the source?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Technology Rounds","b":"Deep dives: Migration tab . # Strategy & assessment Q1 What are the phases of a migration? Assess, plan, migrate (pilot → waves → incremental → cut-over), validate, and adopt and decommission. Q2 Lift-and-shift vs restructure? Lift-and-shift keeps the structure (fast). Restructure redesigns IA (for example flattening subsites into hubs). Q3 Top risks found in assessment? Large lists and libraries, long paths, item-level permissions, orphaned users, customizations (WSPs, workflows, InfoPath, add-ins), checked-out files and heavy versions. Q4 What is SMAT? The SharePoint Migration Assessment Tool for on-prem farms, which produces risk CSVs. Q5 Why migrate in waves? To reduce risk, learn from the pilot, keep freeze windows short and manage support load. # ShareGate Q6 Copy structure vs copy content? Structure recreates sites and lists (optionally with content). Content copies items into existing lists with filters and mappings. Q7 What is Insane mode? Copying through the SharePoint Migration API via Azure for speed and less throttling. Q8 How do you make re-runs safe? -Merge for structure and IncrementalUpdate copy settings for content. Q9 How do you handle departed users? Map them to a placeholder account in user mappings. Q10 How do you turn folders into metadata? Property mapping in Copy content to set columns, then copy into a flatter library. Q11 How do you script hundreds of sites? ShareGate PowerShell driven by CSV wave files, with mapping files, Insane mode, reports per site and parallel sessions. Q12 Why migrate the term store first? Managed metadata values reference term GUIDs, which must exist in the target. # Microsoft tools & scenarios Q13 SPMT vs Migration Manager? SPMT is a desktop tool for SharePoint Server and file shares. Migration Manager is centralized with agents and cloud connectors (Box, Dropbox, Google). Q14 Supported native path 2010 → SE? 2010 → 2013 → 2016 → SE via database attach, or a third-party tool. Q15 What's hard about tenant-to-tenant? Identity mapping, domain moves, Teams and OneDrive coordination, sharing links and licences. Q16 What does native cross-tenant SharePoint migration leave behind? A redirect at the source URL. Q17 File share permissions approach? Simplify to site and library level with groups. Don't copy chaotic NTFS ACLs one-to-one. Q18 Where should H: drives go? OneDrive. # Execution & validation Q19 Three-pass"},{"t":"Azure, Entra ID & Microsoft Graph Interview Questions","c":"interview","u":"articles/interview/azure-graph-qa.html","s":"App registrations and permissions, Sites.Selected, managed identity and Key Vault, Azure Functions, webhooks, storage choices, messaging, APIM, monitoring, Graph APIs, batching, delta, change notifications, OAuth flows and throttling.","k":"interview, azure, entra id, microsoft graph, oauth, questions","h":"Identity · App registration vs enterprise application? · Delegated vs application permissions? · Why certificates for SharePoint app-only? · What is Sites.Selected? · Benefits of managed identity? · How do you give a managed identity Graph permissions? · scp vs roles claims? · When to use on-behalf-of? · Azure services · Functions hosting plans? · Replacement for SharePoint timer jobs? · Webhook validation? · Max webhook subscription lifetime? · Why queue webhook notifications? · How do you know what changed after a webhook? · Table Storage index? · User-delegation SAS? · Service Bus vs Event Grid? · Why APIM in front of Functions? · Logic Apps vs Power Automate? · How do you correlate SPFx and Function telemetry? · Microsoft Graph · Graph vs SharePoint REST? · Set a lookup column with Graph? · When use upload sessions? · Graph batch limit? · Delta query? · Graph change notification lifecycle events? · Graph Search refiners? · Handling 429? · Decorated traffic?","l":"Advanced","x":"Technology Rounds","b":"Deep dives: Azure tab · Graph, REST & PnP tab . # Identity Q1 App registration vs enterprise application? The app registration is the global definition (credentials, permissions requested). The enterprise app (service principal) is the per-tenant instance with consents and assignments. Q2 Delegated vs application permissions? Delegated: the app acts for a signed-in user (intersection of rights). Application: app-only, tenant-wide, always admin consent. Q3 Why certificates for SharePoint app-only? SharePoint REST and CSOM reject app-only tokens obtained with client secrets. Certificates (or managed identity) are required. Q4 What is Sites.Selected? An application permission with no access until per-site roles (read, write, manage, fullcontrol) are granted. Q5 Benefits of managed identity? No secrets in code or config. Azure manages the credentials for tokens to Key Vault, Storage, SQL and Graph. Q6 How do you give a managed identity Graph permissions? App role assignment via Microsoft Graph PowerShell. Q7 scp vs roles claims? scp holds delegated scopes. roles holds app roles (application permissions or assigned roles). Q8 When to use on-behalf-of? When a middle-tier API calls another API as the user (for example SPFx → Function → Graph). # Azure services Q9 Functions hosting plans? Consumption/Flex (event-driven), Premium (VNet, no cold start), Dedicated. Q10 Replacement for SharePoint timer jobs? Timer-triggered Azure Functions with an Entra app or MI and Sites.Selected. Q11 Webhook validation? Echo validationtoken as text/plain within 5 seconds. Q12 Max webhook subscription lifetime? 180 days, so renewal is required. Q13 Why queue webhook notifications? To respond quickly (5-second limit) and process reliably with retries. Q14 How do you know what changed after a webhook? GetChanges with a stored change token. Q15 Table Storage index? PartitionKey + RowKey only. Q16 User-delegation SAS? A SAS signed with an Entra-issued delegation key. No account keys and short-lived. Q17 Service Bus vs Event Grid? Service Bus brokers messages (queues, topics, sessions). Event Grid distributes events with push delivery. Q18 Why APIM in front of Functions? JWT validation, rate limits, caching, transformation, versioning and export to Power Platform connectors. Q19 Logic Apps vs Power Automate? Same engine. Logic Apps is for IT and integration (IaC, VNet, Azure Monitor). Powe"},{"t":"InfoPath, SharePoint Designer & Legacy Workflow Interview Questions","c":"interview","u":"articles/interview/infopath-legacy-qa.html","s":"InfoPath architecture and data, rules and data connections, Forms Services, retirement and migration paths, SharePoint 2010/2013 workflows, SPD actions and mapping to Power Automate.","k":"interview, infopath, sharepoint designer, legacy workflows, questions","h":"InfoPath · What is an XSN? · Form library form vs list form? · What are promoted properties? · Where does InfoPath logic hide? · What is a UDCX file? · What is an administrator-approved template? · Which service app does InfoPath Forms Services depend on? · How do you migrate repeating tables? · What replaced InfoPath? · Legacy workflows · Where do 2010 workflows run? · Where do 2013 workflows run? · 2013 workflow capabilities over 2010? · When were they retired in SPO? · What does App Step do? · How do you replace \"Wait for field change\"?","l":"Intermediate","x":"Technology Rounds","b":"Deep dives: InfoPath & Legacy tab . # InfoPath Q1 What is an XSN? The InfoPath form template: a cabinet file with schema, views, manifest, rules, connections and optional code. Q2 Form library form vs list form? Form libraries store XML files (with promoted fields). List forms store data in list columns. Q3 What are promoted properties? XML fields surfaced as library columns for views, search and workflows. Q4 Where does InfoPath logic hide? Rules (validation, formatting, action), form load and submit rules, view switching, formulas, data connections and code-behind. Q5 What is a UDCX file? A centrally managed data connection file in a data connection library (endpoint and auth info). Q6 What is an administrator-approved template? A full-trust or code-containing XSN deployed by a farm admin through Central Administration. Q7 Which service app does InfoPath Forms Services depend on? The State Service. Q8 How do you migrate repeating tables? To child lists or tables with a parent lookup, edited via galleries or collections (Power Apps) or NAC repeating sections. Q9 What replaced InfoPath? Power Apps (canvas or customized list forms), form formatting, NAC forms and SPFx form customizers, depending on complexity. # Legacy workflows Q10 Where do 2010 workflows run? Inside SharePoint on WF 3.5 (web app and timer service). Q11 Where do 2013 workflows run? In Workflow Manager (now SharePoint Workflow Manager) outside SharePoint. Q12 2013 workflow capabilities over 2010? Stages with transitions, loops, dictionaries, Call HTTP Web Service, App Step. Q13 When were they retired in SPO? 2010 workflows in 2020. 2013 workflows on 2 April 2026. Q14 What does App Step do? It runs the enclosed actions with the workflow app's permissions. Q15 How do you replace \"Wait for field change\"? A separate flow triggered on modification with a trigger condition."},{"t":"Scenario-Based Interview Questions","c":"interview","u":"articles/interview/scenario-based-qa.html","s":"Practical scenarios interviewers love, covering slow lists, failing flows, permission leaks, migration blockers, add-in retirement, Copilot oversharing, approval escalations, InfoPath replacement, SPFx failures and production incidents, with structured answers.","k":"interview, scenarios, troubleshooting, architecture, real world, questions","h":"Performance & data · A list with 80,000 items shows threshold errors in a view. What do you do? · A canvas app on SharePoint returns incomplete search results. · Users say the intranet home page is slow. · Automation · An approval flow sends duplicate emails. · A critical flow stopped running over the weekend. · Business wants approvals that can wait 60 days. · Security & governance · An employee found salary files through Copilot. · A partner should see only one library on a project site. · Audit asks who changed a deviation's severity last year. · Migration & modernization · Add-ins stopped working in April 2026. What now? · ShareGate reports thousands of \"user not found\" warnings. · After migration, managed metadata columns are empty. · A 2-million-file library must move quickly. · 60 InfoPath forms must be replaced in 6 months. · SPFx & integration · An SPFx web part works for admins but not users. · An SPFx web part must call SQL with secrets. · Users see \"web part not found\" after deployment. · Webhook processing randomly misses changes. · Delivery · A stakeholder wants a \"quick Power App\" for 5,000 users with premium data. · Your sprint demo failed because of environment differences. · The client wants everything migrated \"as-is\" including 15 years of files. · Production incident: SharePoint site returns 403 for a department. · A Nintex O365 workflow must move to NAC in 2 weeks. · How do you decide between Power Automate and Azure Functions?","l":"Advanced","x":"Scenario & Leadership Rounds","b":"Answer scenarios with structure: clarify → diagnose → options → recommendation → prevention . # Performance & data Q1 A list with 80,000 items shows threshold errors in a view. What do you do? Identify the filter and sort columns, index them (script it if the UI blocks), make the first filter indexed and selective, use folders or metadata navigation if needed, and archive closed items. For code, page with RowLimit and indexed OData filters. Q2 A canvas app on SharePoint returns incomplete search results. Delegation. Search isn't delegable, so only the first 500–2,000 rows are searched. Use StartsWith on indexed columns, server-side search via a flow, or move to Dataverse. Q3 Users say the intranet home page is slow. Measure with Page Diagnostics and the dev dashboard. Look for heavy SPFx bundles, too many web parts and roll-ups, large images, Embed iframes and unoptimized Highlighted content scopes. Fix with lazy loading, fewer web parts, image optimization and caching. # Automation Q4 An approval flow sends duplicate emails. Probably a \"created or modified\" trigger re-firing on the flow's own update, or concurrent runs. Add trigger conditions (status, editor), set concurrency control, and make the flow idempotent. Q5 A critical flow stopped running over the weekend. Check run history and the connection (expired password or licence of the owner?), whether the flow is turned off (owner left?), and throttling. Fix by moving to a solution with a service-account connection reference, co-owners and failure alerts. Q6 Business wants approvals that can wait 60 days. Flows max out at 30 days. Persist status on the item, use timeouts with escalation, and split into restartable flows (or a state machine pattern). # Security & governance Q7 An employee found salary files through Copilot. Copilot exposed existing oversharing. Immediately restrict the site (remove broad groups, restricted access control or content discovery), investigate sharing links, run data access governance reports tenant-wide, apply sensitivity labels, and run owner access reviews. Q8 A partner should see only one library on a project site. Prefer a separate site or library with unique permissions for the partner group. Enable guest sharing only on that site with expiration, and use specific-people links. Q9 Audit asks who changed a deviation's severity last year. Dataverse auditing (if enabled at "},{"t":"Technical Lead & Solution Architect Interview Questions","c":"interview","u":"articles/interview/lead-architect-qa.html","s":"Questions for senior roles, covering architecture trade-offs, platform selection, governance, ALM, estimation, stakeholder management, team leadership, transitions and knowledge transfer, quality and risk, with model answers.","k":"interview, technical lead, architect, leadership, governance, questions","h":"Architecture & platform · How do you choose between SharePoint lists, Dataverse and Azure SQL? · How do you decide between Power Apps, SPFx and a custom web app? · What non-functional requirements do you always check? · How do you design for SharePoint Online throttling? · What's your approach to security design? · Delivery & governance · How do you set up ALM for a Power Platform + SPFx program? · How do you estimate a migration project? · How do you handle scope creep? · How do you govern citizen development? · How do you measure solution success? · Leadership · How do you lead a team supporting 50+ SharePoint applications? · How do you run a transition from another vendor? · How do you mentor developers in a mixed low-code/pro-code team? · Tell us about a difficult stakeholder situation. · How do you handle a production incident as lead? · How do you balance speed and quality in low-code projects? · How do you keep up with Microsoft 365 changes? · How would you introduce Copilot safely? · What's your view on technical debt in SharePoint estates? · Why should we hire you as a lead?","l":"Advanced","x":"Scenario & Leadership Rounds","b":"# Architecture & platform Q1 How do you choose between SharePoint lists, Dataverse and Azure SQL? Assess data complexity (relations), volume, security granularity, audit needs, user licensing, who builds and maintains it, and integration needs. Lists suit simple M365-licensed apps, Dataverse suits relational and secure Power Platform apps, and SQL suits existing or reporting-heavy LOB data behind APIs. Record the decision in an ADR. Q2 How do you decide between Power Apps, SPFx and a custom web app? Audience and licensing, UX and performance requirements, team skills, ALM expectations, and where the experience lives (SharePoint pages, Teams, standalone). Mixed solutions are common: Power Apps for data entry, SPFx for intranet UX, APIs for logic. Q3 What non-functional requirements do you always check? Security and compliance, identity, performance and scale (thresholds, delegation), availability, accessibility, monitoring, licensing, data residency, retention, supportability and ALM. Q4 How do you design for SharePoint Online throttling? Minimize calls (delta, batching, selects), use app identities per workload, back off with Retry-After, decorate traffic, schedule heavy jobs off-peak, and use the Migration API for bulk content. Q5 What's your approach to security design? Least privilege (Sites.Selected, scoped roles), managed identities and Key Vault, no secrets in clients, group-based access, labels and DLP, threat modelling for integrations, and access reviews. # Delivery & governance Q6 How do you set up ALM for a Power Platform + SPFx program? Environments (dev, test, prod) with managed solutions, environment variables and connection references, Git source control (unpacked solutions and SPFx repos), pipelines (Azure DevOps or GitHub), solution checker, approvals and release notes. Q7 How do you estimate a migration project? Assessment-driven: volumes, complexity classes per site, customizations rebuild effort, pilot throughput measurements, wave plan, plus testing, communication, hypercare and contingency based on risks. Q8 How do you handle scope creep? A signed BRD baseline, a change request process with impact analysis (scope, cost, timeline), backlog prioritization with the product owner, and transparent trade-offs. Q9 How do you govern citizen development? Environment strategy, DLP, Managed Environments, a CoE kit inventory, maker onboarding, prom"},{"t":"Hands-On Coding Exercises for SharePoint, SPFx, Power Fx and PowerShell Interviews","c":"interview","u":"articles/interview/coding-exercises.html","s":"Practical tasks you may get in technical rounds, with model solutions, covering REST and PnPjs queries, an SPFx component with paging, Power Fx formulas, flow expressions, PnP PowerShell reports, CAML, KQL and a webhook receiver.","k":"interview, coding, exercises, spfx, power fx, powershell, rest","h":"SharePoint APIs · Write a REST call that returns the 10 most recently modified open items with the owner's email. · With PnPjs, add an item and then update it in one batch with another item. · Write CAML for Status = Open AND DueDate before today, ordered by DueDate. · KQL: PDFs in the legal site modified in 2026 containing \"indemnity\". · SPFx · Write a React hook that loads items and cancels state updates on unmount. · Call an Entra-protected API from SPFx. · Power Platform · Power Fx: show \"Overdue\" in red when due date passed and not closed. · Power Fx: save header and child lines in one go. · Flow expression: days between Created and now. · PowerShell · PnP PowerShell: report lists with more than 5,000 items across all sites. · Grant an app write access to one site with Sites.Selected. · Azure · Minimal webhook receiver logic (pseudocode).","l":"Advanced","x":"Scenario & Leadership Rounds","b":"Try each task before opening the answer. # SharePoint APIs Q1 Write a REST call that returns the 10 most recently modified open items with the owner's email. HTTP HTTP Copy GET /_api/web/lists/getbytitle(&#x27;Deviations&#x27;)/items?$select=Id,Title,Modified,Owner/EMail&$expand=Owner&$filter=Status ne &#x27;Closed&#x27;&$orderby=Modified desc&$top=10 Accept : application/json;odata=nometadata Q2 With PnPjs, add an item and then update it in one batch with another item. TypeScript TypeScript Copy const [b, exec] = sp. batched (); const list = b. web . lists . getByTitle ( &#x27;Deviations&#x27; ); list. items . add ({ Title : &#x27;New A&#x27; }); list. items . getById ( 42 ). update ({ Status : &#x27;Closed&#x27; }); await exec (); Q3 Write CAML for Status = Open AND DueDate before today, ordered by DueDate. XML XML Copy < View > < Query > < Where > < And > < Eq > < FieldRef Name = &#x27;Status&#x27; /> < Value Type = &#x27;Choice&#x27; > Open </ Value > </ Eq > < Lt > < FieldRef Name = &#x27;DueDate&#x27; /> < Value Type = &#x27;DateTime&#x27; > < Today /> </ Value > </ Lt > </ And > </ Where > < OrderBy > < FieldRef Name = &#x27;DueDate&#x27; Ascending = &#x27;TRUE&#x27; /> </ OrderBy > </ Query > < RowLimit > 500 </ RowLimit > </ View > Q4 KQL: PDFs in the legal site modified in 2026 containing \"indemnity\". KQL KQL Copy indemnity FileExtension:pdf path:\"https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/legal\" LastModifiedTime>=2026-01-01 # SPFx Q5 Write a React hook that loads items and cancels state updates on unmount. TypeScript TypeScript Copy export function useItems ( service : { get(): Promise < any []> } ) { const [items, setItems] = React . useState < any []>([]); const [error, setError] = React . useState < string >(); React . useEffect ( () => { let alive = true ; service. get (). then ( r => alive && setItems (r)). catch ( e => alive && setError (e. message )); return () => { alive = false ; }; }, [service]); return { items, error }; } Q6 Call an Entra-protected API from SPFx. TypeScript TypeScript Copy const client = await this . context . aadHttpClientFactory . getClient ( &#x27;api://deviation-api&#x27; ); const res = await client. get ( &#x27;https://fn.azurewebsites.net/api/deviations&#x27; , AadHttpClient . configurations . v1 ); const data = await res. json (); # Power Platform Q7 Power Fx: show \"Overdue\" in red when due date passed and not closed. "}]};
